1},{"name":"World Resources Institute (WRI)","count":1},{"name":"XyloPlan","count":1},{"name":"Yale University","count":1}],"access":[{"name":"source","count":478},{"name":"paywall","count":30}]},"records":[{"nnn":319,"title":"Ensuring a Better Future: Innovative Financing for Resilient Communities","authors":"Kanika Singh, Alexander Meeks, Caroline Teare","date":"2026-09-09","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Milken Institute","Marsh","The Nature Conservancy","Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","FM Global"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://milkeninstitute.org/content-hub/research-and-reports/reports/ensuring-better-future-innovative-financing-resilient-communities","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Milken Institute Financial Innovations Lab report, produced in partnership with Marsh, examines barriers to community-level resilience financing and proposes five innovative frameworks: a stakeholder-driven Community Plan with insurer engagement, a private-sector-led revolving loan fund, a district-backed resilience bond, a resilience innovation technology investment fund, and a state policy playbook. Grounded in the 2025 Los Angeles wildfires ($76\u2013$131B in losses), the report draws on 75 stakeholder interviews and a Los Angeles workshop to identify capital structures that align divergent investor risk/return requirements at scale.","insights":["Global natural disaster economic losses have exceeded $200B annually since 2016, with insured losses topping $100B for six consecutive years through 2025, yet uninsured losses now account for more than half of total global losses, signaling a widening protection gap that transfers costs to government and community balance sheets.","The 2025 LA Eaton and Palisades fires caused $76\u2013$131B in estimated losses; one year later, 7 in 10 survivors remained displaced and over half faced losses exceeding their annual income, with a $5B underinsurance gap documented at the household level.","Florida mangroves provided an estimated $1.54B in flood-risk-reduction benefits during Hurricane Irma (2017), averaging $7,500/hectare, with Lee County alone receiving $802.6M in benefits, a quantified unit-economics proof point for nature-based coastal protection.","A Marsh/Nature Conservancy study found that combining wildfire buffer zones with updated building codes would have reduced wildfire losses in Paradise, CA by up to 42%; IBHS FORTIFIED roof standards, if universally applied in Hurricane Sally's path, could have cut policyholder losses 65% and insurer losses 75%.","FM Global's Resilience Credit, a 5% premium discount launched at $300M, estimated $20B in client loss reductions; doubling the credit to 10% (costing $825M in foregone premiums) is projected to save clients an additional $35B/year, demonstrating a quantifiable insurer-client ROI from ex-ante resilience investment.","Seoul's Cheonggyecheon stream restoration yielded a 76% increase in foot traffic for local businesses, 30\u201340% land value appreciation, 15% increase in bus revenues, and a 45% reduction in urban heat island effect, illustrating how resilience ROI can be captured across economic, fiscal, and public health dimensions."],"keywords":["revolving loan fund","community resilience district","wildfire risk mitigation","insurance protection gap","nature-based flood protection","parametric insurance","resilience bond","benefit-cost quantification"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":318,"title":"California's Disaster Recovery Financing Districts: New Tools for Local Recovery","authors":"Alexander Meeks","date":"2026-09-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["California","US"],"institutions":["Milken Institute","California Forward","HR&A Advisors","Insurance for Good","Federal Reserve Bank of New York","Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://milkeninstitute.org/content-hub/research-and-reports/reports/californias-disaster-recovery-financing-districts-new-tools-local-recovery","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Milken Institute report (September 2026) analyzes California's newly authorized Disaster Recovery Financing Districts (DRFDs), created by SB 782 in October 2025 in response to the January 2025 Eaton and Palisades wildfires. It traces the policy lineage from redevelopment agencies through Proposition 13 to today's constrained tax increment financing tools, quantifies projected TIF revenues for the Altadena ($734M) and Unincorporated Santa Monica Mountains ($245M) DRFDs against rebuilding budgets of $2B and $233M respectively, and outlines implementation principles for braiding TIF with grants, CRA lending, revolving loan funds, and innovative insurance arrangements.","insights":["Altadena DRFD projects $734M in present-value TIF revenues over 50 years against a $2B rebuilding and resilience budget, TIF covers roughly 37% of estimated need, confirming it is one tool among many.","Unincorporated Santa Monica Mountains DRFD projects $245M in TIF revenues against a $233M rebuilding budget, making it the more revenue-adequate district, though grant dependence remains significant.","Pacific Palisades DRFD analysis shows a wide $325M\u2013$1.9B TIF revenue range over the district period, reflecting deep uncertainty in rebuilding pace, property turnover, and densification assumptions.","Proposition 13 caps assessed value growth at 2% annually, meaning a home appreciating $100K in market value yields only $280/year in incremental TIF revenue, structural constraint that makes post-disaster TIF slow to ramp.","LA County estimates $18.3M/year in recurring general fund revenue reduction from the Altadena DRFD by 2035 and $751K/year from the Santa Monica Mountains DRFD by 2036, quantifying the fiscal cost to surrounding communities.","Property tax relief policies for fire survivors (Prop 19 transfers, disaster valuation freezes) directly suppress TIF revenue, creating a tension between protecting individual homeowners and generating community-wide recovery capital.","The Delta Region Geologic Hazard Abatement District in Isleton, CA piloted a parametric insurance product disbursing immediate liquidity to property owners upon a flood threshold trigger, cited as a replicable model for LA DRFDs.","CRA-eligible disaster-affected census tracts (within 3 years of declared disaster) open a pathway for private bank lending motivated by Community Reinvestment Act obligations, supplementing scarce TIF dollars for projects that can support low-cost debt."],"keywords":["tax increment financing","disaster recovery financing districts","land value capture","wildfire recovery","Proposition 13","community reinvestment act","parametric insurance","revolving loan fund"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":310,"title":"What Is PACE?","authors":"","date":"2026-08-31","year":2026,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["PACENation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.pacenation.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/PACENation-What-is-PACE-Factsheet.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This PACENation fact sheet (data through August 2023) provides an overview of Property Assessed Clean Energy (PACE) financing, a tool enabling 100% fixed-rate, long-term (up to 30-year) funding for energy efficiency, renewables, water efficiency, resilience, and public health improvements secured by a voluntary property assessment. As of mid-2023, $8.6B has been deployed on 350,000+ residential properties and $6.3B on 3,200+ commercial properties across 30 active states plus D.C. Estimated lifetime community benefits include $3.9B in avoided natural disaster losses, $12B in utility bill savings, and 214,000 job-years created.","insights":["PACE has deployed $14.9B+ cumulatively (through August 2023): $8.6B across 350,000+ residential properties and $6.3B across 3,200+ commercial properties, demonstrating proven deployment at scale across both market segments.","$3.9B in avoided losses from natural disasters is attributed to PACE-financed resilience improvements (estimated over useful lifetime of installed measures through June 2023), providing a concrete quantified benefit for capital allocators evaluating resilience ROI.","The addressable market for deep building retrofits and electrification of U.S. buildings is estimated at $2+ trillion, indicating substantial runway for PACE financing as a private-capital instrument that does not require public subsidy.","PACE assessments are non-recourse and non-accelerating, transferring automatically upon property sale, a structural feature that differentiates PACE from conventional debt and reduces lender risk while simplifying borrower exit.","38 states and D.C. have enacted PACE-enabling legislation, with active programs in 30 states plus D.C., demonstrating broad but still incomplete policy coverage; residential PACE remains limited to California, Florida, and Missouri.","Estimated lifetime community benefits include 53B kWh of energy saved, 14M metric tons of avoided carbon emissions, $12B in utility bill savings, and $29B in total economic impact, offering a multi-dimensional value case for ESG-oriented allocators.","Over 90 active C-PACE lenders are operating across 30+ states, indicating a competitive and liquid private lending market rather than a single-source or government-dependent structure.","PACE covers 100% of hard and soft costs with fixed-rate fully amortizing terms up to 30 years, enabling projects that may be capital-constrained under conventional lending, including retrofits at healthcare, industrial, multifamily, and mixed-use properties."],"keywords":["property assessed clean energy","building retrofits","C-PACE","resilience financing","energy efficiency","non-recourse assessment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":300,"title":"Can Investors Price the Risk? Assessing Municipal Bond Climate Disclosure and Financial Resilience","authors":"Jake Rascoff, Holly Li, Eunyoung Lee","date":"2026-08-18","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Ceres"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ceres.org/resources/reports/can-investors-price-the-risk-assessing-municipal-bond-climate-disclosure-and-financial-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This August 2026 Ceres report analyzes climate risk disclosure quality across 60 municipal bond offerings from the 20 highest climate-risk U.S. metro areas (by Cotality Composite Risk Score). It finds that current disclosure practices are inadequate for investor pricing: one-third of bonds make no climate mention, only 15% disclose governance accountability, and just 12% provide quantifiable metrics. The $4.4 trillion municipal bond market finances over 70% of U.S. infrastructure, making disclosure quality a systemic credit risk issue. The report uses TCFD as the scoring framework and cross-references Kestrel Environmental Scores to identify a \"Silent Achievers\" gap where resilience investments go undisclosed.","insights":["One-third (33%) of 60 sampled municipal bond offerings from the 20 highest-risk U.S. metros make no mention of climate or extreme weather risk, while only 12% provide the quantitative metrics investors need to assess and price risk.","TCFD alignment is highly uneven across pillars: Strategy is addressed in 55% of bonds, Risk Management in 33%, Governance in 15%, and Metrics & Targets in only 12%, the pillar most critical for investor comparison and capital pricing.","The U.S. experienced 23 separate billion-dollar disasters in 2025 totaling more than $115 billion in damages, nearly four times the annual average of the 1990s, directly threatening the tax bases and fiscal stability that underpin municipal bond repayment.","Miami-Dade County's 2025 GO bond exemplifies best practice: it quantifies that a National Flood Insurance Program rating improvement (from a5 to a3) saves residents and businesses an estimated $12 million annually via a 35% flood insurance premium discount, a direct, investor-legible benefit of resilience investment.","LADWP's 2025 Water System Revenue Bonds disclose a layered wildfire insurance program totaling $273.65 million (including a $100M CAT bond for 2025\u20132028), providing concrete financial metrics for wildfire risk management that are largely absent from peer disclosures.","A 'Silent Achievers' cohort, issuers with high Kestrel Environmental Scores but low TCFD alignment, cannot capture potential borrowing cost reductions because their resilience investments are invisible to the bond market; approximately 15% of the $3.2 trillion Kestrel-covered universe exhibits resilience features.","Bonds issued by different entities in the same county can have materially different disclosure quality (e.g., Birmingham metro), confirming that disclosure is not driven by underlying risk but by issuer practice, undermining market comparability."],"keywords":["municipal bond disclosure","TCFD alignment","physical risk pricing","climate risk scoring","bond market transparency","forward-looking scenario analysis","silent achievers"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":331,"title":"IDB Strengthens Belize's Hurricane Resilience with First Catastrophe Swap","authors":"","date":"2026-08-14","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Caribbean","Latin America"],"institutions":["Inter-American Development Bank","Swiss Re"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.iadb.org/en/news/idb-strengthens-belizes-hurricane-resilience-first-catastrophe-swap","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Inter-American Development Bank (IDB) executed its first-ever catastrophe swap for Belize, transferring a layer of hurricane risk to international reinsurance markets via Swiss Re, which provides the underlying parametric coverage. The instrument offers up to $20 million in coverage (maximum $10 million per policy year) running August 2026\u2013May 2028, enabling rapid post-hurricane liquidity. The transaction complements $25 million in IDB contingent credit and $118 million in loans protected by Climate Resilient Debt Clauses, forming a layered disaster risk-financing strategy for Belize under the FIRRe and Ready and Resilient Americas frameworks.","insights":["The catastrophe swap provides up to $20 million in hurricane risk coverage for Belize, capped at $10 million per policy year, spanning two hurricane seasons (August 2026\u2013May 2028).","Belize's total layered disaster risk-finance package reaches at least $163 million when combining the $20M cat swap, $25M IDB Contingent Credit Facility, and $118M in Climate Resilient Debt Clause-protected loans.","The transaction is the IDB's first catastrophe swap, positioning it as a replicable blueprint for transferring sovereign disaster risk to international reinsurance markets across Latin America and the Caribbean.","Swiss Re provides the underlying parametric coverage, meaning payouts are triggered by pre-agreed hurricane parameters rather than assessed losses, enabling faster post-event liquidity access.","Funding for the premium drew on three sources, technical cooperation from the Ready and Resilient Americas Initiative, donor contributions from the French Climate Fund, and the Government of Belize's own contribution, illustrating a blended finance structure for instrument affordability.","Climate Resilient Debt Clauses on $118 million in IDB loans allow Belize to defer principal payments for two years following an eligible natural disaster, reducing post-shock fiscal stress without requiring new borrowing.","The FIRRe (Ready and Resilient Americas financial instruments) framework is the vehicle through which the IDB intends to scale this cat swap model to other LAC sovereigns, signaling a programmatic rather than one-off approach.","The structure demonstrates how a multilateral development bank can act as arranger, rather than risk-bearer, channeling sovereign disaster risk to private reinsurance capacity while using concessional resources to subsidize the premium."],"keywords":["catastrophe swap","parametric coverage","disaster risk financing","sovereign risk transfer","hurricane liquidity","FIRRe"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":480,"title":"United Policyholders Receives Grant from the American Red Cross to Expand Roadmap to Recovery\u00ae and Preparedness Services to Los Angeles Wildfire-Impacted Communities","authors":"","date":"2026-08-13","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["United Policyholders","American Red Cross"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://uphelp.org/press-release-8-13-26/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"United Policyholders, a national 501(c)(3) consumer advocacy organization founded in 1991, announced on 13 August 2026 that it had received a $650,000 grant from the American Red Cross for the Roadmap to Resilience Project, a 17-month initiative expanding insurance recovery assistance to households hit by the 2025 Los Angeles wildfires and increasing disaster readiness and financial health in high-hazard regions across Southern California. The work covers Pacific Palisades, Altadena, Malibu and surrounding fire-impacted areas. United Policyholders will update the printed Disaster Recovery Handbook and launch a digital version in English and Spanish, removing the physical distribution barrier for displaced survivors and covering insurance processes, financial and re-housing decisions and casualty loss tax rules. It will deliver specialized technical training and direct support to Disaster Case Managers and Long-Term Recovery Groups operating on the ground. The organization's own multi-year post-wildfire surveys find that most disaster-impacted households face prolonged negotiations, disputes over restoration cost and insufficient coverage for temporary living expenses. The grant also expands the Roadmap to Preparedness programme, running workshops for homeowners and renters in impacted and high-risk areas to identify and fix coverage gaps before the next disaster and to build insurance literacy.","insights":["The intervention is on the claims side rather than the coverage side: it funds help extracting value from policies households already hold, which is where United Policyholders' own surveys locate the loss, in prolonged negotiation, disputed restoration cost and insufficient additional living expense cover.","Underinsurance for temporary living expenses is named as a recurring finding across multiple post-wildfire surveys, which makes it a structural product defect rather than a feature of one event.","Philanthropic capital, from a disaster relief organization rather than an insurer or a regulator, is paying for the consumer-side capacity that closes the gap between a policy and a settlement.","The preparedness half, workshops on identifying and fixing coverage gaps before a loss, is an attempt to convert post-disaster demand for help into pre-disaster insurance literacy in the same high-hazard communities."],"keywords":["United Policyholders","insurance literacy","underinsurance","additional living expenses","Los Angeles wildfires","claims advocacy","Disaster Case Managers"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":384,"title":"2026 Proposed Changes to the Community Reinvestment Act","authors":"Susie Han","date":"2026-08-12","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Reserve Board","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Enterprise Community Partners","Opportunity Finance Network"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ofn.org/blog/2026-proposed-changes-to-the-community-reinvestment-act/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Opportunity Finance Network (OFN) policy brief analyzes a 2026 joint OCC-FDIC Notice of Proposed Rulemaking that would significantly restructure Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) regulations. The proposal raises asset-size thresholds, exempting an estimated 800 banks from the Community Development test and removing 376 banks from large-bank treatment. OFN estimates $2 billion per year in community finance is at risk. The brief highlights threats to bank-CDFI partnerships, LIHTC and NMTC demand, operating grant eligibility, and data availability, while noting some positive provisions including explicit CRA credit for disaster preparedness and resilience activities.","insights":["The proposed OCC/FDIC rulemaking would move ~800 banks from intermediate to small classification, exempting more than 75% of all U.S. banks from the Community Development (CD) test and putting an estimated $2 billion per year in community finance at risk.","376 banks would shift from large to intermediate classification, reducing their obligation to report small business, small farm, and CD loan data and weakening the standalone investment test that incentivizes LIHTC and NMTC equity investments.","LIHTC has financed ~3.7 million affordable homes since inception; NMTC has deployed $63 billion into low-income communities, supported 888,000+ jobs, and financed 268 million sq ft of commercial real estate, both programs face reduced bank demand if CRA incentives are diluted.","In FY 2024, OFN members received $6.2 billion in borrowed funds from CRA-motivated banks, representing ~46% of their total borrowed funds, illustrating the financial materiality of CRA regulatory structure for CDFI capitalization.","47% of the ~800 banks newly classified as small are headquartered in rural areas, making the impact particularly acute in markets with fewer financial institutions and less access to private capital.","The proposal explicitly grants CRA credit for 'disaster preparedness and resilience activities,' a notable positive provision that could unlock new bank investment flows toward community resilience infrastructure.","Restricting CRA credit for operating grants (including a 15% overhead cap for large banks) would reduce flexible capital to CDFIs, undermining their capacity to reach hard-to-serve borrowers and deploy CRA-eligible lending at scale."],"keywords":["Community Reinvestment Act","CDFI partnerships","bank regulatory reform","Low-Income Housing Tax Credit","New Markets Tax Credit","credit deserts","community development finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":441,"title":"Once Cities Implement a Stormwater Utility, They Don't Stop","authors":"The Epicenter","date":"2026-08-07","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Western Kentucky University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.epicenterinsights.com/the-weekly-once-cities-implement-a-stormwater-utility-they-dont-stop/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A report on the spread of the stormwater utility as a municipal funding mechanism, drawing on Western Kentucky University researcher Warren Campbell's 2026 survey, which counted 2,202 stormwater utilities in the United States, a 22 per cent increase since 2020. A stormwater utility charges property owners according to how much runoff they send into the system and uses the revenue to fund stormwater infrastructure, which the article describes as a blueprint for pricing climate risk into the built environment by shifting the financial burden onto the physical footprint of development. The average monthly single-family residential fee was $6.33. The model scales in both directions: the smallest identified utility serves Indian Creek Village, Florida, population 84, and the largest covers Los Angeles County, population over 10 million. The context is that downpours have grown heavier by an average of 30 per cent across the continental United States and 60 per cent in the Northeast since 1958 while drainage systems have aged. The durability finding is the striking one: of all the utilities Campbell has tracked, only 13 have ever been repealed.","insights":["The durability is the finding: only 13 stormwater utilities have ever been repealed out of more than 2,200, so once adopted the mechanism essentially never reverses, which is rare for a local charge and makes it an unusually reliable revenue base to borrow against.","The fee prices the physical footprint of development rather than assessed value, which is a direct mechanism for charging climate risk to the party creating the runoff.","Scale is not a constraint in either direction, from a village of 84 people to Los Angeles County, which removes the usual objection that a utility only works for large jurisdictions.","An average monthly residential fee of $6.33 is the political price of the mechanism, and it is low enough to explain the near-absence of repeals.","The hazard trend is quantified independently of the mechanism: downpours up an average 30 per cent nationally and 60 per cent in the Northeast since 1958."],"keywords":["stormwater utility","runoff fee","municipal revenue","green infrastructure","heavy rainfall","Los Angeles County","utility adoption","risk pricing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":338,"title":"Colombia Strengthens Climate Resilience with Parametric Insurance for 14,402 Smallholder Farmers","authors":"","date":"2026-08-06","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Latin America"],"institutions":["Guy Carpenter","Swiss Re","Munich RE","Gallagher Re","World Bank","United Nations Development Programme","Insurance Development Forum"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://irff.undp.org/press-releases/colombia-strengthens-climate-resilience-parametric-insurance-14402-smallholder","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Colombian government, led by the Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development (MARD) and FINAGRO, contracted five parametric agricultural insurance policies covering 14,402 smallholder farmers (and up to 41,600 household members), across five departments (Sucre, C\u00f3rdoba, Cundinamarca, Meta, and Choc\u00f3). Policies provide up to US$20.14 million in coverage against drought and excess rainfall, triggered automatically via a satellite-derived Water Balance Index. Delivered through a public-private partnership under the Tripartite Agreement Programme (UNDP, IDF, BMZ/ISF), this marks the first use of parametric insurance by Colombia's departmental governments for agricultural risk management, with explicit intent to scale nationally.","insights":["The five parametric policies provide up to US$20.14 million in coverage for 14,402 smallholder farmers and up to 41,600 household members across five Colombian departments, representing a first-of-its-kind deployment at the departmental government level in Colombia.","Payouts are triggered automatically by a satellite-derived Water Balance Index (rainfall minus evapotranspiration at the municipal level), eliminating post-disaster loss assessments and enabling faster capital disbursement to affected producers, a key basis risk reduction feature for capital allocators evaluating parametric structures.","Premium co-financing by the Government of Colombia and the InsuResilience Solutions Fund (ISF) in year one demonstrates a blended finance model designed to make parametric insurance affordable for sovereign/sub-sovereign agricultural programs and to catalyze private reinsurance capacity.","The initiative reduces smallholder dependence on uncertain or delayed emergency funding, substituting a pre-agreed parametric trigger mechanism for ad hoc disaster relief, a structural shift with implications for sovereign fiscal planning and donor grant programming.","The product was co-designed and co-financed by a consortium of IDF members (Guy Carpenter, Swiss Re, AXA Climate, Munich Re, Raincoat, La Previsora S.A.), illustrating a replicable public-private co-design model that embeds technical, legal, and financial groundwork for scaling across additional departments.","UNDP's role extended beyond product development to institutional capacity building within Colombia's agricultural risk management framework, positioning this as a scalable policy infrastructure investment rather than a one-off pilot."],"keywords":["parametric agricultural insurance","smallholder farmers","El Ni\u00f1o","Water Balance Index","blended finance","InsuResilience Solutions Fund","Colombia agriculture"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":341,"title":"Wildfire Exposed Catastrophe Bond Issuance Soars to $5.183bn Year-to-Date in 2026","authors":"","date":"2026-08-03","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Artemis"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/news/wildfire-exposed-catastrophe-bond-issuance-soars-to-5-183bn-year-to-date-in-2026/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Artemis reports that wildfire-exposed catastrophe bond issuance reached $5.183 billion year-to-date in 2026 (through early July), across 20 individual cat bond series, already matching the full-year 2025 count and nearing the 2025 full-year record of $5.55 billion. The surge reflects growing investor familiarity with wildfire risk models, prudent ILS structuring, and iterative pricing refinements highlighted by reinsurance broker Acrisure Re. Three pure wildfire cat bonds in 144A format have been issued in 2026, one behind the all-time annual record of four set in 2025, signaling that wildfire is becoming a more meaningful standalone peril in capital markets.","insights":["Wildfire-exposed cat bond issuance hit $5.183 billion year-to-date in 2026 (20 deals), already equaling the full-year 2025 deal count and approaching the 2025 full-year volume record of $5.55 billion.","Issuance has grown rapidly from $2.57 billion in 2023 to $2.84 billion in 2024 to $5.55 billion in 2025, more than doubling in two years, and 2026 is on pace to set a new annual record.","Three pure wildfire cat bonds in 144A format were issued in the first half of 2026, one fewer than the four issued across all of 2025, which was the most in market history; this signals the wildfire peril is maturing as a standalone capital markets risk transfer category.","The majority of wildfire-exposed cat bond volume comes from multi-peril transactions where wildfire is one covered peril, a structure that has helped investors build comfort with the risk incrementally.","Acrisure Re attributes investor appetite growth to two factors: iterative pricing and coverage refinements from each fire season, and what it calls a 'profound' effect from improved wildfire risk models on investor confidence.","Growing ILS market capacity for wildfire is providing direct reinsurance support to insurers and policyholders in high-risk areas such as California, where property and livelihood exposure to wildfire remains acute.","The acceleration since 2022 suggests a structural shift in capital market willingness to absorb wildfire risk, with implications for insurance availability and affordability in wildfire-prone regions."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond issuance","wildfire reinsurance capacity","insurance-linked securities","risk capital deployment","ILS market growth"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":312,"title":"The Role of Insurance in Funding and Financing Resilience","authors":"Carolyn Kousky, Xuesong You","date":"2026-08-03","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["California","US","Alabama","Louisiana"],"institutions":["Insurance for Good","California Forward","Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","Guy Carpenter","RMS","Environmental Defense Fund","Encourage Capital","Blue Forest","JPMorgan Chase","Fannie Mae","Natural Resources Defense Council","The Resiliency Company","re:focus partners","Gallagher Re"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://static1.squarespace.com/static/629aa6f68914ce47a1d72993/t/6a71f7ee46dbd20b710581be/1785853934443/I4G+and+CA+FWD+compressed.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This white paper from Insurance for Good and California Forward examines how insurance and parametric risk-transfer instruments can fund and finance physical climate resilience. It evaluates four mechanisms: premium reductions tied to verified mitigation (FORTIFIED roofing, wildfire hardening), post-disaster \"build-back-better\" endorsements, insurer education and policy advocacy, and parametric cat-bond structures. Key proof points include 25\u201355% mandatory premium discounts for FORTIFIED homes in Alabama, a 22% average premium saving in Louisiana, and a first-of-its-kind catastrophe bond by the North Carolina Insurance Underwriting Association that routes up to $2M annually into FORTIFIED roof upgrades during low-loss years.","insights":["FORTIFIED roof construction reduced insured losses by 51\u201372% following Hurricane Sally (Alabama, 2020), with 55\u201374% fewer claims and 14\u201340% lower average claim costs, among the strongest empirical validations of a building standard in the U.S. market.","Alabama mandates premium discounts of 25\u201355% (varying by county hurricane risk) for FORTIFIED homes; Louisiana targets 20\u201330% discounts with an observed average saving of 22%, demonstrating that actuarially-justified mitigation incentives are achievable at scale.","The North Carolina Insurance Underwriting Association embedded a resilience mechanism in a catastrophe bond: NCIUA contributes an extra 0.35% annually into a resilience account that pays out over $2M for FORTIFIED upgrades in years where losses stay below ~$1.68B, creating a pre-funded resilience reserve without new appropriations.","Upgrading to a FORTIFIED roof at time of rebuilding adds only 1\u20133% to new construction costs ($700\u2013$1,700 per home by IBHS estimates), and wildfire-resistant rebuilding adds less than 10% to reconstruction cost, indicating that build-back-better endorsements of \u226410% of replacement cost could cover most necessary upgrades.","Premium reductions face a structural financing gap: discounts are annual and variable, making them insufficient as collateral for upfront retrofit loans. No premium-reduction-backed financing model has yet succeeded at scale, partly because insurers cannot lock in multi-year contracts and face free-rider risk if policyholders switch carriers after retrofits.","California's Safer from Wildfires regulation (2022) requires insurers to offer discounts for up to 12 wildfire risk-reduction measures but does not mandate discount magnitudes; Insurance for Good analysis finds wide variation, with single-measure discounts being minimal and meaningful reductions requiring bundled actions."],"keywords":["FORTIFIED building standard","build-back-better endorsement","parametric cat bond","premium discount mitigation","wildfire home hardening","residual insurance market","actuarial risk pricing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":304,"title":"The Compound Effect: Heat, Water, and the New Risk Landscape","authors":"Ehsan Najafi, Colin Gannon, Auguste Boissonnade, Shree Khare, Max Stephens","date":"2026-08-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Moody's","World Resources Institute (WRI)","Ceres","World Bank Group (WBG)","World Meteorological Organization","United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP)","Union of Concerned Scientists (UCS)","US Environmental Protection Agency","U.S. Army Corps of Engineers (USACE)","Center for Disaster Philanthropy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.moodys.com/web/en/us/site-assets/heat-water-stress-whitepaper-080826.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Moody's Insurance Solutions white paper (August 2026) analyzes the compound physical risk posed by heat stress and water stress, framed around Europe's 2026 heatwave sequence and US industrial exposure data. Using the ORBIS database (~159,000 US water-intensive facilities) and Moody's Climate on Demand (MCoD) models under RCP 4.5 and RCP 8.5, it finds ~49,000 US facilities projected to transition into high or very high water stress by 2100, with Texas (~17,000) and California (~5,800) most exposed. CDP-reported corporate water risk exposure totals $225B (2022). The paper argues parametric insurance structures are better suited than indemnity products to price chronic, compounding perils, and calls on insurers, reinsurers, and investors to embed forward-looking heat and water projections into underwriting, valuation, and capital allocation.","insights":["~49,000 of 159,000 sampled US water-intensive facilities are projected to transition from low/moderate to high/very high water stress by 2100 under RCP 8.5, with Texas (~17,000) and California (~5,800) accounting for the largest concentrations, a material, currently underpriced shift in operational and credit risk for heavy manufacturing (61% of sample), food and beverage (25%), and mining (14%).","CDP-reporting companies disclosed $225B in self-assessed financial exposure to water risk (2022); CDP's 2020 Global Water Report found up to $301B in potential business value at risk if water risks go unaddressed, more than five times the estimated $55B cost of action, a 5:1 benefit-cost ratio for water risk mitigation investment.","Global water demand is projected to outstrip supply by 40% by 2030 (GCEW, 2023), and billions more people are expected to face water shortages by mid-century, framing water stress as a systemic, not idiosyncratic, risk that undermines conventional insurance diversification assumptions.","Europe's 2026 heatwave sequence (May\u2013July) caused over 2,000 deaths in Spain and France combined, strained power grids (Italy peak demand 15% above May levels), forced nuclear output reductions in France due to high river temperatures, and reduced Po River delta flow by two-thirds in under two weeks, illustrating how acute heat events translate rapidly into water infrastructure and energy system stress.","Heat and water stress compound into a third hazard: wildfire. Modeling projects significant increases in fire-weather severity across 68\u201391% of fire-prone areas by 2100, with at least 55% affected by 2040; Europe's 2025 wildfire season was the worst on record (>1 million hectares burned since records began in 2006), directly linking heat-water stress to insured and economic loss escalation."],"keywords":["compound risk","wet-bulb temperature","water stress","parametric insurance","ORBIS dataset","heat stress modeling","chronic physical risk","wildfire compound hazard"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":585,"title":"The Verdict From The Resilient America Summit: Financing Resilience Is A Coordination Problem","authors":"Matt Posner","date":"2026-07-31","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["The Resiliency Company"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.epicenterinsights.com/the-verdict-from-the-resilient-america-summit-financing-resilience-is-a-coordination-problem/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article of 31 July 2026 in The Epicenter by Matt Posner, contributing author and Head of Public Finance for The Resiliency Company, reporting three takeaways from the first Resilient America Summit, held on 23 July in Chicago. The summit brought nearly 100 leaders from state and local government, public finance, insurance, philanthropy, infrastructure, investment and the wider resilience ecosystem together around one question: what will it take to move resilience from a recognised need to something communities can actually finance and deliver at scale. Resilient America is a new initiative of The Resiliency Company and this was its inaugural summit. The article's headline conclusion is that financing resilience is a coordination problem.","insights":["The summit's central claim is that the binding constraint is coordination rather than capital or pricing: the money and the instruments exist, and what fails is agreement between the parties who would have to act together.","The convening list is the useful artifact. Public finance, insurance, philanthropy and infrastructure in one room is the set of parties a blended resilience structure needs, and the summit's premise is that they do not normally meet.","**The author is Head of Public Finance for the company that convened the summit**, so this is a participant's account of its own event. Recorded as provenance: the observation about coordination is worth keeping and the framing is not independent."],"keywords":["Resilient America Summit","coordination problem","public finance","resilience at scale","municipal finance","practitioner convening"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":477,"title":"Paying for Resilience in New York State","authors":"","date":"2026-07-23","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design","New York University","Cornell University","University at Albany"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/paying-for-resilience-in-new-york-state/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Rebuild by Design, with the New York State Adaptation Practitioners Network, published a first-of-its-kind statewide inventory of known public sector climate adaptation costs on 23 July 2026, finding that New York State and its municipalities will need to spend $519 billion of taxpayer money to build, upgrade or retrofit infrastructure through 2050. The statewide per capita cost is approximately $26,000; New York City's is $50,000, the highest of any region, driven by construction and labor costs, limited space for nature-based solutions and the complexity of protecting dense populations and interconnected underground systems. The most expensive categories are sewer and stormwater upgrades and coastal defense on Long Island and in New York City. The analysis compiled over 700 projects and plans through desktop research informed by practitioners statewide, covering storm surge and sea level rise, erosion, grid resilience, stormwater and green infrastructure, shoreline and habitat restoration, disaster preparedness and resilience hubs, heat mitigation, wind and storm damage protection, flood risk reduction and sewer upgrades. The report states the true cost is unknown because most communities lack capacity to conduct a comprehensive needs assessment. On the resource side, the 2022 $4.2 billion Environmental Bond Act has already allocated $2.2 billion, the 2024 Climate Change Superfund Law would assign fossil fuel companies an estimated $75 billion but faces lawsuits from 22 states and industry plus a federal executive order and proposed Congressional preemption, and federal resources continue to shrink. The report cites a U.S. Chamber of Commerce figure that every $1 not invested in disaster resilience can cost up to $33 in lost future economic activity, and notes that credit rating agencies increasingly incorporate climate risk into municipal bond ratings, so communities that fail to invest may face higher borrowing costs.","insights":["A $519 billion need against $2 billion of remaining Environmental Bond Act capacity is the central arithmetic: the identified state funding source covers well under one per cent of the identified need, which is why the report is really about finding a revenue stream.","The per capita framing, $26,000 statewide and $50,000 in New York City, converts an infrastructure total into a household liability and is the number that makes the case politically legible.","The report is explicit that its total is a floor and biased downward in the places with least capacity, because the estimate counts only documented needs and small municipalities have not assessed their risk, so the gap is widest exactly where it is least visible.","The Climate Change Superfund Law is the one identified source at the scale of the problem, $75 billion, and it is under simultaneous attack from state litigation, industry litigation, an executive order and proposed federal preemption, so the largest line item in the funding plan is the least certain.","The credit rating channel is named as a cost of inaction: failing to invest raises the cost of borrowing for the investment, which is a feedback loop that makes delay progressively more expensive."],"keywords":["adaptation cost inventory","Environmental Bond Act","Climate Change Superfund Law","polluter pays","per capita adaptation cost","municipal bond rating","New York State"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":439,"title":"Financing the Missing Asset: Why Municipal Bonds Have Struggled to Finance Climate Resilience","authors":"Matt Posner","date":"2026-07-17","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["The Resiliency Company"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.epicenterinsights.com/financing-the-missing-asset-why-municipal-bonds-have-struggled-to-finance-climate-resilience/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Part one of a series on embedding resilience into public finance. Posner argues the municipal bond market should be the natural home of resilience finance, since municipal bonds already are the backbone of American infrastructure finance and infrastructure investment is fundamentally resilience investment, and that it has failed to become so in two opposite ways. On one side are transactions that are too narrow: bespoke, highly engineered deals, among them environmental impact bonds, blended capital structures and special purpose vehicles, each of which reinvents its own rules and therefore cannot scale, forfeiting the very scalability that makes securities useful. On the other side are transactions that are too wide: general obligation issuances in which resilience outcomes become impossible to track or measure. The result he names is fewer bankable projects, a higher cost of capital, and a market that cannot fund resilience at scale. His proposal is a middle route, standardised enough to scale and specific enough to measure, sized against a cited $1 trillion of needed infrastructure repairs across 2,000 US cities.","insights":["The diagnosis is symmetrical and that is the argument: bespoke deals fail because they cannot scale, and general obligation issuance fails because the resilience outcome cannot be measured, so neither end of the existing market can carry adaptation.","Environmental impact bonds and blended capital structures are named as examples of the too-narrow failure, which is a direct challenge to the instruments the field most often celebrates.","The consequence is priced: fewer bankable projects and a higher cost of capital, so the structural problem shows up as an interest rate rather than as an absence of intent.","The proposed middle route is standardisation, which is the same move the community land trust lending certification and the FORTIFIED standard make in their own markets: a class of deal a lender can underwrite without bespoke diligence."],"keywords":["municipal bonds","resilience finance","environmental impact bond","standardization","cost of capital","general obligation","public infrastructure","scalability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":432,"title":"Road to Resilience: Town Highway Disaster Reserve Benchmarks","authors":"","date":"2026-07-15","year":2026,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Vermont"],"institutions":["Vermont Bond Bank","Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.vtbondbank.org/reservebenchmarks","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Vermont Bond Bank released the first Town Highway Disaster Reserve Benchmarks, a financial planning tool and online database giving every Vermont town, city and village a data-driven estimate of the annual contribution needed to build an adequate disaster reserve for local roads and bridges. The benchmarks combine 16 years of inflation-adjusted FEMA Public Assistance data with the Bond Bank's own database of municipal financial statements and VTrans' Transportation Resilience Planning Tool. The headline finding is encouraging and the caveat is the substance: a typical Vermont community needs to set aside less than 1 per cent of its annual budget to meet its benchmark, but that amount would quadruple without FEMA assistance, and more than 40 per cent of towns would need more than half their annual budget to cover losses from a large event. The release situates the tool in a track record that began after the July 2023 floods, when the Bond Bank helped create the Municipal Climate Recovery Fund, which has since lent more than $33 million to 27 municipalities for recovery, and it is the first of a planned series of Bond Bank disaster resilience tools.","insights":["The federal dependency is quantified rather than asserted: the required municipal reserve quadruples without FEMA assistance, and more than 40 per cent of towns would need over half their annual budget to absorb a large event, which turns federal policy uncertainty into a municipal balance sheet number.","Under 1 per cent of annual budget as the typical benchmark contribution is a genuinely actionable figure, because it is small enough to be adopted by a town meeting.","The method joins FEMA Public Assistance history to the Bond Bank's own municipal financial statement database, which is a combination only a state bond bank could assemble and is what makes the benchmark specific to each town.","A state bond bank is acting as a risk analytics provider and a lender at once, having already deployed $33 million to 27 municipalities through the Municipal Climate Recovery Fund created after the July 2023 floods."],"keywords":["disaster reserve","municipal finance","Vermont Bond Bank","FEMA Public Assistance","town highways","reserve benchmarks","flood recovery","bridge loans"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":542,"title":"Multilateral Development Banks Increased Climate Financing to $163 Billion in 2025, Supporting Climate-Resilient and Sustainable Growth","authors":"","date":"2026-07-13","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Inter-American Development Bank","European Investment Bank","European Bank for Reconstruction and Development","African Development Bank","Asian Development Bank","Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.iadb.org/en/news/multilateral-development-banks-increase-climate-finance-163-billion-2025-supporting-climate","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Multilateral development banks reported record climate financing for 2025 in their joint annual report: $163 billion across all countries of operation, up 19 per cent, of which $103 billion went to low- and middle-income countries, up 21 per cent and double the level of five years earlier. Adaptation finance in those countries rose 31 per cent to $35 billion against mitigation at $68 billion, with private-sector mobilisation of $35 billion. In high-income economies MDBs provided $53 billion for mitigation and $7 billion for adaptation, mobilising $80 billion privately. The reporting is coordinated by the EIB with EBRD assistance and combines data from the major regional development banks.","insights":["Adaptation finance grew faster than mitigation in low- and middle-income countries, 31 per cent against 16 per cent, though the levels remain far apart at $35 billion against $68 billion, so the crossover is in growth rate rather than in volume.","The high-income split is the sharper number: $53 billion for mitigation against $7 billion for adaptation, a ratio of more than seven to one, and MDBs report already meeting or exceeding their 2030 high-income targets five years early.","Private mobilisation is larger in high-income countries than in low- and middle-income ones, $80 billion against $35 billion, so the leverage the banks achieve is weakest where the need is greatest.","The COP29 Baku commitments give the benchmark: $120 billion a year for low- and middle-income countries by 2030 including $42 billion for adaptation, plus $65 billion a year mobilised privately, so the 2025 adaptation figure of $35 billion is within reach of the $42 billion target.","An MDB Climate Finance Dashboard was launched in pilot in April 2026, giving granular breakdowns and the harmonised methodologies behind the headline figures, which matters because MDB climate finance accounting has been contested."],"keywords":["MDB climate finance","adaptation finance","private mobilization","COP29 commitments","joint reporting"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":317,"title":"The Right Tool at the Right Time: CRDs vs. DRDs, What Every California Community Should Know","authors":"","date":"2026-07-13","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["California","US"],"institutions":["California Forward","Resilient Cities Catalyst","City of Miami"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://cafwd.org/news/the-right-tool-at-the-right-time-crds-vs-drds-what-every-california-community-should-know/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This California Forward and Resilient Cities Catalyst explainer compares two special financing district tools enabled under California law: Climate Resilience Districts (CRDs), established in 2022 under SB 852, and Disaster Recovery Financing Districts (DRDs), enacted in 2025 under SB 782 in response to the January 2025 Los Angeles wildfires. Both are built on the Enhanced Infrastructure Financing District (EIFD) legal framework and share financing tools (tax increment financing, benefit assessments, special taxes, and bonds), but differ sharply in timing, scope, and purpose. CRDs are long-term, pre-disaster governance instruments; DRDs are expedited, post-disaster recovery vehicles with narrower spending authority.","insights":["CRDs can be formed at any time by local government decision and are designed for long-term (ongoing) climate resilience planning, investment, and operations and maintenance; DRDs can only be formed within two years of a Governor-declared state of emergency and are purpose-limited to three uses: repair/replace disaster-damaged property, mitigate future disaster risk, and support economic recovery.","DRD formation is expedited, designed to move within months of a disaster, while CRD formation typically takes 1\u20132 years due to a more deliberative process including an infrastructure financing plan requiring multiple public hearings.","Los Angeles County formed two DRDs (Altadena Wildfire Recovery Infrastructure Financing District and Unincorporated Santa Monica Mountains Wildfire Disaster Recovery Financing District) within 40 days of SB 782 being signed into law, demonstrating the speed possible under the expedited framework.","A critical structural limitation of DRDs: tax increment financing, their most prominent tool, builds revenue only as property values recover post-disaster, creating a timing gap that requires communities to secure upfront bridge funding even after a district is established.","CRDs can fund a broader range of activities than DRDs, including ongoing operations of climate resilience projects and staff hiring; neither CRDs nor DRDs can use bond proceeds for operations, programs, or services.","California's first CRD was designated to Sonoma County Regional Climate Protection Authority under SB 852 (2022) but has not yet leveraged financing strategies; the City of Inglewood formed an additional CRD in late 2024 tied to the Inglewood Transit Connector and is drafting its Infrastructure Financing Plan."],"keywords":["climate resilience districts","disaster recovery financing","tax increment financing","Enhanced Infrastructure Financing District","special financing districts","wildfire recovery","California local government finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":389,"title":"FEMA Delivers More than $584 Million to Support Community-Led Resilience and Recovery Across the U.S.","authors":"","date":"2026-07-08","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","New York City Housing Authority","U.S. Army Corps of Engineers (USACE)","Karuk Tribe"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.fema.gov/press-release/20260708/fema-delivers-more-584-million-support-community-led-resilience-and-recovery","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"FEMA announced $584 million in approved funding across four grant programs, Building Resilient Infrastructure and Communities (BRIC, ~$24M), Public Assistance (~$520M), Hazard Mitigation Grant Program (~$33.7M), and Individual Assistance ($6.7M), disbursed to more than 30 states, territories, and Tribal Nations. Funding supports public infrastructure restoration, flood protection, building code upgrades, generator installations, debris removal, and disaster case management. The announcement signals continued federal commitment to pre-disaster mitigation alongside post-disaster recovery, with documented unit-level grants ranging from $140K to $46.7M.","insights":["FEMA's $584M tranche spans four programs: Public Assistance dominates at ~$520M (89%), while pre-disaster mitigation (BRIC + HMGP) totals ~$57.7M (10%), revealing that recovery spending dwarfs prospective resilience investment in this award cycle.","BRIC allocated nearly $8.3M to Hawaii for a community backup generator, one of the largest single BRIC awards, underscoring that energy reliability during outages is a primary infrastructure resilience priority under current federal criteria.","Seventeen BRIC project-scoping grants totaling $3.2M were awarded across 15 states and Tribal Nations, providing early-stage capital to convert community vulnerability into bankable project applications, a pipeline-building function critical for later capital deployment.","Building code adoption and enforcement received discrete BRIC grants in five jurisdictions (NC ~$1.87M, VT ~$1.2M, MD ~$660K, MA ~$505K, Weber County UT ~$140K), demonstrating federal willingness to fund the regulatory infrastructure that raises minimum resilience standards for new construction.","The $46.7M Public Assistance grant to New York City Housing Authority for Hurricane Ida repairs is the single largest disclosed project, illustrating the concentrated fiscal exposure of large public housing portfolios to named storm events and the delayed cost recovery timeline (Ida struck 2021).","HMGP's $6.27M across 13 projects to harden utilities and install generators at water treatment plants, medical facilities, and emergency services in six jurisdictions links critical-facility continuity directly to federal mitigation grant eligibility, a transferable structuring model."],"keywords":["FEMA grant programs","hazard mitigation","public assistance","BRIC program","federal disaster funding","building codes","Tribal Nations resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":440,"title":"Uneven Adaptation: Insurance Pricing and Household Climate Resilience (Hutchins Center Working Paper 109)","authors":"Shan Ge; Ankit Kalda; Varun Sharma; Vikas Soni","date":"2026-07-06","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Brookings Institution","New York University","Indiana University","University of South Florida"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/uneven-adaption-insurance-pricing-and-household-climate-resilience/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Ge, Kalda, Sharma and Soni provide causal evidence on whether insurance pricing makes households harden their homes, using more than 18 million policy observations from Citizens Property Insurance Corporation, Florida's insurer of last resort, from 2002 to 2023. They track five categories of wind and water resilience upgrade: roof-to-wall attachments, roof covering materials, roof deck attachments, secondary water resistance, and opening protection such as shutters or impact windows. Florida is a clean setting because state law requires insurers to offer mitigation discounts calculated as a percentage of premium, so savings grow proportionally as premiums rise, reaching as much as 47 per cent. Identification comes from Citizens implementing rate changes at ZIP code level at different times, isolating premium changes driven by insurer pricing rather than by deteriorating property conditions. The average household shows no statistically significant response, and that average conceals a sharp divergence: wealthier households increase adaptation investment when premiums rise, while lower-income households reduce it, because the higher premium tightens an already binding budget constraint faster than the larger discount can pull.","insights":["The headline is that the average effect is zero and the average is the wrong statistic: premium increases raise adaptation among wealthier households and lower it among poorer ones, so a pricing signal that works in aggregate can be regressive in practice.","The mechanism is a race between two effects of the same price rise, a larger proportional discount and a tighter budget constraint, and which wins depends on the household's financial cushion.","Florida's mandated percentage-of-premium discount structure, reaching up to 47 per cent, is what makes the incentive grow with the premium, so the finding is a property of that design and not of insurance pricing generally.","Identification exploits ZIP-code-level rate changes timed differently by a state insurer of last resort, which separates insurer pricing decisions from property deterioration, a distinction most studies of this question cannot make.","Households in smaller homes, where the upfront cost is lower, are more responsive, which points at upfront cost rather than the discount as the binding constraint for the households that adapt least."],"keywords":["insurance pricing","mitigation discount","household adaptation","Citizens Property Insurance","Florida","wind mitigation","distributional effects","budget constraint"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":342,"title":"Catastrophe Bond Market Records That Were Broken in H1 2026","authors":"","date":"2026-07-06","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Artemis"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/news/catastrophe-bond-market-records-that-were-broken-in-h1-2026/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Artemis reports that H1 2026 set multiple records in the catastrophe bond market, with total issuance across all tracked cat bond types exceeding the prior H1 2025 record of $17.56 billion. The outstanding cat bond market reached nearly $65.6 billion as of June 30, 2026. May 2026 alone set a single-month issuance record of nearly $6.93 billion. Twelve first-time sponsors debuted, 83 transactions closed in the half-year, and 81% of risk capital used indemnity triggers, all records. The findings signal deepening institutionalization of cat bonds as a core reinsurance and risk-transfer instrument.","insights":["Total catastrophe bond issuance in H1 2026 exceeded $17.56 billion (the prior H1 2025 record), with Rule 144A property cat bonds alone reaching nearly $17.3 billion, demonstrating consistent double-digit billion-dollar growth in a single half-year period.","The outstanding catastrophe bond market reached nearly $65.6 billion as of June 30, 2026, up from approximately $63.9 billion at end of Q1 2026, reflecting net growth even against the highest maturities in market history.","May 2026 set a single-month issuance record of nearly $6.93 billion, roughly $1 billion above the prior record of $5.93 billion set in May 2025, illustrating the acceleration of deal pacing and investor appetite.","83 cat bond transactions closed in H1 2026 alone, matching or exceeding full-year totals for most years in market history except 2023 (95), 2024 (~93), and 2025 (122); the full-year 2026 total is on pace to set an all-time annual record.","12 first-time sponsors debuted in H1 2026 (vs. 11 in H1 2025), and the majority of debut sponsors become repeat issuers, a structural demand driver that will sustain issuance growth beyond current cycle dynamics.","81% of risk capital issued in H1 2026 used indemnity triggers (vs. 79% in H1 2025), reflecting growing participation by primary insurance carriers that prefer indemnity structures to integrate cat bonds tightly into their reinsurance programs.","The growth in sponsor count, transaction volume, and indemnity trigger use collectively signal that cat bonds are transitioning from a specialist instrument toward mainstream reinsurance infrastructure, with implications for insurance-linked securities fund managers and direct institutional investors."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond issuance","insurance-linked securities","ILS market growth","reinsurance capital markets","indemnity trigger"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":455,"title":"Paying for Preparedness: Why California Needs Sustained Investment in Climate Resilience","authors":"Nuin-Tara Key; Joel Martinez","date":"2026-07-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["California Forward","Insurance for Good","PRE Collective","Southern California Edison"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://cafwd.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/July_06_2026_Sustained-Revenue-for-Resilience_A-Case-for-Dedicated-Investment-in-Californias-Climate-Future.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A July 2026 report from California Forward and Insurance for Good arguing that California needs sustained rather than episodic investment in climate resilience, opening with what it calls the fiscal imperative. Its distinguishing feature is who produced it: the report draws on an Adaptation Finance Workgroup convened as a multi-sector collaboration spanning municipal finance, insurance, adaptation risk reduction and lending, assembled to advance actionable strategies for community and household climate resilience investment. Named contributors include Kayla Calkins of Megafire Action, Kyra Peyton of CSAA Insurance Group, Shalini Vajjhala of PRE Collective and Anuj Desai of Southern California Edison, so an insurer, a utility, a pre-development specialist and an advocacy organisation are in the same room as the municipal finance participants. California Forward describes itself as a multi-partisan non-profit building a sustainable, resilient and inclusive California economy through a regions-up approach, and Insurance for Good as a non-profit supporting communities and policymakers with insurance-related solutions aligned to social and environmental goals.","insights":["The workgroup composition is the substance: municipal finance, insurance, risk reduction and lending in one body is the coalition any household-level resilience financing mechanism would need, and assembling it is the report's real output.","Framing the argument as a fiscal imperative rather than a climate one is a deliberate choice for a multi-partisan organisation, and it puts the case in terms a state budget process can act on.","The presence of both an insurer and an electric utility among the named contributors connects the two parties that bear California's wildfire loss, which is where the financing question actually sits.","Sustained investment is the explicit ask, against a status quo of post-disaster appropriation, which is the same critique of episodic federal grant funding that the Brookings work makes.","Insurance for Good is the organisation behind the proposal, which places the argument with a group working on insurance design for community resilience rather than with a carrier or a regulator."],"keywords":["California","sustained investment","adaptation finance workgroup","municipal finance","insurance","household resilience","fiscal imperative"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":454,"title":"Responding to Risk: Tracking Financial Institutions\u2019 Progress on Adaptation and Resilience - CPI","authors":"Lizzie Russler; Frederick Fabian; Valerio Micale","date":"2026-07-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/responding-to-risk-tracking-financial-institutions-progress-on-adaptation-and-resilience/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Climate Policy Initiative brief tracking how financial institutions are progressing on adaptation and resilience, built on the Net Zero Finance Tracker, a not-for-profit dashboard assessing how 1,500 of the world's largest financial institutions respond to climate risk and drive real-economy outcomes. The tracker draws on over 600 datasets from 58 sources and organises its assessment across Targets, Implementation and Impact, with the explicit purpose of distinguishing symbolic commitments from measurable action. That design is the brief's analytical spine: rather than asking whether institutions have made adaptation commitments, it asks whether those commitments have implementation and impact behind them. The work was externally reviewed by specialists from Arc, Cadlas, CDP, Earth Capital Nexus, GFANZ, MSCI and the NGFS, and funded by the Hewlett Foundation, the Laudes Foundation and Bloomberg Philanthropies.","insights":["The Targets, Implementation and Impact structure is the contribution: it is built to separate a stated commitment from evidence of action, which is the distinction most adaptation pledge tracking cannot make.","Covering 1,500 institutions from over 600 datasets across 58 sources makes this a census rather than a sample, so the absence of adaptation activity in a category is itself evidence.","Adaptation and resilience are assessed within a tracking infrastructure originally built for net zero, so the metrics may carry mitigation assumptions that were not re-derived for adaptation.","The external reviewer list spans a rating agency, a central bank network and the sector's own alliance, which is unusual breadth and strengthens the methodology's standing."],"keywords":["financial institutions","adaptation and resilience","Net Zero Finance Tracker","targets and implementation","climate risk","GFANZ","NGFS"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":333,"title":"How 3 States Value and Invest for a More Resilient Future","authors":"Kristiane Huber","date":"2026-07-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Massachusetts","Minnesota","Rhode Island"],"institutions":["Pew Charitable Trusts","Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.pew.org/en/research-and-analysis/articles/2026/07/01/how-3-states-value-and-invest-for-a-more-resilient-future","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Pew Charitable Trusts article profiles how Massachusetts, Minnesota, and Rhode Island are building comprehensive resilience investment frameworks, quantifying future climate costs, prioritizing assets, measuring outcomes, and deploying innovative financing. Minnesota projects climate-driven costs of $17B\u2013$57B annually by mid-to-late century without adaptation investment, with resilience costing 8\u201315x less. Massachusetts estimates $90B\u2013$130B in climate investment needs through 2050 and is advancing a $2B+ bond bill and a Resilience Revolving Fund. Rhode Island's Resilient Rhody Infrastructure Fund offers a revolving loan model for green infrastructure, floodproofing, and wetland restoration.","insights":["Minnesota's Climate Adaptation and Resilience Cost Study projects annual climate-driven costs rising from $17B at mid-century to as much as $57B by end of century without additional adaptation investment; resilience investment is estimated to cost 8\u201315x less than inaction.","Massachusetts estimates that addressing climate risks will require $90B\u2013$130B in investment through 2050, but its Municipal Vulnerability Preparedness program in 2026 could only fund ~25% of community requests with $28.7M awarded.","Rhode Island's Resilient Rhody 2025 plan inventoried 130,000+ publicly owned assets, narrowed to ~40 critical assets for further analysis, and identified 79 resilience actions costing an estimated $112M\u2013$304M to complete.","Rhode Island's Resilient Rhody Infrastructure Fund (approved June 2025) is a state-sponsored revolving loan program offering below-market-rate loans for green stormwater infrastructure, culvert upgrades, wetland/dune restoration, floodproofing, and structure elevation, demonstrating how revolving loan funds shift resilience financing from episodic to systematic.","Massachusetts' proposed Mass Ready Act (>$2B environmental bond bill) would create a Resilience Revolving Fund providing low-interest loans to municipalities, water/wastewater utilities, and Tribal governments for stormwater management, floodplain protection, and nature-based solutions.","All three states have developed indicator-linked metrics frameworks (e.g., ResilientMass Metrics Framework) to track whether resilience investments reduce risk and reach vulnerable communities, enabling adaptive management of capital allocation.","State practitioners emphasize that grants alone are insufficient for resilience at scale and that peer-to-peer learning across states, on financing mechanisms, resilience standards, and metrics, can prevent duplicative effort and stretch collective resources."],"keywords":["revolving loan fund","cost of inaction","statewide vulnerability assessment","adaptive management","municipal resilience funding","resilience metrics"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":326,"title":"Resilience Finance Guidebook for Local Governments: From Resilience Goals to Financeable Projects","authors":"Victoria Salinas","date":"2026-07-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute","Aon","Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","Nicholas Institute for Energy Environment & Sustainability Duke University","ICLEI-Local Governments for Sustainability USA"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://import.cdn.thinkific.com/1074369/ikM1AARAQF6YN1eBs6h7_Resilience%20Finance%20Guidebook%20for%20Local%20Governments.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Resilience Finance Guidebook for Local Governments, produced by Duke University's Nicholas Institute and ICLEI USA (July 2026), is a structured translation framework helping local governments convert resilience goals into financeable projects. It diagnoses why resilience investments stall between clear need and available capital and offers a six-step Resilience Finance Diagnostic. The guide introduces six financial value pathways (avoided loss, reliable infrastructure, operational continuity, workforce reliability, faster recovery, and co-benefits), and presents case studies from Hoboken NJ, Longmont CO, Denver Water, Philadelphia, Mount Vernon WA, San Antonio, Chicago, Harris County TX, Hampton VA, Tulsa, and Pasco WA, illustrating layered capital strategies across grants, bonds, utility rates, special districts, environmental impact bonds, and catalytic capital. Key figures include a $2.5B Harris County flood bond, a $25M DC Water environmental impact bond, a 45% NFIP premium discount for Tulsa's Class 1 CRS rating, and adaptation returns of $10+ per dollar invested.","insights":["A World Resources Institute 2025 analysis of 320 adaptation investments across 12 countries found every dollar invested in adaptation generates more than $10 in benefits over 10 years, with average returns of 20%\u201327%.","Aon reports 2024 U.S. natural disaster economic losses of $218B, of which only $113B (52%) was insured, leaving households, businesses, and governments exposed to $105B in uninsured losses.","The National Institute of Building Sciences finds federal mitigation grants save ~$6 per $1 invested; adoption of modern building codes saves ~$11 per $1 invested; above-code design saves ~$4 per $1 spent across large building portfolios.","IBHS FORTIFIED homes in Alabama suffered 55%\u201374% fewer claims and 14%\u201340% less severe claims than non-certified homes after Hurricane Sally (2020); FORTIFIED designation also carries ~7% higher resale values in coastal Alabama and reduces expected multifamily insurance costs by an average of $1,533/year in windborne-debris-prone regions (payback under 7 years on a ~$10,000 retrofit).","Philadelphia's Green City, Clean Waters program ($2.4B over 25 years) was associated with a 10.3% increase in residential property values near green stormwater infrastructure, ~$1.3B in added property value, ~$18M in additional annual property tax revenue, and an estimated $4B total economic impact over 25 years.","Mount Vernon, WA's $31M floodwall removed 223 buildings from the regulatory floodplain, produced a 40% reduction in NFIP premiums for affected property owners (~$139,000 per building removed), and catalyzed $10M in private investment in a formerly derelict property.","A New York State study on the Rockville Centre community microgrid found that avoiding a one-day outage could produce ~$9.7M in social welfare benefits, including $5M in avoided lost sales and $3.1M in avoided regional GDP."],"keywords":["benefit-cost analysis","capital stack","value capture","avoided loss","community rating system","green stormwater infrastructure","forest resilience bond","state enabling authority"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":400,"title":"FEMA's Building Resilient Infrastructure and Communities (BRIC): Recent Developments","authors":"Diane P. Horn","date":"2026-06-30","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","US Department of the Treasury","Congressional Research Service"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12609","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Congressional Research Service Insight tracks developments in FEMA's Building Resilient Infrastructure and Communities (BRIC) grant program through June 2026. BRIC is the largest U.S. competitively awarded pre-disaster mitigation funding source, with $5.795 billion available across FY2020\u2013FY2025. The document details the Trump Administration's April 2025 attempt to terminate BRIC, subsequent litigation by 20 states, court injunctions, and a December 2025 permanent injunction ruling the termination unlawful. As of mid-2026, FEMA issued a combined FY2024\u2013FY2025 NOFO for $1 billion under court order, while $882 million in IIJA-appropriated funds remained at risk of reversion to Treasury.","insights":["BRIC is the largest U.S. competitively awarded pre-disaster mitigation program, with $5.795 billion available across FY2020\u2013FY2025; total demand across those cycles reached $15.665 billion (325% oversubscribed), signaling massive unmet need relative to supply.","Only 21.6% ($1.013 billion) of the $4.622 billion selected for funding across FY2020\u2013FY2023 had been obligated as of March 29, 2026, revealing a severe execution bottleneck that predates the 2025 termination attempt.","Oversubscription rates varied sharply by cycle: FY2020 was 620% oversubscribed ($3.1B requested vs. $500M available), FY2022 was 157% ($3.6B vs. $2.295B), and FY2023 was 540% ($5.4B vs. $1B), illustrating persistent and growing demand for federal pre-disaster mitigation capital.","The administration's April 2025 termination attempt put approximately $882 million in IIJA-appropriated BRIC funds at risk of reversion to Treasury, directly threatening project pipelines in all 50 states, territories, and tribes.","A federal court issued a permanent injunction on December 11, 2025, ruling the BRIC termination unlawful; a follow-up enforcement order on March 6, 2026, required FEMA to issue the FY2024 NOFO within 21 days, demonstrating that litigation can serve as a mechanism to preserve federal resilience funding commitments.","The court's ruling explicitly does not compel FEMA to award specific grants, nor does it prevent FEMA from replacing BRIC with a different mitigation program, leaving long-term program continuity uncertain and creating investability risk for project sponsors relying on BRIC funds.","Obligated projects that had not yet started construction were subject to cancellation under FEMA's April 2025 guidelines, while only projects with active construction could continue, a critical distinction for project finance timelines and shovel-readiness requirements."],"keywords":["pre-disaster mitigation grants","BRIC termination litigation","Stafford Act Section 203","DRRA 2018","FEMA grant program","federal mitigation funding"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":594,"title":"IMF Finds Climate Disasters Threaten Sovereign Debt as Adaptation Becomes an Economic Necessity","authors":"CoE-EDP, VisionRI","date":"2026-06-29","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global","Latin America"],"institutions":["International Monetary Fund"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.devdiscourse.com/article/science-environment/3941812-imf-finds-climate-disasters-threaten-sovereign-debt-as-adaptation-becomes-an-economic-necessity","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Devdiscourse article of 29 June 2026 reporting an International Monetary Fund finding that climate disasters significantly increase sovereign default risk, with every 1 per cent rise in disaster-related losses as a share of GDP raising default odds by 2 to 3 per cent, and framing climate change as a growing fiscal challenge. The report it describes concludes that greater investment in climate adaptation, supported by concessional finance such as Official Development Assistance, can strengthen resilience, reduce disaster losses and improve long-term debt sustainability for vulnerable countries.","insights":["The elasticity is the reason to hold this: **a 1 per cent of GDP increase in disaster losses raising default odds by 2 to 3 per cent** converts physical risk into sovereign credit risk with a coefficient, which is what a debt-for-resilience swap or a climate-resilient debt clause needs in order to be priced.","**This is a third-party account of an IMF working paper rather than the paper itself**, so the figures in it are reported at one remove and should be traced to the IMF publication before being relied on.","The publisher is a news aggregator and the body carries substantial site furniture, so the citable content is the elasticity and the policy conclusion rather than any analysis."],"keywords":["sovereign default risk","disaster losses","debt sustainability","concessional finance","Official Development Assistance","fiscal risk","climate resilient debt clause"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":590,"title":"Adapting to Adversity: Poverty and Climate Risks in Latin America and the Caribbean (Chapter 4, Improving Adaptation)","authors":"Alejandro de la Fuente; Miki Khanh Doan; Samuel Freije-Rodriguez","date":"2026-06-26","year":2026,"resource_type":"Book","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Latin America","Caribbean"],"institutions":["World Bank","Green Climate Fund","Global Environment Facility","United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/978-1-4648-2314-5","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A World Bank International Development in Focus volume, Adapting to Adversity: Poverty and Climate Risks in Latin America and the Caribbean, by Alejandro de la Fuente, Miki Khanh Doan and Samuel Freije-Rodriguez, DOI 10.1596/978-1-4648-2314-5. Its adaptation investment section gives the region's adaptation finance in figures. Funding comes from governments, the private sector, NGOs, philanthropies and community groups, but the biggest share is international public finance delivered as grants, loans and equity, and it is heavily concentrated in Brazil, Colombia, Costa Rica and Mexico. From 2003 to 2023 multilateral climate funds approved $5.7 billion for 614 projects in the region, with the Green Climate Fund, the Clean Technology Fund and Brazil's Amazon Fund making up 67 per cent of that total. The Green Climate Fund is the largest player, backing 37 projects in 13 countries with $2.42 billion over that period, including $43 million in Colombia and $33 million in Bolivia in 2023 alone; the Clean Technology Fund provided $718 million through the World Bank and the Amazon Fund $684 million in grants. Bilateral donors including Germany and the United Kingdom also feature. Since 2015 the GCF has mobilised more than $40 billion including co-financing across more than 100 countries, and per the UNFCCC the region obtained 22 per cent of climate financing from multilateral climate funds in 2015 to 2016.","insights":["Three funds accounting for 67 per cent of twenty years of multilateral adaptation approvals in a whole region is a concentration finding, and it means the region's adaptation finance is effectively three credit committees rather than a market.","$5.7 billion across 614 projects is an average of about $9 million per project over two decades, which is the scale problem stated arithmetically: these are not sizes institutional capital can underwrite without aggregation.","Concentration in Brazil, Colombia, Costa Rica and Mexico is the allocation counterpart of the concentration by fund, and it is the pattern the WRI philanthropy record and the IFC disclosures both describe from the supply side.","The volume's frame is poverty rather than infrastructure, so it connects adaptation finance to household vulnerability rather than to asset exposure."],"keywords":["adaptation finance","Green Climate Fund","Clean Technology Fund","Amazon Fund","multilateral climate funds","Latin America and the Caribbean","poverty","co-financing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":343,"title":"The Green Guarantee Company Closed Its First Two Transactions Mobilising USD 70 Million to Support the Mission 300 Initiative in Africa","authors":"","date":"2026-06-23","year":2026,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["African Development Bank","World Bank","Standard Chartered","Green Climate Fund","Fitch Ratings","Green Guarantee Company","Development Guarantee Group"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatefinancelab.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/GGC-closed-its-first-two-transactions-PR.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Green Guarantee Company (GGC), managed by the Development Guarantee Group and funded by the Green Climate Fund, KfW, FCDO, Nigerian Sovereign Investment Authority, and Norfund, closed its first two transactions in June 2026, mobilising USD 70 million to support the World Bank/African Development Bank Mission 300 energy access initiative. The first is a USD 20 million framework guarantee to Bank of Africa UK for mini-grid development in Nigeria (~340,000 people, 13 MW). The second is a USD 50 million guarantee backing a green bond listed on the London Stock Exchange, arranged by Standard Chartered for UK/US institutional investors, funding AFC's distributed solar PAYGO receivable securitisations across Sub-Saharan Africa (~4.3 million people).","insights":["GGC mobilised USD 70 million across two inaugural transactions, demonstrating that a BBB-stable-rated, DFI-backed guarantor can unlock institutional private capital for Sub-Saharan African energy access at scale.","The USD 50 million green bond guarantee enables a private placement to institutional investors (Legal & General, Calvert Impact Capital, Ceniarth) by elevating credit quality of AFC's PAYGO solar receivables securitisation to investment grade, illustrating how guarantees can bridge the gap between emerging-market risk and developed-market investor appetite.","The USD 20 million Nigeria mini-grid facility is projected to reach ~340,000 people with new or improved energy access and install 13 MW of renewable capacity, avoiding ~250,000 tCO2e per year, providing concrete unit economics for energy access guarantees.","The USD 50 million AFC green bond is expected to expand clean energy access for ~4.3 million people across Sub-Saharan Africa, with the structure designed to be replicable and to recycle DFI/MDB capital into private financing channels.","GGC's guarantee capacity is USD 1 billion, suggesting these two inaugural deals represent only 7% of capacity, signalling a large pipeline; the chairman explicitly noted 'a very strong project pipeline in EMDEs.'","The transaction establishes a blueprint combining a bond private placement, a third-party investment-grade guarantee, and PAYGO receivables securitisation, a replicable structure for scaling distributed renewable energy finance in Africa.","Technical assistance from the Global Green Growth Institute (via the Global Trust Fund) and the FCDO MOBILIST programme, alongside a Second Party Opinion from Sustainable Fitch, underscores the role of blended finance support in making first-of-kind capital markets structures viable."],"keywords":["energy access","guarantee facility","PAYGO solar","mini-grid","emerging markets","private capital mobilisation","green bond","Sub-Saharan Africa"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":314,"title":"Advancing Nature-Based Insurance to Protect People and Nature","authors":"Megan A. Kelso, Rae Taylor-Burns, Guillermo Franco, Jane Carter Ingram, Borja G. Reguero, Michael W. Beck","date":"2026-06-23","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global","US","California"],"institutions":["United Nations Environment Programme","Guy Carpenter","Environmental Defense Fund","The Nature Conservancy","Moody's","Swiss Re","World Economic Forum","Federal Emergency Management Agency","U.S. Army Corps of Engineers (USACE)","Quantified Ventures","Climate Policy Initiative","World Resources Institute","Wharton Risk Center","World Bank","re:focus partners","RMS","S&P Global","Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures","Resources for the Future","Co-Impact","Center for Coastal Climate Resilience at UC Santa Cruz","Pollination"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s43247-026-03753-z","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This 2026 perspective in Communications Earth & Environment advances the concept of \"nature-based insurance\", combining nature-based solutions (NBS) with risk-transfer instruments such as parametric and indemnity insurance. Authored by researchers at UC Santa Cruz, Guy Carpenter, and Pollination, it identifies five frontiers: modeling habitats in industry risk models, pricing nature's risk-reduction benefits into premiums, de-risking green investments via catastrophe wrappers and environmental impact bonds, insuring ecosystems directly through parametric policies, and partnering with innovative insurers. The paper quantifies relevant market sizes (global property insurance premiums ~$1.2 trillion/year; nature-related bond issuance ~$113 billion/year) and documents real case studies including the Mesoamerican Reef parametric insurance ($1.025M paid out), the Belize Blue Bond catastrophe wrapper (3-point credit rating jump, $180M for conservation), and a Sierra Nevada wildfire policy with 39% lower premiums due to ecological forest management.","insights":["Global property insurance premiums are estimated at $1.2 trillion annually; redirecting even a small percentage toward nature-based risk reduction could unlock tens of billions of dollars per year for adaptation and conservation.","The Mesoamerican Reef parametric insurance program has paid out $1.025 million cumulatively across ~800 km\u00b2 of reef; extrapolating to 80,000 km\u00b2 of global coral reefs implies potential average annual funding of $22.6 million through this mechanism alone.","A 2025 Sierra Nevada wildfire insurance policy explicitly priced ecological forest management into premiums and deductibles, resulting in premiums 39% lower and deductibles 89% lower than standard, the first documented case of NBS risk reduction reflected in commercial wildfire insurance pricing.","The Belize Blue Bond catastrophe wrapper facilitated a sovereign debt restructuring that reduced annual debt payments by $12 million/year, committed $4.2 million/year to conservation for 20 years, and produced a 3-point jump in Belize's S&P sovereign credit rating.","Salt marshes reduced flood damages from Hurricane Sandy by 15% (quantified using Moody's RMS hurricane model); mangroves reduce storm damages by >25% in Florida across a wide range of storm intensities, figures directly usable in insurance underwriting.","Less than a quarter of annual global disaster losses are insured; in Asia the insured share is under 10%, leaving a coverage gap exceeding $120 billion on that continent alone, the adaptation finance gap in developing countries is $279\u2013$334 billion/year.","Coral reefs in the US provide over $1.8 billion in flood risk reduction benefits annually; natural habitat buffers could have reduced damages from the 2018 Paradise Fire by 21%, both findings support actuarial crediting of intact ecosystems."],"keywords":["parametric ecosystem insurance","catastrophe wrapper","habitat risk modeling","nature-based solutions","reef insurance","debt-for-nature swap","insurance pricing signals","adaptation finance gap"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":489,"title":"Carlyle Rethinks Portfolio Risk to Give Weather Insurance a Bigger Role","authors":"","date":"2026-06-22","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Carlyle Group","Marsh","Mubadala Investment Company","World Resources Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2026/06/22/874852.htm","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Carlyle Group, working with the insurance broker Marsh, has built a framework for portfolio risk that makes asset values reflect the insurance consequences of severe weather, and unveiled it at London Climate Action Week in June 2026. Steve Hatfield, co-head of global sustainability at Carlyle, says there has been no consistent mechanism allowing insurers to recognise how asset hardening reduces risk, so climate risk is not priced into valuations until coverage becomes expensive or is withdrawn after a loss. The framework has four steps: estimate the probability an asset is hit by an extreme weather event, including gradual value erosion; assess the gap between the asset's current resilience and what would make it resistant; estimate how resilience upgrades reduce expected losses; and have insurers use those calculations to offer better underwriting terms, including premium credits, deductible reductions and broader coverage. Mubadala and the Danish pension manager Sampension are among the institutional backers, alongside contributions from engineers, insurers and climate risk experts. Marsh's Amy Barnes says underwriters recognised the issue but did not know how to address it, and that designing in resilience is far cheaper than retrofitting. The article notes the relevance to data centre construction in exposed locations and cites a 2025 World Resources Institute study indicating adaptation investment could yield a tenfold return over a decade.","insights":["The framework's load-bearing step is the fourth: it only works if insurers convert a modelled loss reduction into premium credits, deductible reductions or broader coverage, which the document states as a design requirement rather than as an aspiration.","The stated defect is a missing mechanism, not missing information: insurers could not recognise asset hardening, so a resilience upgrade earned no pricing response and therefore no return the investment committee could see.","Hatfield names the specific failure that stalled adoption inside Carlyle, an inability to show investment teams a near-term cash flow return on resilience spending, which locates the barrier in payback period rather than in risk awareness.","Marsh's point that insurance resilience expertise sits in sustainability functions and does not reach underwriting is an organisational explanation for why the price signal has been absent.","The framework is pre-commercial: Hatfield says he has previewed it with carriers and expects them to road test it, so no premium credit has actually been paid at the time of publication."],"keywords":["asset hardening","premium credit","underwriting terms","Carlyle","Marsh","portfolio risk","return on resilience","data centres"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":536,"title":"Turning Adaptation Ambition into Investment at Scale","authors":"Yamide Dagnet, Doug Sims, Jorge Gastelumendi","date":"2026-06-17","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Natural Resources Defense Council","Atlantic Council","TPG","Invesco","Lightsmith Group"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nrdc.org/resources/turning-adaptation-ambition-investment-scale","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An account of the FINI initiative, Fostering Investable National Planning and Implementation for Adaptation and Resilience, developed by NRDC with the Atlantic Council's Climate Resilience Center. Its diagnosis is that National Adaptation Plans do not translate into investable opportunities: project pipelines lack the elements investors need, risk, returns and insurability are not clearly defined or communicated, data for resilience projects is limited so risks are hard to price, and because risk drives return, ambiguity inflates a project's cost of capital into a cycle of underinvestment and escalating damage. FINI's purpose is to fix the system connecting global and domestic investors to national and local priorities.","insights":["The mechanism named for underinvestment is priced rather than moral: unquantified risk raises the cost of capital, which makes projects less viable, which reduces the evidence base, which keeps risk unquantified. Ambiguity itself is the cost driver.","Adaptation finance needs are put at 12 to 14 times current public flows, and private capital is described as moving largely toward mitigation rather than adaptation, with the conditions to attract investment weakest where adaptation is needed most.","The gap is specifically that NAPs do not distinguish projects that can generate cash flows or savings, and are therefore suitable for private investment, from those that cannot. That triage is the missing step between a plan and a pipeline.","Capital is nonetheless described as already moving, with named examples: TPG Rise Climate in climate-resilient agriculture and crop shock tolerance, Invesco's dedicated adaptation funds, and Lightsmith Group backing satellite monitoring and water systems, with spending expected to exceed $1 trillion a year.","The misalignment problem is stated from both sides: private deals are being done but are often not connected to existing NAPs and not reaching the most vulnerable, while finance ministries and central banks find the efforts fragmented and too slow.","The worked illustration is small and physical, cool roofs made with solar-reflective paint in Ahmedabad, which sets the scale of the interventions against the trillion-dollar framing."],"keywords":["FINI","National Adaptation Plans","investable pipeline","cost of capital","insurability","adaptation finance gap"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":608,"title":"World Bank approves $400m Morocco program to expand finance for climate, disaster, and cyber risks","authors":"Reinsurance News","date":"2026-06-15","year":2026,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Africa"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.reinsurancene.ws/world-bank-approves-400m-morocco-program-to-expand-finance-for-climate-disaster-and-cyber-risks/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A report that the World Bank's Board of Directors has approved the $400 million Morocco Climate and Risk Finance Program, aimed at strengthening Morocco's financial resilience to climate, disaster and cyber risks while helping unlock private capital for climate infrastructure. The programme will develop disaster and cyber insurance instruments to expand risk transfer capacity, strengthen institutional frameworks, reinforce digital payments infrastructure to accelerate the flow of finance following shocks, and build the capacity of financial regulators to oversee climate and cyber risks affecting banks and insurers. To facilitate climate infrastructure investment it establishes a Project Preparation Facility to build a pipeline of commercially viable projects across renewable energy, energy efficiency, sustainable transport and water infrastructure, and it aims to de-risk private investment through blended finance structures and capital market instruments. Over five years the stated targets are to mobilise up to $400 million in private capital, put in place $1 billion in pre-arranged disaster financing, and extend cyber risk coverage to at least 20 financial entities. The Board also approved a separate $250 million Morocco Digital Transformation Acceleration Program supporting the Digital Morocco 2030 strategy.","insights":["**Digital payments infrastructure is named as resilience finance.** The speed at which money reaches a household after a shock is set by the payment rails rather than by the instrument, so a fast-paying parametric policy over slow rails is not fast.","A $1 billion pre-arranged disaster financing target against $400 million of programme money is a 2.5 times leverage claim on contingent capacity rather than on deployed capital, which is a different and cheaper kind of mobilisation than the private-capital target sitting beside it.","**Cyber sits alongside climate as a peril throughout**, including in the regulator capacity-building and the 20-entity coverage target, so the programme treats the two as one insurability problem rather than as separate portfolios.","A Project Preparation Facility appears again, as it does in Meridiam's Urban Resilience Fund and in the Massachusetts and Rhode Island planning grants. Four records across three continents identify the same binding constraint: not capital, but bankable projects."],"keywords":["Morocco","pre-arranged disaster financing","Project Preparation Facility","cyber risk","digital payments","blended finance","de-risking","World Bank program"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":494,"title":"Counties Regroup After FEMA\u2019s Resiliency Grant Program Resumes","authors":"Meredith Moran","date":"2026-06-15","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Washington"],"institutions":["National Association of Counties (NACo)","Federal Emergency Management Agency","National Institute of Building Sciences (NIBS)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.naco.org/news/counties-regroup-after-femas-resiliency-grant-program-resumes","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"FEMA's Building Resilient Infrastructure and Communities programme was ended by the Trump Administration in April 2025, halting roughly 2,000 active infrastructure and disaster mitigation projects. A coalition of 22 states and the District of Columbia sued FEMA, the Department of Homeland Security and the United States; a federal judge ruled for the states in December, ordering FEMA to reinstate BRIC and restore over $3.6 billion in canceled funding, and the agency resumed the programme on 25 March. Counties are now reassessing projects left in limbo. Clatsop County, Oregon and Columbia Memorial Hospital had been set to receive $14 million to build a tsunami evacuation zone into a hospital on the Cascadia subduction zone; the county pushed construction forward in early 2026 with federal funds still unresolved, stretching its reserves. Roughly two dozen Washington state projects worth more than $150 million were paused. Pierce County's deputy emergency management director Luke Meyers says restarting has been slow and questions whether original project scopes still hold given cost increases. The reinstated programme now prioritises shovel-ready projects and eliminates phased initiatives, with FY2024-2025 applications due 23 July. Meyers notes that a setback levee funded by hazard mitigation grants limited damage in Orting during atmospheric river flooding that produced Washington's largest public infrastructure damage total in over four decades, and argues federal programmes should work more like block grants with longer performance periods. The article cites the 2019 National Institute of Building Sciences finding of a $6 return for every $1 of public mitigation funding.","insights":["The reinstatement changed the product as well as restoring the money: BRIC now funds only shovel-ready projects and eliminates phased initiatives, which excludes exactly the long-lead work that needs a planning phase before construction.","Clatsop County's response, spending its own reserves to keep a $14 million tsunami project on schedule while federal funds were in limbo, shows a local balance sheet absorbing federal programme risk, and only a county with reserves can do that.","Cost escalation during the pause is the unmeasured cost of the cancellation: Meyers's question is whether the original scopes are still deliverable at the original grant amounts, which turns a restored dollar figure into a reduced project.","The Orting setback levee is a natural experiment that the county documented, limited damage in a watershed that would traditionally have flooded worse, which is avoided-loss evidence generated by the disaster rather than by a model.","The ask is for block-grant flexibility and longer periods of performance, which is a request to change the instrument rather than its size, and follows directly from the restart friction the article describes."],"keywords":["BRIC","hazard mitigation grant","shovel-ready","Cascadia subduction zone","setback levee","block grant","period of performance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":605,"title":"Mexico Doubles Parametric Catastrophe Insurance to About $575m at 2026 Renewal","authors":"Steve Evans","date":"2026-06-10","year":2026,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Latin America"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/news/mexico-doubles-parametric-catastrophe-insurance-to-575m-at-2026-renewal/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An Artemis report by Steve Evans of 10 June 2026 on Mexico doubling its parametric catastrophe insurance for 2026 into 2027. The Ministry of Finance and Public Credit secured 10.4 billion pesos of catastrophe insurance protection in total, a doubling from the prior year, of which the main component is 10 billion pesos, around US$575 million, of parametric catastrophe insurance placed for the 2026 to 2027 coverage year. The renewal runs from 5 June 2026 to 5 May 2027 and transfers risk from earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, hurricanes and floods of medium to high severity, and has two components: a 400 million peso Loss Management Fund and the 10 billion peso parametric cover, structured to guarantee a timely financial response to high-severity catastrophes. Agroasemex, S.A., a Mexican government insurance institution, is the conduit for the global market capacity supporting the arrangement, with the parametric cover placed on a reinsurance basis to protect the insurer, and it has served the same role for the country's catastrophe bonds over two decades in partnership with reinsurers, development banks and capital markets investors. Mexico operates a federal strategy for Financial Management of Disaster Risks with insurance arrangements running from mid-year, has been a beneficiary of World Bank facilitated catastrophe bond protection since the groundbreaking CAT-Mex Ltd. transaction of 2006, and was most recently in the cat bond market in May 2024 securing $420 million of parametric protection against earthquakes and Atlantic hurricanes.","insights":["The sovereign's stated reason for choosing parametric cover is the benefit rather than the price: predictable, responsive and fast-paying protection. Mexico has been buying this for twenty years and has converged on speed as the thing worth paying for.","**Agroasemex is the structural detail worth keeping.** A state insurance institution acting as conduit means the sovereign reaches reinsurance and capital markets through a regulated insurer rather than directly, which is the pattern a government without its own insurer cannot copy and the reason Mexico can renew annually at scale.","Pairing a 400 million peso Loss Management Fund with the parametric layer is a retention-and-transfer split: the fund absorbs the frequent losses and the parametric cover the severe ones.","Doubling coverage in a single renewal is a demand signal rather than a supply one, and it dates precisely: June 2026, following the 2024 catastrophe bond renewal at $420 million."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","sovereign disaster risk finance","Agroasemex","Loss Management Fund","CAT-Mex","catastrophe bond","risk retention","Mexico"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":493,"title":"Arcadis and Jupiter Intelligence Introduce PRICE Framework to Help Organizations Close the Climate Adaptation Investment Gap","authors":"","date":"2026-06-10","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Arcadis","Jupiter Intelligence","United Nations Environment Programme"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/arcadis-and-jupiter-intelligence-introduce-price-framework-to-help-organizations-close-the-climate-adaptation-investment-gap-302795872.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Arcadis and Jupiter Intelligence launched the PRICE Adaptation Framework on 10 June 2026, a methodology for comparing climate adaptation options, quantifying return on investment and prioritising resilience spending. The five steps are: Pinpoint loss drivers, Rank adaptation options, build the Investment case by quantifying avoided losses and ROI, set a Capital strategy connecting resilience decisions to funding, and Execute and evidence by translating planning into measurable delivery. It is supported by Arcadis Climate Risk Nexus and Enterprise Decision Analytics and by Jupiter's Adaptation Hub. Two test cases are reported: in the flood-prone Meyerland neighborhood of Houston, an analysis of 100 single-family homes found positive ROI for dry floodproofing at 52 per cent of locations assessed; in Slovenia, a modeled flood wall for an industrial facility that had suffered up to US$60 million of losses in a single event showed a projected ROI of 245 per cent. Arcadis reports applying the approach with the State University of New York, Northeast US regional transit clients and major real estate owners. The release cites UNEP's 2025 Adaptation Gap Report on adaptation finance needs of $310 to $365 billion a year by 2035, 12 to 14 times current flows, and a $14 damage avoided per $1 of coastal protection.","insights":["The Houston result is the useful number: dry floodproofing cleared a positive ROI at only 52 per cent of 100 homes assessed, so a blanket retrofit programme in a flood-prone neighborhood would be half uneconomic on this method, which is an argument for screening rather than for coverage.","The two test cases sit at opposite ends of the distribution, 52 per cent of homes marginal against a 245 per cent ROI on a single industrial flood wall, which shows the method mainly separates concentrated high-value exposure from dispersed residential exposure.","The C step, capital strategy, is what distinguishes this from a risk analytics product: the framework's claim is that it connects an avoided-loss calculation to a funding route, which is the step most climate risk tools stop short of.","Both vendors are commercial and the framework is their joint product, so the ROI figures are self-reported and carry no independent verification."],"keywords":["PRICE framework","adaptation ROI","dry floodproofing","avoided losses","Arcadis","Jupiter Intelligence","Meyerland"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":607,"title":"Total Catastrophe Bond Issuance Hits $16.1bn Year to Date in 2026, 144A Cat Bonds Over $15.8bn","authors":"Artemis","date":"2026-06-09","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["California Earthquake Authority"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/news/total-catastrophe-bond-issuance-hits-16-1bn-ytd-in-2026-144a-cat-bonds-over-15-8bn/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An Artemis market report on catastrophe bond issuance in 2026, recording total settled issuance across Rule 144A deals and analysed private transactions of $16.1 billion year to date, with settled 144A issuance alone above $15.8 billion. The breakdown is almost $15.44 billion of 144A property catastrophe bonds, almost $385 million of 144A cat bonds on other lines such as specialty, and just over $286 million of private cat bonds or cat bond lites. Seventy-one issuances had settled with a further seven in the pipeline, projecting at least 78 by 30 June against the previous first-half record of 72 set in H1 2025, so 2026 will break the record for the most deals to market and settle in a first half. The seventy-first to settle was the California Earthquake Authority's $425 million Sutter Re 2026-1. The article's initial projection for H1 2026 total issuance was about $16.96 billion, second only to the $17.56 billion of H1 2025, and it carries two dated updates: on 10 June, after new Kilimanjaro III Re offerings for Everest, the projection rose to just over $17.49 billion, and on 12 June the author judged the market on course for new second-quarter and first-half records. Issuance was already more than $3.5 billion ahead of H1 2024.","insights":["**The article is a snapshot of a moving number and says so in its own text**, revising its projection twice within three days from $16.96 billion to over $17.49 billion. Any figure taken from it has to be cited with its update date.","The composition is the more durable finding: property catastrophe risk is about 96 per cent of 144A issuance, and private cat bonds are under 2 per cent of the total. A resilience-linked security entering this market enters an overwhelmingly property-catastrophe channel.","Deal count breaking a record while dollar volume does not is a market-structure signal: average deal size is falling, which usually means new and smaller sponsors are reaching the market. That is the direction a public-sector resilience sponsor would need.","The California Earthquake Authority's $425 million Sutter Re 2026-1 is named as the settling deal, which puts a public-sector sponsor in a market otherwise dominated by re/insurers."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond issuance","Rule 144A","cat bond lite","Sutter Re","California Earthquake Authority","market volume","insurance-linked securities"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":603,"title":"Home Insurance and the Unraveling of Florida Communities","authors":"The Conversation","date":"2026-06-09","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":[],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.64628/AAI.hn7vj795d","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article in The Conversation of 9 June 2026, DOI 10.64628/AAI.hn7vj795d, by a researcher who has spent over a decade studying how property markets adapt to the changing financial realities of climate change in Florida, the Netherlands and beyond. It opens on Shore Acres, a low-lying bayfront neighbourhood of St Petersburg, in November 2024, two months after Hurricane Helene pushed several feet of water into homes there even though the storm's centre stayed far offshore, and days after Milton made landfall nearby as a major hurricane, the two arriving within 13 days of each other. What the author found was starkly unequal recovery on a single street: dozens of older homes from the postwar building boom gutted, interiors piled in the street, for-sale signs on many of them, alongside pristine newer homes raised on posts far above their neighbours in line with newer building codes and showing no sign a storm had passed. The argument is that these costs are driven by more than major disasters: soaring property insurance rates are repricing life in Florida and will worsen as hurricanes intensify, and the current way of managing those risks and costs is not the only option.","insights":["**The elevated houses are the finding.** On one street, homes built to the newer code were untouched while their postwar neighbours were gutted, which is an unusually clean natural experiment in what a building standard is worth, observed rather than modelled.","Helene put several feet of water into Shore Acres **with its centre far offshore**, which is the same lesson the MetroCat trigger design encodes: surge is not proportional to proximity or to landfall intensity, and an instrument keyed to wind at landfall can miss the loss entirely.","For-sale signs on the damaged homes and none on the elevated ones is the distributional consequence stated plainly: the households that cannot afford to rebuild to code are the ones who leave."],"keywords":["property insurance","Shore Acres","Hurricane Helene","Hurricane Milton","building codes","elevation","unequal recovery","insurance repricing","Florida"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":470,"title":"The Weekly: The Blended Capital Stack That Could Move Resilience Finance at Scale","authors":"","date":"2026-06-05","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Metropolitan Water District of Southern California"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.epicenterinsights.com/the-weekly-the-blended-capital-stack-that-could-move-resilience-finance-at-scale/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An argument that funding climate resilience is a coordination problem rather than a capital problem, built on a historical parallel. In 2025, climate-driven disasters in the United States caused an estimated $115 billion in damages across 23 separate billion-dollar events, and the article's contention is that the capital to address the underlying risk already exists while the coordination to move it does not. Drawing on a piece by Lily Bui of SoCal Grantmakers, it recalls that early twentieth century Los Angeles outgrew its local water supply and that reaching the Colorado and Owens Rivers required new institutions, new financing mechanisms and unprecedented coordination across jurisdictions: California created the Metropolitan Water District, and voters approved bonds during the Great Depression to finance the Los Angeles and Colorado River Aqueducts, turning a fragmented system of local wells into a regional network able to sustain growth at scale. The article proposes the same shift for resilience finance, from isolated wells to a coordinated watershed in which resources are intentionally pooled, directed and circulated across geographies, sectors and time horizons.","insights":["The framing claim is falsifiable and useful: capital is not the constraint, coordination is, and the evidence offered is that $115 billion of 2025 losses coexists with available capital that does not move.","The Metropolitan Water District precedent is the strongest part of the argument, because it shows a fragmented local system being consolidated by creating an institution and a financing mechanism together, and doing so during the Great Depression.","The watershed metaphor is operationalised as pooling across geographies, sectors and time horizons, the last of which is the hard one: resilience benefits accrue over decades while most funding cycles are annual.","This is a weekly newsletter format, so the substance is the argument and the cited figures rather than original analysis, and the Bui article it draws on is the primary source."],"keywords":["blended capital stack","coordination problem","Metropolitan Water District","pooled capital","resilience finance","billion-dollar disasters","philanthropy"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":504,"title":"Updated Climate Bonds Resilience Taxonomy Methodology (CBRT v1.1)","authors":"Climate Bonds Initiative (institutional)","date":"2026-06-04","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Bonds Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatebonds.net/data-insights/publications/updated-climate-bonds-resilience-taxonomy-methodology","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Annexes I to VI of the Climate Bonds Resilience Taxonomy methodology document, version 1.1, June 2026. The taxonomy defines the target climate impact that each adapting measure addresses, and Annex I sets out how an adapting measure is assessed against a global threshold in four steps. First, define the investment context for the assessment, setting boundaries that correspond to the specific implementation of the measure, which may be more granular than the activity or even the asset it sits in: installing water-efficient fixtures and fittings is assessed in the specific context in which they are installed. Three further considerations may apply, setting out the investment's context of vulnerability to climate change, an explicit statement of intent to reduce the vulnerabilities identified, and a clear direct link between the investment and that vulnerability. Second, establish an appropriate timescale, normally the expected time horizon for implementing the measure, covering the expected lifespan of both the measure and the activity it is implemented in. Third, use the investment-appropriate global threshold defined in the taxonomy, subject to further development and refinement including by Technical Working Groups. Fourth, define a counterfactual against which progress toward the threshold can be measured.","insights":["The counterfactual requirement is what separates a resilience taxonomy from a green one: an adapting measure has no emissions to count, so the only way to say it worked is against a stated alternative, and requiring that alternative be defined up front is the discipline the field has been missing.","Assessment at the level of the specific implementation rather than the activity or the asset is the move that makes ordinary capital expenditure eligible: a fixture in a building can qualify without the building qualifying.","The three-part intent test, vulnerability context, stated intent, direct link, is designed to stop adaptation-washing, where spending that would have happened anyway is relabelled once a taxonomy exists.","Global thresholds remain subject to development by Technical Working Groups, so the taxonomy is a framework with the numbers still to come, and an issuer cannot yet self-certify against it.","Tying the timescale to the lifespan of both the measure and the activity it sits in prevents the common trick of claiming a long-lived benefit from a short-lived intervention."],"keywords":["Climate Bonds Resilience Taxonomy","adapting measure","global threshold","counterfactual","adaptation-washing","labelled bonds","CBRT"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":406,"title":"The Impacts of Climate Change Are Already Here: How Resilient Are We?","authors":"","date":"2026-06-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Europe","Global"],"institutions":["Resilience First","Jupiter Intelligence"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://resiliencefirst.org/news/the-impacts-of-climate-change-are-already-here-how-resilient-are-we/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This report summarizes a Resilience First roundtable held on 2 June 2026 at London SXSW, convening senior representatives from insurance, finance, transport, health, and telecommunications to assess organisational climate resilience. It finds preparedness improving but uneven, with persistent gaps in asset resilience, infrastructure planning, and long-term scenario analysis. Key themes include cascading cross-sector interdependencies, the underfunding of adaptation relative to mitigation, data gaps for physical risk quantification, and the need for CFO-level financial framing to unlock resilience investment.","insights":["Capital continues to flow predominantly toward mitigation rather than adaptation, driven by short-term incentive structures and decision-making frameworks that fail to capture long-term resilience value, a persistent structural barrier identified by cross-sector practitioners.","Investment decisions in organisational resilience are ultimately driven by CFOs, making translation of climate risk into financial risk language (cost of inaction, quantified externalities) a prerequisite for unlocking capital.","Human health impacts, including the northward spread of tropical diseases and heatwave effects on workforce productivity and safety, were identified as the most compelling near-term catalyst for corporate resilience action, framed as a duty-of-care obligation.","Cascading cross-sector risks (where failure in one system triggers disruption across others) represent a leading dimension of climate vulnerability, yet no unified or widely accessible framework exists to map these interdependencies.","Data gaps for physical climate risk quantification are a binding constraint: scientific data exists but is not communicated in ways accessible to business decision-makers, and confidentiality concerns further inhibit cross-sector data sharing.","Preparedness is uneven across organisations and sectors: while climate resilience is increasingly integrated into core business strategy with growing risk intelligence capabilities, significant gaps persist in asset resilience, infrastructure planning, and scenario analysis.","Participants called for embedding resilience into policy cycles, asset planning, and system design coherently across sectors and geographies, with cross-sector coordination currently fragmented, lacking centralised oversight and weak local-to-national integration."],"keywords":["cross-sector interdependencies","organisational resilience","physical climate risk data","CFO engagement","adaptation finance gap","cascading risk"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":313,"title":"Low Cost, High Yield: The Adaptation and Resilience Investment Opportunity for Infrastructure","authors":"Karina Whalley, Alma Djelic, Anthony Schrapffer, Matthieu Renard","date":"2026-06-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["International Finance Corporation","Climate Bonds Initiative","AXA Climate","Scientific Climate Ratings"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/2026/the-adaptation-and-resilience-investment-opportunity-for-infrastructure.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This IFC/AXA Climate report (June 2026) makes a quantitative financial and policy case for adaptation and resilience (A&R) investment in infrastructure, focused on emerging markets and developing economies (EMDEs). Using three Brazilian infrastructure assets (a 1,000 km, 500 kV transmission line in Mato Grosso, a water reservoir in Southeastern Brazil, and a S\u00e3o Paulo motorway concession), the report models NAV impacts and IRRs of targeted A&R measures under intermediate- and high-emissions scenarios. Headline findings: $8.6 protected per dollar invested in T&D, $6.3 in water, and $2.2 in roads; IRRs up to 886%. The report catalogues seven financing mechanism categories, maps structural barriers to A&R investment, and profiles live instruments including a USD 340 million blended finance fund, a USD 1.3 billion water facility, and the world's first CBI-certified resilience bond.","insights":["Across three Brazilian infrastructure business cases, targeted A&R measures costing less than 10% of NAV protect $2.2\u2013$8.6 in asset value per dollar invested over the asset's lifetime, with IRRs ranging from 56% (road bundle, short loan) to 886% (T&D firebreaks, 15-year loan); these high IRRs reflect the low cost of selected measures relative to avoided climate losses, not full project economics.","Without A&R, cumulative climate hazards erode approximately 22% of NAV under intermediate-emissions scenarios and up to 30% under high-emissions scenarios across the assets studied, confirming physical climate risk as a material valuation concern for infrastructure investors and lenders.","Loan maturity is the single most important financing lever: extending loan tenor from 6 to 15 years can double IRRs for road A&R measures, while interest rate variations produce comparatively minor effects, a critical insight for DFIs and development banks structuring concessional finance.","Firebreaks for the Mato Grosso transmission line reduce annual service outages from 10.8 to 1.6 days (85% reduction) and cut NAV losses from 30.3% to 7.6% under the high-emissions scenario, at a cost of only ~3% of NAV, the clearest documented unit-economics proof point in the report.","Climate impacts already cost low- and middle-income countries an estimated USD 390 billion annually (1\u20132% of GDP); without action, losses could increase 12\u201322% in middle-income and up to 33% in low-income countries by 2100, with the global cost of inaction estimated at USD 1.27 trillion between 2022 and 2100."],"keywords":["benefit-cost ratio","net asset value","emerging markets infrastructure","physical climate risk valuation","long-tenor financing","resilience-linked bonds","performance-based contracts","infrastructure debentures"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":305,"title":"Climate Resilience as Strategy: Scaling Corporate Action on Physical Risks from Climate Change","authors":"Verena Radulovic, Tessa Ide, Tucker Van Aken, Dee Yang","date":"2026-06-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Center for Climate and Energy Solutions (C2ES)","SystemIQ","S&P Global","Marsh","First Street Foundation","Swiss Re","Aon","FM Global","Climate Policy Initiative (CPI)","WBCSD","Climate Bonds Initiative","Jupiter Intelligence","Moody's","World Meteorological Organization","Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures","The Resiliency Company","Insurance for Good","BlackRock","ISS STOXX","Bloomberg"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.c2es.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Resilience-as-Strategy_Final.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This June 2026 report by C2ES and Systemiq, based on interviews with over 40 organizations including two dozen companies, assesses where corporations stand on physical climate risk management and what is needed to scale action. Key findings: 65% of public companies cite physical climate risk in filings, but only 25% of those acting have taken strategic measures. Without adaptation, major companies globally face projected annual losses of $1.2 trillion by the 2050s. The report proposes four practical infrastructure elements (a guidance catalog, a maturity model, a standardized corporate climate resilience framework, and sector-specific playbooks), and documents significant valuation, governance, and financing gaps holding companies back.","insights":["Major companies globally face projected annual losses of $1.2 trillion annually by the 2050s from physical climate risk if no adaptation action is taken, with large- and mid-cap companies already experiencing average annual losses of $88 billion in asset damage and $1.1 trillion in business interruption.","65% of public companies cite physical climate risk in SEC filings, more than double the 2001 share, but only 25% of those that have begun acting have taken genuinely strategic measures such as adapting products, services, or delivery models.","Among companies that have invested in operational resilience, 82% report positive financial or reputational results, 68% cite increased investor interest, 67% report improved insurance terms, and 56% say they have secured better lending conditions, per MSCI Institute.","A Bloomberg study of 2,900 companies found those with 10% higher physical climate risk exposure faced a weighted average cost of capital 22 basis points higher than peers; Dow Jones companies with insignificant physical risk exposure generated returns approximately 1.7x higher than peers with moderate exposure over the past decade.","FM Global's resilience credit program, premium offsets that clients reinvest in engineering improvements, had grown to $825 million (a 10% premium offset for eligible policies) by 2026 and driven a potential reduction in economic impact of nearly $80 billion, illustrating a scalable insurer-corporate co-investment model.","Adaptation finance reached an all-time high of $63 billion annually in 2021/22, but tracked private sector adaptation finance averaged only $4.7 billion per year for 2019\u20132022, a severe gap relative to need and indicative of the capital allocation problem the report targets."],"keywords":["corporate adaptation planning","physical risk valuation","enterprise risk integration","resilience maturity model","adaptation finance gap","insurer underwriting incentives","supply chain disruption"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":469,"title":"DEQ Announces $1.6M Nature-Based Flood Resilience Project in Bladenboro","authors":"","date":"2026-05-29","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.deq.nc.gov/news/press-releases/2026/05/29/deq-announces-16m-nature-based-flood-resilience-project-bladenboro","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"North Carolina's Flood Resiliency Blueprint awarded $1.6 million to the Bladenboro South Main Street Flood Resiliency Project, a nature-based scheme to create floodplain storage and restore natural streamflow so as to reduce recurring flooding in downtown Bladenboro. The town has been hit three times in eight years, by Tropical Storm Debby in 2024, Hurricane Florence in 2018 and Hurricane Matthew in 2016, with extensive damage to businesses and homes, and has already relocated businesses out of the floodplain. The project works by clearing and widening a blocked culvert to increase outlet capacity and restore natural streamflow, and pairs that with planting trees and native plants in the floodplain to stabilise stream banks, which together create over 3.9 million gallons of floodplain storage, enough to cover 12 acres at one foot depth, while controlling erosion and improving water quality and wetland habitat. The vendor is Resource Environmental Solutions, and the announcement names the state senator and representative who helped secure it.","insights":["The intervention is mostly a culvert: clearing and widening a blocked outlet is the core engineering, with planting added for bank stability and water quality, which makes the nature-based label partly a co-benefit on a drainage fix.","3.9 million gallons of storage for $1.6 million gives a directly comparable unit cost against the Swannanoa and Rosman awards under the same programme.","Three major floods in eight years is the pattern that justifies capital spending rather than repeated recovery, and the town had already relocated businesses out of the floodplain before seeking this award.","Delivery is through a private ecological restoration vendor rather than a nonprofit or the municipality, which is a different delivery model from the same programme's other awards in the same month."],"keywords":["Flood Resiliency Blueprint","floodplain storage","culvert capacity","nature-based","Bladenboro","recurring flooding","stream restoration"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":271,"title":"SEC Proposes Rescission of Climate-Related Disclosure Rules","authors":"U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission","date":"2026-05-29","year":2026,"resource_type":"Regulatory / Legislative Document","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["SEC (U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2026-49-sec-proposes-rescission-climate-related-disclosure-rules","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The SEC formally proposed rescinding its 2024 Climate-Related Disclosure Rules in their entirety, arguing the rules exceed the agency's statutory authority. Follows the March 2025 vote to cease defending the rules and the Eighth Circuit's September 2025 order requiring the Commission to determine the rule's fate. Materially changes the federal disclosure landscape for physical and transition climate risk.","insights":["The proposal is to rescind the climate disclosure rules in their entirety on the ground that they exceed the agency's statutory authority, with policy reasons offered as independent and additional rather than primary.","The procedural history is the substance: rules adopted March 2024, stayed April 2024 pending Eighth Circuit litigation, defence abandoned March 2025, and the petitions held in abeyance from September 2025 until the Commission either reconsiders by notice-and-comment or renews its defence. This rescission is that reconsideration.","Materiality is named as the organising principle, with the Commission stating that disclosure obligations should avoid the practical effect of dictating corporate behaviour and be imposed only where expected benefits justify the costs.","The rules being rescinded covered greenhouse gas emissions, management of climate-related risks and the financial statement effects of severe weather events, so the physical-risk disclosure that resilience analysis depends on is inside the scope of the rescission.","A 60-day comment period from Federal Register publication is the remaining procedural step."],"keywords":["SEC","climate disclosure","rescission","physical risk","regulation","federal","Scope emissions","10-K"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":284,"title":"Funding Climate Resilience Is a Coordination Problem, Not a Capital Problem","authors":"Lily Bui","date":"2026-05-28","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["The Epicenter","The Resiliency Company"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.epicenterinsights.com/funding-climate-resilience-is-a-coordination-problem-not-a-capital-problem/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This opinion piece by Lily Bui of SoCal Grantmakers argues that the primary barrier to climate resilience finance is coordination failure, not capital scarcity, with 2025 U.S. climate disasters causing an estimated $115 billion in damages from 23 events. Using the Los Angeles aqueduct as a historical analogy, it outlines a blended capital stack model where philanthropy funds pre-development, CDFIs and impact investors translate projects for institutional capital, and public funding scales de-risked projects. Three case studies illustrate the model: Florida affordable housing with IBHS FORTIFIED standards, the EPA's Greenhouse Gas Reduction Fund, and post-LA-fire recovery through the Resilient LA Delta Fund.","insights":["In 2025, U.S. climate-driven disasters caused an estimated $115 billion in damages across 23 separate billion-dollar events, yet the article's central argument is that sufficient capital exists, the gap is structural coordination, not volume.","Adaptation and resilience projects (forest management, urban cooling, early warning systems, home hardening) rarely align with a single funding source and remain heavily dependent on philanthropic and public funding, unlike climate mitigation (solar, wind, EVs) which has legible repayment structures for private capital.","The article proposes a tiered blended capital stack: philanthropy absorbs early-stage and pre-development risk; CDFIs and impact investors bridge and translate; public capital scales and de-risks; private institutional capital enters only after uncertainty is reduced, each layer performing a distinct, non-substitutable function.","The Resilient LA Delta Fund uses a Robert Wood Johnson Foundation guarantee alongside philanthropic capital to de-risk CDFI lending for post-Los Angeles wildfire recovery and resilience projects, with the explicit goal of eventually attracting larger private and institutional capital as repayment performance stabilizes.","Climate resilience districts using tax increment financing are highlighted as a promising place-based mechanism to capture value and reinvest it in long-term resilience infrastructure, potentially reducing dependence on episodic grant funding.","The EPA's Greenhouse Gas Reduction Fund is cited as a federal catalytic capital model that coordinates grants, concessional debt, private investment, and technical assistance through community lenders and green banks, though it is noted as currently stalled amid political and legal challenges."],"keywords":["blended capital stack","pre-development gap","capital coordination","CDFIs","IBHS FORTIFIED standards","resilience districts","tax increment financing","polycrisis"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":270,"title":"Climate Adaptation Finance as the Next Frontier of Public Financial Management","authors":"Alishba Khan","date":"2026-05-26","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Europe"],"institutions":["ICAEW (Institute of Chartered Accountants in England and Wales)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.icaew.com/insights/viewpoints-on-the-news/2026/may-2026/climate-adaptation-finance-as-the-next-frontier-of-public-financial-management","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"ICAEW argues that adaptation finance is emerging as the central priority for public financial management, warning that without robust outcome-based metrics public climate finance will remain fragmented and input-driven. Cites the $212B/year adaptation funding gap and Standard Chartered's $1:$12 benefit estimate to argue for stronger PFM systems to measure and optimize adaptation investments.","insights":["The framing question is not whether to fund adaptation but whether public financial systems can measure it: without robust metrics, adaptation finance stays fragmented and input-driven rather than outcome-oriented.","Adaptation has no single universal metric equivalent to carbon emissions in mitigation finance, which is why governments fall back on proxy indicators for exposure, sensitivity and adaptive capacity, and why spending is not comparable across countries or sectors.","Public adaptation finance is described as running through four interlinked channels, domestic budget allocations, concessional and multilateral finance, risk transfer mechanisms, and blended structures, with African Risk Capacity's parametric cover named as the rapid post-disaster liquidity example.","The Netherlands Delta Programme and Bangladesh's cyclone early warning are offered as cases where indicators are integrated into governance, against many developing countries where the same indicators sit fragmented across institutions and outside fiscal decision-making.","The UK's Climate Change Act 2008 is cited as a model for institutionalising climate risk in public financial management through a legally binding assessment and adaptation planning cycle."],"keywords":["public financial management","adaptation finance","PFM","outcome metrics","climate budget","ICAEW","fiscal management"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":269,"title":"Resilience-Adjusted Credit Risk: Operationalising Climate Adaptation in Financial Decision-Making","authors":"Cambridge Institute for Sustainability Leadership (CISL), Banking Environment Initiative","date":"2026-05-26","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global","Europe"],"institutions":["UK Centre for Greening Finance and Investment (CGFI)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.cisl.cam.ac.uk/news-and-resources/publications/resilience-adjusted-credit-risk-operationalising-climate-adaptation","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Cambridge Institute for Sustainability Leadership's Banking Environment Initiative introduces the Resilience-Adjusted Credit Risk (RACR) framework, a forward-looking approach that integrates physical climate risk exposure, insurance adequacy, and resilience measures into core credit metrics such as probability of default and loss given default. The framework provides banks a method to operationalise climate adaptation in lending decisions.","insights":["The framework's central move is to make adaptation investment a credit variable: it adjusts probability of default and loss given default to reflect both hazard exposure and the borrower's capacity to reduce it, recognising avoided loss as a PD and LGD reducer.","Current credit frameworks are described as assuming insurance is a static condition of lending, and the Insurance Adequacy component replaces that assumption with coverage verification, premium trajectory and an uninsurability horizon.","Six barriers are named as self-reinforcing rather than independent: prudential regime gaps, a revenue paradox in which adaptation benefits are not captured, low observed climate bad debts, attribution failures that hide climate losses inside standard risk categories, a horizon mismatch between credit tenor and physical risk, and a widening insurance blind spot. Invisible losses suppress urgency, which suppresses investment, which perpetuates the absence of evidence.","The framework is deliberately modular, letting banks begin with qualitative overlays and triage screens and add quantitative integration as data infrastructure develops, which is an adoption path rather than a standard.","Scaling is argued to require four things no single institution can build: a common adaptation taxonomy, shared data and modelling infrastructure, resilience-linked insurance pricing that rewards verified vulnerability reduction, and recognition of resilient assets in capital requirements. The EU Infrastructure Supporting Factor, the Monetary Authority of Singapore capital relief pilot and PRA SS5/25 are cited as early signals."],"keywords":["RACR","credit risk","physical climate risk","banking","insurance","adaptation","probability of default","CISL"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":606,"title":"Scalable Investment Platforms, Not Capital, Are Holding Back Climate Adaptation Finance in Asia","authors":"Eco-Business","date":"2026-05-21","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Asia","Latin America"],"institutions":["Temasek","Climate Fund Managers"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.eco-business.com/news/scalable-investment-platforms-not-capital-are-holding-back-climate-adaptation-finance-in-asia/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An Eco-Business report from Temasek's Ecosperity summit in Singapore, on a panel discussing the bankability of climate adaptation and resilience. Jeb Victorino, investment director at the Netherlands-based investment firm Climate Fund Managers, argues that scalable investment platforms rather than scarce capital are the main bottleneck for climate adaptation finance in Asia: there is ample private money looking at climate-aligned opportunities in the region but no multiplier effect from structures that can absorb capital at scale and channel it into resilience projects. The opportunity he identifies is in innovating and creating replicable platforms and repeatable capital structures so that investors can fund at scale even with limited resources, enabling private capital to match the public sector's magnitude in financing adaptation. He cites a debt-for-nature transaction structured three years earlier in which Ecuador exchanged about US$1.628 billion of its existing international bonds for a new US$656 million marine bond used to protect 150,000 hectares of mangroves in the Galapagos area, and says similar transactions were replicated for Vietnam and the Philippines through a US$1 billion blended finance facility targeting water, sanitation and oceans infrastructure in emerging markets.","insights":["**The panel's diagnosis is that the bottleneck is replicable structures rather than capital**, so the constraint it identifies is transaction design and repeatability rather than the supply of money.","The Ecuador numbers are the concrete ones: **$1.628 billion of old bonds exchanged for a $656 million marine bond**, so the conservation funding comes out of the discount on distressed sovereign debt rather than from new money.","Replication is the claim to test. The same structure reaching Vietnam and the Philippines through a $1 billion facility is the multiplier the panel describes, and it is a resilience structure being deliberately templated rather than built bespoke.","Naming 150,000 hectares of mangroves ties the financial structure to a physical protective asset, which is what distinguishes a debt-for-nature swap from a general-purpose refinancing."],"keywords":["debt-for-nature swap","marine bond","replicable platforms","bankability","blended finance facility","mangroves","Galapagos","adaptation finance Asia"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":521,"title":"Understanding PPPs for climate adaptation and resilience: A guide for city and regional authorities","authors":"Federico Aili, Valerie Brown, Leon Kapetas, Fani Gelagoti","date":"2026-05-20","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Resilient Cities Network","World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://resilientcitiesnetwork.org/understanding-ppps-for-climate-adaptation-and-resilience-a-guide-for-city-and-regional-authorities-2/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A guide for city and regional authorities on using public-private partnerships for climate adaptation, written by Resilient Cities Network with Grid Engineers. It explains PPP concepts, contract models such as DBFOM and its variants, payment mechanisms and revenue schemes, the finance structure of a PPP and the preparation cycle from identification to long-term contract management. Its distinctive content is climate risk allocation: distinguishing frequent, extreme and rare events and internal from external risks, on the argument that transparent risk sharing is what keeps a project financially viable while protecting the public interest. It is deliberately cautionary, naming unequal negotiating power, limited transparency, unclear incentives and lock-in to costly inflexible contracts as real risks, and stops short of design and implementation guidance.","insights":["The guide's central technical contribution is a climate risk allocation framework that separates frequent, extreme and rare events and internal from external risks, rather than treating climate risk as one category to be assigned to one party.","It defines a PPP by performance-based payment, a long-term agreement in which the private partner designs, finances, builds, operates or maintains a public asset and is paid on performance, which is what connects it to outcomes-based finance rather than to procurement.","Bankability is named as the thing a well designed PPP improves for adaptation projects, which is the specific market failure that makes adaptation hard to finance at municipal scale.","Three illustrative applications carry the argument: a stormwater PPP in an emerging market country, a revenue-generating urban dune system for coastal protection, and a flood-resilient bus rapid transit PPP. The dune example is the clearest case of a resilience asset carrying its own revenue line.","The guide is explicit that PPPs are not a quick fix and that many local authorities lack the legal, institutional and capacity base to negotiate them, so it positions itself before the design stage rather than as an implementation manual."],"keywords":["public-private partnership","climate risk allocation","DBFOM","bankability","municipal adaptation finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":378,"title":"IBHS's FORTIFIED Program Surpasses 100,000 Designations","authors":"","date":"2026-05-20","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Louisiana"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://ibhs.org/ibhs-news-releases/ibhss-fortified-program-surpasses-100000-designations/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS) announced in May 2026 that its FORTIFIED\u00ae program (a voluntary, beyond-code construction and re-roofing standard designed to reduce storm losses from high winds, hurricanes, and wind-driven rain), has surpassed 100,000 designations nationwide. More than half of those designations were earned in the past three years, driven by state grant programs in Alabama, North Carolina, and Louisiana, insurer incentives, and industry partnerships. Independent research following Hurricane Sally (2020) confirmed that FORTIFIED homes suffered significantly less damage than conventionally built homes, validating the program's loss-reduction claims and its role in stabilizing insurance markets.","insights":["FORTIFIED has surpassed 100,000 designations nationwide as of May 2026, with more than 50% earned in the last three years, indicating accelerating adoption rates driven by public-private investment.","Post-Hurricane Sally (2020) independent research by the University of Alabama confirmed FORTIFIED-standard homes experienced significantly less damage than conventionally constructed neighbors, providing real-world loss-reduction proof points for insurers and capital allocators.","State grant programs are the primary demand catalyst: Alabama's Strengthen Alabama Homes (SAH) grant, North Carolina Insurance Underwriting Association grants covering 18 coastal counties, and Louisiana's Fortify Homes Program have each driven large-scale regional adoption.","Alabama Insurance Commissioner explicitly linked FORTIFIED adoption to reduced claims and a stronger state insurance market, framing resilient construction as a market-stabilization tool, a direct signal to insurers and reinsurers pricing wind risk.","The program has expanded from single-family residential to commercial buildings and multifamily communities, broadening the addressable market and opening pathways to institutional and portfolio-scale resilience investment.","A first-of-its-kind alliance with GAF to certify FORTIFIED roofing contractors addresses workforce and supply-side constraints that could otherwise cap adoption, representing an industry infrastructure investment in resilience delivery capacity.","Additional states (Kentucky, Maine, Minnesota, Mississippi, Oklahoma), are developing or considering mitigation grant legislation, indicating a policy pipeline that could substantially grow the designation count and associated loss-reduction impact."],"keywords":["beyond-code construction","wind loss reduction","home hardening grants","FORTIFIED standard","insurance incentives","storm damage mitigation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":468,"title":"DEQ Announces $1.2M for Flood Resilience Projects in Swannanoa and Rosman","authors":"","date":"2026-05-19","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","North Carolina"],"institutions":["North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.deq.nc.gov/news/press-releases/2026/05/19/deq-announces-12m-flood-resilience-projects-swannanoa-and-rosman","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"North Carolina's Flood Resiliency Blueprint awarded more than $1.2 million for two flood resilience projects in Swannanoa and Rosman, both in the French Broad River Basin and both in communities that had not previously received Blueprint funding. Together the projects will create nearly 9 million gallons, or 27 acre-feet, of flood water storage, restore 3,000 feet of tributary streams and three acres of wetlands, stabilise streambanks and install natural stormwater infrastructure. In Swannanoa, $822,780 goes to the regional nonprofit RiverLink to lead a project on a river severely damaged by Hurricane Helene, creating 1.6 million gallons of storage and capturing and filtering pollutants, with the site identified during field inspections for a damage assessment. In Rosman, the award goes to Conserving Carolina to work on 30 acres of former agricultural fields, slowing flood waters and creating floodplain storage to protect downstream infrastructure and property. The release places these in programme context: the Blueprint had previously funded more than $8.8 million across 16 projects in the French Broad River Basin, and has now funded 84 projects across six river basins totalling more than $43 million.","insights":["The programme reports its output in hydrological units rather than dollars alone, 9 million gallons of storage from $1.2 million, which is the kind of unit cost a state can use to compare nature-based against grey alternatives.","Both awards go to regional nonprofits, RiverLink and Conserving Carolina, rather than to municipalities, so the delivery model routes state resilience money through land-focused civil society organisations.","Deliberately funding basins and communities that had not yet received Blueprint money is a distributional rule inside the programme rather than a competitive outcome.","At 84 projects across six river basins for more than $43 million, the average award is around $500,000, which sets the scale of project this instrument is designed for.","Restoring 30 acres of former agricultural fields to floodplain is land use change financed as flood infrastructure."],"keywords":["Flood Resiliency Blueprint","floodplain storage","nature-based flood resilience","Hurricane Helene","French Broad River","stream restoration","North Carolina"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":399,"title":"Parametric Insurance for Natural Disasters: Frequently Asked Questions","authors":"Diane P. Horn, Baird Webel","date":"2026-05-19","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","The Nature Conservancy","National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration","Congressional Research Service"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12670","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Congressional Research Service Insight (updated May 2026) provides a primer on parametric (index-based) disaster insurance for Members of Congress, prompted by the FEMA Review Council's May 2026 recommendation to develop a parametric funding model for Public Assistance grants. It explains the mechanics of parametric versus indemnity insurance, outlines advantages (speed, flexibility, coverage breadth) and disadvantages (basis risk, regulatory gaps), and surveys U.S. and international examples including CCRIF payouts, state and municipal parametric wind and flood policies, Puerto Rico earthquake/hurricane coverage, and wildfire catastrophe bonds issued by the Los Angeles Department of Water and Power.","insights":["CCRIF paid nearly $19.3 million to Dominica within 14 days of Hurricane Mar\u00eda in 2017, and $85 million to five Caribbean countries within two weeks of Hurricane Beryl in 2024, demonstrating parametric insurance's speed advantage over traditional indemnity claims that can take months or years.","The FEMA Review Council (May 2026) recommended developing a parametric funding model for Public Assistance grants as a lump-sum formula grant, signaling a potential federal policy shift that could reshape disaster recovery finance for state and local governments.","Basis risk is the central underwriting challenge: the New Orleans School District's 2024 parametric wind policy (100 mph trigger) did not pay out after Hurricane Francine despite school facility damage, illustrating how trigger design can leave real losses uncompensated.","The Los Angeles Department of Water and Power has issued three parametric wildfire catastrophe bonds since 2020, each with a different trigger, transferring wildfire risk directly to capital market investors, a replicable structure for utility wildfire exposure.","New York City used parametric flood coverage in 2023 as a financing mechanism for an emergency assistance program specifically targeting low- and moderate-income households, linking parametric instruments to distributional equity outcomes.","Puerto Rico purchased parametric coverage for both hurricanes and earthquakes in 2023 to satisfy FEMA's requirement to maintain insurance after receiving Public Assistance, showing how federal conditionality can drive parametric market adoption.","Most parametric policies in the U.S. are purchased by public-sector entities; the retail market (Jumpstart, StormPeace, FloodFlash, Recoop) is growing but remains nascent, indicating significant unmet demand and market-building opportunity."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","basis risk","disaster risk transfer","index-based insurance","FEMA reform","catastrophe bonds","public assistance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":285,"title":"Advancing Adaptation in Southeast Asia","authors":"Ashwin Balasubramanian, Mekala Krishnan, Annabel Farr","date":"2026-05-18","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Asia"],"institutions":["McKinsey Global Institute","McKinsey & Company"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/sustainability/our-insights/advancing-adaptation-in-southeast-asia?stcr=7C458EAA44534ABFA1F77B3487E06566&cid=mgp_opr-eml-alt-mst-mgp-glb--&hlkid=9e3d1a3134dd4962a81d64563b0ad61a&hdpid=733a94e3-3746-4b42-b74f-51d06055b69d#/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This McKinsey Global Institute article (May 2026) quantifies the adaptation gap in Southeast Asia across 11 countries and 20 intervention measures. The region currently spends ~$12 billion per year on adaptation against a $37 billion annual need today and ~$84 billion by 2050 under a 2\u00b0C scenario. More than 80% of required spending targets measures with benefit-to-cost ratios above 3:1, rising to ~7:1 at 2\u00b0C. The article identifies barriers to finance, outlines roles for businesses, insurers, banks, institutional investors, and governments, and highlights blended finance, parametric insurance, resilience bonds, and land-value capture as key instruments.","insights":["Southeast Asia faces an annual adaptation gap of ~$25 billion today ($37B needed vs. $12B spent), widening to ~$72 billion by 2050 at 2\u00b0C warming ($84B needed); this gap persists even as the region already spends ~$18 per capita per year on adaptation.","More than 80% of required adaptation spending in Southeast Asia targets measures with benefit-to-cost ratios above 3:1; at 2\u00b0C the average BCR across measures rises to ~7:1, making adaptation economically compelling relative to inaction.","Active cooling (air conditioning) alone accounts for 48% of the $84B annual adaptation cost at 2\u00b0C, with heat-related measures (heat stress + heat waves) comprising 71% of total spending, signaling large addressable markets for cooling technology and efficiency finance.","By 2050, ~91% of Southeast Asia's population could live in climate-hazard-exposed areas (vs. ~50% today), with heat wave exposure rising from 16% to >80% of the population, materially increasing physical risk for asset owners and lenders in the region.","Even if adaptation spending grew in proportion with GDP growth, only ~$39B (46%) of the $84B 2050 annual need would be met, demonstrating that economic growth alone cannot close the gap and that dedicated financing mechanisms are required.","Adaptation cost in some countries (e.g., Indonesia, Philippines) would require close to 40% of average monthly income per person, highlighting severe distributional constraints and the need for subsidies, social protection, and concessional capital to reach lower-income populations.","Less than 15% of flood-exposed coastlines in Southeast Asia are adequately protected (vs. ~25% globally), and outside Singapore fewer than 20% of people have air conditioning access, quantifying the current protection deficit for infrastructure investors and insurers."],"keywords":["adaptation gap","benefit-cost ratio","Southeast Asia resilience","blended finance","physical risk pricing","heat stress","coastal protection","build resilience first"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":272,"title":"Jamaica Targets Upsized $200m of Parametric Insurance from New IBRD CAR Jamaica 2026 Catastrophe Bond","authors":"Artemis.bm","date":"2026-05-18","year":2026,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Caribbean","Latin America"],"institutions":["World Bank","Artemis"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/news/jamaica-now-targets-upsized-200m-of-parametric-insurance-from-new-catastrophe-bond/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Government of Jamaica raised the target size for its third catastrophe bond, IBRD CAR Jamaica 2026, to $200M of parametric named-storm and hurricane protection, facilitated by the World Bank. The upsizing reflects strong ILS investor appetite and continued use of cat bonds by sovereign issuers to transfer hurricane risk away from public balance sheets.","insights":["This is a sponsor returning to the market immediately after a full payout: Jamaica's second cat bond was exhausted paying $150 million in full after Hurricane Melissa in October 2025, with funds reaching the government within weeks, and the third issuance upsized from $150 million to $200 million.","The upsizing came with tightening rather than widening pricing: risk margin guidance narrowed from an initial 6.5 to 7.25 percent range to 6.75 to 7 percent, against an initial expected loss of 2.48 percent, so investor appetite absorbed a larger deal after a total loss.","The structure is a per-occurrence parametric named storm and hurricane cover issued by the IBRD under its global debt issuance facility and Capital-At-Risk notes programme, so the sovereign accesses capital markets protection through a multilateral rather than directly.","The term covers four hurricane seasons to maturity in May 2030, which sets the sovereign's protection horizon and its re-entry date.","Jamaica's sponsorship history, $185 million in 2021, $150 million in 2024 and $200 million in 2026, is a rare case of a sovereign parametric programme with a demonstrated payout and continuous renewal."],"keywords":["Jamaica","catastrophe bond","parametric insurance","hurricane","World Bank","IBRD","sovereign risk transfer","ILS"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":373,"title":"Louisiana Orders Every Property Insurer to Discount Fortified Homes by January 2027","authors":"","date":"2026-05-13","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Louisiana"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","Verisk","Moody's","RMS","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Insurance Business Magazine"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.insurancebusinessmag.com/us/news/risk-compliance-legal/louisiana-orders-every-property-insurer-to-discount-fortified-homes-by-january-2027-575095.aspx","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Louisiana Regulation 136 (Fortified Homes Premium Discounts), promulgated via LDI Bulletin 2026-04 signed April 29, 2026, mandates that every property and casualty insurer authorized in the state provide premium discounts on FORTIFIED-designated residential properties by January 1, 2027. Discounts are set by a Louisiana-specific benchmark table built by the NAIC using Verisk and Moody's hurricane catastrophe models across three IBHS designation levels (Roof, Silver, Gold) and three geographic regions. The regulation eliminates insurer discretion on discount levels, making premium reduction a compliance requirement rather than a voluntary offering.","insights":["Louisiana mandates FORTIFIED home premium discounts effective January 1, 2027, removing insurer discretion, discounts are set by a state-prescribed benchmark table, making this one of the most prescriptive home-hardening insurance incentive regimes in the U.S.","The benchmark discount table was built by the NAIC's Catastrophe Risk Management Center of Excellence using two industry-standard hurricane catastrophe models (Verisk/AIR and Moody's/RMS), providing a replicable, model-driven methodology that other states could adopt.","Three IBHS FORTIFIED tiers (Roof, Silver, Gold) are modeled at varying mitigation levels, and discounts are regionalized across North, Central, and South Louisiana, enabling geographic granularity that maps to actual hurricane risk gradients.","Modeled hurricane expected loss reductions are converted directly into premium discounts after adjustment for insurer expense loads derived from publicly available Louisiana homeowner rate filings, creating a transparent actuarial bridge between physical risk reduction and policyholder savings.","The regulation creates a direct financial incentive linkage between home-hardening investment (IBHS FORTIFIED designation) and insurance cost, which could accelerate adoption of beyond-code construction standards and reduce insurer loss exposure in hurricane-prone areas.","Carriers face operational compliance requirements across rating systems, underwriting workflows, and policyholder documents, insurers or producers unable to demonstrate discount application by January 1, 2027 face regulatory exposure.","Because the methodology document is publicly available and the discount benchmarks are posted at ldi.la.gov/fortifiedbenchmarks, the actuarial assumptions underpinning the discounts are contestable by carriers through the modeling layer, creating a defined channel for industry pushback."],"keywords":["FORTIFIED homes","premium discount mandate","hurricane catastrophe model","home hardening","IBHS standards","building resilience standards","rate filing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":262,"title":"Flood Risk, Insurance, and Housing in the United States","authors":"Suvy Qin, John L. Voorheis","date":"2026-05-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Bureau of Economic Research"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.3386/w35204","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This NBER working paper (Qin & Voorheis, May 2026) uses confidential linked administrative and survey microdata from the US Census Bureau's Environmental Impacts Frame to provide the first comprehensive household-level analysis of flood risk distribution in the United States from 1999\u20132023. Key findings overturn aggregate-data conclusions: high-income households (especially the top 1%) are disproportionately exposed to flood risk; the share of households in floodplains has remained stable despite population growth; and a vulnerable subpopulation of low-income, uninsured homeowners in single-family homes, only ~1.74% of floodplain households, faces compounded risk across income, structure, and property-damage dimensions. Insurance cost burdens are sharply regressive, with premiums consuming 10\u201315% of income for the lowest-income vigintile versus under 1% for the top.","insights":["Aggregate-data analyses produce aggregation bias: they show a monotonically decreasing income gradient in flood risk exposure, but household-level microdata reveal a non-monotonic relationship where the top 1% of income earners have ~19% floodplain exposure, higher than any other income group, including the lowest quartile (~16%).","The share of US households in floodplains has remained essentially stable from 1999\u20132023 (e.g., parcel-level FEMA exposure held between 14.2\u201314.4%), despite absolute counts rising from ~11 million to ~15 million, reflecting overall population growth rather than increasing relative flood risk exposure.","Only ~1.74% of floodplain households are simultaneously low-income, uninsured, and in single-family homes, the group vulnerable across all three dimensions of income, structure type, and property damage risk.","Insurance cost burdens are sharply regressive: premiums average 10\u201315% of household income for the lowest income vigintile of homeowners, versus below 1% for the top income vigintile, even as absolute premium levels are higher for high-income owners.","Renters face higher floodplain exposure rates than homeowners (18% vs. ~12% by end of sample period), but are not liable for structural property damages, their primary risks are personal property loss and dislocation.","When second homes are included, top-income households' floodplain exposure rises from ~18% to ~27%, and the top 1% income group holds 7% of total floodplain home value versus ~3.5% of non-floodplain home value, indicating severe concentration of at-risk asset value.","Black and Hispanic homeowners are more likely to be uninsured in both floodplain and non-floodplain areas; in floodplains, uninsured homeowners without a mortgage were ~4 percentage points more likely to be in a floodplain than insured homeowners (~15% vs. ~11%) in 2023."],"keywords":["flood insurance gap","floodplain exposure","aggregation bias","income gradient","microdata","uninsurance rate","National Flood Insurance Program","housing tenure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":611,"title":"Cayman Considers Catastrophe Bond, More Parametric Insurance to Expand Hurricane Protection","authors":"Artemis","date":"2026-04-27","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Caribbean"],"institutions":["Caribbean Catastrophe Risk Insurance Facility"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/news/cayman-considers-catastrophe-bond-more-parametric-insurance-to-expand-hurricane-protection/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An Artemis report, drawing on the Cayman Compass, that the government of the Cayman Islands is considering expanding its hurricane protection ahead of the 2026 Atlantic season by potentially issuing its own catastrophe bond while simultaneously expanding its parametric insurance coverage. The islands are highly hurricane-prone and very low-lying, which makes them extremely vulnerable to storm surge. Minister for Finance and Economic Development Rolston Anglin said the ministry had decided to increase natural catastrophe coverage, explaining that the current coverage level had been in place for a considerable period while exposure had increased, the country had grown, and there were more assets at risk. Two options are described: the government issuing its own catastrophe bond, or increasing its coverage in CCRIF SPC, formerly the Caribbean Catastrophe Risk Insurance Facility, a regional catastrophe fund providing specialised parametric insurance to Caribbean and Central American governments. Anglin said all options would be looked at, including catastrophe bonds, increasing traditional coverage and parametric solutions, and assessed holistically, and on the cat bond specifically that it comes down to cost and whether the cost justifies going down that route.","insights":["**The minister states the decision rule out loud**: it comes down to cost, and to whether the cost justifies the route. That is a finance ministry saying what would actually make it issue, rather than an argument that it should.","The stated reason for needing more cover is growth, not climate: the country has grown and there are more assets at risk, so the buyer attributes rising exposure to development rather than to hazard.","The choice between issuing a bond and buying more CCRIF cover is the choice between bespoke and pooled, and it is the decision a small sovereign faces that a large one does not. CCRIF exists because Cayman alone is too small to interest the market efficiently.","The instrument is being considered rather than placed, so the durable content is the reasoning; whether Cayman issued is a separate fact to check later."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond","parametric insurance","CCRIF SPC","sovereign risk transfer","storm surge","Cayman Islands","risk pooling","cost of cover"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":529,"title":"Mayor Wu Releases 2030 Climate Action Plan to Reduce Carbon Emissions and Strengthen Climate Resilience","authors":"","date":"2026-04-27","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["City of Boston"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.boston.gov/news/mayor-wu-releases-2030-climate-action-plan-reduce-carbon-emissions-and-strengthen-climate","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Mayor Michelle Wu announced Boston's 2030 Climate Action Plan, an implementation plan for reducing community-wide greenhouse gas emissions 50 percent by 2030 and reaching carbon neutrality by 2050 while increasing resilience to sea level rise and extreme weather. The plan covers buildings, transportation, energy, workforce development, coastal resilience and protection from extreme weather, and is framed as a shift from city government goals to citywide coordinated action. Progress is to be tracked through 22 metrics on a public implementation dashboard.","insights":["Boston reports being on track for a 48 percent emissions reduction by 2030 through existing programmes alone, so the plan's additional actions are closing a two-point gap rather than carrying the whole target.","The resilience half is specific about instruments of control rather than spending: screening City capital projects for future climate risk, the Coastal Flood Resilience Overlay District, Net Zero Carbon Zoning, and the Building Emissions Reduction and Disclosure Ordinance.","The regional argument for coastal protection is asset based: New England's prosperity is said to depend on Boston's hospitals, universities, highway and rail network and food systems, which is the case for protecting infrastructure that is not the city's own.","The strategies are projected to support approximately 67,000 full-time jobs annually, which is the economic rather than avoided-loss side of the case.","Accountability is designed in as 22 tracked metrics on a public dashboard over five years, covering buildings meeting the zoning and disclosure standards, heat pumps and weatherisation, tree plantings for cooling, transit ridership and climate job training graduates."],"keywords":["climate action plan","coastal flood resilience overlay","BERDO","climate budgeting metrics","urban heat"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":273,"title":"First Street Expands Climate Risk Coverage from Real Estate to Companies and Infrastructure","authors":"First Street","date":"2026-04-27","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["First Street Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/first-street-expands-climate-risk-coverage-from-real-estate-to-companies-and-infrastructure-302753492.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"First Street launched two new analytical modules, a Company Module that connects physical climate risk to corporate earnings, credit, and valuation across operating footprints and supply chains, and a Complex Assets Module covering transportation, data centers, energy, and industrial campuses. Extends the firm's property-level methodology to corporate and infrastructure asset analysis.","insights":["The expansion is a move from asset-level hazard modelling to financial translation: the Company Module connects site and supplier disruption through to earnings, credit and valuation, so an analyst can judge when physical risk becomes material to the P and L.","The Complex Assets Module extends analysis from individual properties to infrastructure systems, transport networks, data centres, energy systems and industrial campuses, and identifies the specific segments driving vulnerability rather than scoring a whole asset.","The stated gap being closed is consistency: investors lacked a single framework in which property-level exposure, supply chain interdependency and financial outcome could be evaluated together, which is what pricing across due diligence, underwriting and capital allocation requires.","The organisation's own path, founded as a research nonprofit and now selling an enterprise suite, is a case of peer-reviewed public-interest modelling becoming commercial risk infrastructure.","Coverage is claimed across billions of buildings worldwide with models quantifying likelihood and severity of flood, wildfire and extreme temperature into damage, downtime and financial loss."],"keywords":["First Street","physical climate risk","REITs","infrastructure","supply chain","corporate climate risk","credit","valuation"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":229,"title":"Climate Health Implementation Manual","authors":"","date":"2026-04-24","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://hdl.handle.net/10986/44800","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Climate Health Implementation Manual (CHIM) serves as a comprehensive guide for integrating climate considerations into health systems, emphasizing the critical need for climate-resilient systems to achieve universal health coverage. It provides practical tools and strategies across governance, data management, workforce training, sustainable procurement, and waste management, encouraging multisectoral collaboration to enhance health outcomes amidst climate challenges.","insights":["The CHIM is a roadmap for integrating climate health considerations into existing health systems.","Building climate-resilient health systems is essential for achieving universal health coverage.","Nearly 50% of the World Bank's health commitments support climate action as part of health system strengthening.","Climate-smart investments can lower operating costs and enhance health service reliability during extreme weather events.","Environmental sustainability in health procurement can achieve cost savings and ensure supply security.","Pooling and regional procurement can enhance environmental criteria without sacrificing cost-effectiveness.","New procurement contracts can leverage innovative structures like Product-as-Service to ensure accountability.","Blended finance mechanisms can effectively support the development of climate-smart health products."],"keywords":["sustainability","procurement","governance","public health","supplier assessment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":140,"title":"New Method to Raise Investment Funds for Projects That Restore Coastal Wetlands for Climate Adaptation","authors":"Mike Pe\u00f1a","date":"2026-04-24","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Center for Coastal Climate Resilience at UC Santa Cruz"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://news.ucsc.edu/2026/04/quantifing-value-coastal-ecosystems/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A new methodology has been developed to create investment opportunities for coastal wetland restoration and protection projects, by quantifying their storm protection benefits. This innovative approach enables the generation of Coastal Resilience Assets, which can be sold to investors, ultimately aiming to address the funding gap for nature-based climate adaptation solutions.","insights":["The new methodology quantifies the dollar value of storm protection provided by coastal wetlands.","Coastal Resilience Assets can be sold to private and public investors, creating a new funding avenue for wetland restoration projects.","A web-based calculator has been released to help project developers assess the financial value of mangrove conservation.","The approach shifts funding from traditional debt-based models to proactive investments in nature-based solutions.","The methodology has been evaluated and published by Verra, ensuring a standardized approach to resilience credits.","Wetlands like mangroves have shown significant storm damage reduction, with estimates of $4.1 billion saved during Hurricane Ian.","The framework provides a clear investment opportunity for parties interested in reducing climate risks.","California is positioned as a global leader in developing mitigation markets to support coastal community resilience.","This methodology helps demonstrate the economic value of nature-based solutions, encouraging investment in flood risk reduction.","The project emphasizes the need to improve accountability and transparency in climate adaptation financing."],"keywords":["Coastal Resilience Assets","mangrove conservation","flood risk reduction","standardized investment","nature-based solutions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":274,"title":"Climate Risk Threatens REIT Revenue: First Street Analysis of 45,000 Properties Across 65 REITs","authors":"Sara Wolak","date":"2026-04-22","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["First Street Foundation","HousingWire"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.housingwire.com/articles/climate-risk-reit-revenue-first-street/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Analysis of 45,000 properties across 65 publicly traded REITs found that climate disruption already costs ~1.1% of revenue ($3.1B). Under a 1-in-100-year severe-weather scenario, average losses reach ~15% of revenue and total losses near $43B, with nearly one-third of firms potentially losing 20%+ of annual revenue. Highlights material physical-risk exposure in listed real estate.","insights":["The exposure is already in earnings rather than pending: climate disruption is said to cost the typical REIT about 1.1 percent of annual revenue, roughly $3.1 billion across the index, before any tail event.","The tail is severe and concentrated: 98 percent of REITs would lose revenue in a 1-in-100-year event, more than half would lose at least 10 percent and nearly a third more than 20 percent, with an index-wide 1-in-100 loss of about $43 billion and a 1-in-500 loss near 30 percent of annual revenue.","Self-insurance is emerging as the response to cover becoming expensive or unobtainable, with some REITs reserving against loss rather than paying premiums, which moves the risk onto the balance sheet where it is less visible.","The report's own framing is that real estate is a geographic commitment, so unlike other asset classes the exposure cannot be relocated, and US assets are more than 80 percent of global REIT locations with heavy concentration in California, Texas and Florida.","REITs already using forward-looking climate data showed nearly 7 percentage points less revenue at risk in severe scenarios, a relationship the report explicitly cautions is not necessarily causal, and wildfire is currently the most severe hazard while wind is expected to become the primary driver of future losses."],"keywords":["REIT","First Street","climate risk","flood","wildfire","wind","real estate","1-in-100"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":233,"title":"Scaling Up Investment for a Resilient Power Sector: The Role of Robust Disaster-Risk Financing","authors":"Selena Jihyun Lee, Bolormaa Chimednamjil","date":"2026-04-20","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","Energy Sector Management Assistance Program (ESMAP)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/44685","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The document discusses the urgent need for increased financial investment in the power sector to manage escalating climate and disaster risks, with developing countries requiring up to $365 billion annually by 2035. It highlights a significant gap in funding and emphasizes the importance of effective deployment of disaster risk financing (DRF) strategies. A layered risk management approach, incorporating insurance and contingent financing, is essential for maintaining liquidity and enabling rapid recovery in the power sector, ultimately linking financial resilience with broader economic growth and infrastructure improvements.","insights":["Developing countries need $310 to $365 billion annually for climate adaptation by 2035.","International public finance for adaptation dwindled to $26 billion in 2023.","The investment need for climate adaptation is 12 to 14 times greater than current funding.","Disaster risk financing (DRF) can sustain liquidity and speed up recovery processes.","Risk layering in DRF includes mechanisms for risk retention, contingent financing, and risk transfer.","Mexico's catastrophe bond program showcases effective use of DRF in disaster management.","Regional risk pools, like the CCRIF, provide cost-effective insurance solutions for small island states.","Systemic financial planning is crucial to avoid ad hoc borrowing and fiscal crises after disasters.","Integrating DRF considerations in public-private partnerships can enhance investment resilience.","Physical resilience measures must complement financial protection mechanisms for effective climate adaptation."],"keywords":["disaster risk financing","investment mobilization","risk management","infrastructure resilience","public-private partnerships"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":139,"title":"2026 Behavioral Health Report","authors":"Katie Patton, Clara Petrucelli, Maggie Jackson, Austin Miller, Allison Oakes","date":"2026-04-20","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Trilliant Health"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.trillianthealth.com/market-research/reports/2026-behavioral-health-report?utm_source=linkedin&utm_medium=paid&utm_campaign=2026%20Behavioral%20Health%20Report&hsa_acc=506296765&hsa_cam=973806283&hsa_grp=608705543&hsa_ad=1215707853&hsa_net=linkedin&hsa_ver=3&li_fat_id=cd629835-e14d-4f73-9188-f5f7713cb31b#intro","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 2026 Behavioral Health Report highlights a significant worsening of the behavioral health crisis in the U.S. since the COVID-19 pandemic, with nearly one in four adults experiencing mental illness and drastic increases in substance use mortality. It underscores disparities in treatment access, particularly among young adults and multiracial individuals, while emphasizing the rising demand for telehealth services amidst a backdrop of provider shortages and burnout. The projected economic impact of untreated mental health issues is substantial, calling for a coordinated response to enhance accessibility and effectiveness in behavioral health services.","insights":["The prevalence of mental illness has increased by 5.7 percentage points, affecting nearly 23.4% of adults in 2024.","Telehealth for behavioral health treatment accounted for 65.6% of all telehealth visits in 2024, representing a significant shift in care delivery.","Untreated mental illness is projected to impose a substantial economic burden on the U.S., estimated to exceed $477.5 billion annually.","Over 65% of adults cite financial barriers as a primary obstacle to accessing behavioral health services.","The shortage of psychiatrists is acute, with less than 10% of behavioral health providers being MDs or DOs, primarily led by master-level clinicians.","Emergency department visits for alcohol and substance use disorders grew from 33.8% to 39.1% between 2018 and 2024."],"keywords":["mental health","substance use","telehealth","provider shortages","economic burden"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":216,"title":"Where Rising Climate Risks and Insurance Costs Will Hit Hardest","authors":"Manann Donoghoe","date":"2026-04-16","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["The Brookings Institution"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/where-rising-climate-risks-and-insurance-costs-will-hit-hardest/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report discusses how rising climate risks and homeowners insurance costs are exacerbating affordability issues, particularly in disadvantaged communities. It highlights the increasing premiums that homeowners face in climate-exposed areas and the variable impact on different demographics, emphasizing the role of adaptive capacity in determining how households respond to these challenges. Without proactive policy changes, the instability in the homeowners insurance market may further entrench existing wealth disparities and barriers to homeownership.","insights":["Rising premiums in the homeowners insurance market threaten affordability and access to homeownership for many Americans.","Low-income homeowners are particularly vulnerable, often dropping or reducing coverage in response to increased insurance costs.","Disadvantaged communities, especially low-income Black and Latino families, bear a larger financial burden from climate risks and insurance instability.","Adaptive capacity, or the ability to respond to climate risks, varies significantly across different demographic groups and regions.","The insurance market instability is influenced by a lack of political will to address systemic climate risks.","ZIP codes categorized as 'adaptable' face the highest premium increases but typically have higher wealth, allowing residents to absorb those costs more easily.","Vulnerable and at-risk ZIP codes, even with smaller absolute premium increases, may experience greater financial strain relative to their lower wealth.","The relationship between race and insurance premium changes indicates that Black-majority ZIP codes are more likely to experience significant impacts from insurance market instability.","California serves as a significant case study for understanding the relationship between climate risks, insurance, and housing affordability.","Future policies should integrate housing considerations with climate resilience strategies to combat growing disparities."],"keywords":["insurance market","homeownership","affordability","adaptive capacity","disadvantaged communities"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":581,"title":"Urban Planning for Climate Resilience: Evaluating Multi-Level Network of Resilience Plans in Miami-Dade County","authors":"Sarbeswar Praharaj; Shouraseni Sen Roy; Sofia Bond; Mirna Obeid; Tyreke Walker","date":"2026-04-15","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of Miami","Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scs.2026.107405","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article in Sustainable Cities and Society by five University of Miami authors, evaluating the network of resilience plans in Miami-Dade County through an in-depth content analysis of 37 plans. The question is not whether individual plans are good but whether multi-level, cross-sectoral strategies across a network of plans are coordinated toward a unified long-term outcome. The authors build methods for assessing a network of plans on equity, integration with other proposals, dynamic relationships across governance levels, and measurable frameworks for assessing the co-benefits of implementation. They find that flood resilience and environmental management dominate while health and equity remain underrepresented; that the plans converge on expanding green space, improving canal systems and installing flood barriers while their approach to greenhouse gas mitigation stays loosely defined; and that neighbourhood-level plans consistently outperform city and county plans on most indicators. Their conclusion is that weak integration and limited feedback across the planning network, together with the absence of co-benefit evaluation mechanisms, undermine coordinated climate-resilience governance. The introduction cites the IPCC's Sixth Assessment estimate that without adaptation, flooding alone will cause $14 to $20 trillion in infrastructure and property damage by 2100, with over 1 billion people at risk of coastal hazards each year.","insights":["Neighbourhood plans outperforming city and county plans is the finding with the most consequence for financing, and it runs against how resilience capital is actually organised: money is raised and allocated at the county and city level, where the plans are weakest, while the strongest plans sit at the scale with no balance sheet.","The absence of co-benefit evaluation mechanisms is named as a governance failure, and it is the same gap the Rebuild by Design CRA guidebook runs into from the financing side: a park that is also a stormwater asset cannot be underwritten on a benefit nobody measures.","Thirty-seven plans for one county is itself the finding about integration. A network that large with weak feedback produces convergence on visible interventions, green space, canals and barriers, and divergence on the things that need coordination.","Health and equity being underrepresented across the network matters for instrument design, because those are the benefits that justify public and concessional capital rather than commercial capital."],"keywords":["resilience plans","Miami-Dade County","plan network analysis","coastal resilience","co-benefits","climate governance","equity","flood barriers"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":160,"title":"California Can\u2019t Afford to Wait: The Fiscal Case for Sustained Resilience Investment","authors":"Nuin-Tara Key, Joel Martinez","date":"2026-04-15","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["California Forward","Insurance for Good"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.insuranceforgood.org/blog/california-cant-afford-to-wait","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource argues that California urgently needs to increase its investments in resilience to mitigate the financial impact of climate risks, as the state faces a growing gap between the necessary funding and available revenue. Climate disasters exacerbate costs for citizens and governments, highlighting the need for innovative funding strategies to ensure long-term resilience investment amidst a volatile budget environment.","insights":["California's economic losses from climate disasters could reach up to $131 billion due to wildfires alone in 2025.","The state is projected to face structural deficits of approximately $35 billion annually starting in 2027-28.","Under-investment in resilience leads to multiplied costs, including significant financial impacts on households and local governments.","Insurance companies are withdrawing from high-risk areas, increasing liability for the California FAIR Plan.","California's reliance on personal income tax and capital gains complicates the sustainability of resilience funding.","Many existing resilience programs depend on non-durable funding sources, limiting their longevity and effectiveness.","Other states have successfully implemented dedicated revenue strategies for climate adaptation, demonstrating viable funding alternatives.","Proactive investment in resilience can reduce emergency response costs and stabilize insurance markets.","Dedicated revenue for resilience can complement existing funding mechanisms while addressing California's unique fiscal context."],"keywords":["fiscal sustainability","disaster recovery","funding strategies","economic impact","insurance market"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":255,"title":"A Systematic Review of Nature-Positive Climate Risk Transfer and Financing Instruments","authors":"Alina Bill-Weilandt, David Lallemant, Vivien Chan, Meherwan Rohinton Patel, Perrine Hamel","date":"2026-04-09","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Nanyang Technological University","Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03388-0","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This systematic review analyzes nature-positive climate risk transfer and financing instruments (CRTFIs) that aim to enhance climate resilience through support for ecosystem services. By identifying 33 distinct CRTFIs and creating a typology for categorization, the review reveals significant funding gaps, particularly in private sector investment, and highlights the need for innovative financing strategies that incorporate equity considerations and robust risk modeling. The findings emphasize the economic potential of nature-based solutions while addressing challenges in measurement and reporting.","insights":["Nature-based solutions (NBS) significantly enhance climate resilience but are underfunded.","The majority of global investment in NBS comes from public sources, indicating a need for greater private sector involvement.","There are significant equity gaps in the existing literature on NBS financing.","Two-thirds of reviewed projects do not quantify hazard regulation benefits of nature-based solutions.","Innovative financial instruments can help close adaptation and nature finance gaps by transferring or reducing climate risks."],"keywords":["ecosystem services","public-private partnerships","risk assessment","biodiversity","financing instruments"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":236,"title":"Loans to Lifelines: How Lending Innovations Enhances Climate Resilience","authors":"Karen Lewin, Juan Camilo Fl\u00f3rez Falla, Grecium Kachingwe","date":"2026-04-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Consultative Group to Assist the Poor (CGAP)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.cgap.org/research/podcast/loans-to-lifelines-how-lending-innovations-enhances-climate-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The podcast episode explores innovative lending approaches, specifically recovery lending and contingent lines of credit, designed to support frontline communities during climate crises. Traditional microfinance institutions (MFIs) often reduce lending after disasters, but these new methods enable timely financial assistance, helping individuals and businesses quickly rebuild their livelihoods. The discussion includes successful examples from Malawi and Colombia, highlighting how these strategies can enhance resilience and maintain lender-client trust.","insights":["Traditional microfinance often decreases lending during crises, worsening recovery for affected communities.","Recovery lending provides customized post-disaster loans to help businesses restore operations.","Contingent lines of credit activate based on specific weather triggers, providing timely financial support without lengthy procedures.","Successful recovery lending leads to high repayment rates, demonstrating its effectiveness in rebuilding livelihoods.","Preparation and training within MFIs before disasters are crucial for effective response and recovery lending.","Predictive weather models can enhance the effectiveness of contingent credit lines by informing clients ahead of potential disasters.","Flexible funding from donors is essential for scaling these innovative lending solutions.","Inclusive finance institutions can become more resilient by investing in early-warning financial tools for clients.","Building trust through reliable support during crises helps MFIs maintain long-term client relationships."],"keywords":["microfinance","financial innovation","risk management","disaster recovery","predictive analytics"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":345,"title":"A Way to Tackle Climate Change and Sovereign Debt","authors":"Emily Hardy, Dan Helmeci","date":"2026-03-26","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum","World Wildlife Fund","The Nature Conservancy","Pew Charitable Trusts","Inter-American Development Bank","African Development Bank","European Investment Bank","Columbia University Climate School","IP Quarterly"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://ip-quarterly.com/en/way-tackle-climate-change-and-sovereign-debt","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This article from IP Quarterly's \"Carbon Critical\" column examines debt-for-nature swaps as a complementary financial mechanism to jointly address sovereign debt distress and climate vulnerability, particularly in low- and middle-income countries and Small Island Developing States. It traces the instrument's history from 1982 to recent landmark transactions, including Ecuador's $1.6B swap (2023) and El Salvador's $1B swap (2024), and analyzes deal structures, scale limitations, credit enhancement roles of DFIs like the U.S. DFC, and the intergenerational equity dimensions of both crises. The article advocates for endowment-based trust fund structures to sustain conservation and adaptation outcomes beyond electoral cycles.","insights":["The Gal\u00e1pagos debt-for-nature swap produced $1.126 billion in lifetime debt forgiveness, reduced the coupon rate to 5.64% from a prior range of 17\u201326%, and redirected $450 million toward conservation, representing one of the most financially impactful transactions to date.","Belize's debt-for-nature swap reduced the principal by $189 million and cut the country's debt-to-GDP ratio by 12 percentage points, demonstrating a measurable macroeconomic benefit alongside conservation outcomes.","Between 2021 and 2023, over 3.4 billion people lived in countries whose governments spent more on debt interest payments than on health or education, underscoring the fiscal crowding-out that debt swaps seek to partially address.","In 2021, 40% of Small Island Developing States were either highly indebted or in debt distress, a population disproportionately exposed to climate hazards such as tropical cyclones, creating a vicious borrowing-and-reconstruction cycle.","Hurricane Maria caused damages equivalent to 226% of Dominica's GDP in 2017, illustrating how climate disasters force climate-vulnerable, high-debt nations into even more expensive borrowing for reconstruction rather than resilience investment.","Roughly 50 debt-for-nature swaps have occurred since 1987, but recent deals (Ecuador $1.6B in 2023; El Salvador $1B in 2024) still represent only a small fraction of those countries' total sovereign debt ($39.9B and $31.6B respectively), highlighting the scale gap.","The U.S. Development Finance Corporation has provided political risk insurance in over half of debt-for-nature deals since 2020, but its potential withdrawal under the Trump administration creates a credit-enhancement gap that the African Development Bank or European Investment Bank could fill."],"keywords":["debt-for-nature swap","sovereign debt distress","credit enhancement","trust fund endowment","small island developing states","intergenerational equity","development finance institutions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":213,"title":"State Insurance Regulators Issue Nationwide Homeowners Market Data Call","authors":"","date":"2026-03-26","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://content.naic.org/article/state-insurance-regulators-issue-nationwide-homeowners-market-data-call","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"State insurance regulators, during the NAIC 2026 Spring National Meeting, announced a nationwide data call aimed at gathering comprehensive homeowners insurance market data down to the ZIP code level. This initiative seeks to enhance understanding of insurance options, assess costs and accessibility, monitor financial stability of insurers, and evaluate mitigation efforts in preparation for severe weather.","insights":["The data call will collect the most extensive homeowners insurance policy data in the U.S.","It aims to provide ZIP code-level insights on coverage, costs, and availability.","Regulators will evaluate how different coverage options and deductibles impact accessibility and pricing.","The call will include data on various policy types such as home, renter, and condo insurance.","Insurers with a minimum relevant premium of $50,000 will be required to participate in the data call.","Data requested will cover policy years from 2018 to 2025.","The public will have an opportunity to comment on the report before its finalization.","A public report is scheduled for release in early 2027 following data validation and analysis.","The initiative is seen as a consumer protection effort by state regulators.","The NAIC will oversee the data collection process and report development."],"keywords":["insurance regulation","data collection","consumer protection","policy analysis","financial monitoring"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":159,"title":"CA FWD's 2026 Vision for a Resilient, Sustainable, and Inclusive Economy","authors":"","date":"2026-03-25","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["California Forward"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://cafwd.org/resources/ca-fwd-2026-vision/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"California Forward (CA FWD) is a California-based policy and economic development organization whose 2026 program portfolio targets the intersection of climate resilience, fiscal stability, and inclusive economic growth. Key initiatives include the Resilience District Incubator (piloting sustained local revenue streams for risk reduction), the California Resilience Implementation Accelerator (seed funding and technical assistance for regional projects), a wildfire policy workgroup, an adaptation finance framework, and community benefits agreement standards for clean energy development. The document functions as an organizational prospectus rather than a quantitative study, but maps the institutional architecture CA FWD is building to mobilize capital and policy for climate resilience at the state and regional level.","insights":["CA FWD's Resilience District Incubator is designed to pilot local and regional mechanisms for generating sustained revenue streams dedicated to risk reduction and resilience investments, a structure analogous to special assessment districts or tax increment financing applied explicitly to climate resilience.","The California Resilience Implementation Accelerator couples seed funding with hands-on technical support and cross-sector learning networks, suggesting a blended-finance facilitation model intended to de-risk and accelerate project pipelines at the regional level.","CA FWD frames climate risk explicitly as a 'fiscal and economic issue,' signaling an intent to translate physical risk into public budget and insurability terms, the language capital allocators and ratings agencies use, rather than purely environmental terms.","The Wildfire Policy Workgroup and planned reform of California's Rainy Day Fund indicate an effort to embed climate volatility buffers directly into state fiscal architecture, with implications for municipal credit quality and state bond ratings.","The Energy Projects Benefits Agreement Database and associated community of practice represent an attempt to standardize community-developer agreements across clean energy projects, potentially reducing transaction costs and legal uncertainty for developers and project financiers.","CA FWD explicitly targets incoming gubernatorial and legislative leadership with roadmaps on Bioeconomy and Battery Manufacturing, positioning climate-economy integration as an industrial policy imperative rather than a regulatory compliance issue."],"keywords":["fiscal resilience","wildfire policy","community benefits agreements","resilience district","bioeconomy","battery supply chain","adaptation finance framework","state-regional governance"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":558,"title":"Why Climate Adaptation Is Key to US Energy Expansion","authors":"Lindsey Hall; Rick Lord; Matt Macfarland; Kuntal Singh; Paul Munday","date":"2026-03-19","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["S&P Global"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.spglobal.com/en/research-insights/special-reports/look-forward/energy-futures/climate-adaptation-key-us-energy-expansion","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An S&P Global thought leadership report asking whether major US power producers are prepared for climate physical risk, published 19 March 2026 and explicitly not a rating action. Using the S&P Global Sustainable1 Climate Physical Risk data set under the SSP2-4.5 scenario, it projects that the S&P 500 electric utilities, multi-utilities and independent power producers in its sample face nearly $68 billion in annual financial impact from climate physical risks by 2040, rising to about $77 billion by 2050, in the absence of adaptation. Against that, it uses S&P Global Corporate Sustainability Assessment data on which companies have disclosed a context-specific climate adaptation plan, and finds that nearly one third of planned US power generation capacity is being developed by companies that lack one. US electricity demand is projected to rise 42 per cent by 2050 in S&P Global Energy's base case.","insights":["The $68 billion figure is deliberately a worst case: the model excludes the companies' own adaptation and resilience efforts, so the number is the size of the problem adaptation investment is available to offset rather than a forecast of realised loss. That construction is what makes it usable as a benefit ceiling.","The report separates routine utility hardening from a climate adaptation plan as the CSA defines it, a context-specific plan that follows from a climate risk assessment of individual assets. On that definition the utilities sector's standard practice does not automatically qualify, which is why a sector that is a leader in resilience still shows a disclosure gap.","Linking the disclosure gap to planned capacity rather than to company count is the analytically useful move: it says how much of the forthcoming US grid is being built by firms without an adaptation plan, which is a forward-looking exposure measure rather than a backward-looking one."],"keywords":["physical climate risk","electric utilities","adaptation plan","SSP2-4.5","Corporate Sustainability Assessment","disclosure gap","power generation capacity","financial impact projection"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":433,"title":"Council Passes Climate Resiliency Legislation Sponsored by Councilmembers Luedtke, Friedson and Glass","authors":"","date":"2026-03-18","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Montgomery County Green Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://mcgreenbank.org/council-passes-climate-resiliency-legislation-sponsored-by-councilmembers-luedtke-friedson-and-glass/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Montgomery County Council passed Bill 2-26, a fuel-energy tax amendment sponsored by Councilmembers Dawn Luedtke, Andrew Friedson and Evan Glass, which lets the Montgomery County Green Bank apply fuel-energy tax revenue it already receives to climate resilience and adaptation projects alongside its existing renewable energy and energy efficiency financing. The release is explicit that the bill creates no new tax and is budget neutral: what changes is the permitted use of an existing revenue stream. The resilience projects named as newly eligible are increasing drainage capacity, protecting electrical systems in buildings, expanding tree canopy, installing early flood warning systems and creating green infrastructure to capture stormwater. Councilmember Friedson notes that earlier legislation, the 2022 Montgomery County Green Building Now Act, increased the Green Bank's capacity from $10 million to $230 million, which is the balance sheet this scope expansion now applies to adaptation.","insights":["The instrument is a scope change rather than new money: an existing green bank with an existing dedicated revenue stream is permitted to finance adaptation, which is budget neutral and therefore far easier to pass than a resilience levy.","The capitalisation matters more than the bill: the Green Bank went from $10 million to $230 million under 2022 legislation, so extending its mandate to resilience puts a substantial existing balance sheet behind adaptation.","Green banks were built for decarbonisation and this is a worked example of one being redirected to adaptation, which is a replicable route for the many US jurisdictions that already have one.","The eligible project list is concrete and unglamorous, drainage capacity, building electrical protection, tree canopy, early flood warning, stormwater capture, which is what a financeable municipal resilience pipeline actually looks like."],"keywords":["green bank","Montgomery County","fuel-energy tax","climate resilience financing","flood mitigation","heat resilience","budget neutral","local legislation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":548,"title":"Sustainable Debt Market Nears USD7 Trillion in Aligned Issuance, Demonstrating Strong Global Momentum","authors":"","date":"2026-03-17","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global","Europe","US"],"institutions":["Climate Bonds Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatebonds.net/news-events/press-room/press-releases/sustainable-debt-market-nears-usd7-trillion-aligned-issuance-demonstrating-strong-global-momentum","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Climate Bonds Initiative's press release for the 15th Global State of the Market report, covering 2025. Cumulative aligned issuance of green, social, sustainability and sustainability-linked debt reached USD6.8 trillion by the end of 2025, with annual aligned issuance above USD1 trillion for the third consecutive year and more than 400 new issuers entering the market. Green labels account for about two thirds of the cumulative total. The release highlights adaptation and resilience finance as a rapidly emerging priority for sustainable capital markets and names the world's first Climate Bonds Certified resilience bond, issued by the Tokyo Metropolitan Government in 2025.","insights":["THE FIRST CERTIFIED RESILIENCE BOND IS NAMED HERE: the Tokyo Metropolitan Government issued it in 2025 under Climate Bonds certification, which is the market's first instance of an adaptation instrument carrying a third-party resilience label rather than a green one.","Green remains dominant at 64 per cent of aligned GSS+ issuance in 2025 and above USD4 trillion cumulatively, with USD653.5 billion in 2025 alone, the second-highest annual volume on record, so the resilience label is arriving into a market whose shape is already set by mitigation.","Sustainability-linked bonds are the fastest growing and the smallest, rising 46 per cent year on year to USD14 billion, against sustainability bonds at USD217.3 billion and social bonds at USD141.2 billion.","Geography is concentrated: Europe took 45 per cent of aligned annual volume and USD3 trillion cumulatively, Asia-Pacific issued USD305.6 billion in 2025, and North America is approaching USD1 trillion cumulatively, with the United States still the largest cumulative source despite reported pushback on environmentally focused initiatives.","The market's reach is widening even as volumes plateau: 109 countries have now issued aligned sustainable debt and more than 400 new issuers entered in 2025, against annual volumes slightly below 2024 and 2021.","Climate Bonds sets the opportunity against the whole bond market, valued above USD100 trillion, which is the scale argument for why debt markets rather than concessional finance are the route to resilience investment."],"keywords":["GSS+ issuance","certified resilience bond","Tokyo Metropolitan Government","green bonds","sustainability-linked bonds","Global State of the Market"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":278,"title":"S&P Global Ratings: Global Sustainable Bond Market to Consolidate in 2026 at $800-900 Billion","authors":"","date":"2026-03-12","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["S&P Global"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://press.spglobal.com/2026-03-12-S-P-Global-Ratings-Forecasts-Global-Sustainable-Bond-Market-Will-Consolidate-In-2026-with-Issuance-Levels-at-800-900-billion","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"S&P Global Ratings forecasts global sustainable bond issuance to consolidate at $800B-$900B in 2026 as regional trends diverge and the market matures. Adaptation, transition, and digital infrastructure investments are expected to complement activity from repeat issuers, with green bonds remaining roughly 60% of total volume.","insights":["The forecast is consolidation rather than growth: global sustainable bond issuance stabilising at $800 billion to $900 billion in 2026, described as the era of rapid expansion giving way to measured growth.","The named headwinds are rising debt maturities, shifting policy priorities and a more competitive capital market, so the plateau is attributed to issuer conditions rather than to weakening demand for the label.","Regional trends diverge, with Europe remaining the largest market, US issuance slowing, and Asia-Pacific, Latin America and the Middle East sustaining activity.","US municipal issuers are noted as continuing to fund transportation, water infrastructure and climate resilience projects while preferring conventional bonds to avoid the additional reporting that a label carries, which is a direct cost-of-label finding for resilience finance.","Analysts expect the market to shift its emphasis from growth to credibility, transparency and measurable outcomes, which raises the weight on taxonomies and verification."],"keywords":["S&P Global","sustainable bonds","green bonds","transition","adaptation","issuance forecast","2026"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":382,"title":"Louisiana Regulation Sets Mandatory Discounts for Fortified Homes","authors":"","date":"2026-03-10","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Louisiana"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","Verisk","RMS","Moody's","Insurance Journal"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/southcentral/2026/03/10/861210.htm","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Louisiana's Regulation 136 mandates that insurers provide hurricane premium discounts to residential properties achieving IBHS Fortified designation, effective January 1, 2027. Discounts are geographically tiered, higher in central and south Louisiana's higher-risk coastal zone than in the north, and benchmarked using catastrophe models from Verisk and Moody's. The state's companion Louisiana Fortify Homes Program provides grants of up to $10,000 for roof fortification; over 11,000 Fortified roofs have been completed statewide, including 4,100+ through grants. The regulation links resilience investment directly to insurance premium reductions, creating a replicable policy-to-market mechanism for hurricane-exposed residential properties.","insights":["Regulation 136 establishes mandatory hurricane premium discounts for IBHS Fortified homes in Louisiana, effective January 1, 2027, covering all new or renewed residential property insurance policies, translating a voluntary resilience standard into a binding financial incentive.","Existing insurer practice already shows discounts of 15%\u201330% for Fortified roofs, providing a market-derived range against which the new mandatory benchmark discounts can be measured; the regulation locks in and standardizes this range.","Discounts are geographically tiered using the Louisiana Coastal Zone boundary and CAT model outputs from Verisk and Moody's (RMS), demonstrating how risk analytics can directly underpin premium discount schedules and create regionally differentiated financial incentives.","The Louisiana Fortify Homes Program has delivered over 4,100 grant-assisted Fortified roofs (grants up to $10,000 each), implying at least $41M in potential grant deployment; total statewide Fortified roof count exceeds 11,000, indicating significant private uptake beyond the grant program.","Fortified roofs are engineered to withstand wind speeds up to 130 mph, providing a quantifiable physical performance standard that directly grounds the insurance discount in measurable hazard reduction.","The regulation creates a direct policy linkage between a third-party resilience standard (IBHS Fortified), state grant programs, catastrophe modeling, and mandatory insurer premium adjustments, a replicable model for other hurricane-exposed states.","Capital allocators (insurers, lenders, ratings agencies) gain a state-sanctioned, model-backed discount table, reducing basis risk in pricing Fortified vs. non-Fortified properties and potentially supporting differentiated mortgage or insurance underwriting criteria."],"keywords":["Fortified home standard","hurricane premium discount","IBHS Fortified","catastrophe modeling","mandatory discount regulation","residential mitigation grants"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":277,"title":"Climate Bonds Initiative 2026 Sustainable Finance Trends Report","authors":"","date":"2026-03-09","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Bonds Initiative","TodayESG"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.todayesg.com/cbi-releases-2026-sustainable-finance-trends/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Climate Bonds Initiative's annual outlook projects sustainable bond issuance at ~$900B in 2026, with rising emphasis on adaptation-labelled, transition, and resilience-aligned instruments. Identifies the maturation of repeat issuers and accelerating allocation to physical adaptation and digital infrastructure as defining 2026 themes.","insights":["Adaptation and resilience investment is named as becoming the core of climate finance rather than an adjunct, with the Climate Bonds Resilience Taxonomy offered as the framework covering seven adaptation and resilience themes.","Cumulative global green, social and sustainable bond issuance is put at $6.75 trillion by 2025, which is the scale against which any resilience-labelled segment should be read.","Taxonomies are described as expanding beyond climate toward broader environmental goals, with biodiversity a likely 2026 focus and multilateral development banks having jointly developed a nature finance taxonomy in 2025.","Power grid investment is flagged as excluded from climate financing in many emerging markets and developing economies, which the Initiative is trying to reprioritise so grids can absorb future renewable generation.","The document is a secondary summary of the Climate Bonds report rather than the report itself, so its figures are reported at one remove."],"keywords":["Climate Bonds Initiative","sustainable bond","green bond","transition bond","issuance forecast","adaptation","2026 outlook"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":622,"title":"Moody\u2019s Ratings Publishes First In-Depth Report on Puerto Rico in Five Years","authors":"Puerto Rico Fiscal Agency and Financial Advisory Authority","date":"2026-03-05","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","Caribbean","Puerto Rico"],"institutions":["Moody's","Puerto Rico Fiscal Agency and Financial Advisory Authority"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://docs.pr.gov/files/AAFAF/Press_Room/PRESS%20RELEASES/2026/For-1st-time-in-5-years-Moodys-Ratings-publishes-in-depth-report-on-PR.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A press release of 5 March 2026 from the Puerto Rico Fiscal Agency and Financial Advisory Authority welcoming Moody's Ratings' publication of an Issuer In-Depth report on the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, titled Record Employment Levels Reached in 2025 Despite Ongoing Economic Obstacles. The release records that this is the first time in years that one of the three major nationally recognized credit rating agencies has published substantive commentary focused specifically on Puerto Rico's economy and government finances, and the administration of Governor Jenniffer Gonzalez Colon treats the renewed engagement as a signal of improved standing with the rating agencies and the capital markets. The Moody's findings highlighted are total nonfarm employment reaching an annual average of 963,100, the territory's highest since 2009 and up 15.3 per cent since 2020; private sector employment at a record 778,100 in December 2025 with broad gains across leisure and hospitality, healthcare, construction and retail trade; unemployment below 6 per cent since July 2023; and a steadily improving labour force participation rate. The release's own headline also claims economic resilience and improvements to the energy grid.","insights":["The useful fact is the **re-engagement itself**: a rating agency resuming substantive coverage of an issuer after years of silence is a capital-markets access event, and for a territory that went through both bankruptcy and Hurricane Maria it is the clearest available signal of restored standing.","Grid improvement appears in the headline and barely in the body, which is the gap to record. Puerto Rico's grid is the most consequential resilience question the territory faces and this release treats it as a supporting claim behind employment statistics.","**It is a government press release about a rating agency report**, so the citable content is the employment figures and the fact of publication; the first-party document is the Moody's Issuer In-Depth."],"keywords":["Puerto Rico","Moody's Ratings","issuer in-depth","credit rating engagement","employment recovery","energy grid","capital markets access"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":335,"title":"FORTIFIED Discount Ranges by Carrier","authors":"","date":"2026-03-05","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Louisiana"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","Citizens Property Insurance Corporation","Liberty Mutual","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Louisiana Watershed Initiative","Houma Times"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://houmatimes.com/?p=224279","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This news article from the Houma Times covers Louisiana's expanding fortified roof program and the Louisiana Insurance Commissioner's effort, coordinated with a national panel, to establish mandatory minimum premium discounts (targeted at 20\u201330%) for homeowners with certified fortified roofs. The article documents current carrier discount ranges (State Farm 19\u201335%, USAA 19\u201337%, Liberty Mutual 40%, Allstate 26\u201330%, Louisiana Farm Bureau 25%, Louisiana Citizens 7\u201320%, Progressive 6\u201310%), the state's $30M annual grant program offering $10,000 per homeowner, and Alabama's benchmark discount tiers (25\u201355%) as a reference point. Over 11,100 fortified roofs have been installed in Louisiana since 2021, with demand far exceeding supply of grants.","insights":["Louisiana's fortified roof program has resulted in 11,100+ certified roofs since 2021: 4,100+ via $10,000 state grants and 7,000+ installed outside the lottery system, demonstrating meaningful private-market uptake beyond public subsidy.","The state Legislature doubled annual investment in fortified roofs from $15M to $30M in 2025, signaling sustained public commitment to supply-side subsidy as a property insurance market stabilization tool.","Carrier discount ranges vary significantly: Liberty Mutual offers a flat 40% wind-premium discount vs. Progressive's 6\u201310%, illustrating that without a regulatory floor, homeowner savings are highly carrier-dependent and opaque.","Louisiana is moving to require minimum benchmark discounts (~20\u201330%) on wind-portion premiums for fortified roofs, with an actuarial escape valve, a model that links mitigation investment directly to insurance premium reduction without a hard mandate.","Alabama, the national leader with 56,000 fortified roofs, uses a tiered discount structure (25\u201335% for Fortified; 35\u201345% for Silver; 45\u201355% for Gold) that Louisiana is studying, suggesting a replicable policy architecture for other hurricane-exposed states.","Demand far exceeds supply: 10,400+ homeowners applied for only 1,000 grants in the last cycle, indicating a substantial unmet market and potential for scaled financing instruments beyond lottery-based grants.","Governor Landry has solicited Louisiana Workers' Compensation Corp. reserves to expand the fortified roof grant program, illustrating creative public capital mobilization outside standard legislative appropriation.","The Insurance Institute for Business and Home Safety (IBHS) sets the three-tier certification standard (Fortified, Silver, Gold), providing a standardized, third-party-verified framework that underpins both regulatory and insurer discount structures."],"keywords":["fortified roof discount","homeowner insurance premium","wind mitigation","grant program","insurance regulation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":126,"title":"Global Debt Report 2026: Sustaining Debt Market Resilience Under Growing Pressure","authors":"","date":"2026-03-04","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["OECD"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/global-debt-report-2026_e9d80efd-en.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Global Debt Report 2026 analyzes the resilience of global debt markets under increasing pressure from historical borrowing levels, shifting investor behaviors, and rising interest rates. With sovereign and corporate debts reaching record highs, the reliance on short-term funding has intensified, increasing refinancing risks. The report highlights the critical impacts of macroeconomic factors, including inflation and geopolitical tensions, on market stability and liquidity, while also noting the transformations in investor profiles and the growing role of non-bank financial institutions.","insights":["Global sovereign borrowing is projected to reach USD 18 trillion in 2026, with refinancing requirements at an all-time high for OECD countries.","Corporate borrowing levels have surged to USD 13.7 trillion, driven by capital-intensive demands, especially in the AI sector.","There is a significant increase in the reliance on Treasury bills for funding, reflecting a shift towards shorter maturities, which elevates refinancing risks.","Despite rising interest rates, corporate credit spreads remain low due to improved liquidity rather than enhanced credit quality.","A structural shift in the investor base is occurring, with more price-sensitive investors emerging, which may compromise market stability.","Elevated real yields signal concerns regarding the sustainability of fiscal paths, with median 10-year real yields reaching approximately 1.5%.","The presence of hedge funds in the bond markets is growing, potentially increasing liquidity but also raising volatility risks during market stress.","Governments and corporations are adjusting their debt issuance strategies towards shorter maturities, impacting risk management and payment burdens."],"keywords":["debt sustainability","market stability","investor behavior","liquidity","geopolitical risks"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":568,"title":"Strategic Recalibration of Climate Finance: Maximizing Impact, Managing Disruption","authors":"Victoria Barbary; Dan Carol","date":"2026-03-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Milken Institute","OECD"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://milkeninstitute.org/content-hub/research-and-reports/reports/strategic-recalibration-climate-finance-maximizing-impact-managing-disruption","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Milken Institute report by Victoria Barbary and Dan Carol, dated March 2026, proposing a distribution model for global climate finance across mitigation, adaptation and resilience. It takes the New Collective Quantified Goal agreed at COP29 in Baku in November 2024, known as the Baku Finance Goal, which tripled the 2009 target of $100 billion to $300 billion a year by 2035 as a compromise against the $1.3 trillion developing nations had sought. The authors note that OECD data for 2022 show developed nations providing $115.9 billion, roughly a third of the target, and that at around 26 per cent annual growth the $300 billion looks achievable on momentum alone, but that reaching it is not the same as allocating it well. The report's contribution is a model for the optimal distribution of the $300 billion across the three pillars and for using the private sector to reach toward the $1.3 trillion, written against geoeconomic and technological disruption that is raising energy demand and straining public funding commitments.","insights":["The framing separates two questions that are usually merged: whether the Baku target will be met, which the authors think momentum answers, and how the money should be split between mitigation, adaptation and resilience, which nothing currently answers.","Treating resilience as a third pillar distinct from adaptation is a deliberate choice, and it makes the authors' figures non-comparable with accounting that uses two categories and folds resilience into adaptation.","The $300 billion against the $1.3 trillion ask is the gap the private-sector half of the model has to close, so the report is effectively a mobilisation ratio argument rather than a public finance one.","The authors are explicit that this continues their own 2024 and 2026 strategy decks, so the record is one position in a series rather than a standalone analysis."],"keywords":["Baku Finance Goal","New Collective Quantified Goal","climate finance allocation","mitigation adaptation resilience","private capital mobilization","COP29","COP30"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":325,"title":"Minnesota Climate Adaptation and Resilience Cost Study: The Projected Costs of Climate Change Impacts and Adaptation Actions Under Future Emissions Scenarios","authors":"Industrial Economics, University of Minnesota Climate Adaptation Partnership, Consensus Building Institute, Two Degrees Adapt","date":"2026-03-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Minnesota"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","US Environmental Protection Agency","Minnesota Pollution Control Agency","Industrial Economics Inc.","University of Minnesota Climate Adaptation Partnership","Two Degrees Adapt","Consensus Building Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.pca.state.mn.us/sites/default/files/lrcc-mn-1sy26.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Minnesota Pollution Control Agency's March 2026 report to the Legislature, produced with Industrial Economics (IEc), the University of Minnesota, and partner consultants, quantifies projected costs of climate change impacts and adaptation across five sectors (Health and Wellbeing, Agriculture and Livestock, Transportation and Electricity Infrastructure, Buildings, and Ecosystems), under three emissions scenarios (Intermediate, High, Very High) and three future time periods (2040\u20132099). Without additional adaptation, Minnesota faces $17\u2013$22 billion per year in quantified climate damages by Mid-Century and $36\u2013$57 billion by End-of-Century. Adaptation investments of $2.5\u2013$4.1 billion per year yield a cost-of-inaction-to-adaptation ratio of 8\u201315:1, less than 1% of Minnesota's ~$500B GDP. The study uses FrEDI, CMIP6 GCMs, FEMA HAZUS, and county-level datasets, and calls for a dedicated statewide adaptation financing plan.","insights":["Without additional adaptation, Minnesota's quantified climate change costs reach $17\u2013$22 billion/year by Mid-Century (2040\u20132059) and $36\u2013$57 billion/year by End-of-Century (2080\u20132099) under the High emissions scenario; these figures are likely conservative as many cost categories could not be quantified.","The benefit-cost case for adaptation is strong: quantified annual costs of inaction are 8\u201315 times larger than corresponding adaptation costs ($2.5\u2013$4.1 billion/year average over 2040\u20132099), with adaptation costs less than 1% of Minnesota's 2024 GDP of ~$500 billion.","Premature mortality from heat and degraded air quality (including wildfire PM2.5 at $6.1\u2013$7.1B/year and ozone at $440M\u2013$510M/year at Mid-Century) accounts for roughly half the quantified cost of inaction, valued using EPA's Value of a Statistical Life, which the study projects at $23.1M (2050), $30.0M (2070), and $38.0M (2090) in 2024 dollars.","Building-level flood adaptation illustrates scalable marginal returns: a $140M/year increase in annual adaptation spending (High vs. Moderate scenario) avoids $1.3B/year in flood damage at Mid-Century; $570M/year by End-of-Century avoids $6.4B in residual flood damage, a BCR exceeding 11:1 at the margin.","Agricultural irrigation investment of $700M/year for corn, soybean, and wheat by End-of-Century could prevent $3.1B/year in crop productivity losses (BCR >4:1); proactive transportation upgrades of $0.8B/year could prevent $4.3B+ in annual repair and delay costs at Mid-Century, both demonstrating strong unit economics for forward-looking capital allocation."],"keywords":["adaptation cost quantification","cost of inaction","benefit-cost ratio","FrEDI framework","floodproofing","premature mortality valuation","invasive species management","wildfire suppression costs"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":11,"title":"An Uninsurable Country","authors":"Rob Moore","date":"2026-02-24","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Natural Resources Defense Council"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nrdc.org/resources/uninsurable-country","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report 'An Uninsurable Country' highlights a pressing home insurability crisis in the U.S., driven by escalating climate-related risks and inadequate state-level responses. With millions of homeowners facing rising costs and limited insurance options, especially in disaster-prone regions, the report emphasizes the urgent need for state governments to reform insurance markets, enhance building codes, and explore alternative solutions like parametric insurance to safeguard communities and ensure property insurability.","insights":["Between 7% and 13% of homes in the U.S. are currently uninsured.","Rising incidents of severe weather lead to increased insurance premiums and limited coverage options.","Older homes are often not built to withstand modern climate risks, making them harder to insure.","A growing number of homeowners are turning to state-created insurers of last resort as private coverage becomes scarce.","Disparities exist with low-income and minority homeowners more likely to be uninsured.","Parametric insurance is emerging as a viable alternative to traditional insurance models.","Failure of conventional insurance models highlights the urgency for innovative solutions.","Effective state leadership is essential to address the growing risks and ensure community resilience."],"keywords":["property insurance","insurance reform","risk management","disaster preparedness","natural hazards"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":22,"title":"Climate-Related Disasters Can Push Up the Cost of Debt","authors":"Sofia Anyfantaki, Marianna Blix Grimaldi, Carlos Madeira, Simona Malovana, Georgios Papadopoulos","date":"2026-02-19","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["European Central Bank (ECB)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/blog/date/2026/html/ecb.blog20260219~5954458037.en.html?utm_campaign=climate-risks-amp-government-borrowing-costs-eu-must-urgently-strengthen-adaptation-verisk-s-p-partnership-and-more&utm_medium=referral&utm_source=www.climateproof.news","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt discusses how climate-related disasters affect sovereign debt costs, particularly emphasizing that such disasters can lead to higher bond yields, especially in developing and highly indebted countries. It highlights the interplay between climate risks, fiscal policy, and debt sustainability, providing evidence that transition risks and physical climate risks influence sovereign bond valuations.","insights":["Climate change significantly impacts fiscal policy and debt sustainability.","Acute climate-related disasters can increase sovereign bond yields in the medium term.","High-emission countries tend to face higher yields, especially in emerging markets.","Fiscal capacity influences how climate shocks affect sovereign debt costs.","Investors are increasingly factoring in climate transition risks when pricing sovereign bonds.","Severe climate events lead to immediate and pronounced responses in borrowing costs, especially in developing countries.","The relationship between carbon emissions and bond yields has strengthened post-Paris Agreement.","Chronic climate risks do not directly impact sovereign yields but acute risks do.","Countries with high debt experience more persistent effects on yields from climate shocks."],"keywords":["fiscal impact","transition risk","sovereign bonds","developing economies","debt sustainability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":21,"title":"Glaciers to Farms (G2F) Regional Program","authors":"","date":"2026-02-18","year":2026,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Europe","Asia"],"institutions":["Columbia Climate School"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://news.climate.columbia.edu/2026/02/18/new-climate-finance-initiative-supports-climate-adaptation-efforts-in-glacier-dependent-regions/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Glaciers to Farms (G2F) program, recently funded with $250 million by the Green Climate Fund and supported by an additional $3.25 billion from the Asian Development Bank, aims to enhance climate adaptation in nine glacier-dependent countries. This initiative will address the systemic challenges posed by glacier melt, including water security and agricultural sustainability, through interconnected projects that strengthen local capacities and improve health and governance in vulnerable communities.","insights":["The G2F program targets nine developing countries heavily reliant on glacier-fed water systems.","It represents one of the largest funding mechanisms specifically for glacier-dependent communities.","The initiative includes 25 interconnected projects across major glacier-fed river basins.","Accelerated glacier melt poses serious risks to freshwater availability and increases natural hazards.","Over 340 million people in targeted regions face livelihood threats due to glacier melt.","The program emphasizes the importance of integrated social aspects of climate adaptation, including health infrastructure.","Adequate adaptation finance must be accompanied by ongoing mitigation investments.","The need for climate adaptation in high mountain regions is urgent as glacier melt rates increase.","Effective climate adaptation investments rely on robust, decision-relevant climate data.","The Asian Development Bank highlighted deficiencies in current climate investments in the region."],"keywords":["glacier melt","agriculture","water governance","vulnerable communities","climate data"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":528,"title":"Verisk and S&P Global Energy Collaborate to Deliver Insurance-Adjusted Climate Risk Intelligence","authors":"","date":"2026-02-17","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Verisk","S&P Global"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.verisk.com/company/newsroom/verisk-and-sp-global-energy-collaborate-to-deliver-insurance-adjusted-climate-risk-intelligence/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Verisk and S&P Global Energy announced a data-sharing collaboration to let financial and insurance institutions quantify the insured and uninsured financial impacts of future climate and near-term natural catastrophe events. Verisk's near-present catastrophe risk data is integrated into S&P Global Sustainable1's Climanomics physical climate risk platform, and S&P's climate-adjusted inland flood data is combined with Verisk event simulations to generate future-projected climate events modelled through 2050, delivered through Verisk's Touchstone catastrophe modelling platform. The stated target uses are insurance underwriting, bank portfolio stress testing and lending strategy, asset management portfolio analysis, and real estate.","insights":["The gap the collaboration claims to fill is the insured versus uninsured split: catastrophe models price insurable loss and climate risk platforms price physical exposure, and neither on its own tells an institution how much of a projected loss would actually be covered.","That gap is attributed to regulators rather than to clients: the release says the absence of insurance data was identified by regulatory authorities and emerging central bank stress tests, which is what makes this a supervisory-driven product rather than a commercial one.","Inland flood events are modelled forward to 2050 for the first time in this pairing, which is the concrete new artifact as distinct from the integration itself.","The intended movement is from measurement to action: the release frames banks and insurers moving beyond stress testing toward risk mitigation and lending strategies, and asset managers identifying and mitigating climate-driven risks across real estate holdings.","The word used for what this provides is 'auditable', repeatedly, which is the quality that matters for a disclosure or capital requirement and not for an internal estimate."],"keywords":["catastrophe modelling","insured versus uninsured loss","physical climate risk platform","central bank stress testing","inland flood projection"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":283,"title":"Climate Bonds Initiative Picks for 2026","authors":"","date":"2026-02-10","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Bonds Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatebonds.net/data-insights/publications/picks-2026","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Climate Bonds Initiative's 15th annual Global State of the Market report covers the full-year 2025 aligned GSS+ (green, social, sustainability, and sustainability-linked bond) debt market. Cumulative aligned volume reached USD6.8tn out of USD8.1tn total recorded, with USD1.0tn priced in 2025 alone, the third consecutive trillion-dollar year. A dedicated spotlight on adaptation and resilience (A&R) highlights the launch of the Climate Bonds Resilience Taxonomy (CBRT), the Tokyo Metropolitan Government's EUR300mn world-first certified resilience bond (7x oversubscribed), and AIIB's AUD500mn climate adaptation bond return issuance, signaling A&R's emergence as the next frontier in sustainable finance.","insights":["Aligned GSS+ cumulative volume reached USD6.8tn by end-2025 (83% of USD8.1tn total recorded), with USD1.0tn priced in 2025, the third consecutive year exceeding the trillion-dollar threshold, led by green (64%), sustainability (21%), and social (14%) labels.","The Tokyo Metropolitan Government issued the world's first Climate Bonds Certified resilience bond (EUR300mn, October 2025), attracting EUR2.2bn in demand (7x oversubscribed) and tightening 6bps from initial guidance, saving the issuer nearly EUR1m over the bond's lifetime, a direct proof point that resilience labeling commands investor premium.","AIIB's AUD500mn climate adaptation bond (January 2025) priced 7bps tighter than its 2023 debut (+51bps vs. +58bps), demonstrating that repeat resilience issuers can achieve improving price discovery and lower cost of capital over time.","Climate-related disaster economic losses average above USD330bn annually (UNDRR/Sendai Framework midterm review); weather-related disasters drove 92% of insurance losses in 2025; Cyclone Ditwah alone caused USD4.1bn in physical damage equivalent to ~4% of Sri Lanka's GDP, with ILO estimating a potential 16% GDP impact (USD16bn).","Developing countries require USD194\u2013366bn per year for adaptation (up to 18x current flows, per UNEP), while the USD100tn global bond market is identified as well-positioned to absorb rising resilience costs, framing the investable opportunity for capital allocators.","The Climate Bonds Resilience Taxonomy (CBRT), launched in 2025, identifies more than 1,400 potential investments across seven resilience themes (cities, infrastructure, health, agriculture, natural systems, industry, and others), providing the first science-based cross-sector framework for credible A&R investment classification."],"keywords":["GSS+ bond market","resilience taxonomy","adaptation bonds","sustainable debt","green bond issuance","Climate Bonds Resilience Taxonomy","sovereign green bonds"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":74,"title":"Resilience at Scale Means the Municipal Bond Market","authors":"Matt Posner","date":"2026-02-03","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["California Forward","Insurance for Good","The Resiliency Company"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://cafwd.org/news/resilience-at-scale-means-the-municipal-bond-market/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The blog emphasizes the crucial role of the municipal bond market in financing large-scale climate resilience efforts. It argues that resilience is a capital-intensive and public-good focus that cannot be adequately supported by private capital or sporadic federal programs alone. By strategically aligning municipal bond issuance with resilience goals, governments can foster long-term investment in infrastructure that benefits communities and economies while enhancing collaboration among various stakeholders.","insights":["The municipal bond market is essential for financing large-scale climate resilience as it provides low-cost, long-term financing for public goods.","Resilient infrastructure yields public benefits that are typically not captured by traditional private investment models.","Simply labeling bonds as 'green' or 'resilient' does not significantly impact borrowing costs or market dynamics.","A mere 10% alignment of annual municipal bond issuance with resilience could provide approximately $50 billion annually for adaptation projects.","Governments must adopt a holistic approach to resilience financing, integrating risk assessment and climate exposure into fiscal decision-making.","Existing public financial structures (e.g., state bond banks) should be realigned rather than creating new, parallel systems.","Resilience financing needs to be framed as a form of economic development, not merely cost avoidance.","Engaging diverse local economic actors can shift financing from isolated spending to collaborative solutions.","Recognition of climate risks in budgeting and operational strategies is critical for effective public finance."],"keywords":["municipal bonds","public finance","risk assessment","economic development","collaboration"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":596,"title":"The Resilience Nexus: How Europe\u2019s Security, Competitiveness and Climate Goals Are Interlinked","authors":"Systemiq; University of Oxford","date":"2026-02-02","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["Europe"],"institutions":["SystemIQ","University of Oxford"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.systemiq.earth/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/The-Resilience-Nexus-Research-Paper.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Resilience Nexus, a February 2026 research paper by Systemiq with Oxford University on how Europe's security, competitiveness and climate goals are interlinked. Its argument is that the three are not isolated challenges but dynamically linked across what it calls the resilience nexus, through compounding threats and joint opportunities: climate adaptation is key to European security, the energy, materials and food transitions are needed to meet competitiveness and security ambitions, and a competitive economy is needed to support industrial capacity and investment in both security and climate action. It sets that against rising geopolitical tension prompting European rearmament while the threat spectrum widens to include hybrid interference against cyber systems and critical infrastructure, with Ireland's undersea cables carrying a quarter of transatlantic data as a worked example.","insights":["Placing climate adaptation inside a security argument is a different route to the same capital: a defence budget and a resilience budget are the same money when the asset is critical infrastructure.","Hybrid interference against cyber systems and critical infrastructure is treated as commensurate with climate hazards rather than as a separate category, which is what an all-hazards resilience instrument would require.","The undersea cable example is a concentrated single-point exposure, where the loss distribution is driven by where the assets are rather than by how strong the threat is."],"keywords":["resilience nexus","European security","competitiveness","critical infrastructure","hybrid threats","undersea cables","compounding threats","climate adaptation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":595,"title":"The Resilience Agenda: Meeting Europe\u2019s Security, Climate, and Competitiveness Goals Together","authors":"Systemiq; Clingendael Institute; University of Oxford","date":"2026-02-02","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Europe"],"institutions":["SystemIQ","Clingendael Institute","University of Oxford"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.systemiq.earth/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/The-Resilience-Nexus-White-Paper.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Resilience Agenda, subtitled Meeting Europe's security, climate and competitiveness goals together, a February 2026 white paper by Systemiq with the Clingendael Institute and Oxford University. It is the argument and recommendation companion to the same project's research paper, and its central proposition is stated flatly: European security, climate action and economic competitiveness are complementary and reinforce each other, and cannot be traded off one against another. It opens on Europe standing at a crossroads with security firmly back on the agenda following Russia's invasion.","insights":["The white paper and the research paper are the two halves of one project and should be read as a pair, with this one carrying the agenda and the other the evidence. They were checked rather than assumed distinct: 0 of 40 sampled passages overlap in either direction.","The refusal to trade off the three goals is the political claim the paper makes: it argues about how to get resilience funded inside a rearmament budget cycle, and it is not a finding about returns.","**The file and the title disagree.** The document is distributed as `The-Resilience-Nexus-White-Paper.pdf` while its own title page reads `THE RESILIENCE AGENDA`, so the two names for this paper are both in circulation.","The Clingendael Institute appears on this paper and not on the research paper, which is consistent with the division of labour: a Dutch institute of international relations on the agenda, Oxford on the analysis."],"keywords":["resilience agenda","European security","competitiveness","climate action","rearmament","policy complementarity","Clingendael"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":370,"title":"Commissioner Lara and Assemblymember Calderon Announce Legislation Transforming the California FAIR Plan","authors":"","date":"2026-02-02","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["California","US"],"institutions":["National Association of Insurance Commissioners","California Department of Insurance"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.insurance.ca.gov/0400-news/0100-press-releases/2026/release005-2026.cfm","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Make It FAIR Act (AB 1680), announced by California Insurance Commissioner Ricardo Lara and Assemblymember Lisa Calderon, proposes a legislative overhaul of the California FAIR Plan, the state's insurer-of-last-resort, following a comprehensive Department of Insurance examination that found the FAIR Plan failed to comply with 17 of 32 critical recommendations. The legislation targets claims handling deficiencies exposed by the 2025 Los Angeles wildfires, mandates comprehensive homeowners coverage, governance transparency, climate risk reporting, and capital planning, while $22.4 billion in insurance claims have already been paid since January 2025.","insights":["$22.4 billion in insurance claims distributed since January 2025 following the 2025 Los Angeles wildfires, described as the fastest claim payment on record, alongside $6 billion in federal, state, local, and private donations committed.","The California Department of Insurance examination found the FAIR Plan had failed to comply with 17 of 32 critical recommendations across financial condition, corporate governance, and consumer protections, more than half of all areas reviewed.","The Make It FAIR Act would require the FAIR Plan to offer comprehensive homeowners coverage (including water damage and liability) in a single policy, addressing a structural gap that currently forces policyholders to purchase separate supplemental policies at additional cost.","The California FAIR Plan's governing board has actively resisted Commissioner Lara's reform efforts since 2019, including litigation to prevent expanded coverage mandates, signaling deep structural resistance that legislative action is now attempting to override.","The legislation mandates climate risk assessment and climate-related financial risk reporting aligned with NAIC standards, which more than 85% of national insurance markets already follow, a material step toward standardized physical risk disclosure in the residual market.","Formal capital and liquidity management planning is required under the bill, directly addressing solvency concerns for a plan exposed to catastrophic wildfire events, a key consideration for ratings agencies and reinsurance counterparties.","The Department has taken formal legal action against the FAIR Plan for illegally denying hundreds of smoke damage claims after the 2025 LA wildfires, illustrating how claims-handling failures in the residual market translate into systemic consumer harm and regulatory liability."],"keywords":["FAIR Plan reform","residual insurance market","wildfire claims handling","smoke damage denial","insurer of last resort","climate risk disclosure","insurance market withdrawal"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":75,"title":"Pricing Climate Risk: Hurricane Models and Home Insurance over the Last Two Decades","authors":"Judson Boomhower, Patricia Born, Benjamin Collier, Tobias Huber","date":"2026-02-02","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of California San Diego","Florida State University","University of Wisconsin-Madison","University of Georgia"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=6170366","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource analyzes the relationship between homeowners insurance premiums and the increasing risk of hurricanes in Florida over the past two decades, highlighting a significant increase in premiums in response to rising expected property losses. It emphasizes the impact of catastrophe models, market dynamics, and the challenges posed by limited insurer diversification and high reinsurance costs on affordability and pricing strategies in the insurance market.","insights":["Expected property losses from hurricanes in Florida increased by 50% from 2006 to 2023.","Homeowners insurance premiums grew by over 200% during the same period, indicating disproportionate increases compared to expected losses.","For every $1 increase in expected losses, hurricane premiums increased by approximately $5.82.","The rising premiums are influenced by a lack of diversification among local insurers and increasing reinsurance costs.","Changes in catastrophe modeling have not significantly affected the pricing of non-hurricane premiums.","Population growth and development in hurricane-prone areas are putting additional pressure on Florida's homeowners insurance market.","Florida domestic insurers charge more per dollar of expected loss compared to larger, diversified companies due to limited capacity to diversify risk.","The disparity in assessments of expected loss among different catastrophe models adds complexity to premium pricing.","Insurance premiums for homeowners in Florida increased by over 200% from 2006 to 2023."],"keywords":["homeowners insurance","hurricane risk","insurance premiums","catastrophe models","reinsurance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":372,"title":"Sustainable Insurance Strategy","authors":"","date":"2026-02-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Regulatory / Legislative Document","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["California","US"],"institutions":["National Association of Insurance Commissioners","California Department of Insurance"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.insurance.ca.gov/01-consumers/180-climate-change/sustainable-insurance-strategy.cfm","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The California Department of Insurance's Sustainable Insurance Strategy is a regulatory modernization initiative led by Commissioner Ricardo Lara to stabilize California's homeowners insurance market amid climate-driven risk and availability crises. Key measures include mandatory insurer coverage commitments in wildfire-distressed ZIP codes (85% of properties), introduction of catastrophe modeling in rate-making, FAIR Plan modernization, and reinsurance regulation updates. The Department reports $6.6 billion saved in consumer premiums through rate filing scrutiny (2019\u20132025) and 6 insurance groups now expanding in California under the strategy.","insights":["California's average homeowner insurance premium ($1,571) remains slightly above the national average ($1,512), suggesting that stringent regulatory oversight has not eliminated affordability pressure, even as it may have constrained outsized rate increases.","Six homeowners insurance groups are now expanding in California under the Sustainable Insurance Strategy, compared to zero in 2025, a measurable early market-stabilization signal that capital allocators can track as a recovery indicator.","The Department saved consumers $6.6 billion through rate filing scrutiny across property, commercial, and auto lines from 2019\u20132025, and separately refunded $3.3 billion in auto premiums during the pandemic, quantifying the financial stakes of regulatory intervention in insurance pricing.","The strategy mandates that insurers write policies covering at least 85% of properties in distressed areas (662 distressed ZIP codes as of the snapshot), creating a first-ever legal obligation for insurers to serve high-risk communities, a structural shift that changes the investability calculus for insurers operating in California.","The FAIR Plan, California's insurer of last resort, held 668,609 homeowner and commercial policies as of December; reducing reliance on the FAIR Plan is a central goal, with short-term increases treated as a market-stress indicator and long-term decreases as a recovery signal.","Introduction of catastrophe modeling in rate-making is a landmark regulatory change: for the first time, California insurers can use forward-looking risk models in rate filings, aligning premium pricing more closely with physical climate risk, a move with direct implications for how insurers and reinsurers price wildfire exposure."],"keywords":["wildfire insurance market","FAIR Plan","catastrophe modeling","rate-making regulation","insurance availability","distressed ZIP codes","reinsurance regulation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":215,"title":"Homeowners Insurance: Premiums Generally Tracked Inflation but Rose More in Disaster-Prone Areas","authors":"","date":"2026-02-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["U.S. Government Accountability Office (GAO)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-26-107867.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The GAO report on homeowners insurance reveals that while premiums have tracked inflation nationally, disaster-prone areas experience significantly higher increases, particularly in states like Florida, Louisiana, and Oklahoma. Availability and affordability of insurance in these regions are declining due to rising losses from natural disasters, prompting the implementation of mitigation strategies and regulatory reforms aimed at improving the insurance landscape. Homeowners are encouraged to adopt risk-reducing measures like FORTIFIED construction, and emerging alternatives such as parametric insurance are gaining attention.","insights":["Homeowners insurance premiums rose nationwide but more significantly in disaster-prone areas.","In disaster-prone regions, premiums increased by 25% or more from 2019 to 2024.","33 states are exploring grant programs to encourage homeowners to improve resilience against natural disasters.","FORTIFIED roofs are shown to reduce insurance losses compared to traditional roofs.","Regulatory approval times for premium increases vary significantly by state, impacting insurance availability.","Stakeholders support federal policies that incentivize risk mitigation through tax credits or deductions.","Insurance availability can improve with effective risk management and mitigation strategies.","The National Flood Insurance Program highlights the federal role in providing insurance in high-risk situations.","Insurers must demonstrate that their premium calculations are justified based on risk assessment."],"keywords":["insurance premiums","disaster risk","homeowner affordability","risk mitigation","regulatory approval"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":85,"title":"Let\u2019s Stop Pretending Capital Will Flow to Nature-Based Solutions on Its Own","authors":"Shaun O\u2019Rourke","date":"2026-01-29","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/blog/lets-stop-pretending-capital-will-flow-to-nature-based-solutions-on-its-own","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The article discusses the significant gap in funding for nature-based solutions, emphasizing that despite billions of dollars allocated for such projects, very little capital is flowing due to underdeveloped ideas and a lack of experienced project developers. The focus is on the necessity of bridging the gap between project developers and investors to facilitate funding and achieve substantial climate impact.","insights":["Billions earmarked for nature-based solutions remain unused due to underdeveloped projects.","Most nature-based projects are not investment-ready, stalling capital flow.","Investors are reluctant to share deal preferences, leading to blind pitches from project teams.","Successful projects often come from experienced project teams that can navigate complex funding structures.","There is a significant shortage of project developers qualified to advance climate initiatives.","Developing a project into an investable asset requires extensive time and resources.","Blending grants with commercial financing can enhance project viability and attract investment.","Project developers are crucial for connecting capital to viable nature-based solutions.","A systematic approach is needed to empower developers and effectively scale nature-based projects."],"keywords":["sustainable agriculture","investment readiness","project development","ecosystem services","collaborative financing"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":179,"title":"Climate Change Risk Index and Municipal Bond Disclosures of United States Drinking Water Utilities","authors":"Zia J. Lyle, Jeanne M. VanBriesen, Constantine Samaras","date":"2026-01-26","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Carnegie Mellon University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-03044-z","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The study analyzes the climate risk exposure of 1,455 municipal drinking water utilities in the U.S., revealing that 67 million customers are served by high-risk utilities, especially in the West and Northeast. It highlights significant gaps in climate risk awareness and disclosures in municipal bonds, emphasizing the financial implications of climate change on utilities and the need for targeted assistance and better governmental support for climate adaptation planning.","insights":["67 million customers are serviced by drinking water utilities classified as high climate risk.","36% of municipal bonds analyzed were issued by high-risk utilities without any mention of climate change.","65% of the 146 highest risk utilities did not mention climate change in their bond statements.","A total bond debt of approximately $39.3 billion is represented by analyzed utilities, with $9.2 billion from those at high climate risk.","The developed climate risk index can help identify utilities needing targeted assistance for climate resilience.","Governmental support is essential to assist high-priority utilities in preparing for climate change."],"keywords":["vulnerability assessment","operational resilience","financial implications","risk awareness","municipal bonds"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":356,"title":"C-PACE CRE Lending Is Suddenly Seeing Record Deals","authors":"","date":"2026-01-23","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Nuveen","CNBC"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/23/c-pace-cre-lending-record-deals.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This CNBC article reports on record growth in Commercial Property Assessed Clean Energy (C-PACE) financing, driven by institutional investor appetite for long-duration, fixed-rate, senior-lien instruments secured by tax assessments on commercial real estate. Nuveen closed a $465 million C-PACE deal for The Geneva office-to-residential conversion in Washington, D.C., the largest in history, and originated $2.1 billion across 53 deals in 2025. Peachtree closed a $176.5 million deal for the Rio Hotel & Casino in Las Vegas. Cumulative C-PACE investment reached nearly $10 billion through 2024, with 40 states now holding enabling policies.","insights":["Nuveen originated $2.1 billion in C-PACE loans across 53 deals in 2025 alone, implying an average deal size of ~$40 million, and has originated over $5 billion in total, establishing it as the dominant institutional C-PACE lender.","The single largest C-PACE deal in history closed in January 2026: a $465 million loan from Nuveen for The Geneva office-to-residential conversion in Washington, D.C., demonstrating the instrument's scalability for major urban adaptive reuse.","Cumulative U.S. C-PACE investment reached nearly $10 billion through end-2024, with double-digit annual growth over the past five years; active state programs expanded from 6 in 2015 to 32 across 40 states by 2026.","97% of Nuveen's C-PACE project pipeline combines energy efficiency and climate resiliency upgrades rather than pure renewable energy, signaling that cost reduction and physical risk mitigation, not decarbonization optics, are the primary commercial drivers.","C-PACE's senior tax-lien security structure makes it attractive to long-term institutional investors (insurance companies, pension funds) in a higher-for-longer rate environment where traditional CRE bank lending has contracted sharply; banks represent ~50% of CRE lending but have pulled back on new construction.","Peachtree's retroactive C-PACE structure for the Rio Hotel & Casino ($176.5M) illustrates the instrument's use as rescue capital, allowing owners to recapitalize recently completed projects and pay down senior bank debt, extending the product's utility beyond construction financing.","Peachtree aggregates and securitizes C-PACE loans sold to insurance companies, creating a capital markets pipeline that links building-level resilience upgrades to institutional fixed-income portfolios."],"keywords":["C-PACE financing","property assessed clean energy","commercial real estate lending","senior tax lien","office-to-residential conversion","green securitization","building retrofits"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":567,"title":"Supporting Investor Action on Physical Climate Risk: Adaptation and Resilience Priorities for 2026","authors":"Lucia Graham-Wood; Anne Chataigne","date":"2026-01-22","year":2026,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global","Europe"],"institutions":["Institutional Investors Group on Climate Change","S&P Global","Swiss Re","AXA","Mott MacDonald","Lloyds Banking Group","Global Association of Risk Professionals"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.iigcc.org/insights/adaptation-resilience-corporate-engagement-priorities-2026","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An IIGCC insight by Lucia Graham-Wood setting out how institutional investors are beginning to bring physical climate risk into corporate engagement, and what IIGCC will provide its members in 2026. It cites S&P Global's estimate that without adaptation climate change could cost companies up to USD 1.2 trillion annually by the 2050s. Investors are opening engagements in sectors with large fixed asset bases, utilities, mining, oil and gas and infrastructure, and the suggested first questions are a company's process for appraising physical risk, direct operational disruption against indirect supply chain effects, insurance implications including premium affordability and coverage availability, and what adaptation options are under way. Corporate disclosure is thin but emerging, with utilities and real estate furthest ahead, and some companies classify physical risk as immaterial without explanation. Asset-level vendor data is incomplete and inconsistent, and the insight points to a GARP report benchmarking 13 vendors. The work builds on IIGCC's Climate Resilience Investment Framework and the updated Physical Climate Risk Appraisal Methodology, PCRAM 2.0, whose fourth case study covers a last-mile logistics warehouse in Spain and was developed with AXA IM Alts, Mott MacDonald and Swiss Re.","insights":["Insurance appears here as a diligence question rather than as a product: premium affordability and coverage availability are things an investor asks a company about, which makes the insurance market a transmission channel into equity analysis rather than a separate sector.","The measurement problem is stated precisely and is the reason this is hard: unlike decarbonisation, which has a single metric in emissions, physical and nature-related risk cannot be reduced to one number, so there is nothing to benchmark a portfolio against.","Companies declaring physical risk immaterial without explanation is the specific disclosure failure investors are told to push on, and it is more tractable than asking for better modelling.","PCRAM 2.0 is the methodology described, and this document is a clean route to it: the publisher's own page for the methodology carries injected spam."],"keywords":["physical climate risk","corporate engagement","stewardship","PCRAM","Climate Resilience Investment Framework","asset-level data","premium affordability","double materiality"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":276,"title":"State of Finance for Nature 2026: Nature in the Red, Powering the Trillion Dollar Nature Transition Economy","authors":"UNEP, with ELD Initiative and Global Canopy","date":"2026-01-22","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Environment Programme"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.unep.org/resources/state-finance-nature-2026","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"UNEP's 2026 State of Finance for Nature finds that for every dollar invested in nature, $30 flow to nature-negative activities ($7.3T in 2023). Only $220B supported nature-based solutions, with private finance contributing just $23B. Introduces a Nature Transition X-Curve framework to phase out harmful subsidies and scale high-integrity NbS, calling for $571B/year by 2030.","insights":["The headline is a ratio rather than a gap: $7.3 trillion a year flows into nature-negative activities against $220 billion supporting nature-based solutions, roughly 30 to 1.","The public and private shares are inverted between the two sides: most of the $7.3 trillion nature-negative flow is private, at $4.9 trillion from utilities, industrials, energy and basic materials plus $2.4 trillion in harmful subsidies, while 90 percent of protective investment is public and only 10 percent private, with private NbS spending at $23 billion.","The stated requirement is a 2.5 times increase in NbS investment to $571 billion a year by 2030 to meet CBD, UNFCCC and UNCCD goals, paired with turning around the nature-negative flows, which the report treats as one transformation rather than two.","Reforming and repurposing existing capital flows, public and private, is named as the most powerful available tool, which puts subsidy reform ahead of new money in the sequence.","International public finance for NbS was 22 percent above 2022 and 55 percent above 2015, so the public side is growing while the private side remains the binding constraint."],"keywords":["UNEP","NbS","state of finance for nature","X-Curve","harmful subsidies","biodiversity","nature finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":53,"title":"When Actuaries See the Future: Climate Risk, Insurance, and Your Wallet","authors":"Bill Frist","date":"2026-01-21","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Florida","California"],"institutions":["Forbes"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/billfrist/2026/01/21/when-actuaries-see-the-future-climate-risk-insurance-and-your-wallet/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt discusses how actuaries view climate change not merely as an environmental issue but as a critical matter of risk management that influences insurance markets and financial products. It highlights the growing recognition of climate risk in insurance pricing, the impact on households, and the interconnected nature of these risks as they compound. The urgency of understanding and addressing climate risk is emphasized, promoting a shift from panic or denial to a more informed conversation about probabilities and management.","insights":["Actuaries are trained to assess long-term risks, translating future climate hazards into present-day financial implications.","Climate risks are actively being incorporated into insurance premiums and financial products, reflecting real economic ramifications.","The definition of risk differs from uncertainty; risk has measurable probabilities while uncertainty embodies unknown factors.","Natural disasters and climate events are increasingly interconnected, leading to cascading risks that complicate insurance modeling and pricing.","Insurance premiums are rising, making it financially unrealistic for many households to maintain coverage, leaving significant assets exposed.","Geographic and climatic changes, especially in states like Florida and California, reveal early signals of risk that are often overlooked by the general public.","The concept of climate as a threat multiplier highlights how it exacerbates existing vulnerabilities across various sectors.","Market responses to rising hazards often occur before individual recognition of risk, pointing to broader systemic issues in risk pricing.","Government-backed insurers are bearing a significantly higher risk as private insurers withdraw from high-risk markets.","A shift in perspective is needed to address climate change as an issue of financial risk management rather than solely an environmental crisis."],"keywords":["actuarial science","risk management","insurance pricing","economic implications","market dynamics"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":280,"title":"From Wildfire Risk to Resilience: The Investment Case for Action","authors":"World Economic Forum","date":"2026-01-17","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global","US","California"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum","PwC"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/publications/from-wildfire-risk-to-resilience-the-investment-case-for-action-2026/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"World Economic Forum report builds the investment case for proactive wildfire resilience, covering avoided-loss valuation, insurance dynamics, nature-based fuel management, and blended-finance instruments. Targets investors, insurers, and public agencies with a portfolio of intervention archetypes ready for capital deployment.","insights":["The spending asymmetry is the core finding: UNEP is cited that more than half of wildfire expenditure goes to response while planning receives around 0.2 percent of total wildfire budgets, so the money sits almost entirely downstream of the risk.","Three independent return estimates are assembled: roughly $210 in avoided future economic losses per $1 invested in wildfire-resistant construction in Los Angeles rebuilding, NIBS benefit-cost ratios of about 2:1 to 8:1 for measures such as WUI code compliance, and a US Chamber of Commerce estimate of more than $22 in lost future activity per $1 not invested in preparedness.","The paper's proposal is explicitly an instrument question: scaling financial instruments that let investors earn returns from avoided losses, which is the Benefit to Instrument link rather than another case for prevention.","The protection gap is quantified at around 56 percent of global wildfire losses uninsured between 2000 and 2023, attributed to insurers withdrawing from high-hazard regions over rising losses, modelling uncertainty and pricing constraints.","Four pillars are proposed to make prevention financeable, finance and insurance, tech and data and governance, nature-based solutions, and community coordination, with the stated requirement that prevention be measured, priced and financed over multiple years rather than annually."],"keywords":["wildfire","WEF","resilience investment","insurance","NbS","fuels management","avoided losses"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":615,"title":"Global Risk Pooling Mitigates Financial Risk From Drought in Hydropower-Dependent Countries","authors":"Rosa Isabella Cuppari; Tamlin M. Pavelsky; Gregory W. Characklis","date":"2026-01-16","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-67082-z","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article in Nature Communications by Rosa Isabella Cuppari, Tamlin M. Pavelsky and Gregory W. Characklis on pooling drought risk across hydropower-dependent countries. More than 50 countries rely on hydropower for over a quarter of their electricity generation, 56 by the paper's own count, and for some, hydropower sales are also an important source of government revenue, which makes them vulnerable to drought and the revenue losses that follow: the first half of 2023 alone saw an 8.5 per cent decline in global hydropower generation from drought, with China down 20 per cent. Governments can absorb the losses of publicly owned generators, but doing so creates fiscal pressure and can lead to lower bond ratings. Index-based financial instruments are an alternative, though data collection and index design are hard. Using remotely sensed hydrometeorological data the authors construct index insurance contracts to manage drought-related financial risk, and find that low correlations in drought across these countries allow cost reductions when the risks are pooled: pooling the contracts yields average savings of 54 per cent against managing the same risk individually through reserves.","insights":["**A 54 per cent saving against self-insurance through reserves is the number to carry.** It is a direct measure of what diversification is worth in adaptation finance, and it is large enough to change a finance ministry's choice between holding a reserve and buying cover.","Using remotely sensed data solves the constraint that kills index insurance in data-poor places: no ground station network is required, which is why the approach travels to the countries that need it most.","The causal chain the paper states is specific and priced: **drought cuts hydropower revenue, the government absorbs the loss, and the fiscal pressure lowers the bond rating**, with the pooled insurance interrupting it at the first step.","Low cross-country drought correlation is the whole basis of the result and also its fragility: a synchronised multi-basin drought would break the pool."],"keywords":["index insurance","risk pooling","hydropower","drought","remote sensing","sovereign bond rating","reserves","diversification savings"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":362,"title":"Uncertainty Surrounding Federal Disaster Funding Looms over State Budgets","authors":"Page Forrest, Jad Maayah","date":"2026-01-15","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","Pew Charitable Trusts","Urban Institute","National Association of Counties (NACo)","National League of Cities","The Pew Charitable Trusts"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.pew.org/en/research-and-analysis/articles/2026/01/15/uncertainty-surrounding-federal-disaster-funding-looms-over-state-budgets","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Pew Charitable Trusts article (January 2026) examines how growing uncertainty around federal disaster assistance, particularly potential restructuring of FEMA, is straining state budgets already under fiscal pressure. It documents specific funding disruptions in 2025 (cancellation of the Building Resilient Infrastructure and Communities program, halted Hazard Mitigation Grant Program allocations, delayed Emergency Management Performance Grants), state-level responses such as New Mexico's $30M contingency appropriation and new disaster reserve funds in Minnesota and Massachusetts, and two competing federal reform proposals: raising the public assistance threshold fourfold and shifting to an advance-funding model. An Urban Institute analysis found six states would have received zero federal post-disaster aid from 2008\u20132024 under the higher threshold scenario.","insights":["An Urban Institute study found that six states (Arizona, Maine, Michigan, Nevada, Ohio, and Pennsylvania), would have received no federal post-disaster public assistance between 2008 and 2024 if the Trump administration's proposed fourfold increase in the damage threshold were applied retroactively; Virginia would have lost $534M (88% of total funding received) and Iowa $497M ($155 per capita).","Nearly 73% of counties that experienced a presidentially declared disaster in the prior 10 years still had outstanding FEMA reimbursement claims more than two years old, totaling between $237M and $665M, forcing local governments to use bonds or short-term financing to bridge cash flow gaps.","New Mexico passed emergency legislation in 2025 allocating an additional $30M to its Appropriation Contingency Fund for rapid disaster response, citing slower and smaller FEMA reimbursements, illustrating how federal funding uncertainty is directly triggering state budget action.","The Building Resilient Infrastructure and Communities (BRIC) program was canceled in April 2025, placing stormwater infrastructure projects in Louisiana and New Jersey on hold pending legal resolution, demonstrating how federal mitigation grant disruptions cascade into specific capital project delays.","States have not received Hazard Mitigation Grant Program allocations since spring 2025 and FEMA halted Emergency Management Performance Grants in October 2025, compressing state and local capacity to fund both preparedness and risk-reduction projects simultaneously.","A proposed shift from reimbursement-based to advance-funding grants could accelerate disaster recovery cash flow for counties, but carries the risk of underestimating long-term recovery costs since initial damage assessments frequently miss full scope, creating a structural mismatch between speed and fiscal adequacy."],"keywords":["federal disaster funding","state fiscal stress","FEMA restructuring","disaster reserve funds","public assistance threshold","advance funding model","hazard mitigation grants"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":188,"title":"Tackling the Insurance Protection Gap: Leveraging Climate Mitigation and Nature to Increase Resilience","authors":"Matt McFarland","date":"2026-01-15","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Wildlife Fund","Crugnola Humbert Consulting"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://wwf.panda.org/discover/our_focus/climate_and_energy_practice/?14877466/Insurance-Protection-Gap-Policy-Brief","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource analyzes the widening insurance protection gap exacerbated by climate change and nature loss, emphasizing the need for integrated approaches that combine traditional insurance mechanisms with nature-based solutions to enhance resilience. It highlights critical barriers faced by the insurance industry, such as affordability, lack of awareness, and inadequate risk management, while advocating for collaborative efforts among stakeholders, including governments and the private sector, to address climate-related challenges effectively and improve coverage across communities.","insights":["The insurance protection gap is widening, particularly in developing economies, due to rising climate risks and inadequate insurance models.","Integrating nature-based solutions into insurance frameworks is essential for improving resilience and reducing vulnerability to climate impacts.","Public investment in ecosystem protection offers a cost-effective strategy for enhancing insurance coverage and economic stability.","Affordability issues and unpredictable climate risks hinder insurance uptake, especially in high-risk areas.","Governments are increasingly stepping in as insurers of last resort due to market inadequacies, requiring careful design to avoid maladaptive behaviors.","Health risks related to climate change are rising, with potential impacts on health insurance costs and socio-economic inequality.","Collaborative public-private partnerships are crucial for effectively managing the insurance protection gap and fostering a holistic approach to climate risk management."],"keywords":["ecosystem services","affordability","risk management","public-private partnerships","financial stability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":445,"title":"Climate Resilience Districts: A Powerful Tool Waiting for California","authors":"Ned Resnikoff","date":"2026-01-13","year":2026,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["California Forward"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://cafwd.org/news/climate-resilience-districts-a-powerful-tool-waiting-for-california/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"California Forward, working with Resilient Cities Catalyst, examines why the Climate Resilience District created by SB 852 in 2022 remains almost entirely unused, as groundwork for a Resilience District Incubator and a statewide call for pilot communities. A CRD is a special district that one or more local governments may form to address climate mitigation, adaptation or resilience within a defined project area, which can be a single neighbourhood or an entire region, and it can draw on a wide range of revenue-raising tools including tax increment financing, under which the district locks in today's property values and borrows against future growth. The article's argument is that California local governments face climate costs they cannot meet from a tax base constrained by Proposition 13's limits on property tax revenue, and that CRDs are the vehicle the legislature built for exactly that gap. To date only one CRD exists, the Sonoma County Regional Climate Protection Authority, and even that uses its district as a regional governance structure without having used any of the financing tools available to it.","insights":["The instrument exists in statute and is unused, which is a different problem from a missing instrument: one district in four years, and that one has not used any of its financing powers, so the binding constraint is adoption capacity rather than legal authority.","Proposition 13 is named as the reason the ordinary tax base cannot fund resilience in California, which makes tax increment financing through a special district the workaround rather than a preference.","Tax increment financing locks in present property values and borrows against future growth, so the district is betting that resilience investment raises the value it captures, which ties repayment to the benefit.","The project's own next step is a statewide call for pilot communities, so the document is a diagnosis written to be acted on rather than a retrospective."],"keywords":["climate resilience district","SB 852","tax increment financing","Proposition 13","special district","California","local revenue","Sonoma County"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":623,"title":"Governor Ron DeSantis Announces Major Insurance Rate Relief as Florida\u2019s Reforms Deliver Results","authors":"Florida Department of Financial Services","date":"2026-01-12","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Citizens Property Insurance Corporation","Florida Department of Financial Services","Florida Office of Insurance Regulation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://myfloridacfo.com/news/pressreleases/press-release-details/2026/01/12/icymi--governor-ron-desantis-announces-major-insurance-rate-relief-as-florida's-reforms-deliver-results","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A press release of 12 January 2026 from the Florida Chief Financial Officer's office reporting that Governor Ron DeSantis announced statewide insurance rate relief for Florida homeowners, with Citizens Property Insurance policyholders seeing premium reductions from Spring 2026 at renewal. The approved rates give the vast majority of Citizens policyholders statewide a decrease, with a statewide average reduction of 8.7 per cent: over 330,000 policyholders across all 67 counties see decreases and more than 150,000 receive reductions of 10 per cent or greater. South Florida, which had some of the highest litigation-driven insurance costs in the state, sees the largest reductions: approximately 27,000 homes in Broward County at an average 14.1 per cent, approximately 42,000 in Miami-Dade at 14.0 per cent, approximately 26,000 in Palm Beach at 11.9 per cent, and more than 1,000 homeowners in Monroe County at 11.3 per cent with over 8,000 wind-only policies reduced or held flat. The release attributes the reductions to comprehensive insurance and tort reforms, with statements to that effect from the Governor, Chief Financial Officer Blaise Ingoglia and Insurance Commissioner Mike Yaworsky.","insights":["**Premiums are reported FALLING by 14 per cent in Miami-Dade**, which runs against the direction of travel most Florida insurance reporting describes and is the figure the release is built around.","**The attribution is the state's, not a measurement.** The release credits tort and litigation reform and says nothing about hazard, exposure or physical risk reduction, so the causal claim is asserted by the parties announcing it and is untested in the document.","The county pattern is the analytically interesting part: the largest reductions land where litigation costs were highest, not where hazard is lowest. If that holds, a material share of what Florida homeowners were paying was legal-system cost rather than risk cost, which is a different problem from the one adaptation finance is built to solve.","Citizens is the state insurer of last resort, so its rate filing is a public-sector price rather than a market one. Its movements are policy decisions as much as actuarial ones, which is the caveat to attach to any use of these figures.","The wind-only policies in Monroe County are called out separately as reduced or held flat, which is worth noting: the most hazard-exposed coverage in the most exposed county is the one that did not fall."],"keywords":["Citizens Property Insurance","rate reduction","tort reform","premium affordability","wind-only policy","Florida insurance market","insurer of last resort"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":488,"title":"Despite Federal Backsliding, US States and Municipalities Are Still Planning for Climate Resilience","authors":"Manann Donoghoe","date":"2026-01-09","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Brookings Institution","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Georgetown Climate Center"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/despite-federal-backsliding-us-states-and-municipalities-are-still-planning-for-climate-resilience/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Manann Donoghoe of Brookings Metro maps the US federal climate resilience system and the sub-national planning that has grown despite it. Since 1980 there have been over 400 billion-dollar disasters costing $2.9 trillion, and in 2024 a major disaster was declared every four days. Unlike 87 per cent of countries globally, the US has no federal legal or policy structure coordinating nationwide resilience planning; instead at least 39 programmes across at least 10 agencies have accreted through the 1988 Stafford Act, the 1974 Housing and Community Development Act, WIFIA, the 1976 Energy Conservation and Production Act and others, with FEMA, EPA and NOAA managing most. From 2019 to 2023 FEMA spent an average $39.5 billion a year on disaster response and recovery, and GAO projects $26 to $134 billion a year by 2050; between FY2010 and FY2018, 88 per cent of FEMA grants targeting resilience were available only after a federal disaster declaration. The Disaster Recovery Reform Act, the IRA and the IIJA increased pre-disaster funding, but post-disaster funding still dwarfs it: BRIC requests rose every year to $5.7 billion in 2023 against roughly $1 billion of available funds. BRIC has also benefited wealthier communities, with 78 per cent of 2023 funding going to East and West Coast states, because the National Risk Index underweights vulnerability and overweights exposure, so communities with higher property values and thus higher expected losses are prioritised over communities where poverty and rental precarity drive vulnerability. Heatwave deaths, which are not eligible for federal disaster declarations, now exceed deaths from all other disasters combined. At the sub-national level, 272 entities have undertaken resilience or adaptation planning since 2005, publishing 347 plans, with 55 including major cities planning two or more times; thirteen states have appointed a chief resilience officer.","insights":["The distributional finding is the most consequential: the National Risk Index underweights vulnerability and overweights exposure, so a federal allocation tool routes mitigation money toward high property values, and 78 per cent of 2023 BRIC funding went to coastal states as a result. The scoring rule, not the appropriation, is what makes the programme regressive.","BRIC demand at $5.7 billion against roughly $1 billion available is a five-to-one oversubscription, which means the binding constraint is appropriation rather than pipeline, the opposite of the diagnosis in the urban resilience finance literature.","88 per cent of FEMA resilience grants between FY2010 and FY2018 required a disaster declaration first, which is the structural reason the US system pays for recovery: the money is legally attached to the event it was meant to prevent.","Heatwave deaths now exceed all other disaster deaths combined and heat is not eligible for a federal disaster declaration, so the largest and fastest-growing mortality risk sits entirely outside the funding architecture.","State-level mandates work: California and Massachusetts require municipal planning and produce over 24 per cent of all US plans between them, which is the clearest evidence in the report that a legal requirement rather than a grant is what generates plans.","Resilience planning is not partisan at the sub-national level, with Louisiana, Mississippi, Florida and South Carolina all carrying plans or a chief resiliency officer, and the pattern breaks only in interior red states facing drought and heat rather than hurricanes."],"keywords":["BRIC","National Risk Index","Stafford Act","chief resiliency officer","pre-disaster mitigation","100 Resilient Cities","resilience planning"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":73,"title":"Challenges and Opportunities in Scaling Climate-Resilient Housing Solutions in the United States","authors":"Nadia A. Seeteram, Linda Shi, Katharine J. Mach, Aliz\u00e9 Carr\u00e8re, Trinish Chatterjee, Anna Garner, Radley M. Horton","date":"2026-01-08","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Columbia University","Cornell University","University of Miami"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-68595-x","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource underscores the pressing need for scaling climate-resilient housing solutions in the United States, particularly in coastal communities, by analyzing insights from expert interviews. It highlights significant barriers such as policy inadequacies, financing limitations, and affordability issues, while stressing the necessity for government intervention and multi-sectoral collaboration to foster long-term resilience against climate risks.","insights":["Current risk reduction strategies are largely insufficient, focusing on small-scale actions.","Affordability and climate resilience are interconnected concerns in U.S. housing markets.","Government intervention and multi-stakeholder collaborations are crucial for fostering resilient housing.","Regulatory barriers and insurance behaviors hinder effective housing solutions.","Data accessibility related to climate risk is essential for informed decision-making.","Managed retreat programs present opportunities but raise social justice concerns."],"keywords":["housing","collaboration","affordability","policy reform","data accessibility"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":31,"title":"A New Financing Model for Frontline Solutions","authors":"Alix Lebec","date":"2026-01-07","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Fast Company","LEBEC Capital"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.fastcompany.com/91470147/a-new-financing-model-for-frontline-solutions","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The article describes an innovative blended-finance model that pools philanthropic grants, concessionary capital, and outcome-based payments to support under-resourced communities facing extreme weather risks. By structuring investments around pay-for-success contracts and rigorous impact measurement, the approach creates a revolving fund that recycles repaid capital into new resilience projects. This model aligns incentives among donors, service providers, and local stakeholders to scale community-led infrastructure and social initiatives while sharing risk and demonstrating tangible cost savings.","insights":["The argument is that this is an allocation problem rather than a capital shortage: US strategic philanthropy reached $557 billion in 2023 while climate resilience and decarbonisation solutions took under 2 percent of overall giving, and donor advised funds hold more than $326 billion of which only a fraction is deployed catalytically.","Philanthropy is positioned as first-loss capital and guarantee capital whose function is to de-risk an unproven investment thesis so that debt and equity investors can participate, which makes grant money an underwriting input rather than a substitute for investment.","Restricted grants are named as the mechanism that keeps organisations in survival mode, tying them to programme outputs on short cycles rather than to long-term outcomes.","Technical assistance grants are given the same de-risking role as first-loss capital, creating a pathway for investors into early-stage models.","A $50 trillion intergenerational wealth transfer over the next decade is offered as the reason to mainstream these tools now, with next-generation holders described as wanting philanthropy and investment integrated in one portfolio."],"keywords":["blended financing","pay-for-success","revolving fund","impact measurement","community-led projects"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":29,"title":"Cat Bonds on the Nile: A Risk-Sharing Approach to the Nile Waters Dispute","authors":"Junius O. Williams","date":"2026-01-06","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Africa"],"institutions":["OpinioJuris"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://opiniojuris.org/2026/01/06/cat-bonds-on-the-nile-a-risk-sharing-approach-to-the-nile-waters-dispute/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt discusses the introduction of the Grand Ethiopia Renaissance Dam (GERD) and the resulting disputes among Ethiopia, Sudan, and Egypt over water rights and environmental risks. It introduces the concept of catastrophe bonds (cat bonds) as a potential financial solution to address the concerns of downstream countries regarding environmental disasters while allowing Ethiopia to develop its resources. The proposal involves creating structured financial instruments that manage risk, although complexities arise in defining trigger events and addressing the impacts on the countries involved.","insights":["The GERD, completed by Ethiopia, is Africa's largest hydropower project but has led to conflicts with Sudan and Egypt over water resource management.","Ethiopia aims to increase electricity access with the dam, while Egypt and Sudan argue that it threatens their water resources and flood security.","International law principles regarding equitable resource sharing are invoked by all parties, highlighting the complexity of the dispute.","Catastrophe bonds could provide a financial mechanism to address risks associated with the dam, serving as a compromise between Ethiopia's development and downstream countries' protection.","The World Bank could facilitate this cat bond arrangement, helping to mitigate risks without adding debt to Ethiopia's balance sheet.","Defining trigger events for cat bond payouts presents challenges, as not all impacts can be easily quantified or attributed directly to the dam.","The proposal raises questions about the fairness of risk-sharing among countries with different capacities for negotiation and water management.","Complex humanitarian conditions in Sudan complicate its participation in negotiations regarding any financial arrangements.","There is potential for this cat bond approach to serve as a model for resolving other transboundary water disputes globally."],"keywords":["Nile River","catastrophe bonds","Grand Ethiopia Renaissance Dam","transboundary water","environmental management"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":427,"title":"Resilience Isn\u2019t About Bouncing Back. It's About Building Differently. [Episode 29]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Abby Ross (guest)","date":"2026-01-05","year":2026,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","The Resiliency Company","Probable Futures"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g2qNd3Wr-xA","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Abby Ross of The Resiliency Company describes an organisation whose stated goal is to mobilise as much capital as possible into resilient infrastructure, on the premise that infrastructure will be rebuilt anyway and is currently being rebuilt to the standard that already failed. She calls it a do tank rather than a think tank, working on the conditions for success: new financial vehicles, new networks and new commercial businesses. The concrete mechanism she describes is resilient home lending. After a disaster a homeowner must know the engineering solutions exist, sort through competing standards, assemble contractors who understand resilience, and then find the money, and the resilient rebuild costs roughly $25,000 more than rebuilding to code. A lower-rate loan, an eight percent conventional rate against seven and a half for a resilient home, is reported to be enough incentive to change the decision. She notes most banks have never done resilient home lending and many have never done construction lending. On the commercial side, partners representing about $2.5 trillion in market capitalisation co-authored a playbook, From Vulnerability to Value, aimed at the misalignment that the people holding the risk in what is built are not the people deciding what gets built. She notes that state and local governments are the largest asset owners and that municipal bonds have funded 80 percent of US infrastructure.","insights":["The lending mechanism is precisely specified and small: a roughly $25,000 gap between a code rebuild and a resilient rebuild, closed by a half-point rate reduction, is reported to be sufficient to change the homeowner's decision.","Post-disaster is named as both the moment of maximum openness to change and the cheapest moment to build resilience in, which makes the speed of assembling lenders and contractors the determining variable.","The commercial real estate misalignment is stated directly: the parties holding the risk in a finished building are not the parties deciding what gets built, so the intervention has to reach investors, pre-development, contractors, owners and tenants along the chain.","Municipal bonds having funded 80 percent of US infrastructure, with state and local governments as the largest asset owners, locates the largest available lever for resilience standards in public debt markets rather than in private capital.","Most banks have never made a resilient home loan and many have never made a construction loan, so the constraint on the product is lender capability rather than borrower demand."],"keywords":["resilient home lending","capital mobilization","commercial real estate","municipal bonds","resilience standards","post-disaster rebuilding","From Vulnerability to Value","adaptation finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":30,"title":"Physical Climate Risk Creates Challenges and Opportunities in US Municipal Finance","authors":"Aayushi Mishra, Adavit Arun, Evan Kodra, Erika Smull, Owen Woolcock, Tom Doe, Justin Marlowe, Auroop R. Ganguly","date":"2026-01-05","year":2026,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Northeastern University","Harvard University Graduate School of Design","University of Chicago Harris School of Public Policy","Municipal Market Analytics","Center for Public Enterprise"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s44284-025-00365-0","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Nature Cities review article examines how physical climate risks (flooding, wildfires, sea level rise, and extreme heat), interact with the US $4.2 trillion municipal bond market to create a \"climate-debt doom loop.\" The authors identify three reinforcing challenges: climate risk is systematically underpriced in bond markets (a 10% increase in SLR exposure yields only a 5.3 basis point rise in credit spreads); abrupt repricing could severely disadvantage fiscally fragile, high-risk municipalities; and municipal adaptation planning is structurally misaligned with municipal finance. The paper proposes innovations including standardized climate disclosures, a federal infrastructure finance authority, parcel taxes, resilience bonds, and state credit enhancement programs to disrupt the loop.","insights":["A 10% increase in sea level rise exposure corresponds to only a 5.3 basis point rise in municipal bond credit spreads, concentrated in longer-dated bonds, short-term bonds show no meaningful climate risk adjustment, indicating systemic underpricing across the $4.2 trillion municipal bond market.","Predominantly Black communities pay an estimated 0.44% more in annualized borrowing costs, a 'Black tax' totaling $110 million in 2019 alone, while a separate study found a racial penalty of 19 basis points totaling $900 million annually across the municipal bond market, linking structural racism to compounded climate-financial vulnerability.","The US infrastructure funding gap stands at $2.6 trillion, and adaptation projects require governments to cover roughly one-third of costs just to break even; a debt-only approach to adaptation can worsen short-term fiscal health and accelerate the climate-debt doom loop.","Insurer withdrawal from high-risk states (Florida, Louisiana, California) is shifting disaster cost burden to municipalities and state-backed last-resort plans, increasing reliance on debt markets that do not yet price climate risk adequately, a structural feedback that reinforces the doom loop.","Bond defaults due to natural disasters have been rare (only 12 in the past 15 years), but the Paradise Redevelopment Agency default post-2018 Camp Fire and Clyde, TX's 2023 default illustrate emerging precedents that could trigger abrupt investor repricing.","Virginia Beach's $567 million voter-backed flood bond and Miami's $400 million Forever Bond represent contrasting outcomes: the former used participatory design to ensure benefits outweigh debt service; the latter produced mixed equity results, illustrating how instrument design determines distributional impact."],"keywords":["municipal bond market","climate-debt doom loop","bond repricing","bluelining","credit rating agencies","fiscal equity","insurer withdrawal","physical risk pricing"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":626,"title":"Hurricane Loss Mitigation Program 2025 Annual Report","authors":"Florida Division of Emergency Management","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Florida Division of Emergency Management"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.floridadisaster.org/dem/mitigation/hurricane-loss-mitigation-program/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Hurricane Loss Mitigation Program 2025 Annual Report, prepared by the Florida Division of Emergency Management and dated 1 January 2026, under Governor Ron DeSantis and Executive Director Kevin Guthrie. It is the programme's statutory annual accounting of a recurring $10 million appropriation and its sub-allocations, which in this reporting year include $6.5 million, $7 million, $3 million and $2.8 million lines. Mitigation is the report's dominant vocabulary, appearing 86 times against 66 for hurricane and 8 for resilience.","insights":["**A recurring annual appropriation is a different instrument from a one-off programme.** A standing $10 million line means Florida funds hurricane loss mitigation as an operating commitment rather than as a response, which is what lets a delivery capability persist between storms.","The report is where the money behind Florida's mitigation grant programmes is enumerated, so it shows the budget constraint those programmes operate under rather than the mechanism they use.","The vocabulary throughout is mitigation rather than resilience, because Florida's statutory language predates the resilience framing; a search on resilience alone would miss the state's largest standing mitigation commitment.","An annual report is evidence of delivery rather than of design: it reports what was spent rather than what an instrument was intended to do."],"keywords":["Hurricane Loss Mitigation Program","annual appropriation","wind mitigation","state mitigation funding","Florida Division of Emergency Management","delivery reporting"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":566,"title":"Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) Case Studies","authors":"Alexander Tellides; Allison Shao; Claire McLean; Daniel Gunton; Pei Li Chua","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/wp-content/uploads/CRA-Case-studies.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The case studies volume accompanying Rebuild by Design's Community Reinvestment Act work, published January 2026 and running to 221,940 characters. Its inner title is Mobilizing Private Investments in Equitable Climate Infrastructure Under the New Community Reinvestment Act Guidelines, a final report prepared for Rebuild by Design by a capstone team of Alexander Tellides, Allison Shao, Claire McLean, Daniel Gunton and Pei Li Chua and dated March 2025. Where the bank guidebook argues the case to lenders, this volume carries the evidence: background, methodology and sectioned case material on mobilising private investment into equitable climate infrastructure under the CRA.","insights":["This and the bank guidebook are a deliberate pair from one publisher and should be read as one body of work, with the guidebook as the argument and this as the evidence. They were checked for duplication rather than assumed distinct: 0 of 40 sampled passages of the guidebook appear here.","The two documents carry different dates internally. The capstone report is dated March 2025 and the published volume January 2026, so the research predates the publication by most of a year and predates the guidebook's account of the 2024 rescission.","A capstone team rather than a staff research unit produced the underlying report, so the analysis was commissioned rather than institutional."],"keywords":["Community Reinvestment Act","case studies","private investment mobilization","equitable climate infrastructure","CRA guidelines","capstone report"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":565,"title":"Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) Bank Guidebook","authors":"Rebuild by Design","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design","Climate Central"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/wp-content/uploads/CRA-Bank-Guide.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A guidebook from Rebuild by Design, dated January 2026, on using the Community Reinvestment Act to finance community-wide resilience infrastructure in low and moderate income communities before extreme weather strikes rather than after it. It opens with the exposure: citing Climate Central, the US has experienced 426 extreme weather events with damages over $1 billion since 1980, costing over $3.1 trillion, and those events fall disproportionately on low and moderate income communities, particularly ones shaped by the legacy of redlining. The CRA was created to counter the economic effects of redlining and has long funded affordable housing, small business and community development, and in 2023 it was updated to call explicitly for investments in disaster preparedness and climate resilience. Those updates were rescinded in 2024, and the guidebook's position is that banks can still fund resilient infrastructure under the 1995 rules. Its central observation is that the CRA has not yet been used to fund large-scale community-wide infrastructure at all, which is why guidance on what is possible is the gap it sets out to fill. Community-wide infrastructure means neighbourhood to large-scale projects that reduce extreme weather and sea level rise risk while supporting everyday wellbeing, the worked example being a park that captures rainwater in a flood and serves as community space the rest of the time.","insights":["The argument is an alignment argument rather than an obligation one: lowering community risk lowers the bank's own risk, because the assets on the bank's books are the property, loans and mortgages inside the community being protected. CRA credit is then a second benefit rather than the motive.","The regulatory history matters more than the guidance. A 2023 amendment naming disaster preparedness and climate resilience was rescinded in 2024, so the route this guidebook describes runs through the 1995 rules and depends on an interpretation rather than on an explicit authorisation.","That the CRA has never funded large-scale community-wide infrastructure is the finding rather than the premise: a statute with decades of deployment in exactly the most exposed communities has no track record on the asset class.","The multi-benefit framing is what makes a project CRA-eligible and resilient at once, and it is also the hardest thing to underwrite: a park that is a stormwater asset in a flood has a benefit stream that only partly shows up in any single year's cash flow."],"keywords":["Community Reinvestment Act","CRA credit","community-wide infrastructure","low and moderate income","redlining","stormwater","bank exposure","multi-benefit infrastructure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":547,"title":"In the Hot Seat: Financing Asia's Heat Resilience","authors":"Ella Tan","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Asia","Global"],"institutions":["Milken Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://milkeninstitute.org/content-hub/research-and-reports/reports/hot-seat-financing-asias-heat-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A study of how to finance heat resilience in Asia, structured around the roadblocks to private financing, the business case for heat-resilience investment, and five routes for leveraging private capital: risk transfer products, project de-risking instruments, results-based incentives, capital markets, and market creation. It then sets out four enablers for scaling: policy and regulatory strengthening, development finance institutions and multilateral development banks as mobilisers of private capital, insurers as critical enablers, and data, technology and measurement. The framing is that global priorities are shifting from mitigation to adaptation while roughly 90 per cent of private climate fund investment has historically gone to mitigation.","insights":["Heat is treated as a HORIZONTAL threat rather than a sector hazard, cutting across health, labour productivity, infrastructure and agriculture, which is why the report argues it resists the project-by-project financing model used for flood defence.","The five capital routes are ordered by maturity rather than by size: risk transfer products first, then project de-risking, results-based incentives, capital markets and finally market creation, which is the honest sequence for an asset class that does not yet exist at scale.","Insurers are named as critical ENABLERS rather than as capital providers, which is the distinction that matters: their contribution is pricing and data that let other capital in, not their own balance sheets.","The report sets heat resilience against a long policy record rather than treating it as new: France's multi-ministry heat strategy after the 2003 heat wave and India's first heat action plan in 2013 are given as the precedents that private finance has not yet matched.","COP30 is used as the timing argument, with Beat the Heat, a health sector adaptation plan for Brazil and an Extreme Heat Risk Governance Framework all launched within four days, plus adaptation indicators expected in the Global Goal on Adaptation framework.","The commercial signal offered is small and concrete, from HVAC systems to cooling vests for drivers at the Singapore Grand Prix, which sets the current state of heat-resilience commercialisation against the financing ambition."],"keywords":["heat resilience","risk transfer","de-risking instruments","results-based incentives","market creation","Asia adaptation finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":546,"title":"Building Corporate Climate Resilience: Key Insights from the 2025 Corporate Climate Resilience Foresight Series","authors":"Verena Radulovic, Theo Bachrach","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Center for Climate and Energy Solutions (C2ES)","Resilience First","Zurich Insurance Group","Marsh"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://resiliencefirst.org/news/resilience-first-and-c2es-launch-building-corporate-climate-resilience-report/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A synthesis of the 2025 Corporate Climate Resilience Foresight Series, six dialogues convened by C2ES and Resilience First with forty senior leaders from 24 organisations across manufacturing, energy, technology and financial services. It presents a strategic framework for corporate climate resilience and finds that awareness of climate risk has grown substantially while translating it into sustained action has not, because companies face competing priorities, data limitations and governance structures not designed for long-term systemic threats. Leading organisations are framing resilience as a source of competitive advantage rather than a cost centre.","insights":["Resilience is framed as a systems problem rather than an organisational one: individual preparedness fails when supply chains, infrastructure and communities remain vulnerable, which is why a company cannot buy its way to resilience alone.","Governance is identified as the determinant rather than budget: organisations that embed climate into core decision processes, engineering standards and strategic planning achieve more comprehensive resilience than those that fund resilience projects separately.","Heat and water are reported as universal threats across all sectors and geographies represented, cascading into operations, workforce, finances and communities, which is a narrower and more actionable finding than a general hazard list.","The reported driver of internal resource allocation is external: customer requirements, investor expectations and regulatory frameworks, which means the lever on corporate resilience spending sits with counterparties and supervisors rather than with the company.","Early integration is claimed to cost significantly less than retrofitting or responding to disruption, which puts the corporate case on timing rather than on the size of the eventual loss.","The two host organisations are an insurer and a broker, Zurich Insurance for the community stakeholder dialogue and Marsh McLennan for the corporate governance dialogue, so the risk transfer industry convened the governance conversation."],"keywords":["corporate climate resilience","governance","systemic risk","extreme heat","water stress","adaptive capacity"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":541,"title":"United Nations Plan of Action on DRR for Resilience","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction","World Bank","United Nations Development Programme","World Meteorological Organization","Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.preventionweb.net/sendai-framework/united-nations-plan-action-drr-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 2025 progress report on the UN Plan of Action on Disaster Risk Reduction for Resilience, subtitled Pooling capacities and resources for prevention, published by UNDRR in Geneva. The Plan is supported by more than fifty UN funds, programmes, specialised agencies and related organisations, and 34 of them reported progress in 2025. The report carries a special focus on pooling capacities and resources for prevention, and functions as the UN system's own accountability record against the Sendai Framework rather than as an analysis of any financing instrument.","insights":["The Plan's coverage is the striking fact: more than fifty UN entities support it, from the IMF and World Bank to UPU and WIPO, which makes disaster risk reduction a system-wide obligation rather than a UNDRR programme.","Reporting is partial and the report says which entities reported: 34 organisations submitted progress in 2025 against the larger list of supporters, so the coverage boundary is visible rather than implied.","The special focus is on pooling capacities and resources for PREVENTION rather than response, which is an allocation claim about where disaster money should sit in the cycle.","It is published under CC BY-NC IGO with an explicit note that the work may be freely quoted with acknowledgement, which makes it a citable source without a licence question.","UNDRR names its core donors, Sweden, Norway, Japan, Switzerland and Finland among others, and states that the views are the authors' rather than the donors'."],"keywords":["Sendai Framework","disaster risk reduction","UN system coordination","prevention","progress reporting"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":537,"title":"Harnessing nature for resilience","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Marsh","World Economic Forum"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.marsh.com/en/risks/climate-change-sustainability/insights/harnessing-nature-resilience.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Marsh article on nature-based solutions as part of climate risk management, arguing they address a dual challenge: increasing resilience to physical climate risk while reversing the decline of the natural ecosystems that have historically been the first line of defence. It sets out layered green and gray protection, combining wetlands, marshes, dunes and urban green infrastructure with levees and flood walls, and then turns to financing: quantifying the protective value of nature in financial terms is named as the crucial step toward mainstreaming, and the nature finance landscape is surveyed across sustainability-linked bonds, direct investment in natural asset companies, environmental and biodiversity credits, and sustainable forestry and agriculture.","insights":["The measured result the article rests on is an insurance one: research along Florida's coastline found that the frequency of insurance claims approximately halved in areas of high coastal habitat protection, even after accounting for other flood risk factors.","That is the specific form the article says mainstreaming requires, quantifying the protective value of nature in FINANCIAL terms, because a claims-frequency effect is something an underwriter can price where an ecological benefit is not.","The UK's Biodiversity Net Gain legislation is given as the live example of a regulatory instrument creating a funding stream for natural flood risk management, and the article notes other regions are exploring similar frameworks.","The nature finance landscape is split by maturity rather than by type: sustainable forestry and agriculture are named as offering more immediate investable opportunities with attractive returns, while biodiversity credits and nature technology are said to require further development and policy support.","The WEF Global Risks Report 2026 is cited as ranking extreme weather events and biodiversity loss and ecosystem collapse as the top global risks over ten years, which is the framing that makes the two problems one.","Hybrid green and gray systems are described functionally: natural buffers reduce flood surges, dissipate wave energy and manage stormwater, complementing engineered defences, with co-benefits in biodiversity, urban heat relief and carbon sequestration."],"keywords":["nature-based solutions","green and gray infrastructure","Biodiversity Net Gain","natural asset companies","insurance claim frequency","nature finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":512,"title":"Climate Resilience Grant Program in New York","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["The Nature Conservancy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nature.org/en-us/about-us/where-we-work/united-states/new-york/stories-in-new-york/climate-resilience-grant-program/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Nature Conservancy's Climate Resilience Grant Program funds New York based community organisations for projects that conserve lands and waters delivering multiple climate benefits, including flood mitigation, migration corridors, carbon storage, water quality and recreation, and also funds climate-focused planning, strategy development and capacity building. The programme is grounded in the Resilient and Connected Network, a mapping of landscapes covering 33 per cent of the continental United States built by more than 100 Nature Conservancy scientists over a decade, and directs grants toward New York's role as the connector between the central and northern Appalachians. Alongside grants the programme protects natural floodplains and shorelines, including projected future floodplains, through land acquisitions and conservation easements.","insights":["In its first three years the programme funded 39 projects with 23 organisations, deploying roughly $1.2 million at an average grant of about $31,000 against a $50,000 cap, so grants are sized to early-stage capacity gaps rather than to capital projects.","The Resilient and Connected Network, mapped by more than 100 Nature Conservancy scientists over a decade, covers 33 per cent of the continental United States and is the prioritisation layer the grants are allocated against, which makes this a data-directed conservation capital programme rather than an open call.","The Orange County Land Trust used a grant to add 423 acres, creating about 3,000 acres of contiguous protected forest running from New Jersey to the Catskills, which is how a small grant leverages a land transfer of much larger scale.","The Genesee Valley Conservancy acquired 200 acres of wetland for eventual transfer to New York State, a structure in which private conservation capital temporarily bridges to public ownership and de-risks state land acquisition.","The programme funds conservation easements and acquisitions in PROJECTED FUTURE floodplains and shorelines rather than only current ones, which anticipates moving flood boundaries instead of locking in static regulatory maps.","It explicitly targets organisations with limited capacity, infrastructure and resources, positioning philanthropic grant capital as a first mover into gaps that larger public or private finance does not reach.","Flood mitigation, water quality, carbon storage, habitat and recreational access are co-delivered by the same parcels, which is the multi-benefit stack any future payment-for-ecosystem-services or green bond structure over this land would have to value."],"keywords":["conservation easement","climate resilience grants","habitat connectivity","floodplain protection","capacity-building grants","nature-based solutions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":496,"title":"\u02bb\u0100ina Restoration Through Community Governance to Advance Climate Resilience in the Hawaiian Islands","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA)","University of Hawaii"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://coast.noaa.gov/states/stories/hawaii.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A NOAA Climate Resilience Regional Challenge project summary for \u02bb\u0100ina Restoration through Community Governance to Advance Climate Resilience in the Hawaiian Islands, led by the University of Hawai\u02bbi Sea Grant College Program with an award of $68,497,799. The goal is a reinvigorated moku system, a Native Hawaiian framework for traditional land tenure and contemporary biocultural stewardship, delivered by a collaborative of fourteen state and county agencies, academia, national and local nonprofits and collectives of Indigenous and local stewards, with activities centred on five moku, one each on Kaua\u02bbi, O\u02bbahu, Moloka\u02bbi, Maui and Hawai\u02bbi. Six actions: conserving and restoring forests and watersheds by removing invasive species and planting native vegetation as green breaks and food forests to reduce wildfire risk and sediment loading onto coral reefs; enhancing regional community-based coastal stewardship through shoreline management and adaptation strategies, wetland restoration in two wildlife sanctuaries, transfer of 423 acres on Maui to the public domain for shoreline restoration and sea level rise adaptation pathways, managing tourism pressure on the Polol\u016b Trail, and an inventory of historic and cultural coastal sites; restoring marine abundance through loko i\u02bba fishponds, limu and coral reefs, including expanding a high school aquaculture programme to restock native mullet statewide; sharing knowledge across island-scale and statewide networks; reducing risk and improving disaster resilience through community-led wildfire management, a homeowner's hazard handbook and support for community resilience hubs; and advancing community governance models that address historical power imbalances and expand community-based monitoring.","insights":["The award is structured around a governance system rather than a hazard: restoring the moku framework is the intervention, and the flood, fire and reef work are what it delivers, which inverts the usual grant logic.","Land transfer of 423 acres into the public domain is a property-rights instrument inside a federal grant, and it is what makes the shoreline restoration and the sea level rise adaptation pathway on that land possible at all.","The wildfire chain is causally explicit, invasive removal and native planting reduce both fire risk and sediment loading onto reefs, so one action buys a terrestrial and a marine benefit, which is the multi-benefit case adaptation appraisal usually cannot count.","Advancing community governance to address historical power imbalances is named as a funded action rather than as a principle, which makes it a deliverable that can be assessed.","$68.5 million to a Sea Grant programme rather than to a state agency puts a university in the fiscal agent role for a fourteen-partner collaborative, which is a capacity choice as much as a funding one."],"keywords":["moku system","Climate Resilience Regional Challenge","Indigenous stewardship","loko i\u02bba","wildfire risk","coral reefs","community governance","Hawaii"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":495,"title":"Regional Adaptation for Climate Resilience of Monterey Bay Coastal Communities","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://coast.noaa.gov/states/stories/california.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A NOAA Climate Resilience Regional Challenge project summary for Regional Adaptation for Climate Resilience of Monterey Bay Coastal Communities, led by the California Marine Sanctuary Foundation with an award of $71,100,000. The project addresses the region's highest priority climate risks, flooding and wildfire, through four integrated strategies, each using nature-based approaches. Regional collaboration and capacity building centres on the new Monterey Bay Climate Adaptation Action Network as the hub for collective planning and coordination, with a focus on representation by tribes and disadvantaged communities and inclusive membership across universities, community-based organisations, nonprofits, research institutions and all levels of government. Flood risk reduction implements nature-based solutions that strengthen shorelines and restore and expand floodplains and salt marshes to accommodate flood waters that would otherwise damage homes and infrastructure. Wildfire risk reduction targets the large, high-intensity fires becoming more common, aiming to reduce risk to the more than 120,000 residents living in coastal watersheds classified as high or very high wildfire risk, with Indigenous knowledge central to the strategy. Workforce development trains young adults, college students and professionals through local and state colleges, universities and community-based nonprofits specialising in wetland restoration, with recruitment emphasised from disadvantaged communities. The programme behind it is the Climate Resilience Regional Challenge, funded by approximately $575 million that Congress appropriated through the Inflation Reduction Act in 2022 for competitive award to coastal resilience projects.","insights":["A $71.1 million single award out of a $575 million national programme means roughly one dollar in eight went to this one region, so the Challenge concentrated rather than spread its money.","Bundling flood and wildfire into one award is unusual and matters: these hazards are normally funded by different programmes, and combining them is what lets one regional network manage both.","Workforce development inside a capital award is the recognition that the constraint on deploying nature-based solutions at this scale is people who can design and build them, not money.","The award funds creating the Monterey Bay Climate Adaptation Action Network rather than assuming one exists, which is the same origination-capacity gap Glasgow and Marin County each paid to close.","The 120,000 residents in high or very high wildfire risk watersheds is the beneficiary count this award would be evaluated against, and it is stated up front."],"keywords":["Climate Resilience Regional Challenge","Monterey Bay","nature-based solutions","wildfire risk reduction","workforce development","Inflation Reduction Act","NOAA"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":457,"title":"NOAA Climate Resilience Regional Challenge","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://coast.noaa.gov/funding/resilience-challenge/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The NOAA Climate Resilience Regional Challenge programme page, describing a $573 million competitive grant investment administered by NOAA's Office for Coastal Management for collaborative projects that increase coastal communities' resilience to extreme weather. The programme names four components it treats as important: risk reduction, community engagement, regional coordination and enduring capacity. The page carries the programme's own reported outcomes to date, including over 300 community adaptation plans, more than 3,100 acres of habitat restored, 6,570 acres of land acquired for conservation, over 2,000 acres of nature-based infrastructure, more than $174.8 million invested in infrastructure with $79.4 million dedicated to resilience planning, over 56,000 individuals engaged, $2.85 million invested in education, $1.125 billion in economic activity against a reported 213 per cent return on investment, $24.66 million invested in workforce development, 6,006 jobs created, $1.61 million in research and monitoring and $72.6 million in habitat restoration. It links to the funded project map, a fellowship programme and grantee resources.","insights":["The programme reports a 213 per cent return on investment and $1.125 billion in economic activity against a $573 million appropriation, which is a self-reported figure from NOAA's own economic impact estimate and should be traced to that source before being cited.","Enduring capacity is named as one of four core components alongside risk reduction, which means the programme funds institutional durability rather than only projects.","The split between infrastructure investment at over $174.8 million and resilience planning at $79.4 million puts roughly a third of the programme into planning, which is unusually high for a federal infrastructure programme.","Land acquisition at 6,570 acres exceeds habitat restoration at 3,100 acres, so the programme is buying exposure out of harm's way as much as rebuilding ecosystems."],"keywords":["Climate Resilience Regional Challenge","NOAA","coastal resilience","competitive grants","nature-based infrastructure","enduring capacity","return on investment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":407,"title":"West Virginia Atlas of Disaster","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","West Virginia"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Department of Housing and Urban Development","U.S. Chamber of Commerce","First Street Foundation","AECOM","Milken Institute","Urban Institute","National Institute of Building Sciences"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/wp-content/uploads/Atlas-of-Disaster-West-Virginia.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The West Virginia Atlas of Disaster (January 2026), produced by Rebuild by Design, is a county-level risk and federal spending diagnostic for West Virginia's 1.8 million residents. It documents 23 federally declared disasters (2011\u20132024), $951M in FEMA and HUD post-disaster funding ($531 per capita, 9th highest nationally), and an ASCE infrastructure grade of D. All 55 counties face high flood risk; 28 have had five or more disasters. The report quantifies costs of inaction, maps social vulnerability hotspots, and recommends shifting to pre-disaster mitigation finance, including a small insurance surcharge projected to unlock $1.3B in resilient infrastructure investment.","insights":["West Virginia received $951M in FEMA and HUD post-disaster funding from 2011\u20132024 ($531 per capita), ranking 9th highest nationally in per capita disaster spending, signaling chronic underinvestment in pre-disaster mitigation relative to post-disaster outlays.","AECOM estimates the cost of inaction at $55B in the next decade for coastal storm and flood-event-related damages nationwide; the U.S. Chamber of Commerce documents a 13:1 benefit-cost ratio for hazard mitigation investment, and every $1 not invested can cost up to $33 in lost future economic activity.","A small insurance surcharge mechanism is estimated to support $1.3B in resilient infrastructure investment in West Virginia alone, illustrating how modest insurance-sector policy changes can unlock significant pre-disaster capital.","All 55 West Virginia counties face high flood risk; 46% of state roads, 57% of fire stations, 38% of schools, and 37% of commercial properties are projected at flood risk by 2050, creating a broad infrastructure liability with direct implications for municipal credit quality and insurance pricing.","McDowell County holds the highest Social Vulnerability Index score (0.87) in the state, and historically redlined neighborhoods nationally face 25% higher flood risk and temperatures 4.7\u00b0F hotter during heat waves, equity dimensions that affect where mitigation capital is most needed and least available.","Lincoln County leads West Virginia with 12 disaster declarations in 13 years, demonstrating that repetitive-loss geographies are identifiable and targetable for pre-disaster investment and risk transfer instruments.","The June 2016 West Virginia flash flood cost $1B, damaged 1,500+ roads and bridges, and killed 23 people, quantifying the single-event infrastructure liability that resilience investments are designed to avoid."],"keywords":["West Virginia flood risk","per capita disaster spending","pre-disaster mitigation","insurance surcharge","social vulnerability index","hazard mitigation benefit-cost","repetitive loss counties","infrastructure report card"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":402,"title":"Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) Community Guidebook","authors":"Joie Zhang, Pei Li Chua, Daniel Gunton, Claire McLean, Allison Shao, Alex Tellides","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design","Local Initiatives Support Corporation (LISC)","Federal Reserve Board","New York City Housing Authority","WBCSD","JPMorgan Chase","U.S. Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/wp-content/uploads/CRA-Community-Guide.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The CRA Community Guidebook, published by Rebuild by Design in January 2026, is a practical toolkit for community-based organizations (CBOs) in low- to moderate-income (LMI) neighborhoods seeking to leverage the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) to fund climate resilience infrastructure. It explains the CRA's regulatory framework, identifies qualifying multi-benefit projects (green infrastructure, resilience hubs, stormwater systems, coastal barriers), and provides step-by-step guidance for engaging banks, CDFIs, and regulators. The guide makes the financial case for banks, citing a 27% average ROI on adaptation investments and $13 in avoided damages per $1 spent on preparedness, and documents examples including a $100B U.S. Bancorp community benefits plan and $7M post-Harvey CDFI lending in Houston.","insights":["Every $1 spent on disaster preparedness saves $13 in damages, cleanup, and lost economic activity, a ratio the guide uses to make the business case for CRA-eligible climate infrastructure investments to banks.","The average ROI for climate adaptation investments is cited as 27%, framing resilience as one of the most cost-effective forms of risk management for bank loan portfolios.","From 1998\u20132018, 57% of disaster-impacted U.S. counties contain census tracts where banks may receive CRA credit, indicating substantial geographic overlap between climate exposure and CRA eligibility.","Between 2011 and 2024, extreme weather cost $117.9 billion in federal disaster relief, affected 80% of states, and struck 99.5% of congressional districts, establishing the scale of unaddressed climate risk in CRA-eligible communities.","Community advocacy via CRA public commenting has generated large commitments: U.S. Bancorp ($100B, 5-year plan), JPMorgan Chase ($30B racial equity commitment), Flagstar/NYCB ($28B community benefit agreement), and BBVA Compass ($10B plan including $2.1B in LMI mortgages).","CRA-eligible climate projects span three scales: small ($2K\u2013$25K/unit, e.g., weatherization, solar for LMI homes), medium ($250K\u2013$5M, e.g., resilience hubs, green schoolyards), and large ($10M\u2013$1B+, e.g., stormwater systems, coastal barriers), each with distinct bank/CDFI funding pathways.","Regulators (OCC, Federal Reserve, FDIC) have explicitly affirmed that both pre-event resilience plans and post-event disaster recovery activities in LMI areas qualify for CRA credit, creating a regulatory pathway for proactive climate investment.","CDFIs serve as critical intermediaries between banks and communities: one example shows ECLF and DC Green Bank co-funding solar installation on affordable housing generating 2,500+ MWh of renewable energy annually."],"keywords":["community reinvestment act","LMI communities","CDFI intermediaries","bank accountability","multi-benefit infrastructure","public commenting","disaster preparedness lending"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":380,"title":"Premium Discounts for Structures Built or Retrofitted According to Building Standards Established by the Insurance Institute for Business and Home Safety (IBHS)","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Regulatory / Legislative Document","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Louisiana"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","Citizens Property Insurance Corporation","Liberty Mutual","Chubb","Zurich Insurance Group","Swiss Re","Louisiana Department of Insurance"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://ldi.la.gov/docs/default-source/documents/propertycasualty/act-533-fortified-discount-report-2025.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This is the 2026 legislative report from the Louisiana Department of Insurance (LDI) implementing Act 533 of 2024, which mandates that all admitted insurers file actuarially justified premium discounts for homeowners who build or retrofit to the IBHS FORTIFIED\u00ae Roof standard. As of July 1, 2026, over 40 insurer groups have filed approved discounts ranging from 1% to 54%, applied primarily to the wind and hail portion of premiums. The report serves as the official public registry of approved FORTIFIED\u00ae Roof discounts under Louisiana law, directly linking mitigation investment to insurance cost reduction.","insights":["Over 40 distinct insurer groups have filed IBHS FORTIFIED\u00ae Roof discounts in Louisiana as of July 1, 2026, with approved discounts ranging from 1% (Farmers Property & Casualty condo/landlord) to 54% (FCCI/Monroe Guaranty/HDI Global/Universal Fire & Casualty in highest-risk zones), demonstrating a wide but real market for risk-differentiated pricing based on mitigation.","Most insurers apply discounts specifically to the wind and hail portion of the premium rather than total premium, meaning the effective percentage reduction on total premium may be lower than the stated discount, a critical nuance for cost-benefit analyses of FORTIFIED\u00ae Roof retrofits.","Zone-tiered discount structures (e.g., Louisiana Citizens Property Insurance Corporation: 7.2% Zone 3 to 24.3% Zone 1; State Farm: 19%\u201339% for homeowners) reveal that insurers price mitigation benefit proportionally to underlying hazard exposure, providing implicit actuarial validation that FORTIFIED\u00ae Roofs reduce wind/hurricane losses most in high-risk coastal areas.","The Liberty Mutual/Safeco group offers a 40% discount under an 'IBHS Home' designation, and Chubb's Executive Risk Indemnity offers 45.62%, among the highest in the registry, suggesting that for high-value or commercially underwritten policies, FORTIFIED\u00ae Roof certification can materially reduce insurance cost of ownership.","State Farm's discount range of 19%\u201339% for homeowners and 7%\u201320% for manufactured homes, combined with USAA's 19%\u201337% range, indicates that the two largest personal lines writers in the U.S. have filed actuarially justified discounts, lending market legitimacy to FORTIFIED\u00ae Roof as a standardized risk-reduction metric."],"keywords":["IBHS FORTIFIED Roof","insurance premium discount","wind hardening","Louisiana Fortify Homes Program","building standard certification","home retrofits","wind and hail pricing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":368,"title":"Texas Windstorm (TWIA) Sponsoring $450m Alamo Re 2026-1 Catastrophe Bond","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Texas"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","Artemis"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/news/texas-windstorm-twia-sponsoring-450m-alamo-re-2026-1-catastrophe-bond/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA), Texas's residual market property insurer, is sponsoring Alamo Re Ltd. Series 2026-1, a new catastrophe bond targeting at least $450 million in capital markets reinsurance against Texas named storms and severe thunderstorms. The issuance comprises three tranches (Class A $200M, Class B $200M, Class C $50M) on an indemnity trigger and annual aggregate basis, with Hannover Re fronting via retrocession. If completed alongside existing in-force cat bonds, TWIA's total cat bond risk transfer could approach or exceed its 1-in-50-year probable maximum loss funding requirement of ~$2.28 billion, potentially displacing traditional reinsurance sources entirely in 2026.","insights":["TWIA is targeting $450M in new cat bond capacity via Alamo Re 2026-1 across three tranches, with spread guidance ranging from 6\u20137% (Class A, lowest risk) to 11\u201312% (Class C, highest risk), illustrating the cost curve for layered catastrophe risk transfer in a residual market context.","Prior to this issuance, TWIA held $2.45B in outstanding cat bond risk transfer (Alamo Re and Bluebonnet Re combined); with ~$900M maturing in June 2026, the effective in-force position would drop to $1.55B, making the $450M deal necessary to approach its legislative 1-in-50-year PML funding requirement of ~$2.28B.","TWIA's 1-in-50-year PML mandate requires only ~$2.28B total risk transfer in 2026, meaning the $450M issuance slightly overshoots the net-new requirement of ~$330M, giving the board optionality to redeem older tranches or reduce traditional reinsurance purchases.","The deal structure uses a Bermuda-based special purpose insurer (Alamo Re Ltd.) with Hannover Re as the ceding reinsurer in a retrocession fronting arrangement, a repeating structure TWIA has used across multiple prior cat bond issuances, demonstrating the replicability of this SPI/fronting model for residual market insurers.","Class B and C tranches carry initial expected losses of 3.63% and 5.01% respectively, with attachment probabilities of 4.49% and 5.65%, these metrics quantify the pricing of mid- to lower-layer Texas windstorm risk for capital market investors.","TWIA has been a continuous cat bond sponsor since 2014, and if 2026 in-force coverage is maintained, the capital markets could fund the near-entirety of its reinsurance tower, a notable structural shift away from traditional reinsurance for a state-backed residual insurer."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond","residual market insurer","Texas windstorm","reinsurance tower","indemnity trigger","annual aggregate","special purpose insurer","Bermuda ILS"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":364,"title":"Catastrophe Bond and ILS Market Report, Q2 2026","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Verisk","Asian Development Bank","Swiss Re","Zurich Insurance Group","Aon","Chubb","The Nature Conservancy","The Everglades Foundation","Artemis"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/catastrophe-bond-ils-market-report-q2-2026.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Artemis Q2 2026 Catastrophe Bond & ILS Market Report documents a record $11.3 billion of cat bond issuance across 48 transactions in Q2 2026, surpassing the previous quarterly record by $842 million (8%). H1 2026 issuance reached nearly $18 billion, making 2026 the second-largest issuance year ever behind 2025's $25.6 billion record. The outstanding cat bond market reached a record $65.6 billion at end-June 2026. The report also features Verisk analysis showing U.S. severe thunderstorm insured losses rising from ~$4 billion annually (1998\u20132005) to over $40 billion (2021\u20132025), and a Twelve Securis essay on combining public and private ILS for portfolio efficiency.","insights":["Q2 2026 cat bond issuance hit a record $11.3 billion, 73% above the 10-year quarterly average, marking the fourth consecutive Q2 issuance record and the first quarter ever to exceed $11 billion.","H1 2026 issuance of ~$18 billion already exceeds full-year issuance for every year in the market's history except 2025 ($25.6 billion), signaling sustained structural growth in cat bond capital supply.","The outstanding cat bond market reached a record $65.6 billion at end-June 2026, up 7% from year-end 2025's then-record $61.3 billion, demonstrating compounding market growth driven by issuance outpacing maturities.","Average cat bond spreads tightened to 6.63% in Q2 2026 (from 7.23% in Q2 2025), and the average multiple fell to 2.29 (from 3.14), the lowest Q2 multiple since 2021, a direct indicator of reinsurance market softening and strong investor demand compressing risk pricing.","Nine new sponsors entered the cat bond market in Q2 2026, a quarterly record, including sovereigns (Tajikistan, Kyrgyz Republic via Asian Development Bank) and insurers from Germany (Gothaer) and Florida, broadening the geographic and counterparty diversity of the asset class.","U.S. severe thunderstorm average annual insured losses increased ~10x from ~$4 billion (1998\u20132005) to over $40 billion (2021\u20132025), driven by both exposure concentration growth (claims rising from 1.3M to 3.5M annually) and claim severity (residential reconstruction costs up 63.7% between 2014\u20132024), with direct implications for multi-peril cat bond structures re-introducing aggregate features.","Texas accounted for 95% of all Q1 2025 national CAT claims, with five ST events each exceeding $1 billion in Texas in 2024 alone, a concentration risk relevant to sponsors and investors in US multi-peril and Texas-specific cat bond tranches."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond issuance","insurance-linked securities","ILS market growth","severe thunderstorm losses","reinsurance market softening","parametric trigger","cat bond spreads","risk transfer"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":358,"title":"Statewide Resilience Plan, Fiscal Year 2026-27","authors":"","date":"2026-01-01","year":2026,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["Florida","US"],"institutions":["Village of Key Biscayne","Miami-Dade County Office of Resilience","Florida Department of Environmental Protection"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://floridadep.gov/sites/default/files/ORCP_RF_26-27%20State%20Plan%20PFT.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Florida's Statewide Resilience Plan (SRP) for Fiscal Year 2026-27 allocates $160 million in grants across 23 new projects and three supplemental awards to municipalities and water utilities throughout the state. Projects target stormwater infrastructure, wastewater treatment plant hardening, lift station rehabilitation and relocation, and pump station upgrades in coastal and flood-prone communities including Miami-Dade County, the Florida Keys, Fort Myers, Vero Beach, and Broward County. The program addresses compounding flood and sea level rise risks to water and wastewater systems.","insights":["The plan is an award schedule rather than a strategy document: it lists named grantees, project names and dollar awards, so it is a record of where state resilience money actually went.","Water and wastewater utility resilience dominates the funded list, with treatment plant flood mitigation, electrical system upgrades, lift stations and pump stations recurring across Fort Myers, Miami-Dade, Clearwater, Key Largo, Cooper City, Palm Beach County and Marathon.","Several of the largest line items are additional funding for awards made in a previous cycle, including $21.4 million and $5.8 million to the South Florida Water Management District and $7.2 million to Fort Myers, so the plan is partly topping up projects that were previously underfunded.","Every award carries the caveat that it remains subject to further review for activity and expense eligibility, so the published figures are commitments rather than disbursements."],"keywords":["stormwater infrastructure","wastewater resilience","utility hardening","pump station","lift station rehabilitation","Florida Keys","coastal flood infrastructure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":426,"title":"Come Dream, Come Build Affordable Homes [Episode 28]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Nick Mitchell-Bennett (guest)","date":"2025-12-29","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US","Texas"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Wells Fargo","Urban Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AwGWAmBSdZM","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Nick Mitchell-Bennett runs a community development organisation in Brownsville, Texas, in a persistent-poverty region where more than 20 percent of the population has lived below the federal poverty line for at least 30 years. He describes three linked innovations: a small-dollar loan product built as an alternative to payday lending, which has made over 160,000 loans totalling about $158 million at roughly $900 each and saved borrowers an estimated $160 million in fees and interest against a Texas payday loan; a modular housing programme begun after winning the Wells Fargo Affordable Housing Breakthrough Challenge in December 2020, now roughly doubling output each year toward 36 units, with buyers choosing among 29 design options online while the factory builds standard boxes; and homebuyer readiness counselling that takes six to twelve months. His most pointed argument is about insurance. As carriers leave or price out, buyers and rental owners are pushed toward non-admitted carriers with less regulation, while Texas insurer capitalisation rules have not been updated in 25 years. He is confident a company can pay a single apartment fire and doubtful it could pay after a category five hurricane destroyed a whole portfolio, which means the certificate of insurance held by tax credit investors and lenders may be worth less than it appears.","insights":["The insurance warning is a capital adequacy argument with a direct effect on affordable housing finance: tax credit investors and lenders accept a certificate of insurance as sufficient, while the carrier behind it may be non-admitted and capitalised under rules 25 years old, so the protection standing behind the investment may not survive a correlated loss.","Market exit pushes borrowers into what he calls the payday lending market of insurance, the lightly regulated non-admitted segment, which mirrors the credit market dynamic his own loan product was built to counter.","The small-dollar loan result is measurable at scale: over 160,000 loans averaging about $900, roughly $158 million lent, and about $160 million saved against the payday alternative, so avoided cost roughly equals principal advanced.","Modular construction is used to remove cost without removing choice, with 29 buyer-selected options presented against a standardised factory product, on the argument that limited choice is not what drives cost down.","Philanthropic capital is distributed very unevenly by place, cited as roughly $950 per head in New York City against about $21 to $25 in the Rio Grande Valley and about two dollars on Indian reservations, which constrains which communities can pursue catalytic capital at all."],"keywords":["payday lending alternative","small-dollar loans","modular housing","non-admitted carriers","insurer capitalization","affordable housing","tax credit investors","persistent poverty","Rio Grande Valley","philanthropic capital"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":266,"title":"The Spatially Variable Effects of Mangroves on Flood Depths and Losses from Storm Surges in Florida","authors":"Siddharth Narayan, Christopher J. Thomas, Kechi Nzerem, Joss Matthewman, Christine Shepard, Laura Geselbracht, Michael W. Beck","date":"2025-12-26","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Cell Reports Sustainability","The Nature Conservancy","Moody's","Reask","Center for Coastal Climate Resilience at UC Santa Cruz","East Carolina University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crsus.2025.100531","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed study (Cell Reports Sustainability, 2025) uses a catastrophe-industry-standard, kilometer-scale flood and loss model to quantify the spatially variable economic benefits of mangrove forests in reducing storm surge property damages in Florida. Mangroves reduce average annual storm surge losses by $67 million in Collier County (~$270,000/km\u00b2/year), with 73% of benefits accruing for storms with return periods under 50 years. During Hurricane Irma (2017) mangroves avoided ~$725 million in damages (14%); during Hurricane Ian (2022), ~$4.1 billion (30%). The study provides critical evidence for treating mangroves as quantifiable natural infrastructure in insurance pricing and coastal risk management.","insights":["Mangroves reduce average annual storm surge losses in Collier County, Florida by $67 million, equivalent to ~$270,000 per km\u00b2 per year, an order of magnitude higher than a prior county-level estimate of $38,000/km\u00b2/year from Sun and Carson.","73% of annual mangrove flood-loss reduction benefits accrue for storms with return periods under 50 years, and over 55% for return periods under 30 years, within a typical US home mortgage lifetime, making these benefits financially tangible to lenders and homeowners.","During Hurricane Irma (2017), mangroves avoided ~$725 million in storm surge property damages (14% reduction); during Hurricane Ian (2022), ~$4.1 billion (30% reduction), across a smaller spatial footprint of roughly 250 km\u00b2 of mangrove forest in Collier County.","Mangroves always reduce flood losses for properties landward of the mangrove edge (100% of landward hexagons show net benefit in all three event cases), but can increase damages for properties inside or seaward of mangrove forests due to surge impoundment, a critical spatial nuance for underwriters and planners.","The study uses an insurance-industry catastrophe risk model (Moody's RMS), making findings directly legible to (re)insurers, risk pricing analysts, and bond issuers: the methodology supports integration of mangrove value into premium structures, cat bond design, and resilience investment incentives.","Over $4 billion in avoided storm surge damages during the 2017 and 2023 hurricane seasons in south Florida was attributable to mangroves, yet less than 3% of post-Sandy federal recovery funding went to green infrastructure, indicating a major allocation gap."],"keywords":["mangrove flood protection","storm surge loss modeling","catastrophe risk model","natural infrastructure valuation","coastal property damage","average annual loss"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":425,"title":"The Living Laboratory of Norfolk [Episode 27]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Kyle Spencer (guest)","date":"2025-12-22","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","RISE","United States Army Corps of Engineers","Federal Emergency Management Agency","United States Department of Housing and Urban Development"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qQGNIPYYRao","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Kyle Spencer, who leads resilience for the City of Norfolk, describes a programme that has grown from one or two people into a team of about ten funded from the general fund, and a coastal flood protection project being delivered with the Army Corps of Engineers. The city is roughly 95 percent built out, so the design uses flood walls, a hybrid flood wall and levee, pump stations and storm surge gates of the kind built in New Orleans after Katrina. His argument for acting before a storm is a return figure: recent studies put roughly $13 back for every dollar spent on resilience investment, and he contrasts this with the Army Corps pattern of completing a feasibility study, shelving it, and implementing only once a storm releases the money. Norfolk's living laboratory with RISE, originating in the HUD response to Hurricane Sandy and topped up by the state, lets the city buy proven products, and the competitive challenge process has been used to justify sole-source procurement. On insurance, the city has about 80 percent flood insurance uptake among those in the floodplain against a typical 30 to 50 percent, and wants to keep it after the flood defences are built, since a Harvey-scale rainfall would overwhelm any stormwater system; the city is exploring a community-based approach to covering deductibles so that higher deductibles can keep policies affordable without leaving households exposed.","insights":["The insurance design problem is stated precisely: once large flood control systems are built, households have an incentive to drop flood cover, but residual risk remains, so the city wants to preserve its unusually high 80 percent uptake rather than let protection substitute for insurance.","A community-based mechanism to pay deductibles is floated as the way to keep premiums affordable through higher deductibles without leaving households exposed, which places a collective instrument between the household and the insurer.","Acting before the storm is argued from a roughly $13 return per dollar of resilience investment, set against the Army Corps pattern of shelving a feasibility study until a disaster releases the funding.","The non-federal match can come from state or any non-federal source, which is what determines the pace of a large Corps project and makes state appropriation the binding constraint.","The Defense Community Infrastructure Program is used as a funding source outside the fence line, tying military mission readiness to municipal resilience spending on roads and stormwater backflow prevention."],"keywords":["Norfolk","storm surge barrier","Army Corps of Engineers","flood insurance uptake","deductible","resilience return on investment","Dutch Dialogues","municipal resilience","living laboratory"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":55,"title":"Where to Restore and Conserve? A Regional Benefit Cost Analysis of Coral Reef Protection and Restoration for Coastal Flood Resilience","authors":"Borja G. Reguero, Camila Gaido, Curt D. Storlazzi, Valerie Pietsch McNulty, Denise I. Perez, Michael W. Beck","date":"2025-12-22","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Caribbean","U.S. Virgin Islands"],"institutions":["University of California Santa Cruz","U.S. Geological Survey","The Nature Conservancy"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2025.128166","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed study provides one of the first regional-scale, spatially explicit benefit-cost analyses (BCA) of coral reef conservation and restoration for coastal flood risk reduction in the U.S. Virgin Islands (Saint Croix, Saint John, Saint Thomas), designed to meet FEMA Hazard Mitigation Grant Program requirements. Using hydrodynamic modeling (XBeach, SWAN) and FEMA HAZUS damage functions, the study finds that current reefs prevent $43.6 million USD in annual flood damages and protect 481 people. Ecological restoration (ER) and hybrid restoration (HR) are both cost-effective, with up to 10.9 km of coastline meeting a BCR threshold of 1.5, opening pathways to federal hazard mitigation funding for nature-based solutions.","insights":["Current coral reefs in the U.S. Virgin Islands prevent $43.6 million USD in annual flood damages and protect 481 people per year, representing 87% of the region's $51.4 million USD annual flood risk.","Reef degradation (1 m elevation loss) would nearly double annual flood damages across reef-fronted areas, increasing expected annual damages from $45.4M to $88.7M, a 95% increase, demonstrating the asset value of existing natural infrastructure.","Ecological restoration (ER) achieves BCR thresholds exceeding 1.5 along 10.9 km of coastline and BCR >3 along 6.9 km, despite providing less absolute flood mitigation than hybrid restoration (HR), because ER costs ($1,500/linear meter) are roughly half those of HR ($3,057/linear meter).","Hybrid (gray-green) restoration delivers greater absolute flood risk reduction but lower cost-effectiveness than ecological restoration, illustrating the tradeoff between maximizing total protection and maximizing return on investment, HR is best suited for highest-risk shoreline segments.","Conservation and restoration priority areas are strongly correlated (Pearson r = 0.86), meaning areas with the greatest existing reef protection value also tend to be the best candidates for restoration investment, simplifying spatial prioritization for allocators.","The study was funded under FEMA's Hazard Mitigation Grant Program and is explicitly designed to meet federal BCA requirements, marking a replicable template for unlocking pre- and post-disaster mitigation grants for nature-based solutions globally.","In June 2023, FEMA awarded $38.6 million to the first-ever coral reef restoration hazard mitigation project in Puerto Rico, establishing policy precedent that complements this analytical framework and signals growing federal investability of reef-based solutions."],"keywords":["coral reef restoration","benefit-cost analysis","FEMA hazard mitigation","nature-based coastal protection","hybrid reef restoration","expected annual damage","U.S. Virgin Islands"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":524,"title":"Handbook for Livable and Resilient Cities: Chapter 4 Instruments, Enforcement, Monitoring, and Evaluation","authors":"","date":"2025-12-18","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery","World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.gfdrr.org/en/publication/handbook-livable-and-resilient-cities","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Chapter 4 of the GFDRR Handbook for Livable and Resilient Cities, on the instruments cities use to implement risk-informed urban planning and on enforcing, monitoring and evaluating them. It covers regulatory and incentive instruments, zoning and land-use controls, building codes, financial and land management tools, and capacity building and public engagement, and treats the central challenge as coordinating disaster risk management and climate risk management components with the instruments that execute them. Worked examples include graduated planning controls in Australia's Hawkesbury-Nepean Valley and land value capture funding flood resilience in Barranquilla, Colombia.","insights":["Land value capture is presented as an accessible starting instrument for cities with limited capacity, with betterment levies and capital gains tax named as the most popular forms, and Barranquilla in Colombia given as a case where a betterment levy funded resilience and road infrastructure.","Zoning is framed as the instrument that prevents the CREATION of new risk rather than reducing existing risk, by defining where urbanisation and critical infrastructure should be avoided, which separates it cleanly from retrofit instruments.","Building codes are described as translating hazard and risk information into technical requirements for materials, design loads and construction practice, and the chapter argues most current codes still address gravity, earthquake and wind while needing to evolve for floods, wildfires and extreme temperatures.","A city without hazard and risk assessments is told to treat that as a short-term priority, because every downstream instrument in the chapter depends on the assessment to define compatible land uses and development standards.","Plans are required to be flexible enough to absorb new hazard information by updating zoning, land use, performance standards and building regulations, which makes the instrument set a cycle rather than a one-time adoption.","Australia's Hawkesbury-Nepean Valley is used to show graduated planning controls in a hazard-prone area, with land use, construction type, density and setbacks all varying by vulnerability level rather than by a single flood line."],"keywords":["land value capture","betterment levy","risk-informed urban planning","building codes","zoning and setbacks"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":138,"title":"Public Development Banks\u2019 Climate Commitments 2025: Trends and Progress of PDB Climate Ambition","authors":"Neil Chin, Shreya Bansal, Arjun Rawal","date":"2025-12-18","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/public-development-banks-climate-commitments-2025-trends-and-progress-of-pdb-climate-ambition/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report 'Public Development Banks\u2019 Climate Commitments 2025' assesses the role of Public Development Banks (PDBs) in climate finance, identifying both progress and significant gaps in climate ambition across different institutions. It highlights the importance of capacity building and regional collaboration to enhance PDBs' effectiveness in meeting climate goals by 2025, emphasizing the need for a structured, long-term approach to support less ambitious banks in improving their climate commitments.","insights":["Public Development Banks (PDBs) are essential to achieving climate and development goals.","The share of PDBs aligning with the Paris Agreement has increased from 28% in 2020 to 45% by 2025.","High ambition PDBs control a considerable amount of assets totaling USD 9.3 trillion, significantly influencing climate finance distribution.","Capacity building initiatives are often short-term and fragmented, lacking the necessary support for institutional transformation.","There is a critical need for enhanced support for PDBs with limited climate commitments to mobilize broader climate finance participation.","Collaborative efforts in countries like Brazil are vital for creating enabling conditions that elevate PDB climate ambition.","Machine learning and AI tools are being utilized for analyzing and categorizing climate commitments to improve data accuracy.","Peer-to-peer learning and knowledge sharing are essential for sustaining institutional capacity after initial support."],"keywords":["Public Development Banks","capacity building","collaboration","technical assistance","ambition clusters"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":297,"title":"The Growth Story Behind Insurance-Linked Securities","authors":"Mariagiovanna Guatteri","date":"2025-12-16","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["CFA Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rpc.cfainstitute.org/blogs/enterprising-investor/2025/the-growth-story-behind-insurance-linked-securities","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This CFA Institute blog post by Mariagiovanna Guatteri surveys the growth and maturation of the insurance-linked securities (ILS) market as of mid-2025. The global catastrophe bond market has surpassed $56 billion in outstanding size, with 2025 on pace to be the largest issuance year on record at $17.2 billion through mid-year. The piece covers performance (Swiss Re Global Cat Bond Index returned 9.89% in the first ten months of 2025), risk events including the Los Angeles wildfires ($40 billion in insured losses) and a $150 million World Bank catastrophe bond payout for Jamaica after Hurricane Melissa, and the expanding role of parametric and index-based structures in institutional portfolios.","insights":["The catastrophe bond market surpassed $56 billion in outstanding notional size in 2025, exceeding prior records, with $17.2 billion in new issuance in the first half of the year alone, more than 75% of which included wildfire bond coverage.","The Swiss Re Global Cat Bond Index returned 9.89% in the first ten months of 2025, and since 2002 cat bonds have produced positive monthly returns nearly 90% of the time, demonstrating low correlation to traditional financial markets.","The Los Angeles wildfires in early 2025 caused approximately $40 billion in insured losses, the largest wildfire-related insured loss on record, stress-testing ILS structures without triggering widespread market disruption.","Hurricane Melissa triggered a 100% payout of a $150 million World Bank Catastrophe Bond for Jamaica, illustrating both the sovereign risk-transfer utility and the full-loss tail risk embedded in parametric structures.","Institutional allocators typically target 1%\u20133% of portfolio exposure to ILS; modeling suggests allocations up to 10% could improve portfolio metrics, but investors remain cautious given the asymmetric, event-driven return profile.","Diversification within the ILS market has broadened beyond US hurricane: recent issuances include $182 million for UK flood, $105 million for Canada earthquake and severe convective storms, and $100 million for French terrorism coverage.","Inflation has an indirect positive effect on ILS: higher insured asset values increase demand for risk transfer (widening spreads) while floating-rate coupons tied to Treasury money market funds directly benefit investors in high-rate environments.","Innovation in deal structure, particularly index-based solutions and parametric products, is expanding the addressable market by enabling faster payouts and more efficient risk transfer, supporting continued institutionalization."],"keywords":["catastrophe bonds","insurance-linked securities","parametric risk transfer","portfolio diversification","cat bond index","wildfire insured losses","uncorrelated returns"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":298,"title":"Rating Action: Moody's Ratings Downgrades City of Corpus Christi, TX's Issuer and Related Ratings to A1; Outlook Negative","authors":"","date":"2025-12-11","year":2025,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Moody's"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://ratings.moodys.com/ratings/600026426","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Moody's Ratings downgraded Corpus Christi, TX's issuer, general obligation limited tax, sales tax revenue, and combined utility revenue ratings two notches to A1 (from Aa2/Aa3) in December 2025, with a negative outlook, citing acute water supply depletion risk. The city anticipates demand will exceed supply by April 2027, with a Level 1 Emergency threshold potentially triggered by November 2026. The city carries ~$2 billion in total debt and is pursuing groundwater, effluent reuse, and well-drilling projects to avert crisis. ESG factors were explicitly material to the action.","insights":["Corpus Christi's water supply is projected to be insufficient to meet demand by April 2027, a Level 1 Emergency threshold could be triggered as early as November 2026, giving the city fewer than 12 months of implementation margin at the time of rating action.","The two-notch downgrade (Aa2\u2192A1 issuer; Aa3\u2192A1 utility) on ~$2 billion in outstanding debt directly demonstrates how acute physical water stress can translate into measurable credit deterioration for a U.S. municipal issuer.","Persistent drought along the Texas Gulf Coast and heavy industrial water use are the primary supply drivers; the Lake Corpus Christi and Choke Canyon reservoirs (Western Supply) have not received sufficient rainfall for replenishment, accelerating the emergency timeline.","The city is pursuing three near-term supply diversification interventions: groundwater access from the Evangeline Aquifer, effluent reuse for industrial users, and well drilling (Nueces Groundwater Program Western Well Field targeted for completion May 2026), all running parallel to the emergency trigger date, leaving near-zero margin for delay.","Financial reserves are projected to decline to 31% of FY2026 revenue due to capital spending; if the fund balance ratio falls below 25%, Moody's has signaled a further downgrade, quantifying a specific fiscal threshold linked to physical risk.","The utility restructured its rate design to shift revenue toward fixed charges and reduce volumetric rates, improving coverage stability even amid residential conservation, a replicable structural response to demand-side uncertainty under water stress.","Resident median income is ~82% of national median, constraining the city's political capacity to levy the significant rate increases and debt issuance required to fund water supply solutions, creating a distributional equity tension between fiscal necessity and affordability."],"keywords":["water supply scarcity","municipal credit downgrade","drought risk","ESG credit materiality","groundwater diversification","utility rate structure","Texas Gulf Coast"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":28,"title":"Advancing Adaptation: How Evolving Hazards Could Shape the Agenda","authors":"Mekala Krishnan, Olivia White, Sylvain Johansson, Sven Smit, Annabel Farr, Kanmani Chockalingam","date":"2025-12-11","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["McKinsey Global Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/mgi/our-research/advancing-adaptation-how-evolving-hazards-could-shape-the-agenda","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The McKinsey Global Institute report \u201cAdvancing adaptation: how evolving hazards could shape the agenda\u201d examines how rising physical climate risks, from sea-level rise and extreme heat to shifting precipitation patterns, will reshape investment and policy priorities over the coming decades. It quantifies growing exposures, highlights the substantial adaptation financing gap, and outlines a three-horizon framework for scaling resilience, from near-term protective measures to systemic integration and long-term transformational change. The report calls for stronger data and standards, greater public-private collaboration, and targeted nature-based and engineered solutions to mobilize the estimated trillions needed for effective adaptation.","insights":["The framing claim is that change is uneven in three dimensions at once: not everywhere, not in the same way, and not all at once. Freezing is expected to decline, and drought or flood risk may fall in small areas even while rising elsewhere, so an adaptation cost model cannot assume monotonic increase.","About 40 percent of the Earth's landmass already experiences severe heat, destructive wildfire, prolonged drought or severe flooding, and some 35 percent experiences freezing days, so the baseline is current weather extremes rather than a future scenario.","Heat stress, defined as more than four weeks a year of high temperature and humidity affecting labour productivity, expands by roughly 25 percent of global landmass at 1.5 degrees and more than 50 percent at 2 degrees, with already-exposed regions going from about 12 weeks to more than 16.","Hazards are defined using protection standards typical of developed economies, which is a consequential methodological choice for a global cost estimate because it sets the level of protection being priced.","The authors state they are not climate scientists and that the results are directional assessments rather than high-resolution predictions, offered as the basis for an order-of-magnitude assessment of adaptation costs and benefits. The companion report prices 20 proven, cost-effective adaptation measures."],"keywords":["physical risk","nature-based solutions","data standards"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":27,"title":"Advancing Adaptation: Mapping Costs from Cooling to Coastal Defenses","authors":"Mekala Krishnan, Olivia White, Sylvain Johansson, Sven Smit, Annabel Farr, Kanmani Chockalingam","date":"2025-12-11","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["McKinsey Global Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/mgi/our-research/advancing-adaptation-mapping-costs-from-cooling-to-coastal-defenses?utm_source=www.climateproof.news&utm_medium=newsletter&utm_campaign=mckinsey-flags-cooling#/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The document provides a comprehensive analysis of adaptation costs associated with various climate hazards, focusing on eight types of hazards including heat stress, wildfires, droughts, and flooding. It assesses current protection measures, future adaptation costs, and the efficacy of different adaptation strategies across demographic groups. The research utilizes external climate models to estimate exposure to these hazards and emphasizes the importance of establishing clear thresholds for protection standards.","insights":["The study examines eight climate hazards grouped into four categories: heat, wildfire, drought, and flooding.","Adaptation measures can be sector-specific or cross-cutting, addressing vulnerabilities and exposures in diverse contexts.","Costs of adaptation are estimated based on both capital expenditures and operational expenses through 2050.","A variety of thresholds are used to define hazards, which influence exposure estimates and the required adaptation measures.","Maladaptation poses risks wherein adaptation efforts can inadvertently create new vulnerabilities or negative consequences.","The analysis aligns with IPCC data regarding climate hazard confidence and their projected impacts across different global regions.","The findings suggest that as global temperatures rise, higher levels of adaptation will be necessary to mitigate increasing climate risks.","Uncertainties inherent to climate modeling necessitate ongoing research and flexibility in adaptation planning.","Demand for adaptation measures could increase significantly as populations face growing exposure to various climatic hazards.","The research highlights the need for continuous assessments to evaluate the effectiveness and economic viability of adaptation strategies."],"keywords":["exposure","vulnerability","adaptation measures","cost-effectiveness","hazard thresholds"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":424,"title":"Finding Inspiration in an Atlas of Solutions [Episode 24]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Amy Chester (guest)","date":"2025-12-08","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Rebuild by Design","Federal Emergency Management Agency","United States Department of Housing and Urban Development"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F83QZ27uWrE","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Amy Chester of Rebuild by Design describes two bodies of work. The first is the original competition, which carved a portion out of roughly $65 billion of disaster recovery funds and offered it to interdisciplinary teams on three conditions: work with communities and local governments on the ground, develop something that would not otherwise have been done, and address multiple challenges with multiple benefits. Ten teams worked across the region, projects were taken to the public for support, and seven were awarded. The Hoboken resiliency park is the worked example, a concrete structure beneath a park that pumps and holds water from twenty surrounding blocks while functioning as ordinary public space above; Hoboken has paired it with a waterfront park, an interior flood wall and streets redesigned for both pedestrian safety and water retention. The second body of work is the atlas series, which lets anyone look up their own county's federally declared disasters from 2011 to 2024, see how much FEMA and HUD money arrived and what that is per capita, and connect to their congressional representative. She notes an internal analysis, initially unpublished, finding that disaster funding to red and blue states was broadly similar, and a separate finding that 77 percent of New York City parks will intersect a future flood zone.","insights":["The competition's conditions functioned as the instrument: money was offered only for work co-developed with communities, that would not otherwise have happened, and that delivered multiple benefits, which is outcome-shaping through eligibility rather than through evaluation.","Governments are described as reluctant to publish a big idea because they fear being held accountable for implementing it, which is a political constraint on ambition that later funding availability exposed.","The atlas turns federal disaster spending into a constituent-level fact, county by county and per capita, with a route to a representative, which makes an accountability instrument out of data that was already public.","Treating parks as major infrastructure is supported by the finding that 77 percent of New York City parks will intersect a future flood zone, which makes the parks budget a flood budget.","The unpublished red-state and blue-state comparison of disaster funding found broadly similar totals, which is evidence against a partisan-allocation reading of federal disaster money."],"keywords":["Rebuild by Design","disaster recovery funds","Atlas of Disaster","Hoboken","resiliency park","FEMA","HUD","design competition","flood infrastructure","accountability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":32,"title":"New Analysis of Home Sales Makes Economic Case for Resilient Coastal Forests","authors":"Mike Pe\u00f1a","date":"2025-12-05","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Center for Coastal Climate Resilience at UC Santa Cruz"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://news.ucsc.edu/2025/12/mangroves-zillow-home-prices/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A new study highlights the economic benefits of mangrove forests for coastal homeowners in Florida, revealing that proximity to these natural features can preserve home values by $20,000 to $60,000 during storm events. The research provides the first market-based evidence showing homebuyers value the storm-protective qualities of mangroves, encouraging financial support for their protection and restoration.","insights":["Homes near mangroves in coastal Florida experience smaller price declines after storms compared to those further away.","The average financial protection per $1 million home from mangroves ranges from $20,000 to $40,000, with some instances up to $60,000.","This study is the first to use housing market data to quantify the storm-protection value of mangrove ecosystems.","Strong financial incentives exist for insurers, developers, and investors to support mangrove conservation due to their economic benefits.","The research indicates that homebuyers actively perceive and are willing to pay for the protective benefits of mangroves.","Mangrove protection aligns with broader climate adaptation strategies, offering economic and ecological advantages.","The study supports the concept of valuing ecosystems and biodiversity within financial markets.","Protection and restoration of mangroves can mitigate climate change impacts and increasing hurricane frequency.","Additional benefits of mangroves include protection of non-housing assets like agricultural land, infrastructure, and tourism.","Future work is needed to develop financial tools to facilitate investment in mangrove conservation."],"keywords":["mangroves","home values","hurricane protection","ecosystem services","real estate"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":193,"title":"Fintech Innovation in Green Finance: A Disruptive Force or a Complementary Tool?","authors":"Fengsheng Chien, Yunqian Zhang, Muhammad Sadiq","date":"2025-12-03","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Fuzhou University of International Studies and Trade","City University of Macau","Taylor's University","VIZJA University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.iref.2025.104658","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource analyzes the significant role of FinTech innovation in advancing green finance, highlighting its benefits in transparency and efficiency, especially within developed economies, while also addressing the challenges faced by emerging markets due to regulatory gaps. It discusses various innovative solutions, including central bank digital currencies and regulatory sandboxes, aimed at fostering climate tech investments, particularly in countries like Nigeria and Indonesia, and emphasizes the necessity for tailored global policies to promote equitable integration of FinTech in the green finance sector.","insights":["FinTech significantly improves the transparency and efficiency of green finance markets, especially in developed economies.","Emerging markets face structural challenges in green finance, but FinTech tools can help improve access to funds and risk management.","The uncontrolled growth of FinTech in emerging markets can lead to increased financial risks, including cybersecurity threats.","Countries with strong regulatory frameworks experience higher yields from the integration of FinTech in green finance.","CBDC implementation in Nigeria demonstrated a reduction of interest rates by 4.5% for SMEs in the renewable sector.","Regulatory sandboxes can create exempt environments to foster climate-tech investments while maintaining regulatory stability.","Fintech innovations are potential enablers for achieving climate goals through improved lending practices."],"keywords":["FinTech","transparency","renewable energy","digital currency","regulatory sandbox"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":124,"title":"Risk Ownership in a Time of Rising Climate Costs: Rethinking Who Pays, Who Benefits, and How We Move Forward","authors":"Nuin-Tara Key, Carolyn Kousky, Joshua Mendoza","date":"2025-12-03","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["California Forward","Insurance for Good"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.insuranceforgood.org/blog/cafwd-i4g2","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The second installment in a blog series discusses the escalating economic risks due to climate change and the need to rethink risk ownership. As the costs of climate-related shocks increase, current financing systems for resilience are found lacking. This blog emphasizes aligning responsibility, incentives, and investments across sectors to effectively manage risk and integrate resilience as a mainstream investment rather than an afterthought.","insights":["The rising costs of climate change are straining households, businesses, and communities, necessitating a reevaluation of risk ownership.","Current systems for financing resilience are fragmented and ill-equipped to address today's climate threats.","Measuring and valuing avoided losses is crucial to encourage investments in risk mitigation.","Bridging the communication gap between engineers, financial decision-makers, and budget managers is essential for scaling resilience solutions.","Insurance alone cannot solve the funding gap for resilience; collaborative, multi-sector approaches are needed.","New models for risk ownership must emerge, recognizing the shared nature of climate-related risks.","Innovative financing tools can unlock demand for resilience investments, making them more appealing for stakeholders.","Public-private funding pools and resilience districts can provide alternative paths for funding resilience projects.","Stakeholder incentives must be aligned to ensure that all parties benefit from mitigation efforts without unfairly bearing costs."],"keywords":["risk management","collaboration","investment strategies","governance models","financial tools"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":98,"title":"Funding and Financing Options for Resilience Investments","authors":"Nuin-Tara Key, Carolyn Kousky, Tai Michaels","date":"2025-12-03","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["California Forward","Insurance for Good"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.insuranceforgood.org/blog/funding-and-financing-for-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The first part of a blog series by CA FWD and Insurance for Good discusses the urgent need for funding and financing strategies to enhance climate resilience amid rising climate change costs. Highlighting key challenges and options for resilience investments, it emphasizes the critical role of both funding and financing in mitigating the economic impacts of climate change and adapting communities effectively.","insights":["Climate change is projected to reduce global income by at least 20% over the next 25 years.","Extreme weather events cost the world $16 million per hour, accumulating to $2 trillion over the last decade.","Many communities struggle to implement adaptation projects due to insufficient funding.","Funding refers to actual dollars for investments, while financing includes methods to manage large sums over time.","There are five primary strategies for securing funds for resilience investments: use current budgets, save over time, pay later via debt, use insurance-like financing, and seek grants.","Budget constraints and competing priorities hinder local and state governments from adequately addressing climate adaptation.","Projected future losses are often not tracked on balance sheets, complicating cash flow management for investments.","Many markets fail to fully price climate risks, limiting the effectiveness of insurance and lending.","Community benefits from resilience investments are often diffuse, complicating funding and recognition of their value."],"keywords":["risk reduction","infrastructure","adaptation projects","budget constraints","economic impacts"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":612,"title":"Reflection Group on Mobilising Climate Resilience Financing: Final Report","authors":"Reflection Group on Mobilising Climate Resilience Financing","date":"2025-12-02","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Europe"],"institutions":["European Commission"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://climate.ec.europa.eu/document/download/a38d4653-652e-49de-ade9-7e081765ed2e_en?filename=news_20251202_report_en.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The final report of the Reflection Group on Mobilising Climate Resilience Financing, published through the European Commission's climate site on 2 December 2025. Its disclaimer is explicit: the considerations are compiled under the aegis of the Group's members, it is not an official Commission document, it does not reflect an official Commission position, and nothing in it commits the Commission or precludes any policy outcome. Its recommended actions are organised by actor rather than by theme, in four sets: the public sector; joint action by public and market operators; the competent authorities, meaning sector ministries, standards offices and regional and local governments; and insurance. The body covers the state of play and expectations for climate resilience, the business and economic case for resilience split into economic risks and opportunities, business risks and opportunities and the alignment of the two, climate resilience communication including improved understanding of risks and public-private dialogue, and filling information gaps and improving data structure and accessibility.","insights":["**The recommendations are organised by actor rather than by theme**, so the report says who does each thing rather than what should happen, and it gives insurance a section of its own rather than folding it into the private sector.","Separating the business case from the economic case, and then devoting a section to aligning them, concedes the thing most resilience advocacy elides: what is worth it to a society is not automatically worth it to a firm, and the gap between them is where an instrument has to sit.","The disclaimer matters for how it is cited: this is expert-group advice carrying no Commission commitment, so it is evidence of what was recommended in Europe in late 2025 rather than of what the EU intends.","A dedicated chapter on information gaps and data accessibility puts it alongside the IIGCC record on incomplete asset-level vendor data and the WMO record on underfunded climate services. Three independent sources now name information as a binding constraint rather than a preliminary."],"keywords":["climate resilience financing","European Commission","expert group","recommended actions","business and economic case","insurance protection gap","data accessibility"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":423,"title":"The Long-Term Benefits of Nature-Based Solutions [Episode 22]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Leonard Nelson (guest)","date":"2025-12-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Natrx","United States Army Corps of Engineers","RISE"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x6Z6RhNynKo","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Leonard Nelson, CEO and co-founder of Natrx, makes the asset-versus-liability case for nature-based coastal infrastructure. Hard, impervious structures speed water up, which is what makes storm surge dangerous, while reefs, oyster beds and woody debris slow it down; and where a sea wall or road is a depreciating liability from the moment it is built, a nature-based installation is an asset that gains value over time. His economic argument is that traditional construction is now expensive enough, in materials and in the multi-year study, permitting and mitigation cycle it requires, that nature-based approaches frequently come out cheaper. He notes that the long lead times of conventional waterfront construction push designers to overbuild because they get only one attempt, which inflates both budgets and timelines, whereas nature-based techniques can be adapted incrementally. On permitting he reports broad regulatory consensus, citing the Army Corps of Engineers' Engineering With Nature programme, and describes offering agencies simple habitat-to-impact ratios, where a sea wall scores negative. He also names the obstacle plainly: inertia in a roughly $10 trillion global heavy infrastructure market where people are used to doing things the way they are used to doing them.","insights":["The framing that carries the financial weight is asset versus liability: a sea wall or road begins depreciating on completion, while a nature-based installation accrues value, which changes how the investment should be carried and financed rather than only how it is justified.","Overbuilding is presented as a consequence of process, not of engineering caution: a five-year permitting and mitigation cycle means one attempt, so designers oversize, which is a procurement-driven cost that incremental nature-based approaches avoid.","Habitat-to-impact ratios are used as the simple metric offered to resource agencies, and a sea wall scores negative on it, which is how permitting consensus is built without case-by-case argument.","The stated impediment is mindset inside a roughly $10 trillion global heavy construction market rather than cost or regulation, and clients who seriously evaluate the option with an economic benefit almost always choose it.","The company's path ran through RISE's federally originated innovation lab, which is a concrete instance of public catalytic funding producing a scaling private supplier."],"keywords":["nature-based solutions","living shorelines","Engineering With Nature","coastal infrastructure","habitat to impact ratio","sea walls","natural capital","adaptive management"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":265,"title":"Resilience Taxonomy for U.S. Infrastructure","authors":"Monica Reid, April Strid","date":"2025-12-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Kestrel"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://kestrelesg.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Kestrel-Resilience-Taxonomy-for-US-Infrastructure.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Kestrel 360's \"Resilience Taxonomy for U.S. Infrastructure\" (December 2025) is a practitioner-facing methodology document that codifies how the firm identifies and scores resilience features across nine municipal infrastructure sectors, roads/bridges, multimodal transportation, K-12 schools, higher education, airports, drinking water, wastewater, electric utilities, and healthcare. Drawing on benchmarking of over 15,000 municipal bond series covering more than $3T par, Kestrel maps sector-specific resilience indicators to its Sustainability Scores, citing a $4\u2013$10 return per $1 invested in infrastructure resilience, and positions these scores as inputs for investors pricing physical and transition risk in the U.S. municipal bond market.","insights":["Kestrel has benchmarked over 15,000 series of municipal bonds covering more than $3T par value, giving the taxonomy an empirical foundation rare in ESG/resilience rating methodologies.","The document cites a $4\u2013$10 return for every $1 spent on infrastructure resilience, sourced from WRI (2025) and the Global Commission on Adaptation (2019), providing a high-level BCR range relevant to capital allocators evaluating resilience investments.","Resilience features are embedded directly into Kestrel's Sustainability Scores (Environmental and Social sub-scores) rather than treated as a separate overlay, meaning resilience quality already influences bond-level scoring visible on Bloomberg and Investortools, key distribution channels for institutional fixed-income investors.","The taxonomy organizes indicators at two levels, community (equitable access, pre-disaster planning, emergency preparedness) and organization (vulnerability assessments, resilience plans, contingency plans), enabling both equity-focused and credit-focused users to locate relevant disclosures.","Sector-specific indicators reveal high granularity: e.g., airport runways extended for heat adaptation, bidirectional EV bus chargers with vehicle-to-grid capability at K-12 schools, and High Temperature Low Sag (HTLS) conductors for electric utilities, each representing capital expenditure line items that practitioners and engineers can verify in bond documents.","The framework explicitly links resilience to financial materiality: 'Sustainability and resilience are likely to become material drivers of asset valuations as the growing frequency of extreme weather events affects insurance markets and federal emergency support,' signaling a pricing-shift argument for allocators."],"keywords":["municipal bond scoring","infrastructure resilience indicators","sustainability taxonomy","physical risk integration","sector benchmarking"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":51,"title":"The Resilience Dividend: How Adaptability Builds Long Term Value","authors":"Felicia Jackson","date":"2025-12-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Forbes"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/feliciajackson/2025/12/01/the-resilience-dividend-how-adaptability-builds-long-term-value/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The article discusses the increasing importance of resilience in the face of climate change and socio-economic disruptions. It emphasizes that traditional corporate efficiency is becoming a liability, while adaptability and resilience are emerging as essential components for long-term value creation. The narrative highlights the need for financial systems and businesses to innovate and invest in resilience-oriented practices, as they grapple with rising risks associated with climate-related shocks.","insights":["Political uncertainty is affecting financial and corporate stability, prompting a shift toward resilience as a performance benchmark.","Traditional notions of efficiency are exposed as vulnerabilities in an unpredictable world, highlighting the need for flexibility.","Resilience is defined as the capacity to live and thrive amid change, rather than merely returning to the status quo.","Climate-driven disasters are straining insurance models, leading to higher premiums and reduced coverage in affected areas.","Resilience investments can yield significant economic returns, with a report suggesting that every dollar invested may generate four dollars in benefits.","Barriers to resilience investment include policy failures, mispricing of climate risk, and short-term market focuses.","Creating equitable resilience is essential, as social inequalities affect outcomes and benefits.","Resilience needs to shift from a project-oriented approach to a structural principle integrated into decision-making across sectors.","The concept of resilience is gaining traction as a foundation for long-term growth and competitiveness in turbulent times.","Addressing climate risk will require innovative financial mechanisms and systemic integration of resilience in fiscal frameworks."],"keywords":["innovation","sustainability","financial stability","economic growth","adaptability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":510,"title":"CCRIF SPC Annual Report 2024 - 2025","authors":"","date":"2025-11-28","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["CCRIF SPC"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ccrif.org/publications/annual-report/ccrif-spc-annual-report-2024-2025?language_content_entity=en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"CCRIF SPC, the Catastrophe Risk Insurance Facility of the Caribbean and Central America, reports on 2024-2025. Established in 2007 after Hurricane Ivan, it is the world's largest parametric insurance programme and the first multi-country, multi-peril risk pool, created to close the liquidity gap and give governments rapid access to funds after a disaster without adding to their debt. It now has 35 members, has made 82 payouts totalling over US$483 million over two decades, offers seven products and carries US$1.4 billion of coverage purchased. Cover spans tropical cyclones, earthquakes, excess rainfall, and the fisheries and electric and water utilities sectors, with payouts made within 14 days of an event so governments, public utilities and public entities can meet urgent needs, stabilise budgets and accelerate recovery. Risk pooling secures better reinsurance prices and so makes cover more affordable to members. In 2024 Hurricane Beryl produced US$84.5 million in payouts to seven members including governments, electric and water utilities and a tourist attraction. The report notes the protection gap is widening: in developing countries only 5 per cent of natural-hazard losses are insured, against about 50 per cent in developed nations. Alongside insurance, CCRIF funds scholarships and internships for Caribbean youth, small community resilience grants and capacity building for regional organisations.","insights":["The 14-day payout is the product, not a service level: CCRIF was built to close a liquidity gap rather than an indemnity gap, so speed rather than completeness of cover is what it sells.","US$483 million of payouts over eighteen years against US$1.4 billion of coverage currently purchased shows a facility whose value is in the tail: most years pay nothing and the structure has to survive them.","Risk pooling is named as the affordability mechanism: the pool buys reinsurance at a better price than its 35 members could individually, so aggregation is what lowers the cost of cover.","The 5 per cent against 50 per cent insured-loss comparison is the protection gap stated as a ratio between developing and developed countries, and it is the number that makes the case for a public risk pool rather than a private market.","Adding fisheries, utilities and tourism assets to a sovereign facility extends parametric cover to the sectors whose revenue failure is what actually strains a small state's budget after a storm."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","risk pooling","liquidity gap","CCRIF","sovereign disaster risk financing","protection gap","Hurricane Beryl"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":219,"title":"How a US Home Insurance Fix Is Becoming a Problem","authors":"Prinz Magtulis, Soumya Karwa","date":"2025-11-25","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","California","Florida"],"institutions":["Reuters"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.reuters.com/graphics/USA-ECONOMY/FAIR-INSURANCE/lgpdqnqamvo/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The increase in dependence on FAIR (Fair Access to Insurance Requirements) plans for home insurance in the U.S., particularly in disaster-prone states like California, is straining these plans financially as climate change intensifies natural disasters. Homes in high-risk areas are increasingly unable to obtain private insurance, leading to a surge in FAIR plan enrollment. The limited coverage they provide, along with the rising claims due to frequent disasters, creates a cycle of financial instability for FAIR plans, as they are often unable to collect sufficient premiums to cover claims.","insights":["The number of homes under FAIR plans in California rose from approximately 222,000 in 2020 to over 322,000 in 2023, marking a 46% increase in three years.","24 private insurers exited the fire risk market in California during the same period, contributing to the increased reliance on FAIR plans.","FAIR plans often provide only minimal coverage and may struggle financially due to the surge in claims following natural disasters.","In 2021, claims in Louisiana's FAIR plan exceeded premiums by over 1,500%, highlighting the financial vulnerability of these plans.","As more homeowners shift to FAIR plans due to increased costs of private insurance, the financial pressure on FAIR plans intensifies.","FAIR plans are not-for-profit entities that require sufficient premiums to remain sustainable; many incurred financial losses recently.","Home insurance premiums for FAIR plans in disaster-prone states have increased significantly, with Louisiana's Citizens plan raising rates by over 730% since 2019.","Risk reduction strategies, such as home modifications and state subsidies for low-income households, are recommended to mitigate the insurance crisis.","The cycle of increasing premiums and private insurer exits creates challenges for homeowners in high-risk areas, forcing them into FAIR plans or leaving them uninsured."],"keywords":["insurance","wildfire","natural disasters","homeowners","premiums"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":422,"title":"The Need for Audacious Goals [Episode 20]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Shalini Vajjhala (guest)","date":"2025-11-24","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","PRE Collective"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QVaKhhr9ETI","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Shalini Vajjhala, executive director of the PRE Collective and a trained architect, argues that resilience projects fail at pre-development, which is nobody's job and therefore everybody's problem. She uses a relay race as the organising image: the question is who is responsible for running each leg and who carries the baton between, for example, a transportation department and a water utility. Her worked example is Hoboken, a single square mile shaped like a bowl that took twelve feet of standing water in Hurricane Sandy, where early collaboration on a six-acre parcel became an almost $100 million resiliency park that reads as a community asset rather than as flood infrastructure. Her financial argument is that avoided flooding is historically something we are terrible at valuing and paying for, so it becomes a public good, and the fix is to ask where the problem shows up on a balance sheet. She cites small water utilities whose electricity costs have gone from about 30 percent of budget to 50 or 60 percent as an example of finding the motivated party, the Montgomery County Green Bank's hybrid line of credit and loan that makes the proactive repair possible and is more cost effective than deferral, and a North Carolina insurance product that rewards risk reduction as a template for closing the loop between a state insurer and a retrofit programme.","insights":["Pre-development is identified as the unowned stage where resilience projects die, so the innovation is institutional, a body whose job is to carry projects from vision to capacity to money.","Finding who is losing money is offered as the practical method for locating a motivated counterparty, illustrated by small water utilities whose electricity costs have risen from roughly 30 percent of budget to 50 or 60 percent.","The Montgomery County Green Bank's hybrid of a line of credit and a loan shows financing that requires repayment can still be more cost effective than deferring a repair, which is the case that has to be made for resilience lending.","A closed loop between a state insurer and a resilient retrofit programme is what North Carolina is said to have demonstrated with a first-of-its-kind product rewarding risk reduction, and the gap elsewhere is that the two programmes do not talk to each other.","Governance innovations are argued to come before financial instruments, because no single corporation or state can protect a supply chain, and aligning parties around their own financial interests is prerequisite to any instrument."],"keywords":["pre-development","green bank","resilience financing","Hoboken","resiliency park","water utilities","insurance incentives","collective action","project delivery"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":309,"title":"Where COP30 Showed Progress \u2013 And Where Work Remains","authors":"Angela Churie Kallhauge","date":"2025-11-23","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global","Latin America"],"institutions":["Environmental Defense Fund","United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction (UNDRR)","World Bank Group (WBG)","Green Climate Fund","NDC Partnership"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://blogs.edf.org/climate411/2025/11/23/cop30-outcome/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This EDF blog post, authored by Angela Churie Kallhauge and published November 23, 2025, assesses COP30 outcomes from Bel\u00e9m, Brazil. Key results include: the launch of the Tropical Forests Forever Facility (TFFF) with a multibillion-dollar pledge from 50+ countries (20% earmarked for Indigenous Peoples and local communities); the Wildfire Action Accelerator Pledge across 18 countries; a COP30 agreement to triple adaptation finance for developing countries by 2035; the Open Coalition on Compliance Carbon Markets (18 countries); and commitments targeting a 30% methane reduction by 2030 to prevent 180,000 premature deaths annually.","insights":["COP30 Parties agreed to triple adaptation finance support for developing countries by 2035, representing a significant quantified commitment to resilience investment scale-up.","The Tropical Forests Forever Facility (TFFF) launched with a multibillion-dollar pledge from 50+ countries, with 20% of funding dedicated to Indigenous Peoples and local communities, embedding equity into forest finance architecture.","The Global Methane Pledge targets a 30% methane reduction by 2030; achieving it could prevent 180,000 premature deaths annually, giving capital allocators a quantified health co-benefit to attach to methane mitigation investments.","Agriculture accounts for 40% of annual global methane emissions from human activities; a survey of 150+ agricultural finance institutions found most view climate risk as a financial risk requiring management and already offer sustainability-related products to farmers.","The Open Coalition on Compliance Carbon Markets launched with 18 countries, aiming to link and align compliance carbon markets, a structural development that could lower transaction costs and unlock investment flows for emissions reductions.","The J-REDD+ Coalition was created to bring high-integrity jurisdictional forest credits to market, signaling a potential supply increase in a carbon credit category relevant to corporate and sovereign buyers.","The Wildfire Action Accelerator Pledge, spanning 18 countries and 30+ Indigenous and local community organizations, frames wildfire risk management as requiring community-centered approaches and 'innovative finance', an investability signal for integrated fire management projects."],"keywords":["COP30","forest finance","carbon markets","methane reduction","wildfire management","adaptation finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":239,"title":"Financing Adaptation: 11 Financial Instruments That Help Build Climate Resilience","authors":"Carter Brandon, Aarushi Aggarwal, Bradley Kratzer, Valerie Laxton, Celine Novenario","date":"2025-11-19","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wri.org/technical-perspectives/climate-adaptation-finance-financial-instruments","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The World Resources Institute's study on climate adaptation finance analyzes 162 cases of financial instruments that assist countries and investors in building resilience against climate impacts. It highlights that while adaptation investments can yield substantial economic, social, and environmental benefits, a significant adaptation finance gap remains, necessitating innovative approaches and a diversified range of financial tools to meet the growing demands for climate resilience projects.","insights":["Investing $1 in adaptation can yield over $10 in benefits over ten years.","Diverse financial instruments beyond grants and concessional loans support climate adaptation efforts.","Most adaptation finance structures utilize a programmatic approach, with 75% demonstrating pooled characteristics.","64% of analyzed instruments focus on reducing risks, while 31% are aimed at managing risks.","Collaboration among various stakeholders is essential for scaling adaptation finance effectively."],"keywords":["sustainable development","financial instruments","risk management","public-private partnerships","innovation"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":178,"title":"From Bonds to Blended Finance: How a Diverse Range of Financial Instruments Are Financing Climate Adaptation and Resilience","authors":"Carter Brandon, Aarushi Aggarwal, Bradley Kratzer, Rebecca Carter, Valerie Laxton, Katie Ross","date":"2025-11-19","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.46830/wriwp.25.00061","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource analyzes a diverse range of financial instruments that are mobilizing capital for climate adaptation, with a focus on blended finance in low- and middle-income countries. It highlights the increasing use of pooled finance mechanisms and multicountry instruments as means to tackle physical climate risks. The study underscores the importance of innovation, collaboration, and public finance in closing the substantial adaptation finance gap, revealing a trend towards risk reduction over management in funding strategies.","insights":["A total of 11 distinct financial instruments are identified for climate adaptation.","Blended finance is the most frequently utilized instrument, particularly in low- and middle-income countries.","64% of analyzed cases focus on financing physical risk reduction rather than management.","The number of multicountry financial instruments has increased significantly, from 16% in 2015 to 47% in 2024.","High expected economic returns from adaptation investments average around 27%.","Diverse financial instruments cater to various physical climate risks, including droughts, floods, and ecosystem degradation.","The adaptation finance landscape remains insufficient, with an annual financing gap of up to $366 billion.","Partnerships among multiple actors are crucial in the deployment of bonds for climate adaptation projects.","Insurance and risk transfer mechanisms have become critical in high-income countries.","Further exploration into the suitability of different financial instruments for various climate risks and contexts is needed."],"keywords":["blended finance","risk reduction","pooled finance","collaborative funding","investment strategies"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":500,"title":"The Nature Conservancy Launches Sixth Year of the New York Climate Resilience Grant Program","authors":"","date":"2025-11-18","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["The Nature Conservancy (TNC)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nature.org/en-us/newsroom/crgp-2026/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Nature Conservancy announced the sixth year of its New York Climate Resilience Grant Program, offering grants of up to $50,000 for conservation and climate adaptation projects that protect resilient lands, secure floodplains and shorelines and help communities adapt. Since launching in 2021 the programme has awarded more than $2.5 million to 35 organisations for 62 projects, funding land acquisitions that conserve and connect resilient forests, protect floodplains and shorelines, and support planning and capacity building. Grantees include land trusts, conservation nonprofits, municipalities, Indigenous tribes and academic institutions across New York State; in 2025 it funded six land protection projects and six planning and capacity-building initiatives across the Adirondacks, Hudson Valley and Western New York. A worked example is Hudson Taconic Lands, which in 2023 used the grant to cover long-term care costs for nearly 200 acres of the donated Boehme Legacy Forest, keeping the forest intact and preventing development. The programme sits inside TNC's Connectivity, Climate and Communities Fund alongside the Resilient and Connected Appalachians Grant Program, which offers up to $100,000 for permanent protection of resilient and connected lands. The two focus on connectivity, a network of resilient lands and waters allowing species to adapt; climate, protecting carbon-storing forests and helping communities adapt to flooding; and communities, supporting resilient communities and sustainable livelihoods.","insights":["Grants of up to $50,000 averaging about $40,000 across 62 projects is small-grant philanthropy doing something specific: covering the stewardship and transaction costs that make a land protection deal closable, which no capital programme funds.","The Hudson Taconic example is the clearest illustration: the grant paid for long-term CARE of donated land, not for acquiring it, and without that the donation would have been a liability the recipient could not accept.","Funding planning and capacity building on equal terms with land protection, six and six in 2025, is a recognition that the constraint on small conservation organisations is organisational rather than financial.","Eligibility spanning land trusts, municipalities, tribes and universities makes this a rare instrument that can reach an Indigenous nation and a town government through the same application.","$2.5 million over five years is modest against an infrastructure programme, which is the design: the money funds connective tissue and convening rather than capital works."],"keywords":["climate resilience grants","land protection","The Nature Conservancy","floodplains","capacity building","connectivity","New York"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":421,"title":"How a Coastal Community Created a Living Laboratory to Advance Resilience [Episode 18]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Paul Robinson (guest)","date":"2025-11-17","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","RISE"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=blzvXHQ5Nuo","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Paul Robinson, founder and executive director of RISE, a Norfolk-based non-profit, describes a living laboratory for coastal resilience solutions. RISE does not fund research and development; it funds pilots, typically at $250,000 to $300,000 for a 12 to 18 month demonstration, and uses the region itself as the test environment so that a company can put sensors in stormwater pipes, build expandable walls or install living shorelines and show that the thing works. The stated purpose of the demonstration is to get the company its next step, paying customers and investment, which makes RISE a bridge across the gap between a promising technology and a procurable product. The model originated in the federal response to Hurricane Sandy, which led to state action creating an innovation lab, and it is coupled to a municipal buyer, since the City of Norfolk has used the competitive challenge process to justify sole-source procurement and move a product into its regular budget.","insights":["The instrument is a small, time-boxed pilot grant, $250,000 to $300,000 over 12 to 18 months, deliberately scoped to demonstration rather than research, which is a different risk position from either grant-funded R and D or venture capital.","The competitive challenge process doubles as procurement infrastructure: because a city selected a solution through it, the city could justify sole-source procurement and fast-track the product into its regular budget.","The chain from a federal disaster response through state funding to a non-profit innovation lab to a for-profit company at scale is the structural point, and shows federal policy producing a market rather than a project.","Using a region as a living laboratory gives a vendor a reference deployment, which is named as the thing that unlocks the next customer and the next investment."],"keywords":["living laboratory","pilot funding","coastal resilience","Norfolk","innovation challenge","procurement","living shorelines","stormwater","RISE"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":256,"title":"Financing Climate Change Adaptation: Turning Risk into Resilience","authors":"Subodh Mishra","date":"2025-11-17","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Harvard Law School Forum on Corporate Governance","ISS-Corporate"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://corpgov.law.harvard.edu/2025/11/17/financing-climate-change-adaptation-turning-risk-into-resilience/?utm_source=chatgpt.com","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt discusses the accelerating trend of adaptation finance as a crucial part of climate resilience funding, highlighting the rapid growth of adaptation bonds and the increasing market emphasis on corporate involvement. Despite notable gains, especially from corporate issuers, significant challenges remain, including market volatility, uneven sector participation, and geographic disparities in financing needs. The text outlines the strategic importance of adaptation finance and identifies emerging innovative instruments aimed at overcoming these hurdles.","insights":["The number of climate change adaptation bonds increased from 39 in 2017 to 601 in 2024.","Issuance volumes for these bonds surged from 23 billion to nearly 268 billion over the same period.","Adaptation finance is viewed as a vital tool for sustainable development and poverty eradication.","Corporate issuers accounted for 54% of adaptation-related bonds by 2024, surpassing supranational agencies for the first time.","Natural disasters caused global insured losses exceeding $100 billion in the first half of 2025.","Regulatory frameworks are increasingly mandating the disclosure of climate risks and adaptation strategies from companies.","Barriers to engaging the private sector include lack of standardized metrics and the long-term nature of adaptation projects.","Adaptation finance is disproportionately concentrated in high-income countries, far from meeting the needs of low-income nations.","Market signals indicate a potential new stage of innovation in adaptation finance, supported by improved regulatory frameworks."],"keywords":["bonds","investment","risk management","sustainable development","market trends"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":516,"title":"World\u2019s first impact bond on weather and climate data opens for contributions","authors":"","date":"2025-11-14","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Meteorological Organization","United Nations Environment Programme","United Nations Development Programme","World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://wmo.int/media/news/worlds-first-impact-bond-weather-and-climate-data-opens-contributions","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Systematic Observations Financing Facility, a United Nations fund created by UNEP, WMO and UNDP, opened contributions toward a Systematic Observation Impact Bond seeking USD 200 million by the end of 2026, announced as part of the COP30 Action Agenda. The instrument would frontload investor capital against long-term donor commitments, with contributions tied to results independently verified by WMO through the measured increase in internationally shared weather and climate data. If backed it would enable 30 Least Developed Countries and Small Island Developing States to meet the Global Basic Observing Network standard. Structuring options are designed and the outstanding work is identifying issuers and partners.","insights":["The bond is an outcomes instrument on a physical, independently measurable quantity: contributions are tied to the verified increase in internationally shared weather and climate data, verified by WMO, rather than to project delivery or to a proxy indicator.","It would raise USD 200 million to enable 30 Least Developed Countries and Small Island Developing States to meet the Global Basic Observing Network standard, and is claimed to raise internationally shared data five-fold.","The World Bank estimate underpinning the case is that closing the global basic weather and climate data gap could generate USD 5 billion in direct annual benefits and unlock USD 160 billion in wider economic gains across agriculture, energy, water and transport.","ECMWF finds that closing the data gap in these countries would cut weather forecast errors by 30 per cent in Africa and 20 per cent in the Pacific, with benefits reaching local forecasts within 12 hours and global forecasts within three to four days, which is the argument that observation in poor countries is a global public good rather than aid.","The instrument is not yet issued: structuring options are designed and suitable issuers and partners are still being identified, so the document describes a proposal rather than a closed transaction."],"keywords":["impact bond","outcomes-based finance","early warning systems","weather observation","global public good"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":429,"title":"Mangroves Slash Hurricane Damage in Florida by Billions","authors":"Siddharth Narayan (lead author, quoted); Christine Shepard (quoted)","date":"2025-11-13","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["The Nature Conservancy","East Carolina University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://blog.nature.org/science-brief/mangroves-slash-hurricane-damage-in-florida-by-billions/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A science brief from The Nature Conservancy reporting a Cell Reports Sustainability study that puts a dollar figure on mangrove flood protection in Florida. Mangroves in Collier County reduce average annual storm-surge losses by $67 million, equivalent to about $270,000 in avoided losses per square kilometre per year across roughly 250 square kilometres. During Hurricane Irma in 2017 they cut damages by $725 million, or 14 percent, and during Hurricane Ian in 2022 by $4 billion, or 30 percent. The modelling runs at kilometre-scale resolution using insurance-industry-standard catastrophe risk models, over synthetic storms representing 100,000 years of tropical cyclone activity plus reconstructions of both hurricanes. The distributional finding matters as much as the headline: properties landward of mangroves always see reduced damage, while those inside or seaward of the forest see more variable outcomes. More than half of the avoided losses come from storms with return periods under 30 years, that is, within a typical mortgage cycle. The brief closes on a gap: over $100 billion was appropriated for federal recovery after the 2017 hurricane season while little of it went to repairing the natural infrastructure that did the protecting.","insights":["The benefit is concentrated in frequent, lower-intensity storms: more than half the avoided losses come from events with return periods under 30 years, which is inside a typical mortgage cycle and therefore inside the horizon a lender or insurer prices against.","The study is built on insurance-industry-standard catastrophe risk models, which is what makes the result usable for inclusion in risk pricing and insurance frameworks rather than only for conservation advocacy.","Benefits are directional, not uniform. Landward properties always gain; properties inside or seaward of the mangroves see variable outcomes, so a per-property valuation cannot be inferred from the county aggregate.","The stated estimates are conservative by construction: the friction coefficient used to model mangrove resistance sat at the low end of the validated range.","The financing gap is named explicitly: over $100 billion in federal recovery funding after 2017 against $4 billion in avoided damages delivered by south Florida mangroves in 2017 and 2023, with few recovery dollars used to restore the natural infrastructure damaged in those storms."],"keywords":["mangroves","storm surge","avoided losses","natural infrastructure","catastrophe model","Collier County","Hurricane Ian","Hurricane Irma","flood risk reduction","benefit valuation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":148,"title":"How to Get Finance Flowing to Climate Adaptation","authors":"Gaia Larsen, Natalia Alayza, Molly Caldwell","date":"2025-11-13","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wri.org/insights/scaling-adaptation-finance","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This excerpt discusses the urgent need to increase financial flows toward climate adaptation, particularly in developing countries, where funding remains limited despite growing recognition of its economic benefits. It outlines strategies to mobilize additional resources, including concessional finance, multilateral development bank support, private sector engagement, and financial regulation, emphasizing the importance of coordinated action among stakeholders.","insights":["Climate adaptation finance remains severely underfunded, necessitating an annual investment of $400 billion by 2035 in developing countries.","Every $1 invested in adaptation can yield up to $10 in economic, social, and environmental benefits within a decade.","Multilateral development banks (MDBs) have been the largest providers of adaptation finance, but their capacity needs to be expanded.","Private sector involvement in adaptation finance is critical, as current public funding covers over 90% of adaptation finance.","Government regulations typically do not account for climate risks, highlighting the need for the integration of resilience into existing regulatory frameworks.","Debt-for-resilience swaps could alleviate some financial burdens on developing countries and redirect funds toward adaptation efforts.","Country platforms can help align diverse sources of finance with country-led adaptation initiatives.","Incorporating climate risk into financial disclosures is essential to encourage investment in resilience-building measures.","The global opportunity for adaptation finance could lead to the creation of 280 million jobs by 2035.","Effective enforcement and support for new regulations addressing climate impacts are crucial for enhancing resilience."],"keywords":["investment","finance","policy","infrastructure","regulation"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":520,"title":"WBCSD and GRP Climate Resilience Awards \u2013 showcasing real and cross-cutting solutions alongside COP30 Belem","authors":"","date":"2025-11-12","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Business Council for Sustainable Development","Global Resilience Partnership","Standard Chartered","Google"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wbcsd.org/news/wbcsd-and-grp-climate-resilience-awards-showcasing-real-and-cross-cutting-solutions-alongside-cop30-belem/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"WBCSD and the Global Resilience Partnership launched Climate Resilience Awards for Business to surface scalable private sector adaptation solutions ahead of COP30 in Belem, the first COP focused on adaptation and implementation. The awards drew 140 submissions from 45 countries across more than 30 sectors. Winners span innovative technologies, innovative methodologies and innovative financing: Google for AI-powered global riverine flood forecasting giving free warnings up to seven days ahead, Wave Save-Slamdam for modular reusable flood barriers that also store water for irrigation, CP Group for paddy methane reduction and soil carbon, Build Up Nepal for locally produced unfired eco-bricks, Standard Chartered for financing deals demonstrating adaptation and resilience as an investable asset class, Vermi-farm Initiative for digital finance to over 18,000 farmers, and Solos of Brazil as regional prize.","insights":["140 submissions from 45 countries across more than 30 sectors is the measurable output, and it is offered as evidence of private sector adaptation activity rather than as a curated shortlist.","Only one of the award categories is financing, won by Standard Chartered for deals demonstrating adaptation and resilience as an investable asset class, which indicates how thin the financing field still is relative to technology and methodology entries.","The SLAMDAM case carries independent monitoring rather than a claim: in Burundi the barriers reduced flood losses and enabled dry-season irrigation of 48 hectares, with intercropped corn, beans and vegetables and household income doubling through year-round harvests.","The recurring pattern across submissions is dual purpose, where a resilience asset also produces revenue or a service in normal conditions, which shifts the investment case from avoided loss to combined avoided loss and income."],"keywords":["COP30 adaptation","private sector resilience","investable asset class","flood forecasting","dual-purpose resilience assets"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":551,"title":"Why adaptation finance is moving up the climate finance agenda \u2014 and how financial institutions can engage at COP30","authors":"","date":"2025-11-10","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Environment Programme Finance Initiative","United Nations Environment Programme"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.unepfi.org/themes/climate-change/why-adaptation-finance-is-moving-up-the-climate-finance-agenda-and-how-financial-institutions-can-engage-at-cop30/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"UNEP FI's briefing for financial institutions on why adaptation finance is moving to the mainstream of climate action and what to watch at COP30. It defines adaptation finance as funding directed at building resilience, from flood-resistant infrastructure and drought-resilient agriculture to early warning systems and risk-transfer mechanisms, and frames it for institutions as risk management, investment opportunity and strategic relevance at once. Two developments carry the argument: the global goal on adaptation, with a shortlist of indicators and the first global adaptation scorecard, of which 11 indicators track adaptation finance flows; and the Baku to Belem Roadmap launched at COP29 to mobilise USD1.3 trillion in climate finance annually with a push to triple adaptation finance by 2030.","insights":["ELEVEN OF THE PROPOSED GLOBAL ADAPTATION INDICATORS TRACK FINANCE FLOWS from public, private and international sources, which turns adaptation finance from a voluntary category into something institutions will be expected to quantify, tag and report.","UNEP's Adaptation Gap Report 2025 puts the private sector's potential contribution at around USD50 billion a year, and explicitly conditions it on policy action and blended finance rather than presenting it as available capital.","The Baku to Belem Roadmap's emphasis on NON-DEBT OR LOW-DEBT solutions names the alternatives to lending together: guarantees, blended finance, catastrophe bonds, parametric insurance, risk pools and country investment platforms.","The pitch to institutions is framed as three things at once, risk management, investment opportunity and strategic relevance, which is how UNEP FI addresses the objection that adaptation is a cost rather than a market.","Aligning portfolios with the emerging indicators early is presented as a way to demonstrate leadership before the standards harden, which is a disclosure-timing argument rather than a returns argument."],"keywords":["adaptation finance","global goal on adaptation","Baku to Belem Roadmap","parametric insurance","risk pools","blended finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":332,"title":"Transformed by Tragedy: How Alabama Is Becoming a Model in Disaster Resilience","authors":"","date":"2025-11-10","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Alabama"],"institutions":["Pew Charitable Trusts","Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.pew.org/en/research-and-analysis/articles/2025/11/10/transformed-by-tragedy-how-alabama-is-becoming-a-model-in-disaster-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Pew Charitable Trusts article chronicles Alabama's evolution into a disaster resilience model following the April 27, 2011 tornado outbreak that killed 240 people. It details the Strengthen Alabama Homes (SAH) program (a first-in-the-nation grant program offering homeowners up to $10,000 for wind-resistant retrofits in exchange for insurance premium discounts), and the 2023 establishment of the Alabama Resilience Council (ARC) via Executive Order 736. The piece traces the ARC's recommendations, Pew's advisory role, and the 2025 Alabama Resilience Council Act legislation aimed at codifying resilience practices into state law.","insights":["The Strengthen Alabama Homes (SAH) program offers grants of up to $10,000 per homeowner for wind-resistant roof retrofits linked directly to insurance premium discounts, a rare documented mechanism linking public mitigation grants to private insurance cost reduction.","SAH has been replicated in at least 10 other states (Arkansas, Florida, Georgia, Kentucky, Louisiana, Minnesota, Mississippi, New Mexico, Oklahoma, and South Carolina), demonstrating strong replicability of the subsidy-for-premium-reduction model across diverse state regulatory environments.","Alabama's Executive Order 736 (May 2023) established the Alabama Resilience Council, positioning resilience as an operational government function, not merely a policy aspiration, by mandating cross-agency coordination and private-sector collaboration.","The ARC's October 2024 report recommended a chief resilience officer (CRO) position, a comprehensive statewide risk and vulnerability assessment, full asset inventory, and cross-agency coordination, a governance blueprint with direct implications for credit rating and bond investor assessments of state fiscal resilience.","House Bill 603 advanced unanimously out of committee in 2025, signaling bipartisan legislative support for codifying resilience, though no floor vote occurred before session end; reintroduction is expected in 2026, creating a near-term policy catalyst for resilience investment in Alabama.","The proposed Alabama Resilience Council Act would require the state to prioritize multi-benefit actions (those simultaneously reducing disaster risk while advancing environmental stewardship, economic stability, and public health), a co-benefits framing that aligns with green bond and sustainability-linked instrument eligibility criteria."],"keywords":["home hardening grants","wind mitigation","chief resilience officer","state resilience legislation","insurance premium discounts","Alabama Resilience Council"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":473,"title":"New Environmental Defense Fund Report Urges New York City to Take Unified Approach to Housing and Climate Crisis to Protect Communities from Future Displacement","authors":"","date":"2025-11-06","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["Environmental Defense Fund (EDF)","Regional Plan Association","Cornell University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.edf.org/media/new-edf-report-urges-new-york-city-take-unified-approach-housing","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Environmental Defense Fund, the Regional Plan Association, the Association for Neighborhood and Housing Development and Cornell University released a report, Policy Solutions for the Climate and Housing Crisis in New York City: Safe Housing for Ensuring Long Term Resilience, known as SHELTR, on 6 November 2025. It finds that over 19,000 homes housing more than 40,000 residents are at risk of being lost to coastal flooding by 2040, a number that could triple by 2070 on current trends, while more than half of New Yorkers are rent-burdened and low income and communities of color face both flooding and displacement disproportionately. The report sets out four policy solutions: adopt a comprehensive flood resilient land use framework to increase healthy, safer and affordable housing development; make the aging housing stock resilient through a one-stop shop for comprehensive retrofit assistance; build community wealth in low coastal flood risk neighborhoods where population growth might create affordability displacement; and establish a resilience fund to support adaptation infrastructure. The release notes the report followed a storm a week earlier that killed two people after two inches of rain fell in Brooklyn and Queens in hours, and recalls that 56 per cent of buildings flooded during Hurricane Ida.","insights":["The report's framing is that flood risk and housing affordability are one problem, because both push the same households out, so a resilience intervention that raises housing costs has not solved anything.","Two of the four solutions are financing asks, a one-stop retrofit assistance shop and a dedicated resilience fund, and the other two are land use, which places the recommendation on both sides of the capital-and-regulation boundary.","The third recommendation is the unusual one: it treats safe, low-flood-risk neighborhoods as the place where climate displacement will next appear, as affordability pressure rather than as water, which is a second-order displacement mechanism most adaptation plans do not anticipate.","The quantified exposure, 19,000 homes and 40,000 residents by 2040 tripling by 2070, is the number a New York City resilience fund would have to be sized against, and the report does not price it."],"keywords":["SHELTR","flood resilient land use","resilience fund","retrofit assistance","climate displacement","rent burden","New York City"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":257,"title":"The Resilience Opportunity: Unlocking Climate Resilience Through Public-Private Collaboration","authors":"Anne Christianson, Eric White, Dean Muruven, Varad Pande, Dave Sivaprasad, Christian Xia","date":"2025-11-06","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum","Boston Consulting Group (BCG)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/publications/the-resilience-opportunity-unlocking-climate-resilience-through-public-private-collaboration/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource outlines strategies for enhancing climate resilience through public-private collaborations, highlighting the necessity of private sector engagement in addressing climate risks. It presents a strategic framework, exemplified by the RISCO model, which monetizes benefits from flood mitigation and carbon sequestration, thus creating sustainable investment opportunities that align public aims with private sector returns and demonstrate the potential for scalable resilience projects.","insights":["Climate resilience is increasingly viewed as a proactive investment rather than a cost burden.","The estimated annual investment need for scalable, collaborative climate resilience infrastructure is projected to reach up to $520 billion by 2050.","Structured public-private collaborations can unlock scalable, investable solutions for climate resilience.","The RISCO model combines inclusive enterprise financing, co-benefit monetization, and insurance provision to foster climate resilience.","Public funding alone is insufficient to meet climate resilience investment demands, necessitating significant private sector involvement.","Blended finance is essential for facilitating early-stage investments and catalyzing innovative resilience approaches, especially in emerging markets.","Monetizing co-benefits from ecosystem restoration can create new pathways for investment in climate resilience.","Businesses have a strategic opportunity to actively engage in resilience solutions by aligning their operations with climate adaptation efforts."],"keywords":["public-private partnerships","investment strategies","infrastructure development","risk management","nature-based solutions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":534,"title":"Michael R. Bloomberg Launches Next Era of Local Leadership to Formalize Role of Cities, States, and Regions in Global Climate Action","authors":"","date":"2025-11-05","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Bloomberg Philanthropies","C40 Cities Climate Leadership Group","Global Covenant of Mayors for Climate and Energy","World Resources Institute","United Nations"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.bloomberg.org/press/michael-r-bloomberg-launches-next-era-of-local-leadership-to-formalize-role-of-cities-states-and-regions-in-global-climate-action/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Bloomberg Philanthropies committed $168 million to scale subnational climate leadership, announced at the COP30 Local Leaders Forum, building on more than $770 million committed to date for local climate progress. The commitment provides core support to C40 and the Global Covenant of Mayors and is organised around five pillars, the first being uniting national and local governments through the CHAMP coalition, which 77 countries have joined and through which local priorities are built into national climate and finance plans. The framing is a shift for cities, states and regions from independent testbeds of climate action to partners driving implementation at scale.","insights":["The stated mechanism is not direct project funding but coalition capacity: core support to the networks, C40 and the Global Covenant of Mayors, that convene subnational governments, which makes this an institutional rather than a capital intervention.","CHAMP is the substantive instrument named, with 77 countries joined, and its function is to build local priorities into national climate AND FINANCE plans, which is the channel by which a city's pipeline reaches sovereign budgets.","$168 million is set against more than $770 million already committed, so this announcement is roughly a fifth again of a decade of prior commitment rather than a new programme.","The UN Secretary-General's framing in the release is that local leaders are first responders to the climate emergency and need to be equipped with the finance and tools to act, which places the finance gap at the subnational level rather than the national one.","Resilient infrastructure appears in the list of what local leaders deliver, alongside cutting emissions, cleaning air and public transport, so adaptation is one strand of a general climate action commitment rather than its purpose."],"keywords":["subnational climate finance","CHAMP","C40","philanthropic commitment","multilevel governance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":507,"title":"Quantifying the Flood Risk Reduction of Coastal Nature-Based Solutions in the Caribbean: Implications for Developing Insurance Products","authors":"David Lumbroso, Adrian Champion, Sam Currie","date":"2025-11-03","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":[],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/jfr3.70141","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Lumbroso and colleagues model the flood risk reduction delivered by coral reefs and mangroves in the Caribbean and ask what it implies for insurance products. Tourist-related businesses, central to the economies of many small island states, are often vulnerable to coastal flooding, and nature-based solutions can reduce that risk, but there has been little work accurately quantifying the benefit or its insurance implications. The paper estimates expected annual damage to buildings in the Caribbean with and without coastal nature-based solutions in place. Both reefs and mangroves have the potential to reduce expected annual damage; in the case of reefs the effectiveness depends on their health, and unhealthy reefs increase expected annual damage in some cases. The authors identify the limiting factor for developing traditional indemnity products that take account of reefs and mangroves as the accurate quantification of the reduction they deliver, which would require significant updates to the catastrophe models the insurance industry uses, plus tailoring to specific locations. Parametric products offer a potential mechanism for financing the restoration of damaged reefs and mangroves, though further research is needed to align payout triggers with the actual damage these ecosystems sustain. The paper frames the insurance sector's interest as three questions: how much do coastal nature-based solutions reduce damage to businesses; can any risk reduction be used to lower businesses' premiums; and can reefs and mangroves themselves be insured so that finance is available to restore them after a hurricane.","insights":["The three questions in the introduction decompose the problem exactly: measure the flood risk reduction the wetland delivers, convert it into a premium reduction, and then insure the asset that produces it.","The finding that unhealthy reefs can increase expected annual damage is the one that complicates every reef insurance product, because it means the asset's protective value is conditional on maintenance and can go negative.","The named blocker for indemnity products is the catastrophe model rather than the underwriting: an insurer cannot price a reef's protection until the model represents it, so the reform needed is in the vendor models the whole market shares.","Parametric cover is proposed for restoring the ecosystem rather than for protecting the businesses behind it, which is a different use of the instrument: the payout rebuilds the natural asset so the protection continues.","Aligning a parametric trigger with damage to a reef is harder than for a building, because reef damage is not a simple function of wind speed or surge height, which is where the paper says more research is needed."],"keywords":["coral reefs","mangroves","expected annual damage","reef insurance","parametric restoration","catastrophe models","Caribbean"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":420,"title":"How Can We Better Understand Wildfire Risks? [Episode 14]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Nancy Watkins (guest)","date":"2025-11-02","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Milliman","Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xxg9Vl8xHNc","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Nancy Watkins, a principal at Milliman running an actuarial consulting practice specialising in climate resilience and catastrophic risk, explains why homeowner mitigation does not show up in premiums. The information that matters most, roof construction, ember screens, whether the lowest six inches of the structure is flammable, is invisible to catastrophe modellers because it has never been collected at the home level. Her proposed remedy is a mitigation record analogous to a credit report: the homeowner can see what was inspected and what it said, while insurers, fire scientists, utilities and modellers receive it at appropriate levels of aggregation so that consumer privacy is preserved. She describes the failure the current gap produces, a homeowner who passes a fire department inspection and receives a non-renewal notice the next day, with no synchronicity between what the trusted fire authority required and what the financial counterparty recognises. She is critical of policy prescriptions that mandate credit for mitigation without the data to see it, and of government backstops that create a false sense of security while leaving unstated who ultimately pays for risk not priced into the premium. She notes that the standards, including IBHS Wildfire Prepared Home and Neighborhood, are recent, with almost everything changing since about 2017.","insights":["The reason mitigation is not priced is a missing dataset rather than insurer unwillingness: home-level mitigation attributes have never been collected, so the model cannot see them however strong the mandate to credit them.","A credit-report analogue is proposed as the institutional form: the homeowner sees their own record, and insurers, modellers, utilities and fire scientists receive aggregated views, which solves the privacy objection that blocks home-level data sharing.","The compliance failure is concrete and corrosive: a homeowner passes a fire department inspection and is non-renewed the next day, which destroys the incentive to mitigate by breaking the link between the authority requiring the work and the counterparty pricing it.","Mandates that models must reflect mitigation are criticised as instructing a horse to drink water that is nowhere near it, since the requirement does not create the data the model would need.","A government residual programme is argued to create a false sense of security while leaving unanswered who bears the excess risk that is not priced into the premium."],"keywords":["catastrophe modeling","mitigation credit","wildfire","non-renewal","actuarial","IBHS","Wildfire Prepared Home","home-level data","residual market","risk pricing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":627,"title":"Biodiversity Finance Trends 2025","authors":"The Nature Conservancy","date":"2025-11-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Latin America","Europe","Oceania","Caribbean"],"institutions":["The Nature Conservancy","OECD","United Nations Environment Programme","Inter-American Development Bank","Global Environment Facility"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nature.org/en-us/about-us/where-we-work/europe/stories-in-europe/biodiversity-finance-dashboard-2025/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Biodiversity Finance Trends 2025, published November 2025, tracking progress towards financing the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and accompanying the 2025 Biodiversity Finance Trends Dashboard. It is organised by KMGBF target. Target 15 covers businesses assessing, disclosing and reducing biodiversity-related risks and negative impacts, with France's Article 29 as the worked example. Target 18 covers reducing harmful incentives by at least $500 billion a year and scaling up positive incentives, with Brazil's PNCPD. Target 19a covers raising international biodiversity finance from developed to developing countries to at least $20 billion a year by 2025 and $30 billion by 2030, broken into multilateral flows, private philanthropy and private finance mobilised by public finance. Target 19 covers mobilising $200 billion a year from all sources, broken into domestic resource mobilisation, global private financial flows, synergies with climate finance and access to funds, with the Australia Nature Repair Market, the Bahamas Nature Bonds project, IFACC and the Global Biodiversity Framework Fund as named cases. The report frames 2025 as the milestone year for the $20 billion international target.","insights":["**The tracking is organised by Kunming-Montreal target with named national mechanisms rather than by aggregate total**, so each flow is attached to the commitment it is meant to satisfy.","**The Bahamas Nature Bonds project and the Australia Nature Repair Market are the two named instruments that valorise an ecological benefit**, rather than mechanisms that move public money toward nature without pricing it.","The synergies with climate finance section is where the report treats biodiversity and climate money as overlapping rather than parallel, and it names the double counting that overlap creates.","Its staged title was the web headline of the story page that links it, Halfway to 2030: What the Biodiversity Finance Dashboard Reveals, rather than the document's own. The document title is Biodiversity Finance Trends 2025 and the record needs correcting to it."],"keywords":["Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework","biodiversity finance gap","harmful incentives","nature bonds","domestic resource mobilisation","Nature Repair Market","private finance mobilisation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":460,"title":"Hurricane-Induced Risk Contagion in Commercial Real Estate: Evidence from Hurricane Sandy","authors":"Lu Fang; Lingxiao Li; David Scofield; Abdullah Yavas","date":"2025-11-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Florida International University","California State University","Fullerton","University of Wisconsin-Madison"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4921589","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Fang, Li, Scofield and Yavas ask what hurricane destruction does to the price of nearby undamaged commercial real estate, using Hurricane Sandy as a natural experiment. They merge Real Capital Analytics transaction records with FEMA building-level damage data spatially, then use difference-in-differences and event study designs to identify price spillovers across property types. The result is sharply sector-specific: negative spillovers appear only in the office sector, where undamaged properties near severely Sandy-damaged sites saw price declines of 8 to 15 per cent that persisted for up to four years. Other property types show no comparable effect. The authors interpret the office declines as a capitalisation of heightened spatial contagion risk, that is a forward-looking reassessment by investors of interconnected physical and market vulnerabilities, rather than as direct damage or a temporary dislocation. They frame the broader stakes as systemic: large flooding events affect insurance and lending market stability, asset valuations, and the investment behaviour of both households and institutions.","insights":["The loss is contagious and priced: undamaged office properties near severely damaged sites fell 8 to 15 per cent, so exposure is a function of the neighbourhood rather than of the building, which is the same collective-risk structure the wildfire literature describes for homes.","The effect persisted up to four years, which rules out a short-lived dislocation and makes it a repricing rather than a disruption.","Only the office sector shows the effect, which is the finding that needs explaining and suggests the mechanism runs through tenant and market interdependence rather than through physical vulnerability alone.","Merging transaction records with FEMA building-level damage data is what allows damaged and undamaged neighbours to be separated, and is the design most spillover studies cannot achieve.","If contagion is priced into undamaged neighbours, then the beneficiaries of a protective investment extend well beyond the properties it physically shields, which widens the base that could fund it."],"keywords":["commercial real estate","Hurricane Sandy","spatial contagion","price spillover","difference-in-differences","FEMA damage data","office sector","asset pricing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":235,"title":"Rethinking Resilience: Adapting to a Changing Climate: Ch 3: Markets and the Missing Tools for Resilience","authors":"Forhad Shilpi, Matthew E. Kahn, Claudia Berg","date":"2025-10-31","year":2025,"resource_type":"Book","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/978-1-4648-2158-5","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Chapter 3 of 'Rethinking Resilience' emphasizes the urgent need for developing economies to adopt proactive policies for climate resilience. The chapter outlines how lower-income countries suffer disproportionately from climate impacts, advocating for strategies that focus on household income growth, public information systems, and robust insurance markets. It also criticizes current financial mechanisms for their inadequacies and calls for innovative approaches to better address the complexities of climate adaptation.","insights":["Lower-income countries are more impacted by climate-related disasters compared to wealthier nations.","Proactive policies are essential for effective climate shock resilience in developing countries.","Building reliable public information systems is critical for enhancing community preparedness.","Robust insurance markets can protect vulnerable households and businesses from climate risks.","A comprehensive five-pronged strategy is proposed for enhancing resilience: income growth, public information, insurance, infrastructure, and public interventions.","Current financial markets lack adequate tools to support climate resilience initiatives.","Collaboration between public and private sectors is key to developing sustainable financial resources.","Financial mechanisms must adapt to address the complexities of climate adaptation projects.","Flexibility in financial products is vital for facilitating better adaptation strategies."],"keywords":["disaster preparedness","sustainability","policy intervention","financial tools","investment strategies"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":499,"title":"WMO Unveils New Financing Partnership Initiative to Safeguard Forecasting Backbone","authors":"","date":"2025-10-30","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Meteorological Organization (WMO)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://wmo.int/news/media-centre/wmo-unveils-new-financing-partnership-initiative-safeguard-forecasting-backbone","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The World Meteorological Organization unveiled the WMO Weather, Climate and Water Intelligence Commons at an Investors Forum on 27 and 28 October 2025, to be formally launched in 2026. Secretary-General Celeste Saulo describes it as not a fund in the traditional sense but an investment in continuity and confidence, pooling public, philanthropic and private resources so that data flows freely, systems stay interoperable and innovation reaches those who need it. It seeks to mobilise at least US$100 million over five years to close financing gaps in global weather, climate and water monitoring, prediction and service delivery, leveraging billions in existing public sector investment and reinforcing rather than duplicating existing climate financing mechanisms; it supplements WMO's budget from member contributions. The rationale is that the global public infrastructure the world depends on is critically underfunded and increasingly fragile, that market-driven approaches cannot ensure full coverage or system reliability, and that emerging technologies such as AI depend on robust public infrastructure and data. It is built on four pathways: sustain and optimise the global observing system; strengthen global data interoperability and prediction; expand high-impact services and early warnings; and capacity and user co-creation. Saulo told the forum that global weather-related losses reached $318 billion in 2024, half of it uninsured, and that every dollar invested in early warnings and climate intelligence saves up to fifteen. She noted that a six-minute supercell storm in La Chaux de Fonds in July 2023 caused more than 100 million Swiss francs of damage, more than WMO's annual budget.","insights":["The La Chaux de Fonds comparison is the argument in one line: a single six-minute storm cost more than the annual budget of the organisation that forecasts storms worldwide, which is a statement about how the global observing system is financed.","Calling it a Commons rather than a fund is a deliberate framing: the asset is shared public data infrastructure whose value is destroyed by exclusion, so the financing has to be pooled rather than transactional.","$100 million over five years to leverage billions in existing public investment is a maintenance argument rather than a growth one, protecting capacity that already exists from degradation.","The AI point is the newest part of the case: commercial weather models trained on public observations depend on that data continuing to be collected and shared, which gives private technology firms a direct stake in funding the backbone.","Fifteen-to-one on early warnings is a return on INFORMATION rather than on physical protection, which is an unusually high ratio for a system that builds nothing."],"keywords":["WMO Commons","early warning systems","global public good","climate intelligence","blended financing","observing system","interoperability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":128,"title":"In the Race to Resilience, Adaptation Finance Is a Stumbling Block","authors":"Jennifer Chang, Adrian Garza, Dawn Holland, Natasha Brereton-Fukui, Rebecca Karnovitz","date":"2025-10-30","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Moody's","Swiss Re","New Development Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.moodys.com/web/en/us/insights/data-stories/emerging-markets-credit-exposure-most-affected-by-severe-weather.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Moody's special report, published 30 October 2025, on why adaptation finance remains elusive even as adaptation measures could rein in mounting economic costs. Moody's analysis finds physical climate risks, including drought, hurricanes, storm surge and sea level rise, could drive economic loss of around 17 per cent of global GDP by 2050 under current policies against a scenario with no physical risk and no new transition policies. The headline masks a disparity: advanced economies lose around 10 per cent of output by 2050 while emerging markets lose closer to 20 per cent, partly because emerging markets depend more on agriculture and tourism. Under global net zero by 2050 the losses narrow to about 5 and 9 per cent respectively. Emerging markets combine high exposure with limited financial capacity spread across competing priorities, and with sparse insurance: using Swiss Re data on ten-year natural catastrophe losses from 2015 to 2024, protection gaps run from 22 per cent in the UK and 43 per cent in the US to 79 per cent in Mexico, 91 per cent in India and 94 per cent in China, exceeding 90 per cent in some highly exposed, densely populated regions of Asia, Latin America and Africa. Pledges to the UN Fund for responding to Loss and Damage totalled $789 million as of end June 2025. The report warns of a negative spiral in which the rising cost of physical risk and rising debt constrain adaptation investment, reinforcing exposure and indebtedness, and notes that even in advanced economies insurers are raising premiums and withdrawing from areas they consider uneconomical. A Brazil case study covers the May 2024 Rio Grande do Sul floods, which the Economic Commission for Latin America costs at around BRL89 billion, about $17 billion or 15 per cent of state GDP, damaged or destroyed over 60,000 homes and produced $1 billion of insured losses, the country's highest on record.","insights":["The negative spiral is the mechanism to take from this: rising physical risk raises debt, and rising debt constrains the adaptation investment that would reduce the risk, so exposure and indebtedness reinforce each other. That is a debt-sustainability argument for concessional adaptation finance rather than a moral one.","The protection gap ladder, 22 per cent in the UK, 43 per cent in the US, 79 per cent in Mexico, 91 per cent in India, 94 per cent in China, is sourced to Swiss Re rather than asserted, so the cross-country comparison carries an external provenance.","$789 million pledged to the Loss and Damage Fund against losses measured in percentage points of global GDP is the arithmetic that makes international transfer mechanisms marginal, which the report states plainly.","Brazil's 36-month suspension of debt payments to the federal government, freeing about BRL11 billion, is an instrument worth noting: sovereign-to-subnational debt relief used as post-disaster liquidity, which is close to what a climate-resilient debt clause does at sovereign level.","Eco Invest is the concrete design, blended finance plus currency hedging plus public credit lines, and currency hedging is the piece most adaptation finance proposals omit even though it is often what actually blocks foreign capital.","The net zero comparison matters for framing: even under global net zero, emerging markets still lose about 9 per cent of output, so adaptation is not an alternative to mitigation but a requirement under every scenario."],"keywords":["adaptation finance","protection gap","Loss and Damage Fund","Eco Invest","blended finance","currency hedging","Rio Grande do Sul","sovereign sustainable bond"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":123,"title":"Returns on Resilience: Investing in Adaptation to Drive Prosperity, Growth, and Competitiveness","authors":"Guido Schmidt-Traub, Patricia Ellen, Veerle Haagh, Julia Turner, Annabel Mahgerefteh, Paul Limpens, Tassilo Bismarck, Pippi Durie, Nikita Jesaibegjans, Ilan Schleif, Vinicius Natacci, Alexandra Philips, Naomi Desai, Rebecca Gibbs, Nicholas Sukitsch, Wendy Woods, Sonya Hoo, Maxine Yee, Wouter Nientker","date":"2025-10-30","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["SystemIQ","BCG"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.systemiq.earth/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/FULL-REPORT-FINAL-Returns-on-resilience-Oct-2025.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource emphasizes the urgent need for strategic investments in climate adaptation as a pathway to bolster economic growth, maintain competitiveness, and enhance sustainability across various sectors. It highlights that effective adaptation measures yield significant returns, reduce vulnerabilities, create job opportunities, and support long-term stability for communities and businesses alike, while advocating for collaboration between public and private sectors to mobilize financial resources effectively.","insights":["Investment in climate adaptation significantly stimulates economic growth and job creation.","There is a strong correlation between effective climate resilience strategies and enhanced financial performance.","Proactive adaptation measures help mitigate future risks and safeguard assets across sectors.","Public-private partnerships are essential for unlocking funding and promoting successful adaptation initiatives.","Innovative financing mechanisms can facilitate and amplify investments in climate adaptation efforts."],"keywords":["economic growth","sustainability","public-private partnerships","job creation","investment strategies"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":151,"title":"UN Report: Five Charts Which Explain the \u2018Gap\u2019 in Finance for Climate Adaptation","authors":"Ayesha Tandon, Cecilia Keating","date":"2025-10-29","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["CarbonBrief","Carbon Brief"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.carbonbrief.org/un-report-five-charts-which-explain-the-gap-in-finance-for-climate-adaptation/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 2025 UNEP Adaptation Gap Report (\"Running on empty\") finds that developing countries need $310\u2013365bn annually by 2035 for climate adaptation, yet received only $26bn in international adaptation finance from developed nations in 2023, a 12\u201314\u00d7 gap. Developed countries are on track to miss the Glasgow Climate Pact's goal of $40bn by 2025, and the NCQG's $300bn annual target for 2035 is deemed insufficient once inflation and the mitigation/adaptation split are accounted for. Private-sector flows to adaptation stood at just $5bn in 2023, with a realistic ceiling of $50bn by 2035.","insights":["Developing countries face an adaptation finance gap of $284\u2013339bn per year by 2035, with current international flows of ~$26bn covering only 7\u20138% of estimated needs, a 12\u201314\u00d7 shortfall.","Developed-country international adaptation finance fell from $27.9bn in 2022 to $25.9bn in 2023, driven by a decline in multilateral development bank funding, which accounted for 57% of flows.","The Glasgow Climate Pact goal of $40bn in adaptation finance by 2025 requires 12% compound annual growth; actual growth from 2019\u20132023 was only 7%, and foreign aid fell 9% in 2024 with further cuts of 9\u201317% projected in 2025.","Non-concessional loans exceeded concessional ones for the first time in 2023, raising long-term affordability and equity concerns for recipient countries and creating a potential 'adaptation investment trap' where climate disaster costs deepen indebtedness.","The NCQG's $300bn annual target by 2035, covering both mitigation and adaptation, translates to only $78bn for adaptation under a minimum scenario (26% share), against inflation-adjusted needs of $440\u2013520bn by 2035.","Even a maximum adaptation scenario under the NCQG yields only $166bn for adaptation by 2035, still far short of estimated needs, while private-sector flows are capped at a 'realistic' $50bn/year, covering 15\u201320% of needs.","Least-developed countries (LDCs) and small island developing states (SIDS) received $10.4bn and $1.2bn respectively in 2022\u201323, against a combined estimated need of $50bn/year; per-capita flows declined in both groups.","87% of countries have at least one national adaptation plan or strategy, but 36 are expired or outdated, 25 countries have none, and implementation reporting remains thin, only 7% of BTR data points address adaptation results."],"keywords":["adaptation finance gap","international public finance","NCQG","national adaptation plans","concessional loans","least developed countries","multilateral development banks"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":146,"title":"Adaptation Gap Report 2025: Running on Empty - The World Is Gearing Up for Climate Resilience, Without the Money to Get There","authors":"","date":"2025-10-29","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Environment Programme"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.59117/20.500.11822/48798","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Adaptation Gap Report 2025 reveals a significant financial shortfall in climate adaptation, with developing countries requiring $310 billion to $365 billion annually by 2035, while international public finance flows have declined to $26 billion. The report underscores the urgent need for effective national adaptation planning, enhanced reporting mechanisms, and innovative financing models to mobilize both public and private investment. It highlights that despite increasing recognition of climate adaptation's importance, barriers such as funding shortages, limited technical capacities, and fragmented governance impede progress, necessitating a collective global effort to close the adaptation finance gap and ensure resilience for vulnerable populations.","insights":["Developing countries need $310 billion to $365 billion annually for effective adaptation by 2035, while current funding is only $26 billion.","The adaptation finance gap stands between $284 billion and $339 billion per year, indicating a critical need for increased financial support.","Private sector investment potential is estimated at $50 billion annually, which remains underutilized compared to adaptation needs.","Innovative financing models are essential to mobilize private investments and enhance adaptation initiatives, requiring coordinated public-private partnerships.","A holistic approach, incorporating technological advancements and gender equality considerations, is vital for effective adaptation finance."],"keywords":["vulnerabilities","governance","transparency","sustainability","public finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":419,"title":"How Can We Insure Our Communities from Extreme Weather? [Episode 12]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Charlie Sidoti (guest)","date":"2025-10-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","InnSure"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AUt7nBExMIc","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Charlie Sidoti, executive director of InnSure, a non-profit funded organisation working on insurance innovation, argues that insurance products, distribution and the way risk is financially backed are all far less innovative than they could be, and that the opening is at the local level. He describes parametric triggers that pay on a metric such as wind speed, which can move money into a community quickly, and microinsurance for small losses that are devastating to households with no financial slack but are ignored in a conversation dominated by billion-dollar events. His structural point is that with a protection gap above 50 percent, state and local governments are already the largest insurers whether or not they recognise it, and so can afford to take risk deliberately, do the research and development, and create a market rather than treat post-disaster spending as grant money going out the door. He reports chambers of commerce asking about pools, and proposes a further product category: performance guarantees on the built environment, on the argument that high-efficiency buildings are probably more resilient and that guaranteeing the relationship would unlock financing for adaptation investment. His closing formulation is that a community should plan for its insurability, not only its resilience.","insights":["If the protection gap exceeds 50 percent, the public sector is already the largest insurer by default, which reframes disaster spending as an uncosted insurance book that could be deliberately designed, priced and capitalised instead.","Performance guarantees on the built environment are proposed as a new product category, on the argument that guaranteeing a building's resilience performance would unlock financing for adaptation investment that currently cannot be underwritten.","Insurer innovation is described as aimed at the insurer's own risk balance sheet rather than at the community's problem, which produces better pricing and market exit at the same time.","Local knowledge of where risk actually sits is presented as an asset that distant underwriters lack, giving community development organisations a basis to provide coverage and mobilise capital that the incumbent market cannot.","Microinsurance is argued for on distributional grounds: small uninsured losses are devastating precisely for households with no wealth buffer, and those losses are invisible in a market organised around large events."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","microinsurance","protection gap","community insurance pool","performance guarantee","insurance innovation","local government risk","insurability planning"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":306,"title":"Issues and Options: Centering Adaptation Finance at COP30","authors":"","date":"2025-10-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Center for Climate and Energy Solutions (C2ES)","United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP)","Green Climate Fund","Climate Policy Initiative (CPI)","Natural Resources Defense Council (NRDC)","World Bank Group (WBG)","Financial Stability Board","NDC Partnership"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.c2es.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Centering-Adaptation-Finance-at-COP30.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This C2ES discussion paper (October 2025) analyzes options for centering adaptation finance at COP30 in Bel\u00e9m, following the expiration of the COP26 goal to double international public adaptation finance to developing countries by 2025. The paper finds the annual adaptation finance gap stands at $337 billion, that only $28.9 billion was provided in 2022, and that even achieving the doubling target ($38.8 billion/year) would close only 5% of the gap. It maps pathways (including a new adaptation finance goal, maximizing the Adaptation Fund and GCF, and leveraging the NCQG's Roadmap to 1.3T), while identifying political and methodological obstacles such as double-counting risk and geopolitical headwinds from ODA cuts.","insights":["The annual adaptation finance gap between developing country needs and finance provided is approximately $337 billion, per UNEP's 2025 Adaptation Gap Report, making even ambitious public finance targets marginal in closing it.","The COP26 doubling goal set a threshold of $38.8 billion/year by 2025; developed countries provided $28.9 billion in 2022, still short of the target, and ODA cuts in 2025 are expected to reduce total adaptation finance further.","Even full achievement of the COP26 doubling goal would reduce the adaptation finance gap by only 5%, underscoring the inadequacy of public finance alone and the necessity of private capital mobilization through blended finance and innovative instruments.","Bilateral public finance constituted nearly one-third of adaptation finance in 2022; with many donor countries' climate finance commitments expiring by end-2025, predictability for adaptation pipelines is materially threatened.","The GCF has allocated 55% of its portfolio to adaptation in grant-equivalent terms, but total adaptation programming reached only $7.3 billion in 2024, illustrating the scale gap between institutional priority and actual disbursement.","The Adaptation Fund has repeatedly failed to meet its $300 million fundraising target, and expected share-of-proceeds from Article 6.4 carbon markets will take years to materialize, limiting near-term replenishment prospects.","A new adaptation finance goal faces specific methodological risks: double-counting with the NCQG's $300 billion/year by 2035 target, unclear tracking of loss-and-damage versus adaptation expenditures, and absence of agreed accounting methodologies."],"keywords":["adaptation finance gap","NCQG","Global Goal on Adaptation","COP30","bilateral public finance","blended finance mobilization","developing country finance access"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":90,"title":"Financial Value of Nature: Coastal Housing Markets, Mangroves, and Climate Resilience","authors":"Teng Liu, Brook Constantz, Galina Hale, Michael W. Beck","date":"2025-10-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Florida","US"],"institutions":["Center for Coastal Climate Resilience at UC Santa Cruz","National Bureau of Economic Research","CEPR"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/rof/rfaf061","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource examines the financial value of mangroves in moderating property values in coastal housing markets during hurricanes in Florida, demonstrating that proximity to mangroves not only protects homes from significant price declines post-storm but also suggests a strong market willingness to invest in mangrove conservation as a nature-based solution for climate resilience.","insights":["Mangroves significantly reduce property value decline during hurricanes, providing essential storm protection.","Closer proximity to mangroves correlates with lesser price depreciation and increased housing price stability after hurricanes.","The width of mangrove forests enhances their protective benefits on property values.","Investments in mangrove conservation can yield substantial economic benefits for property owners and developers.","House prices are positively influenced by the presence of mangroves, particularly in medium-risk flood areas.","The reduction in the probability of losing over 25% of property value due to mangroves is between 3% to 7%.","Hurricanes impact not only directly affected areas but also broader regional housing market perceptions.","Comprehensive analyses of multiple hurricanes underscore the consistent protective role of mangroves."],"keywords":["mangroves","property values","flood risk","housing market","coastal defense"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":70,"title":"Global Landscape of Climate Finance 2025: EMDE Spotlight","authors":"Baysa Naran, Varun Shankar, Matthew Price, James Dixon, Sasha Abraham, Jose Diaz, Mairead Barron","date":"2025-10-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/global-landscape-of-climate-finance-2025-emde-spotlight/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Global Landscape of Climate Finance 2025: EMDE Spotlight' report highlights the critical role of financing for emerging markets and developing economies (EMDEs) in addressing the global climate crisis. Covering 157 countries that account for nearly 90% of the global population, the report underscores the interplay between climate finance needs and development opportunities. With over USD 1 trillion in climate finance mobilized in 2023, predominantly from domestic sources, the need for adaptive strategies in EMDEs is emphasized as integral to sustainable development and achieving climate resilience.","insights":["EMDEs encompass 157 countries, representing almost 90% of the global population.","Over 40% of the global economy is located within these EMDEs.","Climate finance in EMDEs surpassed USD 1 trillion in 2023, with 80% sourced domestically.","Rapid urbanization and industrial growth in EMDEs increase energy needs and vulnerability to climate impacts.","Strong domestic planning and policies are vital for sustainable development and tapping into climate finance opportunities.","Investment in climate adaptation drives economic diversification and job creation.","Adaptation and resilience strategies are becoming core components of national development agendas.","International finance plays a crucial role alongside domestic efforts in addressing climate challenges.","The success of EMDE climate transitions will significantly influence global emissions trajectories."],"keywords":["emerging markets","sustainability","economic diversification","investment opportunities","energy access"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":431,"title":"Reimagining Insurance and Resilience in a Changing World","authors":"","date":"2025-10-23","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Georgia","Duke University","National Science Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://engineering.uga.edu/reimagining-insurance-and-resilience-in-a-changing-world/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The University of Georgia and Duke University, with partners from the insurance and climate data industries, launched the Center for Innovation in Risk, Catastrophes, and Decisions, funded by a five-year $1.5 million National Science Foundation grant and eight founding industry members contributing collectively over $500,000 a year. The centre uses an industry-university collaborative research model to rethink how risk is assessed, mitigated and insured, with the stated aim of translating research into financial and policy solutions. Its named priority is property insurance for homes in high-risk areas, where rebuilding costs and disaster frequency have made insurers wary, pushing prices up, options down and some insurers out of the most exposed communities. The alternative it proposes to explore is communities coming together to self-insure part of their disaster risk, on the argument that residents who reduce their collective exposure benefit directly and so have a clear incentive to do so. Duke's Mark Borsuk, the centre's director, frames the centre's value as being an impartial party able to pilot such studies with communities in ways private insurers cannot.","insights":["The centre positions itself as an impartial party that can pilot insurance structures private insurers cannot, which identifies the missing actor in insurance innovation as a trusted convener rather than a new product.","Community self-insurance is the specific structure under study, with the incentive argument that reduced collective exposure benefits residents directly, aligning mitigation with the party holding the risk.","The funding structure is itself the model: a five-year $1.5 million NSF grant leveraging over $500,000 annually from eight industry members, so public research money is buying industry participation rather than substituting for it.","Insurer withdrawal from the most at-risk communities is stated as the starting condition, so the research agenda is set by market exit rather than by a scientific gap."],"keywords":["CIRCAD","community self-insurance","insurance innovation","National Science Foundation","catastrophe risk","university-industry research","property insurance","risk mitigation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":117,"title":"In First Six Months, Cost of Weather Catastrophes on Pace to Break a Record","authors":"Scott Dance","date":"2025-10-22","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["The New York Times"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/22/climate/trump-noaa-weather-tracking.html","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This New York Times article (October 2025) reports that Climate Central, led by former NOAA scientist Adam Smith, has revived the federal billion-dollar disaster database after the Trump administration halted NOAA's updates. Through the first half of 2025, U.S. extreme weather caused $101.4 billion in damages, the costliest first half on record, driven largely by the Los Angeles wildfires ($60B+). The piece documents escalating disaster costs and the policy implications of shifting federal disaster relief responsibilities to states.","insights":["U.S. extreme weather caused $101.4 billion in damages in the first six months of 2025 alone, the most expensive first-half on record, signaling accelerating physical risk for capital allocators.","The Los Angeles wildfires caused over $60 billion in damage, nearly doubling the inflation-adjusted record for U.S. fire damage, set in 2018 (Paradise/Northern California fires at ~$32B).","Annual billion-dollar disaster frequency has surged from an average of 3 per year in the 1980s to 19 per year over the last decade, representing a roughly 6x increase in event frequency.","Inflation-adjusted annual U.S. disaster costs reached $182.7 billion in 2024, a record high, establishing a benchmark for insurers, lenders, and municipal bond analysts pricing physical risk.","The Trump administration's decision to stop updating the NOAA billion-dollar disaster database removes a key public data input used by the insurance industry, policymakers, and researchers, creating a data gap that a nonprofit (Climate Central) is now filling with the same methodology.","A single tornado outbreak (March 14\u201316, 2025, central and southern U.S.) caused $10.6 billion in damage, illustrating the materiality of severe convective storm risk for property and casualty insurers.","The administration is expected to recommend FEMA structural changes by end of November 2025, with a stated policy goal of shifting disaster relief costs from federal to state governments, a potential repricing trigger for state and local government credit risk.","Four of the five most costly U.S. disaster seasons have occurred since 2017, establishing a clear recent-period trend that stress-testing models anchored to long-run historical averages will systematically underestimate."],"keywords":["billion-dollar disasters","disaster cost tracking","NOAA database","wildfire damage","severe convective storms"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":43,"title":"Disaster Recovery Is an $8 Trillion Driver of US Growth","authors":"Eric Roston","date":"2025-10-21","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Bloomberg Intelligence"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://www.bloomberg.com/features/2025-disaster-industrial-complex-us-economy/?embedded-checkout=true","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Bloomberg feature investigates the \"disaster industrial complex\", the sprawling, largely unacknowledged sector of the US economy devoted to preparing for and recovering from climate disasters. Using Hurricane Helene's devastation of Asheville, North Carolina as a case study, the article quantifies how disaster-related spending has grown from ~$80B/year in the 1990s to nearly $1 trillion in the 12 months ending June 2025, representing roughly 36% of all US GDP growth since 2000. It profiles the supply chain of recovery, from engineering giants like Tetra Tech to hardware retailers like Home Depot, and examines the financial and governance risks posed by FEMA's retrenchment under the Trump administration.","insights":["Disaster-related spending accounted for approximately 36% of all US GDP growth since 2000, rising from ~$80B/year in the 1990s to nearly $1 trillion in the 12 months ending June 2025, according to Bloomberg Intelligence research.","A Bloomberg Intelligence 'Prepare and Repair Index' of ~100 large public companies in the disaster economy outperformed the S&P 500 by 6.5% per year from October 2015 to October 2025, offering a concrete equity signal for capital allocators.","Hurricane Helene's total recovery cost for North Carolina was estimated at $59.6 billion; federal and state agencies disbursed $5.7 billion to the region through September 2025, illustrating the chronic gap between damage and public funding.","FEMA's retrenchment under the Trump administration, including closure of pre-disaster mitigation grant programs and denial of routine aid requests, is shifting cost burden to households, insurers, and municipal bond markets, with one economist warning bond markets will 'punish places that aren't resilient.'","Hi-Wire Brewing's River Arts District facility suffered damage several times greater than $100,000 in cleanup costs alone; the building owners recorded over $750,000 in total losses, two-thirds covered by insurance, while business interruption insurance had not paid out as of reporting date.","Buncombe County spent $26 million on Helene recovery, of which $9 million (35%) went to a single engineering contractor (Tetra Tech), illustrating the concentration of recovery spending in large professional services firms.","Duke Energy had 2.5 million customers in the Carolinas without power after Helene, and restoration took until mid-November, a roughly six-to-seven week outage, underscoring the economic drag of prolonged power disruption."],"keywords":["disaster industrial complex","disaster recovery spending","Prepare and Repair Index","FEMA retrenchment","municipal bond risk","Hurricane Helene"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":418,"title":"How Do We Protect Our Homes from Hurricanes? [Episode 11]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Mark Fowler (guest); Lars Powell (guest)","date":"2025-10-20","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Alabama"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Alabama Department of Insurance","University of Alabama","Habitat for Humanity"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=39bA5KaZR-c","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Alabama Insurance Commissioner Mark Fowler and Dr Lars Powell of the Alabama Center for Insurance Information and Research describe the state's FORTIFIED programme and the Strengthen Alabama Homes grant scheme that funds retrofits. The founding logic, attributed to former Commissioner Jim Ridling, is that nothing changes insurance rates until the economics of what happens after a storm change, which means building stronger and sustaining less damage. The measured result is that fortified homes generate roughly 75 percent fewer claims, and that the claims that do occur are 20 to 40 percent less severe. The evidence base came from Hurricane Sally in 2020, the first hurricane to pass over a critical mass of fortified roofs, after insurers approached the department with their own data; the study followed once that proprietary data could be used. Alabama now has close to 60,000 FORTIFIED homes, and roughly 20 percent of single-family residential properties in the two coastal counties have been built or retrofitted to the standard. The retrofit itself is modest and largely invisible, using stronger fasteners, ring shank nails and sealed roof edges.","insights":["The headline result is an avoided-loss measurement an insurer can underwrite against: approximately 75 percent fewer claims, and 20 to 40 percent lower severity on the claims that remain.","The design question the episode closes on is the Benefit to Instrument gap directly: fortification is paid upfront by the homeowner while the savings accrue over time to insurers and policyholders, so the open problem is how to bring those future savings forward to fund the retrofit today.","The evidence came from insurers' own post-Sally data, which was initially withheld as proprietary, so the public benefit case depended on getting private loss data released.","Approximately 60,000 FORTIFIED homes statewide and about 20 percent of single-family stock in the two coastal counties is the adoption level achieved by a grant programme, which is the counterfactual against which a market-based mechanism would have to be judged.","The programme is residential only; commercial buildings, condominiums, power lines, roads and bridges are named as unfunded and needed, so the instrument gap extends beyond housing."],"keywords":["FORTIFIED","Strengthen Alabama Homes","retrofit grants","insurance claims reduction","Hurricane Sally","roof standards","avoided losses","state insurance regulation","resilience incentives"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":533,"title":"Physical Climate Risk in Asset Management","authors":"Michele Azzone, Matteo Ghesini, Davide Stocco, Lorenzo Viola","date":"2025-10-15","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Politecnico di Milano","Institut Polytechnique de Paris","Arca Fondi SGR"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.19307","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A model for putting physical climate risk into asset management decisions, built by extending the Vasicek multivariate firm value model with downward jumps representing extreme climate events. Jump frequency and size depend on a firm's geographic exposure and on how much of its revenue is linked to physical assets, calibrated from the publicly available Notre Dame Global Adaptation Initiative country index and balance sheet data. The model produces expected and unexpected portfolio losses and a Value-at-Risk add-on for a climate-stressed scenario. The findings are that expected losses increase over time with pronounced differences across sectoral indices, and the policy implication drawn is that additional capital buffers are needed to offset physical climate losses, particularly in asset-intensive sectors.","insights":["The model's design choice is to extend machinery institutions already run rather than replace it: a modification of Vasicek, which is itself the multivariate generalisation of the Merton firm value model, so physical risk enters through an existing credit framework rather than a parallel climate model.","Calibration is deliberately cheap and reproducible, using the publicly available ND-GAIN country index plus balance sheet data covering most listed stocks worldwide, which the authors present as enabling low-cost implementation by financial institutions.","Physical risk is modelled as a jump rather than as added volatility, which is what lets it hit firm asset value discontinuously and then transmit to equity priced as a call option on that value with debt as the strike.","The transmission chain is stated explicitly: physical risk hits tangible assets and operating costs such as relocation and insurance, which lowers profits and repayment capacity, which reaches listed stock prices.","The output is a Value-at-Risk add-on for a climate-stressed scenario evaluated across time horizons, which is the form a supervisor or a risk committee can act on rather than a scenario narrative.","The authors claim this is the first model in the literature that is multivariate, applies at portfolio level, and can be calibrated for any stock portfolio from widely available data, and they note the framework accepts other loss definitions such as those computed for Mexican companies or from private data providers."],"keywords":["firm value model","jump process","Value-at-Risk add-on","ND-GAIN index","capital buffers","physical climate risk"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":127,"title":"Municipal Resilience Bonds for Earthquake Risk Reduction: A Financing Model for Istanbul\u2019s Infrastructure","authors":"Yang Song, Francesca Medda","date":"2025-10-15","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["Global","Europe"],"institutions":["University College London"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10217996/7/Medda_Municipal_resilience_bonds_for_earthquak.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource presents a comprehensive analysis of Municipal Resilience Bonds (MRBs) as a financing model aimed at enhancing Istanbul's infrastructure to mitigate earthquake risks. It explores innovative financial mechanisms that facilitate investment in urban resilience, emphasizing the need for collaboration between public and private sectors, data-driven assessments, and community engagement to ensure successful implementation and sustainable urban development.","insights":["Municipal resilience bonds could serve as a new financing tool for urban earthquake preparedness.","Collaboration between public and private sectors is essential for successful implementation of resilience bonds.","Investing in resilience now can save costs related to disaster recovery in the future.","Public awareness and engagement will be crucial for the acceptance of resilience bonds among stakeholders.","The model can be replicated in other urban areas facing similar earthquake risks."],"keywords":["infrastructure","urban resilience","financial instruments","public-private partnerships","disaster risk reduction"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":192,"title":"11 Trends Set to Shape Sustainable Debt in 2026","authors":"Annabelle Palmer","date":"2025-10-11","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Environmental Finance"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.environmental-finance.com/content/analysis/11-market-trends-set-to-shape-sustainable-debt-in-2026.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Environmental Finance article previews the agenda for the Sustainable Debt EMEA Conference 2026, synthesizing market participant views on eleven themes shaping sustainable debt markets. It covers transition finance frameworks, the mainstreaming of adaptation and resilience finance, labelled debt value propositions, blue bonds, nature-based finance, EU Green Bond Standard uptake, and emerging market growth. The piece signals that physical climate risk pricing is increasingly embedded in both labelled and vanilla fixed income, and that resilience is becoming as central to investor analysis as decarbonisation.","insights":["Physical climate risk pricing is moving into mainstream fixed income: investors are scrutinizing how physical risk is priced into issuances across labelled and vanilla bonds, signaling resilience is becoming as analytically central as decarbonisation.","Adaptation finance is transitioning from niche to mainstream, sovereigns are actively seeking to embed adaptation components into debt strategies to fund municipal and resilient infrastructure, though corporates are still lagging.","The EU Green Bond Standard (EUGBS) came into force at end of 2024 but uptake remains limited; issuers cite added burden and some investors feel the market has moved on, a key test of whether regulatory rigor can drive adoption without stifling innovation.","Nature-based finance faces a fundamental bankability gap: projects like reforestation, mangrove restoration, and flood resilience deliver high environmental impact but limited direct financial returns, requiring concessional or blended finance to be structured at scale.","Blue bonds, validated by examples such as Tideway's blue bond, are evolving from novelty to mainstream; water finance is identified as the most tangible near-term entry point for scaling nature-related instruments and risk disclosures.","Sustainability-linked instruments have stalled due to credibility concerns but cautious optimism exists around a reset, particularly in sovereign issuance and potential linkage to Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs).","Emerging markets are identified as the clearest growth frontier for sustainable debt, with sovereign sustainability-linked bonds, blended finance, and MDB-supported structures seen as key unlock mechanisms, but data gaps, liquidity, and deal size remain barriers."],"keywords":["transition finance","labelled debt","blue bonds","nature-based finance","EU Green Bond Standard","sustainability-linked bonds","emerging markets debt"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":110,"title":"Tokyo Metropolitan Government to Issue World\u2019s First Climate Bond Certified Using the Resilience Criteria and Taxonomy","authors":"","date":"2025-10-10","year":2025,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Bonds Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatebonds.net/news-events/press-room/press-releases/tokyo-metropolitan-government-issues-worlds-first-climate-bonds-certified-using-resilience-taxonomy","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Tokyo Metropolitan Government (TMG) is set to issue the world's first Climate Bond certified under the Climate Bonds Resilience Criteria and Taxonomy, marking a significant milestone in adaptation and resilience finance. The bond aims to fund various projects that bolster Tokyo's resilience against climate-related risks, impacting the lives of 14 million residents and establishing a precedent for resilience-focused investment globally.","insights":["The TOKYO Resilience Bond is the first bond globally to receive certification under the Climate Bonds Resilience Criteria and Taxonomy.","Proceeds from the bond will finance crucial infrastructure projects to improve flood and storm resilience in Tokyo.","The introduction of the Resilience Taxonomy expands investment opportunities within the Climate Bonds Certification framework.","TMG aims to protect its 14 million residents from severe climate risks like flooding and typhoons through innovative financial strategies.","The certification highlights a shift towards prioritizing adaptation and resilience in sustainable finance.","Such initiatives reflect a growing global urgency to mitigate the impacts of climate change.","This move positions Tokyo as a leader in climate adaptation finance, potentially influencing other cities worldwide.","The bond's independent verification by Rating and Investment Information, Inc. ensures its credibility and market confidence.","This initiative is part of TMG's broader commitment to sustainable development and urban resilience."],"keywords":["Tokyo","sustainable finance","investment","infrastructure","disaster management"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":416,"title":"How Does Extreme Weather Impact Insurance? [Episode 9]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Carolyn Kousky (guest)","date":"2025-10-06","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Environmental Defense Fund","United States Department of the Treasury"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kq9Atw7cFXk","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Carolyn Kousky, acting Chief Economist at the Environmental Defense Fund and founder of Insurance for Good, sets out the state of the US homeowners market and what to do about it. The figures given are that rates rose 10.4 percent on average in 2024 on top of 12.7 percent the year before, that there are roughly 620,000 non-renewals a year according to the US Treasury, that about 2 percent of California homeowner policies received non-renewal notices with Lake County near 8 percent, and that recent estimates put 11 million households without insurance. She argues the price rise is not only hazard: pandemic supply chain and labour disruption and the inflation that followed raised rebuilding costs everywhere, not only in known hotspots. Her framing question is whether insurance is a market product or a benefit households and communities deserve, because the answer determines which policy interventions are legitimate. She proposes baseline coverage standards so that shopping on price is not unknowingly shopping on coverage, describes community-level products that would pay a fixed sum quickly for non-property losses such as evacuation and commuting costs, and notes that going uninsured is concentrated among those without a mortgage, since lenders will not permit it.","insights":["Most resilience investment cannot be repaid by premium savings: risk is collective, depending on neighbours and on how surrounding natural lands are managed, so the avoided losses and co-benefits accrue to the community while the premium reduction accrues to one policyholder. The financing question is how to fund work that is economically sensible but not privately recoverable.","Going without insurance is structurally concentrated among households with no mortgage, because lenders will not allow it, which means the uninsured population is shaped by lender requirements rather than by risk appetite.","The price increase is substantially a rebuilding-cost story, from supply chain, labour and inflation, which is why it showed up across the country rather than only in known hotspots.","Baseline coverage standards are proposed because homeowner policies differ materially and a consumer shopping on price is unknowingly shopping on coverage, which surfaces only after a loss.","A community-level product paying a fixed sum immediately for non-property losses, such as evacuation or higher commuting costs, is offered as both faster and less contentious than negotiating an indemnity claim."],"keywords":["homeowners insurance","non-renewal","protection gap","uninsured households","baseline coverage standards","parametric insurance","flood insurance","affordability","community insurance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":532,"title":"Innovative Financial Instruments for the Mobilization of Private Sector Investment in Climate Change Mitigation and Adaptation in Developing Countries","authors":"Deborah Murphy, Maribel Hernandez, Edoardo Carlucci, David Kramer, Philip Gass, Anas Rahman, David Uzsoki","date":"2025-10-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["International Institute for Sustainable Development","Deutsche Gesellschaft fur Internationale Zusammenarbeit"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.iisd.org/publications/report/financial-instruments-investment-climate-change","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An analysis of eight innovative financial instruments for mobilising private sector finance for climate adaptation and mitigation in developing countries, with options for donors and development partners on using public finance to increase their use. The eight are the Adaptation Benefit Mechanism, biodiversity credits, carbon taxes, carbon trading mechanisms, credit guarantees, debt-for-nature and climate resilience swaps, pooled investment funds, and sovereign green bonds and sovereign sustainability-linked bonds. The report assesses each for scalability and replicability and concludes that the determining factors are ease of implementation, supportive legal and regulatory frameworks, and institutional knowledge.","insights":["The instruments are argued to compound rather than compete: credit guarantees can facilitate green bond issuances, pooled investment funds and debt-for-nature and climate resilience swaps, so the report's recommendation is combination rather than selection.","The common limit named across all eight is not capital but capacity: the time and resource-intensive nature of design and implementation, lack of knowledge to use the instruments, and complex operating environments.","A strength claimed for these instruments beyond their financial effect is signalling: a developing country government's use of one indicates commitment to climate action and willingness to use scarce public funds in innovative ways, which is a reputational rather than a cash mechanism.","Least developed countries are singled out as often lacking the underlying conditions, with limited financial and capital market development, which means the instruments are least available where the need is greatest.","Robust project pipelines are named as a precondition specifically for green bonds, pooled investment funds and carbon trading, so pipeline development is prior to instrument choice rather than a consequence of it.","Transparency is treated as a necessary condition rather than a desirable one, requiring guidance and taxonomies plus the capacity to monitor and report on both instrument use and climate outcomes."],"keywords":["Adaptation Benefit Mechanism","credit guarantees","debt-for-nature swap","sovereign sustainability-linked bonds","biodiversity credits","pooled investment funds"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":211,"title":"Building a $300MM+ Pipeline for Natural Climate Solutions Projects","authors":"","date":"2025-10-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/rfi-natural-climate-solutions-case-study","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This resource outlines the efforts of Quantified Ventures (QV) to build a $300 million-plus pipeline for investable natural climate solutions projects, particularly in forestry and wood products, by sourcing and vetting over 120 potential projects and advancing seven into active investor engagement despite challenges in financing pathways.","insights":["QV identified and reviewed more than 120 natural climate solutions projects.","The initiative was funded by the U.S Endowment for Forestry and Communities.","A structured due diligence process was crucial in advancing investment-ready projects.","The priority was given to projects showing scalable potential in emissions reduction.","Challenges included a lack of structured investment opportunities and uncertain federal funding.","Direct engagement with green banks in multiple states expanded the investor pool.","Standardized data rooms and financial models enhanced project readiness.","There is a documented need for early-stage development equity and technical assistance.","The model established by QV can adapt to future project developments."],"keywords":["natural climate solutions","investment readiness","project feasibility","sustainable forestry","financial modeling"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":604,"title":"Climate resilience technology: An inflection point for new investment","authors":"McKinsey & Company","date":"2025-09-29","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["McKinsey & Company"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/sustainability/our-insights/climate-resilience-technology-an-inflection-point-for-new-investment","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A McKinsey article of 29 September 2025 arguing that demand for technologies supporting climate resilience and adaptation could create a $1 trillion opportunity for private capital by 2030. It sets out the loss backdrop: in 2024 the United States had 27 billion-dollar climate disasters, three times the annual average of the previous 44 years, and in the first half of 2025 alone the world saw more than $162 billion in economic losses from climate catastrophes. It attributes the scale of that damage to the growth of economic exposure in high-climate-risk areas, the changing climate itself, and the limitations of prior investments in resilience and adaptation. What it identifies as new is not the science but shifts in market behaviour, an increased focus by corporate customers and consumers on building resilience and managing physical climate risk, and it expects those market signals to intensify.","insights":["**Three times the 44-year average in a single year** is the exposure statistic the article leads with, and it attributes the rise first to the growth of economic exposure in high-risk areas rather than to the hazard itself.","Naming the limitations of prior resilience investment as one of three drivers is unusually candid for a market-sizing piece: it concedes that money has already been spent without commensurate effect, which is the question the Resilience Dividend Valuation Model exists to answer.","A $1 trillion technology market by 2030 is a demand-side sizing: it counts what buyers would pay for resilience products, which is a different quantity from what the avoided loss is worth.","The article's central claim is that what has changed is market behaviour rather than the science, which puts the inflection point in insurance pricing and capital allocation rather than in new physical evidence."],"keywords":["climate resilience technology","adaptation market","billion dollar disasters","physical climate risk","private capital","market signals","economic exposure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":415,"title":"How Can We Reduce the Risk from Wildfire? [Episode 8]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Lori Moore-Merrell (guest)","date":"2025-09-29","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","United States Fire Administration"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7_i-OUeoN-4","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Lori Moore-Merrell, former United States Fire Administrator, argues that the fire problem has to be addressed through the existing building stock rather than through new construction, because there is already enough housing in high-risk areas to determine the risk profile whatever future codes require. She traces the US Fire Administration to the 1974 Fire Prevention and Control Act and the America Burning report that preceded it, written when fire was killing up to 12,000 people a year. Her central proposal is a property-level risk index built from variables including structural separation, roof material, siding material and occupancy, applied across a whole neighbourhood because a home that is not hardened becomes fuel that ignites its neighbours. She is explicit about the intended use: the index would be read by insurers to decide insurability and set premiums. She names the obstacles honestly, that building materials have not been tracked over time and codes have often not been enforced or adopted, and she argues the third leg of the solution, after modelling and policy adoption, is insurers using their leverage to get action without withdrawing from the market.","insights":["The proposed risk index is designed for an insurance use case from the outset: its purpose is to let an insurer see what mitigation a property has done and price it, which is the missing link between household action and premium.","Risk is treated as a neighbourhood property rather than a parcel property, because an unhardened home becomes fuel for its neighbours, so the index would be applied across all homes where structural separation is short.","The data problem is historical and unglamorous: building materials have not been tracked over time and codes were often not adopted or enforced, so the record needed to price mitigation does not exist yet.","The instruction to insurers is two-sided, to use their leverage to compel action but not to walk away from the market, which names the tension between underwriting discipline and availability.","Existing stock, not new construction, is identified as the determinant of the risk profile, which reframes codes as insufficient on their own and retrofit as the main lever."],"keywords":["wildfire risk index","home hardening","building codes","insurance premiums","US Fire Administration","structural separation","community risk reduction","fast fire","lithium-ion batteries"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":492,"title":"Cost-Benefit Analysis of Electricity Resilience Projects: State of the Art and Gap Analysis","authors":"Nina J. Hebel, Benjamin D. Leibowicz, Juan Pablo Carvallo","date":"2025-09-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Texas at Austin","Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40518-025-00278-5","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A review of cost-benefit analysis for electricity resilience projects, motivated by the fact that utilities and regulators must weigh resilience benefits against costs that are passed to customers. The authors distinguish reliability, short-duration routine interruptions with localised impact, from resilience, widespread higher-impact events that occur less frequently, and note that reliability standards exist while resilience standards generally do not. Resilience enhancements are classified as planning-based, including undergrounding transmission and distribution lines, stronger poles and elevating or relocating flood-prone substations, or operation-based, including network reconfiguration, microgrid islanding, mobile energy resources and load restoration. Operational measures can be cheap; undergrounding requires large upfront capital that raises rates. The paper proposes a CBA framework of five elements, risks, physical impacts, power interruptions, economic impacts and resilience projects, and reviews methods for each. Costs are straightforward to estimate; benefits, particularly avoided interruption costs, are not, and practitioners commonly perceive current CBA tools as insufficiently mature for real-world practice. Value of lost load estimates come mostly from surveys, with Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory's ICE Calculator the main source, but revealed-preference approaches exist using interruptible service programme participation, backup generator purchases and home battery adoption following California's Public Safety Power Shutoffs. Direct VOLL application is only valid for shorter interruptions, because long-duration outages produce indirect spillovers that survey responses do not capture; the literature proposes input-output, computable general equilibrium and macro-econometric regional models instead, and a hybrid approach that uses interruption costs.","insights":["The reason grid resilience is under-invested is located precisely: the costs are easy to estimate and the benefits are not, so a regulator facing a rate increase sees a certain cost against a contested benefit, and rejects it.","Practitioners' own view that CBA tools are not mature enough for real-world use is the operative finding, because it means the barrier is tool credibility rather than the absence of a method.","Value of lost load is the entire benefit side and it is mostly survey-based, which is the weakest possible foundation for a rate case; the revealed-preference alternatives, backup generator purchases and battery adoption after Public Safety Power Shutoffs, are what would make the number defensible.","VOLL is explicitly invalid for the long, widespread outages that resilience investment exists to prevent, so the standard benefit metric does not apply to the standard use case, and the CGE hybrid is the proposed bridge.","The multiple-benefit-stream gap is the same problem the triple dividend literature names: a project that delivers resilience, reliability and operating cost reductions has no accepted method for adding them up without double counting, so it is appraised on one stream and underfunded."],"keywords":["value of lost load","ICE Calculator","grid hardening","undergrounding","microgrid","cost-benefit analysis","computable general equilibrium","power interruption"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":438,"title":"The Market Value of Natural Capital: Evidence from Wetland Changes and Immobile Assets","authors":"Claudio Rizzi; Ryan Lewis","date":"2025-09-25","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["IESE Business School","University of Colorado Boulder"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4038371","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Rizzi and Lewis use geographic variation in exposure to wetland change to build a market-based measure of natural capital, and find that wetland loss reaches local asset markets through two distinct channels. The first is flood mitigation: after floods, municipal bond yields spike in areas with substantial upstream wetland destruction. The second is water quality: upland wetland loss increases downstream water pollution and so raises remediation costs for municipalities. They also estimate that a loss of 100 hectares of upstream wetlands reduces the prices of flood-exposed properties by $5,208. The paper positions these as market estimates of the cost of human-driven natural capital destruction, which the authors argue is an important parameter for understanding the benefits of climate and environmental policy. The framing literature is the natural capital accounting tradition, from Costanza and colleagues' $33 trillion a year estimate in 1997 and $125 trillion update in 2014, to Dasgupta's observation that produced capital per person doubled between 1992 and 2014 while natural capital per person fell by nearly 40 per cent.","insights":["The contribution is a price rather than a valuation: wetland loss shows up in municipal bond yields after floods and in flood-exposed property prices, so natural capital destruction is measured by what markets charge rather than by modelled ecosystem service value.","Two channels are separated, flood mitigation and water pollution remediation cost, and the second is a municipal operating expense rather than a disaster loss, which widens the fiscal case for wetland protection beyond flood events.","A loss of 100 hectares of upstream wetlands reduces flood-exposed property prices by $5,208, which is a per-hectare number a mitigation banking or payment-for-services scheme could be priced against.","The bond yield effect appears after floods rather than continuously, so the market prices the protective loss when it is realised, which is the same lag that makes pre-disaster investment hard to finance.","The natural capital backdrop is stark and cited rather than asserted: produced capital per person doubled from 1992 to 2014 while natural capital per person fell nearly 40 per cent, per Dasgupta."],"keywords":["natural capital","wetlands","municipal bond yields","flood mitigation","water pollution","property prices","ecosystem services","market-based valuation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":133,"title":"Miami Cat-5 Storm Would Test Economy and Insurance Market Even with Federal Aid","authors":"Denise Rappmund, Valentina Gomez, Hetty Chang, Thomas Jacobs, Timothy Blake","date":"2025-09-24","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Moody's"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://www.moodys.com/research/US-Public-Finance-Florida-Miami-Cat-5-storm-would-test-economy-Sector-In-Depth--PBC_1458001","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Moody's Ratings sector-in-depth report (September 2025) models the fiscal, insurance, and economic consequences of a hypothetical Category 5 hurricane making direct landfall near Homestead, FL, 40 miles south of downtown Miami. Total modeled statewide losses reach $232 billion, exceeding inflation-adjusted Hurricane Katrina losses by 15%. The analysis examines how Florida's public insurance entities (FHCF, Citizens, FIGA), state reserves of $14.2 billion, and federal aid exceeding $140 billion would collectively fund recovery, while concluding that the southeast Florida economy would remain durably weaker due to uninsured losses and higher long-term insurance costs.","insights":["A Cat 5 direct hit on Miami-Dade would produce $232 billion in statewide modeled losses, $212 billion to building stock in the Tri-County area alone, making it the costliest U.S. natural disaster in history, 15% above inflation-adjusted Hurricane Katrina losses.","Federal aid could exceed $140 billion (>50% of Tri-County economic losses), but even at a haircut to 75% reimbursement for public assistance (vs. typical 90\u2013100%), Florida would face an additional ~$5 billion unreimbursed cost, roughly equal to its $4.9 billion rainy day fund.","Insured losses for the Tri-County region are estimated at $114 billion, while total uninsured losses reach ~$98 billion in southeast Florida; after federal individual assistance, roughly $30 billion would remain entirely uncovered by property owners, a direct balance-sheet hit to households and businesses.","Florida's public insurance framework would require ~$17.8 billion in total new debt: $7.05 billion in post-event assessment-backed bonds to pay FHCF reinsurance claims, $7.5 billion in pre-event debt to recapitalize FHCF and Citizens, plus restructuring of $3.25 billion in existing pre-event debt, all issued in tranches over 2026\u20132029.","Assessment-backed debt levied on Florida's $92 billion direct written premium base would raise typical homeowner/auto combined premiums by ~$150/year (1.5% assessment), adding to already-elevated insurance costs and suppressing long-run housing affordability and regional GDP.","The Moody's Analytics macroeconomic model projects a steep near-term GDP drop followed by rapid rebound, but southeast Florida remains permanently below its pre-storm baseline due to population loss, reduced labor force, and structurally higher insurance/housing costs."],"keywords":["hurricane scenario analysis","public insurance entities","assessment-backed debt","Florida insurance market","catastrophe modeling","uninsured losses","FHCF","Citizens Property Insurance"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":472,"title":"RMC Communities Secure over $4 Million in Municipal Vulnerability Preparedness Grants for Climate Resilience","authors":"","date":"2025-09-23","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Mystic River Watershed Association"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://mysticriver.org/news/2025-9-23/resilient-mystic-collaborative-communities-secure-over-5-million-in-municipal-vulnerability-preparedness-grants-for-climate-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Massachusetts Governor Healey's announcement of $28.7 million in FY2026 Municipal Vulnerability Preparedness grants included just over $4 million for Resilient Mystic Collaborative communities. The RMC, convened in September 2018 by ten Mystic watershed communities and the Mystic River Watershed Association and now led by senior staff from twenty cities and towns plus state, regional and non-governmental partners, has secured over $160 million in state, federal and foundation funding since it began, a total that includes federal grants later canceled by the Trump Administration: $50 million for the Island End River Flood Resilience Project in Chelsea and Everett, and roughly $1 million for extreme heat and health initiatives. The FY2026 awards are $2,451,500 to Woburn for a heat-resilient nature park at Hurld Park with an accessible playground, splash pad, shade trees and green stormwater infrastructure, complementing a $3 million federal earmark for a stream, wetland and floodplain area; $1,367,300 to Somerville for urban heat resilience at Blessing of the Bay Park, replacing asphalt with a shaded greenway using trees, tree trenches and native plants; and $313,600 to Chelsea and the North Suffolk Office of Resilience and Sustainability for resilient zoning and design recommendations addressing stormwater flooding, coastal flooding and urban heat. Against $8,116,737 requested, $4,130,900 was awarded, 51 per cent of funding and 27 per cent of projects.","insights":["The award rate is the fact worth keeping: 51 per cent of requested dollars but only 27 per cent of projects, so the programme funded the larger applications and rejected proportionally more of the small ones.","A voluntary collaborative of twenty municipalities has assembled $160 million across state, federal and foundation sources since 2018, which is evidence that the binding constraint on small-municipality resilience funding is application capacity rather than eligibility.","Federal cancellation removed $51 million from that cumulative total, including a single $50 million flood resilience project, so one policy reversal is worth roughly a third of what the collaborative raised in seven years.","The Woburn award stacks a $2.45 million state grant onto a $3 million federal earmark on one site, which is how a small city assembles a project budget from separate programmes with separate scopes.","The Chelsea award funds zoning and design recommendations rather than construction, which buys the regulatory condition that makes private development carry part of the stormwater and heat burden."],"keywords":["Municipal Vulnerability Preparedness","Resilient Mystic Collaborative","urban heat","green stormwater infrastructure","resilient zoning","grant stacking","award rate"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":129,"title":"Catastrophic Events in an Uncertain Future: A Pending $41 Trillion Bill for Businesses and Governments to Resolve","authors":"Viola Lutz, Chris Lafakis, Kamile Rudaviclute, Jake Carr, Richard Loeser, Caroline Binkley, Elisa Loy, Isabel Llorente Garcia","date":"2025-09-23","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Moody's"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.moodys.com/web/en/us/insights/physical-transition-risk/catastrophic-events-in-an-uncertain-future-a-pending-41-trillion-bill-for-businesses-and-governments-to-resolve.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The analysis by Moody's indicates that by 2050, the global economic impact of physical risks from catastrophic climate events could reach $41.4 trillion, equating to a 14.5% loss in global GDP, with two-thirds of these losses stemming from chronic issues like sea level rise. The paper highlights how businesses and governments must adapt to impending physical risks while outlining the potential credit implications and risks faced by corporate and real estate sectors.","insights":["By 2050, global economic losses from physical risks could total $41.4 trillion, representing a 14.5% GDP loss.","Chronic factors such as sea level rise and productivity loss account for roughly two-thirds of the estimated economic damages.","The US alone may face economic losses amounting to 9.5% of GDP, equating to $6 trillion, by 2050.","Projected annual damage costs from natural disasters in the US could be 26% higher in 2050 than in 2020.","The impact of physical risks may reduce operating margins for US companies by 2.0 percentage points, representing potential damages of $1.5 trillion.","Default probabilities for commercial real estate (CRE) loan portfolios could rise by nearly 18%, with expected losses increasing by 20% or more.","Leaders must rethink their financial strategies to incorporate an understanding of long-term physical risks and their potential economic impacts.","Modeling and analytics of physical risks are crucial for assessing potential credit impacts and risk management across sectors.","Economic fallout from significant natural disasters can have longer-lasting effects than typical recessions, affecting creditworthiness."],"keywords":["natural disasters","economic impact","credit risk","business strategy","financial modeling"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":414,"title":"How Does a Community Rebuild After a Devastating Wildfire? [Episode 7]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Timothy Vordtriede (guest)","date":"2025-09-22","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Altadena Collective"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E--L9NbVrVI","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Timothy Vordtriede, an architect who lost his own home in the January 2025 Eaton Fire, describes the Altadena Collective, founded with two colleagues to give fire-affected homeowners reliable information and collective purchasing power during rebuilding. The scale is stated: the fire burned over 10,000 acres within a day, was not contained for nearly 24 days, killed 19 people and destroyed roughly 9,000 buildings, of which about 6,500 were residential, making it the second most destructive fire in California history. His critique of the default process is that an insurer telling a survivor to go get any bid produces a generic rebuild priced against how claims are evaluated rather than against what was lost. The Collective's interventions are practical and financial: aggregate purchasing of lumber, tile and roofing so contractors can draw on a shared materials network, block-level land surveys in place of one-off surveys costing $4,000 to $5,000 per parcel, a negotiated supplier catalogue that trades the vendor's per-household sales cost for a passed-through discount, and a non-profit owner's representative service offering pro bono bid levelling and construction oversight to those who qualify.","insights":["The cost savings come from changing the unit of transaction from the household to the block: a land survey that costs $4,000 to $5,000 per parcel becomes affordable when blocks are surveyed together, and a shared fence is negotiated across two owners.","A supplier agreed to pass on savings because the Collective removed its per-household acquisition cost, which is a straightforward transfer of the aggregator's value to the survivors rather than a subsidy.","The insurance claims process is identified as the thing shaping what gets rebuilt: told to obtain any bid, a survivor prices a generic structure, so the rebuild tracks claim evaluation rather than the home that was lost or the fire-resilient home that should replace it.","Rebuilding to fire-resilient standards is presented as the point of coordinating materials purchasing, not as an add-on, with the aim of not rebuilding the same tinder boxes.","Debris removal finished in eight months against an expected 12 to 14, so the binding constraint moved to design, permitting and financing faster than the recovery playbook assumed."],"keywords":["Eaton Fire","Altadena","wildfire rebuilding","group purchasing","owner's representative","insurance claims","fire-resilient materials","disaster recovery","collective action"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":206,"title":"Adaptation Can Support Credit Strength, but Faces Race to Keep Up with Climate Risks","authors":"Jennifer Chang, Elise Gilliam, Donovan Hotz, Lucia Lopez, Rahul Ghosh","date":"2025-09-22","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Moody's"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://www.moodys.com/research/doc--PBC_1450816","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Moody's Ratings sector report (September 2025) examines how climate adaptation investment supports credit strength across sovereigns, cities, utilities, and corporates globally. It documents $318 billion in 2024 natural catastrophe economic losses and a 57% global protection gap, and finds that EM adaptation spending ($28B in 2022) is a fraction of the $215B\u2013$387B annual need. The report reviews 170 sovereign NDCs/NAPs, case studies of PG&E, FPL, and SoCal Edison, and highlights innovative instruments including climate resilient debt clauses, debt-for-nature swaps, and sustainable bonds, which now represent ~23% of green and sustainability bond proceeds.","insights":["In 2024, natural catastrophes caused $318B in economic losses and $137B in insured losses, revealing a 57% global protection gap; 2025 insured losses were already $145B by April with a 10% chance of exceeding $300B for the full year (Swiss Re).","Emerging market adaptation spending totaled only $27.5B in 2022 against estimated annual needs of $215B\u2013$387B through 2030, a gap larger than the ~$300B annual capex of leading US data center hyperscalers.","A WRI study estimates each $1 invested in adaptation yields over $10.50 in benefits over 10 years based on avoided losses, economic gains, and social and environmental benefits; the US National Institute of Building Sciences finds natural hazard mitigation saves $6 for every $1 spent.","PG&E, SoCal Edison, and FPL collectively spent over $75B on grid resilience since 2005; PG&E alone invested over $20B in wildfire mitigation since emerging from bankruptcy in 2020, supporting a March 2025 credit upgrade to Ba2/Baa1.","Adaptation- and nature-related use of proceeds reached 23% of green and sustainability bond issuance in 2024; since 2022, over $590B has been issued for adaptation- and nature-related projects, still representing only 22% of total green and sustainability bond issuance.","Global economic losses from natural catastrophes totaled $2 trillion from 2017\u201324, equivalent to total global clean energy investment in 2024 alone, underscoring the scale of unpriced physical risk relative to mitigation capital flows.","NGFS short-term scenarios estimate that compound extreme weather events and policy stagnation over 2025\u201330 could produce GDP declines of 12.5% in Africa, 7% in South America, 4.1% in Asia, and 4.8% in North America and the EU in shock years."],"keywords":["protection gap","sovereign credit ratings","NDC adaptation planning","insured losses","physical climate risk","nature-based solutions","emerging market finance"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":434,"title":"Rethinking Our Assumptions and Financing Tools for Community Resilience in the Face of Growing Climate Loss and Risk","authors":"Matt Posner; Xavier de Souza Briggs","date":"2025-09-15","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Brookings Institution","Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/rethinking-our-assumptions-and-financing-tools-for-community-resilience-in-the-face-of-growing-climate-loss-and-risk/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Posner and Briggs argue that the United States has made real progress on adaptation awareness, planning and engineering, and almost none on how it pays for resilience. Their diagnosis has two halves. Local public finance is still dominated by a tax-and-bond model which they call essential but insufficient. And the prevailing federal grant approach, which frames resilience capital as free money awarded after a disaster, has created unrealistic expectations and actively discouraged proactive, locally driven investment. Their prescription is to reorient public finance around three principles: shared risk, shared value drawn especially from avoided loss and other resilience dividends, and new pricing. They argue this is not speculative, pointing to pioneering examples across a range of states and localities as proof the approach can work and scale, and they frame the goal as infrastructure that reduces loss, protects the insurability of property and preserves affordability, three outcomes they treat as one problem rather than three.","insights":["The federal grant model is identified as an active disincentive rather than merely insufficient: framing resilience capital as free money awarded after disaster strikes has discouraged proactive local investment, which means more grant money would not fix the behaviour.","Insurability is named as one of the three things resilient infrastructure protects, alongside loss reduction and affordability, which ties municipal capital spending directly to whether private cover remains available.","The three principles offered, shared risk, shared value from avoided loss, and new pricing, are each mechanisms for capturing the resilience dividend rather than for raising new capital.","The authors insist local financial innovation must match the scale and urgency, and the fictional Mayor Joe narrative is used to show how a city can secure every available external dollar and still have no durable funding base."],"keywords":["public finance","tax-and-bond","resilience dividend","avoided loss","shared risk","new pricing","BRIC","local innovation","insurability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":228,"title":"Bridging the Gap in Disaster Risk Financing Through Integrating Physical and Financial Resilience: Disaster and Climate Adaptation Financing Framework","authors":"","date":"2025-09-13","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery (GFDRR)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/43803","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Bridging the Gap in Disaster Risk Financing through Integrating Physical and Financial Resilience' resource provides an in-depth analysis of the Disaster and Climate Adaptation Financing Framework (DCAFF) developed by the World Bank. It aims to enhance resilience in developing countries susceptible to natural disasters by integrating physical risk reduction strategies with financial readiness, highlighting the significance of strong institutional foundations, tailored financial instruments, and collaborative partnerships to create sustainable disaster resilience and mitigate economic losses.","insights":["Over 7,200 disasters have led to significant economic losses and fatalities in developing countries, necessitating improved disaster readiness.","DCAFF emphasizes the integration of financial instruments within supportive ecosystems, involving policy alignment and private sector engagement.","Robust risk management and data-driven analytics are essential for informed decision-making and the effectiveness of disaster risk financing.","Proactive frameworks like DCAFF can improve public spending efficiency and attract private investments for disaster-related resilience.","Successful disaster resilience initiatives require multi-stakeholder collaboration, strong policy frameworks, and customized financial products tailored to beneficiaries' needs."],"keywords":["disaster risk management","financial instruments","public-private collaboration","institutional capacity","stakeholder collaboration"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":205,"title":"Wildfire Risk Management and Financial Forecasting: Establishing Effective Strategies amid New Legislation","authors":"Nickolas Alvarado, Cedric Chamberland, Mary Daly","date":"2025-09-10","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Oliver Wyman"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.oliverwyman.com/our-expertise/insights/2025/oct/wildfire-risk-management-financial-forecasting.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt discusses the rising wildfire risk in the U.S., largely driven by human activity, including electrical power generation. It emphasizes the importance of public utilities adapting to new legislation that modifies their liability concerning wildfires. Actuarial services and risk management strategies are highlighted as essential tools for utilities to forecast potential liabilities and maintain financial stability amid increasing wildfire threats.","insights":["85% of wildfires in the U.S. are caused by human activity, with significant contributions from electrical power generation.","Recent legislation in various states aims to modify the liability of public utilities regarding wildfire risks and damage.","California's new laws impose strict liability on utilities for wildfire damage caused by their equipment, even without negligence.","Utilities are increasingly required to create and submit wildfire risk mitigation plans to reduce their liability.","Actuarial methods can help utilities estimate wildfire risk and potential liabilities through simulation and assessment models.","There is a need for multidisciplinary teams to manage wildfire risk effectively, incorporating expertise from various fields.","Funding models established in state legislation may include investor funding, customer payments, or self-insurance.","Future wildfire risks are expected to escalate due to climate change, necessitating adaptive risk management strategies.","Supportive legislative frameworks provide utilities with guidelines that can help mitigate liability if they follow approved risk mitigation practices."],"keywords":["wildfire risk","public utilities","risk management","actuarial services","legislation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":412,"title":"How Can Targeted Information Lead to Action and Effectively Mitigate the Risk from Wildfire? [Episode 5]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Dave Winnacker (guest)","date":"2025-09-08","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","XyloPlan","Marin Wildfire Prevention Authority","Hoover Institution","United States Marine Corps"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vVuotry3pek","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Dave Winnacker, co-founder of XyloPlan and a fire-service veteran, argues that wildfire loss is not inevitable even though wildfire is, and that the binding constraint is action rather than knowledge. His technical claim is that wildfire risk modelling inherited its metric from timber loss, which is driven by fire intensity, while communities are destroyed by fire speed, and that most structure loss occurs in the first or second operational period of a fast-moving fire. He sells prioritized mitigation reports to homeowners, developers and, since the start of the year, insurers, on the argument that a mitigation the insurance industry cannot see or value has not solved the problem, because insurance is annual while wildfire is abstract. He is critical of activity-based proxies, using Firewise neighbourhood status as the example: forming one signals intent, and it is the work the designation encouraged that should be valued, not the designation. His prioritization argument is that a master plan exceeding what a community can execute is worse than useless, so the unit of work is the neighbourhood or the homeowners association, taken to full mitigation one at a time.","insights":["The metric itself is the argument: wildfire risk models built to price loss of merchantable timber index on fire intensity, while communities burn from fire speed, so a model appropriate to forests is being used to price risk to homes.","A mitigation the insurance industry cannot see or value is treated as not having solved the problem, which makes insurer valuation methodology, not homeowner behaviour, the binding constraint on mitigation finance.","Activity-based proxies are named as the failure mode: Firewise neighbourhood status signals intent and is being valued as though it were risk reduction, when the work it facilitated is what should be valued.","Prioritization is framed as the deliverable rather than the plan: a master plan exceeding a community's capacity is counterproductive, so the question is the minimum number of properties needing treatment and how to cluster them.","The neighbourhood or homeowners association is proposed as the right unit of mitigation, because consensus among 200 homes is achievable where consensus among 20,000 people is not, and partial mitigation everywhere fails on the worst days."],"keywords":["wildfire risk modeling","fire speed","mitigation valuation","insurance underwriting","Firewise","prioritized mitigation","wildland urban interface","risk pricing","home hardening","XyloPlan"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":252,"title":"Finance Solutions for Nature: Pathways to Returns and Outcomes","authors":"","date":"2025-09-04","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum","McKinsey Global Institute","McKinsey & Company"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/publications/finance-solutions-for-nature-pathways-to-returns-and-outcomes/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report 'Finance Solutions for Nature: Pathways to Returns and Outcomes' presents an overview of the developing nature finance sector, urging the implementation of innovative financial mechanisms like sustainability-linked bonds and nature asset companies to draw capital towards nature-positive investments. It outlines ten priority finance solutions aimed at tackling biodiversity and ecosystem challenges while providing a framework for investors to navigate the complexities of this evolving financial landscape.","insights":["Nature finance is complex and fragmented but rapidly emerging as a strategic investment area.","Standardization of metrics and robust reporting is essential for scaling up nature finance solutions effectively.","Innovative financial instruments like sustainability-linked bonds and natural asset companies have significant potential to attract institutional investment.","Debt-for-nature swaps and parametric insurance are crucial mechanisms for redirecting funds toward conservation and enhancing resilience.","Impact funds are growing rapidly as a key source of capital for nature-related projects.","There is a need for collaborative efforts between public and private entities to improve access to nature finance solutions, especially for smaller projects.","Internal Nature Pricing (INP) encourages corporations to invest in nature-positive initiatives by monetizing their negative impacts on natural capital.","The biodiversity finance landscape is expanding, creating diverse investment opportunities focused on environmental outcomes."],"keywords":["biodiversity","natural capital","sustainable investment","ecosystem services","impact investing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":141,"title":"Beyond the Payoff: How Investments in Resilience and Disaster Preparedness Protect Communities","authors":"","date":"2025-09-04","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["U.S. Chamber of Commerce","Allstate","U.S. Chamber of Commerce Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.uschamber.com/security/beyond-the-payoff-how-investments-in-resilience-and-disaster-preparedness-protect-communities","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report 'Beyond the Payoff' underscores the urgent need for investments in disaster resilience and preparedness to protect communities, illustrating that each dollar not spent can lead to economic losses of up to $33. It advocates for strategic, community-focused funding, efficient governance, and collaboration between public and private sectors to enhance disaster readiness and recovery, while also highlighting the substantial long-term savings that can be achieved through proactive investments.","insights":["Investing $1 in disaster preparedness averts up to $33 in future economic losses.","Strategic resilience funding can prevent significant job losses in disaster-prone areas.","High investments in resilience can substantially cut long-term GDP losses.","Effective coordination between public and private sectors is essential to building community resilience.","Proactive community engagement fosters a culture of preparedness.","Using risk data effectively aids in making informed decisions about investments in resilience.","Federal funding distribution for disaster resilience needs to be more efficient and timely."],"keywords":["disaster preparedness","economic impact","community resilience","risk management","funding efficiency"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":556,"title":"Under Pressure, Overdue: The Portfolio Approach and Financing Cities for Resilience","authors":"Sadiq Currimbhoy; Allison Ahern; Nathaniel Echeverria; Lina Liakou; James Neimeister","date":"2025-09-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Resilient Cities Network","Resilient Horizons"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://resilientcitiesnetwork.org/under-pressure-overdue-the-portfolio-approach-and-financing-cities-for-resilience/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Resilient Cities Network report, lead-authored by Sadiq Currimbhoy of Resilient Horizons with four R-Cities contributors, arguing for a portfolio approach to financing city resilience rather than the project-by-project route cities are pushed toward. It follows a March 2024 convening on investing in resilience and was published in September 2025. The report treats a city's resilience needs as a portfolio to be financed as a whole, which changes what an investor is being asked to underwrite and what a city has to be able to show, and connects that to observable market signals including insurance non-renewal rates in the counties most exposed to climate risk.","insights":["The portfolio framing is the argument, not a presentational device: financing a bundle rather than a project changes the diligence burden, the tenor and who can plausibly hold the risk, and it is the structural answer to the fact that individual municipal resilience projects are too small and too slow to attract institutional capital.","R-Cities is registered in New York, Amsterdam and Singapore, and the report is written for cities in all three regions, so its claims should not be read as US-specific even where the evidence is.","The report reaches for insurance market data, non-renewal rates by county exposure as of December 2024, as evidence about CITY FINANCE rather than about insurance, using the insurer's withdrawal as a signal of fiscal exposure."],"keywords":["portfolio approach","city resilience finance","municipal finance","insurance non-renewal","capital mobilization","Resilient Cities Network","urban resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":411,"title":"How Do We Protect Our Homes from the Growing Threat of Wildfires? [Episode 4]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Mark Brown (guest)","date":"2025-09-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Marin Wildfire Prevention Authority","Marin County Fire Department","CAL FIRE","California FAIR Plan","California Department of Insurance","Los Padres National Forest"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lHcQ0YCaPwY","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Mark Brown, executive officer of the Marin Wildfire Prevention Authority and formerly a deputy fire chief with 30 years at Marin County Fire, makes the case for shifting effort from suppression to pre-fire work, on the grounds that firefighters start every fire behind. His subject is Zone Zero, the combustible-free zone within five feet of a home, and the problem he is solving is adoption at community scale: the risk one household carries depends on what its neighbours do, and homeowners facing a long inspection list do nothing. The structural approach he describes aims to create a market for Zone Zero services where none exists. On finance, his stated first objective is insurance availability rather than price, and he reports that parts of Marin have seen a 200 to 300 percent increase in homes covered by the California FAIR Plan, with one community at roughly 27 percent of homes on FAIR Plan policies, which cover wildfire only and so require a wraparound policy for everything else.","insights":["Availability is named as the problem to solve before price: the objective is to restore insurability first and discuss premium reduction second, which reverses the usual framing of mitigation as a route to cheaper cover.","The residual market is carrying the shift in measurable terms: a 200 to 300 percent rise in FAIR Plan coverage in parts of Marin, and roughly 27 percent of homes in one community on FAIR Plan policies, which cover wildfire alone and require a second wraparound policy.","Avoided-loss evidence is offered from the 2017 Thomas Fire at Montecito, where prior fuel breaks and home hardening accompanied 1,300 homes impacted and 12 structures destroyed, and where one crew could do the work that would otherwise need four.","Wildfire risk is treated as a collective good rather than a private one, since a hardened home reduces its neighbours' risk, which is the argument for a community-scale intervention rather than a per-household subsidy.","A single-mission public authority funded solely for wildfire mitigation is put forward as the institutional innovation, on the argument that a general fire department can only give mitigation a fraction of its attention."],"keywords":["Zone Zero","home hardening","defensible space","wildland urban interface","California FAIR Plan","insurance availability","wildfire mitigation","residual insurance market","avoided losses","special district"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":103,"title":"Global Landscape of Climate Finance 2025","authors":"Baysa Naran, Varun Shankar, Pedro de Arag\u00e3o Fernandes, James Dixon, Joe Burnett, Sasha Abraham, Sean Stout, Jake Connolly, Costanza Strinati","date":"2025-08-26","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/global-landscape-of-climate-finance-2025/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Global Landscape of Climate Finance 2025 report reveals significant growth in global climate finance, which reached USD 1.9 trillion in 2023, driven largely by private sector contributions and addressing emerging economies' financing disparities. With a growing emphasis on innovative financing solutions for adaptation and resilience, the report underscores the necessity for enhanced collaboration and transparency in funding mechanisms to meet net-zero goals and emphasizes the need for substantial investments across various sectors, particularly clean energy and agriculture, to effectively tackle climate challenges.","insights":["Private climate finance contributions surpassed USD 1 trillion for the first time in 2023.","Mitigation finance dominated global climate flows with USD 1,780 billion allocated in 2023.","Emerging economies require more catalytic capital to enhance climate finance access.","Investment in clean energy is increasingly driving GDP growth in countries like China.","Despite growth, a substantial gap remains, necessitating USD 6 trillion annually to meet climate goals by 2030.","Adaptation finance tracking faces challenges that may lead to underestimation of actual flows.","Public finance fell by 14%, highlighting the need for increased national development finance contributions.","Collaborative efforts between public and private sectors are critical to mobilize sufficient capital for climate action.","A notable disparity in adaptation finance needs exists, particularly in vulnerable regions like Sub-Saharan Africa."],"keywords":["investment growth","private sector contributions","renewable energy","innovation","resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":410,"title":"How Do We Make Permanent Affordable Housing a Community Asset? [Episode 3]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Ashon Nesbitt (guest)","date":"2025-08-25","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Florida Housing Coalition"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fhGiaS6xWX0","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Ashon Nesbitt, CEO of the Florida Housing Coalition, explains the community land trust as a structural answer to the expiry of affordability: the trust retains the land under a ground lease, removing it permanently from the speculative market, so that public subsidy is not lost when a subsidy term ends. The financing problem he describes is that CLT mortgages are still written as one-off transactions, and his organisation has built standards and a certification so that lenders can underwrite them against known criteria and the resulting mortgages can reach the wider financial system. He then joins that to resilience: because a CLT property is intended to stand in perpetuity, it is worth building to a standard above Florida's already strong code, and he argues the mortgage will follow the insurance, so that if financing becomes unavailable in high-hazard areas property values fall in exactly the communities already devalued. He names the perverse incentive directly, that insurance priced on replacement cost can raise the premium on a more resilient and more expensive building, and asks how the risk reduction can be credited back.","insights":["The load-bearing argument is a pricing one: insurance priced on replacement cost penalises a more resilient, more expensive building, so the risk reduction the owner paid for is not credited. He asks for lower cost of capital or insurance savings to close the gap, and states that those mechanisms do not exist in Florida today.","A lending certification for community land trusts is being used as the bridge from one-off CLT mortgage transactions to standardised products the broader financial system can absorb, which is the same standard-as-market-access move the insurance argument asks for and has not yet won.","The mortgage follows the insurance: if coverage becomes unobtainable, financing follows, and depressed property values land on communities that were already devalued, which makes insurance availability a credit and wealth question rather than only a coverage one.","Permanent affordability and resilience are argued to reinforce each other, because a property held in perpetuity justifies a higher build standard than a property whose affordability term expires.","Florida's building code is described as already exceeding most states, so the live question is incentivising building above code rather than raising the floor."],"keywords":["community land trust","permanent affordability","ground lease","mortgage finance","lending certification","replacement cost insurance","building code","property values","hurricane risk","cost of capital"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":574,"title":"Preparing Regulation for Climate-Resilient Restoration: Learning From Emerging Technologies","authors":"Mary C. Fisher; Marissa L. Baskett; Andrew C. Baker; Ashley Kidd; Michael R. Springborn; Tyler A. Scott","date":"2025-08-22","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of California Davis","University of Miami","National Science Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/conl.13128","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A perspective in Conservation Letters, volume 18 issue 4, first published 22 August 2025, arguing that the gap between the literature on climate-resilient ecosystem restoration and its practice is partly created by regulation. Current paradigms in resource and environmental management, and the regulatory structures attached to them, can unintentionally contribute to the implementation gap. The authors' proposal is to borrow regulatory approaches developed for emerging or disruptive technologies, specifically regulatory greenhouses or sandboxes and risk-weighted regulation, and apply them to restoration interventions that account for climate change. The work was funded by NSF DISES award 2108002, with additional support to one author from the University of California Agricultural Experiment Station.","insights":["Risk-weighted regulation is the transferable idea: it scales the regulatory burden to the expected harm of an intervention rather than treating every novel action as high risk, which is the same logic a lender applies to a borrower.","A regulatory sandbox is a financing question disguised as a permitting one. An intervention that cannot be trialled cannot build the evidence a funder needs, so the regulatory bottleneck and the capital bottleneck are the same bottleneck at different points.","The author list spans population biology, economics and public policy with a University of Miami coral scientist among them, which is why the restoration examples reach beyond terrestrial conservation.","The record was staged with a July date and the journal states 22 August 2025 as first publication."],"keywords":["climate-resilient restoration","regulatory sandbox","risk-weighted regulation","implementation gap","emerging technology regulation","ecosystem restoration","permitting"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":19,"title":"ResilientMass Finance Strategy","authors":"Executive Office of Energy and Environmental Affairs, ResilientMass","date":"2025-08-22","year":2025,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Massachusetts"],"institutions":["Commonwealth of Massachusetts"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.mass.gov/info-details/resilientmass-finance-strategy#what-is-resilientmass","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The ResilientMass Finance Strategy presents a comprehensive framework for Massachusetts to tackle climate change impacts through a whole-of-government approach, requiring an investment range of $90 billion to $130 billion until 2050. It emphasizes proactive investments in critical infrastructure, community engagement, and adaptable financing mechanisms to enhance resilience, streamline project delivery, and ensure equitable access to resources across all communities.","insights":["Massachusetts faces increasing climate-related challenges, requiring an estimated investment of $90 billion to $130 billion for resilience measures by 2050.","Early interventions can significantly reduce costs, with potential savings of $6 to $13 for every dollar invested in resilience.","The strategy promotes community engagement and equitable access to financial resources, especially for disadvantaged populations, during project implementation.","Innovative financing models, including pay-for-performance contracts, are essential for sustainable and effective investment in resilience projects.","A centralized tracking framework is crucial for monitoring project progress and ensuring accountability in resilience investments."],"keywords":["infrastructure","community engagement","funding mechanisms","capacity-building","adaptive governance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":610,"title":"More Resilient by Design: North Carolina\u2019s Flood Resiliency Blueprint","authors":"North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality","date":"2025-08-21","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","North Carolina"],"institutions":["North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://edocs.deq.nc.gov/MitigationServices/DocView.aspx?id=6482&dbid=0&repo=MitigationServices","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"More Resilient by Design: North Carolina's Flood Resiliency Blueprint, the Spring 2025 update from the Department of Environmental Quality's Division of Mitigation Services. The Blueprint exists by statute: in 2021 the General Assembly passed Section 5.9(c) of Session Law 2021-180 directing DEQ's Division of Mitigation Services to develop it, and in 2022 Section 22 of Session Law 2022-75 added guidance on how the Blueprint should use data to support decision making and to reduce the cost and complexity for local governments of developing projects that reduce flood risk. Separately, Session Law 2021-180 Section 40.7(a) as amended by Session Law 2022-43 Section 2(a) appropriated $96,000,000 to DEQ for flood mitigation projects in prioritised river basins, to be used after the initial draft Blueprint was completed.","insights":["**The appropriation is conditioned on the planning artifact existing first**: $96 million for flood mitigation in prioritised river basins, available only once the draft Blueprint was complete. North Carolina's version of that sequence builds one statewide data product rather than funding workshops municipality by municipality.","The legislature's stated aim is to **reduce the cost and complexity for local governments to develop projects**, which writes the project-preparation gap into statute as the thing the money is meant to fix.","Prioritising by river basin rather than by jurisdiction lets the allocation see upstream and downstream together, which a municipality-by-municipality grant programme cannot do.","The document is labelled a Spring 2025 update while the record carries August 2025. The label is the document's own and the discrepancy is recorded rather than resolved, since no precise publication date appears."],"keywords":["Flood Resiliency Blueprint","river basin prioritisation","flood mitigation appropriation","Session Law 2021-180","project development cost","North Carolina","statewide data product"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":132,"title":"What If a Once-In-A Generation Hurricane Were to Hit South Florida This Year?","authors":"Chris Lafakis, Adam Kamins, Theresa Lederer, Jacob van Nieuwkerk","date":"2025-08-21","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Moody's","Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.moodys.com/web/en/us/insights/data-stories/economic-consequences-of-hurricane-hitting-south-florida.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Moody's special report, published 21 August 2025, modelling the macroeconomic consequences of a rare Category 5 hurricane making landfall at Homestead, Florida in 2025, using Moody's RMS catastrophe models and the Moody's Economic Exposure Database. Economic losses along the storm path total $232 billion, with 90 per cent, or $212 billion, concentrated in Miami-Dade, Broward and Palm Beach counties, which together account for 33 per cent of Florida's GDP and between 33 and 46 per cent of state GDP in transportation and warehousing, information, retail and wholesale trade, finance and insurance, professional and business services and real estate. Wind damage to buildings drives the majority of losses, with the remainder from storm surge and precipitation-induced flooding. Both scenarios modelled exceed $200 billion, which would make it the costliest hurricane in US history, surpassing Hurricane Katrina's inflation-adjusted $195 billion. The moderate scenario assumes $144 billion of federal assistance modelled on the Katrina response, under which homeowners insurance premiums rise 60 per cent from Q4 2025 within five years and nearly triple by end-2036. The severe scenario assumes no supplemental federal relief: insurers become insolvent or non-renew widely, premiums rise 200 per cent within five years and quadruple by end-2036, insurance becomes unaffordable and drives outmigration. Miami-Dade gross metro product falls 30 per cent in Q4 2025, far worse than a typical recession, and remains 5 per cent below baseline in the severe scenario. Population falls 7 per cent temporarily under the moderate scenario and nearly 20 per cent under the severe one by 2030. Insurance costs are currently about 6.5 per cent of the cost of owning and operating a home in Miami and rise to 20 per cent by 2030 under the severe scenario, lowering house prices 12 per cent.","insights":["The insurance channel, not the physical damage, does the lasting harm: insurance costs rising from 6.5 to 20 per cent of the cost of owning a home is what lowers house prices 12 per cent and drives the outmigration, so the permanent loss runs through the premium rather than through the wind.","The two scenarios differ only in whether federal aid arrives, and that single variable moves the five-year premium increase from 60 per cent to 200 per cent and the population loss from 7 per cent to nearly 20 per cent. Federal disaster relief is therefore an insurance-market stabiliser as much as a rebuilding fund.","$232 billion of loss with 90 per cent in three counties is the concentration figure any Florida resilience instrument is sized against, and those same three counties carry a third of state GDP, so the fiscal and the physical exposure coincide exactly.","A 30 per cent quarterly decline in Miami-Dade gross metro product is far outside recession experience, which is the report's argument that a Category 5 in Miami is not a large disaster but a different category of event.","Permanently higher insurance costs reduce the labour force and therefore GDP growth indefinitely, so the modelled recovery is incomplete under both scenarios: the economy does not return to its pre-event path."],"keywords":["Category 5 hurricane","Miami-Dade","catastrophe model","insurance premiums","outmigration","house prices","gross metro product","federal disaster relief"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":181,"title":"Managing Climate Risk: Implications for Local Governments","authors":"Susan Crawford, Daevan Mangalmurti","date":"2025-08-20","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Carnegie Endowment for International Peace"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2025/08/managing-climate-risk-implications-for-local-governments?lang=en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource highlights the growing challenges local governments face in managing climate risks due to extreme weather events, emphasizing their critical role in adaptation against fiscal constraints. It calls for comprehensive frameworks prioritizing data transparency, consistent funding for adaptation projects, and equitable investment to protect low-income communities. The need for innovative insurance models and effective governance structures is underscored to ensure communities are prepared for climate impacts while balancing socio-economic equity.","insights":["Local governments are primarily responsible for climate adaptation, affecting their fiscal capacity and tax revenues.","Public trust in local governments is higher than in other levels of government, providing a unique opportunity for local leaders.","High climate risks can result in declining property values, which have negative implications for local tax revenue.","Data transparency and collaboration with insurers are essential for informed risk management and decision-making.","Innovative insurance models, such as parametric insurance, are necessary to address climate risks effectively.","Effective adaptation governance requires clear authority and frameworks to prioritize investment in resilience.","Consistent funding is crucial for long-term adaptation efforts and can be sourced from diverse channels.","Disinvestment strategies may be necessary in chronic threat areas to optimize resource allocation.","Research is urgently needed to better understand climate risk impacts on municipal finance and lower-income communities."],"keywords":["local governance","fiscal capacity","insurance models","investment strategies","community resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":616,"title":"Managing Basis Risks in Weather Parametric Insurance: A Quantitative Study of Diversification and Key Influencing Factors","authors":"Hang Gao; Shuohua Yang; Xinli Liu","date":"2025-08-19","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global","Asia"],"institutions":["China Agricultural Reinsurance Corporation","Geneva Association"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1057/s41288-025-00360-5","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article in The Geneva Papers on Risk and Insurance, volume 51 pages 84 to 111 for 2026, received 16 August 2024, accepted 31 July 2025 and published online 19 August 2025, by Hang Gao of China Agricultural Reinsurance Corporation with Shuohua Yang and Xinli Liu. Weather parametric insurance pays on a weather index rather than a loss assessment, which improves claims efficiency, reduces moral hazard and improves fairness, but its market share stays limited because of basis risk, the mismatch between actual loss and payout, appearing either as actual loss without payout or payout without actual loss. The paper isolates basis risk as a structural feature of the parametric trigger, decoupled from its financial consequences for insurer or policyholder, and studies it through Monte Carlo simulations of diversified contract portfolios. Three findings: portfolio basis risk and its volatility both decrease as the number of contracts rises; basis risk follows deterministic patterns set by the ratio of the exposure-to-weather-station distance to the hazard footprint radius, which gives a geometry-driven reference; and hazard event severity does not significantly affect basis risk, so catastrophic severity should not hinder the development of parametric insurance. The conclusion is that basis risk is inherent but manageable through portfolio strategy and geospatial optimisation, and that this is most actionable for insurers, who can diversify at a scale individual policyholders cannot.","insights":["**The distance-to-footprint-radius ratio turns basis risk into a stated geometry rather than a judgement**, so whether a given set of measurement stations is well or badly sited becomes a calculation rather than an opinion.","Finding three removes a standing objection: **severity does not drive basis risk**, so the argument that parametric cover is unsuited to catastrophic perils does not survive. That matters for every sovereign and ecosystem parametric record here.","The insurer-versus-policyholder asymmetry is the practical conclusion. Basis risk is manageable by diversification, and only a party holding many contracts can diversify, so a single municipality or trust buying one parametric policy cannot reduce its own. **That is an argument for pooled structures rather than bespoke ones.**","Isolating basis risk from its financial consequences is the methodological move that makes the result general: the geometry holds whoever bears the mismatch."],"keywords":["basis risk","weather parametric insurance","Monte Carlo simulation","portfolio diversification","hazard footprint radius","geospatial optimisation","moral hazard"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":409,"title":"How Should Climate Policy Integrate Community Knowledge? [Episode 2]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Manann Donoghoe (guest)","date":"2025-08-18","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Brookings Institution","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Pew Research Center"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H-7PG27XhyU","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Manann Donoghoe, a climate resilience researcher in Brookings Metro's Center for Community Uplift, describes a project building a comparative resilience index across US communities, scored zero to one from publicly available datasets, and then using it as an identification tool rather than a ranking: find the communities scoring high, visit them, and learn from civic leaders what the data cannot show. He argues the drivers worth testing are civic infrastructure, public infrastructure, community organisation strength and wealth. On policy he identifies two openings that survive a changed federal climate posture, disaster policy reform and home casualty insurance, both of which address climate impacts without using the vocabulary of climate change. His closing argument is that the next wave of innovation should be in funding mechanisms that transfer resources to communities and households for resilience building, on the model of international adaptation funds, and he frames resilience investment as something that should be targeted as routinely as inflation or unemployment.","insights":["The proposed next frontier is explicitly a funding-mechanism question rather than a policy-language one: a domestic pot of capital, modelled on international adaptation funds, that under-resourced communities can draw on for disaster risk reduction and infrastructure.","Disaster policy and home casualty insurance are named as the two live openings for resilience work under a federal posture hostile to climate framing, because both are addressed in their own vocabulary rather than as climate policy.","The resilience index is designed as an identification tool for positive cases, not a scoring of failure: the stated purpose is to direct policy attention to communities where something is working and to scale what is found there.","An estimated 137 million people, 41 percent of the US population, live in areas regularly affected by major disasters, and FEMA declared a major weather-related disaster every four days in 2024.","The data half of the index is presented as insufficient on its own, with the qualitative site visits treated as a required second pass rather than as colour, which is a methodological commitment rather than a presentational one."],"keywords":["community resilience index","disaster policy reform","adaptation fund","FEMA","climate justice","civic infrastructure","home casualty insurance","resilience funding mechanisms","Brookings Metro","local adaptation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":479,"title":"Investing in Cities: Strengthening Urban Resilience Finance","authors":"Jessie Press-Williams, Allison Ahern, Wadeisor Rukato","date":"2025-08-15","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Resilient Cities Network","Howden","Cities Climate Finance Leadership Alliance"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://resilientcitiesnetwork.org/investing-in-cities-strengthening-urban-resilience-finance/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Cities Climate Finance Leadership Alliance, Howden and the Resilient Cities Network convened a high-level roundtable during 2025 London Climate Action Week on public-private models for unlocking urban climate resilience investment, and this reflection summarises the anonymised takeaways. CCFLA research puts city climate mitigation needs at approximately $4.3 trillion a year through 2030. Seven themes emerged: coordination is the constraint rather than capital, since deployment is hampered by fragmented planning and incoherent instruments; blended finance lacks a clear definition and needs creative application of grants, concessional loans and philanthropic capital to de-risk long-term infrastructure; insurance can unlock capital by making projects bankable and by supplying risk expertise, but must be embedded from project design rather than added afterwards; replicable models and aggregation across cities diversify risk and cut transaction costs; restrictive regulation, particularly in developing economies, blocks investment and sandboxing could help; partnerships need clear roles and better matchmaking in a fragmented ecosystem; better data on avoided losses and co-benefits lowers the cost of capital; and investment models should be inclusive rather than purely commercial. The Glasgow case from the Global Risk and Resilience Fellowship, a joint Howden and R-Cities programme, embedded an insurance professional in city leadership to bridge the gap between Glasgow's \u00a330 billion Greenprint for Investment and what banks require. Glasgow learned to reframe climate risk from liability to investment opportunity, but it still took nearly three years to mobilise \u00a34 million.","insights":["The Glasgow numbers are the most useful thing in the document: a \u00a330 billion investment prospectus and three years to mobilise \u00a34 million, which puts the real constraint at origination capacity rather than at capital availability.","The stated remedy for that gap was a person, an insurance professional embedded in city leadership, which says the missing input was underwriting fluency inside the public body rather than a new instrument.","The roundtable's insurance point is a sequencing claim: insurance has to be embedded at project design to make a project bankable, because retrofitting coverage onto a finished design cannot change the risk that was built in.","Participants assert there is no shortage of capital, which shifts the entire diagnosis from mobilisation to pipeline, and is the opposite of how the adaptation finance gap is usually framed.","The takeaways are anonymised and summarised, so nothing here is attributable or verifiable against a named institution's position."],"keywords":["urban resilience finance","blended finance","bankability","Greenprint for Investment","aggregation","CCFLA","Global Risk and Resilience Fellowship"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":50,"title":"The Economic Commitment of Climate Change","authors":"Maximilian Kotz, Anders Levermann, Leonie Wenz","date":"2025-08-13","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Nature","Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research","Potsdam University","Columbia University","Mercator Research Institute on Global Commons and Climate Change"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16835838","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource titled 'The Economic Commitment of Climate Change' synthesizes research on the economic impacts of climate change, revealing a projected median reduction in global income of 17% by mid-century, driven by updated methodologies incorporating subnational data and historical trends. It presents an empirical analysis of the relationship between climate conditions and economic growth, highlighting disparities in impacts on different socio-economic regions, particularly poorer nations. The findings underscore the importance of climate mitigation efforts, adaptation strategies, and the ongoing discourse on climate justice.","insights":["The revised estimates show a 17% reduction in global income by mid-century due to climate change.","Methodological updates were made to improve robustness against data inaccuracies, particularly related to Uzbekistan.","The likelihood of damages exceeding mitigation costs has increased to 91% under the new analysis.","Committed damages are especially pronounced in regions with historically low emissions and socioeconomic resilience.","Different climate variables show varied individual impacts on economic output, evidencing the complexity of climate-economy interactions.","The analysis highlights that poorer regions have experienced lower growth rates than their richer counterparts, potentially influenced by climate change.","Future economic damages can be projected by applying estimated empirical model parameters to climate scenarios.","Mitigation costs are assessed as the difference in per capita GDP under various scenarios to evaluate trade-offs."],"keywords":["economic growth","socioeconomic vulnerability","regression analysis","historical trends","income disparity"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":408,"title":"When Affordability Is a Challenge, How Do We Address Extreme Weather? [Episode 1]","authors":"Stuart Yasgur (host); Krista Egger (guest)","date":"2025-08-11","year":2025,"resource_type":"Podcast","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Economic Architecture","Enterprise Community Partners","Habitat for Humanity","Wells Fargo"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ui7HD3NTmRU","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Krista Egger, vice president of Building Resilient Futures at Enterprise Community Partners, argues that affordable housing and climate resilience are one problem rather than two, because the households least able to absorb a shock are the ones living in the least resilient stock. She describes Enterprise's Green Communities certification, now running about 10,000 new certifications a year against a cumulative total near 200,000 homes, and the several state and city housing departments that already condition their affordable-housing allocations on it. The unbuilt piece is financial: she wants a mechanism by which insurers would recognise the certification as evidence that a property will withstand a given hazard, and price it. The conversation also covers self-insurance and risk pools formed among affordable-housing owners as a response to withdrawing coverage, industrialized construction and the shortage of lenders experienced in financing it, and Enterprise's regional Resilience Academies, which combine peer learning, hazard identification across a portfolio and pass-through grant funding.","insights":["A green building certification is being proposed as an insurance-pricing signal, not only a design standard: the ask is that insurers treat Green Communities certification as evidence a property will withstand a hazard and reduce premiums accordingly. That link does not exist yet, which is the gap the episode names.","Several state and city housing departments already use the certification to allocate affordable-housing funding, so the standard has become a public-capital allocation rule ahead of becoming a private-capital pricing rule.","Affordable-housing owners are responding to coverage withdrawal by self-insuring and by forming risk pools among like-minded owners, a sector-level risk-transfer response rather than a per-property one.","Financing, not engineering, is the named barrier to industrialized construction: there are few lenders experienced with the instrument types it needs.","Extreme heat is described as causing more fatalities than any other climate disaster while attracting less attention, because the building survives and the occupants do not, which keeps it out of loss statistics built on structures."],"keywords":["affordable housing","green building certification","Green Communities","insurance premium reduction","self-insurance","risk pooling","industrialized construction","extreme heat","resilience academies","climate risk reduction"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":392,"title":"Federal Judge Temporarily Halts BRIC Grant Program Termination","authors":"Brett Mattson, Naomi Freel","date":"2025-08-06","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","National Association of Counties (NACo)","US Department of the Treasury","National Association of Counties"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.naco.org/news/federal-judge-temporarily-halts-bric-grant-program-termination","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This NACo news article reports on the federal court-ordered temporary halt to the Trump administration's termination of FEMA's Building Resilient Infrastructure and Communities (BRIC) grant program. On April 4, 2025, FEMA announced it would not allocate $750 million for BRIC that year and would cease funding previously approved projects. Twenty states sued, and in August 2025 a federal judge ruled the administration lacked authority to repurpose Congressionally allocated funds. Since its 2020 launch, BRIC had awarded more than $5 billion in grants for hazard mitigation projects including flood control, wildfire prevention, stormwater management, and building code strengthening, covering up to 75% of project costs.","insights":["FEMA's BRIC program awarded more than $5 billion in hazard mitigation grants since its 2020 launch, covering up to 75% of project costs for flood control, wildfire prevention, stormwater management, and building code upgrades.","The Trump administration's April 2025 cancellation withheld $750 million in BRIC allocations for FY2025 and halted funding for previously approved, in-progress projects, creating immediate pipeline disruption for county-level mitigation investments.","A federal court temporarily blocked reallocation of the BRIC funds in August 2025, ruling the administration lacked authority to repurpose Congressionally appropriated funds, a significant separation-of-powers constraint on executive rescission of pre-disaster mitigation grants.","The ruling does not release funds to states but prevents their use for non-BRIC purposes, leaving counties in a legal limbo: projects cannot proceed, but funds cannot be permanently redirected either.","Counties in disaster-prone areas face a forced shift from proactive mitigation spending to reactive post-disaster recovery dollars, a dynamic that historically increases total federal disaster expenditure and reduces community resilience.","The cancellation signals a federal policy shift toward state and local responsibility for disaster preparedness, reducing the federal match that made large-scale infrastructure mitigation projects financially viable for local governments.","NACo and the Intergovernmental Disaster Reform Task Force are actively lobbying Congress for federal disaster reform, indicating the BRIC dispute is part of a broader intergovernmental conflict over disaster finance architecture."],"keywords":["BRIC program","hazard mitigation grants","federal disaster policy","pre-disaster mitigation","FEMA grant cancellation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":12,"title":"Insurance for a FAIR Future","authors":"Alfonso Pating","date":"2025-08-06","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","California","Florida"],"institutions":["Natural Resources Defense Council"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nrdc.org/resources/insurance-fair-future","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The document discusses the challenges faced by FAIR Plans in providing insurance coverage in the context of increasing climate-related risks. As private insurers withdraw from high-risk areas, property owners are increasingly reliant on state-backed FAIR Plans, leading to a cycle where these plans absorb more high-risk policies and face rising costs, threatening their financial viability. The document proposes rethinking the structure and operation of FAIR Plans to ensure continued availability of property insurance and promote resilience measures.","insights":["FAIR Plans are under significant stress due to climate-fueled extreme weather events, leading to increasing enrollment and rising costs.","Private insurers are retreating from high-risk areas, resulting in more reliance on state-backed FAIR Plans as the insurer of last resort.","The current structure of FAIR Plans leads to unsustainable financial models that require bailouts from private insurers.","Effective changes to FAIR Plans are needed to maintain their role in the property insurance market and prevent a 'cycle of doom' where costs continue to rise.","Incorporating hazard mitigation and climate adaptation measures could improve the sustainability of FAIR Plans and reduce risk exposure.","Access to affordable insurance is disproportionately affected for low-income communities, necessitating targeted subsidies and support.","Proposed reforms include not allowing insurers to recoup assessment costs from policyholders and encouraging community-level hazard mitigation.","The financial health of FAIR Plans could be strengthened by retaining policyholder surpluses and investing in higher-yield options.","Authorize FAIR Plans to sell catastrophe bonds as a means to enhance their financial stability and manage catastrophic risks.","Implementing community-based insurance products linked to hazard mitigation could foster cooperation between insurers, governments, and homeowners."],"keywords":["insurance market","affordability","risk management","hazard mitigation","disaster recovery"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":25,"title":"The Carbon Cost of Coastal Adaptation: A Performance Evaluation Methodology for Nature-Based Solutions","authors":"Charles Waldheim, Pamela Conrad","date":"2025-08-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Harvard University Graduate School of Design","Salata Institute for Climate and Sustainability at Harvard University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://salatainstitute.harvard.edu/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Salata_OfU_NbS_Methodology_Report-FINAL-7-31-2025.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The article discusses the potential of low-carbon, nature-based solutions for coastal adaptation, highlighting the importance of redesigning coastal infrastructure to reduce carbon emissions. It emphasizes the need for collaboration among designers, builders, and policymakers to adopt more sustainable materials and practices, thereby improving coastal resilience while minimizing environmental impact.","insights":["Nature-based solutions (NbS) offer substantial benefits over traditional concrete and steel approaches, including reduced carbon emissions and cost savings.","Using lower-carbon alternatives and sourcing materials locally can significantly decrease the carbon footprint of coastal resilience projects.","Replacing high-emission materials, such as traditional fill, with sustainable options can lead to lower emissions and improved carbon impact.","Increasing vegetation at coastal sites not only enhances carbon sequestration but also contributes to biodiversity and public health.","Simple design modifications can significantly decrease the amount of material used, directly reducing emissions associated with coastal adaptation infrastructure.","Implementing nature-based solutions at scale can lead to net positive environmental outcomes, reversing the trend of contributing to climate change.","Collaboration among stakeholders from project inception is crucial to effectively integrating low-carbon materials and practices.","Establishing carbon limits and updating codes for infrastructure can help institutionalize nature-based solutions as the standard practice.","Potential global emissions reductions from implementing these strategies in coastal projects could equal 40 years worth of New York City's emissions.","Designers and engineers play a critical role in shifting the approach to coastal resilience towards sustainability."],"keywords":["sustainability","biodiversity","infrastructure","collaboration","ecosystem"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":197,"title":"Scaling Finance and Investment for Climate Adaptation: Input Paper for the G20 Sustainable Finance Working Group","authors":"","date":"2025-07-28","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["OECD"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1787/eeec8b52-en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The document serves as an input paper for the G20 Sustainable Finance Working Group, advocating for scaled finance and investment in climate adaptation due to the stark disparity in funding compared to mitigation efforts. It outlines essential strategies and challenges in mobilizing resources, particularly in developing countries, emphasizes innovative financing mechanisms like blended finance and resilience bonds, and underscores the importance of public-private partnerships and effective coordination among stakeholders to enhance climate resilience efforts across multiple sectors.","insights":["Current adaptation finance is severely inadequate, with only $76 billion mobilized in 2022 compared to $1.3 trillion for mitigation.","Blended finance and innovative instruments are crucial for attracting private sector investment in climate adaptation.","Developing countries face significant barriers such as lack of data and insufficient institutional frameworks impeding adaptation investments.","Public-private partnerships are essential for enhancing private investment in climate-resilient infrastructure.","Innovative financial mechanisms like resilience bonds and debt-for-adaptation swaps can provide alternative funding solutions for adaptation."],"keywords":["investment mobilization","public-private partnerships","financial mechanisms","institutional capacity","risk mitigation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":184,"title":"Climate Change and Insurance: Embracing Resilience for Private Market Survival","authors":"John A. Roper, David G. Casagrande, Paolo Bocchini","date":"2025-07-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Sustainable Development","Lehigh University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/sd.70106","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource examines the impact of climate change on the insurance industry, highlighting the increasing risks from natural disasters and the urgent need for private insurance in risk management. It advocates for collaborative efforts between public and private sectors to fund resilience through innovative financing mechanisms, such as resilience bonds, and emphasizes the importance of investing in resilient infrastructure, especially in vulnerable communities, to mitigate damages and enhance the financial sustainability of insurers.","insights":["Private insurance is crucial for managing natural disaster risks, but climate change is challenging its sustainability.","There is a concerning gap between actuarial risk and premium pricing, which disproportionately impacts vulnerable communities.","Investments in resilience can lead to significant long-term savings and improved recovery times from disasters.","Public-private partnerships are essential for addressing funding challenges in resilience but face organizational hurdles.","Innovative financing like resilience bonds can create mutual benefits for both public and private sectors, enhancing community resilience."],"keywords":["natural disasters","risk management","infrastructure","insurance market","community"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":490,"title":"Bracing for Impact: A Systems Strategy for Climate Resilience in the US","authors":"Dee Yang, Tucker Van Aken, Laurie Schoeman","date":"2025-07-17","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["SystemIQ","Lightsmith Group","Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.systemiq.earth/reports/bracing-for-impact/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Systemiq's July 2025 strategic blueprint for closing the US resilience gap. It opens with the exposure: 27 billion-dollar weather disasters in 2024 causing $183 billion in direct damages, a projected $1.2 trillion a year in physical climate risk costs to global companies by mid-century, $1.5 trillion of potential climate-driven devaluation of US homes, and at least $6.6 trillion of cost to the US economy over twelve years. Against that, every $1 of pre-disaster mitigation saves an estimated $13, and the resilience dividend, the economic, social and environmental benefits beyond avoided losses, is often twice the value of avoided losses. Capital needed is put at $100 to $200 billion a year. The report names seven systemic barriers: adaptation is not mainstreamed and its benefits are not visible; incentives reward recovery over prevention because insurance, mortgage and lending systems rarely price risk reduction, with flat mortgage rates and uniform premiums subsidising inaction; the financing ecosystem lacks taxonomies, scalable instruments, catalytic capital mechanisms and project preparation; private sector engagement ignores shared system dependencies; capacity is constrained across sectors; the solution set is poorly defined with no shared framework for comparing and sequencing interventions; and coordination mechanisms are lacking. The strategy works across three fronts. Framing: quantify the case for resilience with context-specific models, clarify the resilience toolbox as a shared typology, and embed resilience in investment frameworks and capital allocation. Coordination: cross-sector resilience networks, regional innovation hubs as living labs, and open-source resources. Execution: expand the pipeline of investable projects through project development facilities, develop innovative financing mechanisms such as resilience bonds structured around triple dividends.","insights":["The second barrier is stated bluntly: insurance, mortgage and lending systems rarely price risk reduction, and flat mortgage rates and uniform premiums actively subsidise inaction, so the financial system pays for recovery and charges nothing less for prevention.","The proposed instrument, a resilience bond structured around triple dividend metrics, is the direct answer to that barrier, because it would make the avoided loss, the growth and the co-benefits all cashflow-relevant rather than only the first.","Project development facilities are named as the missing layer between a resilience plan and an investable project, and the report observes that BRIC did this nationally before its cancellation, so the gap is a policy decision rather than an unsolved design problem.","The report is explicit that the resilience dividend is often twice the avoided loss, which means an appraisal that counts only avoided damages understates the return by roughly a factor of three and is the main reason the $13:1 ratio understates the case.","Every recommendation is paired as bottom-up and top-down, which follows from the diagnosis that the failure is coordination rather than either local initiative or national funding alone."],"keywords":["resilience gap","resilience dividend","resilience bonds","project development facility","triple dividend","unavoidable opportunity","resilience delivery unit"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":354,"title":"U.S. Municipalities Are Battling Growing Flood Risk with Innovative Insurance Solutions","authors":"","date":"2025-07-09","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","California","Florida"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","Floodbase","InsTech"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.instech.co/knowledge-centre/u-s-municipalities-are-battling-growing-flood-risk-with-innovative-insurance-solutions/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This article from InsTech describes the growing flood insurance gap facing U.S. municipalities and profiles Fremont, California as the first U.S. city to purchase a citywide parametric flood insurance policy, enabled by Floodbase's satellite- and AI-driven flood monitoring technology and distributed by specialty insurer Amwins. The piece argues that FEMA payouts are slow, partial, and may disappear under the current administration, making parametric insurance (which pays out within days of a trigger event, without damage assessments), a compelling alternative. Fremont's 2017 flood retroactively illustrates a modeled payout of over $710,000 under the new policy structure.","insights":["Flood costs the U.S. an estimated 1\u20132% of GDP annually, yet municipal flood risk remains largely underinsured; California municipalities alone saw $5\u20137 billion in economic losses in 2023, of which 80% was uninsured.","Hurricanes Milton and Helene (2024) caused a combined insured loss of $44 billion against total economic losses of $112 billion, implying an insurance gap of roughly $68 billion for a single hurricane season.","Fremont, CA became the first U.S. municipality to purchase a citywide parametric flood insurance policy; Floodbase's retroactive modeling estimates a payout of over $710,000 had the policy been active during the 2017 atmospheric river event.","Parametric flood insurance pays out within days of a trigger event because no damage assessment is required, directly addressing municipalities' reported frustration with FEMA's slow and often incomplete reimbursements.","Unlike FEMA payouts, parametric insurance proceeds can be used for loss of tax revenue, a major but previously uninsurable economic loss category for flood-affected municipalities.","Floodbase uses satellite imagery, near-real-time national stream gauge data, and AI to provide continuous global flood monitoring, enabling trigger-based parametric policies at city scale, a capability previously unavailable for flood peril.","The potential elimination of FEMA under the Trump administration heightens urgency for municipalities to adopt alternative risk transfer instruments; parametric insurance is positioned as a scalable substitute.","Parametric flood insurance is well-established for earthquake, drought, and wildfire but has been technically constrained for flood due to real-time modeling challenges at scale, Floodbase's technology represents a market-opening breakthrough for this peril."],"keywords":["parametric flood insurance","municipal flood risk","flood data analytics","insurance gap","atmospheric river","satellite flood monitoring"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":540,"title":"Climate Risk Capital Modelling: Market Trends","authors":"","date":"2025-07-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Lloyd's","Bank of England","Prudential Regulation Authority"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://assets.lloyds.com/media-651c0e64-c1d0-4f97-90f7-883c69fe2ef2/1cec7cba-a0b3-4744-b9e0-2a21f0558f57/Climate%20Risk%20-%20Market%20Trends%20-%20Published.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Lloyd's report on how its syndicates measure, manage and model climate change risk in their capital models, drawn from responses to the Climate Change Questionnaire that Actuarial Oversight issued in 2024. It defines climate risk across three types, physical, litigation and transition, and reports market practice against each, isolating the syndicates that Lloyd's own high-level assessment suggests are materially exposed. The exercise is explicitly exploratory and is intended to shape Lloyd's capital and validation guidance and climate risk oversight from 2026 onward. It is a state-of-the-market report and not a gap analysis against the CP10/25 consultation, which it largely predates.","insights":["LITIGATION RISK IS TREATED AS A THIRD CATEGORY ALONGSIDE PHYSICAL AND TRANSITION, defined as disputes arising from a party's contribution to climate change, from the physical consequences, or from the laws and duties related to it. Most climate risk frameworks carry only two.","Lloyd's set explicit materiality criteria rather than asking every syndicate the same question: more than 37.5 per cent of one-in-200 gross losses from LCM5 weather perils as a share of syndicate ECA for physical risk, more than 10 per cent of gross written premium in D&O and general liability for litigation risk, and more than 3 per cent investment exposure to transition-exposed sub-sectors.","Those criteria put 38 syndicates in the physical bucket, 41 in the litigation bucket and only 3 in the transition bucket, which is a striking asymmetry: litigation exposure is broader across this market than transition exposure.","The identified gap is not that syndicates model climate badly but that some do not quantify it at all: a small proportion cannot quantify physical climate risk exposure and a larger proportion cannot quantify climate litigation risk, including some Lloyd's assesses as materially exposed.","Lloyd's is careful that not adjusting a vendor catastrophe model is not automatically wrong, because its oversight of Principle 2a already tests whether present-day climate change is suitably captured, so the absence of an adjustment is a question rather than a finding.","The transition-risk sector list is taken from the Bank of England's CBES scenarios, the sectors with gross value added losses above 30 per cent, which ties a Lloyd's capital exercise directly to a central bank stress-testing framework."],"keywords":["capital modelling","climate litigation risk","syndicate exposure","PRA SS3/19","CBES","catastrophe model adjustment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":348,"title":"IDB Launches Disaster Risk Instruments and Expands Coverage","authors":"","date":"2025-07-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Latin America","Caribbean"],"institutions":["Inter-American Development Bank","IDB Invest"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.iadb.org/en/news/idb-launches-disaster-risk-instruments-and-expands-coverage-2-billion","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Inter-American Development Bank (IDB) and IDB Invest announced a multi-instrument expansion of disaster risk financial protection for sovereign and private clients in Latin America and the Caribbean. Key elements include: a $1 billion increase in the Contingent Credit Facility for Natural Disasters (reaching $5 billion total by 2026), a $1 billion expansion of Climate Resilience Debt Clauses (reaching $4.2 billion total by 2026), a new Regional Disaster-Risk Transfer Program connecting countries to reinsurance and capital markets (initially targeting Belize, Honduras, and Panama), and a first-of-its-kind MDB private-sector Business-Resilience Program from IDB Invest offering debt principal deferrals and tenor extensions of up to two years.","insights":["The IDB is expanding disaster-risk coverage by a combined $2 billion across two instruments, $1 billion added to its Contingent Credit Facility for Natural Disasters (now $5 billion total) and $1 billion added to Climate Resilience Debt Clauses (now $4.2 billion total), all targeted at Latin America and the Caribbean by 2026.","Climate Resilience Debt Clauses allow eligible sovereign borrowers to temporarily pause debt-service payments after qualifying natural disasters, freeing fiscal space for emergency response without triggering default; the IDB's total coverage reaches $4.2 billion by 2026.","The new Regional Disaster-Risk Transfer Program is designed specifically for small and vulnerable economies with limited market access, channeling extreme-event risk from hurricanes, floods, droughts, earthquakes, and wildfires to reinsurance and capital markets, with Spain and France as potential bilateral supporters.","IDB Invest's Business-Resilience Program is the first multilateral development bank mechanism to extend debt-clause protections to private-sector borrowers across agribusiness, infrastructure, energy, and tourism, allowing principal deferrals and tenor extensions of up to two years, setting a benchmark for MDB innovation in development finance.","The package builds on the IDB's 'Ready and Resilient Americas' regional program launched in March, signaling a systematic institutional shift toward pre-arranged, contingent financial instruments rather than post-disaster emergency lending.","The expansion of eligible countries under the Contingent Credit Facility and the inclusion of regional risk-pooling mechanisms suggest a scalable model for sovereign catastrophe liquidity that could be replicated by other MDBs or regional development banks."],"keywords":["contingent credit facility","debt clauses","disaster risk transfer","multilateral development bank","risk pooling","sovereign resilience","private sector resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":20,"title":"Mass Ready Act","authors":"Executive Office of Energy and Environmental Affairs, Gvr. Maura Healey, Lt. Gvr. Kim Driscoll","date":"2025-06-24","year":2025,"resource_type":"Regulatory / Legislative Document","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Massachusetts"],"institutions":["Commonwealth of Massachusetts"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.mass.gov/info-details/mass-ready-act","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Mass Ready Act is a comprehensive $2.9 billion initiative designed to enhance Massachusetts' infrastructure, protect natural resources, and strengthen local economies. It focuses on improving flood resilience, upgrading essential utilities, safeguarding water quality, and promoting food security while streamlining environmental processes. The Act aims to create robust communities for future generations, ensuring environmental preservation and public health.","insights":["The Act allocates significant funds for infrastructure improvements, including $764 million for state property upgrades and $401.6 million for dam and flood control projects.","Investments in local economies and agriculture are emphasized, with $125 million dedicated to food security programs.","The establishment of a Resilience Revolving Fund will provide low-interest loans for community resilience projects.","The Act addresses environmental protection with $505 million invested in clean water infrastructure and PFAS contamination remediation.","Provisions for enhancing biodiversity and protecting wildlife habitats are included, ensuring sustainability for future generations.","Streamlining environmental permitting processes is intended to reduce costs and expedite housing development.","The focus on modernizing policies around agriculture and animal health aims to foster economic viability in the sector.","Investments in recreational and green spaces are designed to improve community quality of life and environmental health.","The bill aims to create a comprehensive approach to managing climate challenges while focusing on public health and safety."],"keywords":["infrastructure","environmental protection","local economies","flood resilience","sustainable practices"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":101,"title":"Global Assessment Report (GAR) on Disaster Risk Reduction: Resilience Pays: Investing and Financing for Our Future","authors":"Jenty Kirsch-Wood","date":"2025-06-23","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.undrr.org/gar/gar2025","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Global Assessment Report (GAR) 2025 emphasizes the urgent need for investment in disaster risk reduction (DRR) as a response to the increasing economic and humanitarian impacts of climate change. Highlighting the escalating costs of disasters, the report calls for proactive risk management strategies, effective financial instruments, and international cooperation to enhance resilience and sustainable development, particularly for vulnerable populations, while advocating for improved methodologies in disaster cost assessments to better capture the full range of impacts.","insights":["Disaster costs have increased significantly over the decades, with the economic burden reaching unprecedented levels due to climate-related events.","Investment in disaster risk reduction (DRR) is not only necessary but economically beneficial, with strategies like risk transfer and innovative financing offering favorable returns.","Vulnerable populations, including refugees, face heightened risks from climate change, necessitating proactive investment in mitigation and resilience-building efforts.","Innovative financial solutions, like blended finance, resilience bonds, and catastrophe bonds, play a crucial role in funding disaster risk management initiatives.","Data gaps often lead to underestimations of disaster impacts, underscoring the need for improved methodologies in disaster cost assessments.","Disasters disrupt education and economic stability, with multi-hazard events leading to cascading economic effects that require comprehensive risk management strategies.","Access to early warning systems and disaster insurance remains uneven, reinforcing the necessity for improved coverage, especially in developing regions."],"keywords":["disaster risk management","economic stability","vulnerability","insurance coverage","sustainable development"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":44,"title":"How Catastrophes Limit Prosperity \u2013 Weather Disasters Are a Growing Burden, Also for Many Industrialized Countries","authors":"","date":"2025-06-11","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Munich RE"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.munichre.com/en/insights/natural-disaster-and-climate-change/weather-disasters-in-industrialized-countries.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Weather disasters are increasingly costly for major economies, with losses relative to gross national income rising significantly since the 1980s. An analysis finds that countries like the USA, Germany, and India are experiencing heightened financial burdens from these events, emphasizing the need for preventative investments to mitigate future costs.","insights":["Significant increase in weather disaster losses has been observed in eight of the ten largest industrialized countries since the 1980s.","The USA, Germany, and India reported the highest average losses in relation to their gross national income over the past five years.","In the US, weather-related losses increased to approximately five times the levels seen from 1980 to 1989.","Flooding and severe thunderstorms are major contributors to weather-related losses in North America and Europe.","Investment in prevention strategies can yield substantial returns, potentially saving $10 for every $1 spent.","China has effectively reduced average weather disaster losses relative to its economic output through significant investments in flood protection.","The percentage of uninsured losses remains high in many countries, complicating the financial recovery from disasters.","Japan's high level of prevention measures significantly mitigates its risk from natural disasters.","In Brazil, a combination of floods and droughts poses severe threats to economic stability, exacerbated by climate change.","The insurance gap underscores the need for improved coverage and risk management strategies across all regions."],"keywords":["economic impact","natural disasters","insurance gap","flood protection","climate change"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":602,"title":"Connecticut Public Act 25-33 (Substitute Senate Bill No. 9)","authors":"Connecticut General Assembly","date":"2025-06-10","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US","Connecticut"],"institutions":["State of Connecticut"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/2025/ACT/PA/PDF/2025PA-00033-R00SB-00009-PA.PDF","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Connecticut Public Act 25-33, Substitute Senate Bill No. 9, an act concerning the environment, climate and sustainable municipal and state planning and the use of neonicotinoids and second-generation anticoagulant rodenticides. Its first two sections, both effective 1 July 2026, are flood insurance disclosure mandates. Section 1 requires every insurer that delivers, issues for delivery or renews a homeowners or renters policy for a residential dwelling in the state to give the insured a notice prescribed or approved by the Insurance Commissioner explaining clearly, conspicuously and in plain language that the policy does not cover loss caused by flood, and that insurance is available under separate flood policies, with information on flood insurance eligibility and access. Section 2 requires each creditor in a mortgage loan transaction, not later than ten days before closing, to notify the applicant in writing that standard homeowners policies do not cover flood damage and related losses, that flood damage may occur regardless of whether the property is in a designated flood zone, and that the applicant may wish to consult a licensed insurance producer or surplus lines broker.","insights":["**The operative provision is a flood disclosure delivered ten days before mortgage closing**, which fixes the moment of disclosure to a point where the buyer can still act on it rather than at contract signature.","The clause that flood damage may occur **regardless of whether the property is in a designated flood zone** is a direct legislative answer to the map-inadequacy half of the same recommendation. The state cannot fix a federal map, so it requires the disclosure the map fails to make.","Placing the duty on the **creditor** as well as the insurer is the load-bearing choice. The lender has the borrower's attention ten days before closing and carries the collateral risk, so the disclosure lands at the moment a buyer can still act on it.","Disclosure is the cheapest resilience instrument available to a legislature: no capital, no risk transfer, no subsidy. Whether it changes behaviour is the open question the act does not answer.","The act bundles flood disclosure with municipal planning and with rodenticide regulation, which is how a disclosure mandate passes. Recorded because a reader searching for flood insurance law would not expect to find it here."],"keywords":["flood insurance disclosure","mortgage closing","homeowners insurance","designated flood zone","Insurance Commissioner","creditor duty","Public Act 25-33"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":49,"title":"The Compelling Investment Case for Climate Adaptation","authors":"Carter Brandon, Bradley Kratzer, Aarushi Aggarwal, Harald Heubaum, Celine Novenario","date":"2025-06-03","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wri.org/insights/climate-adaptation-investment-case","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report highlights the significant returns on investment for climate adaptation measures, emphasizing that $1 invested can yield over $10.50 in benefits due to avoided losses and various economic, social, and environmental advantages. It calls for a shift in perception to view adaptation not as a cost but as a lucrative investment opportunity crucial for achieving resilient development despite a current funding gap of $359 billion annually.","insights":["Extreme weather events are increasing in frequency and intensity, leading to significant economic damages.","The annual adaptation finance gap is estimated at $359 billion, indicating a critical need for enhanced funding.","Investing in adaptation can yield high returns, with $1 generating over $10.50 in benefits over a decade.","Adaptation investments create a 'triple dividend' of avoided losses, induced economic growth, and social/environmental benefits.","Health-focused adaptation projects can achieve returns exceeding 78%, improving public health and productivity.","Disaster risk management investments yield high returns, averaging nearly 36%, by safeguarding lives and infrastructure.","Water-related adaptation efforts average around 19% returns, highlighting the importance of water management.","Adaptation efforts can also help reduce greenhouse gas emissions, creating synergies between adaptation and mitigation.","Improved data collection and analysis is needed to accurately assess the benefits of adaptation investments.","Failing to invest in adaptation not only exposes communities to risks but also misses out on substantial socio-economic benefits."],"keywords":["investment","economics","infrastructure","development","sustainability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":519,"title":"Investing in Resilience: Three Case Studies in Climate Adaptation","authors":"","date":"2025-06-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Ceres","IFM Investors","Breckinridge Capital Advisors","Impax Asset Management"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ceres.org/resources/reports/investing-in-resilience-three-case-studies-in-climate-adaptation","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Ceres brief drawn from a roundtable of its Investor Network, presenting three investor case studies in climate adaptation: IFM Investors on how infrastructure equity owners create long-term value through adaptation, Breckinridge Capital Advisors on incorporating forward-looking climate data into municipal bond credit assessment, and Impax Asset Management on engaging companies to improve risk disclosure and management. It argues adaptation is both a risk management necessity and a multi-trillion-dollar market opportunity, and identifies risk assessment, disclosure quality and adaptation financing as the obstacles that keep it underfunded.","insights":["More than 90 per cent of global climate finance still goes to mitigation, and adaptation investment is projected to cover only one sixth of expected physical climate risk costs by 2030, which is the allocation gap the brief is written against.","The stated potential return on adaptation investment is up to $43 for each $1 spent, against annual losses of up to $1.2 trillion by 2050 for the world's largest companies without adaptation.","All three investors report that markets misprice tomorrow's climate realities today, which they treat as a barrier to adaptation funding and simultaneously as the source of the opportunity for long-horizon capital.","IFM applies adaptation at acquisition rather than after it: climate risk screening in due diligence, an explicit willingness to buy the inland asset over the coastal one at equal characteristics, and a required climate transition plan covering adaptation for every acquired asset.","Breckinridge applies forward-looking climate projections rather than historical trends inside municipal bond credit assessment, which is one of the few concrete routes by which physical risk reaches a price in this brief.","The brief names investors' most common error as looking only at direct operations while ignoring supply chains, infrastructure networks and communities that transmit climate shocks across a whole portfolio."],"keywords":["adaptation investment","physical climate risk","municipal bond credit","infrastructure equity","climate risk mispricing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":142,"title":"Strengthening the Investment Case for Climate Adaptation: A Triple Dividend Approach","authors":"Carter Brandon; Bradley Kratzer; Aarushi Aggarwal; Harald Heubaum","date":"2025-05-29","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute","SOAS University of London"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.46830/wriwp.25.00019","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This WRI working paper (June 2025) introduces the AdapTDR database, a first-of-its-kind compilation of 320 climate adaptation investments totaling $133 billion, approved by multilateral development banks and international climate finance institutions across 12 countries from 2014 to 2024. Using the triple dividend of resilience (TDR) framework alongside standard cost-benefit analysis, the study finds that $1 invested in adaptation yields over $10.50 in benefits, with an average EIRR of 26.7%. Second and third dividends (induced economic and social/environmental benefits) are double the value of avoided losses, and nearly half of investments generate mitigation co-benefits, opening pathways to carbon finance and private capital mobilization.","insights":["US$1 invested in adaptation yields over $10.50 in benefits over a 10-year period; 320 evaluated investments cost $133 billion total and may generate $1.4 trillion in benefits, with an average EIRR of 26.7% and median EIRR of 20.3%, well above the informal 10% MDB threshold.","Second and third dividends (induced economic development and social/environmental benefits) are, on average, double the value of first-dividend avoided losses, meaning over half of all monetized adaptation benefits accrue regardless of whether a climate disaster occurs, fundamentally de-risking the investment case.","Nearly half (44.7%) of all 320 evaluated adaptation investments are expected to generate GHG emissions reductions, with energy (85.7%) and forestry (75%) subsectors showing the highest rates; this creates direct eligibility for carbon finance layered onto adaptation structures.","Health and disaster risk management subsectors show the highest average EIRRs (78.5% and 35.9% respectively), driven primarily by avoided mortality valuations; sustainable agriculture and forestry show high returns (27.2% and 39.5%) via poverty-reduction and mitigation co-benefits.","Water subsectors (supply/sanitation: 19.4% avg. EIRR; flood management: 18.2%) are systematically undervalued, less than 20% of water investments monetize avoided losses, suggesting realized returns are substantially higher than reported.","Only 8.4% of the 320 investments valued all three dividends in monetary terms, and 74% of projects citing avoided-loss benefits did not monetize them, confirming that current EIRRs and BCRs are underestimates across the portfolio."],"keywords":["triple dividend of resilience","adaptation finance gap","economic internal rate of return","multilateral development banks","cost-benefit analysis","mitigation co-benefits","AdapTDR database","benefit-cost ratio"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":436,"title":"New Jersey's Flood Risk = Financial Risk","authors":"","date":"2025-05-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","New Jersey"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design","New York University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/new-jersey-2026/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Rebuild by Design's parcel-level analysis of New Jersey flood exposure, covering all 3.4 million residential, commercial and industrial parcels in the state and classifying each into one of four displacement risk groups. The headline figures are that nearly 1.3 million New Jerseyans live in high flood risk areas, half of them lower income residents who may lack the resources to relocate, and that by 2050 nearly one in six land parcels statewide will face high flood risk, threatening $435.9 billion in present property value and $5.9 billion in annual property tax revenue. The report also examines insurance rates and non-renewal risk, noting that some of the highest-flood-risk counties are also seeing higher non-renewal rates, and sets the exposure against a housing market with a 0.5 per cent homeowner vacancy rate and 3.6 per cent for renters. Its three recommendations are to create reliable state funding for resilient infrastructure, raised through a bond act or a minimal surcharge on certain insurance types to buy down risk; to expand the Blue Acres voluntary buyout programme with case management and social services; and to plan for short and long term migration.","insights":["The framing turns flood risk into fiscal risk: $5.9 billion in annual property tax revenue at severe risk by 2050 is a state and municipal revenue exposure, not a property owner's loss, which is what makes it a public finance problem.","The funding recommendation names two specific instruments, a state bond act or a minimal surcharge on certain insurance types to buy down risk, the second of which uses the insurance system to capitalise mitigation.","Non-renewal risk is found to concentrate in the same counties as the highest flood risk, so insurance withdrawal compounds rather than offsets the exposure.","A 0.5 per cent homeowner vacancy rate is the binding constraint on the relocation recommendations: the analysis classifies parcels by displacement risk into a market with almost nowhere for displaced households to go.","The parcel-level method across all 3.4 million properties is what allows displacement risk groups at all, and it is paired with the state's Atlas of Disaster finding of 14 federal disaster declarations from 2011 to 2024 costing over $7.9 billion."],"keywords":["flood risk","property tax revenue","displacement","parcel-level analysis","Blue Acres buyout","insurance non-renewal","New Jersey","managed migration"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":196,"title":"Climate Risk and Local Government Debt Decisions","authors":"Bin Li, Chensheng Wang, Wendai Lv","date":"2025-05-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Asia"],"institutions":["Research in International Business and Finance","Beijing University of Chemical Technology"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ribaf.2025.103010","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed empirical study examines how climate risk shapes local government debt decisions across 277 Chinese prefecture-level cities from 2010\u20132020, using urban investment bonds (municipal investment bonds) as a proxy for government debt. Using fixed-effects, spatial Durbin, and instrumental variable models, the study finds that rising physical climate risk (measured by frequency of extreme weather events) significantly increases local government debt balances. Two primary mechanisms are identified: expanding infrastructure investment demand and contracting land-based fiscal revenues. Spatial spillover effects and policy heterogeneity, particularly interactions between low-carbon pilot mandates and debt-control policies, further shape debt outcomes. The findings carry direct implications for green bond instrument design, fiscal resilience, and intergovernmental transfer policy in climate-stressed jurisdictions.","insights":["Climate risk (measured by extreme weather event frequency) increases local government bond balances by a coefficient of 0.139\u20130.144 (significant at 5%) across 277 Chinese prefecture-level cities over 2010\u20132020, confirming that physical risk systematically drives expansionary debt behavior.","Two transmission mechanisms are empirically validated: (1) investment demand expansion, greater local government infrastructure investment demand amplifies climate risk's effect on debt (interaction term LGI\u00d7Risk: 2.120, p\u003c5%); and (2) revenue pathway contraction, higher housing prices attenuate climate risk's debt impact (HP\u00d7Risk: \u22120.007, p\u003c1%), meaning declining real estate markets force heavier borrowing.","Spatial Durbin Model results reveal significant spatial spillover: climate risk in one city raises debt in neighboring cities (indirect effect: 0.393, p\u003c1%), while the direct effect is negative (\u22120.262, p\u003c5%), yielding a positive total effect (0.131, p\u003c10%). This means debt policy responses propagate regionally and cannot be managed in isolation.","Policy heterogeneity is decisive: low-carbon pilot city designation amplifies climate risk's debt effect (coefficient 0.280, p\u003c1%), while key debt-mitigation province designation renders the effect statistically insignificant (coefficient \u22120.037), demonstrating that strict debt oversight can override climate-driven borrowing impulses.","When both low-carbon and debt-control policies apply simultaneously, climate risk's effect on debt is also insignificant (coefficient 0.121), suggesting the two policy frameworks partially cancel each other out, a governance conflict with direct implications for financing climate adaptation."],"keywords":["municipal investment bonds","land finance","extreme weather events","fiscal decentralization","spatial spillover","low-carbon pilot cities","debt sustainability"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":180,"title":"How Climate Change May Be Reshaping the Landscape for Municipal Debt Issuances","authors":"Jayden Sangha","date":"2025-05-27","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["MunicipalBonds.com"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.municipalbonds.com/education/how-climate-change-may-be-reshaping-muni-landscape/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This article examines the impact of climate change on the municipal bond market, highlighting how rising natural disaster costs are reshaping the financial landscape for municipalities in the U.S. It notes that despite the growing evidence of climate risks, municipal bonds are often issued without adequate pricing for these risks, leading to questions about long-term financial stability for municipalities reliant on bonds for infrastructure and disaster recovery.","insights":["Municipalities face escalating costs due to climate change impacts, leading to increased borrowing from the municipal bond market.","Climate risks are not currently factored into the pricing of municipal bonds despite clear evidence of their impact.","Research indicates a minimal effect on bond yields, even for issuers with significantly higher climate risk exposure.","Rating agencies prioritize short-term financial metrics over long-term climate risk assessments in their evaluations.","Disaster recovery has historically relied on federal assistance, creating a false sense of security for investors.","Legislative restrictions in certain states hinder the consideration of climate risks in investment decisions.","The demand for municipal debt often overshadows underlying climate-related risks due to limited supply.","Future municipal debt issuance may rise as federal funding becomes more available for climate-resilient infrastructure.","Municipal bond investors need to reassess their evaluations by incorporating climate risk alongside traditional metrics.","The evolving reality of climate change will eventually necessitate a shift in how municipal bonds are priced and perceived."],"keywords":["municipal bonds","disaster recovery","infrastructure","investment risk","federal funding"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":204,"title":"Building Momentum: Flood and Coast: Innovative Financing for Climate Adaptation","authors":"Callum Ellis, Morgan Jones, Ed Kilcommons, and Swenja Surminski","date":"2025-05-21","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["Global","Europe"],"institutions":["Marsh","Oliver Wyman"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.oliverwyman.com/our-expertise/insights/2025/jul/innovative-financing-strategies-climate-adaptation.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt discusses the urgent need for innovative financing strategies to support climate adaptation efforts in response to rising risks from extreme weather events, particularly coastal erosion and flooding. It highlights the significance of collaboration among government entities, local authorities, and private investors to develop scalable funding mechanisms. Specific initiatives, such as the CLIFF initiative and the Severn Valley Water Management Scheme, illustrate approaches to address financial challenges and create adaptive resilience solutions for communities at risk.","insights":["Large-scale adaptation solutions are essential to reduce the impact of extreme weather events.","In the UK, adapting to flood risks could cost over 2 billion annually by 2030 and possibly 10 billion per year when all climate risks are considered.","Collaborative financing strategies are key to developing innovative funding mechanisms that unite public and private funding.","The CLIFF initiative aims to address coastal erosion risks through innovative funding mechanisms, aiding over 10,000 properties in the UK.","A dual financing approach is effective, combining short-term disaster recovery with long-term adaptation initiatives.","Understanding the investor landscape is critical to attracting finance, necessitating the quantification and monetization of adaptation benefits.","Governments need to enhance climate adaptation efforts by enabling local authorities to strategically access and manage funds.","Broadening funding criteria to include socioeconomic benefits can facilitate access to resources for adaptation projects.","Promoting cross-council collaboration and scaling projects can attract more significant investments and reduce risks.","There is a shift towards viewing adaptation investments as viable business propositions rather than relying solely on grants."],"keywords":["collaboration","funding mechanisms","coastal erosion","flood management","investment strategies"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":147,"title":"Can the Private Sector Plug the Adaptation Finance Gap?","authors":"Debbie Hillier","date":"2025-05-10","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/stories/2025/06/can-the-private-sector-plug-the-adaptation-finance-gap/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This World Economic Forum opinion piece (June 2025) critically examines whether private sector capital can close the adaptation finance gap in developing countries, ahead of COP30. It finds that while private investment has a role, particularly in agriculture and middle-income countries, structural barriers, limited direct financial returns, and overstated blended finance leverage ratios make it insufficient to meet the estimated $300 billion annual adaptation need. The piece cites forthcoming research from the Zurich Climate Resilience Alliance and calls for mandatory polluter-pays levies and expanded public finance obligations.","insights":["Developing countries' adaptation needs stand at approximately $300 billion per year, yet only ~10% is currently met through international climate finance, a structural funding gap that is widening as emissions reduction targets are missed.","Private external finance to emerging markets and developing countries (excluding China) is currently only ~$30 billion annually; the Independent High Level Expert Group estimates it must reach $450\u2013500 billion by 2030, a 15\u201318x increase in five years.","Blended finance leverage ratios are far below the often-cited 1:6 benchmark: ODI finds 1:0.75 for all developing countries and just 0.37 for low-income countries; McKinsey estimates a ceiling of 1:1.6; Convergence finds 1:1.8, undermining the 'billions to trillions' narrative.","The Global Commission on Adaptation's 'Adapt Now' report estimates that $1.8 trillion invested globally from 2020\u20132030 could generate $7.1 trillion in net benefits, but these returns are largely social and environmental, not directly capturable by private investors.","Quoted ROI figures (Standard Chartered: $12 per dollar; some estimates up to $43 per dollar) reflect societal benefits including public goods, not private financial returns, making most adaptation projects non-bankable for commercial investors without subsidy.","Engineered coastal protection (e.g., sea walls) remains almost entirely public-sector funded in developing countries; decades of attempts to attract private finance have largely failed, illustrating the limits of private capital for pure public goods.","Agriculture represents the largest share of recent blended finance deals in developing countries and holds the most realistic near-term potential for private adaptation investment, particularly in middle-income countries."],"keywords":["adaptation finance gap","blended finance leverage","polluter-pays principle","developing country finance","private capital mobilization","COP30","bankable projects"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":254,"title":"Pricing Climate Risks: Evidence from Wildfires and Municipal Bonds","authors":"Woongchan Jeon, Lint Barrage, Kieran James Walsh","date":"2025-05-03","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["ETH Zurich"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5240295","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The study investigates the financial impact of climate-driven wildfire risks on U.S. municipal bond markets, finding that municipalities facing higher wildfire risks incur significantly increased borrowing costs, with a one standard deviation rise in risk correlating to an increase of 14 to 26 basis points in bond spreads. The analysis reveals disparities in impacts across demographics and local revenue structures, indicating that the incorporation of future wildfire risks into bond pricing is crucial for understanding climate-related financial impacts on local governments.","insights":["Municipalities with higher future wildfire exposure incur increased borrowing costs.","A one standard deviation increase in future wildfire risk is associated with a 14-26 basis point increase in municipal bond spreads.","The financial impacts are notably greater in areas with higher minority populations and those reliant on local revenues.","Rising wildfire risks correlate with increasing yields-to-maturity on municipal bonds, indicating higher perceived risk by investors.","Fiscal implications of wildfire risks can lead to property tax declines and budget deficits for municipalities."],"keywords":["economic impact","borrowing costs","credit spreads","financial markets","fiscal health"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":552,"title":"Our Resilient Village: A Comprehensive Resilient Infrastructure Strategy for the Village of Key Biscayne","authors":"Village of Key Biscayne","date":"2025-05-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Village of Key Biscayne","National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration","Southeast Florida Regional Climate Change Compact"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://files.keybiscayne.fl.gov/Documents/Elevating%20Our%20Island%20Paradise/Projects/Resilient%20Key%20Biscayne%20-%20FINAL%20-%20booklet.pdf?t=202505010911570&t=202505010911570","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A municipal resilient infrastructure strategy for the Village of Key Biscayne, a barrier island of about 15,000 residents sitting less than five feet above sea level south of Miami. It sets out the hazards the island faces, an inadequate gravity-fed stormwater system, above-ground power and telecommunications lines, roadway flooding and shoreline erosion on both the bayside and oceanside, and then states how the work will be paid for. The financing section is the part that matters to this corpus: it names the four mechanisms local governments use, taxes, fees, grants and loans or bonds, and says the Village will rely heavily on loans and bonds. Residents approved a $100 million Resilience Infrastructure General Obligation Bond by referendum in 2020, repaid through an additional ad valorem assessment on property values. The Village is also drawing on the Clean Water State Revolving Fund and the federal Water Infrastructure Finance and Innovation Act for low-interest municipal loans, has secured $1.2 million in grants and is awaiting a decision on a further $5.2 million, against more than $1 billion of state resilience investment made available in Florida.","insights":["A voter-approved general obligation bond is the financing route here, which makes the instrument accountable to a referendum rather than to a market: the $100 million was authorised in 2020 by the residents who will pay the ad valorem assessment servicing it.","The document pairs an explicit sea level rise design assumption with the financing decision. The Village adopted NOAA's Intermediate High projection, 10 to 17 additional inches by 2040 and 21 to 40 inches by 2070, chosen by Council because it is adaptable-to rather than the least or most extreme, and recommended for assets whose design lives end before 2070.","CWSRF and WIFIA appear as working municipal instruments with a borrower attached rather than as policy proposals, so the record shows the federal programmes in use rather than in description.","The document states no printed publication date anywhere. The date recorded is the publisher's own file timestamp in the URL, 1 May 2025, not a date the document asserts."],"keywords":["general obligation bond","ad valorem assessment","sea level rise","stormwater","WIFIA","Clean Water State Revolving Fund","Key Biscayne","municipal resilience finance","coastal adaptation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":403,"title":"Atlas of Disaster: Kentucky","authors":"","date":"2025-05-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Kentucky"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design","Citizens Property Insurance Corporation","The Nature Conservancy","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Department of Housing and Urban Development","Georgetown Climate Center","Urban Institute","First Street Foundation","AECOM","U.S. Army Corps of Engineers (USACE)","Village of Key Biscayne","U.S. Chamber of Commerce","North Carolina Office of Recovery and Resiliency (NCORR)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/kentucky","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Rebuild by Design's \"Atlas of Disaster: Kentucky\" (May 2025) documents the scale and distribution of climate disaster losses in Kentucky between 2011 and 2024, quantifying $1.53 billion in combined FEMA and HUD CDBG-DR post-disaster assistance across 23 federally declared major disasters. The report diagnoses structural financing gaps, Kentucky has no statewide adaptation plan, and proposes two primary funding mechanisms: a voter-approved statewide ballot measure and a 2% surcharge on property and casualty insurance projected to generate $3.3 billion over 10 years for resilient infrastructure investment, leveraged through bonding.","insights":["Kentucky received $1.53B in combined FEMA ($1.14B) and HUD CDBG-DR ($386M) post-disaster assistance across 23 federally declared major disasters from 2011\u20132024, equating to $341 per capita, 13th highest nationally, with all 120 counties impacted.","A proposed 2% surcharge on certain property and casualty insurance lines is modeled to generate $3.3 billion over 10 years for resilient infrastructure; revenues would be leveraged through bonding to amplify capital and create bondholder accountability.","Kentucky has no statewide adaptation plan, creating a governance and coordination gap that compounds financial vulnerability; 18 counties simultaneously exhibit high social vulnerability and low energy reliability, concentrating investable risk.","107,900 Kentucky adults were displaced by disasters as of 2024 (roughly 3.16% of the adult population, nearly double the national average), with 6,200 Kentuckians never returning home, signaling permanent community destabilization with long-term fiscal consequences.","Homeowners' insurance premiums in Kentucky jumped 22% from 2022 to 2023, reaching ~$3,000/year average, while 27,700 Louisville properties face flood risk over 30 years; rising insurance costs are compounding the cycle of destruction and rebuild in flood-prone areas.","FEMA's Community Rating System already delivers 5\u201345% flood insurance discounts for participating communities; only 37 Kentucky communities participate as of October 2022, representing a significant untapped mechanism for household cost reduction tied to public investment.","Extreme heat is projected to cause 300 additional deaths annually in Kentucky by 2039 and 460 by 2059; average summer temperatures in Louisville have already risen 3.4\u00b0F since 1970, with 46 days above 94.6\u00b0F projected annually by 2050 versus 7 today."],"keywords":["disaster declarations","post-disaster federal assistance","insurance surcharge","flood mitigation","social vulnerability","statewide ballot measure","Community Rating System","extreme heat mortality"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":376,"title":"What Incentives Are States Offering to Make Houses Less Vulnerable to Extreme Weather Damage?","authors":"","date":"2025-05-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)","Brookings Institution - Metropolitan Policy Program","North Carolina Office of Recovery and Resiliency (NCORR)","Brookings Institution","The Brookings Institution"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/what-incentives-are-states-offering-to-make-houses-less-vulnerable-to-extreme-weather-damage/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Brookings Institution article surveys state-level grant and loan programs designed to make homes more resilient to extreme weather, focusing on wind/hurricane hardening (Alabama, Florida, Kentucky, Louisiana, Minnesota, North Carolina, South Carolina, Oklahoma) and wildfire (California) and flood (New York). Most wind programs fund roof retrofits to the IBHS FORTIFIED Home\u2122 standard, with grants typically up to $10,000. Documented outcomes include 20\u201340% lower insurance claim severity for fortified homes post-Hurricane Sally, a 35% reduction in claims after major Carolina hurricanes, and a 7% resale price premium for FORTIFIED homes in Alabama. Florida's program has allocated over $700 million since revival; California's wildfire pilot has deployed $117 million across six high-risk counties.","insights":["Alabama's Strengthen Alabama Homes program has issued over $86 million in grants to fortify more than 8,700 homes since 2016; a post-Hurricane Sally analysis found FORTIFIED-standard roofs experienced 20\u201340% lower claim severity, and total damages across all homes would have been ~75% lower if every home met that standard.","A University of Alabama study found FORTIFIED-certified homes in Baldwin and Mobile counties commanded a resale price premium of nearly 7%, exceeding the typical retrofit cost, a concrete unit-economics proof point for capital allocators pricing home improvement loans and mortgage products.","Florida's My Safe Florida Home program found every dollar spent on mitigation translated to $1.50 in avoided damage loss; the legislature has allocated over $800 million since the program's 2006 launch (including $280 million in 2025), signaling sustained public-sector demand for home hardening at scale.","North Carolina data show FORTIFIED roofs produced 35% fewer insurance claims after four named hurricanes (Matthew, Florence, Dorian, Isaias) and 63% fewer claims from unnamed storms between 2018 and 2024, providing insurers and reinsurers with statistically grounded loss-reduction evidence.","South Carolina homeowners reported premium savings of up to 24% annually after SC Safe Home retrofits, a direct link between physical hardening and insurance cost reduction that supports the investability of home hardening programs through insurance-premium-linked underwriting.","Oklahoma's industry-funded Strengthen Oklahoma Homes program caps contributions at $10 million per year from insurers, demonstrating a private-sector funding model for resilience grants that does not rely on state or federal appropriations, a replicable structure for other high-risk states."],"keywords":["home hardening grants","FORTIFIED Home standard","roof retrofitting","insurance premium discount","wildfire home hardening","wind mitigation","state resilience programs"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":33,"title":"Building Resilience Through Climate Adaptation: Overcoming Biases to Position for New Opportunities While Minimizing Losses","authors":"Sarah Kapnick","date":"2025-05-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["JPMorgan Chase"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.jpmorgan.com/insights/sustainability/climate/unlocking-resilience-through-climate-adaptation","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This excerpt emphasizes the critical need for climate adaptation in response to increasing climate-related risks. It highlights the historical focus on mitigation over adaptation, detailing the substantial economic losses anticipated from extreme weather events if adaptation measures are not adopted. The report presents adaptation not only as a tool for risk management but also as a strategic investment opportunity that can yield significant returns over time. By reshaping decision-making frameworks and overcoming biases, organizations can proactively invest in adaptation strategies to build resilience and capitalize on emerging opportunities.","insights":["Adaptation finance has been historically underfunded compared to mitigation, with only about $65 billion annually, far below expected needs.","Climate adaptation is projected to yield returns of $2 to $43 for every dollar spent, highlighting its potential as a strategic investment.","The financial impacts of climate change will become more pronounced with projected annual losses of $1.2 trillion for large corporations by the 2050s without adaptation.","Cognitive biases, like recency bias, can lead to poor decision-making in the face of climate risks, making adaptation efforts more urgent.","Different sectors and regions are at varying levels of adaptation readiness; understanding these differences can guide effective investment strategies.","Leaders in business need to develop 'climate intuition' to better recognize and act upon the necessity for adaptation.","Failing to adapt can lead to significant value loss and is counterproductive in the face of growing climate-related risks.","Some entities (early adapters) are more proactive in adopting adaptation measures, while others (procrastinators) wait, which can result in missed opportunities.","Municipalities increasingly recognize the importance of adaptation planning, even though the market has yet to fully reward these efforts.","Investing in adaptation not only mitigates risks but also positions businesses to leverage new opportunities arising from a changing climate."],"keywords":["risk management","investment opportunity","economic losses","climate risks","strategic evaluation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":620,"title":"Accountants of Adaptation? Cost Benefit Analysis and the Politics of Resilience","authors":"Katinka Wijsman","date":"2025-04-30","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["Utrecht University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geoforum.2025.104290","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article in Geoforum, DOI 10.1016/j.geoforum.2025.104290, by Katinka Wijsman of the Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning at Utrecht University, on cost benefit analysis and the politics of resilience. It enters a debate in which resilience is cast either as technocratic expertise handling complexity through top-down managerial intervention, or as marking the limit of expert knowledge such that planning is futile, both readings treating resilience as depoliticising. The author argues neither grapples with the fact that expertise is not only deployed but demanded in order to bring about interventions after climate change, nor with the unsettled and unsettling nature of expert knowledge and its production. The paper examines expertise in the making of resilience with attention to how it can open political possibilities by facilitating rather than short-circuiting debate, and does so through the practice of cost benefit analysis in coastal resiliency projects in New York City, showing how an expert practice is used to articulate and to debate what resilience actually consists of.","insights":["**The paper is about what cost benefit analysis DOES politically rather than what it computes**, treating it as a site of argument over what counts as a benefit rather than as a neutral measuring device.","The finding that expertise can open rather than close political debate cuts against the standard critique and has a practical consequence: a contested benefit estimate may be more useful to a decision than a confident one, because it forces the assumptions into the open.","New York City coastal resiliency projects are the case material, so the argument is grounded in a specific set of appraisals rather than in the method in the abstract.","It is a critical human geography paper rather than an economics one, and its evidence is documentary and interview-based, so it supports an argument about how the tool is used rather than any number."],"keywords":["cost benefit analysis","politics of resilience","expertise","politicization","coastal resiliency","New York City","depoliticization"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":119,"title":"Is Coastal Climate Adaptation Infrastructure Worth Public Investment? Evidence from Stated Preference Economic Valuation and Cost-Benefit Analysis","authors":"Haoluan Wang","date":"2025-04-11","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of Miami"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2025.125294","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed article conducts a discrete choice experiment (DCE) among 850 South Florida residents across Broward, Miami-Dade, and Palm Beach Counties to elicit willingness to pay (WTP) for three coastal climate adaptation infrastructure types (seawalls, mangrove forests/salt marshes, and coastal dunes/beach renourishment), and combines aggregate WTP with engineering cost estimates to perform cost-benefit analysis. All three project types yield positive net benefits, but green infrastructure benefit-cost ratios (4.70\u20135.64) are more than double that of grey infrastructure (2.02) over a 50-year lifespan, providing a quantitative basis for prioritizing public investment in nature-based coastal protection in South Florida.","insights":["All three coastal adaptation infrastructure types examined (seawalls, mangrove/salt marsh, and coastal dunes/beach renourishment), are worth public investment: aggregate WTP exceeds total project costs across all lifespan scenarios (10\u201350 years) for 10 miles of coverage serving ~170,387 households.","Green infrastructure benefit-cost ratios are more than double grey infrastructure: over 50 years, seawalls yield a BCR of 2.02, mangroves 4.70, and coastal dunes 5.64, with dune projects also carrying the lowest total cost ($0.19B vs. $0.37B for seawalls).","Residents are willing to pay $61\u2013$63 per household per year more for green infrastructure (mangroves or dunes) than for seawalls, reflecting residents' valuation of co-benefits such as carbon sequestration, wildlife habitat, and tourism/recreation that are not priced in markets.","Benefit-cost ratios rise monotonically with project lifespan: extending active use from 10 to 50 years significantly increases net benefits for all project types, favoring long-term commitments and durable funding mechanisms over short-cycle investments.","Respondents living within the 100-year floodplain exhibit higher WTP, while risk-averse individuals exhibit lower WTP, a finding with implications for targeted outreach and the design of tax or fee structures that reflect differential flood exposure.","Construction cost differentials are large: seawalls cost $7.95M/mile to build vs. $2.00M/mile for dunes and $5.80M/mile for mangroves, making green infrastructure cheaper to build even before accounting for higher public willingness to pay.","Familiarity with coastal adaptation infrastructure significantly predicts WTP; prior research shows Black residents are less likely to be familiar with such strategies, pointing to an equity gap in who may benefit from public investment if engagement is unequal."],"keywords":["willingness to pay","benefit-cost ratio","discrete choice experiment","nature-based solutions","coastal protection","green infrastructure","sea level rise","South Florida"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":187,"title":"The Growing Void in the U.S. Homeowners Insurance Market: Who Should Bear the Rising Cost of Climate Change?","authors":"Mona Hemmati, Ian P. Gray, Steven G. Bowen","date":"2025-04-10","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","California","Florida"],"institutions":["NPJ Climate Action","Columbia University","Gallagher Re"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s44168-025-00231-8","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The U.S. homeowners insurance market is in crisis due to climate change, with insurers retreating from high-risk areas and homeowners facing coverage gaps, rising costs, and inadequate protections. To address these challenges, the report emphasizes the need for improved data transparency, updated building codes, incentivization for individual adaptations, and collaborative efforts among stakeholders to build a more resilient insurance landscape.","insights":["The increasing frequency and intensity of climate-driven disasters is straining the homeowners insurance market.","Private insurers are withdrawing from high-risk areas, leading to a reliance on financially unstable government-backed programs.","Limited accessibility to climate risk data complicates pricing and underwriting practices for insurers.","Reforms must prioritize vulnerable communities and equitable funding mechanisms to support climate-related insurance.","Updated building codes are crucial for minimizing damage from climate-related events and enhancing property resilience."],"keywords":["homeowner coverage","climate risk","data transparency","building codes","financial stability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":447,"title":"New Guidance Outlines Best Practices for Municipal Bond Issuers Facing Growing Climate Threats","authors":"","date":"2025-04-08","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Ceres"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ceres.org/resources/news/new-guidance-outlines-best-practices-for-municipal-bond-issuers-facing-growing-climate-threats","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Ceres press release announcing 'Leading with Transparency', a guide for state and local governments on integrating resilience planning with climate risk disclosure in the municipal bond market. The framework is layered across four places a government can disclose: financial statements, bond issuance documents, standalone climate action reporting, and the resilience plan itself, which the report treats as a necessary precursor to disclosure rather than a parallel activity. Five worked examples are named: Miami-Dade County Water's 2024 Official Statement for Water Revenue Bonds, the City of Boston's Series 2023 general obligation bonds, Seattle City Light's 2023 audited financial statements acknowledging hydro risk, DC Water's TCFD-aligned ESG and resilience report, and the City of New Orleans's annual comprehensive financial report. Ceres puts current disclosure in the $4 trillion municipal market at inconsistent and inadequate, and frames the timing against recent climate-related municipal defaults and downgrades, including S&P's downgrade following the Los Angeles wildfires.","insights":["The report's own forecast is the load-bearing number: climate adaptation projects are expected to drive a doubling of municipal debt issuance over the next decade, which makes disclosure quality a precondition for the market that resilience finance depends on.","A resilience plan is positioned as a prerequisite for disclosure rather than a separate workstream, so the sequence runs plan, then disclose, then issue.","The five named examples are all utility or general obligation issuers rather than labelled green deals, which locates the disclosure practice in ordinary municipal borrowing.","Climate-related downgrades are cited as already happening, with S&P downgrading bonds after the Los Angeles wildfires, so the pricing consequence is present rather than prospective."],"keywords":["municipal bonds","climate disclosure","resilience planning","official statement","TCFD","credit downgrade","Ceres","public finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":253,"title":"Leading with Transparency: A Guide to Strengthening Climate Disclosure and Resilience in the Municipal Bond Market","authors":"Brian McCartan","date":"2025-04-08","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Ceres"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ceres.org/resources/reports/leading-with-transparency-improving-climate-risk-disclosure-by-municipal-governments","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The guide 'Leading with Transparency' underscores the critical need for municipal governments to enhance climate-related financial disclosures to secure funding from the $4 trillion municipal bond market, especially as communities confront increasing climate risks. By adopting best practices in transparency and strategic risk management, municipalities can improve their access to capital, bolster their resilience efforts, and proactively address the financial vulnerabilities associated with climate change.","insights":["Municipal governments are crucial in safeguarding communities from climate-related disasters and need robust disclosures to access funding.","The municipal bond market saw $508 billion issued in 2023, highlighting its significance for resilience and disaster response funding.","Investors are increasingly assessing climate risks before investing, indicating a market shift in risk evaluation.","Current climate risk disclosures among municipal issuers are inconsistent, leading to insufficient information for investors.","Adopting comprehensive climate resilience plans and enhancing financial disclosures are essential strategies for municipalities.","Regular monitoring of resilience programs and tracking metrics is vital for adapting strategies to evolving climate risks.","Governance structures for climate resilience should be clearly defined in disclosures to improve coordination across departments.","Federal and state regulators need to provide better guidance and resources for municipal bond issuers to enhance climate disclosures."],"keywords":["financial disclosure","risk assessment","transparency","sustainability","policy actions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":182,"title":"Managing the Climate Change-Fueled Property Insurance Crisis","authors":"Lilith Fellowes-Granda, Marc Jarsulic, Alexandra Thornton","date":"2025-04-02","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Center for American Progress"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.americanprogress.org/article/managing-the-climate-change-fueled-property-insurance-crisis/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This policy report from the Center for American Progress analyzes the accelerating collapse of U.S. private property insurance markets driven by correlated climate losses, and proposes federal and state policy remedies. It documents $183 billion in U.S. climate disaster damages in 2024, $112.7 billion in insured losses, and a 33% average premium increase from 2020\u20132023. Key findings include First Street Foundation's projection of $1.47 trillion in net property value losses over 30 years due to insurance pressures, and a $20 billion NFIP debt to Treasury. Recommendations span resilience investment, risk disclosure, geospatial modeling tools, insurer data reporting, and reform of FAIR plans and the NFIP.","insights":["In 2024, the U.S. sustained 27 billion-dollar weather and climate disasters totaling $183 billion in damage, with insured losses reaching $112.7 billion, a 36% increase over 2023.","Homeowner insurance premiums rose an average of 33% from 2020 to 2023 (derived from mortgage escrow data); households in the most disaster-affected areas saw premiums rise 14.7% faster than inflation vs. a national average of 8.7% above inflation from 2018\u20132022.","First Street Foundation projects $1.47 trillion in net property value losses over 30 years attributable to insurance pressures and shifting consumer demand, a direct balance-sheet risk for mortgage holders, GSEs, and banks.","1 in 13 homeowners is currently uninsured, leaving at least $1.6 trillion in unprotected market value; nearly $13 trillion in outstanding household mortgage debt depends in part on insurance coverage.","Property reinsurance rates in the U.S. rose between 45% and 100% in 2023 alone, with those costs typically passed through to consumer premiums, compressing insurer margins and accelerating market exit.","The NFIP carries more than $20 billion in outstanding debt to the U.S. Treasury (not counting $16 billion forgiven by Congress in 2017), reflecting chronic underpricing of flood risk at approximately 30% below actuarially fair rates.","The California FAIR plan faces approximately $5 billion in wildfire exposure from the 2025 Los Angeles fires; a $1 billion assessment on private insurers was approved, with up to $500 million potentially passed to policyholders statewide.","Census tracts that are majority Black, Hispanic, or Native American are 50% more vulnerable to wildfire (accounting for both risk and adaptive capacity) than other tracts, underscoring that insurance market failure has pronounced distributional equity implications."],"keywords":["insurance market failure","FAIR plans","NFIP reform","property value loss","insurance affordability","correlated climate risk","flood risk disclosure","residual market"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":514,"title":"New York City Climate Budgeting Fiscal Year 2026","authors":"","date":"2025-04-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["New York City Mayor's Office of Management and Budget"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nyc.gov/assets/omb/downloads/pdf/exec25/exec25-nyccb.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"New York City's second annual Climate Budgeting publication, issued as a feature of the Executive Budget for Fiscal Year 2026, reports on a process that integrates climate considerations systematically into the city's financial planning: a $115.1 billion expense budget and $196.7 billion of planned capital funding across Fiscal Years 2025 to 2035. It sets out a four-step cycle of assessing progress and identifying gaps, communicating insights, developing and prioritising actions, and implementing and reporting outcomes. The substantive analysis is a climate alignment assessment of planned capital projects against flood and heat resiliency and against emissions reduction, together with new methods for inventorying adaptation measures and forecasting their effect. New York City was the first large United States city to adopt climate budgeting, beginning in 2023.","insights":["Planned capital funding includes nearly $12 billion for projects addressing flood risk and more than $2 billion addressing heat risk, of which more than $1 billion addresses both.","A further $46.5 billion of projects include some scope elements contributing to flood or heat resiliency or use the city's Climate Resiliency Design Guidelines, which is a far larger figure than the dedicated allocation and shows resilience entering projects whose primary purpose is something else.","The assessment names the gaps as well as the spending: over $1 billion for projects in high-risk flood areas and on the waterfront that do not yet incorporate flood resiliency measures, and over $200 million in heat vulnerable areas that do not, which is a public admission of misalignment inside a budget document.","More than $8 billion of capital is allocated to emissions reduction with over $14 billion more contributing to it alongside other priorities, while $7 billion is identified as still including fossil fuel equipment rather than electric alternatives.","The specific resilience line items are small against the capital totals: flood sensor expansion at $0.6 million, flood risk mitigation for businesses at $1.2 million, forestry management at $3 million and Advanced Capital Planning at $3.5 million."],"keywords":["climate budgeting","municipal capital planning","flood resiliency investment","heat resiliency","Climate Resiliency Design Guidelines"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":484,"title":"Bridging the Gap: InSAR, Urban Subsidence, and the Imperative for Proactive Resilience in South Florida","authors":"Jean-Pierre Bardet","date":"2025-04-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of Miami Climate Resilience Institute","National Institute of Standards and Technology","Miami-Dade County"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jci3.70000","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Jean-Pierre Bardet of the University of Miami synthesises a decade of interferometric synthetic aperture radar studies of land subsidence in South Florida and argues that the policy response has remained reactive despite consistent evidence. Early work by Fiaschi and Wdowinski found 2 to 3 mm a year of subsidence in reclaimed western Miami Beach; Zanjani et al. documented 2 to 8 cm of vertical displacement across 35 coastal buildings on Miami's barrier islands between 2016 and 2023 and linked much of it in structures built after 2014 to the construction process itself, load-induced creep in sandy layers within the limestone that can persist over a decade and reach 300 m from an active site. The 2024 NIST assessment processed over 500 SAR images from Sentinel-1 and COSMO-SkyMed covering Miami-Dade to Brevard County, identified over 50,000 persistent scatterers and reported mean displacement velocities to plus or minus 0.8 mm a year: Miami Beach 3.1 mm a year on unconsolidated reclaimed land under urban loading, Brickell and Downtown 2.8 mm a year from limestone dissolution, Fort Lauderdale 1.9 mm a year from groundwater extraction, West Palm Beach 1.2 mm a year from natural compaction. Fifteen per cent of Miami-Dade high-rises showed differential settlement exceeding 2 mm a year variance across their foundations; buildings under structural loads above 200 kPa subsided 2.1 times faster than those in low-density zones; and proximity to wellfields explained 65 per cent of the variability in displacement rates. After the June 2021 Champlain Towers South collapse, Miami-Dade tightened 40-year recertification and Florida Senate Bill 4-D in May 2022 mandated milestone inspections for buildings of three storeys or more, forbade waiver of reserve funds for essential repairs and established a state condominium database, but no mandate for subsidence monitoring was created and building codes were not updated for differential settlement risk.","insights":["A mitigation cost of 5 to 10 per cent of the risk reduced implies a benefit-cost ratio of 10:1 to 20:1, and the measures are still not adopted, so the barrier the paper identifies is not the return.","The stated reasons for inaction are legal liability, monitoring cost and fragmented governance, and only the second is a financing problem, which means a financing instrument alone would not unlock this even at a 10:1 return.","Proposal two, subsidence risk zones with mandatory property disclosure, is the one that would move markets: a disclosure regime converts a measured millimetre-scale displacement into a price, and a 23 to 24.5 per cent GDP share resting on real estate is exactly why nobody has enacted it.","Fifteen per cent of Miami-Dade high-rises showing differential settlement above 2 mm a year is the number a condominium insurer, a lender or a reserve study would need, and it is a survey-wide statistic that no individual building owner currently receives.","The construction-induced subsidence finding is a liability allocation problem hiding inside a geotechnical one: if new building causes settlement up to 300 m away and lasting over a decade, the cost falls on neighbouring owners who are not party to the development.","Senate Bill 4-D forbade waiving reserve funds, which is a financing rule rather than an engineering one, and is the only post-Surfside measure that changed how buildings pay for their own maintenance."],"keywords":["InSAR","land subsidence","Champlain Towers South","Senate Bill 4-D","differential settlement","property disclosure","Miami-Dade","structural health monitoring"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":316,"title":"Overburdened: The Dramatic Increase in Homeowners Insurance Premiums and Its Impacts on American Homeowners","authors":"Sharon Cornelissen, Douglas Heller, Ethan Weiland, Michael DeLong","date":"2025-04-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Consumer Goods Forum","Citizens Property Insurance Corporation","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Fannie Mae","Robert Wood Johnson Foundation","Guy Carpenter","Urban Institute","Department of Housing and Urban Development","US Department of the Treasury","Consumer Federation of America"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://consumerfed.org/media/legacy/OverburdenedReport.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This April 2025 Consumer Federation of America report analyzes proprietary Quadrant Information Services data to document homeowners insurance premium increases across nearly every U.S. ZIP code from 2021 to 2024. The typical homeowner saw premiums rise 24% ($648/year) to $3,303 annually, outpacing 13% cumulative inflation. Florida leads at $9,462/year. Collectively, American homeowners paid an estimated $21\u2013$27 billion more in 2024 than in 2021. The report recommends public reinsurance, mandatory insurer data disclosure, and federally backed risk mitigation investment programs.","insights":["From 2021 to 2024, average U.S. homeowners insurance premiums rose 24% ($648/year) to $3,303 annually for a $350,000 replacement-value home with a mid-range credit score, 11 percentage points above cumulative inflation of 13% over the same period.","Premiums increased in 95% of U.S. ZIP codes; one-third of ZIP codes saw increases exceeding 30%, demonstrating that the affordability crisis is nationwide, not concentrated in coastal or high-risk states alone.","American homeowners of single-family homes collectively paid an estimated $21 billion more in 2024 than in 2021; including condo owners, renters, and manufactured-home owners raises the total to approximately $27 billion in additional annual premiums.","Florida is the most expensive state at $9,462/year average ($789/month), driven by recent major hurricanes (Ian 2022, Idalia 2023, Helene and Milton 2024); the state-backed insurer of last resort, Citizens Property Insurance, held 1.3 million policies as of September 2024.","States with the largest absolute dollar increases were Florida (+$2,118), Louisiana (+$1,775), and Kentucky (+$1,426); largest percentage increases were Utah (+59%), Illinois (+50%), Arizona (+48%), and Pennsylvania (+44%), all inland, non-coastal states.","Research cited in the report found that a $500 premium increase was linked to a 20% rise in the likelihood of mortgage delinquency within 12 months, directly connecting insurance affordability to housing finance system stability.","Rural ZIP codes saw 22% premium increases vs. 25% in urban/suburban areas, with 2024 rural averages of $3,317, dispelling the notion that rural areas are insulated from the crisis."],"keywords":["homeowners insurance premiums","property insurance affordability","insurance market withdrawal","public reinsurance","force-placed insurance","insurance data transparency"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":177,"title":"\u2018Not If, but When\u2019: Cities Face Rising Climate Risk as Downgrade Jolts U.S. Municipal Bonds","authors":"Gaye Taylor","date":"2025-03-26","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["The Energy Mix"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.theenergymix.com/not-if-but-when-cities-face-rising-climate-risk-as-downgrade-jolts-u-s-municipal-bonds/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The recent downgrade of the Los Angeles Department of Water and Power by S&P has prompted concerns over the resilience of the U.S. municipal bond market amid increasing climate risks. While some experts warn of potential financial strains stemming from climate-related disasters, municipalities in both the U.S. and Canada, particularly cities like Toronto, Montreal, and Vancouver, are increasingly recognizing these risks and leveraging green bonds to finance climate-related initiatives, signaling a positive development toward climate risk awareness and adaptation.","insights":["S&P downgraded the creditworthiness of the Los Angeles Department of Water and Power, indicating cracks in the municipal bond market.","In Canada, municipalities are proactively disclosing climate-related financial risks and utilizing green bonds to fund climate initiatives.","The recent wildfires in Los Angeles illustrate the impact of climate change on municipal finances and bond ratings.","Municipalities are experiencing delayed financial repercussions from climate disasters, often facing budget deficits years after events occur.","Insurers are withdrawing from high-risk areas, further straining the financial resources of municipalities reliant on property taxes.","FEMA's role as a financial backstop for disaster recovery is becoming more fragile, potentially affecting municipal bond markets.","The demand for municipal green bonds is increasing, with Toronto raising over $1 billion to support net-zero goals.","Investments in resilience and adaptation can help prevent major financial losses related to climate impacts.","A sector-wide focus on climate risk is driving municipalities to adapt and strengthen their financial resilience."],"keywords":["municipal finance","bond market","climate impact","financial risk","green investment"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":555,"title":"How Philanthropy Can Boost Adaptation Finance in Developing Countries","authors":"Marilou Uy; Carter Brandon","date":"2025-03-21","year":2025,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Africa"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute","World Bank","Climate Policy Initiative","World Economic Forum","Center for Global Development"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wri.org/insights/philanthropy-adaptation-finance-developing-countries","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A World Resources Institute commentary by Marilou Uy and Carter Brandon setting out five ways philanthropic foundations could work with multilateral development banks to scale adaptation finance in developing countries. It frames the gap with numbers: adaptation and resilience financing for developing countries remains far below the $215 billion to $387 billion needed annually by 2030, while total philanthropic giving for climate change is estimated at $9.9 billion to $16.4 billion a year, of which only about $60 million goes to adaptation. Foundation climate-related support to developing countries has tripled over five years to $112 million for Africa, which is 6 per cent of total foundation funding. The authors note that even a ninefold increase in foundation climate grant-making in Africa would reach only $1 billion, about 2 per cent of the region's estimated annual adaptation need to 2030. The five avenues include co-financing projects with MDBs and providing grant financing to the Fund for Responding to Loss and Damage operationalised at COP28.","insights":["The argument for philanthropic capital in adaptation is structural rather than moral: many adaptation actions reduce future climate risk without generating the financial returns required to repay an MDB loan, so grant money is the only tranche that fits that risk-return shape.","The authors are explicit that the constraint is not only volume but debt: the 2024 IHLEG report emphasised mobilising funding that does not add to a nation's debt, which rules out most of the instruments a middle-income borrower would otherwise reach for.","The 900 per cent arithmetic is the most useful number in the piece. It sets a ceiling on what philanthropy can do alone, which is why every one of the five avenues is a leverage mechanism on MDB balance sheets rather than a direct grant channel.","Philanthropic climate giving is 6.1 per cent of an estimated $885 billion of total giving in 2023, and adaptation's share of that is roughly 0.4 per cent, so adaptation is a rounding error inside a rounding error."],"keywords":["adaptation finance gap","philanthropy","multilateral development banks","co-financing","Loss and Damage Fund","concessional finance","grant financing","Africa"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":630,"title":"Climate Regulatory Risk and Corporate Bonds","authors":"Lee H. Seltzer; Laura Starks; Qifei Zhu","date":"2025-03-15","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Bureau of Economic Research","Federal Reserve Bank of New York","University of Texas at Austin","Nanyang Technological University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w29994/w29994.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"NBER Working Paper 29994, issued April 2022 and revised March 2025, by Lee H. Seltzer of the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, Laura Starks of the McCombs School of Business at the University of Texas at Austin, and Qifei Zhu. The paper asks whether climate risk affects the risk assessment and pricing of corporate securities, particularly for firms facing potential regulatory restrictions. Using a shock to expected climate regulations as the identifying event, the authors find that climate regulatory risks causally affect both bond credit ratings and yield spreads. A structural credit model then locates the mechanism: the increased spreads for high-carbon issuers, especially those in stricter regulatory environments, derive from changes in firms' asset volatilities rather than their asset values. The conclusion drawn is that regulatory uncertainty itself affects security pricing, with implications for corporate decisions, portfolio management and policymaking. The paper is circulated for discussion and has not been peer reviewed or reviewed by the NBER Board.","insights":["**Volatility rather than value is the finding that matters.** The spread widening on high-carbon issuers is not the market marking down expected cash flows, it is the market pricing uncertainty about the rules, which predicts that spreads move on policy news without any change in the underlying asset.","The identification comes from a shock to expected climate regulation rather than from a correlation, so the result is causal on ratings and yield spreads rather than associative.","The direction of the effect is worth stating precisely: the instrument reprices in response to REGULATORY risk, not in response to a resilience benefit, so it prices the policy environment rather than the physical one.","The paper is explicitly not peer reviewed and not reviewed by the NBER Board, which the working paper series states on its own cover, so that caveat travels with any figure taken from it."],"keywords":["climate regulatory risk","corporate bonds","credit ratings","yield spreads","asset volatility","structural credit model","carbon intensity"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":97,"title":"For the World\u2019s Largest Companies, Climate Physical Risks Have a $1.2 Trillion Annual Price Tag by the 2050s","authors":"Matt MacFarland, Rick Lord, Tyyra Linko","date":"2025-03-10","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["S&P Global Sustainable1"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.spglobal.com/sustainable1/en/insights/special-editorial/ceraweek-physical-risk","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"At CERAWeek, S&P Global analysts and industry leaders examined escalating losses from extreme weather and the need to integrate physical hazard insights into risk management. Insurers are recalibrating underwriting models and repricing policies, while corporates leverage high-resolution mapping and scenario analysis to pinpoint asset vulnerabilities. New data-driven platforms were unveiled to deliver real-time visualization of flood, wildfire and storm exposure, supporting investment in resilience measures. Panelists emphasized cross-sector collaboration and innovative finance instruments to strengthen infrastructure preparedness and manage future disruptions.","insights":["The headline number is explicitly a no-adaptation counterfactual: $1.2 trillion annually by 2050 for the S&P Global 1200 under SSP2-4.5, assuming no adaptation measures and unadjusted for inflation, so it measures the cost of inaction rather than a forecast.","The costs are dominated by chronic hazards rather than the acute events that attract attention: extreme heat and water stress account for the majority of projected impact, while wildfires and cyclones supply the imagery.","Utilities face the largest exposure, with the average electric utility projected at $4.6 billion annually in the 2050s, about 4.6 times the all-industry average, and the report notes utilities are nonetheless further ahead on adaptation planning than most sectors.","The estimates cover impacts to company assets and operations only, and do not incorporate changes to demand or revenues, so they are a floor rather than a full financial impact.","Physical risk assessment has become common in utilities and energy while far fewer companies quantify the financial impact of that risk, which is the gap between disclosure and pricing."],"keywords":["physical risk","risk modeling","infrastructure resilience","insurance","data analytics"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":350,"title":"Climate-Resilient Debt Clauses: A Primer for FiCS Members","authors":"Maura Cravero, Karla D. Gonz\u00e1lez Esquinca, Taarika Peres","date":"2025-02-28","year":2025,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global","Caribbean","Latin America"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative","World Bank","Inter-American Development Bank","African Development Bank","Asian Development Bank","European Investment Bank","International Finance Corporation","Fitch Ratings","Climate Bonds Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/climate-resilient-debt-clauses-a-primer-for-fics-members/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This February 2025 primer, authored by Climate Policy Initiative for the Finance in Common Summit (FiCS) Lab Working Group, explains climate-resilient debt clauses (CRDCs), contractual terms that temporarily freeze sovereign debt service following specified disaster triggers. It covers CRDC design features (triggers, deferral terms, NPV neutrality), ratings implications (Fitch confirmed ratings-neutral for borrowers and MDBs), current adoption by MDBs and export credit agencies, and a decision tree guiding development finance institutions through feasibility assessment. Grenada's 2024 hurricane clause activation suspended USD 12 million in interest payments, illustrating real-world mechanics.","insights":["Grenada's 2015 hurricane bond CRDC was triggered in late 2024, suspending USD 12 million in interest payments, equivalent to 11% of the bond's value, providing the first documented activation of a sovereign CRDC in a bond context.","Fitch confirmed CRDCs are ratings-neutral for both borrowing sovereigns and MDBs, provided clauses are NPV-neutral and activation is treated as contractual rather than arrears, removing a key barrier to broader adoption.","CRDCs are structured to be NPV-neutral for creditors, deferring rather than canceling repayments, but carry a latent 'de facto junior status' risk: if a sovereign later restructures, rescheduled CRDC payments fall within restructuring scope while non-CRDC creditors continued receiving payments.","UK Export Finance, the first ECA to offer CRDCs (April 2023), has signed or nearly finalized CRDC-containing lending arrangements with eight borrowing countries, backed by GBP 7 billion (~USD 9.1 billion) in sustainable financing since 2019 plus a committed GBP 2 billion (~USD 2.5 billion) pipeline.","Caribbean Development Bank stochastic modeling (with Lazard, 2024) found that simultaneous CRDC triggering across a large share of its portfolio could materially strain its liquidity, requiring dedicated liquidity buffers and risk mitigation policies before scale adoption, CDB currently holds an AA+ credit rating it must protect.","The ICMA 2022 standardized term sheet specifies 1\u20132 year deferrals of principal and interest, up to three deferrals per instrument, and four repayment modalities, providing a practical framework that has already influenced MDB and DFI policy design.","At least six major MDBs (World Bank, IDB, EIB, AfDB, EBRD, ADB) and one ECA (UKEF) have committed to or are piloting CRDCs, with the IDB offering principal payment options to 12 eligible countries and the World Bank targeting Small States and SIDS."],"keywords":["state-contingent debt","sovereign debt deferral","disaster risk finance","debt sustainability","small island developing states","parametric triggers","multilateral development banks","export credit agency"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":66,"title":"Climate Investment Planning and Mobilization Framework","authors":"","date":"2025-02-26","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["NDC Partnership","Green Climate Fund"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://climateinvestment.ndcpartnership.org/?_gl=1%2A1afbsey%2A_ga%2AMTcxMTIxMDUzNy4xNzcwMjI2MTkw%2A_ga_75XVT9LFB8%2AczE3NzAyMjYxOTAkbzEkZzEkdDE3NzAyMjcxOTIkajUwJGwwJGgw","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Climate Investment Planning and Mobilization Framework, developed by the NDC Partnership and the Green Climate Fund, is a dynamic resource designed to support countries in effectively mobilizing climate finance. It focuses on identifying investable projects, fostering stakeholder engagement, and enhancing institutional capacities, while ensuring that approaches to investment planning are evidence-based and adaptable to continually evolving climate needs. The framework emphasizes the integration of climate strategies with broader development goals and the critical role of both public and private sectors in achieving climate objectives.","insights":["The framework is a living document that evolves through stakeholder feedback and new knowledge.","Strong institutional arrangements and stakeholder engagement are crucial for effective implementation of climate investment strategies.","Evidence-based decision-making is key for identifying and prioritizing investment needs aligned with climate goals.","Feedback loops facilitate ongoing learning and adaptation of policies and investment strategies.","Private sector engagement is essential for unlocking finance and innovative solutions for climate investments.","Holistic finance mobilization approaches are necessary to scale up investments across all sectors.","Mapping financing sources to investment needs is critical for effective mobilization.","Prioritization methodologies should reach stakeholder consensus to streamline resource allocation."],"keywords":["institutional capacity","funding strategy","adaptive management","investment prioritization","socio-economic impacts"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":106,"title":"Investing in the Nation\u2019s Future: Why Congress Should Emulate Energy States\u2019 Sovereign Wealth Funds","authors":"Nathan Brooks, Mark Haggerty","date":"2025-02-18","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Center for American Progress"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.americanprogress.org/article/investing-in-the-nations-future-why-congress-should-emulate-energy-states-sovereign-wealth-funds/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This report advocates for the establishment of a federal sovereign wealth fund modeled after state sovereign wealth funds, which effectively save and invest revenue from fossil fuel extraction to provide stable financial resources for state and local governments. It highlights the vulnerability of energy-producing regions to fluctuations in fossil fuel revenue and proposes strategies to ensure sustainable economic growth by diversifying investments and stabilizing funding for essential services.","insights":["States have successfully created sovereign wealth funds to manage and invest fossil fuel revenues for long-term financial stability.","Lea County, New Mexico, experienced a dramatic increase in tax revenue from fossil fuels, underscoring the volatility of such incomes.","Congress is the only major fossil fuel-producing entity without a sovereign wealth fund, leading to reliance on short-term revenue sharing.","A federal sovereign wealth fund could stabilize payments to local governments and mitigate economic risks linked to energy production.","Successful models from states like New Mexico and Alaska illustrate how permanent funds can provide intergenerational benefits.","Investment strategies for a federal fund should target economic diversification and support the energy transition.","Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs) and other intermediaries can facilitate investments that align with local needs.","The proposed fund must be governed transparently and involve stakeholder representation to ensure accountability.","Achieving adequate returns on a federal fund requires careful management of the investment strategy, balancing risk and sustainability.","There is an existing ecosystem of rural-focused financial institutions that could partner with a federal fund to enhance its impact."],"keywords":["sovereign wealth funds","economic diversification","public investment","financial stability","community development"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":497,"title":"Planning for Resilient Infrastructure in a Higher-Cost Environment","authors":"Sebastian Torres, Pamela Frederick, Neene Jenkins, Christopher Jumper, Sarah Snyder, Michael Wertz","date":"2025-02-12","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Nixon Peabody","Battery Park City Authority","JPMorgan Chase","Assured Guaranty","Moody's","First Street Foundation","Federal Emergency Management Agency","American Society of Civil Engineers (ASCE)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.bondbuyer.com/national-outlook-2025/planning-for-resilient-infrastructure-in-a-higher-cost-environment","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Bond Buyer National Outlook 2025 panel, published as a full transcript, moderated by Sebastian Torres, Counsel in the Project and Public Finance Practice Group at Nixon Peabody, with Pamela Frederick, CFO of the Battery Park City Authority; Neene Jenkins, Head of Municipal Research at JP Morgan Asset Management; Christopher Jumper, Director at Assured Guaranty; Sarah Snyder, Managing Director at Ramirez & Co; and Michael Wertz, Vice President and Senior Analyst at Moody's Ratings. On scale, Wertz cites an ASCE figure of about $7.4 trillion of infrastructure need over thirty years, an EPA assessment of $1.2 trillion for water and sewer alone, and a First Street Foundation estimate of $1.5 trillion of property value at risk over thirty years, mostly through insurance and the cost of home ownership; he argues the burden falls where it has since the 1960s, on state and local governments, and that even with the IIJA in place a $2 to $4 trillion gap would remain. Jenkins notes 27 billion-dollar weather and climate disasters in the last year, three times the annual level since 1980. On meeting the need, Frederick describes Battery Park City as a high-resource issuer inside a coordinated lower Manhattan coastal resiliency programme of separate but interconnected projects, on a five-year horizon for its third resiliency project, and argues that smaller issuers need efficient access to capital through state bond banks, with the tension between losing local control and gaining organised state or federal funding. On public-private partnerships, Frederick and Snyder agree they broaden access but require a project that can support a private return and a cash flow stream to secure project finance debt, which many resilience projects lack; Jenkins has seen private investment but nothing at the scale of the need.","insights":["The panel's own framing of demand is the useful correction: appetite for the debt is not in doubt, the open question is the price and the disclosure burden that comes with it, which turns resilience finance into a disclosure problem rather than a capital-availability one.","Wertz's position that steady rate and tax increases sized to need are a hallmark of good governance is a rating agency saying out loud that raising rates improves credit, which inverts the political instinct that rate increases are a sign of distress.","The affordability finding is distributional and specific: debt loads are broadly affordable, but the southern Gulf coast and tornado alley combine the highest exposure with the weakest tax and rate base, so the places that most need to borrow are the least able to.","Frederick names the structural reason public-private partnerships rarely fit a resilience project: there has to be a stream of cash flows to secure the debt, and a sea wall protects value rather than generating revenue, which is the Benefit to Instrument gap in one sentence.","The loss of the municipal tax exemption is treated as the live risk across every answer, and Frederick's response, preparing to access overseas markets with their higher disclosure standards, is the clearest statement of what that would cost an issuer operationally.","Wertz's desalination anecdote is the lead-time argument: a plant built for drought preparedness finished after the drought ended and was never started up, so a project's value depends on delivery speed as much as on design.","Jumper's list, rate stabilisation funds, easing covenants and opening lower liens, is what issuers are actually doing to absorb resilience capital cost, and all three trade future flexibility for present affordability."],"keywords":["municipal bonds","tax exemption","rate stabilisation fund","disclosure","public-private partnership","state bond bank","Battery Park City","lead time"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":135,"title":"Mapping Global Financial Risks Under Climate Change","authors":"Antoine Mandel, Stefano Battiston, Irene Monasterolo","date":"2025-02-05","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Finance Alpha","University of Paris 1 Pantheon-Sorbonne","Ca' Foscari University of Venice","University of Zurich","CEPR","Utrecht University"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-025-02244-x","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Nature Climate Change analysis (March 2025) develops CLIMACRED-PHYS, a structural credit-risk model that maps global climate-related physical risks onto bond and equity valuations for 2,934 firms in the MSCI ACWI universe (~85% of global investable equity). Using bottom-up geolocation of ~130,000 production facilities across six hazard types and three RCP scenarios, the model finds that under an intermediary scenario (RCP 6.0, 2050), mean equity losses range from 10\u201313% and bond losses from 1.5\u20133.7%, with 99th-percentile equity VaR reaching up to 17.4%. Cross-border risk transfer from Asian production facilities to European and North American capital holders is a key structural finding.","insights":["Under the intermediary climate scenario (RCP 6.0, 2050), the mean equity loss on a portfolio representative of global market capitalization ranges from 10.12% to 12.88%, and the 99th-percentile (VaR) equity loss ranges from 13.78% to 17.36%, substantially amplifying the direct physical capital loss of 2.22% mean / 3.8% VaR.","Financial amplification ratios (ratio of equity loss to direct productive capital loss) range from 1 to 5 depending on firm leverage, consistent with prior flood-specific analyses. Amplification in the tail (VaR) is more acute than at the mean, indicating non-linear risk escalation at portfolio extremes.","In the high-end scenario (RCP 8.5, 2080), additional losses relative to the current climate scenario reach up to 3 percentage points for equities and 80 basis points for bonds, quantifying the marginal cost of unmitigated warming above current conditions.","Cross-border risk transmission is material: direct climate impacts in eastern, southeastern, and southern Asia are partly exported to firms headquartered in Europe and North America. European firms show particularly high equity-impact-to-direct-impact ratios, making them primary recipients of external climate shocks.","Eastern Asia holds 23.7% of global capital value but absorbs 39.4% of global impacts (impact-to-value ratio 1.66), driven by tropical cyclones and coastal floods. Southern Asia's impact-to-value ratio reaches 1.78. Northern America holds 36.8% of value but only 22% of impacts (ratio 0.60), reflecting geographic diversification benefits.","Tropical cyclones are the single largest hazard by aggregate impact, followed by coastal floods, river floods, winter storms, and wildfires. Compound impacts (multi-hazard) at the 99th percentile reach 4.32% capital destruction and 6.14 business-interruption days under RCP 8.5/2080."],"keywords":["physical risk valuation","CLIMACRED-PHYS","equity loss amplification","cross-border climate risk","probability of default","asset-level geolocation","RCP scenarios","MSCI ACWI"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":587,"title":"Rhode Island Municipal Resilience Program","authors":"Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank","date":"2025-02-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Rhode Island"],"institutions":["Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.riib.org/solutions/programs/municipal-resilience-program/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank's programme specification for its Municipal Resilience Program, which descends from Resilient Rhody, the state's first comprehensive climate resilience action strategy released by Governor Raimondo in July 2018. The programme provides direct support to cities and towns to complete a municipal-driven workshop called Community Resilience Building, bringing climate information together with local knowledge to identify top hazards, current challenges and community strengths, and from that to identify priority projects. Workshops are delivered and funded by the Bank and its selected consultant, with trained facilitators, workshop facilitators and scribes assigned to each municipality, so municipal burden is minimal. On completing the workshop a municipality is designated a Resilient Rhody Municipality, which makes it eligible to apply annually for dedicated MRP Action Grants. Eligible projects must have been identified through the workshop, must improve climate resilience, and must result in design, engineering or construction: action grants are explicitly not available for research activities such as studies and strategic plans. Eligible work falls under the Bank's broad portfolio and can include dam retrofits or removal, road elevation, floodproofing or elevation of pump stations, berms and levees, culvert resizing, green stormwater infrastructure, solar and battery backup power, energy efficiency, watershed restoration, urban tree planting and coastal and riparian resiliency. Municipalities must match grants with a 25 per cent local project cost share. Cohorts are listed from 2019 onward.","insights":["A state **infrastructure bank** running the workshop-then-grant sequence is the interesting part. The Bank is a lender, so the resilience programme sits inside an institution that can also finance the projects it identifies, which is a route from appraisal to capital that a grant agency does not have.","The 25 per cent local cost share is the difference from the Massachusetts template this programme copied, and it is a real design choice: it filters for municipal commitment and it filters out the poorest municipalities, which is the equity cost of a match requirement.","Excluding studies and plans from action grants while fully funding the workshop stage means the Bank pays for appraisal itself and refuses to pay for it twice. That separation is what stops a resilience programme becoming a consultancy pipeline.","The eligible-works list is unusually broad for a resilience grant, spanning dam removal, road elevation, green stormwater infrastructure, battery backup and urban tree planting, because it inherits the Bank's existing programme portfolio rather than defining a new one."],"keywords":["Municipal Resilience Program","Community Resilience Building","action grant","local cost share","Resilient Rhody","infrastructure bank","green stormwater infrastructure","culvert resizing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":531,"title":"Scaling up finance and investment for climate change adaptation OECD Net Zero+","authors":"Kristina Feikova","date":"2025-02-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["OECD"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/02/scaling-up-finance-and-investment-for-climate-change-adaptation_0bcbbdbf/b8d425a2-en.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An OECD policy paper on why global adaptation finance remains well below need and what policy can do about it. It presents recent evidence on the finance and investment gap and sets out the enabling environment: detailed funding strategies for National Adaptation Plans, mainstreaming adaptation into public budgeting, and policy coherence. For financial markets it argues for enhanced transparency in financial disclosures, aligned private and public incentives, and expanded risk transfer mechanisms. For developing countries it argues the binding question is better strategic use of international public finance, naming five actions: assessing whether providers' spending plans are consistent with climate finance goals, supporting enabling environments, improving development practices and systems, leveraging public and blended finance to mobilise private investment, and exploring innovative financing sources.","insights":["Public budgeting is treated as an adaptation finance instrument in its own right: mainstreaming adaptation considerations into the budget process, rather than running adaptation as a programme beside it, is named as integral to the enabling environment.","National Adaptation Plans are said to need detailed FUNDING strategies rather than plans alone, which locates the gap between ambition and investment in the absence of a costed financing route.","Risk transfer is placed alongside disclosure and incentive alignment as one of three market-side requirements, which puts insurance in the enabling environment rather than treating it as a separate sector.","The paper's developing-country argument is about allocation rather than volume: the key action is assessing the consistency of international providers' spending plans with climate finance goals, which is a question about what existing money is doing.","It was developed under the OECD Horizontal Project on Climate and Economic Resilience and drew on six OECD committees including the Insurance and Private Pensions Committee and the Committee of Senior Budget Officials, which is why budgeting and insurance sit together in one paper."],"keywords":["adaptation finance gap","National Adaptation Plans","public budgeting","risk transfer","blended finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":347,"title":"Debt for Climate Swaps: A Primer for FiCS Members","authors":"Jyoti Sharma","date":"2025-02-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative","Inter-American Development Bank","The Nature Conservancy","World Bank","International Finance Corporation","African Development Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/debt-for-climate-swaps-a-primer-for-fics-members/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Climate Policy Initiative primer, prepared for the FiCS Lab Debt-for-Climate Swaps working group (February 2025), defines and explains debt-for-climate (DFC) swaps, instruments that cancel, exchange, or refinance sovereign debt in return for commitments to invest savings in climate or conservation projects. It traces the instrument's history from Bolivia (1987) through landmark deals in Seychelles ($21.6M, 2015), Belize ($364M blue bond, 2021), and Ecuador ($656M blue bond, 2023, the largest ever), and sets out key design conditions for effectiveness, including additionality, fiscal space, creditor coordination, and the roles of multilateral and national development banks in credit enhancement, monitoring, and capacity building.","insights":["Ecuador's 2023 DFC swap converted USD 1.6 billion of commercial debt into a USD 656 million blue bond loan, the largest ever debt-for-nature conversion, requiring a 60,000 km\u00b2 expansion of marine reserves and combining IDB guarantees with USDFC political-risk insurance for the first time at a multilateral institution.","Belize's 2021 blue bond deal retired USD 553 million of public debt at a 45% discount for USD 364 million, committing 30% of ocean territory to protection; the USDFC provided $610 million in political-risk insurance as credit enhancement.","Seychelles' 2015 swap bought back USD 21.6 million in official bilateral Paris Club debt at a discounted price of USD 20.2 million, expanding protected marine areas from 0.04% to 30% of national waters, demonstrating outsized conservation outcomes from a relatively small transaction.","DFC swaps are most effective when limited fiscal space, not policy will, is the primary barrier to climate investment; in debt-distress situations they are generally less effective because savings are needed for relief rather than new climate commitments.","MDBs typically cover only ~70% of required credit enhancement via guarantees, creating a structural gap that regional public development banks could fill through first-loss/second-loss insurance layering, potentially unlocking a wider pipeline of swap transactions.","Additionality is a critical design requirement: concessional inputs (guarantees, donations, credit enhancements) must generate incremental environmental benefits beyond planned government spending, verified through impact metrics.","Preparation timelines of 2\u20134 years are typical, reflecting complexity in project identification, multi-party coordination, and negotiation, implying significant transaction-cost barriers that capacity-building by national development banks could reduce."],"keywords":["debt-for-nature swaps","sovereign debt conversion","blue bonds","marine conservation finance","credit enhancement","public development banks","fiscal space"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":131,"title":"Natural Habitats Can Reduce Flood Losses","authors":"Christian Decker, Anna Retsa, Gillian Rutherford","date":"2025-02-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Swiss Re"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.swissre.com/institute/research/topics-and-risk-dialogues/climate-and-natural-catastrophe-risk/coastal-and-flood-protection-natural-habitats.html?utm_campaign=cpn-2186_mcr-ad-hoc-2024_group_global_2024&utm_medium=socialMedia_organic&utm_source=linkedin&utm_content=climaterisk_n/a_publication_en&utm_term=socialOrganicCoastalFloodPost1of2","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Swiss Re Institute's analysis reveals that natural coastal habitats, such as mangroves, coral reefs, and salt marshes, significantly mitigate flood losses in Florida. Their presence reduces the frequency of insurance claims from lower severity flood events by approximately 50%, highlighting the importance of preserving these ecosystems for improved coastal protection and financial resilience against flooding.","insights":["Florida has the highest coastal property exposure to storm surges in the US.","40% of NFIP claims in Florida coastal areas were due to lower severity events.","High BES coastal protection areas had a claim frequency of only 1%, compared to 2.8% in less protected areas.","Natural habitats can reduce insured flood loss frequency by 42% to 65%.","The degradation of coastal habitats can lead to increased flood damages, estimated at USD 385 million annually in Florida.","Natural barriers like mangroves and coral reefs provide quantifiable flood protection benefits.","Insurers can collaborate with local stakeholders to support ecosystem preservation.","Nature-based solutions can yield high benefit-to-cost ratios in coastal flood protection.","Innovative insurance products can incentivize the restoration of coastal habitats post-storm."],"keywords":["storm surge","insurance claims","ecosystem services","natural barriers","flood mitigation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":334,"title":"Africa Delivers the First Mechanism for International Cooperation on Climate Change Adaptation Under the Paris Agreement","authors":"","date":"2025-01-29","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Africa","Global"],"institutions":["African Development Bank","UNFCCC Adaptation Private Sector Initiative","NDC Partnership","AllAfrica"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://allafrica.com/stories/202501310503.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The African Development Bank's Adaptation Benefits Mechanism (ABM), piloted across Africa since 2019, became the first non-market approach registered on the UNFCCC's Non-Market Approaches Platform in December 2024, following submission by Uganda at COP29 in Baku. ABM certifies quantified adaptation benefits using rigorous methodologies and independent verification, enabling project developers to monetize certified resilience benefits via Adaptation Benefits Supply Agreements and access capital markets. The mechanism supports Paris Agreement reporting, SDG tracking, and ESG frameworks, targeting new public and private adaptation finance flows for African countries.","insights":["ABM is the first non-market approach registered on the UNFCCC Non-Market Approaches Platform (December 2024), establishing a novel international cooperative mechanism under Article 6.8 of the Paris Agreement specifically for adaptation finance.","The mechanism certifies quantified adaptation benefits through rigorous methodologies and independent verification, making resilience improvements and incremental costs transparent, directly addressing the measurement gap that has historically kept adaptation off institutional investors' balance sheets.","Adaptation Benefits Supply Agreements allow project developers to monetize certified resilience benefits and access capital markets, creating a nascent tradeable instrument analogous to carbon credits but for adaptation outcomes.","ABM was submitted to the UNFCCC by Uganda at COP29 (2024) with initial support from six African nations (Nigeria, Kenya, Madagascar, Benin, Gambia, Guinea) and a coalition of international organizations and corporations, indicating early but broadening political and institutional backing.","The mechanism explicitly targets de-risking of adaptation investments for African project developers, positioning it as a blended-finance enabler that can mobilize new and additional public and private sector capital for interventions not otherwise affordable to end beneficiaries.","ABM data supports Paris Agreement reporting, SDG tracking, and ESG frameworks simultaneously, potentially reducing reporting costs for investors with multiple compliance obligations.","The African Development Bank holds a dual role (as a participant in ABM and as a provider of financial, technology, and capacity-building support), which may affect perception of independence but also signals strong institutional backing for scale-up."],"keywords":["adaptation benefits certification","non-market approaches","Article 6.8","UNFCCC registration","adaptation monetization","Africa adaptation finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":17,"title":"Weather and Waste: Rethinking Small Business Support Following a Natural Disaster","authors":"Kristen Fanarakis","date":"2025-01-28","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Milken Institute"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://milkeninstitute.org/content-hub/research-and-reports/reports/weather-and-waste-rethinking-small-business-support-following-natural-disaster","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This January 2025 Milken Institute executive summary diagnoses the fragmented federal response to small business disaster recovery and proposes a seven-point reform agenda. It documents a 65% small business failure rate following natural disasters (40% never reopen; 25% of those that do close within a year) and situates this against 24 billion-dollar weather disasters in the U.S. in 2024 alone. Key recommendations include a cross-agency Small Business Resilience czar, a national disaster resource hub, an SBA long-term recovery loan fund, standardized grant applications, and targeted tax credits and insurance relief.","insights":["FEMA estimates 40% of small businesses never reopen after a natural disaster, and 25% of those that do reopen close within one year, a cumulative 65% failure rate that represents unmeasured waste on federal and state small-business investment.","The frequency of billion-dollar U.S. weather disasters has risen from an average of 3.3 per year in the 1980s to more than 17 per year over the past decade, dramatically expanding the exposure of small businesses to climate-related disruption.","As of November 1, 2024, there were 24 confirmed billion-dollar weather-related disasters in the U.S. in that year alone, one of the costliest years since 1980, signaling accelerating fiscal pressure on post-disaster recovery systems.","Federal disaster recovery programs are spread across more than 30 federal entities, creating coordination failures documented after Hurricane Sandy; state and local officials reported that navigating these programs resulted in missed funding opportunities.","The report frames small business failure after disasters as government waste (not just overspending, but spending that fails to maximize return on public investment), and calls for efficacy metrics alongside fiscal discipline in disaster recovery budgeting.","A proposed standardized post-disaster application (modeled on the FAFSA) could reduce administrative barriers for small business owners seeking grants and loans, potentially improving capital deployment speed after disasters.","Denial of business interruption insurance claims for businesses unable to open due to loss of municipal utilities is identified as a coverage gap requiring immediate grant-based policy remedy.","One-time tax credits for small businesses that contributed time, resources, or space to community recovery are proposed as an incentive mechanism to recognize and encourage private-sector first-response roles."],"keywords":["small business disaster recovery","post-disaster survival rate","federal program coordination","business interruption insurance gap","disaster loan fund"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":104,"title":"Healthcare in a Changing Climate: Investing in Resilient Solutions","authors":"Shyam Bishen, Fernando Gomez, Sven Braun, Oliver Eitelwein, Judith Moore, Matthew Watkins","date":"2025-01-17","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum","Oliver Wyman"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/publications/healthcare-in-a-changing-climate-investing-in-resilient-solutions/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource \"Healthcare in a Changing Climate: Investing in Resilient Solutions\" emphasizes the urgent need for healthcare systems to adapt to the increasing health challenges posed by climate change, projecting significant economic and health impacts. By investing strategically in innovative health interventions, particularly within the life sciences sector, it argues that substantial lives can be saved, economic losses reduced, and public health strengthened against climate-related threats, requiring enhanced collaboration among various sectors to drive progress and overcome existing barriers to investment.","insights":["Climate change is projected to lead to an additional 14.5 million deaths and $12.5 trillion in global economic costs by 2050.","Investment of $65 billion in health interventions could save 6.5 million lives and reduce economic losses by $5.8 trillion.","The pandemic demonstrated the potential for rapid collaboration among sectors, which should be applied to climate-related health challenges.","Public-private partnerships are essential for de-risking investments in climate-sensitive health solutions.","Innovative interventions may show varying effectiveness in preventing health impacts compared to economic losses and therefore require distinct assessment approaches."],"keywords":["public health","innovation","life sciences","health systems","collaboration"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":214,"title":"U.S. Department of the Treasury Report: Homeowners Insurance Costs Rising, Availability Declining as Climate-Related Events Take Their Toll","authors":"","date":"2025-01-16","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["US Department of the Treasury"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy2791","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The U.S. Department of the Treasury's Federal Insurance Office has released a comprehensive report highlighting a significant rise in homeowners insurance costs and a decline in availability due to climate-related events. The report covers data from over 330 insurers and reveals alarming trends such as substantial premium increases in high-risk areas, higher nonrenewal rates, and increased operational costs for insurers tied to the frequency and severity of climate disasters.","insights":["Homeowners insurance costs are rising at an annual rate of 8.7% faster than inflation from 2018 to 2022.","Consumers in the highest risk ZIP codes pay on average $2,321 in premiums, which is 82% higher than those in the lowest risk areas.","There is a significant disparity in policy nonrenewal rates, with the highest risk areas experiencing an 80% higher rate than the lowest risk areas.","Insurers face rising operational costs in areas prone to climate-related losses, with higher claims severity in these regions.","The report emphasizes the crucial role of homeowners insurance in protecting consumers and stabilizing local economies.","Historical data indicates that from 2018 to 2022, the U.S. faced 84 billion-dollar disasters costing over $609 billion, highlighting the economic impact of climate events.","The analysis is part of an ongoing collaborative effort to improve data on the homeowners insurance market and address its challenges.","The increased frequency and severity of climate events are creating significant barriers for consumers seeking affordable insurance.","The U.S. Department of the Treasury is promoting transparency by releasing aggregated ZIP Code level data for public access."],"keywords":["insurance market","homeowners","policy availability","economic impact","data transparency"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":107,"title":"Insuring Against Extreme Heat: Navigating Risks in a Warming World","authors":"Daniel Murphy","date":"2025-01-16","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global","California"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum","Allianz"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/publications/insuring-against-extreme-heat-navigating-risks-in-a-warming-world/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource 'Insuring Against Extreme Heat: Navigating Risks in a Warming World' emphasizes the increasing threat of extreme heat due to climate change, which adversely impacts human health, infrastructure, and economies. It advocates for collaborative efforts among the insurance industry, governments, and communities to create innovative insurance products and proactive strategies to enhance resilience. Moreover, it underscores the necessity of nature-based solutions and equitable approaches to address the risks associated with extreme heat, highlighting the urgent need for improved data analytics, community engagement, and effective policies.","insights":["Extreme heat is responsible for approximately 489,000 deaths annually, making it the deadliest climate risk.","Less than 60% of global weather-related losses are insured, leading to a $150 billion burden on governments and communities.","Nature-based solutions, such as tree canopies and cool roofs, can be more cost-effective than traditional infrastructure in mitigating heat effects.","Collaboration between insurers, governments, and communities is essential to develop effective heat action plans and improve resilience.","Early warning systems can significantly reduce mortality rates associated with extreme heat events.","Insurance products designed for other climate risks can be adapted to address extreme heat risks.","Public-private partnerships are essential in implementing large-scale interventions to mitigate extreme heat impacts.","Clear governance and funding frameworks are crucial for effective heatwave management."],"keywords":["insurance innovation","urban resilience","public health","nature-based solutions","community engagement"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":467,"title":"Parametric Insurance for Climate Adaptation in Fisheries and Aquaculture","authors":"Alistair J. Hobday; L. Richard Little; James R. Watson; Claire M. Spillman","date":"2025-01-15","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Oregon State University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11160-025-09920-3","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A review of parametric insurance as a climate adaptation instrument for fisheries and aquaculture, where a predetermined amount is paid out on parameters chosen to reflect expected losses from extreme events. The authors consider its utility, implementation and limitations, and their distinctive emphasis is on ocean forecasting as the enabling infrastructure: forecasting is what allows parametric products to be developed and priced, because anticipating changing environmental conditions across spatial and temporal scales is what informs both the trigger design and the resource allocation decisions the instrument is meant to support. They argue that combining climate-aware policy design with education, capacity building and financial support for industry transition could help seafood producers navigate climate change. Their most consequential claim is that parametric insurance can facilitate a managed retreat from vulnerable or non-viable areas toward more sustainable alternatives or livelihoods, by reducing climate risk in a measured environment, and can thereby support the long-term resilience of seafood industries and coastal communities while protecting marine ecosystem health.","insights":["Parametric cover is argued to enable managed retreat rather than to entrench exposure, by reducing risk in a measured way so producers can move to viable areas or livelihoods, which is the opposite of the moral hazard objection usually raised against subsidised cover.","Ocean forecasting is identified as the binding prerequisite: without forecasts at the right spatial and temporal scale there is no defensible trigger and no basis for pricing, so the data infrastructure comes before the instrument.","The authors pair the instrument with education, capacity building and transition finance, treating insurance alone as insufficient to move an industry.","Fisheries and aquaculture are an unusual and useful test case for parametric design because the insured asset is mobile and the hazard is an ocean state rather than a landfall event."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","fisheries","aquaculture","ocean forecasting","managed retreat","climate extremes","trigger design"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":116,"title":"Hybrid Coral Reef Restoration Can Be a Cost-Effective Nature-Based Solution to Provide Protection to Vulnerable Coastal Populations","authors":"Curt D. Storlazzi,  Borja G. Reguero, Kristen C. Alkins, James B. Shope, Camila Gaido-Lassarre, T. Shay Viehman, Michael W. Beck","date":"2025-01-15","year":2025,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Florida","Puerto Rico"],"institutions":["U.S. Geological Survey","Center for Coastal Climate Resilience at UC Santa Cruz","National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adn4004","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This resource evaluates the cost-effectiveness of hybrid coral reef restoration as a nature-based solution for enhancing coastal flood protection, particularly benefiting vulnerable populations in Florida and Puerto Rico. By demonstrating how restored reefs can significantly reduce wave energy and flood risks, the study highlights the socioeconomic advantages of restoration projects, including substantial annual economic benefits, improved social equity, and the potential for self-adaptation of coral reefs to climate impacts.","insights":["Hybrid coral reef restoration can dissipate up to 97% of wave energy, reducing coastal flooding.","Restoring 20% of regional coral reefs could yield flood risk reduction benefits exceeding restoration costs.","Vulnerable populations, such as minorities and low-income groups, could derive disproportionately higher benefits from coral reef restoration.","Annual economic benefits from restoration are estimated at $335 million in Florida and $56 million in Puerto Rico.","Benefit-to-cost analyses indicate that many coral restoration projects could provide positive returns on investment.","Dynamic flood modeling is essential for accurately assessing coastal flood risks and impacts.","Hybrid coral reefs have the potential for self-adaptation to changing conditions, maintaining protective benefits against rising sea levels.","Investing in coral reef restoration contributes to social equity by enhancing protection for marginalized communities."],"keywords":["coastal protection","flood risk reduction","socioeconomic benefits","nature-based solutions","environmental justice"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":584,"title":"2025 Climate and Catastrophe Insight","authors":"Aon","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Aon"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://assets.aon.com/-/media/files/aon/reports/2025/2025-climate-catastrophe-insight.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Aon's Climate and Catastrophe Insight report for 2025, with a foreword by chief executive Greg Case, covering natural disaster events and loss trends globally and by region across the United States, the non-US Americas, EMEA and Asia-Pacific. Its headline findings are that global economic losses were above average and driven by weather-related disasters, that global insured losses exceeded $145 billion in the sixth costliest year on record, and that global fatalities were among the lowest in 30 years.","insights":["The divergence between the two headlines is the finding: the sixth costliest insured-loss year on record coinciding with fatalities among the lowest in 30 years. Warning systems and disaster management are saving lives while exposure keeps raising the bill, which is the same decoupling the WMO record reports and is the strongest available evidence that the two benefits of resilience investment come apart.","The report quantifies the protection gap, the distance between economic and insured loss, which is the quantity a risk-transfer instrument exists to narrow.","The report is organised by region rather than by peril, so its totals can be used as a denominator for a specific market rather than only as a global headline."],"keywords":["insured losses","economic losses","protection gap","natural catastrophe","loss trends","fatalities","catastrophe insight"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":572,"title":"International Finance Corporation 2025 Climate-Related Disclosures","authors":"International Finance Corporation","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["International Finance Corporation","World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/2025/ifc-ar25-climate-related-disclosures.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The International Finance Corporation's 2025 climate-related disclosures, reporting as the private sector arm of the World Bank Group under the Group's Climate Change Action Plan. The figures that matter to this corpus are the split: IFC committed $8.1 billion of climate finance on its own account, of which $558 million supported adaptation finance, and mobilised a further $16.4 billion in core mobilisation for climate finance. It records the green bond history, a first issue in 2010 at $200 million and a programme that has since crossed $10 billion, a $3 billion Paris-aligned private debt portfolio, and the multilateral development bank announcement of a $120 billion a year ambition. The report defines climate finance as the share of financial resources committed to investments directly linked to climate resilience or low-carbon growth, and describes the WBG Scorecard as the enhanced measurement tool behind its metrics.","insights":["The ratio is the finding: $558 million of adaptation finance inside $8.1 billion of own-account climate finance is under 7 per cent. A disclosure report from the world's largest private-sector development financier is the strongest available evidence for how small the adaptation share of climate finance still is, and it is the institution's own number rather than a third-party estimate.","Mobilisation is twice the balance sheet, $16.4 billion against $8.1 billion, so the institution reports moving more private capital than it commits itself.","Because IFC defines climate finance as resilience or low-carbon linked, the adaptation line is a subset of a category that already includes resilience, so the 7 per cent cannot be read as the whole resilience share without reading the definitions section.","The green bond series, from $200 million in 2010 to over $10 billion cumulatively, is a fifteen-year issuance record from a single issuer, which is the kind of series a resilience-labelled instrument does not yet have anywhere."],"keywords":["IFC","climate finance","adaptation finance","core mobilization","green bond","Paris alignment","World Bank Group Scorecard","climate disclosure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":564,"title":"P\u00e1ramo Wildfire Resilience Facility","authors":"Strata Advisors; Global Innovation Lab for Climate Finance","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Latin America"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative","Strata Advisors","Humboldt Institute","United Nations Development Programme","Bloomberg Philanthropies"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatefinancelab.org/ideas/paramo-wildfire-resilience-facility/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Global Innovation Lab for Climate Finance instrument brief for the Paramo Wildfire Resilience Facility, proposed by Strata Advisors with Bogota's water fund Agua Somos and the Humboldt Institute. Paramos are high-altitude Andean ecosystems that cover 2 per cent of Colombia's territory and supply over 70 per cent of its drinking water; they cover 24 per cent of Bogota's watershed and provide all the city's drinking water, and wildfires affected nearly a third of that area between 1985 and 2022. Between November 2023 and January 2024 more than 340 fires destroyed over 17,000 hectares, overwhelming government funds and forcing crop-spraying planes into fire suppression, against a standing annual disaster response gap of USD 500 million. The Facility bundles two instruments under one governance structure. A wildfire parametric insurance pays out within 24 hours when parameters indicating high-intensity, high-burn-severity fire are met, with premiums paid by ecosystem service beneficiaries, utilities and large corporates dependent on paramo water, and payouts directed to fire brigades and ecosystem stewards. An Adaptation and Recovery Trust Fund finances pre-emptive risk reduction such as dry vegetation removal and community capacity building, and post-fire ecosystem rehabilitation, moving after the pilot to revenue-generating structures including habitat banks and results-based financing. The Bogota pilot mobilises USD 1.3 million, more than half from water-dependent companies, and targets water security for over 12 million people. The long-term plan is USD 8.6 million in grants, USD 3 million of private capital and USD 1.4 million of concessional capital.","insights":["The diagram labels the edge explicitly: **premium reduction through risk mitigation**. The trust fund's spending on vegetation removal and restoration lowers the expected loss the parametric policy underwrites, so the risk reduction pays for itself through the premium, applied to an ecosystem rather than to built infrastructure.","The premium payer is the ecosystem service beneficiary, not the landowner and not the state. Utilities and corporates that depend on paramo water buy the insurance that protects the watershed they draw from, which prices a natural asset by the revenue it protects downstream rather than by its own market value.","The 24-hour payout is aimed at a specific failure the document names: government funds were overwhelmed in the 2023 to 2024 fire season and equipment had to be improvised. Liquidity timing, not loss compensation, is what the instrument delivers, which is the benefit a payout actually provides.","The trust fund's stated path to habitat banks and results-based financing means the concessional layer is designed to become self-funding rather than to be replenished."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","wildfire","paramo","ecosystem services","water security","trust fund","premium reduction","habitat bank","results-based financing","Colombia"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":563,"title":"Tropical Resilience Fund (TREF)","authors":"Impact Earth; Global Innovation Lab for Climate Finance","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Latin America","Asia"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative","United Nations Development Programme","Bloomberg Philanthropies"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatefinancelab.org/ideas/tropical-resilience-fund/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Global Innovation Lab for Climate Finance instrument brief for the Tropical Resilience Fund, proposed by Impact Earth: a USD 100 million closed-ended mezzanine debt fund providing revenue-based loans, convertible debt and bridge loans to early and growth-stage ventures and nature projects that conserve, restore and protect biodiversity while making the investments themselves more climate resilient. It launches with a USD 30 million first close deploying in Latin America and expanding later to Southeast Asia, has a 12-year term with two possible one-year extensions, and targets a net internal rate of return of 10 per cent. The capital structure is a 70:30 senior-to-junior split, USD 70 million senior and USD 30 million junior, with institutional investors, development finance institutions and impact funds expected in the senior tranche and philanthropies and governments anchoring the junior first-loss layer. Risk mitigation combines regional diversification, the first-loss layer and a possible guarantee facility over the portfolio or selected verticals, and investors may allocate through the global fund or through regional compartments such as the Amazon Basin or Southeast Asian tropical forests. The stated barrier it addresses is that high perceived risk, long payback periods and high transaction costs have kept commercial and impact investors away from the early and growth stages.","insights":["The gap it names is a stage gap rather than a sector gap. Capital exists for mature nature projects; what is missing is money willing to sit at the early and growth stage, and the fund's answer is instrument choice, revenue-based loans and convertible debt rather than equity, so returns do not depend on an exit event.","A 70:30 senior-to-junior split is unusually thin on catalytic capital for a fund in this space, and it is the number to watch: it implies the proponents believe a 30 per cent first-loss layer plus regional diversification is enough to make tropical nature lending investment grade to a senior tranche.","Regional compartments are a distribution device as much as a risk one. An investor with an Amazon mandate and an investor with a Southeast Asia mandate can both allocate without either accepting the other's geography, which is how a single vehicle serves mandates that would otherwise each need their own fund.","The 10 per cent net IRR target is the number the structure has to deliver: above a concessional nature vehicle and below commercial private credit, which is the band a blended structure is built to manufacture."],"keywords":["mezzanine debt","first-loss tranche","revenue-based loan","convertible debt","guarantee facility","net IRR","nature-based solutions","blended finance","regional compartment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":559,"title":"Mitigating the Impact of Climate Change in Insurance and Other Financial Services","authors":"Dan Mishra","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Europe"],"institutions":["IBM"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ibm.com/think/insights/climate-change-financial-services-strategy","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An IBM Think commentary by Dan Mishra of IBM's ESG and Environmental Intelligence product group on how insurers and other financial services firms can use weather and climate data in risk management. It opens with loss figures: 25 US extreme weather events in 2023 each causing over USD 1 billion of loss, USD 73.8 billion in total, and the 2021 floods across Austria, Germany, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg causing USD 43 billion of damage, with Berenberg estimating individual insurer claims up to about USD 300 million and reinsurance losses between USD 2 billion and USD 3 billion. It then sets out a three-step process, acquiring tools to assess climate impacts, building an operational strategy around weather data, and engaging customers with new offers, and notes that parametric insurance pays an agreed fixed amount on a triggering event so customers are paid faster because no damage assessment is needed. The piece is written around IBM Environmental Intelligence and carries the promotional apparatus of a vendor page.","insights":["The mechanism worth keeping is the customer-engagement one: an insurer that alerts a policyholder to a threat and tells them how to counter it reduces its own loss and raises retention at the same time, which makes risk information a product rather than an underwriting input.","Parametric insurance is presented for its speed rather than its basis risk, and speed is the seller's argument, so the document states the benefit side of the trade-off without the cost side.","It names a second-order channel: property accepted as loan security may be devalued by longer-term local climate risk even where it has suffered no damage, so the exposure reaches lenders through collateral rather than through claims.","This is vendor content. The figures are cited and checkable, but the structure is a product pitch and the record should be read as such."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","climate data","physical risk","collateral devaluation","transition risk","underwriting","extreme weather losses"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":543,"title":"State of Disaster Philanthropy 2025","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Center for Disaster Philanthropy","Candid"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://disasterphilanthropy.org/state-of-disaster-philanthropy-2025/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Center for Disaster Philanthropy's annual account of disaster philanthropy giving and practice, combining Candid grants data for 2023 with interviews of philanthropy leaders inside and outside the United States. It reports that disaster giving fell as a share of total philanthropy in 2023 while long-term giving shows slow growth, that wildfires and earthquakes drew a significant share alongside epidemics, and that philanthropy is being asked to fill gaps left by constrained government aid agencies. Its central argument is that philanthropy should act before a disaster as well as after it, and it sets out promising practices: thinking long-term, collaborating, and supporting local organisations.","insights":["The headline proportion is small and is the report's own call to action: of $135.3 billion in philanthropic giving recorded by Candid in 2023, $1.2 billion or 0.9 per cent went to disasters, across 9,828 transactions and 3,277 grantmakers.","Those grantmakers are 3 per cent of all grantmakers in the database but the transactions are only 0.6 per cent of all transactions, so disaster giving is spread across many funders making few grants each rather than concentrated in specialists.","The share of giving going to disasters FELL 0.5 percentage points from 2022 to 2023, against total philanthropic giving that rose 1.9 per cent in current dollars per Giving USA, so the decline is relative as well as proportional.","Excluding epidemics changes the story: on that basis 2023 was the second-highest disaster funding year since tracking began in 2012, which is why the report presents pre-pandemic and post-pandemic trends separately rather than as one series.","The report is explicit about what philanthropy cannot do, that it will not and should not be expected to fill all gaps left by government, which is an unusually direct limit statement for a sector advocacy publication.","The two-year data lag is structural rather than incidental: Candid draws primarily from publicly available sources including IRS forms, so the most recent data is typically two years old and any claim about current giving from this source carries that lag."],"keywords":["disaster philanthropy","Candid data","grantmaking","pre-disaster giving","humanitarian funding"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":522,"title":"The Resilience Effect: 10 Super Levers to Catalyse Finance in Climate-Vulnerable Countries","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["SystemIQ","Climate Vulnerable Forum"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.systemiq.earth/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/The-Resilience-Effect-10-super-levers-to-catalyse-finance-in-climate-vulnerable-countries.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Resilience Effect identifies ten priority actions, called super levers, to unlock climate finance in the 70 countries and 1.7 billion people of the Climate Vulnerable Forum V20 group. The ten are strengthening country platforms, unlocking carbon markets at scale, rechannelling Special Drawing Rights, implementing solidarity levies, repurposing harmful subsidies, reforming capital adequacy rules, setting private capital mobilisation mandates for multilateral development banks, scaling local currency solutions, increasing the affordability of sovereign insurance, and updating macroeconomic frameworks to integrate climate and nature. Each is presented as triggering cascading effects across the financial system rather than acting alone, and the report treats the binding constraint as the structure of the financial system rather than the quantity of capital in it.","insights":["The ten levers together are claimed to unlock an additional $210 billion a year by 2030 in V20 countries while avoiding up to $100 billion in annual economic losses linked to climate and nature degradation, which is the report's central quantified claim.","The framing argument is that there is enough capital in the world to finance climate action and that the obstacle is a financial system that penalises countries for investing in adaptation and resilience, so most of the ten levers are rule changes rather than new funds.","Increasing the affordability of sovereign insurance is one of the ten, which places risk transfer alongside capital adequacy reform and SDR rechannelling as a system-level lever rather than a product question.","The V20 group represents more than a fifth of the global population and contributes less than 4 per cent of current global emissions, which is the equity premise the solidarity levy and SDR levers rest on.","The report argues micro, small and medium enterprises must be part of climate finance solutions because they contribute up to 80 per cent of collective V20 GDP, more than 70 per cent of all businesses and up to 90 per cent of employment, which is a demand-side channel most sovereign-level adaptation finance work omits.","Critical enablers are named separately from the levers themselves: fair governance, effective institutions and supportive domestic economic policy, which is the report's acknowledgement that the levers do not work in the absence of state capacity."],"keywords":["climate-vulnerable countries","Special Drawing Rights","solidarity levies","capital adequacy reform","sovereign insurance","local currency solutions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":518,"title":"WILDFIRE RESILIENCE: An Emerging Opportunity for Philanthropy","authors":"Harvey V. Fineberg","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.moore.org/docs/default-source/initiative/wildfire-resilience-initiative/wildfire-resilience-an-emerging-opportunity-for-philanthropy-(january-2025).pdf?sfvrsn=e639630c_0","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation sets out the case for philanthropic capital in wildfire resilience and commits $110 million over the first six years of a projected twelve-year Wildfire Resilience Initiative, while arguing that at least $500 million from philanthropy over the next decade is needed to change the trajectory, starting in Western North America as a proving ground. Three core pillars structure the work: improved and integrated early fire detection, assessment and response; implemented mitigations to reduce community fire disaster risk; and predictive-ecology informed stewardship to reduce ecosystem vulnerability. The document is explicitly an invitation to other funders through the Wildfire Resilience Funders network rather than a description of completed work.","insights":["Moore commits $110 million over six years against an estimated philanthropic need of at least $500 million over a decade, so the document is a gap statement aimed at co-funders as much as a description of its own programme.","The investment case rests on wildfire being a manageable rather than an inevitable problem: the most extreme wildfires have more than doubled in both frequency and magnitude over two decades, extreme fire is projected to rise 30 per cent by 2050 and 50 per cent by 2100, and the average peak daily growth rate of fast fires in the US West has doubled.","One named investment is a satellite constellation able to detect and track fires while still the size of a classroom, updating globally every 20 minutes or less at roughly 400 times the resolution of current satellites, which is a detection-latency intervention rather than a suppression one.","The strategy explicitly targets insurance: fire modelling and risk mitigation innovations are intended to strengthen community hardening and to reform how the insurance industry assesses and incentivises wildfire resilience, which is the route by which philanthropic capital is meant to move private risk capital.","Wildfire costs run to hundreds of billions of dollars annually, and the document frames philanthropy's role as catalytic over a 10 to 12 year horizon rather than as the funder of the solution."],"keywords":["wildfire resilience","philanthropic capital","community hardening","fire detection satellites","insurance incentives"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":517,"title":"Resourcing Climate and Health Priorities: A Mapping of International Finance Flows from 2018\u20132022","authors":"Fabio Cresto Aleina, Oumar Pathe Bah, Emily Barter, Naomi Beyeler, Ibrahima Dieye, Vincent Ebken, Claudio Forner, Maryam Hassimi, Carmen Jia Wen He, Julia Kirr, Greg Kuzmak, Qi Liu, Awa Mbaye, Yacine Ndiaye, Prashant Poondla, Leon Schlick, Christina Schrade, Dennis Taenzler, Alice Westerman, Holly Wheeler, Estelle Willie, Mathilde Wilkens, Rida Zaheer","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Africa"],"institutions":["Rockefeller Foundation","World Health Organization","Green Climate Fund","Adaptation Fund","World Bank","OECD","Inter-American Development Bank","European Investment Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.rockefellerfoundation.org/reports/resourcing-climate-and-health-priorities-a-mapping-of-international-finance-flows-from-2018-2022/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A mapping of international finance flows to climate and health from 2018 to 2022, produced by SEEK Development with adelphi consult and AfriCatalyst for Foundation S, Reaching the Last Mile and The Rockefeller Foundation, as a contribution to the Alliance for Transformative Action on Climate and Health. It traces commitments from bilateral donors, multilateral development banks and multilateral health funds, and argues that health must be treated as a core part of resilience investment rather than a sector adjacent to it. More than 50 funding organisations, country governments, implementing organisations and finance experts contributed through interviews and consultations.","insights":["Climate and health finance commitments reached US$7.1 billion, a very large rise from less than US$1 billion in 2018, which is the report's headline finding on trajectory rather than on adequacy.","That total splits into US$4.8 billion from bilateral donors, US$0.6 billion from four multilateral development banks and US$1.5 billion from two multilateral health funds, so bilateral aid rather than development banking is carrying this flow.","Health programming still receives a small fraction of the estimated US$2.4 trillion per year of climate finance needed for developing countries, which is the gap the mapping exists to size.","3.3 billion people already live with increased health risks because of climate change, and the report's argument is that climate resilience requires climate-resilient people rather than only climate-resilient structures.","Capital adequacy and balance sheet measures have increased the lending headroom of multilateral development banks by over US$400 billion, which the report holds up as available capacity that has not reached climate and health."],"keywords":["climate and health finance","finance flow mapping","climate-resilient health systems","bilateral donors","multilateral development banks"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":476,"title":"Invest in Water and Climate Resilience","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National League of Cities (NLC)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nlc.org/resource/invest-in-water-and-climate-resilience/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The National League of Cities sets out its federal advocacy position on water infrastructure, arguing that local governments provide over 95 per cent of total funding for US water infrastructure and that the federal government should recommit as a significant partner. It frames climate change as both worsening existing water infrastructure problems and creating new ones around quality and availability, and names green and natural infrastructure as a solution delivering stormwater management, longer asset life, cost savings, community resilience, recreation and public health co-benefits. It records that COVID-era revenue losses for water and wastewater utilities, which are overwhelmingly ratepayer funded, will exceed $30 billion combined. The ask has three parts: financing, meaning continuation of the Clean Water and Drinking Water State Revolving Funds and WIFIA plus adding water and sewerage facilities to the private activity bonds exempt from the volume cap; funding, meaning grants for drinking water, wastewater, stormwater, resilience, innovative technologies, workforce and source water protection; and flexibility, meaning the ability to choose green over gray infrastructure, the Integrated Planning Framework, a revised Financial Capability Assessment Framework and longer permit terms. Four supported bills are named, and three mayors describe local projects and pandemic-era service continuity.","insights":["The 95 per cent local funding share is the fact the whole ask rests on: it makes federal water money marginal in volume and decisive at the margin, which is why the request is for financing terms and flexibility rather than for a larger federal share.","The private activity bond volume cap exemption is the most specific instrument-level ask, because lifting it would let water and sewerage borrow tax-exempt without competing for a state's capped allocation.","Flexibility is framed as an affordability instrument rather than a convenience: choosing green over gray, longer permit terms and a revised Financial Capability Assessment all change what a utility must charge ratepayers.","The $30 billion pandemic revenue loss establishes that the ratepayer base is itself a climate-exposed revenue stream, which is the same fragility a resilience capital plan would have to finance against."],"keywords":["State Revolving Fund","WIFIA","private activity bonds","volume cap","green infrastructure","ratepayer affordability","Financial Capability Assessment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":474,"title":"The Insurability Imperative: Using Insurance to Navigate the Climate Transition","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Howden","Allianz","AXA","Munich RE"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.howdengroup.com/uk-en/insurability-climate-report-2025","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Howden Climate Risk and Resilience argues that insurability, whether a risk can be insured at an affordable cost, is now the lens through which future risk, liquidity, valuation, investor appetite and financial stability should be viewed, and opens with Mia Mottley's formulation that what is not insurable is not investable. Six key messages: insurability is a strategic indicator of financial health, because insurers are gatekeepers of capital allocation and a project that cannot secure cover likely cannot secure funding; insurers and brokers are innovation partners who should be invited into early-stage project planning; resilience delivers a competitive edge because insurers reward risk management leadership rather than merely pricing risk; there is more capital for the prepared, since underwriting capacity flows to those who understand insurability rules; organisations must think in models or be modelled out, understanding how Annual Average Loss and Probable Maximum Loss drive premiums; and regulation can kill or catalyse insurability, since mispriced subsidies and outdated zoning make risks uninsurable. Four factors enable insurability: risk modelling, which defines solvency and directs capital and so effectively makes markets; risk management, the variable most within an asset owner's control and the key determinant of long-term insurability; risk sharing, where growing cross-border demand meets finite capital and expertise; and public policy and financial regulation, a force multiplier in either direction depending on alignment with climate science. Four strategies follow: draw lessons from past insurability crises, which exposed flaws in system design and governance rather than technical failures; think like an insurer by treating insurability as a live diagnostic and applying portfolio-level risk modelling.","insights":["The report's central claim inverts the usual direction of the resilience finance argument: rather than asking how to finance resilience, it argues that insurability is the gate on all other finance, so losing cover forecloses investment before any adaptation decision is made.","The fifth message, think in models or be modelled out, is the most operational: an asset owner who does not understand how Annual Average Loss and Probable Maximum Loss enter a premium cannot demonstrate that a resilience upgrade should change either, so the investment earns no pricing response.","Risk management is named as the single factor most within the asset owner's control, and the other three factors in the framework are what make acting on it hard, so the document locates the one lever an owner actually holds.","The regulation message is a warning that mispriced subsidies and outdated zoning are themselves causes of uninsurability, so a public affordability measure can destroy the private market it was meant to protect.","The historical parallels are an argument about sequence: steam boiler insurance and fire departments made new technologies financeable through collaboration between insurers, owners and government, which is the model the report proposes for adaptation.","The emphasis on multi-year relationships rather than transactions comes from how reinsurance treaties work, and implies that a company shopping its property programme annually cannot build the insurability position the report recommends."],"keywords":["insurability","Annual Average Loss","Probable Maximum Loss","catastrophe modelling","protection gap","underwriting capacity","risk partner"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":471,"title":"Atlas of Disaster: Vermont","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US","Vermont"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design","Federal Emergency Management Agency","U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/reports/atlas-of-disaster-vermont/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Rebuild by Design's Vermont Atlas of Disaster compiles county-level data for 2011 to 2024 into eight maps showing where major disasters occurred, where post-disaster support went, where the most socially vulnerable populations live and the energy reliability by utility area, presented within General Assembly and Senate district boundaries so legislators and constituents can use it. Vermont had 25 climate disaster declarations in that period, the seventh highest count in the country; every one of its 14 counties had five or more and nine had ten or more, with Washington County the highest. Per capita spending on climate disasters was $902, the fourth highest in the country, and FEMA plus HUD post-disaster funding totalled $580 million against a population of 643,000. The state holds an ASCE infrastructure report card grade of C. The July 2023 flood dropped 6.23 to 9.05 inches of rain in 38 hours, pushed the Winooski and Lamoille rivers past records set during Hurricane Irene in 2011, damaged over 4,000 homes and 800 businesses and caused $558 million in losses; July 2024 flooding destroyed 68 homes, severely damaged 121, affected 294 businesses with average losses around $180,000 each and destroyed 16 bridges. On financing, the report estimates that $600 million of climate infrastructure could be supported through a small surcharge on certain lines of property-casualty insurance, bonded over ten years, arguing the surcharge is progressive because wealthier residents insure more numerous and more expensive items while poorer residents insure less or not at all, and that participation in FEMA's Community Rating System would lower flood insurance premiums by five to 45 per cent in FEMA flood zones. It estimates $2.5 billion could be raised through a Climate Superfund in Vermont, and records that the Climate Superfund Act passed 106 of 150 in the Vermont House and 26 to 3 in the Senate.","insights":["The two revenue proposals are sized against each other and differ by a factor of four: $600 million from a property-casualty insurance surcharge bonded over ten years against $2.5 billion from a Climate Superfund, so the polluter-pays route is the only one at the scale of the damage.","The equity argument for an insurance surcharge is unusual and worth testing: it is progressive because insurance ownership rises with wealth, which makes a premium levy a proxy wealth tax, but by the same logic it raises nothing from the uninsured households that recover worst.","Vermont's Climate Superfund Act passed 106 to 44 in the House and 26 to 3 in the Senate, margins wide enough to make the polluter-pays levy a cross-party measure rather than a party-line one.","The report's own instrument comparison makes the sharpest distinction in the set: catastrophe bonds pay out after a disaster and raise nothing for resilience planning, while resilience bonds recycle a premium reduction into the project that earned it, which is the difference between risk transfer and risk reduction.","The Community Rating System is the one live mechanism named that already converts local mitigation into a household premium reduction of five to 45 per cent, and it is the existing template for the price signal the rest of the field is trying to build.","$902 per capita in disaster spending against $580 million of FEMA and HUD post-disaster funding for a population of 643,000 is the arithmetic of a small state living on recovery money, which is the case the report makes for a dedicated adaptation revenue stream."],"keywords":["Climate Superfund","property-casualty surcharge","Community Rating System","resilience bonds","environmental impact bond","resilience districts","land value capture","Vermont"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":391,"title":"FEMA Is Eliminating Hazard Mitigation Programs, Leaving Americans Nationwide at Risk as Disasters Worsen","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","Urban Institute","Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety (IBHS)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.urban.org/urban-wire/fema-eliminating-hazard-mitigation-programs-leaving-americans-nationwide-risk-disasters","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Urban Institute analysis examines FEMA's cancellation of the Building Resilient Infrastructure and Communities (BRIC) program and withdrawal of Flood Mitigation Assistance (FMA) funding for 2025. The piece documents the financial stakes: $1 billion in obligated BRIC grants, $2.3 billion in selected-but-unsigned awards, and $600 million in foregone FMA funds, totaling roughly $3.3 billion in jeopardized hazard mitigation investment. Drawing on 2020\u20132024 FMA spending data across 36 states, it argues that eliminating pre-disaster mitigation programs shifts federal spending toward costlier post-disaster response as floods, wildfires, and hurricanes intensify.","insights":["FEMA's cancellation of BRIC puts approximately $3.3 billion in hazard mitigation funding at risk: $1 billion already obligated via signed grant agreements and $2.3 billion selected but not yet formally awarded.","FMA's 2025 withdrawal eliminates $600 million in announced funding; from 2020\u20132024, FMA selected $1.5 billion across 626 projects in 36 states and 164 congressional districts.","Studies by the National Institute of Building Sciences and the US Chamber of Commerce find hazard mitigation generates $6\u2013$13 in benefits for every $1 invested, a benefit-cost ratio that makes these programs among the highest-return federal expenditures.","Louisiana and Texas alone received three-fifths of all FMA-selected funding from 2020\u20132024, indicating highly concentrated geographic exposure to the funding loss.","Hurricanes Helene and Milton caused approximately $220 billion in damages in 2024, underscoring the scale of losses that pre-disaster mitigation is designed to reduce.","Insurance companies are raising rates and withdrawing coverage across U.S. markets in response to escalating disaster costs, compounding the fiscal impact on households and communities that lose access to mitigation funds.","Cancellation disrupts projects already underway and may permanently halt planned projects, wasting community investments in design, stakeholder engagement, and application preparation, a sunk-cost loss not captured in headline funding figures.","The federal government insures flood-exposed properties through the NFIP, meaning reduced FMA investment directly increases federal fiscal exposure to repetitive flood losses.","Every U.S. state and five territories would lose hazard mitigation resources under full BRIC cancellation, giving the cuts a nationwide distributional footprint rather than a regionally concentrated one."],"keywords":["pre-disaster mitigation","BRIC program","Flood Mitigation Assistance","federal grant cancellation","benefit-cost ratio","NFIP","hazard mitigation funding"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":387,"title":"Disaster Resilience: FEMA Should Improve Guidance and Assessment of Its Revolving Loan Fund Program","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","Government Finance Officers Association","U.S. Army Corps of Engineers (USACE)","Department of Housing and Urban Development","Department of Treasury","World Bank","U.S. Government Accountability Office","U.S. Government Accountability Office (GAO)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-25-107331","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This GAO report (GAO-25-107331, February 2025) audits FEMA's Safeguarding Tomorrow Revolving Loan Fund (RLF) program, established under the 2021 STORM Act and appropriated $500 million over five years through FY2026. FEMA awarded $50 million to eight FY2023 recipients and $150 million to 12 FY2024 recipients. The report finds that FEMA's guidance was incomplete, unclear, and inconsistent, causing Louisiana to decline its award, and that FEMA lacks a systematic process to assess program effectiveness. GAO issues two recommendations to improve guidance and institute evidence-based performance assessment.","insights":["FEMA's RLF program was appropriated $500 million over five years (FY2022\u2013FY2026) to capitalize state/territorial/tribal revolving loan funds for hazard mitigation projects; as of January 2025, 20 grants have been selected across FY2023 and FY2024 cycles.","FY2023 awards totaled $50 million (7 states + DC); FY2024 awards totaled $150 million (12 recipients). Louisiana declined its FY2023 award citing incomplete guidance, illustrating how administrative failures can forfeit federal capital deployment.","Loans under the program carry a maximum interest rate of 1%, a maximum loan size below $5 million per project, and repayment periods up to 20 years (30 years for low-income geographic areas), creating a concessional instrument specifically designed for underserved communities.","Six of eight FY2023 awardees cited RLF program flexibility, particularly allowing loans to serve as non-federal cost share for FEMA's BRIC and Flood Mitigation Assistance grant programs, as the primary benefit, unlocking grant programs that communities could not otherwise afford (cost-share requirements of 10\u201325%).","The absence of a benefit-cost analysis requirement distinguishes the RLF from other FEMA Hazard Mitigation Assistance programs and reduces application burden, but also means FEMA cannot quantify avoided losses or benefit-cost ratios, limiting evidence for capital allocators and Congress.","FEMA identified insurance premium reductions and avoided losses as candidate effectiveness metrics but has not institutionalized any measurement process; without this, the program cannot demonstrate return on public investment or inform future appropriations of the remaining $500M authorization."],"keywords":["revolving loan fund","hazard mitigation grants","STORM Act","federal disaster mitigation","concessional lending","benefit-cost analysis","underserved communities","program evaluation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":360,"title":"Resilient Florida Annual Plan: Statewide Flooding and Sea Level Rise Resilience Plan","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["Florida","US"],"institutions":["Village of Key Biscayne","Miami-Dade County Office of Resilience","U.S. Army Corps of Engineers (USACE)","Florida Department of Environmental Protection"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://floridadep.gov/sites/default/files/25-26%20Resilient%20Florida%20Annual%20Plan_1.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Florida Department of Environmental Protection's FY 2025-26 Statewide Flooding and Sea Level Rise Resilience Plan is the annual project-ranking report for the Resilient Florida Program, established in 2021. It presents 143 eligible implementation projects totaling $2.38 billion in project costs, requesting $834.5 million in state funds, with a three-year planning horizon allocating $200 million in Year 1, $200 million in Year 2, and $238.7 million in Year 3 (Option A). Projects span coastal and inland Florida communities, covering stormwater, wastewater, water supply, shoreline, and emergency facility hardening against flooding and sea level rise.","insights":["Since 2021, Florida has invested $1.8 billion through the Resilient Florida Program, funding over 320 planning grants and 365 physical improvement projects, putting nearly 95% of Florida's counties and municipalities on track to complete comprehensive vulnerability assessments by 2026.","The FY 2025-26 cycle received 181 total proposals with a combined project cost of $2.75 billion and a state funding request of $1.03 billion; after eligibility review, 143 projects were ranked with total costs of $2.38 billion and a state funding request of $834.5 million.","Water management district projects alone account for $103.2 million, 51.5%, of anticipated Year 1 state funding ($200 million), prompting the Department to offer an alternative plan (Option A1) excluding those projects, which expands Year 1 funded projects from 19 to 34.","The plan uses a tiered scoring rubric (Chapter 62S-8, F.A.C.) prioritizing: flood/sea level rise risk reduction linked to a vulnerability assessment, compound flooding risk, regionally significant assets, critical asset vulnerability percentage, design readiness, cost share security, nature-based solutions, and financial disadvantage of the community.","Financially disadvantaged small communities (municipalities \u226410,000 population or counties \u226450,000 population with below-state-average per capita income, or any entity with per capita income \u226475% of state average) qualify for a cost share waiver, with several Year 1 projects receiving 0% match requirement.","The three-year rolling plan commits $6.26 million and $1.55 million from Year 1 projects into FY 2026-27 and FY 2027-28 budgets respectively for multi-year projects, with years 2 and 3 totaling $200 million and $238.7 million (Option A) in high-ranking unfunded projects."],"keywords":["vulnerability assessment","stormwater infrastructure","wastewater hardening","grant ranking","cost share","nature-based solutions","sea level rise planning","critical asset database"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":330,"title":"Resilient Rhody 2025 Chapter 6: Future Investment Strategy","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank","Climate Resilience Fund","Arup","State of Rhode Island"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://climatechange.ri.gov/media/2606/download?language=en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Chapter 6 of Rhode Island's Resilient Rhody 2025 plan presents a statewide investment strategy covering 79 climate adaptation actions and 10 priority physical infrastructure solutions, with a total programmatic funding need of $121M\u2013$304M. It benchmarks Rhode Island's resilience spending against peer coastal states, details solution-specific capital cost estimates and funding pathways (federal, state, and non-traditional), and quantifies a 1.56x economic output multiplier for every dollar invested in resilience construction in Rhode Island. The chapter also introduces a four-step project prioritization and funding-matching framework for state and local planners.","insights":["Total programmatic funding need for 79 Resilient Rhody 2025 Actions is $121M\u2013$304M, with the upper bound reflecting early-implementation startup costs and the lower bound reflecting sustained operating costs once programs are established.","Every $1M invested in resilience-related construction in Rhode Island generates approximately $1.56M in total economic output, supports 7 jobs, and produces $625,000 in earnings; a $340M investment would generate ~$530M in output, ~2,380 jobs, and ~$212.5M in earnings.","Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank's Resilient Rhody Infrastructure Fund (RRIF) shift from grants to a revolving loan model means every $1 in public resources can now support $3 in project costs, creating a self-renewing capital platform.","Massachusetts allocates ~1% of its state budget (~$547M/year from a $60.9B FY2026 budget) to environmental and climate programs; if Rhode Island adopted a comparable model it could substantially scale resilience investments beyond current levels.","RIDEM is the single agency associated with the most actions (9) and widest funding range ($12M\u2013$81M); joint CRMC-RIDEM leadership covers 5 additional actions ($7M\u2013$14M), underscoring the centrality of coordinated coastal-environmental management.","Forest-related programs represent the largest single asset-type investment category among included actions ($8M\u2013$65M), driven by high-cost mapping and LiDAR data acquisition; financing climate resilience projects is the second largest ($21M\u2013$42M).","Benchmark capital costs for representative resilience projects range from $1.1M (green stormwater retrofit, Pawtucket) to $2.1B (150-mile 500-kV transmission line upgrade, normalized to RI/2026 dollars), illustrating the multi-order-of-magnitude scale variation across solution types."],"keywords":["revolving loan fund","state resilience benchmarking","economic multiplier","statewide vulnerability assessment","coastal infrastructure hardening","green bonds","nature-based solutions","municipal resilience program"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":328,"title":"Resilient Rhody 2025 Chapter 5: Adaptation & Resilience Solutions","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","Rhode Island"],"institutions":["National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration","US Environmental Protection Agency","Marsh","Arup","State of Rhode Island"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://climatechange.ri.gov/media/2601/download?language=en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Resilient Rhody 2025 Chapter 5 is Rhode Island's statewide climate adaptation solutions guide, developed by Arup, covering ten intervention clusters across critical infrastructure, natural systems, and community resilience. It provides Class 5 cost estimates (\u00b1100%/\u221250%, RSMeans/ENR-normalized), funding pathways (federal PROTECT, PIDP, PROP, CWSRF, DWSRF, NOAA CZM, EPA SNEP, and RI state programs), and consequence-of-inaction ratings for roads/bridges, ports, energy transmission, sewer/stormwater, wastewater treatment, drinking water reservoirs, beaches/wetlands, rivers/streams, schools, and hospitals. Representative exposure findings include Rhode Island Hospital facing >30% facility value damage in a current 100-year flood, and the Salt Pond Region facing 8.5 ft of inundation today rising to 12 ft by 2100.","insights":["Route 114 Corridor currently experiences 5 ft of flooding during a 100-year coastal flood event, projected to increase to 7 ft by 2100; I-95 in Cranston faces a nearly 4 ft increase in 500-year coastal flood levels by 2100, quantifying growing exposure of critical evacuation routes.","Port of Providence and Port of Galilee currently experience approximately 4.5 ft of flooding during a 100-year coastal flood event, projected to rise to 7 ft by 2100; Rhode Island ports generate over $200 million and 2,400 jobs, making port hardening a direct economic protection measure.","Washington County transmission lines face high wind risk from Category 2 wind events (100-year return); undergrounding 1 mile of overhead lines is estimated at $55M\u2013$230M but offers reduced storm-related repair costs and less maintenance than overhead alternatives, illustrating a high-cost/high-resilience tradeoff.","Cranston WWTF experienced sanitary sewer overflow events during 18% of extreme flood events over a four-year period; West Warwick Regional WWTF experienced SSOs during 33% of such events, concrete performance data quantifying current infrastructure failure rates under climate stress.","A 100-year coastal flood event currently causes damage equal to 10\u201330% of total asset value at Cranston WWTF, rising to more than 30% by 2100, establishing an asset-level damage ratio that supports investment prioritization and insurance/bond structuring.","Rhode Island Hospital faces potential damage exceeding 30% of total facility value in a current 100-year coastal flood event; South County Hospital faces 10\u201330% damage under a 2050 100-year event, quantified loss thresholds that should inform hospital credit analysis and insurance underwriting."],"keywords":["sanitary sewer overflow","transmission line undergrounding","managed retreat","saltwater intrusion","evacuation route hardening","wastewater treatment resilience","coastal wetland restoration","voluntary buyout"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":327,"title":"Resilient Rhody 2025 Chapter 7: Funding & Financing Mechanisms Inventory","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Rhode Island"],"institutions":["Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank","Climate Resilience Fund","Federal Emergency Management Agency","State of Rhode Island"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://climatechange.ri.gov/media/2611/download?language=en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Chapter 7 of Rhode Island's Resilient Rhody 2025 state climate adaptation plan is a comprehensive inventory of funding and financing mechanisms mapped to the plan's ten resilience solutions. It catalogs over 60 federal, state, and non-traditional programs spanning grants, revolving loan funds, bonds, insurance tools, and land value capture instruments. Key state vehicles include the Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank's suite of programs (CWSRF delivered $75M in 2024; DWSRF issued $73.2M in FY2024 loans) and RIDEM's Climate Resilience Fund ($4.5M in first-round grants). The document is intended as a practitioner navigation tool for Rhode Island municipalities, state agencies, tribes, and nonprofits seeking capital for resilience infrastructure.","insights":["Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank's CWSRF delivered over $75 million in low-cost financing in 2024 alone for wastewater and stormwater infrastructure, demonstrating the scale achievable through a well-capitalized state revolving fund structure.","RIIB's DWSRF issued seven new loans totaling $73.2 million in FY2024, illustrating that drinking water infrastructure financing at state scale can rival many federal competitive grant programs in deployment volume.","RIDEM's Climate Resilience Fund awarded $4.5 million across 20 projects in its first round, establishing a unit economics baseline of roughly $225K per project for flood prevention, nature-based solutions, and climate adaptation in vulnerable communities.","FEMA's FMA program awarded over $800 million nationwide in FY2023, with Rhode Island using funds for floodplain property acquisitions and home elevations, a repeatable federal subsidy channel for managed retreat and structure elevation.","FEMA's EMPG supported over $355 million in emergency preparedness activities nationwide in FY2024, with Rhode Island directing funds toward interoperable communications and community-based preparedness, underscoring the scale of non-infrastructure resilience spending.","The SOAR Fund allocated approximately $2.3 million to nine projects in eight disadvantaged communities in Rhode Island and southeastern Massachusetts, with funding available through 2027, a model for small-grant equity-focused resilience deployment.","Non-traditional instruments profiled include catastrophe/resilience bonds, blue bonds, environmental impact bonds (pay-for-success), parametric insurance, special assessment districts, TIF, developer impact fees, C-PACE, and CBP3s, providing a layered toolkit that spans insurance, debt, and land value capture."],"keywords":["revolving loan fund","state resilience plan","municipal grant programs","nature-based solutions finance","coastal adaptation funding","Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank","pay-for-success bond","land value capture"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":324,"title":"Resilient Rhody 2025 Chapter 9: Resilience Best Practices","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Climate Bonds Initiative","State of Rhode Island"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://climatechange.ri.gov/media/2621/download?language=en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Chapter 9 of Resilient Rhody 2025 is Rhode Island's state climate resilience plan's best-practices compendium, cataloging 12 practice areas with precedents drawn from across the U.S. and internationally. It covers data tools, parametric and NFIP flood insurance, nature-based green infrastructure, coastal zoning ordinances, climate justice grant programs, regional resilience collaboratives, managed retreat buyouts, statewide resilience standards, and wildfire/drought ordinances. Key financial reference points include $10B in California climate bonds, $190M in NJ buyouts, $100M in Massachusetts MVP grants, and a $14M Environmental Impact Bond in Atlanta.","insights":["The Caribbean Catastrophe Risk Insurance Facility (CCRIF) made 78 parametric payouts totaling ~$390M since 2007, demonstrating that regional parametric risk pools can deliver disaster funds within 14 days without reliance on federal aid, a model Rhode Island is urged to replicate at a regional scale.","California's Proposition 4 (2024) authorized $10B in general obligation bonds for resilience infrastructure including voluntary buyouts, wildfire prevention, coastal resilience, and flood protection, demonstrating how states can unlock large-scale capital for adaptation through bond legislation.","Massachusetts's MVP Program awarded over $100M to 341 communities and funded 700+ projects between 2017 and 2022, establishing a replicable state-funded model for building local and regional resilience capacity through grants and technical assistance.","The Atlanta Proctor Creek project, financed by a $14M Environmental Impact Bond, protected 500 homes from flooding and created dozens of sustainable jobs through community co-designed green infrastructure, a documented proof point linking impact finance instruments to measurable resilience outcomes.","New Jersey's Blue Acres managed retreat program has acquired 1,100+ flood-prone properties since 1995, allocating over $190M to planning and buyouts, showing that voluntary state-led acquisition programs can proactively reduce flood risk and permanently preserve open land.","Massachusetts's RMAT Climate Resilience Design Standards Tool has been used to evaluate over $1B in capital project funding and train 7,000+ practitioners, illustrating how mandated digital design-standards tools translate into consistent, climate-informed infrastructure investment decisions."],"keywords":["managed retreat buyouts","parametric flood insurance","municipal vulnerability preparedness","statewide resilience standards","nature-based cooling","green infrastructure corridors","climate justice grants","wildfire ordinances"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":322,"title":"Nature's Resilience Dividend: How Nature Can Strengthen Sydney's Flood and Coastal Hazard Resilience","authors":"Sam Kernaghan, Alice Simpson-Young, Adam Davis","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Oceania"],"institutions":["AECOM","US Department of the Treasury","Committee for Sydney","Sydney Environment Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://sydney.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Committee-for-Sydney-Natures-Resilience-Dividend-September-2025.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Nature's Resilience Dividend, produced by the Committee for Sydney with AECOM and the Sydney Environment Institute, makes the case for nature-based solutions (NBS) as a primary tool for flood, tidal, and coastal inundation risk reduction in Greater Sydney. The report synthesises global evidence and five international/local case studies (including Auckland's $760M NBS program, New York's $1.45B Big U project, and Queensland's $35M Blue Heart wetlands), to identify five structural barriers and seven actionable directions with 25 recommendations. It argues that NBS must be re-cast as financial assets on government balance sheets and funded through green bonds, blue carbon markets, biodiversity credits, and insurer partnerships rather than short-cycle disaster grants.","insights":["A global comparison found 65% of studies showed nature-based solutions more cost-effective than conventional engineering for hazard mitigation, and 26% found them partially more effective, yet NSW's Flood Risk Management Guideline still rates NBS as 'low value' for anything beyond small, frequent, local flooding.","Auckland's Te Ara Awataha stream daylighting project held 12 million litres of water during a greater-than-1-in-100-year flood event in January 2023, preventing inundation of private properties; its success triggered Auckland Council to commit NZD $760 million to a 10-year NBS flood risk reduction program.","The Big U coastal resilience project in Lower Manhattan, a $1.45 billion hybrid green-grey initiative, protects over 110,000 residents (including 28,000 in public housing), and safeguards billions in infrastructure and property from storm surge following Hurricane Sandy's $19 billion in damages.","The $35 million Blue Heart Sunshine Coast project, Australia's first blue carbon project under the Australian Carbon Credit Units Scheme, aims to restore 5,000 hectares of estuarine wetland over 80 years to increase flood storage capacity along the Maroochy River floodplain.","Commonwealth Disaster Ready Funding allocated only $0.09 billion (1%) to the 'natural' domain between 2018\u201319 and 2022\u201323, versus $6.40 billion (70%) to the built environment, highlighting a structural financing gap that the report's recommendations seek to close through dedicated multi-year NBS funding and novel instruments."],"keywords":["nature-based solutions","blue carbon","floodplain management","green infrastructure valuation","stormwater detention","disaster ready fund","living shoreline","hybrid green-grey infrastructure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":315,"title":"Resilient Rhody 2025 Plan Summary","authors":"Kimberly Korioth","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank","Federal Emergency Management Agency","State of Rhode Island","Rhode Island Department of Environmental Management","Coastal Resource Management Council","Arup","Fuss & O'Neill","Camoin","CIVIC"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://climatechange.ri.gov/media/2651/download?language=en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Resilient Rhody 2025 is Rhode Island's first statutory Statewide Coastal Resilience Plan, mandated by the 2024 \"Act on Coasts.\" It presents 79 prioritized actions, a statewide climate vulnerability assessment of 130,000+ assets across 21 types and five hazards, 10 costed climate adaptation and resilience solutions, and a Future Investment Strategy. Key findings include that 13\u201318% of assessed assets face high or very high coastal flood risk by 2100, and the estimated non-construction funding needed to advance the 79 actions ranges from $119M to $298M. The Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank's Resilient Rhody Infrastructure Fund (RRIF) provides a $1-to-$3 public leverage ratio through a revolving loan model.","insights":["13% of all assessed assets are currently at high or very high coastal flood risk; this rises to 18% by 2100 under the SSP5-8.5 very-high emissions scenario, nearly one in five assets statewide.","The estimated non-construction funding required to implement all 79 Resilient Rhody 2025 Actions ranges from $119M to $298M, with RIDEM holding the largest share of associated actions.","The Rhode Island Infrastructure Bank's Resilient Rhody Infrastructure Fund (RRIF) achieves a 3:1 leverage ratio: every $1 in public resources supports $3 in project costs through a revolving loan structure layered with state bond proceeds, green bonds, and federal infrastructure funding.","Between September 2023 and January 2024 alone, flood disasters triggered more than $22M in FEMA Individual and Household Assistance for Rhode Island residents, illustrating the fiscal cost of inaction.","Stormwater flooding is projected to threaten 100% of assessed critical infrastructure, natural system, and community resilience assets under the most extreme scenario by 2100, making it the single most pervasive hazard in the assessment.","13 of 19 wastewater treatment facilities are at high or very high coastal flood risk by 2100, representing a critical and concentrated infrastructure vulnerability with direct public-health and environmental consequences.","By 2100 under SSP5-8.5, all Rhode Island counties are projected to exceed 30 annual days above 90\u00b0F, with all assessed extreme-heat assets rated high or very high risk, directly threatening grid stability and public health simultaneously.","The plan benchmarks Massachusetts's statutory goal of allocating 1% of state budget to environmental and climate initiatives as a model Rhode Island could adopt to create a durable resilience funding baseline."],"keywords":["statewide vulnerability assessment","revolving loan fund","nature-based solutions","environmental justice","stormwater flooding","coastal flood risk","asset prioritization","resilience hubs"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":88,"title":"Affordable Strategies to Accelerate Lead Service Line Replacement","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Michigan"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures","Michigan Municipal League Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/michigan-lead-service-line-replacement-case-study","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The document discusses the public health issue of lead exposure from lead service lines (LSLs) and the collaboration between QV and the MML Foundation to develop affordable strategies for accelerating lead service line replacements in Dearborn and Bay City, Michigan. The initiative outlines key challenges, identifies priority actions for effective implementation, and explores solutions aimed at gaining the necessary funding, enhancing community engagement, and streamlining processes to support LSL replacements in low-income communities.","insights":["Lead exposure is a significant public health concern, especially for children under six.","Over 9 million households in the U.S. are at risk of lead poisoning due to lead service lines.","The cost of replacing lead service lines is estimated between $28 billion to $47 billion.","Funding from the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law falls short of the total funding required for lead service line replacements.","Bay City managed to reduce costs through in-house staff, while Dearborn faced higher expenses due to contractor reliance.","Capacity to manage LSLR programs is insufficient in both Dearborn and Bay City.","Community engagement and communication are critical, especially in diverse populations.","The partnership aims to establish a state-wide charitable revolving loan fund to support lead service line replacements.","Lessons learned from this initiative can inform national LSLR efforts beyond Michigan."],"keywords":["public health","lead service lines","community engagement","funding strategies","water quality"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":7,"title":"Adaptation Finance and Investment","authors":"","date":"2025-01-01","year":2025,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Africa","Asia"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wri.org/initiatives/adaptation-finance","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The World Resources Institute's Adaptation Finance and Investment initiative focuses on integrating climate risks into financial and decision-making frameworks. Despite the economic benefits of adaptation, substantial financing is lacking, particularly in vulnerable countries. The initiative aims to enhance adaptation investments by collaborating with governments, private sectors, and financial institutions, supporting capacity-building, innovating financial instruments, and conducting essential research.","insights":["Every dollar invested in climate adaptation can yield returns of $2 to $10 across various sectors.","Many entities are not currently incorporating climate risks into their financial decisions.","Vulnerable countries often lack the capacity to make necessary adaptation investments.","WRI is developing programs to build long-term capacity within ministries in vulnerable countries.","A university curriculum initiative is in place to promote climate risk management education.","The Coalition for Climate Resilient Investment (CCRI) aims to mobilize private sector adaptation finance.","Innovative financial instruments are being developed to enhance private sector engagement in climate adaptation.","Research is ongoing to analyze the economics of adaptation investments and their benefits.","WRI collaborates with the IMF and World Bank to assist countries in managing climate risks.","Efforts are underway to align climate investments with international climate agreements."],"keywords":["financial investment","capacity building","risk management","economic analysis","sustainable development"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":287,"title":"Measuring Climate-Related and Environmental Risks for Equities","authors":"Emese Lazar, Jingqi Pan, Shixuan Wang","date":"2024-12-18","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["University of Reading"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2024.123393","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed study (Journal of Environmental Management, 2025) introduces novel climate Value-at-Risk (climate VaR) and climate Expected Shortfall (climate ES) measures that quantify the share of equity downside risk attributable to ESG environmental scores, specifically Emission, Innovation, and Resource Use sub-scores from Refinitiv. Analyzing 802 U.S. firms across 11 sectors from January 2003 to December 2019, the authors find strong sectoral heterogeneity: Energy sector firms can reduce VaR/ES by ~27% on average by improving environmental scores, while Health Care firms see risk increase by ~27%, making environmental investment least cost-effective there. The measures offer regulators, investors, and ratings agencies a transparent, sector-resolved method to incorporate transition risk into capital requirements and portfolio risk management.","insights":["Energy sector firms reduce total downside risk (VaR/ES) by approximately 27% on average when environmental scores improve; the 5th-percentile climate risk ratio for VaR reaches \u221265.3%, indicating that environmental score improvement is highly effective at the extreme tail for this sector.","Health Care is the only sector where improving environmental scores consistently increases firm downside risk, the average climate risk ratio for VaR is +26.5% and for ES is +27.7%, because low-carbon strategies in complex medical supply chains are costly and difficult to implement without offsetting operational benefits.","A statistically significant negative relationship exists between environmental scores and stock returns at low return quantiles (1%\u201330%), implying that firms under financial stress bear additional burden from environmental improvement costs; the relationship turns positive at higher quantiles (above 50%), suggesting environmental investment pays off for well-performing firms.","Climate risk ratios are robust across four model specifications (GARCH-SKT, GJR-GARCH-SKT, GARCH-FZ, CARE) and across risk levels (1%, 2.5%, 5%), with sector rankings nearly unchanged, providing methodological confidence for regulatory capital and stress-testing applications.","Six of eleven sectors (Basic Materials, Consumer Staples, Energy, Financials, Technology, Utilities) show negative mean climate risk ratios, meaning environmental score improvement reduces total downside risk; these sectors are candidates for preferential capital treatment or green loan pricing tied to ESG improvement.","The study covers 802 U.S. firms and 58,290 firm-month observations from January 2003\u2013December 2019 (pre-COVID), enabling stable parameter estimation without pandemic-era structural breaks, a design choice that strengthens applicability to long-horizon regulatory stress tests."],"keywords":["climate Value-at-Risk","climate Expected Shortfall","ESG environmental scores","transition risk","equity downside risk","sectoral heterogeneity","quantile regression","GARCH risk models"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":482,"title":"Mind the Gaps: Plugging the Holes in Financing Climate Adaptation","authors":"Louie Woodall","date":"2024-12-17","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Tailwind Climate"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://impactalpha.com/https-impactalpha-com-mind-the-gaps-plugging-the-holes-in-financing-climate-adaptation/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Tailwind Climate, a Berkeley advisory and investment firm co-founded by Emilie Mazzacurati and Katie MacDonald, released a 112-page Adaptation and Resilience Innovation Playbook identifying three gaps holding back adaptation: a capital gap, a demand gap and an ecosystem gap. Annual global spending on adaptation and resilience solutions is put at $1.4 trillion, with governments spending $737 billion a year, consumers $647 billion and a subset of 80 large corporations $58 billion, figures that include rich-world spending rather than only developing country needs. Against that, only 3 per cent of climate tech startup funding went to pure-play adaptation companies between 2019 and 2023, a total of $4.5 billion, even though such companies make up 12 per cent of climate startups; dual-benefit startups attracted around $27 billion. Mazzacurati attributes the shortfall to uncertainty over what is in scope, a belief that adaptation is a business-to-government market, and the absence of government research and development funding of the kind mitigation received. Capital is also misallocated within adaptation: around 44 per cent flows to climate risk analytics and software while 16 per cent goes to physical risk reduction products and services, which she attributes partly to what corporations are buying and partly to the venture model suiting software returns. The playbook proposes concessional capital at pre-seed and seed stage to carry hardtech to commercialization, notes a dearth of adaptation-focused accelerators, and sorts opportunities into Bright Spots with proven demand and high investment, Hidden Gems with strong demand but limited capital, and Blind Spots where impacts are expected but demand has not yet materialised. Mazzacurati places flood hardtech in the Hidden Gems category.","insights":["The 44 versus 16 per cent split inside adaptation venture funding is the sharpest finding: most adaptation capital buys measurement of risk rather than reduction of it, which means the field is funding the diagnosis and not the treatment.","The demand figures dwarf the supply figures by three orders of magnitude, $1.4 trillion of annual spending against $4.5 billion of cumulative startup funding over five years, so the market failure is in the supply of solutions rather than in willingness to pay.","Mazzacurati's stated reason that VC underfunds adaptation hardtech, fund duration and return expectations suiting software, is a structural feature of the capital rather than a view about the sector, which is why the proposed fix is concessional capital at the seed stage.","The Blind Spots category is a deliberate name for the part of the market that cannot be invested in yet because the hazard has not arrived, which is the adaptation version of the timing problem that makes pre-disaster mitigation hard to finance.","Buyer uncertainty is named as a supply-side distortion: corporations buy climate risk analytics because that is what they know to ask for, which pulls startups toward software and away from physical risk reduction."],"keywords":["adaptation and resilience","capital gap","pure-play","climate risk analytics","concessional capital","hardtech","Tailwind Taxonomy"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":186,"title":"Climate Risks Present a Significant Threat to the U.S. Insurance and Housing Markets","authors":"","date":"2024-12-16","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["US Congress Joint Economic Committee"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.jec.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/democrats/2024/12/climate-risks-present-a-significant-threat-to-the-u-s-insurance-and-housing-markets","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The increasing frequency of climate-exacerbated disasters is significantly threatening the U.S. insurance and housing markets, leading to rising home insurance costs, underinsurance, and in some areas, a lack of available insurance altogether. Proposals for insurance innovations and government interventions to address these challenges are essential for protecting American homeowners and stabilizing the housing market amidst these risks.","insights":["Climate change is increasing the frequency and severity of natural disasters, leading to higher home insurance costs.","Rising uninsurability and higher premiums are disrupting housing markets, particularly in high-risk states like California and Florida.","Many Americans remain underinsured due to inadequate coverage that does not reflect actual climate risks.","Affordable housing construction is being negatively impacted as rising insurance costs make financing more difficult.","State-run insurance plans are becoming the primary option for homeowners in high-risk areas, but often provide limited coverage.","The financial strain from rising premiums and uninsurability could lead to budget shortfalls for local governments and reduced funding for services.","Innovations in the insurance industry, such as new coverage options and better climate risk data, are essential for adapting to climate change impacts.","Federal disaster relief funding is often inadequate and cannot keep pace with the rising demand resulting from inadequate insurance coverage.","Improved building codes and resilience measures can significantly reduce the costs associated with climate disasters.","Legislation and policy reforms are necessary to ensure that the insurance market properly reflects climate-related risks."],"keywords":["natural disasters","insurance market","homeownership","risk assessment","disaster relief"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":115,"title":"The Preparedness Payoff: Sacramento","authors":"U.S. Chamber of Commerce, Allstate","date":"2024-12-03","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["U.S. Chamber of Commerce","Allstate","U.S. Chamber of Commerce Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.uschamber.com/environment/the-preparedness-payoff-sacramento","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report 'The Preparedness Payoff: Sacramento' emphasizes the significant economic benefits of investing in resilience and disaster preparedness in Sacramento, revealing that each dollar spent on these initiatives can save communities up to $10.80 in economic costs related to disasters such as droughts, storms, and wildfires. The study highlights that the unique geography and increasing frequency of climate-related disasters in Sacramento further underline the urgency of these investments.","insights":["Every $1 invested in resilience and preparedness can save Sacramento $10.80 in economic costs.","Investing in resilience mitigates the costs of disasters, with various scenarios showing different ratios of return on investment.","Sacramento is particularly vulnerable to disasters due to its geography, facing severe droughts, wildfires, and storm-related mudslides.","The region has experienced significant economic losses from recent disasters, including billions due to wildfires and drought.","Agriculture, which employs over 20% of Sacramento's workforce, suffers greatly from ongoing drought conditions.","Resilience investments can help preserve local jobs and income, with thousands of jobs saved in various disaster scenarios.","Research indicates that higher severity and frequency of climate events correlate with increased economic risks.","Community engagement and awareness are crucial for supporting and implementing resilience investments.","Federal and local collaboration is vital, as illustrated by the improvements being made with the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.","Sustainable urban planning and water management are essential ongoing efforts to combat climate-related challenges."],"keywords":["economic impact","disaster preparedness","community resilience","flood risk management","urban planning"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":150,"title":"Climate Adaptation Finance: The Challenge for Institutional Investors and Commercial Banks","authors":"Matthew Cox, Luka Lightfoot","date":"2024-11-22","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/stories/2024/11/climate-adaptation-finance-the-challenge-for-institutional-investors-and-commercial-banks/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A November 2024 World Economic Forum article by Baker McKenzie partners examines why institutional investors and commercial banks have contributed less than 3% of climate adaptation finance, while developing countries face an annual gap of $187\u2013$359 billion this decade and need a fourfold increase in adaptation flows. The piece identifies five structural barriers (definitional gaps, data scarcity, disclosure constraints, long timelines, and risk-pricing difficulty), and spotlights two instruments as scalable solutions: debt-for-impact swaps (illustrated by Ecuador's 2023 $656M marine conservation-linked bond) and blended finance deployed through public-private partnerships.","insights":["Private sector actors, institutional investors and commercial banks, contribute less than 3% of total climate adaptation finance, despite adaptation representing a critical component of Paris Agreement goals.","The UN Environment Programme estimates a financing gap for developing countries of $187\u2013$359 billion per year this decade, and the Global Center on Adaptation and Climate Policy Initiative state that adaptation finance flows must increase fourfold.","Although adaptation finance has grown in real terms, it has fallen as a share of overall climate finance, signaling that mitigation continues to crowd out adaptation in capital allocation.","Ecuador's 2023 debt-for-nature swap, exchanging $1.6B face-value sovereign bonds for a $656M marine conservation-linked loan, is the largest such transaction to date and illustrates how political risk insurance and development bank guarantees can enable sovereign debt restructuring to redirect savings to conservation and adaptation.","Ecuador directed more than $300M of debt-restructuring savings to marine conservation around the Gal\u00e1pagos Islands, demonstrating the potential scale of fiscal space created by debt-for-impact mechanisms.","Five structural barriers constrain private adaptation finance: (1) absence of standardized definitions/classification equivalent to green loan principles; (2) insufficient project-level climate risk data; (3) disclosure and data-sharing constraints; (4) return timeline mismatches; and (5) difficulty pricing risk when value is primarily in avoided damages rather than monetary revenue.","The EU Taxonomy Regulation provides a classification pathway that can qualify adaptation activities as 'green,' enabling use of EU Green Bonds, a key enabler for European capital markets to label and fund adaptation projects."],"keywords":["adaptation finance gap","debt-for-nature swap","blended finance","private sector mobilization","EU taxonomy","bankable investments","developing countries"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":444,"title":"States Are Exploring Paths to Finance Climate Resilient Infrastructure","authors":"Fatima Yousofi; Eli Gullett","date":"2024-11-21","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["The Pew Charitable Trusts"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.pew.org/en/research-and-analysis/articles/2024/11/21/states-are-exploring-paths-to-finance-climate-resilient-infrastructure","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Pew reviewed legislation and policy announcements across all 50 states for 2023 and 2024 to map how states are funding and financing climate-resilient infrastructure. The framing figure is a state backlog of nearly $1 trillion for deferred maintenance and needed upgrades to public infrastructure, against which aging and undermaintained systems are increasingly exposed to more frequent disasters, extreme heat, heavy rainfall and drought, all of which raise maintenance costs. The article catalogues the strategies states are using, distinguishing those spending the historic Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act allocations from those experimenting with their own funding and financing mechanisms, and presents them as a snapshot of approaches with named adopters rather than as a ranking. The emphasis throughout is that resilience spending competes with other essential priorities such as health care and education, so the question states are answering is how to manage rising costs while meeting those obligations too.","insights":["The scale is set by deferred maintenance rather than by new adaptation: a backlog of nearly $1 trillion across states means most resilience spending is catching up on existing assets, not building new protection.","A 50-state scan of 2023 and 2024 legislation is the method, which produces a menu of mechanisms with named adopters and makes the article useful as a source of state precedents rather than as an argument.","IIJA implementation and home-grown state mechanisms are treated as two different things, so a state's approach depends on whether it is deploying federal money or raising its own.","The competition with health care and education is stated explicitly, which is the political constraint any state-level resilience instrument has to survive."],"keywords":["state infrastructure finance","deferred maintenance","IIJA","resilience funding","state legislation","public infrastructure","Pew"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":466,"title":"Is Physical Climate Risk Priced? Evidence from Regional Variation in Exposure to Heat Stress","authors":"Viral V. Acharya; Timothy Johnson; Suresh Sundaresan; Tuomas Tomunen","date":"2024-11-09","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Bureau of Economic Research","New York University","Columbia University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w30445/w30445.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Acharya, Johnson, Sundaresan and Tomunen test whether physical climate risk is priced by exploiting regional variation in exposure to heat stress, and they test it across three asset classes at once: municipal bonds, corporate bonds and equities. They find consistent evidence across all three that local exposure to heat stress is associated with higher yield spreads for bonds and higher conditional expected returns for stocks. Using heat stress rather than a coastal hazard is the design choice that makes the result general: heat exposure varies regionally across the whole country rather than concentrating on a coastline, so the test is not confined to the flood-exposed jurisdictions where most climate pricing work is done. The paper was awarded the Q-Group's 2022 Jack Treynor Prize and was revised in November 2024 from a September 2022 original.","insights":["Finding a consistent premium across municipal bonds, corporate bonds and equities is the strength of the paper: a result in one asset class can be a local artefact, and the same sign in three is much harder to explain away.","Heat stress is the methodological choice that matters, because it varies across the entire country rather than along a coast, so the finding is not limited to the flood-exposed jurisdictions that dominate this literature.","A higher yield spread on municipal bonds in heat-exposed regions means the cost of public capital already reflects a hazard most municipal adaptation planning treats as secondary to flooding.","Higher conditional expected returns on equities in exposed regions is the equity-market counterpart: investors require compensation, which is the same thing as a higher cost of capital for firms in those places."],"keywords":["physical climate risk","heat stress","municipal bond spreads","corporate bonds","expected returns","asset pricing","regional exposure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":571,"title":"WMO: Climate Services Support Climate Action","authors":"World Meteorological Organization","date":"2024-11-07","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global","Africa","Asia"],"institutions":["World Meteorological Organization"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://wmo.int/news/media-centre/wmo-climate-services-support-climate-action","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A World Meteorological Organization press release of 7 November 2024 announcing its multi-agency State of Climate Services report. The finance figure is the reason to hold it: of the US$63 billion being spent on climate adaptation, nearly a third goes toward climate-informed investments, and an estimated US$4 to 5 billion of that explicitly supports climate services and early warning activities. In 2024 one third of National Meteorological and Hydrological Services provide climate services at an essential level and nearly one third at an advanced or full level, with Asia and Africa making the clearest gains on the back of targeted adaptation funding, while Latin America and the Caribbean face growing need for early warning against forest fires and drought. On losses, over 2020 to mid-2024 floods were the most frequently reported disaster, heat-related hazards became the leading cause of death at 57 per cent of reported weather, water and climate deaths globally, and storms produced 59 per cent of economic losses, per EM-DAT data cited in the report. The Secretary-General's stated position is that the cost of no action is several times higher than the cost of action.","insights":["The $4 to 5 billion for climate services inside $63 billion of adaptation spending is roughly 7 per cent, and climate services are the input every other adaptation investment depends on for its own targeting. A thin allocation to the information layer is a structural finding rather than a budget detail.","Deaths and losses decouple across hazards: heat kills most, at 57 per cent of reported deaths, while storms cost most, at 59 per cent of economic losses. A financing instrument tuned to economic loss will systematically underweight the hazard that kills, which is why heat is hard to insure.","The release notes that continued investment is not translating into capacity at National Meteorological and Hydrological Services, which is an intermediation finding of the same shape as the Ford Foundation record: money increases while the institutions it is meant to build do not.","Reported deaths are falling while economic losses rise, which the release attributes to better early warning and disaster risk management. That is an avoided-loss benefit statement with an attribution attached."],"keywords":["climate services","early warning systems","State of Climate Services","EM-DAT","adaptation spending","heat mortality","National Meteorological and Hydrological Services"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":100,"title":"The Economic Cost of Extreme Weather Events: Could Cost Economy $2 Trillion over the Last Decade","authors":"Oxera Consulting LLP","date":"2024-11-07","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Oxera","International Chamber of Commerce (ICC)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://iccwbo.org/news-publications/policies-reports/new-report-extreme-weather-events-cost-economy-2-trillion-over-the-last-decade/#top","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Oxera report, commissioned by the International Chamber of Commerce, reveals that extreme weather events have caused an estimated $2 trillion in economic losses globally over the past decade, impacting 1.6 billion people, particularly in vulnerable developing economies that face escalating risks and disproportionate economic impacts. The findings underline the urgent need for enhanced climate action and financial investment to mitigate future disruptions and address the inequalities exacerbated by climate change.","insights":["Extreme weather events have resulted in $2 trillion in economic losses globally over the last decade.","Approximately 4,000 extreme weather events affected 1.6 billion people between 2014 and 2023.","Economic damages from extreme weather have accelerated, with $451 billion in losses reported in the last two years alone.","Developing economies are disproportionately impacted, with some single events costing more than the entire annual GDP of certain countries.","Climate change is an immediate problem, with ongoing adverse economic impacts felt in real-time.","Extreme weather events exacerbate existing social and economic inequalities.","Investment decisions are increasingly influenced by risks associated with extreme weather, potentially limiting growth in certain regions.","Migration and displacement due to climate-related events are likely to become pressing global issues."],"keywords":["economic impact","vulnerability","inequality","infrastructure","displacement"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":268,"title":"Conceptual Note on Adaptation","authors":"","date":"2024-11-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Network for Greening the Financial System"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ngfs.net/en/publications-and-statistics/publications/ngfs-conceptual-note-adaptation","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The NGFS Conceptual Note on Adaptation (November 2024) is the network's first comprehensive exploration of climate adaptation and its implications for financial stability. It makes the case that central banks and supervisors should integrate adaptation into their mandates of price and financial stability, covering: the economic costs of insufficient adaptation (up to USD 1,266 trillion total inaction costs through 2100; ~1% GDP/year losses for low-income countries 2000\u20132019); the triple dividend of adaptation investment (up to USD 12 in economic benefit per USD 1 invested); an adaptation finance gap of USD 194\u2013366 billion/year in developing countries; and the 60% global insurance protection gap (USD 280B total economic losses vs. USD 108B insured in 2023). The note identifies four priority areas for central banks and supervisors: metrics/disclosure, regulatory frameworks, enabling adaptation finance, and international collaboration.","insights":["Extreme weather events linked to climate change reduced GDP by approximately 1% per year on average for low-income countries between 2000 and 2019; in some EMDEs, unmitigated climate change could reduce GDP by more than 12% by 2050.","The total cost of inaction from 2025 to 2100 is estimated at up to USD 1,266 trillion, and 32 million individuals are at risk of falling into poverty by 2030 due to insufficient adaptation.","Adaptation investment returns are high: the World Bank estimated a 1:4 dividend; more recent research (Standard Chartered/KPMG/UNDRR, 2024) finds USD 12 in overall economic benefit for every USD 1 invested. Every USD 1 billion invested against coastal flooding yields USD 14 billion in avoided economic damages.","The annual adaptation finance gap in developing countries ranges from USD 194 billion to USD 366 billion, 10 to 18 times current financing flows, and is projected to widen to USD 315\u2013565 billion by 2050. Only ~4% of global climate finance goes to adaptation, with 98% from public sources.","In 2023, 60% of global exposures were uninsured (total economic losses USD 280B vs. insured losses USD 108B). Annual insured losses have grown 5\u20137% per decade for 30 years. Closing insurance protection gaps is a direct financial stability concern for central banks and supervisors.","Risk-based insurance pricing and impact underwriting can create feedback loops that incentivize physical adaptation: higher premiums for climate-exposed assets signal risk and encourage investment in flood barriers, elevated structures, or managed retreat, ultimately reducing vulnerability and premiums."],"keywords":["insurance protection gap","adaptation finance gap","triple dividend","blended finance EMDEs","physical risk management","impact underwriting","resilience taxonomy","national adaptation plans"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":217,"title":"Adaptation Gap Report 2024: Come Hell and High Water - As Fires and Floods Hit the Poor Hardest, It Is Time for the World to Step Up Adaptation Actions","authors":"","date":"2024-11-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Environment Programme"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.59117/20.500.11822/46497","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Adaptation Gap Report 2024 underscores the urgent need for enhanced climate adaptation actions globally, particularly for vulnerable populations disproportionately affected by climate-related disasters. It highlights significant financial gaps in adaptation efforts and advocates for innovative financing, capacity-building, and effective planning to create equitable and resilient responses to climate change. The report calls for better alignment between Nationally Determined Contributions and National Adaptation Plans, emphasizing the necessity for transformational approaches to close the adaptation finance gap and support sustainable development.","insights":["The world is on track for a temperature rise of 2.6-3.1\u00b0C, necessitating urgent adaptation measures.","Estimated adaptation finance needs for developing countries range from $387 billion to $975 billion annually.","While international public adaptation finance flows increased to $28 billion in 2022, this remains inadequate to meet the urgent needs of vulnerable nations.","The adaptation finance gap is particularly severe in highly vulnerable countries, hindering their ability to effectively respond to climate impacts.","Over 87% of countries have national adaptation planning instruments, but implementation quality and alignment with Nationally Determined Contributions vary significantly."],"keywords":["vulnerability","investment","capacity-building","sustainability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":136,"title":"Sovereigns: Climate Performance and the Cost of Capital","authors":"Kamesh Korangi, Josephine Richardson, Hazel Ilango","date":"2024-10-25","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Anthropocene Fixed Income Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5061689","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource discusses the interplay between sovereign climate performance and the cost of capital, emphasizing the significant role that government policy decisions play in the climate transition. It employs an advanced regression model to explore how climate factors influence government bond yields, identifying key drivers and the evolution of their impact over time, particularly highlighting the unique dynamics within ASEAN and Western European nations.","insights":["Government policy decisions are crucial in mitigating climate risks and shaping the financial stability of sovereigns.","The correlation between climate performance and government bond yields has been increasing, particularly since 2018.","ASEAN countries exhibit unique challenges regarding climate vulnerability and bond yield interactions compared to other regions.","Research indicates that higher emissions correlate with increased bond yields in certain contexts, especially in relatively developed regions like Europe.","Effective climate policies can help mitigate credit risks associated with sovereign debt, potentially lowering the cost of capital for climate-related investments.","The significance of climate-related factors in bond yields can vary significantly over time and by region, indicating a nuanced relationship.","The use of advanced regression models, including SHAP analysis, provides deeper insights into the factors influencing bond yields beyond traditional economic measures.","Innovative financial mechanisms linked to climate performance could enhance investor confidence and improve the credit ratings of vulnerable countries.","Higher climate readiness generally correlates with lower bond yields, although this relationship can vary based on specific socio-economic factors.","Transition risks associated with climate change may not be fully priced into the sovereign debt markets yet, leading to potential mispricings."],"keywords":["government bonds","credit risk","financial stability","emissions","policy decisions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":152,"title":"Understanding Global Concessional Climate Finance 2024","authors":"Baysa Naran, Tinglu Zhang, Ishrita Gupta","date":"2024-10-14","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/understanding-global-concessional-climate-finance-2024/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Understanding Global Concessional Climate Finance 2024' report underscores the urgent need to significantly increase concessional capital finance to meet Paris Agreement goals, especially for vulnerable regions. It highlights trends from 2019 to 2022, revealing a 50% increase in international concessional climate finance to USD 81 billion, though still far below fossil fuel subsidies. The report emphasizes the necessity of blended finance, public-private collaboration, and innovative funding mechanisms to address substantial funding gaps in adaptation and resilience projects, while recommending improvements in coordination and impact metrics.","insights":["Concessional capital finance needs to increase fivefold by 2030 to achieve climate goals.","In 2022, international concessional climate finance reached USD 81 billion, a 50% increase from 2019, but still low compared to fossil fuel subsidies of USD 1.3 trillion.","About 33% of total international concessional finance went to least-developed countries (LDCs), highlighting their critical financing needs.","Blended finance can effectively lower project risks and attract diverse investor types through innovative capital structures.","Technical assistance is crucial for enhancing the commercial viability and impact of climate finance projects.","The presence of risk buffers, like first loss and subordinate equity, increases the attractiveness of investments for institutional players.","Concessional finance has been shown to quadruple private investments for every dollar of donor capital utilized."],"keywords":["blended finance","infrastructure","public investment","risk management","renewable energy"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":60,"title":"Aligning Community, Conservation, and Defense Priorities in the Fort Huachuca Sentinel Landscape","authors":"Chris Wolff; Reed Benoit; Shaun O\u2019Rourke","date":"2024-10-10","year":2024,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/fort-huachuca-case-study","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Fort Huachuca Sentinel Landscape faces severe groundwater depletion affecting ecosystems, military operations, and the local economy. Collaborating with various stakeholders, QV has developed financial strategies and secured over $4 million for groundwater recharge projects. This initiative also aims to establish an $80 million endowment for long-term conservation and operational needs, serving as a model for similar landscapes nationwide.","insights":["Groundwater depletion threatens ecosystems and military readiness in Fort Huachuca.","Collaboration among diverse stakeholders is crucial for effective resource management.","Innovative financing can bridge gaps in existing funding mechanisms for conservation.","Secured funding of over $4 million supports immediate and sustainable groundwater recharge projects.","An $80 million endowment is being structured for long-term stewardship and operational needs.","The partnership model could be replicated across other US Sentinel Landscapes.","Aligning priorities among local communities, conservation agencies, and military partners enhances project success.","A phased investment plan helps manage funding effectively across different stakeholders.","Blended finance approaches accelerate project implementation and ensure ongoing funding.","Demonstrating measurable outcomes strengthens support for conservation initiatives."],"keywords":["groundwater","ecosystems","partnership","endowment","blended finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":149,"title":"Why Private Capital Is Indispensable to Close the Great Development and Climate Financing Divide","authors":"Mahmoud Mohieldin, Manuela Stefania Fulga","date":"2024-10-03","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/stories/2024/10/private-capital-indispensable-development-climate-financing-divide/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This World Economic Forum article (October 2024), co-authored by the UN Special Envoy for Financing Sustainable Development and WEF's Lead for Sustainable Finance, argues that the SDG financing gap in emerging and developing economies has grown from $2.5 trillion annually (2017 estimate) to $4.2 trillion, constituting a \"great financial divide.\" Private capital mobilization reached an all-time high of $21.9 billion in 2022 but represents less than 20% of total funds mobilized. The piece calls for blended finance structures, MDB/DFI de-risking mechanisms, and enabling policy environments, citing Egypt's $14.7 billion green project pipeline as a replicable model, to attract commercial capital to climate, energy, water, and nature investments in developing markets.","insights":["The SDG financing gap in emerging and developing economies has grown from $2.5 trillion/year (2017) to $4.2 trillion/year (2024), driven by COVID-19 impacts, geopolitical fragmentation, and rising debt burdens.","Private capital mobilization for sustainable development reached an all-time high of $21.9 billion in 2022 but still represents less than 20% of total development funds mobilized, illustrating the scale of the structural gap between available and needed private finance.","Renewable energy projects in emerging markets cost up to three times more than in developed countries, with the cost premium driven primarily by macroeconomic risk factors rather than project-specific risks, making de-risking instruments the critical lever.","External public debt service in developing economies rose from 5% of public revenues in 2010 to 8.8% today, with a growing number of countries spending more on interest payments than on development efforts, crowding out public climate and resilience investment.","Egypt's Nexus of Water, Food and Energy programme created a green project pipeline worth $14.7 billion using government and concessional financing as a catalytic layer to unlock private-sector engagement, offered as a replicable model for other developing economies.","WEF's Mobilizing Investment for Clean Energy in Emerging Economies Network published a 'Playbook of Solutions' referencing over 100 policy measures, finance mechanisms, and de-risking solutions that have successfully catalyzed private capital for clean energy in emerging markets.","Global leaders at WEF's Sustainable Development Impact Meetings identified three priority action areas: (1) collaboration across the investment value chain to make projects bankable, (2) strategic deployment of scarce public funds to catalyze commercial capital, and (3) enabling policy environments through strategic reforms."],"keywords":["SDG financing gap","blended finance","emerging markets","private capital mobilization","de-risking mechanisms","development finance"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":545,"title":"Assessing the Impact of Climate Resilience","authors":"","date":"2024-10-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Wellington Management","Woodwell Climate Research Center"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wellington.com/en/insights/measuring-impact-climate-resiliency","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An asset manager's account of why measuring the impact of climate resilience investments is harder than measuring climate mitigation, and what to do about it. The argument is that mitigation impact has a universal quantitative yardstick, greenhouse gases emitted or avoided, and resilience has none: the data is mainly qualitative and situation-specific, it depends on assumptions about local and regional climate effects over long timescales, and the underlying benefit is harm avoided against a counterfactual that did not occur and a baseline that itself moves. Wellington sets out approaches it thinks help investors work through those difficulties while still holding resilience as an impact investment class.","insights":["The measurement problem is stated precisely: the impact of a resilience investment is loss avoided relative to a counterfactual that did not happen, which requires assumptions rather than observation.","A second and subtler difficulty is the moving baseline: the level of protection a flood defence provides must be measured against a future flood regime that is itself changing, so the denominator shifts while the intervention is in place.","Mitigation is used deliberately as the reference case rather than as a rival: the absence of an equivalent to tonnes of CO2 avoided is what makes resilience impact resist the standard impact-investing toolkit.","The recommended answer is a mix rather than a metric, combining qualitative and quantitative data for a holistic assessment, which is an admission that no single number will do the work.","The research base is a named scientific partnership with Woodwell Climate Research Center rather than a vendor risk score, which is unusual among asset managers writing on physical risk."],"keywords":["impact measurement","climate resilience investing","avoided loss","counterfactual","moving baseline"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":102,"title":"Global Landscape of Climate Finance 2024: Insights for COP29","authors":"Baysa Naran, Barbara Buchner, Matthew Price, Sean Stout, Maddy Taylor, Dennis Zabeida","date":"2024-10-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Policy Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/global-landscape-of-climate-finance-2024/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Global Landscape of Climate Finance 2024' report examines the resilience and growth of climate finance, which reached USD 1.46 trillion in 2022, yet underscores the urgent need for increased funding to meet the estimated annual requirement of USD 7.4 trillion by 2030. It highlights disparities between advanced economies and emerging markets and developing economies (EMDEs), the importance of innovative financing solutions, and the critical need for collaboration, data standardization, and capacity building to enhance the effectiveness of climate finance across sectors.","insights":["Climate finance has more than doubled from USD 674 billion in 2018 to USD 1.46 trillion in 2022.","Public sources accounted for 85% of clean transport finance outside China, while private investments are increasingly dominant in the buildings and infrastructure sectors.","Adaptation finance in EMDEs grew faster than mitigation finance, indicating a shift toward urgent climate resilience investments.","There is a significant climate finance gap, especially for least developed countries (LDCs) and small island developing states (SIDS), necessitating increased international public finance support.","Standardization of data and disclosure practices is crucial for improving trust and accountability in climate finance efforts.","Domestic climate finance is becoming increasingly vital, with significant growth in regions like Latin America and South Asia.","Enhanced interoperability among finance providers is needed to maximize resource efficiency and address the fragmented climate finance landscape.","Concessional finance and innovative instruments like debt-for-climate swaps are necessary to promote climate resilience in debt-distressed regions."],"keywords":["sustainability","innovation","data standardization","international collaboration","capacity building"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":183,"title":"Insurance and Climate Risks: Policy Lessons from Three Bounding Scenarios","authors":"Carolyn Kousky, Galen Treuer, Katherine J. Mach","date":"2024-09-27","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["PNAS","Environmental Defense Fund","Miami-Dade County Office of Innovation and Economic Development","University of Miami"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2317875121","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource examines the evolving landscape of insurance markets in Florida amid increasing climate risks, particularly related to hurricane and flood threats. It analyzes three different scenarios, exploring how near-term policy decisions will affect long-term outcomes for insurance markets, housing, and local economies. The scenarios emphasize the need for proactive investments in risk reduction, equitable policy-making, and the potential consequences of inaction as climate hazards intensify.","insights":["Insurance markets in high-risk regions like Florida are experiencing significant stress due to escalating climate hazards.","Public-sector insurance programs are increasingly absorbing risks as private insurers withdraw from high-risk areas.","Sustainable adaptation strategies must directly address rising risks rather than merely shifting them to different sectors.","Inequitable access to insurance can exacerbate income inequality, particularly after disasters strike low-income communities.","Near-term regulatory decisions regarding insurance can have profound effects on future disaster recovery and socioeconomic well-being."],"keywords":["insurance volatility","risk management","housing markets","equity","scenario analysis"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":77,"title":"California Insurers Begin Giving Discounts for Fire-Proofed Homes","authors":"Avery Ellfeldt","date":"2024-09-19","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","Alabama","California"],"institutions":["Politico"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.eenews.net/articles/california-insurers-begin-giving-discounts-for-fire-proofed-homes/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"California has implemented a new program requiring property insurers to offer discounts for homeowners who mitigate wildfire risks, making it the first state in the nation to do so. However, the discounts are often minimal and may not significantly encourage homeowners to invest in fire-proofing measures. Experts suggest that while the long-term benefits may improve as data on effectiveness becomes available, immediate savings are limited and may not justify the costs of the required mitigation measures.","insights":["California is the first state to mandate discounts for wildfire mitigation in property insurance.","Discounts for fire-proofing measures, such as fire-resistant windows, are often minuscule, with some homeowners seeing as little as a $14 saving on a $13,800 premium.","There is a lack of reliable data on the effectiveness of individual wildfire mitigation steps, which has led insurers to offer minimal discounts.","Experts warn that small savings could limit the success of the discount program, making it difficult for homeowners to invest in necessary mitigation upfront.","The California discount program could serve as a model for other states, though many are currently hesitant to implement similar requirements without further data.","A range of property-clearing and design improvements are listed under the state-mandated discounts, but the depth and effectiveness of these strategies remain uncertain.","The approach to discounts varies among insurers, creating inconsistencies that may confuse policyholders.","It's projected that discounts may grow over time as the insurance industry gains more understanding of mitigation effectiveness.","Insurers have cautioned that discounts alone cannot cover the costs of retrofitting homes, and state incentives may be necessary to encourage adoption of wildfire safety measures.","Historical examples from other states, such as Alabama's wind-mitigation discounts, suggest that acceptance and participation may increase as the success of such programs becomes evident."],"keywords":["wildfire mitigation","insurance discounts","home retrofitting","fire-resistant measures","homeowner incentives"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":189,"title":"Beware How Financial Markets Are Pricing in Climate Risk: An Inconsistent Approach and General Lack of Understanding Is Heightening Risk","authors":"Bert Kramer, Bronwyn Claire","date":"2024-09-03","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Ortec Finance"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ortecfinance.com/en/insights/blog/beware-how-financial-markets-are-pricing-in-climate-risk","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The financial markets are currently employing an inconsistent approach to pricing climate risk, resulting in a heightened level of uncertainty and risk across the board. This lack of understanding and uniformity in addressing climate-related risks in financial evaluations is exacerbating vulnerabilities in investment and economic stability.","insights":["Financial markets lack a consistent methodology for pricing climate risk.","Inconsistencies in climate risk evaluation increase overall market vulnerability.","A general misunderstanding of climate risk among investors is prevalent.","The current approach may lead to mispricing of assets affected by climate change.","There is a critical need for standardized frameworks to assess climate risk.","Market participants often underestimate the long-term impacts of climate change.","Failing to accurately price climate risk could lead to significant financial losses.","Greater transparency and communication are needed regarding climate-related financial risks."],"keywords":["financial markets","risk assessment","investment stability","asset valuation","market vulnerability"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":24,"title":"Federal Land Leasing, Energy, and Local Public Finances","authors":"Ana Martinez, Dustin Tingley","date":"2024-08-26","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Harvard University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://salatainstitute.harvard.edu/federal-land-leasing-energy-and-local-public-finances/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource highlights a significant legislative disparity in revenue distribution from fossil fuel versus renewable energy projects on federal land, where fossil fuel revenue is shared with states and local communities while renewable energy revenues primarily benefit the federal government. It underscores the public's strong support for reforming policies to establish a revenue-sharing mechanism for renewable energy similar to that of fossil fuels, emphasizing the need for local community involvement in revenue allocation decisions to enhance economic benefits and social acceptance of energy developments.","insights":["Revenue from fossil fuel projects is shared with states and local communities, while renewable energy revenues largely stay with the federal government.","The Public Land Renewable Energy Development Act (PLREDA) seeks to establish a revenue-sharing mechanism for renewable energy projects on federal land.","Bipartisan support exists for reforming current policy to allow states and local communities to benefit from renewable energy revenues.","Public opinion shows overwhelming support for equitable revenue distribution from energy projects, with 89% of respondents favoring revenue sharing for renewables.","73% of respondents believe that local communities affected by energy development should influence revenue allocation decisions."],"keywords":["federal land","energy policy","revenue allocation","public services","economic impact"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":18,"title":"How Local Governments Avoid Floodplain Development Through Consistent Implementation of Routine Municipal Ordinances, Plans, and Programs","authors":"A R Siders, Jennifer Niemann-Morris, Miyuki Hino, Elizabeth Shields, Lidia Cano Pecharroman, Tess Doeffinger, Logan Gerber-Chavez, Ju-Ching Huang, Alexandra Lafferty, Salvesila Tamima, Caroline Williams, Armen Agopian, Christopher Samoray, Katharine J Mach","date":"2024-08-19","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","New Jersey"],"institutions":["Oxford Open Climate Change","University of Delaware","University of Miami","University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill","Massachusetts Institute of Technology","Georgetown University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfclm/kgae017","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This study examines how local governments in New Jersey effectively limit floodplain development through the consistent application of routine municipal ordinances, plans, and programs. The analysis reveals that a significant majority of towns have successfully avoided new housing development in floodplains from 2001 to 2019 by utilizing common land use management tools rather than relying on innovative legal measures. The findings challenge prevailing narratives about floodplain development and highlight the importance of having a few high-quality management tools, consistent implementation, and local official commitment.","insights":["85% of towns in New Jersey limited new floodplain housing development from 2001 to 2019.","126 towns in New Jersey managed to put none of their new housing in floodplains during the study period.","Robust floodplain avoidance is achievable through routine administrative practices without the need for legal innovations.","The quality and consistent implementation of floodplain management tools are more critical than their quantity.","Local government capacity is a weak predictor of floodplain development outcomes, counter to expectations from previous studies.","Coastal and populous towns can also effectively limit floodplain development despite common assumptions about their vulnerability.","The study indicates a need to focus on municipalities that are successfully avoiding floodplain development to understand best practices.","Developments in floodplains often lead to increased flood risks, emphasizing the importance of managing local land effectively.","Local commitment and coordination among officials greatly influence the successful implementation of floodplain avoidance strategies."],"keywords":["floodplain management","land use","municipal ordinances","disaster risk reduction","hazard mitigation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":198,"title":"G20/OECD Report on Approaches for Financing and Investing in Climate\u2011resilient Infrastructure","authors":"","date":"2024-07-29","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["OECD"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1787/8f6d436a-en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The G20/OECD report emphasizes the urgent need for financing climate-resilient infrastructure, particularly in developing countries facing climate risks. It advocates for integrating climate resilience throughout the infrastructure lifecycle, fostering adaptive planning and collaboration among governments, communities, and the private sector. By leveraging innovative financial instruments, project preparation facilities, and nature-based solutions, the report aims to mobilize resources for sustainable infrastructure that meets both economic and climate resilience goals.","insights":["Developing countries face significant infrastructure investment gaps while being highly vulnerable to climate impacts.","Integrating climate resilience across the infrastructure lifecycle can enhance economic returns and public safety.","Adaptive planning and collaboration are essential for effective climate-resilient infrastructure development.","Innovative financing mechanisms, including blended finance and public-private partnerships, can attract investment for resilience-focused projects.","Nature-based solutions offer co-benefits that enhance climate resilience and community well-being.","Transforming infrastructure plans into bankable projects is crucial for mobilizing investment, particularly in developing regions.","Community engagement plays a vital role in the successful implementation of climate-resilient infrastructure projects.","Investment in flood-resilient infrastructure has the potential to create substantial job opportunities."],"keywords":["infrastructure development","investment strategies","public-private partnership","adaptive planning","nature-based solutions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":346,"title":"Q&A: Can Debt-for-Nature 'Swaps' Help Tackle Biodiversity Loss and Climate Change?","authors":"","date":"2024-07-16","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global","Latin America"],"institutions":["African Development Bank","The Nature Conservancy","Climate Bonds Initiative","Inter-American Development Bank","World Bank","Green Climate Fund","World Wildlife Fund","International Finance Corporation","Asian Development Bank","Carbon Brief"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.carbonbrief.org/qa-can-debt-for-nature-swaps-help-tackle-biodiversity-loss-and-climate-change","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Carbon Brief Q&A examines debt-for-nature swaps, financial agreements in which a conservation organisation or government reduces or restructures a developing country's debt in exchange for investment in local conservation, tracing their origins from 1987 to a current resurgence. Since the 1980s, 145 swaps worldwide have written off $3.7bn in face-value debt, with $2.4bn in Latin America and the Caribbean. The piece weighs proponents' claims that swaps can unlock $100bn+ for developing countries against critics who call them too small, sovereignty-undermining, and unproven for biodiversity outcomes, and examines governance, greenwashing, and human rights concerns through case studies in Ecuador, Seychelles, Gabon, and Bolivia.","insights":["145 debt-for-nature swaps since the 1980s have written off $3.7bn in face value globally, with $2.4bn concentrated in Latin America and the Caribbean, dwarfed by the $443.5bn that low- and middle-income countries paid to service foreign debt in 2022 alone.","A 2023 IIED analysis estimates debt-for-nature and debt-for-climate swaps could collectively free up more than $100bn of debt in developing countries, but critics note current deal sizes are far too small: the landmark Seychelles swap was only $23m and Ecuador's record 2023 swap wrote off $1.1bn against a $9tn total developing-country debt stock.","Ecuador's 2023 Galapagos swap, the largest by debt volume at $1.1bn cancelled, committed $450m to conservation but faced documented governance failures: lack of Indigenous consultation, limited monitoring, no additional international disbursements, and a 20% administration fee to a foreign-owned conservation entity that immediately reduced country savings.","Poland's 1992 swap allocated the highest absolute conservation investment of any swap on record at over $500m; Ecuador's 2023 swap ranks second at over $400m earmarked for conservation, illustrating how deal design, not just volume, determines conservation yield.","A 2023 study found 67 debt-distressed countries collectively host 22% of global biodiversity priority areas, and for 35 of them, all unprotected priority areas could be conserved for a fraction of national debt, establishing a theoretical economic case for swaps even if current execution falls short.","High transaction costs from multilateral negotiations, intermediary fees, and legal advisors erode conservation value; currency risk compounds losses, Zambia's 1989 swap funds were exhausted in one year due to rapid local currency devaluation."],"keywords":["debt-for-nature swaps","sovereign debt restructuring","biodiversity finance gap","conservation conditionality","blue bonds","developing country debt","nature finance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":47,"title":"Ten Years in, D.C.\u2019s Stormwater Credit Market Is Thriving and Still Growing","authors":"","date":"2024-07-15","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["The Nature Conservancy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nature.org/en-us/newsroom/ten-year-anniversary-of-src-market/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Washington D.C.'s Stormwater Retention Credit (SRC) market, established a decade ago, has become a model for innovative stormwater management, allowing developers to purchase credits from green infrastructure projects to meet runoff requirements. This program has significantly increased green spaces and improved water quality, drawing interest from cities globally as a potential solution to similar environmental challenges.","insights":["The SRC market allows developers to buy credits to meet stormwater runoff obligations, promoting green infrastructure deployment.","Since 2014, over 1.7 million credits have been sold, capturing more than 40 million gallons of runoff annually.","The market incentivizes projects in under-resourced areas and supports ecological and social co-benefits.","D.C. is seen as a leader in stormwater credit markets, with cities like Grand Rapids and Cook County developing similar programs.","The Nature Conservancy's involvement highlights the potential for private investment in stormwater solutions.","The SRC market has influenced educational programs at major universities to teach its model to future practitioners.","Financial benefits from green infrastructure investments can yield substantial community returns, such as $7.80 per dollar invested.","The market continues to evolve, with policy updates planned to enhance local participation and support small businesses."],"keywords":["stormwater management","green infrastructure","urban ecology","water quality","sustainability"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":114,"title":"The Preparedness Payoff: The Economic Benefits of Investing in Climate Resilience","authors":"U.S. Chamber of Commerce, Allstate","date":"2024-06-21","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["U.S. Chamber of Commerce","Allstate","U.S. Chamber of Commerce Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.uschamber.com/security/the-preparedness-payoff-the-economic-benefits-of-investing-in-climate-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report titled 'The Preparedness Payoff: The Economic Benefits of Investing in Climate Resilience' outlines the substantial economic advantages of investing in disaster preparedness, revealing that every dollar spent yields significant savings in economic costs, job retention, and damage reduction. The findings highlight that proactive investments not only mitigate the impacts of natural disasters but also enhance community resilience and economic stability, providing a solid return on investment.","insights":["Every $1 invested in disaster preparation saves $13 in total economic costs.","For every dollar invested in resilience, communities can save $7 in economic costs.","Preparedness investments can preserve jobs, with $10.8 billion potentially saving 184,000 jobs in Miami.","Smaller investments, like $83 million for tornado readiness in Nashville, can save over 5,300 jobs.","Investments lead to increased economic activity and workforce retention, even when disasters do not occur.","Disasters exceeding $1 billion in impacts highlight the need for significant preparedness investments.","High-severity disasters require larger upfront investments to mitigate potential damages.","Preparedness measures like early warning systems enhance overall community resilience.","Economic savings include reduced damage, cleanup costs, and less economic damage during disasters."],"keywords":["disaster mitigation","economic recovery","community preparedness","job preservation","investment returns"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":486,"title":"Housing Amenity and Affordability Shape Floodplain Development","authors":"Christopher Samoray, Miyuki Hino, A.R. Siders, Armen Agopian, Katharine J. Mach","date":"2024-06-17","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Miami Climate Resilience Institute","University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill","University of Delaware","Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.landusepol.2024.107216","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Samoray and colleagues analyse 87 million US residential parcels against FEMA Digital Flood Insurance Rate Maps to characterise floodplain housing stock by value and type and to measure how new development changes exposure. Of the 87.1 million parcels, 4.8 per cent, or 4.1 million, are in the Special Flood Hazard Area: 2,818,694 single-family homes, 963,043 multi-family and 261,186 mobile homes. Exposure follows a U-shaped distribution in housing value that deepens over time: 7 per cent of the most expensive quintile and 5.5 per cent of the most affordable quintile are in floodplains, against the middle range, and in the extreme 5 per cent tails the shares reach 9.3 and 6.2 per cent. The pattern splits by geography: in coastal communities the most expensive quintile holds 21.4 per cent of floodplain housing against 11.9 per cent in the middle, while inland the most affordable quintile is 1.6 percentage points above the middle. Using a floodplain price ratio comparing a community's median floodplain value to its county median, 5613 of 10,237 communities have affordable floodplains and 4616 expensive ones; coastal communities average a ratio of 2.13 against 1.06 inland. In the 112 communities with a ratio below 0.25, 38 per cent of floodplain housing stock is mobile homes, falling below 10 per cent as the ratio approaches 1. Between 2001 and 2019 communities with very expensive floodplains built an average of 105 new floodplain residences against 15 for communities with very affordable floodplains. Mobile home construction in floodplains peaked in the 1990s at 6374 a year and fell to 2787 a year after 2000, while single-family homes rose to about three-quarters of new floodplain housing in the 2010s.","insights":["The U-shaped exposure curve is the finding that breaks the standard policy frame: both the richest and the poorest housing is over-represented in the floodplain, and a national policy designed for either one underserves the other.","The bifurcation in housing value is a bifurcation in the ability to self-finance, so the same physical hazard produces two entirely different financing problems, one of affordability and one of moral hazard around protecting expensive private property with public funds.","The floodplain price ratio is the paper's most usable instrument for policy design, because it shows that community-scale averages mask who is actually at risk: 112 communities have floodplain housing worth under a quarter of the local median, and 38 per cent of it is mobile homes.","Exposure is growing fastest where it is most expensive: 105 new floodplain homes per very expensive community against 15 per very affordable one over two decades, so the NFIP's future book is being written on the coast.","The authors name the NFIP pricing dilemma precisely, that premiums must signal risk to affluent coastal owners while staying affordable for inland residents, which is a single instrument being asked to do two opposite things.","Mobile homes in floodplains halved after 2000 while single-family construction rose to three-quarters of new floodplain housing, so the exposure is shifting toward higher-value, more insurable and more politically defended assets."],"keywords":["floodplain development","Special Flood Hazard Area","floodplain price ratio","mobile homes","NFIP pricing","housing value distribution","flood exposure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":258,"title":"Firm Adaptation to Climate Risk in the Developing World","authors":"Arti Grover, Matthew E. Kahn","date":"2024-06-11","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","International Finance Corporation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/1813-9450-10797","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource examines the adaptation of firms in the developing world to climate risks, emphasizing that while extreme weather events typically hinder competitiveness, effective recovery is possible under certain conditions. It highlights the influence of firm capabilities, access to information, and public infrastructure quality on resilience. Additionally, the impacts of gradual climate changes, particularly temperature increases, are analyzed, revealing adverse effects on productivity and labor supply, with a focus on the varying challenges faced by different sectors. The role of government in facilitating adaptation through infrastructure investment and policy support is also stressed.","insights":["Firms' adaptation to climate changes significantly impacts national economic growth in developing countries.","The ability of firms to bounce back from weather events relies on internal capabilities and external conditions.","Real-time weather forecasting enhances risk assessments, influencing strategic decisions.","Market frictions and information asymmetries hinder adaptation speed, especially for smaller firms.","Government infrastructure investments can create moral hazards, leading firms to underestimate risks."],"keywords":["economic growth","public infrastructure","worker productivity","government policy","supply chain risk"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":349,"title":"Climate Resilient Debt Clause \u2014 FAQ","authors":"","date":"2024-06-03","year":2024,"resource_type":"Tool / Resource Hub","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/c81fdf80718a5eb9b3d18c178b5307be-0340012023/original/CRDC-FAQ.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This World Bank FAQ document (last updated June 3, 2024) describes the Climate Resilient Debt Clause (CRDC), a no-cost option embedded in IBRD loans and IDA credits that allows eligible small state and Small Island Developing State borrowers to defer principal and/or interest payments for up to two years following a qualifying natural disaster (tropical cyclone at Category 3+ intensity or earthquake \u2265 Mw 7.0, or damages \u2265 10% of GDP). The instrument is designed to free fiscal space for disaster recovery without triggering debt restructuring, and sits within the World Bank's broader crisis response toolkit.","insights":["The clause is an option the borrower elects into, deferring principal, interest and other loan charges for up to two years after a natural disaster, so it converts a debt service obligation into contingent liquidity without new borrowing.","Eligibility is confined to IBRD and IDA Small State Economies, Small States Forum members and UN-defined Small Island Developing States, with subnational borrowers in those countries also eligible, which is a deliberately narrow perimeter.","The trigger perimeter is earthquakes and tropical cyclones for small islands and other small states, so the clause does not cover slow-onset or non-geophysical climate shocks.","A borrower must be current on debt service to the World Bank to have the clause included, and it will not be added to existing loans during a restructuring with other lenders, so the instrument is unavailable precisely to the most distressed.","It arrived in June 2023 as part of the World Bank's expanded crisis response toolkit approved by the Board, which locates it in a wider shift toward contingent instruments rather than as a standalone product."],"keywords":["debt payment deferral","small island developing states","parametric triggers","sovereign debt clause","disaster recovery financing","multilateral development bank"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":570,"title":"U.S. Environmental Protection Agency 2024-2027 Climate Adaptation Plan","authors":"United States Environmental Protection Agency","date":"2024-06-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["United States Environmental Protection Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2024-06/epas-2024-2027-climate-adaptation-plan-508-compliant.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The US Environmental Protection Agency's Climate Adaptation Plan for 2024 to 2027, published June 2024 and opening with the Administrator's policy statement on climate change adaptation. The statement frames climate change as a direct threat to the agency's own mission of protecting human health and the environment: it exacerbates existing pollution problems and environmental stressors, and its impacts fall disproportionately on low-income communities and communities of colour, children, the elderly, Tribes and indigenous people. It records the May 2021 direction to assistant, associate and regional administrators and the general counsel to incorporate climate adaptation planning proactively into the agency's programmes, policies, rules and operations. The plan is an agency adaptation strategy rather than a financing document: across 192,654 characters the word grant appears seven times, funding six and financing once.","insights":["The distinctive move is that the agency treats climate change as a threat to its regulatory capability rather than only to the public. Adaptation here means keeping the Clean Air Act and Clean Water Act workable under conditions they were not written for, which is a different object from protecting an asset.","It is federal policy rather than finance: financing language is measurably scarce in the text, so the document sets objectives rather than inventorying funding.","The May 2021 internal direction is the provenance that makes the plan auditable: it names who was told to do what, which is what lets a later reader check whether the plan was implemented."],"keywords":["climate adaptation plan","federal agency","environmental justice","regulatory capability","EPA","Tribes","adaptation planning"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":501,"title":"Public Preferences for Coastal Adaptation: Economic Evidence from a Discrete Choice Experiment for Hard Structures and Nature-Based Solutions in Miami, Florida","authors":"Carlie Dario, Renato Molina, David L. Kelly","date":"2024-05-29","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of Miami Climate Resilience Institute","Miami-Dade County"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.marpol.2024.106217","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Dario, Molina and Kelly, all at the University of Miami, use a discrete choice experiment in Miami-Dade County to establish public preferences and trade-offs between hard structures and nature-based solutions for coastal risk reduction, specifically seawalls and mangroves. Respondents favour deploying any coastal measure over the status quo, and show a higher willingness to pay for nature-based solutions than for hard structures. Marginal willingness to pay, computed with representative values from the 2021 American Community Survey for Miami-Dade rather than from the non-representative sample, is $7.31 per month for mangrove rather than seawall type, $10.74 per mile per month for length of protected coastline, $0.42 per month per foot of height and $0.52 per month to avoid one flood day. On average respondents are willing to pay $18.05 a month for a mile of mangrove protection along Biscayne Bay, against $10.74 for a seawall with the same characteristics. Respondents under 65, in the labour force, with a college degree and income above $50,000 were less likely to pick the status quo, as were respondents who did not identify as White and those living within 15 km of the coastline. The authors place their values in range with Chen, Gao and Bi, who found $21.32 a month for seawalls, $18.99 for beach nourishment and $16.09 for stormwater pumps in the same county.","insights":["The $7.31 monthly premium for mangrove over seawall is a directly measured public preference for nature-based solutions, which is the number a Miami-Dade special assessment or stormwater fee would need in order to justify the nature-based option on willingness to pay alone.","Length of protected coastline is worth more per month than height, $10.74 a mile against $0.42 a foot, so residents value extent of protection over depth of protection, which is the opposite of how a hard-structure engineering case is usually argued.","Willingness to pay for the nature-based option at $18.05 a month per mile makes a recurring revenue instrument, a fee or a district levy, arithmetically plausible rather than hypothetical.","Preference for action over the status quo held across the sample, so the political constraint in Miami-Dade is not public willingness but which measure and who pays.","The authors use census-representative values rather than sample means, which is the methodological choice that makes the figures usable for a real revenue estimate rather than only for a ranking."],"keywords":["discrete choice experiment","willingness to pay","mangroves","seawalls","nature-based solutions","Biscayne Bay","coastal adaptation","Miami-Dade"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":227,"title":"Valuing Climate Adaptation Helps Us Orient Our Compass Toward Effective and Resilient Pathways: Climate Adaptation Costing in a Changing World","authors":"","date":"2024-05-23","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Europe"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/41594","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource highlights the urgent need for effective climate adaptation investment strategies in Europe to address increasing climatic shocks and associated economic losses. It emphasizes the importance of valuing climate adaptation through comprehensive costing methodologies, case studies, and stakeholder engagement to guide policymakers in identifying viable investments, enhancing overall resilience, and ensuring that financial resources are allocated effectively across sectors. The report advocates for transformational adaptation strategies and improved collaboration among public and private stakeholders to close the adaptation finance gap.","insights":["Proactive investments in climate adaptation can yield substantial socio-economic and environmental benefits, including cost savings and job creation.","Transformational adaptation is necessary to address long-term climate risks and requires a shift from incremental adjustments to systemic changes.","Effective climate adaptation costing requires comprehensive methodologies and engagement across sectors to enhance decision-making and resource allocation.","Public-private partnerships and innovative financing mechanisms are essential for mobilizing the necessary investments for climate adaptation.","Valuing climate adaptation helps prioritize actions, supports better budgeting, and addresses existing knowledge gaps in adaptation costs."],"keywords":["investment strategies","risk management","public-private partnerships","cost-benefit analysis","stakeholder engagement"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":553,"title":"Tailwind Climate's A&R Taxonomy, World Bank Warns Europe on Resilience, & More","authors":"Louie Woodall","date":"2024-05-20","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global","US","Europe"],"institutions":["Climate Bonds Initiative","World Bank","Climate Policy Initiative","ClimateWorks Foundation","European Commission","International Monetary Fund","Green Climate Fund"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climateproof.news/p/tailwind-climates-ar-taxonomy-world-bank-warns-europe-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An issue of the Climate Proof newsletter by Louie Woodall, dated 20 May 2024, whose lead item is the release of Tailwind Climate's Taxonomy for Adaptation and Resilience Investments. The taxonomy breaks potential adaptation and resilience investments across eight themes and 35 sectors, and for each sector gives examples across four categories: Intelligence, Products and Services, Finance and Insurance, and Enabling Interventions. It maps those investments onto the North American Industry Classification System, the Global Industry Classification system and OECD Development Assistance Committee markers, and links them to the 2023 Sharm-El-Sheikh Adaptation Agenda and the Sustainable Development Goals. Development partners included the Climate Bonds Initiative, whose own Resilience Taxonomy was then in progress, the Global Adaptation and Resilience Investor group, Climate Policy Initiative, ClimateWorks Foundation and Vibrant Data Labs. The issue also covers the World Bank and European Commission series on the economics of disaster prevention in Europe, which recommends an EU-level Disaster Risk Finance strategy, and a round-up of adaptation funding announcements.","insights":["The author's own position shifts inside the piece, and the shift is the argument: he had been dismissive of the proliferation of adaptation taxonomies for classifying companies rather than encouraging investors, and concludes instead that they are interdependent components of one intellectual infrastructure that unblocks capital by making dedicated portfolios buildable and marketable.","Interoperability is the taxonomy's design choice rather than a feature list. Mapping to NAICS, GICS and OECD DAC markers means an investor can apply it inside existing processes, which is what separates an adopted classification from a published one.","Tailwind states that the taxonomy is a classification and exploration tool and must be used alongside impact assessment criteria to determine whether an investment is in fact adaptation, which keeps the burden of proof on the assessment rather than on the label.","The World Bank risk transfer paper's recommendation is an EU-level Disaster Risk Finance strategy setting out how much member states should have to pay to cover catastrophic events, which is sovereign risk retention stated as a policy target."],"keywords":["adaptation taxonomy","Tailwind Climate","TARI","Climate Bonds Resilience Taxonomy","disaster risk finance","Sharm-El-Sheikh Adaptation Agenda","OECD DAC markers","interoperability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":505,"title":"Pilot Climate Scenario Analysis Exercise: Summary of Participants' Risk-Management Practices and Estimates","authors":"","date":"2024-05-09","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Reserve Board","Bank of America","Citigroup","Goldman Sachs","JPMorgan Chase","Morgan Stanley","Wells Fargo"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/files/csa-exercise-summary-20240509.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Federal Reserve's summary of the pilot climate scenario analysis exercise it ran in 2023 with six large bank holding companies: Bank of America, Citigroup, Goldman Sachs, JPMorgan Chase, Morgan Stanley and Wells Fargo. The exercise was exploratory, with no consequences for bank capital or supervision, and was intended to learn about participants' climate risk-management practices and to build the ability of banks and supervisors to identify, estimate, monitor and manage climate-related financial risks. It ran a physical risk module and a transition risk module. Participants took different approaches to constructing the detailed scenarios and translating them into climate-adjusted credit risk parameters, driven by business model, views on risk, data access and prior participation in foreign climate scenario exercises. Most relied on existing credit risk models and assumed the historical relationships between model inputs and outputs continue to hold as the climate and the economy change. Participants reported significant data and modeling challenges, naming a lack of comprehensive and consistent data on building characteristics, insurance coverage and counterparties' own plans for managing climate risk, and in many cases relied on external vendors to fill those gaps. The report also covers governance, internal controls and internal audit arrangements.","insights":["The assumption most participants made, that historical relationships between model inputs and outputs continue to hold as the climate changes, is the one that a climate scenario is designed to break, so the exercise mostly measured how banks stretch existing credit models rather than how they model climate.","Insurance coverage is named as a top data gap, which matters because a mortgage's credit risk under physical stress turns almost entirely on whether the property is insured and at what price, and the banks could not see it.","Reliance on external vendors to fill data gaps concentrates supervisory risk in a small number of model providers, which is the systemic consequence of a data gap rather than the gap itself.","The exercise carries no capital or supervisory consequence by design, which is what makes participation honest and also what limits what it can change.","That six of the largest US banks produced materially different estimates from the same prescribed scenarios is the finding a supervisor would act on, because it means the number is not yet comparable across institutions."],"keywords":["climate scenario analysis","physical risk","transition risk","credit risk models","Federal Reserve","supervision","data gaps"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":303,"title":"Resilience Monetization and Credits Initiative: A Background Paper","authors":"Rania A. Al-Mashat, Marc Jeuland, Jyotsna Puri, Pablo A. Vieira, Mahinour Aboulatta, Saib Ahmad, Jahan-Zeb Chowdhury, Alejandro Diaz-Herrera, Mirna Elsharief, Farida Farghal, Jonathan Phillips, Nada Tawfik, Liilnna Teji, Drew von Glahn","date":"2024-05-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Duke University Nicholas Institute","NDC Partnership","James E. Rogers Energy Access Project"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://energyaccess.duke.edu/publication/resilience-monetization-and-credits-initiative-a-background-paper/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This May 2024 working paper from Duke University's James E. Rogers Energy Access Project introduces the Resilience Monetization and Credits Initiative (RMCI), a proposed framework to bridge the climate adaptation finance gap in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs). The paper proposes resilience credits as a novel asset class designed to mobilize and align public and private capital for climate adaptation in the most vulnerable communities, while ensuring equitable benefit sharing. Adaptation investment is estimated to fall 5\u201310 times short of actual need, motivating the RMCI design.","insights":["Adaptation finance for LMICs falls 5\u201310 times short of actual need, representing one of the largest documented gaps in climate finance relative to risk exposure.","The RMCI proposes resilience credits as a novel asset class, a market-based mechanism intended to align public and private capital around measurable resilience outcomes in vulnerable communities.","The $100 billion annual climate finance target established at COP15 (2009) has not been met for LMICs, and adaptation finance lags mitigation finance disproportionately, signaling a structural allocation failure rather than a resources gap alone.","Equitable benefit sharing is embedded as a design principle of the RMCI, positioning distributional outcomes as a core feature of the credit instrument rather than an afterthought, relevant for ESG allocators and DFIs evaluating social co-benefits.","The RMCI represents an attempt to create a standardized, tradeable resilience credit that could function analogously to carbon credits, potentially opening adaptation to voluntary and compliance market participation by private capital.","By framing resilience as a monetizable asset class, the initiative signals a shift from grant-dependent adaptation toward balance-sheet-legible instruments, a prerequisite for institutional investor participation.","The initiative is authored by a multi-institutional team including academics and practitioners, suggesting an intent to ground the credit design in both empirical methods and real-world implementability."],"keywords":["resilience credits","adaptation finance gap","novel asset class","low- and middle-income countries","benefit sharing","climate credit market"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":456,"title":"Mayor Adams Celebrates Launch of New York City\u2019s First Climate Budgeting Process","authors":"","date":"2024-04-30","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["New York City Mayor's Office of Management and Budget"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nyc.gov/mayors-office/news/2024/04/mayor-adams-celebrates-launch-new-york-city-s-first-climate-budgeting-process","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"New York City introduced its first climate budgeting publication through the Fiscal Year 2025 Executive Budget, becoming the first large city in the United States to adopt climate budgeting and joining London, Oslo and Mumbai in doing so. Climate budgeting incorporates science-based climate considerations into budget decision-making by evaluating how today's actions and spending contribute to longer-term climate targets and needs. The stated purposes are to let the city understand the climate impact of the dollars it spends, identify where more effort is needed, and champion forward-looking investments. The release is explicit that this formally integrates climate into city decision-making so that climate is weighed in all relevant investment decisions, and that it serves both the net-zero-by-2050 emissions goal and resiliency to extreme heat and flooding. Delivery sits with the Mayor's Office of Management and Budget, which has embedded new processes from that year, and Mayor Adams frames it as changing the calculus of government spending by weighing climate impacts at the critical early stages of the budget process.","insights":["The instrument is the budget process itself rather than a fund: embedding climate assessment at the early stage of capital decisions changes what gets proposed, which reaches further than any single financing vehicle.","It covers both mitigation and resilience, assessing capital commitments for alignment with net-zero and separately for alignment with climate resiliency, so adaptation is not folded into an emissions frame.","Being the first large US city, and joining London, Oslo and Mumbai, makes this the reference precedent other US cities will cite, which matters for how quickly the practice spreads.","Placing delivery with the Office of Management and Budget rather than a sustainability office is the structural choice that gives the process authority over spending."],"keywords":["climate budgeting","New York City","capital commitment plan","Office of Management and Budget","net zero","resiliency investment","budget process"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":293,"title":"Climate Risk and Financial Stability: Evidence from Syndicated Lending","authors":"Thomas Conlon, Rong Ding, Xing Huan, Zhifang Zhang","date":"2024-04-27","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University College Dublin","NEOMA Business School","EDHEC Business School","University of Warwick"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/1351847X.2024.2343111","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed study (European Journal of Finance, 2024) develops a bank-level climate risk measure using NOAA Billion-Dollar Weather and Climate Disasters data linked to Dealscan syndicated lending records, then tests whether unexpected physical climate shocks transmitted via cross-state lending increase US banks' individual and systemic risks. Across 12,142 lender-borrower-year observations (42 banks, 1,314 borrower firms, 1999\u20132019), a one-standard-deviation increase in the cross-state climate risk measure raises marginal expected shortfall by 14.7%, value-at-risk (1%) by 10.2%, and systemic risk contribution (1%) by 6.1%. Banks subsequently reduce lending and increase loan-loss provisions. Higher bank profitability partially offsets these effects.","insights":["A one-standard-deviation increase in cross-state climate risk exposure raises banks' marginal expected shortfall (MES) by 14.7%, VaR at 1% by 10.2%, and systemic risk contribution (CoVaR at 1%) by 6.1%, all significant at the 1% level across 12,142 lender-borrower-year observations.","Banks reduce their loans-to-assets ratio and increase loan-loss provision ratios following unexpected climate shocks, indicating post-shock credit contraction and more conservative risk provisioning, both material signals for bank credit allocators and regulators.","The climate risk transmission mechanism operates through cross-state (not same-state) syndicated lending: exposure to borrowers in climate-stressed states raises lender-bank risk even when the bank itself is located in a lower-risk state, identifying a portfolio-level contagion channel.","Bank profitability acts as a partial buffer: for CoVaR1, the aggregate climate-risk coefficient is more than halved (from 0.117 to 0.052) during high-profitability periods, supporting the regulatory implication that maintaining robust bank earnings strengthens financial system resilience against climate risk.","Loans earmarked for operating and capital expenditures amplify the climate risk effect on bank systemic risk (interaction term significant for 5 of 6 risk measures), consistent with the mechanism that climate losses impair borrowers' physical assets and repayment capacity, whereas M&A- and capital-structure-purpose loans show no significant amplification.","The NOAA Billion-Dollar Disasters dataset covering 1980\u20132020 records 290 events, 14,492 deaths, and $1.98 trillion in total losses (CPI-adjusted to 2020); in 2020 alone, losses reached $99.5 billion (~0.5% of US GDP), underscoring the macro-financial scale of physical climate risk."],"keywords":["syndicated lending","bank systemic risk","physical climate risk","climate risk measurement","marginal expected shortfall","CoVaR","loan loss provisions","cross-state lending"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":465,"title":"FEMA Policy 206-23-001: Benefit-Cost Analysis Discount Rate and Streamlined Cost-Effectiveness Approaches","authors":"FEMA (Federal agency policy)","date":"2024-04-24","year":2024,"resource_type":"Regulatory / Legislative Document","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","Office of Management and Budget"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema_policy-206-23-001-bca-discount-rate-and-streamlined-approaches_april-24-2024.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"FEMA Policy 206-23-001 changes two things about how the agency decides whether a hazard mitigation project is cost effective, which is a minimum criterion for funding under the Stafford Act and the National Flood Insurance Act. First, it adopts the OMB Circular A-94 discount rate of 3.1 per cent for benefit-cost analyses of hazard mitigation projects, implementing the federal guidance in Appendix D of that circular. Second, it introduces streamlined cost-effectiveness determinations, allowing subapplicants to submit a cost-effectiveness narrative instead of a full benefit-cost analysis for projects costing less than $1 million. The policy states its principles as reducing complexity and promoting equity of grant programmes, especially for applicants proposing projects that benefit disadvantaged communities, promoting consistency in award decisions, simplifying requirements for Hazard Mitigation Assistance grants, and helping communities implement cost-effective projects that address future risk. FEMA says it will continue engaging with OMB to improve implementation in service of accelerating risk reduction and adaptation nationwide.","insights":["The discount rate is the whole lever: at 3.1 per cent rather than a higher rate, long-dated avoided losses retain far more present value, so projects whose benefits accrue over decades become cost effective that previously were not.","Allowing a narrative instead of a full benefit-cost analysis below $1 million removes a fixed analytical cost that falls hardest on small and under-resourced applicants, which the policy names as an equity measure rather than a simplification.","Cost-effectiveness is a minimum eligibility criterion under statute, so the discount rate is not guidance but a gate: it determines which mitigation projects can be funded at all.","The policy explicitly aims at projects that address future risk, which is the category a backward-looking benefit-cost method tends to reject."],"keywords":["benefit-cost analysis","discount rate","OMB Circular A-94","hazard mitigation assistance","cost effectiveness","FEMA policy","Stafford Act"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":609,"title":"State and Trends in Climate Adaptation Finance 2024","authors":"Global Center on Adaptation; Climate Policy Initiative","date":"2024-04-18","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Global Center on Adaptation","Climate Policy Initiative","International Monetary Fund"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://gca.org/reports/state-and-trends-in-climate-adaptation-finance-2024/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"State and Trends in Climate Adaptation Finance 2024, by the Global Center on Adaptation, an international organisation hosted by the Netherlands, with Climate Policy Initiative as analytical partner. It is structured as an assessment of the adaptation funding gap, then current trends in tracked adaptation finance flows, then an assessment of public and private finance institution adaptation commitments, then an analysis of the instruments deployed for adaptation finance, then challenges and recommendations, then the intersection of adaptation finance and humanitarian assistance. Two annexes cover building resilience to external shocks through the IMF's Resilience and Sustainability Trust, and the methodology behind the finance institution commitment assessment.","insights":["**Section 4 counts adaptation finance by INSTRUMENT rather than by source and sector**, which is the cut that says what the money is actually doing rather than where it came from.","Separating tracked flows from institution commitments is the right structure and a quiet indictment: a commitment is an announcement and a flow is a disbursement, and the report keeps them in different chapters rather than adding them.","The humanitarian assistance chapter is unusual for a finance-flows report: adaptation finance and disaster relief draw on overlapping money, and the report treats that overlap as part of the accounting rather than as an aside.","Annex 1 describes the IMF's Resilience and Sustainability Trust, which gives the report a named concessional sovereign instrument rather than only aggregate flows."],"keywords":["adaptation finance","funding gap","tracked flows","instrument analysis","Resilience and Sustainability Trust","finance institution commitments","humanitarian assistance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":195,"title":"When Climate Meets Real Estate: A Survey of the Literature","authors":"Justin Contat, Carrie Hopkins, Luis Mejia, Matthew Suandi","date":"2024-04-15","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Real Estate Economics","Federal Housing Finance Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/1540-6229.12489","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This article examines the intersection of climate change and the real estate market, outlining the significant risks posed by physical hazards like flooding and wildfires as well as transition risks related to decarbonization policies. The research highlights impacts on housing prices, mortgage performance, and community dynamics, particularly for disadvantaged groups, and calls for further investigation into the intricate relationship between climate risks and real estate.","insights":["Climate change significantly impacts real estate, with increased natural disaster frequency leading to adverse effects on property values and mortgage performance.","Vulnerable communities face heightened challenges in housing affordability and access due to climate risks, particularly in flood-prone areas.","Factors such as energy efficiency and decarbonization policies can influence mortgage performance, exacerbating affordability issues for disadvantaged populations.","Post-disaster scenarios illustrate shifts in demographics and homeownership challenges, particularly for low- and middle-income individuals who may be priced out.","Empirical studies reveal that flood risk discounts and perceptions of climate risks play crucial roles in shaping real estate pricing and demand."],"keywords":["housing market","disaster recovery","energy efficiency","migration","sustainability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":242,"title":"Guide for Adaptation and Resilience Finance","authors":"","date":"2024-04-11","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction","KPMG International","Standard Chartered Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.undrr.org/quick/84482","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Guide for Adaptation and Resilience Finance' emphasizes the urgent need for enhanced private sector investment in climate adaptation and resilience, particularly in emerging markets and developing economies, highlighting significant economic returns from adaptation initiatives and the necessity for clarity in investment definitions. It presents strategies and frameworks to encourage investments in critical areas such as infrastructure and healthcare while addressing barriers to funding and the need for collaboration between public and private sectors.","insights":["Investment in adaptation could yield returns of up to USD 12 for every dollar spent.","There is an annual financing gap of USD 194-366 billion for adaptation in developing countries.","Clear definitions of what constitutes adaptation investments are essential to attract private sector financing.","Public and philanthropic funding must be combined with private investments to effectively scale adaptation finance.","Nature-based solutions are crucial for enhancing resilience across various sectors and should be prioritized."],"keywords":["investment barriers","resilience strategies","public-private partnerships","natural resource conservation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":199,"title":"Infrastructure for a Climate\u2011Resilient Future","authors":"","date":"2024-04-09","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["OECD"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1787/a74a45b0-en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource 'Infrastructure for a Climate\u2011Resilient Future' highlights the urgent need for integrating climate resilience into infrastructure planning and financing to address the escalating impacts of climate change. It underscores the significance of nature-based solutions and public-private partnerships in building sustainable infrastructure while advocating for innovative financing mechanisms and policy reforms to unlock global investments, particularly in developing countries. The report emphasizes the necessity of collaborations, community engagement, and long-term planning strategies to achieve infrastructure that aligns with both sustainable development and climate adaptation goals.","insights":["An estimated annual investment of USD 6.9 trillion is needed by 2030 for infrastructure aligned with sustainable development and climate goals.","Nature-based solutions can enhance infrastructure resilience while providing additional environmental benefits, yet face challenges in regulatory frameworks and capacity building.","Delaying climate resilience measures can lead to substantial additional costs, estimated at USD 1 trillion over ten years in low- and middle-income countries.","Investment in climate-resilient infrastructure can significantly lower future repair costs and damages, with a potential return of USD 4 for every USD 1 invested in developing countries.","Collaboration between public and private sectors is crucial to mobilizing the necessary investments for climate-resilient infrastructure.","Effective stakeholder engagement throughout the infrastructure lifecycle is critical for aligning public interests and enhancing project success.","International cooperation and innovative financing tools are essential for addressing the unique challenges faced by developing countries regarding climate-resilient infrastructure."],"keywords":["sustainable development","public-private partnership","risk management","capacity building","innovation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":526,"title":"Three Ways to Equitably Advance Local Climate Action and Economic Development","authors":"Rebecca Marx","date":"2024-04-03","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Urban Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.urban.org/urban-wire/three-ways-equitably-advance-local-climate-action-and-economic-development","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Three ways cities can pursue local climate action and economic development together and equitably, drawn from a discussion with community organisations in Fresno and Rochester: align short and long term climate, economic development and equity goals inside planning documents; move from community engagement to community ownership of plans, property and infrastructure; and combine multiple funding streams. The framing is that Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act and Inflation Reduction Act money creates the opportunity, that work at the intersection of these goals is still nascent, and that equity has not been consistently operationalised in local climate action plans.","insights":["A forthcoming Urban Institute finding is quoted as the premise: equity has not been consistently evaluated or operationalised in local climate action plans, and community and economic development plans have not consistently integrated climate action, so the two planning tracks are running separately.","Community ownership rather than consultation is the sharpest recommendation: community-based organisations and cooperatives owning planning processes and local assets, including climate resilient and perpetually affordable housing, energy systems and open space, so benefits accrue rather than merely reach.","The barrier named for federal money is capacity, not eligibility: tracking, applying for and meeting reporting requirements for federal funds takes capacity many local organisations lack, so the influx does not reach them.","One practical workaround offered is to seek funding with flexible uses, grants without an explicit climate or equitable development purpose whose parameters still allow climate activities, which is a route around programme-specific capacity requirements.","Vertical integration across state, county and jurisdictional planning bodies is presented as both an equity mechanism and a funding mechanism, because it governs whether money earmarked for states or counties is distributed equitably across jurisdictions."],"keywords":["local climate action planning","community ownership","Inflation Reduction Act","Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act","equitable development"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":145,"title":"Adaptation & Resilience Impact: A Measurement Framework for Investors","authors":"","date":"2024-04-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Environment Programme","United Nations Environment Programme - Finance Initiative (UNEP FI)","Adaptation and Resilience Investors Collaborative","Cadlas"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://www.unepfi.org/themes/climate-change/adaptation-resilience-impact-a-measurement-framework-for-investors/","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This April 2024 guidance, produced by the Adaptation and Resilience Investors Collaborative (ARIC) with UNEP FI support, presents a practical measurement framework for private investors to assess and report adaptation and resilience (A&R) impact. It applies the Impact Management Project's Five Dimensions of Impact (what, who, how much, contribution, risk) to A&R investment, proposes aggregable output- and outcome-level metrics across three impact aspects (people, planet, economy), and embeds impact management into the full investment cycle. The framework targets the emerging multi-trillion-dollar private climate adaptation market and aligns with GIIN IRIS+, GIZ, and Climate Bonds Initiative approaches.","insights":["The World Economic Forum (2024) identified extreme weather as the most significant global risk over the next ten years, underpinning growing private investment in adaptation and resilience, projected to become a multi-trillion-dollar market (WEF, 2022).","The framework distinguishes two investment types with distinct impact pathways: 'enabling' investments (which make others more climate-resilient, e.g. distributing flood warning technology) and 'adapted' investments (which reduce the physical climate risk of the investee itself, e.g. hardening a port against sea level rise), with many investments exhibiting both characteristics.","Three dimensions of resilience capacity, absorptive, adaptive, and transformative, map to increasing intensity of structural change, from parametric insurance to wholesale shifts in agricultural systems, giving investors a clear typology for portfolio differentiation.","Metrics are organized across people (e.g., number of people supported to be more climate resilient), planet (e.g., hectares of habitat under climate-resilient management), and economy (e.g., USD value of assets made more climate resilient), with economy-based value metrics being the most readily aggregable across portfolios.","The framework highlights maladaptation risk, unintended increases in climate vulnerability, as a formal dimension of impact risk requiring due diligence; examples include riverside flood defences that shift flood risk to downstream communities and solar-powered irrigation pumps that deplete groundwater for others.","Counterfactual analysis (with-vs-without investment comparison) is prescribed as the basis for contribution assessment; a worked port infrastructure example shows how comparing 10 vs. 5 flood events per year quantifies the value of a coastal drainage upgrade over a 30-year horizon."],"keywords":["adaptation impact metrics","private investment framework","impact management","maladaptation risk","development finance institutions","five dimensions of impact","climate resilience taxonomy"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":483,"title":"Supervisors Allocate $500,000 to Tackle Sea Level Rise","authors":"","date":"2024-03-20","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["County of Marin"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.marincounty.gov/news-releases/supervisors-allocate-500000-tackle-sea-level-rise","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"On 19 March 2024 the Marin County Board of Supervisors committed $500,000 to a project that will recommend a unified approach and a countywide governance structure for responding to sea level rise, addressing how Marin's city, town, county, utility and transportation agencies might work together. Marin is surrounded on three sides by water and is among the San Francisco Bay Area counties most vulnerable to sea level rise, with over 70 miles of ocean coastline and 40 miles of bay shoreline, much of it low lying and exposed to tidal, creek and storm flooding; neighborhoods with a higher percentage of people of color are disproportionately affected. Assistant County Executive Ariel Espiritu Santo frames a multi-jurisdictional governance model as a way to address disparities and close race-equity gaps while handling long-term planning. The county's Community Development Agency already runs two longstanding programmes, C-SMART for oceanfront communities and BayWAVE for bayfront neighborhoods, plus the Stinson Beach Adaptation and Resilience Collaboration. The 2023 Marin County Community Survey found 77 per cent of respondents said addressing sea level rise was essential or very important, 76 per cent said the same of climate change generally and 90 per cent of improved disaster preparedness. Supervisor Katie Rice notes that significant federal funding is available and that the county is committed to working with towns, city councils, community leaders and special districts to access it collectively. AECOM Technical Services will be contracted to guide the project, funded from 2023-24 fiscal year General Fund balance set aside for climate resiliency.","insights":["The $500,000 buys governance rather than infrastructure, and the stated reason is access to federal money: a fragmented set of cities, towns, utilities and special districts cannot apply collectively, so the county is paying to become an eligible applicant at scale.","Supervisor Rice's framing makes the sequence explicit, years of vulnerability data first and a governance structure second, which places institutional design after assessment and before capital in the local adaptation pipeline.","The money comes from General Fund balance set aside for climate resiliency rather than from a grant or a bond, so this is discretionary local revenue funding the preparatory work that grants will not cover.","Two existing programmes, C-SMART and BayWAVE, already split the county by oceanfront and bayfront, which is the fragmentation the new structure is meant to resolve and a reminder that hazard-specific programmes accrete their own boundaries."],"keywords":["sea level rise governance","Marin County","BayWAVE","C-SMART","multi-jurisdictional","General Fund","federal match readiness"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":249,"title":"Resilience Rating System: A Methodology for Building and Tracking Resilience to Climate Change - Synthesizing Key Lessons from IDA19 Piloting","authors":"","date":"2024-03-19","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/41225","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Resilience Rating System (RRS), developed by the World Bank Group, is a comprehensive methodology designed to evaluate and enhance the resilience of investment projects against climate risks. This system, which rates projects from C to A+ based on their design and expected outcomes, promotes early integration of climate considerations and supports effective decision-making. Piloted across various sectors, it has shown potential for broader impacts, emphasizing the need for sector-specific guidance and robust data to improve project resilience outcomes.","insights":["The RRS distinguishes between resilience of project design and resilience through project outcomes.","Successful application of the RRS relies on early integration of climate considerations in project design.","Robust data and tools are essential for project decision-making related to climate risks.","Collaboration with climate specialists enhances the integration of resilience measures into projects.","The simplicity of the RRS rating system aids decision-makers without deep technical expertise.","Flexibility in the application of RRS allows for adjustments to meet unique project objectives.","The methodology has evolved to include flexibility in rating projects that primarily operate within one resilience dimension."],"keywords":["evaluation","methodology","framework","investment strategy","performance tracking"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":443,"title":"NAIC Adopts First National Climate Resilience Strategy for Insurance to Close Coverage Gaps and Improve Recovery from Natural Disasters","authors":"","date":"2024-03-18","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Association of Insurance Commissioners","California Department of Insurance"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://content.naic.org/article/naic-adopts-first-national-climate-resilience-strategy-insurance-close-coverage-gaps-and-improve","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The National Association of Insurance Commissioners adopted its first National Climate Resilience Strategy for Insurance, developed by the NAIC's Climate and Resiliency Task Force co-chaired by Alaska's insurance director Lori K. Wing-Heier and California Insurance Commissioner Ricardo Lara. The strategy's stated goal is faster and more effective risk reduction by state insurance regulators so that insurance remains available and reliable for communities facing climate risk. Its action plan has four strands: advocating home hardening against wildfire, flood and storm; using catastrophe modelling information; better informing the public of risks; and updating solvency tools to incorporate further analysis of climate risk. The linchpin named is the states' Property and Casualty Market Intelligence data call, which collects and analyses data covering more than 80 per cent of the US property insurance market by premium volume. The release stresses that the strategy is nonpartisan and drew on regulators from large and small, coastal and inland, urban and rural jurisdictions, including island jurisdictions.","insights":["The instrument is a regulator-led data call rather than a rule: the Property and Casualty Market Intelligence collection covering over 80 per cent of the market by premium is what gives state regulators a shared evidence base on availability and pricing.","Home hardening appears as something insurance regulators advocate, which puts a building-standard intervention inside the remit of the bodies that supervise solvency and rates.","Solvency tools are explicitly in scope for updating to incorporate climate analysis, so the strategy reaches capital adequacy and not only consumer protection.","It is the first national strategy of its kind from the NAIC and was reached across a deliberately heterogeneous membership, which is what makes it a coordinating document rather than one state's position."],"keywords":["NAIC","climate resilience strategy","property insurance","PCMI data call","home hardening","catastrophe modeling","solvency","insurance regulation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":625,"title":"Building for Resilience \u2013 My Safe Florida Home Program Offers Solutions","authors":"Citizens Property Insurance Corporation","date":"2024-03-05","year":2024,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Citizens Property Insurance Corporation","Florida Department of Financial Services"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.citizensfla.com/-/building-for-resilience-my-safe-florida-home-program-offers-solutions","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article of 5 March 2024 in the Citizens Property Insurance policyholder newsletter on the My Safe Florida Home program. It opens on Hurricane Ian in southwest Florida in September 2022, where engineers conducting preliminary assessments noted a bright red line separating the vulnerability of buildings constructed before and after Florida's modern building codes, and sets that alongside lessons from Charley in 2004 and Michael in 2018. My Safe Florida Home, administered by the Florida Department of Financial Services, offers free wind mitigation inspections to eligible homeowners statewide, identifying wind resistance features and recommending strengthening upgrades. Qualifying homeowners in windborne debris regions along the coast may also receive up to $10,000 in grants, when available, toward upgrades or retrofits, though the article notes the program was not accepting new grant applications at the time of writing. Retrofits focus on two areas: protecting openings, meaning windows, doors, garage doors, skylights and gable end vents, through hurricane shutters or impact-resistant windows and garage doors; and reinforcing the roof through additional metal connectors installed during replacement, increased roof deck nailing, a secondary water barrier, and hurricane clips and straps. The article states that annual insurance discounts and credits over time help offset the cost of the upgrades, and that a strengthened home is a more attractive and insurable risk.","insights":["**The whole loop is stated in three sentences by the insurer itself**: a state grant of up to $10,000 pays for the retrofit, the retrofit earns annual insurance discounts, and the discounts offset the cost. It is a live consumer programme rather than a proposed structure.","The bright red line from Hurricane Ian is natural-experiment evidence rather than modelling: the damage boundary tracked the building standard.","**The programme was not accepting new grant applications**, which is the constraint worth recording: the instrument works and the appropriation is exhausted, so the binding limit is annual state funding rather than demand or efficacy.","Naming the specific retrofits, roof deck nailing, secondary water barrier, hurricane clips and straps, gives the intervention vocabulary that an insurance discount is actually written against, which is what makes a premium credit verifiable.","That a strengthened home is a more attractive and insurable risk is the underwriting consequence the insurer draws: the programme's own logic is that premiums fall because physical risk fell."],"keywords":["My Safe Florida Home","wind mitigation inspection","insurance discount","retrofit grant","building code","hurricane clips","roof deck nailing","opening protection","Hurricane Ian"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":81,"title":"Municipal Finance Shapes Urban Climate Action and Justice","authors":"Claudia V. Diezmart\u00ednez, Anne G. Short Gianotti","date":"2024-02-16","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Boston University"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-024-01924-4","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed article in Nature Climate Change (March 2024) uses 34 expert interviews with US municipal officials, consultants, and climate finance professionals to examine how urban climate finance tools (public revenues, grants, municipal bonds, loans, and public-private partnerships), shape the implementation of climate action and justice in US cities. The study demonstrates that financial logics tied to creditworthiness, debt affordability, and real estate markets systematically bias cities toward mitigation over adaptation, favor wealthier neighborhoods, and can entrench climate vulnerability and gentrification. It also identifies emerging innovations, such as Denver's Climate Protection Fund and Portland's Clean Energy Community Benefits Fund, that embed justice into climate budgeting.","insights":["Adaptation is systematically disadvantaged in municipal finance: city officials and experts describe it as 'not bankable' or lacking a 'clear business model,' because its benefits appear as future avoided costs rather than present-value revenues, pushing cities toward mitigation over resilience.","Credit rating agency relationships constrain climate investment: cities are incentivized to protect creditworthiness by starving climate and maintenance budgets, one city official reported achieving AAA credit rating 'at the expense of maintaining parks and playgrounds.'","Smaller and under-resourced municipalities face disproportionate barriers to accessing federal climate grants due to administrative complexity, staff capacity shortfalls, and reimbursement-based structures that require upfront spending, creating unequal geographies of climate finance access.","Financial logics incentivize carbon-intensive and flood/wildfire-prone real estate development to protect property tax bases, with respondents noting cities see climate adaptation and insurance reforms as threatening 'the fundamental growth model of a region.'","Temporal mismatches between fiscal cycles and climate needs are a documented constraint: federal COVID-19 assistance grants used for climate action must be spent by December 2026, limiting long-term programme design and community engagement.","Funders (including Bloomberg Philanthropies, the Barr Foundation, and Justice40), are reshaping municipal climate finance by embedding justice requirements into grant programmes, though respondents warn this also shifts decision-making power away from city governments."],"keywords":["municipal bond market","climate justice","urban fiscal policy","adaptation finance barriers","debt financing","just urban transitions","environmental justice funding","credit rating agencies"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":502,"title":"Climate Risk in Mortgage Markets: Evidence from Hurricanes Harvey and Irma","authors":"Pedro Gete, Athena Tsouderou, Susan M. Wachter","date":"2024-02-12","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Miami Climate Resilience Institute","Wharton School","Fannie Mae","Freddie Mac"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/1540-6229.12477","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Gete, Tsouderou and Wachter use the Credit Risk Transfers issued by Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac to ask how mortgage markets would price hurricane risk in the absence of government intervention. Today that risk is priced equally across locations even though it is location-specific. The authors hand-collect a detailed database to exploit the heterogeneous exposure of CRTs to Hurricanes Harvey and Irma and estimate private investors' reaction to hurricane risk with a difference-in-differences specification. They use those estimates to calibrate a model of mortgage lending, simulate hurricane frequencies and mortgage default probabilities in every US county, and derive the market price of mortgage credit risk as implied guarantee fees. The central result is that market-implied guarantee fees in the counties most exposed to hurricanes would be 70 per cent higher than in inland counties.","insights":["The 70 per cent figure is the size of the cross-subsidy: it is what hurricane-exposed counties would pay if the guarantee fee were risk-based, and the gap between that and the uniform fee is what inland borrowers currently fund.","Credit Risk Transfers are the instrument that makes the question answerable, because they are the one place where private capital actually prices agency mortgage credit risk and so reveal a market view the guarantee fee hides.","A uniform national guarantee fee is a resilience policy by omission: it removes the price signal that would otherwise make a borrower or a lender pay attention to hurricane exposure, and therefore removes the incentive to mitigate.","The method generalises: any risk with a CRT exposure and a discrete event can be priced this way, which makes this a template for measuring other climate cross-subsidies inside the agency system."],"keywords":["guarantee fees","Credit Risk Transfer","Fannie Mae","Freddie Mac","hurricane risk pricing","cross-subsidy","mortgage credit risk"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":176,"title":"How Climate Risks Are Shaping Municipal Funding","authors":"Martin Feinstein","date":"2024-02-09","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["DebtBook"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.debtbook.com/blog/how-climate-risks-are-shaping-municipal-funding","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt discusses how climate risks are influencing municipal funding and financing strategies. It highlights the financial burden of climate change on municipalities, including increased insurance costs and reduced revenues during disaster recovery. The importance of municipal bonds and ESG principles for building resilient infrastructure is emphasized, along with federal responses to climate-related disasters and the necessary disclosure practices for municipalities dealing with climate risks.","insights":["Climate change costs the U.S. $150 billion annually due to increasing weather-related disasters.","The financial market often underestimates climate risks, viewing them as long-term issues.","Strategic financing plans, including municipal bonds, are vital for building climate-resilient infrastructure.","High-risk states experience challenges in securing insurance, leading to higher premiums and reduced coverage.","The Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act allocates significant funding for mitigating climate risks.","Municipal ESG bonds are emerging as a means to finance environmentally sustainable projects.","Climate-related disasters can severely strain municipal budgets, impacting their ability to recover.","Disclosure of climate risks is becoming increasingly crucial for municipal issuers to inform potential investors.","Rating agencies will adjust their criteria to account for climate risks in evaluating municipal credit.","The growing frequency of climate events necessitates innovative funding solutions to support resilient infrastructure."],"keywords":["municipal bonds","infrastructure","disaster recovery","insurance costs","federal funding"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":344,"title":"Launch of Green Guarantee Company to Mobilise Billions in Climate Finance","authors":"","date":"2024-02-04","year":2024,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Africa","Asia","Latin America"],"institutions":["Green Climate Fund","Fitch Ratings"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.greenclimate.fund/about/news-and-updates/launch-green-guarantee-company-mobilise-billions-climate-finance","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Green Guarantee Company (GGC), the world's first climate-focused guarantee company, launched operations on 2 February 2024 with a Market Closing Ceremony at the London Stock Exchange. Backed by ~USD 100 million from GCF, FCDO (via MOBILIST), NSIA, USAID (Prosper Africa), and Norfund, GGC will provide up to USD 1 billion in investment-grade (BBB/Stable, Fitch) guarantees for green bonds listed on the LSE and private credit instruments issued by borrowers in developing countries across Africa, Asia, and Latin America, targeting USD 5 billion in guarantee capacity by 2035.","insights":["GGC leverages USD 100 million in initial capital to provide up to USD 1 billion of guarantees, a 10:1 mobilization ratio, with a long-term target of USD 5 billion or more in guarantee capacity by 2035.","GGC holds a BBB/Stable investment-grade rating from Fitch Ratings, enabling institutional investors (pension funds, insurers) bound by investment-grade mandates to access climate debt from developing countries for the first time through standard capital market channels.","Credit enhancement via guarantees is the core mechanism: by lifting borrower credit ratings above investment grade, GGC reduces the cost of long-term hard-currency debt for green infrastructure, renewable energy, and clean transportation projects in ODA-eligible countries.","Priority geographies include India, Indonesia, Brazil, Bangladesh, Philippines, Egypt, Vietnam, and Kenya, countries facing both acute climate vulnerability and sovereign rating pressure post-pandemic that constrains private-sector capital market access.","GGC plans capacity-building support for issuers on climate impact reporting, aiming to establish green bonds and loans from developing countries as a credible, consistently-reported asset class attractive to global institutional portfolio allocators.","The capital structure blends public development finance (GCF, FCDO/MOBILIST, USAID, Norfund) with a sovereign wealth fund co-investor (Nigeria Sovereign Investment Authority), demonstrating a South-South as well as North-South funding architecture.","GGC intends to expand beyond the LSE to other major exchanges and to raise additional private-sector capital as it scales, signaling a replicable platform model rather than a one-off transaction."],"keywords":["credit guarantee","green bonds","developing countries","blended finance mobilization","investment grade","private capital mobilization","ODA-eligible markets"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":120,"title":"Quantifying the Impact of Climate Change on Human Health","authors":"","date":"2024-01-15","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Economic Forum","Oliver Wyman"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.weforum.org/publications/quantifying-the-impact-of-climate-change-on-human-health/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report assesses the profound health impacts and economic ramifications of climate change, projecting an additional 14.5 million deaths and $12.5 trillion in losses by 2050, primarily affecting vulnerable populations. It highlights the urgent need for resilient healthcare systems and comprehensive strategies to prepare for climate-related health crises, exacerbated by extreme weather events such as floods, droughts, and heatwaves, which pose significant risks to public health and equity.","insights":["Climate change is projected to cause 14.5 million additional deaths and $12.5 trillion in economic losses by 2050.","Floods are expected to cause the highest acute mortality risk, leading to an estimated 8.5 million deaths.","Vulnerable populations, particularly in Africa and southern Asia, will bear the brunt of climate-related health impacts.","Heat waves and extreme weather events will significantly increase healthcare costs, with heat-related diseases costing an estimated $7.1 trillion globally.","Psychological impacts such as anxiety and PTSD are common in populations affected by climate-related disasters, indicating a need for mental health support.","Innovative healthcare solutions and governmental policies are necessary to build climate-resilient healthcare systems."],"keywords":["public health","economic impact","mortality","health inequity","risk assessment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":629,"title":"It's Not Easy Being Anti-Greenwashing","authors":"Caroline Bradley","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","Europe","Global"],"institutions":["University of Miami","Securities and Exchange Commission"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4784535","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A 2024 law review article by Caroline Bradley of the University of Miami School of Law, focused on greenwashing as a financial rather than a consumer-protection issue. The argument is that preventing greenwashing requires defining what is and is not green, and that no international agreement on that definition exists, nor in many cases any domestic one. Regulators including the SEC are working on climate-related disclosure rules, but those address the definitional problem only indirectly. The article compares the US and EU approaches and finds them substantially different, most sharply on materiality: the EU's double materiality covers both the impact of climate change on a business and the impact of the business on the climate, while the US focuses on simple materiality, the impact of climate change on the business. EU rules nonetheless reach some US-based enterprises. The two jurisdictions also differ in how politically contested the core concepts of sustainability and of the regulatory state are. The deeper claim is that greenness and sustainability have no inherent meaning, being a function of political processes and of actors seeking to influence how policymakers frame climate change and the role of law, so if regulatory concepts of green are inadequate then enforcement against greenwashing will be inadequate too, and the resulting confusion may itself discourage firms from mitigation. The article extends the charge to governments, which fail to disclose their own climate risks, issue green bonds with no binding commitment to use proceeds for sustainability, subsidise fossil fuels and present inadequate policies as promoting sustainability.","insights":["**The article extends the greenwashing charge to governments**, which issue green bonds carrying no binding commitment to use the proceeds for sustainability, fail to disclose their own climate risks and subsidise fossil fuels while presenting inadequate policy as sustainable.","Single against double materiality is the sharpest difference the article draws: the EU covers both the impact of climate change on a business and of the business on the climate, while the US covers only the first, so a US filing and an EU filing are not answering the same question.","**Definitional emptiness is the load-bearing argument**: greenness and sustainability have no inherent meaning and are a function of political processes, so if the regulatory concept of green is inadequate then enforcement against greenwashing must be inadequate too.","The article is written from a law faculty and treats greenwashing as a financial-regulation problem rather than a consumer-protection one, which is why its remedies are disclosure rules rather than advertising standards."],"keywords":["greenwashing","double materiality","green bond","SEC climate disclosure","sustainability taxonomy","use of proceeds","financial regulation"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":562,"title":"Economics of Drought: Investing in Nature-Based Solutions for Drought Resilience","authors":"Richard Thomas; Jonathan Davies; Caroline King; Johannes Kruse; Mark Schauer; Nina Bisom; Daniel Tsegai; Kaveh Madani","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global","Africa"],"institutions":["United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification","United Nations University","Deutsche Gesellschaft fur Internationale Zusammenarbeit","International Rice Research Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.unccd.int/resources/publications/economics-drought-investing-nature-based-solutions-drought-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A 2024 report published by the UN Convention to Combat Desertification with the Economics of Land Degradation Initiative and the UN University Institute for Water, Environment and Health, subtitled Proaction Pays, on the economics of investing in nature-based solutions for drought resilience. Eight lead authors across WREM, UNU-INWEH, the ELD Initiative and GIZ, and UNCCD. Drought is the subject throughout: the term appears 263 times across the 291,041-character extraction against 67 for resilience and 11 for adaptation. The argument the subtitle states is that acting before a drought costs less than responding to one, and the report assembles the economic case for nature-based rather than engineered responses.","insights":["Proaction pays is the report's own subtitle and its central claim: acting before a drought costs less than responding to one, argued for drought specifically rather than for hazards in general.","The remedy the report costs is nature-based solutions and sustainable land management, so the intervention is land and water management rather than built infrastructure.","Financing nature-based solutions has a chapter of its own, separate from the chapter making the benefits case, so the report treats the money as a distinct problem from the economics."],"keywords":["drought","nature-based solutions","land degradation","economics of drought","proaction","UNCCD","water security","desertification"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":550,"title":"Innovative Climate and Disaster Risk Finance Solutions: Resilience Building and Fiscal Strengthening","authors":"","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Latin America","Caribbean"],"institutions":["Inter-American Development Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://publications.iadb.org/en/innovative-climate-and-disaster-risk-finance-solutions-resilience-building-and-fiscal-strengthening","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Inter-American Development Bank's guide to its Climate and Disaster Risk Finance products for borrowing member countries, aimed at strengthening resilience and fiscal sustainability. Three instruments are set out in turn, each with what it is, its value proposition, its terms and conditions and its current portfolio: the Contingent Credit Facility, the Climate Resilient Debt Clause, and risk transfer products. The case for them is fiscal: natural disasters in Latin America and the Caribbean have affected around 7 million people a year on average since 1980 and caused average annual economic losses of US$9.9 billion in real terms, on a clear upward trend driven by tropical cyclones, floods, droughts and wildfires.","insights":["THE CLIMATE RESILIENT DEBT CLAUSE IS DOCUMENTED HERE WITH TERMS AND A PORTFOLIO, which is a lender's own specification of the instrument rather than a third-party description of it.","The Contingent Credit Facility is pre-arranged liquidity rather than post-disaster lending: the value proposition is that the money is committed before the event, which changes a sovereign's fiscal position at the moment of the shock rather than months afterwards.","All three instruments are presented as fiscal management rather than as project finance, which is the distinction that separates disaster risk finance from adaptation investment: they protect the balance sheet rather than reduce the hazard.","The loss baseline is specific and long: around 7 million people affected a year on average since 1980 and US$9.9 billion in average annual economic losses in real terms, calculated by the IDB from EM-DAT with inflation adjustment.","The guide explicitly frames the products as mobilising public AND private resources for disaster risk management, so the contingent and risk-transfer instruments are positioned as catalytic rather than purely sovereign.","Integrated Finance Solutions appears as a closing section, so the bank expects the three instruments to be combined rather than chosen between, which is a layered risk financing design."],"keywords":["contingent credit facility","climate resilient debt clause","risk transfer products","disaster risk finance","fiscal resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":459,"title":"New York City Climate Budgeting: The City of New York Executive Budget, Fiscal Year 2025","authors":"","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["New York City Mayor's Office of Management and Budget"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nyc.gov/assets/omb/downloads/pdf/exec24-nyccb.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The City of New York's first Climate Budgeting publication, issued with the Fiscal Year 2025 Executive Budget under Mayor Eric Adams and Budget Director Jacques Jiha. The document sets out the city's climate budgeting process and then applies it, which is what distinguishes it from the announcement. It explains the Office of Management and Budget's role in the city's climate challenge and the climate science, health and environmental justice context, describes the process itself, and then reports investments and findings: sustainability investment highlights, resiliency investment highlights, and an assessment of the climate alignment of city capital investments. That assessment carries its own stated methodology and is run twice, once for alignment of the FY2024 to FY2028 Capital Commitment Plan with net-zero emissions and once for alignment with climate resiliency, with additional analysis of co-benefits across air quality, circular economy, ecology and sustainable living. It closes with the city's anticipated progress on citywide and city government greenhouse gas emissions.","insights":["Assessing the capital commitment plan for resiliency alignment separately from net-zero alignment is the methodologically important choice: a single climate score would let emissions performance mask adaptation gaps, and running two assessments makes the resilience shortfall visible.","The published methodology for assessing climate alignment is the transferable artefact, since any city adopting climate budgeting needs a defensible rule for classifying a capital project.","Applying the assessment to a five-year capital commitment plan rather than a single annual budget matches the horizon of infrastructure decisions, which is where climate alignment actually binds.","Co-benefit analysis across air quality, circular economy, ecology and sustainable living is how the document accounts for value that neither emissions nor hazard reduction captures.","This is the underlying publication behind the city's climate budgeting announcement, so it is the primary source for the process and the announcement is the secondary one."],"keywords":["climate budgeting","capital commitment plan","climate alignment methodology","resiliency investment","New York City","co-benefits","municipal budget"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":428,"title":"Resilient Maine: Local Adaptation and Resilience Actions at a Coastwide Scale","authors":"","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","Maine"],"institutions":["National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://coast.noaa.gov/states/stories/maine.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A NOAA Office for Coastal Management project summary for a $69,008,683 award to the Maine Governor's Office of Policy Innovation and the Future under the Climate Resilience Regional Challenge, funded from the roughly $575 million IRA appropriation of 2022. The stated ambition is for Maine to become a national leader in climate resilience among rural states by the end of the decade, supporting the goals of the state climate action plan Maine Won't Wait. Three strands: building enduring community resiliency, which expands the Community Resilience Partnership already reaching 225 communities and funds 20 to 30 subgrants for risk assessment and implementation; investing in nature-based solutions, including living shorelines, changes to state land-use and permitting regulation, and shore corps stewards trained to help communities implement adaptation; and strengthening working waterfronts along 3,500 miles of tidal coastline, where commercial fishing and aquaculture access is treated as economic resilience. The case for urgency is stated concretely: storms in December 2023 and January 2024 caused $90 million in damage to public infrastructure and four deaths.","insights":["Capacity, expertise and funding are named together as the reasons municipalities cannot act, and the programme is structured to supply all three rather than money alone, with nearly 500 municipalities and tribal governments in scope.","Regulatory change is funded as a project component: updating land-use and permitting rules is treated as adaptation infrastructure alongside the physical work.","Working waterfronts are framed as economic resilience rather than as coastal protection, with fishing and aquaculture access, aggregation of facilities and energy efficiency conversions all inside the resilience award.","The damage figure cited as the trigger, $90 million to public infrastructure from two storms in a single winter, is public-asset loss, which is the balance sheet a state can act on directly."],"keywords":["Maine Won't Wait","Community Resilience Partnership","living shorelines","working waterfronts","rural resilience","Climate Resilience Regional Challenge","subgrants","coastal adaptation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":405,"title":"How a Holistic Risk Approach Increases Resilience in the Face of a Changing Climate","authors":"Lara Whitmore, Sandra Hansen, Katy Reyner, Swenja Surminski","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Marsh","Guy Carpenter","The Nature Conservancy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.marsh.com/content/dam/mmc-web/insights/publications/2024/november/adapting-to-climate-challenges-report.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Marsh McLennan white paper argues for a holistic risk management approach that integrates risk assessment, risk financing, and climate adaptation to close growing protection gaps. Drawing on Guy Carpenter reinsurance renewal surveys (January 2024) and Marsh's corporate adaptation survey, it finds that fewer than half of global respondents quantify their climate risk, and that the U.S. protection gap exceeds 40%. The paper highlights catastrophe model applications, parametric insurance, community-based catastrophe insurance (CBCI) pilots in New York City, and return-on-investment frameworks for adaptation interventions as practical tools for building resilience.","insights":["Fewer than half of global (re)insurance clients quantify their climate risk, per Guy Carpenter's post-January 2024 reinsurance renewal survey; 48% of corporate risk managers assess climate risks only qualitatively (Marsh Corporate Adaptation Survey).","The U.S. insurance protection gap exceeds 40%, while UAE insurance penetration (all lines) is only 2.75%, illustrating severe underinsurance in both developed and developing markets.","Global average temperature from July 2023 to June 2024 reached a record 1.64\u00b0C above pre-industrial levels, with named storms (Beryl, Helene, Milton) and Italian hailstorms and floods in Brazil, UAE, and Spain driving insured loss increases.","Parametric insurance is identified as a versatile complement to traditional coverage, capable of extending protection to high-risk assets and supply chains and containing uninsurability risk where conventional underwriters are withdrawing.","Marsh McLennan's community-based catastrophe insurance (CBCI) pilot in New York City uses parametric triggers and group purchasing by local government or community organizations to reduce administrative and distribution costs, expanding flood coverage to lower-income residents.","Catastrophe models can quantify resilience interventions, such as building code improvements and flood defenses, by modifying vulnerability modules, enabling premium-saving estimates to be set against implementation costs and supporting ROI-based adaptation decisions.","Nature-based solutions such as mangroves remain difficult to incorporate into catastrophe models; the paper identifies this as an emerging area requiring industry-academia collaboration, citing The Nature Conservancy and Guy Carpenter's 2021 wildfire NbS quantification report."],"keywords":["protection gap","catastrophe modeling","parametric insurance","total cost of risk","community-based catastrophe insurance","reinsurance renewals","nature-based solutions modeling"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":95,"title":"The Changing Wealth of Nations: Adding Water to the Changing Wealth of Nations","authors":"Michael Vardon","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","Australian National University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099101024134018049/p1784461014b410e01a6f417a945498a467","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt introduces a technical report that adds a focus on water resources to the understanding of wealth dynamics in nations, emphasizing the importance of integrating water management into broader economic and environmental assessments.","insights":["Water resources are critical to national wealth and economic stability.","Integrating water management into economic assessments can lead to more accurate evaluations of wealth.","Climate change impacts water availability, affecting both economies and ecosystems.","Sustainable water management is essential for long-term resilience and prosperity.","Countries must consider water resources when developing climate policies.","Investing in water infrastructure can yield significant economic benefits.","The relationship between water and wealth should be reflected in national accounts.","Water scarcity poses a risk to food security and public health.","There is a growing need for collaborative governance in water management."],"keywords":["water resources","economic assessments","sustainable management","national wealth","infrastructure investment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":94,"title":"The Changing Wealth of Nations: Estimating the Global Contribution of Aquaculture Towards the Changing Wealth of Nations","authors":"Malcolm W. Dickson, Harrison Charo-Karisa, Stefanie Onder, Robert Smith","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","International Institute for Sustainable Development","American University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099101124090531093/P17844612699680971b94c1fc59abc31679","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt discusses a technical report titled 'The Changing Wealth of Nations', which focuses on assessing the global economic impact of aquaculture and its contribution to national wealth, underscoring its significance in the context of changing environmental and economic conditions.","insights":["Aquaculture plays a critical role in global food security.","The report emphasizes the economic value generated by aquaculture.","It highlights the need for sustainable practices in aquaculture.","The assessment of aquaculture's contribution is crucial for policymaking.","Changing wealth dynamics require reevaluation of natural resource contributions.","Aquaculture can mitigate the impacts of climate change on fisheries.","Economic assessments must consider environmental sustainability.","The report aims to inform stakeholders about the benefits of aquaculture."],"keywords":["aquaculture","economic impact","sustainability","food security","natural resources"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":91,"title":"The Changing Wealth of Nations: Global Assessment of the Economic Value of Non-Wood Forest Ecosystem Services","authors":"Juha Siikam\u00e4ki, Mat\u00edas Piaggio, Natalia da Silva, Ignacio \u00c1lvarez, Ziyan Chu","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","International Union for the Conservation of Nature","Universidad de la Rep\u00fablica"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099101124145545368/p17844610f0e240911a4ad138b4c4c8780f","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource provides a comprehensive analysis of the economic value of non-wood forest ecosystem services, utilizing advanced statistical methods, including machine learning and meta-regression, to derive updated local and global estimations. It underscores the critical role these services play in global natural capital wealth, with a focus on biodiversity, recreation, and sustainable development. The findings advocate for informed decision-making regarding conservation, resource management, and policy development, emphasizing the need for localized studies to enhance valuation accuracy in underrepresented regions.","insights":["Non-wood forest ecosystem services account for approximately 29% of the total global wealth from renewable natural resources.","The average marginal value per hectare for non-wood forest products is estimated at US$6.8, while recreation services reach up to US$48 per year.","Machine learning models, including Bayesian Additive Regression Trees, enhance the accuracy in predicting ecosystem service values compared to traditional regression models.","Protected areas contribute significantly to the economic value of non-wood forest services, accounting for about 16% of the total value associated with recreation, hunting, and water services.","The geographic coverage of value estimates is uneven, with notable gaps in developing regions, stressing the importance of localized studies for better conservation and resource management."],"keywords":["ecosystem services","sustainability","biodiversity","economic valuation","forest management"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":89,"title":"The Changing Wealth of Nations: Building Coastal Resilience with Mangroves: The Contribution of Natural Flood Defenses to the Changing Wealth of Nations","authors":"Pelayo Men\u00e9ndez, Michael W. Beck, Sheila Abad, I\u00f1igo J. Losada","date":"2024-01-01","year":2024,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","University of California Santa Cruz","Instituto de Hidr\u00e1ulica Ambiental-IH Cantabria"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/changing-wealth-of-nations/publication/the-changing-wealth-of-nations-technical-reports","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report 'Building Coastal Resilience with Mangroves' evaluates the vital role of mangroves as natural flood defenses from 1995 to 2020, revealing their increasing economic value in protecting coastal communities against rising flood risks attributed to climate change. It illustrates the significance of integrating high-resolution data and socioeconomic factors in understanding flood risks and emphasizes the urgent need for policies that support the conservation and restoration of mangrove ecosystems.","insights":["Mangroves provide essential coastal protection services, significantly mitigating flooding and erosion during storms.","The flood reduction benefits from mangroves increased to a present value of $855 billion by 2020, despite global losses in mangrove cover.","Socioeconomic growth often leads to heightened flood risks, particularly affecting areas previously safeguarded by mangroves.","Data updates indicate a growing number of countries benefiting from mangrove ecosystems, with a rise from 92 to 97 nations.","Mangroves enhance local biodiversity and ecosystem services while promoting sustainable livelihoods for coastal communities.","Investing in mangrove restoration is crucial for enhancing resilience to climate variability and reducing vulnerability to flooding.","Natural flood defenses like mangroves are more cost-effective compared to traditional engineered solutions.","Accurate and high-resolution data on mangrove distribution, socioeconomic factors, and coastal elevation are critical for effective flood risk modeling and management."],"keywords":["natural capital","ecosystem services","flood protection","coastal management","sustainability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":307,"title":"What Did the UN Climate Talks at COP28 Achieve and What's Next?","authors":"Rachel Cleetus","date":"2023-12-23","year":2023,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Union of Concerned Scientists (UCS)","World Bank Group (WBG)","Green Climate Fund","United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP)","United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction (UNDRR)","NDC Partnership"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://blog.ucs.org/rachel-cleetus/what-did-the-un-climate-talks-at-cop28-achieve-and-whats-next/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A December 2023 blog post by Union of Concerned Scientists Policy Director Rachel Cleetus assessing the outcomes of COP28 in Dubai, where nations agreed to the UAE Consensus. The piece evaluates the Loss and Damage Fund operationalization (initial pledges of ~$800 million), the first Global Stocktake's fossil fuel transition language, adaptation finance shortfalls ($194\u2013$366 billion annual gap for developing countries), and the process agreed for a new collective quantified goal (NCQG) for climate finance to be set at COP29. It flags loopholes in transition fuel and CCS language and criticizes insufficient adaptation finance commitments.","insights":["The Loss and Damage Fund was operationalized at COP28 with initial pledges of just under $800 million, including Italy ($108M), France ($108M), UAE ($100M), Germany ($100M), and the US ($17.5M), far below what climate-vulnerable nations require.","The UN Adaptation Gap Report cited in the piece quantifies the annual adaptation finance shortfall for developing countries at $194\u2013$366 billion per year, a benchmark that COP28 adaptation text failed to meaningfully address.","The UAE Consensus calls for globally tripling renewable energy and doubling the annual rate of energy efficiency improvements by 2030, but contains open-ended loopholes referencing 'transition fuels' and carbon capture and storage (CCS) that could extend fossil fuel use.","A process was agreed at COP28 to negotiate a new collective quantified goal (NCQG) for climate finance to be decided at COP29, representing the primary near-term vehicle for scaling public climate finance from wealthy nations.","The first Global Stocktake's energy package language ('transitioning away from fossil fuels in energy systems, in a just, orderly and equitable manner' to achieve net zero by 2050) marks the first global agreement to address fossil fuels but stops short of a phaseout mandate.","The US pledged $3 billion to the Green Climate Fund at COP28 but simultaneously was producing and exporting oil and fossil gas at record levels, illustrating the gap between international pledges and domestic policy.","Adaptation finance doubled-by-2025 commitments from developed countries remained aspirational, with the text only urging a report on doubling from 2019 levels rather than binding commitments.","NDCs (nationally determined contributions) due by 2025 and the NCQG represent the next concrete deadlines for translating COP28 commitments into investable policy signals that capital allocators can price."],"keywords":["Loss and Damage Fund","Global Stocktake","fossil fuel transition","adaptation finance gap","nationally determined contributions","UAE Consensus"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":578,"title":"Enabling Asset-Based Community Development Solutions: Pro-Poor Urban Climate Resilience in Kumasi, Ghana","authors":"Pius Kwabena Appau; Michael Osei Asibey; Richard Grant","date":"2023-12-14","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Africa"],"institutions":["University of Miami","Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology","Miami University"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cities.2023.104723","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article in Cities, available online 14 December 2023, on asset-based community development as a route to pro-poor urban climate resilience in Kumasi, Ghana, by Pius Kwabena Appau of Miami University, Michael Osei Asibey of KNUST and Richard Grant of the University of Miami. The premise is that Ghanaian cities combine weak urban planning capacity with inadequate finance for climate risk, and that the urban poor are excluded from official climate interventions, so community-led approaches are what provide a minimum localised capacity. The authors surveyed 367 households and six governmental agencies and ran focus groups across two long-established informal settlements, investigating perceptions of climate change, knowledge of the asset-based community development approach, its influence on building resilience, and the obstacles to implementing it. Residents demonstrated good understanding of the most salient manifestations of climate change, naming flooding, heat and drought. Three things impede the approach: the absence of formally recognised associations, the difficulty of using community assets, and households' high expectations of government aid. The authors conclude that mainstreaming the approach within urban planning and governance can be improved by emphasising co-benefits and better alignment with a shared vision.","insights":["Households' high expectation of government aid is named as an obstacle to community-led resilience, which is charity hazard operating on unpaid community effort rather than on insurance premiums.","The absence of formally recognised associations is a financing obstacle rather than a social one: without a recognised legal entity there is nothing for a grant, a loan or a guarantee to be made to, which is the constraint any pro-poor adaptation instrument hits first.","Community assets are proposed as a substitute for finance that is not arriving, so the paper describes what the urban poor do when adaptation finance does not reach them rather than designing an instrument.","Residents named flooding, heat and drought unprompted, which is a useful counterpoint to the Miami climate gentrification record where buyers were not reading the hazard at all. Awareness is not the binding constraint in Kumasi; capital and legal standing are."],"keywords":["asset-based community development","informal settlements","Kumasi","pro-poor adaptation","community assets","urban resilience","Global South","adaptation finance gap"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":144,"title":"ROI: The Economic Case for Resilient Design","authors":"","date":"2023-11-30","year":2023,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["AIA","American Institute of Architects"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.aia.org/resource-center/roi-economic-case-resilient-design","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource discusses the economic implications of resilient design in architecture, emphasizing its importance in mitigating the effects of the climate crisis. It outlines the role architects play in creating adaptive built environments that contribute to economic resilience, emphasizing factors such as risk anticipation, system interconnections, and the significance of maintaining business continuity in the face of disasters.","insights":["Resilient design enhances economic resilience, allowing communities to better withstand and recover from economic shocks.","Architects must integrate resilience measures into designs to improve the sustainability and value of the built environment.","The economic impacts of climate-related risks are interconnected across various socio-economic and financial realms.","Establishing economic resilience involves anticipating risks and developing flexibility to mitigate adverse economic incidents.","Direct economic benefits of resilient design can be easily quantified as avoided losses, such as reduced insurance costs.","Indirect benefits may include improved public relations and enhanced ecosystem services resulting from resilient infrastructure.","Investment in resilient retrofits often yields significant returns, as demonstrated by case studies like the Anheuser-Busch seismic retrofit.","Maintaining business continuity is crucial for economic recovery and can prevent high rates of business failure post-disaster.","Infrastructure systems are interrelated, meaning disruptions in one system can lead to cascading impacts on others."],"keywords":["economic resilience","risk anticipation","business continuity","adaptive capacity","infrastructure"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":569,"title":"Turning Science Into Solutions: IBHS's Strategy 2026","authors":"Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety","date":"2023-11-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://ibhs.org/strategy-2026/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety's Strategy 2026, adopted by its board in November 2023 and titled Turning Science into Solutions. It sets out how IBHS intends to convert building science into resilience practice: full-scale structural testing against high wind, wind-driven rain, hail and wildfire identifies the points of failure during those events, and that research then flows into building practices, retrofits and mitigation programmes for residential and commercial properties. IBHS has run its Research Center since 2010 and the strategy positions the institute as the body shaping the scientific knowledge and the solutions available across its core perils. The financing vocabulary is present rather than incidental: FORTIFIED appears 52 times, insurance 41 and insurers 8, so the strategy is written with the transmission from standard to underwriting in view even though it specifies no instrument.","insights":["IBHS is an insurer-funded research body that tests full-scale structures against wind, wind-driven rain, hail and wildfire, so the document is the supply side of the standard that insurance discounts are written against.","The document is an organisational strategy, and it is promoted rather than held because its subject is the peril-by-peril resilience of buildings, not the institute's own operations. That is the line the Arcadis and New York Climate Exchange holds sit on the other side of.","The title names a target year that is not the publication date: Strategy 2026 was adopted by the board in November 2023."],"keywords":["FORTIFIED","building science","wind-driven rain","hail","wildfire","retrofit","mitigation programs","full-scale testing","insurer-funded research"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":506,"title":"2023 Louisiana Statewide Resilience Annual Report","authors":"","date":"2023-11-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US","Louisiana"],"institutions":["State of Louisiana"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://resilience.la.gov/media/ru0cr43b/statewide-resilience-report-v1-final.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The first Louisiana statewide resilience report required under Act 315 of the 2023 Regular Session, arriving at the end of one administration and the start of another. It is a status report on the new structures and processes designed to increase coordination and communication across levels and agencies of government, and a set of recommendations to drive early work in the incoming administration. It is explicitly written to supply language and ideas that local, state and non-governmental entities can use to strengthen federal grant applications under the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act and the Inflation Reduction Act. The framing figure is that Louisiana has the third highest total costs from federally declared billion-dollar disasters in the United States at $290 billion. The report argues that Louisiana's existing strengths, the Coastal Protection and Restoration Authority and the Louisiana Watershed Initiative among them, are proactive resilience successes, but that building resilience requires more than disaster response and more than green or gray infrastructure: it must address the human consequences of disasters, and the state will need to adapt to hazards beyond the coast including extreme heat, excessive rainfall and drought.","insights":["The report states its own instrumental purpose, to supply language for federal grant applications under the IIJA and IRA, which makes it a state capacity-building document as much as a plan and is a candid account of what a resilience report is for.","$290 billion in federally declared billion-dollar disaster costs, third highest in the country, is the exposure figure any Louisiana resilience revenue instrument would be sized against.","The argument that resilience is more than green or gray infrastructure, and must address the human consequences of disasters, is the opening for social and economic programmes in a state framework that has been built around coastal engineering.","Extending the frame past the coast to heat, rainfall and drought is the same widening Brookings observes nationally, and it is what makes a coastal-only authority structurally insufficient.","Every page is marked DRAFT, so this is a working document rather than a final statutory report and should be read as such."],"keywords":["Act 315","statewide resilience","Louisiana","IIJA","IRA","Coastal Protection and Restoration Authority","billion-dollar disasters"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":237,"title":"Climate Adaptation, Resilience, and Financial Inclusion: A New Agenda","authors":"Peter Zetterli","date":"2023-11-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Consultative Group to Assist the Poor (CGAP)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.cgap.org/research/publication/climate-adaptation-resilience-and-financial-inclusion-new-agenda","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource emphasizes the importance of financial inclusion in enhancing climate adaptation and resilience, particularly for low-income and vulnerable communities, including women who face disproportionate impacts from climate change. It highlights the persistent gaps in access to tailored financial products that specifically address the unique climate risks faced by these populations. A collaborative, coordinated approach is needed to integrate financial inclusion with climate adaptation strategies and develop innovative solutions that meet the diverse needs of affected communities.","insights":["Financial inclusion is vital for empowering vulnerable populations to develop autonomous adaptation strategies against climate change.","Over 1.4 billion adults are financially excluded, with a significant concentration in climate-vulnerable countries.","There is a critical need for financial products specifically designed for climate adaptation and resilience.","Women are disproportionately affected by climate change, facing greater financial exclusion and vulnerabilities.","Current financial services often fail to serve the specific needs of low-income communities facing climate risks.","Collaboration among stakeholders is essential to align financial inclusion and climate adaptation efforts effectively.","Innovative solutions and deeper research are required to understand how financial services can better support climate resilience."],"keywords":["vulnerability","gender equity","social protection","collaboration","adaptation strategies"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":173,"title":"Adaptation Gap Report 2023: Underfinanced. Underprepared. Inadequate Investment and Planning on Climate Adaptation Leaves World Exposed","authors":"","date":"2023-11-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Environment Programme"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.59117/20.500.11822/43796","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Adaptation Gap Report 2023 emphasizes the significant shortfall in climate adaptation financing, highlighting urgent needs for increased investment and planning to address the climate vulnerability faced by developing countries. With annual adaptation finance needs estimated to be between $215 billion and $387 billion, current flows fall drastically short at $21.3 billion. The report calls for enhanced international cooperation, innovative funding mechanisms, and comprehensive strategies to address structural inequities and foster resilience among vulnerable populations.","insights":["The adaptation finance gap is estimated between $194 billion and $366 billion per year, indicating a significant shortage compared to financial needs.","Approximately 40% of the global population is located in highly climate-vulnerable regions, underscoring the urgency for scaled-up adaptation efforts.","Despite some increases in adaptation finance commitments from 2017 to 2020, there was a concerning 15% decline in 2021, reflecting a downward trend in financial support.","Only 24% of countries have established monitoring and evaluation systems for assessing adaptation efforts, highlighting a significant gap in effectiveness tracking.","Gender equity and social inclusion considerations are often neglected in adaptation finance, necessitating a focused approach to support marginalized groups."],"keywords":["vulnerability","funding mechanisms","risk management","capacity building","collaboration"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":59,"title":"Scaling Conservation Agriculture Practices Through the Soil and Water Outcomes Fund","authors":"","date":"2023-11-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures","Iowa Soybean Association"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/soil-and-water-outcomes-fund","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Soil and Water Outcomes Fund, in partnership with the Iowa Soybean Association and Quantified Ventures, incentivizes farmers to adopt conservation agriculture practices that improve environmental outcomes such as water quality and carbon sequestration. The fund operates on a pay-for-performance model, enabling cost-effective solutions for addressing water quality challenges while supporting farmers' economic resilience. Since its launch, it has successfully enrolled 1.7 million acres, leading to significant environmental benefits.","insights":["The Soil and Water Outcomes Fund employs a multi-payor financing model to support farmers in implementing conservation practices.","Financial incentives are tied to verifiable environmental outcomes like improved water quality and carbon sequestration.","The program provides greater per-acre payments compared to traditional conservation programs, enhancing farmers' participation.","Since 2020, the fund has enrolled 1.7 million acres across 21 states.","The initiative has resulted in the sequestration of 1.4 million metric tons of greenhouse gases.","It has successfully prevented significant amounts of nitrogen and phosphorus from leaving enrolled fields.","The program's innovative approach has the potential for replication in other U.S. watersheds.","Collaboration between private investors, farmers, and outcome purchasers is essential to the fund's model.","The Soil and Water Outcomes Fund addresses critical water quality challenges faced by agriculture-heavy regions.","The program strategically combines multiple environmental outcomes into a single transaction for cost efficiency."],"keywords":["conservation agriculture","environmental outcomes","pay-for-performance","sustainability","regenerative practices"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":401,"title":"New Study Finds Blue-Green Infrastructure in New York City Would Generate $2 Return on Every $1 Invested","authors":"","date":"2023-10-12","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["Rebuild by Design","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Ramboll"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://rebuildbydesign.org/reports/bgi-for-nyc/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This October 2023 press release announces a joint report by Rebuild by Design and Ramboll, \"Blue-Green Infrastructure: A Business Case for New York City,\" which finds that blue-green infrastructure designed to a 10-year storm standard generates $2.09 in return for every $1 invested. Using city-wide flood and climate-risk modeling across all five boroughs, the study performs a Benefit-Cost Analysis and finds that up to 82% of NYC has a positive business case for managing stormwater with blue-green solutions, and that DEP could save 20% on sewer upgrade costs. The report was released two weeks after the September 29, 2023 flooding event that shut down NYC schools and subways.","insights":["Blue-green infrastructure designed to a 10-year storm standard generates a $2.09 return for every $1 invested in New York City, establishing a documented BCR of approximately 2.09:1.","Up to 82% of New York City's land area has a positive business case for managing stormwater with blue-green infrastructure, indicating broad citywide investability.","Implementing blue-green infrastructure citywide would save NYC's Department of Environmental Protection 20% on costs of upgrading existing sewers to future service levels, reducing gray infrastructure capital expenditure.","City-wide blue-green infrastructure is feasible up to a future 50-year design storm in NYC, meaning the solution scales to more severe future climate scenarios without losing its business case.","151,000 new trees could be planted in NYC within a positive blue-green infrastructure business case, quantifying the co-benefit potential of urban canopy expansion alongside stormwater management.","Co-benefits (including air quality improvement, biodiversity, urban cooling, recreational access, and mental health), are described as essential to closing the business case for blue-green infrastructure, meaning allocators must account for multi-benefit valuation rather than flood damage reduction alone.","Blue-green infrastructure is most feasible when implemented in parks, plazas, and public housing, suggesting that publicly owned land provides the highest-return deployment sites and that public housing residents are direct beneficiaries.","The New York City Panel on Climate Change projects up to 25% more annual rainfall and a 50% increase in days with more than one inch of rain by end of century, establishing the forward-looking risk context that underpins the investment case."],"keywords":["blue-green infrastructure","benefit-cost analysis","stormwater management","urban flood risk","sponge city","co-benefits valuation","cloudburst resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":35,"title":"The Global Costs of Extreme Weather That Are Attributable to Climate Change","authors":"Rebecca Newman, Ilan Noy","date":"2023-09-29","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Reserve Bank of New Zealand","Victoria University of Wellington"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-41888-1","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource evaluates the global economic costs of extreme weather attributable to climate change, estimating approximately $143 billion in annual damages from 2000 to 2019. It utilizes Extreme Event Attribution methodology to highlight the significant mortality and economic impacts tied to such events, while calling for improved data collection and methodologies to accurately reflect these costs and inform effective policy and adaptation strategies.","insights":["Approximately $143 billion per year in extreme weather costs is attributable to climate change.","63% of climate change-induced costs resulted from human loss of life, totaling over $430 billion in value.","High-income countries bear a significant share (47%) of climate change-related economic costs despite lower relative GDP impacts compared to low-income countries.","Floods and storms account for the majority of economic damages attributed to climate change.","The study estimates that climate change is responsible for two-thirds of the economic costs from certain extreme weather events.","Economic costs associated with extreme events are often underestimated, as indirect losses and psychological impacts are frequently excluded from data.","1.4 billion people were estimated to be affected by climate-related extreme weather events from 2000 to 2019, leading to significant but often unquantified economic costs.","Continued efforts to improve data collection and methodological precision in attribution studies are essential for effective climate change policy and planning."],"keywords":["Extreme Weather","Economic Costs","Event Attribution","Human Impact","Data Gaps"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":34,"title":"Can Florida\u2019s Coast Survive Its Reliance on Development?: Fiscal Vulnerability and Funding Woes Under Sea Level Rise","authors":"Linda Shi, William Butler, Tisha Holmes, Ryan Thomas, Anthony Milordis, Jonathan Ignatowski, Yousuf Mahid, Austin M. Aldag","date":"2023-09-29","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Journal of the American Planning Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/01944363.2023.2249866","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Florida\u2019s coastal municipalities face substantial fiscal vulnerability due to sea level rise, which threatens their reliance on property taxes from coastal development. Over half of the state\u2019s municipalities are projected to experience significant revenue losses, highlighting the need for innovative financial strategies and improved governance to address these challenges. Despite the looming financial risks, there is little correlation between municipalities' prioritization of climate adaptation and their fiscal exposure, complicating efforts to enhance resilience along the coast.","insights":["More than half of Florida's 410 municipalities will be impacted by sea level rise, with significant revenue losses anticipated.","Fiscal exposure is disproportionately concentrated in smaller, wealthier, and less diverse municipalities, complicating equitable adaptation efforts.","There is no correlation between local governments' prioritization of climate adaptation and their fiscal exposure to sea level rise.","Rising flood insurance rates are placing a financial burden on residents and local governments.","Public funding for coastal adaptation projects is crucial for sustaining Florida's economy.","Emerging climate finance strategies could help local governments diversify funding sources to adapt to sea level rise.","Insurance markets are adjusting to the risks posed by climate change, affecting homeowners financially.","Collaboration between state and local governments is necessary to address climate challenges effectively."],"keywords":["fiscal vulnerability","municipal finance","urban planning","revenue loss","fiscal stress"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":614,"title":"A Generalized Framework for Designing Open-Source Natural Hazard Parametric Insurance","authors":"Carmen B. Steinmann; Benoit P. Guillod; Christopher Fairless; David N. Bresch","date":"2023-09-02","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global","Africa","Europe"],"institutions":["ETH Zurich"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10669-023-09934-x","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article in Environment Systems and Decisions, volume 43 pages 555 to 568, accepted 5 August 2023 and published online 2 September 2023, by Carmen B. Steinmann, Benoit P. Guillod, Christopher Fairless and David N. Bresch. Parametric insurance lets payouts be triggered by real-time hydrological and meteorological parameters rather than waiting for a damage assessment, so claims settle swiftly and people get funds right after the event. The authors propose a framework for designing parametric schemes and for systematically quantifying the basis risk, the difference between the parameter-based payout and the actual damage, and implement it in CLIMADA, the open-source global risk assessment platform. They illustrate it with two stylised case studies, tropical cyclones in Mozambique and winter storms in France. The data used and the code base provided are globally consistent, open source and readily available, so the methods are applicable in data-scarce areas and accessible to public and private stakeholders, and the approach adapts to other hazards and exposures worldwide.","insights":["**Open source is the point, not a detail.** Parametric scheme design has been the preserve of reinsurers and modelling firms, and a globally consistent open code base lets a municipality, a ministry or a trust design and price its own trigger. That changes who can be a sponsor, which is the constraint the Cayman record states as a cost question.","Choosing Mozambique and France as the two cases is deliberate: one data-scarce and one data-rich, which is how the authors demonstrate the claim about applicability in data-scarce areas rather than asserting it.","CLIMADA is the platform, a documented and auditable open-source risk model rather than a proprietary one, so the hazard assumptions behind any result are inspectable."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","basis risk","CLIMADA","open source","tropical cyclone","European winter storm","scheme design","data-scarce"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":295,"title":"Integrating Climate Risk into an Insurer\u2019s Strategic Asset Allocation","authors":"Tully Cheng, Sarah Peasey, Gilles Drukier, Oliver Little, Flora Xu, Zhengyang Su","date":"2023-09-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global","Europe"],"institutions":["Neuberger Berman"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&source=web&rct=j&opi=89978449&url=https://www.nb.com/handlers/documents.ashx%3Fid%3D35227850-6a41-4b1b-87e2-d7474584ad53&ved=2ahUKEwjN_fmyrumUAxWpKtAFHdQLFewQFnoECBkQAQ&usg=AOvVaw1b35mnOMu1NDjf0qoHyxy-","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This September 2023 Neuberger Berman white paper presents a proprietary \"Climate SAA\" framework that integrates climate-adjusted capital market assumptions, built on Climate Value at Risk (Climate VaR) and a sovereign bond equivalent, into strategic asset allocation (SAA) optimizations for European insurance portfolios. Applied to illustrative UK general insurer and Continental European life insurer portfolios under a 2\u00b0C warming scenario, the framework recovers 21 bps and 16 bps of estimated return respectively versus ex-post climate adjustment, while simultaneously lowering solvency capital requirements (SCR) and reducing financed emissions by up to ~40%, with further reductions possible via emissions constraints.","insights":["Climate SAA recovers 21 bps of estimated return (from 4.94% to 5.15%) for a UK general insurer relative to standard ex-post climate adjustment at ~3% surplus volatility, while also reducing total SCR from 6.1% to 5.4%, a simultaneous improvement in both return and solvency capital efficiency.","For a Continental European life insurer, Climate SAA recovers 16 of 20 basis points of estimated return lost to ex-post climate adjustment (from 3.59% to 3.75%), reducing SCR from 13.6% to 13.4%, at ~4% surplus volatility.","Ex-ante climate integration reduces financed emissions substantially without adding volatility: the UK insurer portfolio's carbon intensity falls from 96 to 61 (\u201335) and carbon footprint from 52 to 31 (\u201321) tons CO\u2082e per $M, purely as a byproduct of optimization.","Adding financed emissions as explicit constraints in the Continental European life insurer's Climate SAA reduces carbon intensity by a further 28\u201334 and carbon footprint by a further 13\u201315, cutting total portfolio emissions roughly 50% versus the standard portfolio, with estimated return declining by only 1\u20132 bps.","The mechanism behind return recovery is counterintuitive: traditionally higher-return asset classes (equities, high yield, EM debt) receive the largest climate-cost penalties (e.g., US HY BB: \u2013163 bps/yr, UK equity: \u201377 bps/yr), so ex-ante optimization shifts allocations toward core fixed income that is penalized less, raising net expected return.","SAA drives up to 90% of variation in portfolio returns over time per the paper's framing, meaning climate risk left unaddressed at this level represents a structurally significant unmanaged exposure for insurers and other institutional allocators."],"keywords":["Climate Value at Risk","strategic asset allocation","solvency capital requirement","insurance portfolio optimization","financed emissions","ex-ante climate adjustment","capital market assumptions"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":404,"title":"Climate Risk Management in the U.S. Insurance Sector: Detailed Analysis of 15 Companies","authors":"","date":"2023-07-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Ceres","National Association of Insurance Commissioners","Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures","Liberty Mutual","Munich RE","Swiss Re","Financial Stability Board","Manifest Climate"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ceres.org/resources/reports/climate-risk-management-us-insurance-sector","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Ceres/Manifest Climate report (July 2023) provides a detailed TCFD-alignment analysis of 15 U.S. insurance companies selected for high disclosure quality, covering governance, strategy, risk management, and metrics and targets across more than 200 climate risk action items. The 15 companies (spanning P&C, life, health, and reinsurance), range from $230M to $75B in direct premiums written. The report finds widespread but shallow disclosure: while most companies integrate climate into ERM and disclose Scope 1/2 metrics, critical gaps remain in board decision-making processes, Scope 3 materiality, scenario analysis breadth, carbon pricing, and climate-linked executive compensation.","insights":["Only 5 of 15 companies disclosed scenario analyses covering all three temperature bands (\u003c2\u00b0C, 1.5\u00b0C, and >2\u00b0C), and only 2 of those also addressed physical risk, transition risk, and time horizons, a critical gap for capital allocators trying to stress-test insurer balance sheets.","None of the 15 companies disclosed the percentage of assets aligned with climate opportunities; only Prudential and Swiss Re disclosed the percentage of assets materially exposed to climate risks, leaving a core portfolio-level data point missing for investors.","Swiss Re adopted an internal carbon price of $100/tonne as of 2021, rising to $200/tonne by 2030, covering Scope 1, 2, and significant upstream Scope 3 emissions, one of only two companies disclosing voluntary carbon pricing and the only one disclosing its trajectory.","Only 1 of 15 companies (Munich Re) disclosed board-level climate-linked pay; only 1 (AFLAC) disclosed executive climate-linked incentives, limiting accountability mechanisms that ratings agencies and institutional investors increasingly require.","Six of 15 companies provided no Scope 3 disclosures at all, and none provided a clear materiality assessment for Scope 3, undermining comparability across the sector for financed-emissions analysis.","Five companies (Swiss Re, Molina, Munich Re, Travelers, John Hancock/Manulife) indicated transition plans; none included detail on resource allocation impacts, annual updates, or processes to review goals, the content most relevant to transition-risk pricing.","Prudential linked a $4 billion five-year credit facility to sustainability targets including GHG reduction and leadership diversity, one of the only examples in the sample of a direct financial instrument tied to climate performance metrics."],"keywords":["TCFD disclosure","insurance sector","NAIC climate survey","enterprise risk management","carbon pricing","financed emissions","scenario analysis","Manifest Climate"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":458,"title":"NOAA Announces $2.6 Billion to Strengthen Resiliency of Coastal Communities","authors":"Sarah Gimont","date":"2023-06-29","year":2023,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Association of Counties","National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.naco.org/blog/noaa-announces-26-billion-strengthen-resiliency-coastal-communities","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A National Association of Counties explainer on NOAA's announcement of $2.6 billion through the Climate-Ready Coasts and Communities programme, funded from the $3.3 billion the Inflation Reduction Act provided to NOAA, with counties directly eligible to apply. The programme comprises five initiatives, of which the post details those most relevant to counties. The Climate Resilience Regional Challenge is a $575 million competitive grant programme split into two tracks: Track One for regional collaborative building and strategy development, with 20 to 25 anticipated grants of $500,000 to $2 million, and Track Two for implementation of resilience adaptation actions, with anticipated awards from $15 million to $75 million. Neither track carries a match requirement. The Climate Ready Workforce initiative offers $60 million in competitive grants and technical assistance for workforce development programmes focused on climate resiliency, with 10 to 20 anticipated grants of $500,000 to $10 million and no local match. The stated focus of the Regional Challenge is reducing climate risk, building local capacity to address extreme weather, and promoting equity and inclusion.","insights":["No match requirement on either track is the detail that matters for under-resourced applicants, since a match is the usual reason a small county cannot pursue a large federal award.","The two-track design funds strategy development separately from implementation, at $500,000 to $2 million and $15 million to $75 million respectively, which is an explicit attempt to pay for the pre-development stage that normally goes unfunded.","Counties being directly eligible rather than passing through a state changes who controls the project, and is the reason a county association is explaining the programme.","A separate $60 million workforce initiative treats delivery capacity as a fundable object, which is the same enduring-capacity concern the programme page names."],"keywords":["NOAA","Climate-Ready Coasts","Inflation Reduction Act","competitive grants","no match requirement","counties","workforce development"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":210,"title":"Insurance Supervision and Regulation of Climate-Related Risks","authors":"","date":"2023-06-26","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["US Department of the Treasury"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/FIO-June-2023-Insurance-Supervision-and-Regulation-of-Climate-Related-Risks.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource outlines the regulatory landscape governing the insurance industry in relation to climate-related risks, emphasizing the crucial role of effective supervision and adaptive frameworks in enhancing resilience and financial stability. It discusses the integration of climate considerations into insurance practices and the need for collaboration among stakeholders to mitigate potential risks, highlighting the ongoing efforts by regulators and the insurance sector to address evolving challenges posed by climate change.","insights":["Effective insurance regulation is essential for managing climate-related risks and enhancing resilience in the insurance sector.","Regulatory frameworks must adapt to the evolving nature of climate risks to ensure financial stability and sustainable practices among insurers.","Collaboration between regulators, insurers, and stakeholders is vital for developing comprehensive climate risk management strategies.","Transparency and disclosure of climate-related risks are critical for improving accountability and stakeholder understanding within the insurance industry.","Consumer education regarding climate risks and insurance coverage is necessary to alleviate misconceptions and enhance engagement with appropriate products."],"keywords":["insurance regulation","risk assessment","sustainability","resilience","collaboration"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":244,"title":"Designing a Climate Resilience Classification Framework to Facilitate Investment in Climate Resilience Through Capital Markets","authors":"","date":"2023-06-20","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction","Climate Bonds Initiative"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.undrr.org/quick/78922","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource presents a comprehensive blueprint for a climate resilience classification framework designed to enhance investment flows through capital markets, addressing the urgent financing needs for climate resilience, especially in developing countries. It proposes structured definitions and criteria based on established global frameworks to categorize resilience investments across various sectors, aiming to mobilize substantial capital towards sustainable development and effective adaptation strategies.","insights":["Investment needs for climate resilience are estimated at US$ 1.8 trillion by 2030, necessitating new mechanisms for capital access.","Seven climate resilience themes have been identified to ensure investments contribute effectively to resilience against climate impacts.","Only 19% of labeled green bonds currently finance resilience-related projects, indicating a significant gap in investment focus.","The classification framework aims to provide consistent standards and clear pathways for assessing and categorizing climate resilience investments.","Stakeholder consultations and evidence-based definitions will ensure robust investment eligibility criteria and enhance investor clarity.","Mobilizing $1.5 trillion towards resilience investments by 2025 through the thematic bond market is a key initiative goal.","Collaboration and systematic engagement with diverse stakeholders are essential for refining and implementing the classification framework.","The framework emphasizes avoiding maladaptation and ensuring alignment with sustainability objectives in investment choices."],"keywords":["sustainable bonds","taxonomy","stakeholders engagement","funding strategies","investment criteria"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":561,"title":"How Much Bilateral and Multilateral Climate Adaptation Finance Is Targeting the Health Sector? A Scoping Review of Official Development Assistance Data Between 2009\u20132019","authors":"Tilly Alcayna; Devin O'Donnell; Sarina Chandaria","date":"2023-06-14","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global","Africa"],"institutions":["Red Cross Red Crescent Climate Centre","London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10266659/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A scoping review in PLOS Global Public Health establishing a baseline for how much bilateral and multilateral climate adaptation finance reached the health sector over the decade 2009 to 2019. Searching international financial reporting databases, the authors estimate that USD 1,431 million, or 4.9 per cent of total multilateral and bilateral adaptation finance, was committed to health activities across the decade, and say that is likely an overestimate of the true figure. Health was largely a co-benefit of projects rather than their principal objective. Most health adaptation projects were in Sub-Saharan Africa, with average project funding comparable to East Asia and the Pacific and to the Middle East and North Africa. Fragile and conflict affected countries received 25.7 per cent of total health adaptation financing. The authors single out the paucity of health indicators in project monitoring and evaluation criteria and the lack of emphasis on local adaptation.","insights":["The finding that health is a co-benefit rather than a principal objective is what makes the 4.9 per cent figure an overestimate rather than an understatement: the money counted was largely committed for something else and credited to health afterwards.","The baseline is decade-scale and database-derived, measuring adaptation finance actually received by sector, which is the backward-looking denominator rather than a forward-looking estimate of need.","The absence of health indicators in project monitoring is a measurement gap with a financing consequence: a sector whose outcomes are not tracked cannot demonstrate the returns that would justify a larger share of the flow."],"keywords":["adaptation finance","health sector","official development assistance","scoping review","co-benefit","fragile and conflict affected states","Sub-Saharan Africa","climate resilient health system"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":481,"title":"Inflation Reduction Act: A Historic Investment in America\u2019s Climate Resilience","authors":"","date":"2023-06-08","year":2023,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA)","U.S. Department of Commerce"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/feature-story/inflation-reduction-act-historic-investment-americas-climate-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"On 6 June 2023 the U.S. Secretary of Commerce announced $3.3 billion in Inflation Reduction Act investments for NOAA, directed at making American communities and the economy ready for and resilient to climate change. NOAA Fisheries receives a share to strengthen science-based management and conservation of marine resources, building on Bipartisan Infrastructure Act investments. The money funds research, observations, modeling, predictions and service delivery against wildfire, flood, drought and extreme heat risks, plus facilities, hurricane hunter aircraft, supercomputing capacity and permitting efficiency. Climate-Ready Fisheries, one of four new NOAA initiatives, supports the nation's $370 billion fishing industry and the states, communities and tribes that depend on it, modernising the stock assessment enterprise to incorporate climate and ecosystem data for real-time advice and long-range projections. NOAA prioritised North Atlantic right whales, red snapper and Pacific salmon on urgency, impact on dependent coastal communities, and where investment would most advance climate resilience. Implementation plans were to be executed between the announcement and 2026.","insights":["The investment is framed as buying predictive capacity rather than physical protection: supercomputing, observations, modeling and stock assessments, which is adaptation spending on the information layer rather than on assets.","NOAA states the exposure it is protecting in economic terms, 1.7 million jobs and $244 billion in annual activity from fisheries and a $370 billion fishing industry, which is the benefit denominator any return-on-resilience calculation for this spending would use.","Prioritisation was explicit and published, on urgency, community dependence and marginal resilience gain, which is a rare public statement of how a federal agency rationed a lump-sum appropriation.","The funding is a one-time appropriation with an implementation window to 2026, so the recurring cost of the capacity it builds is not addressed in the announcement."],"keywords":["Inflation Reduction Act","NOAA Fisheries","Climate-Ready Fisheries","stock assessment","federal appropriation","climate services"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":143,"title":"Using Return on Investment and Resiliency Return on Investment for Preparedness","authors":"James F. Smith","date":"2023-05-25","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Public Administration Review"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/puar.13677","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The paper discusses the importance of using Return on Investment (ROI) and Resiliency Return on Investment (RROI) as tools for decision-making in capital investments aimed at enhancing preparedness and resiliency. It highlights the challenges associated with quantifying benefits, particularly soft benefits, and emphasizes the need for a methodical approach to measure both the hard and soft benefits of investments in law enforcement and airport security. The author argues that computing ROI and RROI, despite their complexities, is crucial for justifying investments against future risks and uncertainties.","insights":["ROI is a fundamental decision-making tool in capital investments but challenging to apply to resiliency measures.","Resiliency Return on Investment (RROI) accounts for benefits associated with enhancing the resiliency of operations and facilities.","Quantifying soft benefits, such as improved public safety and reassurance, is essential but often underrepresented in ROI calculations.","The paper emphasizes that a culture of resiliency is increasingly necessary in the face of frequent disruptions and risks.","Clear communication of ROI and RROI is critical for gaining approval from senior management and stakeholders.","The need for backup power and operational redundancy in airports highlights the complexities involved in calculating RROI.","Investments in training and emergency management personnel are often justified more by soft benefits than by hard benefits.","The document posits that the true value of resiliency investments may not be realized until after a significant incident.","Efforts to capture soft benefits can provide a more comprehensive understanding of the true impacts of resiliency investments.","Decision-makers must be prepared to invest time and resources to compute ROI and RROI effectively."],"keywords":["investment strategy","risk management","emergency preparedness","decision-making","operational efficiency"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":446,"title":"Sea-Level Rise Exposure and Municipal Bond Yields","authors":"Paul Goldsmith-Pinkham; Matthew T. Gustafson; Ryan C. Lewis; Michael Schwert","date":"2023-05-08","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Yale University","Pennsylvania State University","University of Colorado Boulder","National Bureau of Economic Research"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://academic.oup.com/rfs/advance-article/doi/10.1093/rfs/hhad041/7156853?guestAccessKey=2de5aec0-8cff-492f-be02-2695c8744589&login=true","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The published version of the paper establishing that municipal bond markets began pricing sea-level rise exposure in 2013, a date the authors tie to upward revisions in worst-case sea-level rise projections and to the uncertainty accompanying those revisions. The effect is larger for long-maturity bonds and is not solely driven by near-term flood risk, which is the result that separates a climate-uncertainty story from a flood-exposure story. The authors use a structural model of credit risk to quantify the implied economic impact and to distinguish the effect of underlying asset values from the effect of uncertainty itself. Two robustness findings carry the argument: the sea-level rise exposure premium follows a different trend from house prices, and it is unaffected by house price controls, so it is not a housing market effect transmitted to bonds. Their conclusion is that climate uncertainty, rather than expected damage alone, is an important driver of municipal bond prices.","insights":["The premium is attributed to uncertainty rather than to expected loss: it appears in 2013 alongside upward revisions to worst-case projections, is concentrated in long maturities, and survives house price controls, so what the market prices is the width of the distribution.","A 2013 onset is a dateable market response to a change in scientific projections rather than to a disaster, which is unusual evidence that climate information alone moves the cost of public capital.","The maturity structure of the effect means the cost falls on exactly the long-dated borrowing that resilience infrastructure requires, so climate uncertainty raises the price of adapting.","A structural credit risk model is used to separate asset value effects from uncertainty effects, which is what lets the authors put an implied economic magnitude on a yield spread.","The premium trending differently from house prices rules out the simplest alternative explanation, that bond investors are merely tracking local property markets."],"keywords":["sea-level rise","municipal bond yields","climate uncertainty","credit risk","bond maturity","asset pricing","public finance"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":275,"title":"Florida SB 302: Statewide Framework for Nature-Based Coastal Resilience","authors":"","date":"2023-04-28","year":2023,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Florida","US"],"institutions":["Environmental Defense Fund"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.edf.org/media/new-florida-legislation-advances-prioritization-climate-resilience","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Florida Governor DeSantis signed SB 302 into law, establishing a statewide framework and permitting process for nature-based and hybrid coastal resilience projects, and directing the Florida DEP to develop design guidelines for green and hybrid infrastructure. Builds on multi-year stakeholder collaboration since 2022 and strengthens state-local coordination on sea-level rise adaptation.","insights":["The load-bearing provision is the enabling legislation making Florida eligible for FEMA's Safeguarding Tomorrow Revolving Loan Fund, which supplies direct low-interest loans to local governments for hazard risk reduction and is described as reducing the burden of grant applications.","HB 111 expands Sea-Level Impact Projection study requirements from publicly funded projects in the coastal construction zone to projects in all areas threatened by current and projected sea level rise, which widens a study mandate from a coastal strip to a risk footprint.","The revolving loan structure is presented as a route to nature-based infrastructure and to addressing multiple flood risks at once, rather than as generic hazard money.","The bill passed the House unanimously and moved through the Senate, which the release frames as bipartisan support for the framework.","EDF's own recommendation is to continue by prioritising nature-based solutions and science-based decisions, so the organisational position and the legislative fact should not be conflated."],"keywords":["Florida","SB 302","nature-based solutions","coastal resilience","sea-level rise","DEP","permitting","NBS"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":113,"title":"Enhancing the Insurance Sector\u2019s Contribution to Climate Adaptation","authors":"","date":"2023-03-22","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["OECD"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/enhancing-the-insurance-sector-s-contribution-to-climate-adaptation_0951dfcd-en.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource highlights the insurance sector's pivotal role in climate adaptation by providing coverage against climate-related disasters and promoting risk reduction measures. However, increasing climate-related risks and existing regulatory and market constraints pose challenges to insurers, limiting their ability to facilitate resilient rebuilding and invest in long-term risk management. The document suggests necessary regulatory and communication changes to enhance the sector's contribution to climate adaptation and improve overall community resilience.","insights":["Climate change is predicted to increase the frequency and intensity of climate-related disasters, impacting insurance availability.","Adequate insurance coverage can facilitate quicker recovery and limit economic disruption following disasters.","Challenges in providing adequate climate risk assessments hinder the insurance sector's ability to price premiums effectively.","Regulatory and business challenges may impede the integration of climate risk analytics in the insurance sector.","Collective community-based insurance approaches may improve broader resilience against climate-related risks.","Encouraging multi-year insurance contracts may foster an environment where policyholders are more likely to invest in risk reduction."],"keywords":["risk reduction","insurance regulation","premium pricing","community resilience","resilient reinstatement"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":461,"title":"Adaptation Infrastructure and Its Effects on Property Values in the Face of Climate Risk","authors":"David L. Kelly; Renato Molina","date":"2023-03-20","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of Miami"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/725109","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Kelly and Molina measure what adaptation infrastructure does to property prices, using over 400,000 property transactions and 162 infrastructure projects in Miami-Dade County, an area highly exposed to flooding and sea level rise. By exploiting the timing and siting of different adaptation projects they identify significant gains in property values after project completion, concentrated in areas close to the project and in projects that are visually identifiable. Their central estimate is an aggregate mean benefit, net of adaptation cost, of about $0.68 million per project, and almost $300 million in aggregate net benefits across all projects in the sample. They also report that most projects generated positive net benefits, which they read as indicating that the vast majority of adaptation effort in the county is being placed in areas that pass a benefit-cost test. The authors are both at the University of Miami and the work was supported by the university's Laboratory of Integrative Knowledge.","insights":["The benefit is capitalised into property value and is therefore measurable and, in principle, capturable: a mean net benefit of about $0.68 million per project and nearly $300 million in aggregate is exactly the value a tax increment or special assessment district could monetise.","The effect is concentrated in projects that are visually identifiable, which means the market is pricing perceived protection as much as engineered protection, and has a direct implication for how adaptation projects should be designed and communicated if value capture is the funding route.","Finding that most projects pass a benefit-cost test is a validation of existing siting decisions rather than a critique, which is an unusual and useful result for a county already spending on adaptation.","The identification comes from the timing and siting of 162 separate projects against 400,000 transactions, which is a strong design for a question usually answered with hedonic cross-sections."],"keywords":["adaptation infrastructure","property values","Miami-Dade","benefit-cost","value capture","sea level rise","hedonic analysis"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":624,"title":"Safeguarding Tomorrow Revolving Loan Fund: Florida Public Notice","authors":"Florida Division of Emergency Management","date":"2023-03-14","year":2023,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Florida Division of Emergency Management","Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.floridadisaster.org/dem/mitigation/safeguarding-tomorrow-through-ongoing-risk-mitigation-storm-revolving-loan-fund/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A public notice from the Florida Division of Emergency Management's Bureau of Mitigation seeking proposals from communities interested in low interest loans for hazard mitigation projects under the Safeguarding Tomorrow Revolving Loan Fund. The Safeguarding Tomorrow through Ongoing Risk Mitigation Act, the STORM Act, became law on 1 January 2021 and authorises FEMA to provide capitalization grants to eligible entities, meaning states, eligible federally recognized tribes, territories and the District of Columbia, which then make their own funding decisions and award loans directly to local communities. The Division is gathering information with the intent of applying for a capitalization grant under the Fiscal Year 2023 notice of funding opportunity published on 20 December 2022, funding opportunity number DHS-23-STORM-139-00-01, and must solicit projects from local governments for a minimum of six weeks to build a project proposal list to accompany its application; a well-developed list lets the state focus limited loan funds on the local governments most in need and prioritise projects ready for financing. Local governments may apply to the state for a loan and must have adopted their county Local Mitigation Strategy plan; individuals are not eligible. FEMA's four stated priorities are to empower entities through engagement and technical assistance, to create innovative funding solutions by letting applicants leverage loans for the non-federal cost share of other FEMA hazard mitigation assistance programs so underserved communities can access additional resources, to deliver equitable investments with a goal that 40 per cent of the overall benefits of the loan funds flow to underserved communities, and to reduce grant application complexity.","insights":["**The revolving structure is the point**: repayments recapitalise the fund, so a single federal capitalization grant finances successive rounds of local mitigation rather than one round of it.","The leverage mechanism is the sharpest detail. **A STORM loan may be used as the non-federal cost share for another FEMA hazard mitigation grant**, which converts borrowed money into match money and multiplies a community's access to federal grants. That is a genuinely different use of debt from financing the works directly.","The eligibility gate is an adopted county Local Mitigation Strategy plan, so the capital is conditioned on a completed plan rather than on an application.","The 40 per cent equitable-benefit goal is a distributional target attached to a LOAN rather than a grant, and that carries a built-in tension: underserved communities are the least able to carry debt."],"keywords":["STORM Act","revolving loan fund","capitalization grant","non-federal cost share","Local Mitigation Strategy","hazard mitigation","underserved communities","FEMA"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":498,"title":"More than Checking a Box: Delivering Real-World, Validated Forward-Looking Financial Metrics for Physical Climate Risks","authors":"Claire Souch","date":"2023-02-21","year":2023,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Moody's"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.moodys.com/web/en/us/insights/insurance/more-than-checking-a-box-delivering-real-world-validated-forward-looking-financial-metrics-for-physical-climate-risks.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Claire Souch of Moody's RMS argues that anyone estimating physical climate risk, whether for a lending portfolio, for the disruption of critical facilities or for a TCFD report, needs to know how the estimate is derived and how its outputs are validated, because those choices drive financial management decisions made under high uncertainty. The post describes how Moody's models these numbers using Climate on Demand version 2, which integrates physical climate risk modeling from Moody's RMS. It sets out the credibility case: Moody's RMS has worked in physical risk modeling for three decades and maintains 400 risk models covering countries and regions worldwide; those models underpin the highly regulated US$2.5 trillion global insurance industry and are now applied to asset management, investments, credit risk and lending decisions; they are developed and supported by over 1,200 staff including hundreds with doctorates, with sustained research and development investment. The physical risk financial model has evolved to assess compounding hazard impacts and physical damage at a highly granular level.","insights":["The argument is that validation, not resolution, is what makes a climate risk number usable, which is the right emphasis and also the hardest thing for a buyer of these products to check independently.","The provenance claim is the interesting one commercially: the same models that underwrite a $2.5 trillion insurance industry are now being sold into credit and lending, so the insurance sector's risk view is quietly becoming the banking sector's.","That migration is what gives insurance pricing leverage over mortgage and corporate credit decisions, so the paper names the transmission channel rather than assuming it.","The post is vendor marketing and should be read as such: the capability claims are the company's own and the validation method is described rather than shown.","It is nonetheless a useful dated record of when physical risk catastrophe models crossed from insurance into mainstream credit analytics, which is early 2023 on this account."],"keywords":["catastrophe modeling","physical climate risk","model validation","Climate on Demand","TCFD","credit risk","Moody's RMS"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":58,"title":"Exemplary Forestry Investment Fund: A Blended Capital Model to Mitigate Climate Change and Secure the Future of the Maine Woods","authors":"","date":"2023-02-19","year":2023,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","Maine"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures","Exemplary Forestry Management"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/exemplary-forestry-maine","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Exemplary Forestry Investment Fund (EFIF) aims to address both ecological and financial challenges in Maine's North Woods by combining over $90 million of public, private, and philanthropic capital to acquire and manage over 100,000 acres of forest. This fund promotes Exemplary Forestry practices, enhancing long-term forest health, carbon sequestration, and biodiversity, while also providing sustainable financial returns. The initiative recognizes the necessity of a new financial model to support long-term forest stewardship and addresses the urgent climate and biodiversity crises.","insights":["The EFIF is an impact-first investment vehicle blending over $90 million to manage Maine's forests.","Exemplary Forestry practices aim to improve ecological and financial outcomes in the forest sector.","40% of Maine's North Woods are understocked, affecting timber quality and ecosystem services.","80% of Maine's forests have changed ownership in the past 40 years, increasing fragmentation risks.","The fund focuses on long-term conservation management to ensure timber productivity and biodiversity.","Initial acquisitions of 10,000-15,000 acres are planned for 2023-24, with a goal of 100,000 acres by 2029.","The EFIF is self-sufficient at managing 35,000 acres, expected to be reached by 2026.","The fund seeks to monetize ecosystem services like carbon credits to enhance long-term viability.","Community engagement is vital for the fund's success, emphasizing local partnerships and support."],"keywords":["forestry","sustainability","biodiversity","ecosystem services","forest management"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":194,"title":"Unpriced Climate Risk and the Potential Consequences of Overvaluation in US Housing Markets","authors":"Jesse D. Gourevitch, Carolyn Kousky, Yanjun (Penny) Liao, Christoph Nolte, Adam B. Pollack, Jeremy R. Porter, Joakim A. Weill","date":"2023-02-16","year":2023,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Environmental Defense Fund","Boston University","Dartmouth College","First Street Foundation","Resources for the Future","City University of New York"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-023-01594-8","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The analysis reveals significant overvaluation of residential properties in the U.S. housing market, specifically those at risk of flooding, estimated between $121 to $237 billion. This overvaluation is mostly prevalent in coastal areas with inadequate flood risk disclosure laws, disproportionately affecting low-income households and municipalities dependent on property tax revenues. The findings emphasize the need for improved flood risk communication and policy changes to address the financial vulnerabilities imposed by climate change and flooding.","insights":["Residential properties exposed to flood risk are overvalued by $121 to $237 billion.","Overvaluation is particularly evident in coastal counties with lenient or absent flood risk disclosure laws.","Low-income households face heightened risk of losing home equity due to potential property price deflation.","The cumulative annual losses from flooding are projected to rise significantly by 2050, increasing economic strain on property owners.","Properties outside the Special Flood Hazard Areas (SFHAs) are significantly overvalued, highlighting systemic issues in flood risk communication.","The inadequacies of the National Flood Insurance Program's pricing methodology may exacerbate the mispricing of flood risks.","Policy changes regarding flood risk disclosure and housing could lead to either gradual price adjustments or abrupt market corrections.","Improved access to flood risk data for homebuyers may enhance purchasing decisions and market pricing accuracy.","Local governments heavily reliant on property taxes may encounter fiscal shortfalls if property values decline."],"keywords":["flood risk","housing market","economic vulnerability","overvaluation","property valuation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":478,"title":"Miami-Dade County and Municipalities Receive over $39 Million for Resilience Projects from the State of Florida","authors":"","date":"2023-02-07","year":2023,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Miami-Dade County","Florida Department of Environmental Protection"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.miamidade.gov/global/release.page?Mduid_release=rel1675805464368942","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Miami-Dade County and its Resilient305 partners, the cities of Miami and Miami Beach, received over $39 million from the Resilient Florida Grant Program on 6 February 2023, out of 75 grants awarded statewide. Seven of those grants went to Miami-Dade County, with $16,975,000 going directly to county-led projects. The four county projects are raising the banks of secondary canals feeding the C-8 Canal and Biscayne Canal, restoration of Environmentally Endangered Lands at Matheson Hammock, stormwater improvements at the Regional West Waste Transfer Station, and protecting the Virginia Key Wastewater Treatment Plant from sea level rise and storm surge. Miami Beach received funds to adapt the West Avenue neighborhood to sea level rise and to build a retention lake in Bayshore Park; Miami received money for flood improvements in Auburndale. The county frames the awards against the flood risks identified in its Sea Level Rise Strategy and says it continues to seek support for water and sewer protection, land acquisition and natural area restoration.","insights":["Of the $39 million awarded across the Resilient305 partnership, $16.975 million went to county-led projects, so most of the money reached municipal rather than county applicants, which is the distribution a state competitive programme produces when cities apply separately.","Two of the four county projects protect single pieces of water infrastructure, the Virginia Key Wastewater Treatment Plant and the Regional West Waste Transfer Station, which is where a utility's own capital plan and a state resilience grant meet.","The county names its Sea Level Rise Strategy as the document that identified the risk the grants address, so the state grant is buying implementation of a plan the county had already paid to write.","The release lists water and sewer protection, land acquisition and natural area restoration as still-unfunded needs, which is a public statement of the gap the state programme did not close."],"keywords":["Resilient Florida Grant Program","Resilient305","Miami-Dade County","sea level rise","stormwater","Virginia Key","state competitive grant"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":628,"title":"Goldman Sachs 2023 TCFD Report","authors":"Goldman Sachs","date":"2023-01-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global","US","Asia"],"institutions":["Goldman Sachs","Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures","Bloomberg Philanthropies","Asian Development Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.goldmansachs.com/our-commitments/sustainability/tcfd-report-2023/report.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Goldman Sachs' 2023 firmwide TCFD report, its first since 2021, structured on the four TCFD pillars: Governance, Strategy, Risk Management, and Metrics and Targets. The sustainability strategy rests on three pillars, work with clients, management of the firm, and partnerships that address market gaps. On strategy it sets out the Sustainable Finance Framework, the client-facing groups carrying climate expertise, and the firmwide $750 billion sustainable finance commitment. On risk it describes a three lines of defence model, climate risk identification and assessment, and the integration of climate risk into financing and portfolio management alongside other environmental considerations. On metrics it reports progress against 2030 sectoral targets, the operational and supply chain commitment and GHG emissions, and is candid about data challenges. Named execution examples include bookrunning General Motors' first green bond, the Horizon platform, and the Climate Innovation and Development Fund, a blended finance facility with Bloomberg Philanthropies and the Asian Development Bank that financed an 88 megawatt Vietnamese wind farm and bus electrification in Vietnam and India. The report is a transition document throughout: it addresses the impact of climate change on the business and of the business on the climate, and contains no adaptation or physical-resilience programme.","insights":["**It is a transition document throughout, with no adaptation or physical-resilience programme in it**: resilience, adaptation, catastrophe and insurance each appear zero times across 178,428 characters. What it discloses is exposure to the transition, not to the hazard.","It is a **worked example of what a large US bank actually discloses under TCFD**, filed against the four pillars, so it shows the level of specificity a firm chooses when the framework is voluntary.","**The Climate Innovation and Development Fund is the one blended-finance structure here with named deals**: a bank, a philanthropy and a development bank sharing risk to move capital into markets that had priced it out, financing an 88 megawatt Vietnamese wind farm and bus electrification in Vietnam and India.","The report says plainly that data availability and quality still limit what can be measured, which is a disclosure about the limits of its own metrics rather than a claim about performance."],"keywords":["TCFD","climate disclosure","sustainable finance commitment","sectoral targets","blended finance","transition risk","green bond","three lines of defence"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":560,"title":"Sustainable and Resilient Cities of Tomorrow","authors":"Meridiam","date":"2023-01-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Africa","Europe"],"institutions":["Meridiam","Rockefeller Foundation","United Nations Capital Development Fund"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.meridiam.com/assets/sustainable-and-resilient-cities-of-tomorrow/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An impact story from the infrastructure investor Meridiam describing The Urban Resilience Fund, launched in 2021 with the Rockefeller Foundation and the United Nations Capital Development Fund to help large and emerging cities in Europe and Africa respond to urbanisation and climate vulnerability. The fund is structured in three parts: a EUR 20 million catalytic capital fund for project preparation, a EUR 150 million investment fund for OECD countries and a EUR 350 million investment fund for non-OECD countries, specifically Africa. The framing is that project preparation, not capital, is the binding constraint: cities lack both infrastructure expertise and the financial resources to scope potential projects. Worked examples span nature-based coastal defences in Nouakchott, Mauritania, protecting some 75,000 people in low-lying areas from flooding caused by rising sea levels, fibre connections in Indiana and Montabaur, school and childcare spaces in Espoo, Finland, and bus rapid transit in Dakar.","insights":["The EUR 20 million catalytic tranche sits ahead of the two investment funds and pays for project preparation, which is the explicit answer to the stated constraint: cities are not short of capital so much as short of bankable projects, and the fund structure puts money where that gap is.","Splitting the investment funds by OECD and non-OECD membership, EUR 150 million and EUR 350 million, allocates more than twice as much to the non-OECD side, which inverts the usual pattern of adaptation capital concentrating where risk is best priced.","In the Nouakchott case the nature-based coastal defence is delivered through a public-private company with an institutional investor on the equity side rather than through a public works budget.","The document states no publication date. The record carries a January seed date that the document does not confirm."],"keywords":["Urban Resilience Fund","catalytic capital","project preparation","nature-based coastal defence","Nouakchott","urban infrastructure","blended finance","UNCDF"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":320,"title":"The Cascading and Compounding Impacts of Drought","authors":"","date":"2023-01-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction","World Bank","World Economic Forum","American Planning Association (APA)","OECD","National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration","United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification","International Drought Resilience Alliance","National Drought Mitigation Center","University of Nebraska"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.unccd.int/sites/default/files/2023-12/20231207_The%20cascading%20and%20compounding%20impacts%20of%20drought_DRAFT_V4.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This policy brief from the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification (UNCCD), the International Drought Resilience Alliance, and the National Drought Mitigation Center (University of Nebraska) synthesizes the cascading and compounding socioeconomic, ecological, and financial impacts of drought globally. It frames drought as systemic risk (affecting agriculture, energy, transportation, and ecosystems), and outlines a three-pillar resilience framework (early warning, vulnerability assessment, risk mitigation) with sector-specific and whole-of-society recommendations, including blended finance, gender-responsive planning, nature-based solutions, and land degradation neutrality as investment priorities.","insights":["Drought is one of the most expensive natural disasters globally, causing widespread crop loss, land degradation, reduced hydropower production, and broad socioeconomic losses across large areas over time, without leaving the defined destruction path of hurricanes or earthquakes, making financial quantification harder and advocacy weaker.","The Horn of Africa case study illustrates compounding systemic risk: five consecutive drought seasons (2020\u20132022), record UN FAO Food Price Index in 2022 (driven partly by the Ukraine war), and drought-induced food insecurity affecting 23 million people, demonstrating how remote geopolitical events can amplify local drought impacts for the most financially vulnerable populations.","Women spend a collective 200 million hours per day collecting water globally (UNICEF), representing an enormous hidden productivity and health cost that is rarely captured in drought damage assessments or investment cases, creating a measurable equity and economic efficiency argument for water infrastructure in underserved areas.","Vulnerability to drought is framed as substantially a function of social and political resource-allocation decisions, including investments in human capital, environmental systems, and infrastructure, meaning resilience investments can be designed and targeted, not merely reactive.","Nature-based solutions (forest and watershed protection/restoration, land degradation neutrality, riparian buffers) are identified as cost-effective multi-benefit interventions that simultaneously reduce climate risk, improve soil water retention, and deliver ecosystem co-benefits, key attributes for blended finance structuring."],"keywords":["drought systemic risk","cascading hazards","land degradation neutrality","gender-responsive planning","food-water-energy nexus","drought early warning","nature-based solutions drought","compounding climate risk"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":84,"title":"California Wildfire Innovation Fund","authors":"","date":"2023-01-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Blue Forest"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.blueforest.org/finance/blue-forest-asset-management/california-wildfire-innovation-fund/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The California Wildfire Innovation Fund (CWIF) aims to mitigate wildfire risks in California by providing flexible financing to companies and entrepreneurs focused on forest restoration and wildfire prevention. It represents a commitment to industry innovation that protects communities and the environment while also generating financial returns.","insights":["The CWIF is designed to reduce wildfire risks across California.","It offers flexible financing targeted at forest restoration and wildfire mitigation.","The initiative is seen as a responsibility to minimize the impact of climate-induced disasters.","Investments in the CWIF are expected to yield competitive financial returns.","The fund aligns economic viability with environmental and social goals.","Significant investments from organizations like CSAA Insurance Group underline its importance.","The fund represents a sustainable approach to addressing critical environmental challenges.","The initiative highlights the growing trend of financing solutions in climate-related sectors."],"keywords":["wildfire prevention","forest restoration","sustainable solutions","investment innovation","environmental challenges"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":83,"title":"FRB Catalyst Facility","authors":"","date":"2023-01-01","year":2023,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Blue Forest"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.blueforest.org/finance/forest-resilience-bond/catalyst-facility/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The FRB Catalyst Facility is an investment initiative aimed at accelerating funding for various forest restoration projects across the Western U.S., with a focus on preventing catastrophic wildfires and addressing climate challenges. By pooling investments, it plans to finance over $50 million in restoration work while offering returns to investors.","insights":["The FRB Catalyst Facility aims to expedite financing for multiple landscape restoration projects.","It is designed to mitigate the risks of wildfires exacerbated by climate change.","The initiative will potentially finance over $50 million in restoration efforts.","Projects are being implemented in multiple states, including Oregon, California, and Washington.","The facility operates on a pooled investment model that allows for broader financing capabilities.","Active restoration efforts are highlighted in forests like Eldorado, Tahoe, and Rogue River-Siskiyou.","The facility's structure is intended to attract and provide returns for diverse investors.","The approach emphasizes both community safety and environmental sustainability."],"keywords":["investment","wildfire","forest restoration","funding","nonprofit"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":619,"title":"Decoupled Net Present Value: Protecting Assets Against Climate Change Risk by Consistently Capturing the Value of Resilient and Adaptable Investments","authors":"David Espinoza; Javier Rojo; William Phillips; Andrew Eil","date":"2022-12-09","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Geosyntec Consultants"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/23789689.2022.2148453","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An open access article in Sustainable and Resilient Infrastructure, volume 8 supplement 1 pages 323 to 336 for 2023, published online 9 December 2022, by David Espinoza, Javier Rojo, William Phillips and Andrew Eil, on decoupled net present value as a way of protecting assets against climate change risk by consistently capturing the value of resilient and adaptable investments. The method addresses a defect in conventional appraisal: a single risk-adjusted discount rate conflates the time value of money with compensation for risk, and because resilience benefits arrive late and reduce variance rather than raise expected return, standard net present value systematically undervalues them. Decoupling the two components allows the value of resilience and of adaptability, meaning the option to adjust later, to be carried through the calculation rather than discounted away.","insights":["**Decoupling the discount rate is the technical move.** A conventional net present value applies one rate to every year of a project's life, which drives the value of avoided losses in distant years to nearly nothing; the method separates the discounting of protection benefits from the discounting of ordinary cash flows so that a long-horizon benefit survives appraisal.","Treating adaptability as an option with value is the second half of the argument and the more radical one. Conventional appraisal penalises a design that can be changed later, because flexibility costs money up front and shows no return; an option-aware valuation pays for it.","A consulting engineering firm rather than an academic economics department produced this, so the method was built to be used inside project appraisal rather than to be argued in a journal.","It bears directly on the co-benefit gap the Miami-Dade plan network record and the CRA guidebook both identify. A park that is also a stormwater asset has a benefit stream that a single discount rate flattens, and decoupled discounting is a way to keep it."],"keywords":["decoupled net present value","discount rate","real options","adaptability","asset valuation","resilient investment","project appraisal"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":294,"title":"The Impact of Climate Risk on Corporate Credit Risk","authors":"Francesca Bell, Gary van Vuuren","date":"2022-11-22","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Cogent Economics & Finance"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/23322039.2022.2148362","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This 2022 peer-reviewed article from Cogent Economics & Finance develops a quantitative framework for translating climate physical and transition risk shocks into corporate probabilities of default (PD). Using Geometric Brownian Motion (GBM) to simulate 10,000 equity price paths and the KMV/Merton distance-to-default model, the authors construct a scaling factor matrix showing how climate shocks of varying severity (20%\u201380% equity price drops) amplify PDs across credit grades (AAA through CCC/C). The paper focuses on Africa as the most climate-exposed region and produces calibration outputs directly applicable to IFRS-9 provisioning and Basel regulatory capital calculations.","insights":["A 60% climate shock applied to an A-rated corporate produces a ~4x spike in implied PD; the PD recovers roughly exponentially to pre-shock levels within five years absent further shocks, providing a concrete time-decay profile for credit loss provisioning.","Multiple mild shocks (\u226520% equity price drop) arriving at annual or sub-annual frequency cause inevitable corporate default regardless of price drift or post-shock volatility, illustrating that shock frequency matters as much as severity for credit quality.","The scaling factor matrix quantifies PD amplification as a function of both shock size and initial (unshocked) PD: impacts on AAA-rated credits are negligible but scaling factors reach ~4x for CCC/C-rated credits under an 80% physical shock, with transition shocks producing slightly lower but comparable multipliers.","Physical climate shocks increase post-event equity volatility, compounding PD deterioration, while transition shocks (e.g., carbon tax announcements) do not increase volatility because they are anticipated, a distinction with direct implications for how banks should model each risk type under BCBS/EBA frameworks.","Africa faces the most severe climate-driven credit deterioration: Eastern and Western African GDP per capita growth is forecast to fall 15% below baseline by 2050 under a high-warming scenario (+4\u00b0C by 2100), with losses in high-warming scenarios 50%\u201385% higher than low-warming scenarios by 2050 for Central and Western Africa respectively.","EBA Pillar III ESG disclosure standards (mandatory for Basel-compliant banks from 1 January 2024, including Green Asset Ratio reporting) create a regulatory deadline that makes this calibration framework operationally relevant for near-term compliance."],"keywords":["probability of default","KMV model","distance-to-default","ESG credit risk","physical risk","transition risk","African climate exposure","IFRS-9 provisioning"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":243,"title":"Towards a Climate-Risk Data Architecture: Common and Open Risk Metrics to Align Finance with Climate-Resilient Development Goals","authors":"Jenty Kirsch Wood, Nicola Ranger, Benedikt Signer","date":"2022-11-14","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction","UK Centre for Greening Finance and Investment (CGFI)","Global Resilience Index Initiative (GRII)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.undrr.org/quick/75220","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The document discusses the urgent need for a global climate-risk data architecture that provides open and standardized risk metrics to enhance climate adaptation finance. It underscores initiatives like the Global Resilience Index Initiative (GRII) and the UNDRR's Risk Information Exchange (RiX), which aim to facilitate effective financial decision-making aligned with climate-resilient development goals. By promoting common metrics, the resource advocates for improved fiscal resilience, investment strategies, and better integration of climate-related data across sectors.","insights":["A new climate-risk data architecture is crucial for mobilizing finance for climate adaptation and resilience.","Common metrics facilitate the integration of physical climate risks into financial decision-making.","The GRII and RiX initiatives aim to create a shared, open architecture that consolidates risk data.","Standardized climate-risk metrics help assess and compare the resilience benefits of different investments.","Climate stress testing is vital for understanding the resilience of financial institutions to climate impacts.","Data gaps exist in the availability, reliability, and comparability of climate-related financial metrics.","Collaborative efforts among global stakeholders are necessary to close data gaps and enhance climate-risk analytics."],"keywords":["risk metrics","data architecture","investment strategy","risk assessment","infrastructure resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":589,"title":"New York Voters Pass Historic 2022 Environmental Bond Act","authors":"Environmental Defense Fund","date":"2022-11-09","year":2022,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["Environmental Defense Fund","New York State"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.edf.org/media/new-york-voters-pass-historic-2022-environmental-bond-act","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An Environmental Defense Fund press release of 9 November 2022 on New York voters' passage of the Clean Water, Clean Air and Green Jobs Environmental Bond Act the previous day. At $4.2 billion it is the largest environmental bond act in New York State history and was the largest measure on any ballot anywhere in the nation in 2022, and it is expected to support environmental improvements preserving, enhancing and restoring the state's natural resources while creating more than 84,000 local jobs. The release credits a coalition of more than three hundred environmental organisations, unions and business groups, and carries a statement from Kate Boicourt, EDF's Director of Climate Resilient Coasts and Watersheds for New York and New Jersey, framing the vote as New Yorkers saying yes to clean water, clean air, reduced flooding, environmental justice and jobs.","insights":["A **$4.2 billion voter-approved general obligation bond**, authorised by referendum rather than raised in a market, which makes the instrument accountable to the people who will service it.","Reduced flooding is one of five stated purposes alongside clean water, clean air, environmental justice and jobs, so the resilience share of the $4.2 billion is undetermined here. That multi-purpose structure is what gets an environmental bond passed and what makes its resilience benefit hard to attribute.","**This is a stakeholder statement rather than the instrument's own documentation.** The Environmental Defense Fund was part of the coalition that campaigned for the act, so the framing of the result is an advocate's."],"keywords":["environmental bond act","general obligation bond","ballot measure","New York State","green jobs","flood reduction","voter authorisation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":509,"title":"Toward Holistic Coastal Resilience: A Cross-Government Assessment of Louisiana\u2019s Resilience Challenges and Opportunities","authors":"","date":"2022-11-07","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","Louisiana"],"institutions":["State of Louisiana"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://coastal.la.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/AGI-Report-Combined.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A cross-government assessment of Louisiana's resilience challenges and opportunities, produced after Governor Edwards joined ten other states in 2020 by naming a Chief Resilience Officer to advance a cross-agency approach to environmental hazards in the coastal area. After two years of engagement, vulnerability assessments and exploration of adaptation options, the report finds that environmental change in coastal Louisiana is producing direct and indirect consequences for the core missions and functions of every agency in state government, and that the challenges ahead will not be limited to the coastal region. Its central recommendation is that addressing these needs requires a formal and continued emphasis on resilience-building across all hazards and the entire state through permanent positions, structures and processes, with a single focal point for statewide effort, in order to avoid damage to infrastructure and assets, preserve economic and workforce development, sustain the natural environment, protect vulnerable people and cultures, and secure funding to implement adaptation. On vulnerability, the most common exposure across agencies is to buildings and assets such as transportation infrastructure, facilities, vehicles and equipment, which affects both budgets and service delivery; service-providing agencies additionally face scattered clients, damage to their own employees' homes, and new post-disaster demands for programming with no additional staff, while disasters damage the state's attractiveness to business and its workforce retention.","insights":["The finding that matters for finance is indirect: disasters damage a state's attractiveness to business and its workforce retention, which is a revenue effect on the state's own tax base rather than a repair cost, and it does not appear in a damage estimate.","Naming a single focal point and permanent positions is a request for institutional capacity rather than money, which is the same diagnosis Marin County acted on and the same one the Brookings report finds explains why some states plan and others do not.","Service-providing agencies face a compounding failure the asset inventory misses: their clients scatter, their own staff lose homes, and demand rises with no new staff, so the delivery capacity falls exactly when it is needed.","A Chief Resilience Officer appointed in 2020 puts Louisiana among the earliest states to create the role, which is the institutional precondition a statewide adaptation funding strategy needs before it can be written."],"keywords":["Chief Resilience Officer","cross-agency assessment","state vulnerability","Louisiana","coastal resilience","institutional capacity"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":588,"title":"Massachusetts Municipal Vulnerability Preparedness (MVP) Program: State Case Study","authors":"State Resilience Partnership","date":"2022-11-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","Massachusetts"],"institutions":["Commonwealth of Massachusetts"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://stateresilience.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/Massachusetts-MVP-Program-Case-Study_04.03.2023.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A November 2022 case study of the Massachusetts Municipal Vulnerability Preparedness programme, created in 2017 under Executive Order 569 issued by Governor Charlie Baker in 2016, which provides technical assistance to help communities assess climate and natural hazard vulnerabilities and develop strategies to address them. The programme has expanded to cover 97 per cent of Massachusetts municipalities and is frequently used as a template by other states, including Rhode Island. The problem it addresses is stated with numbers: over 50 per cent of the state's residents live along the coast and face a projected one to three feet of sea level rise by 2050, and inland flooding from extreme precipitation, ice jams and dam failure is among the most common hazards. The design has two parts in sequence. Communities first apply for planning grants to run workshops that assess vulnerabilities, gather community input and identify adaptation strategies. Having completed those workshops they are designated MVP Communities and may then apply for action grants to fund the strategies identified, typically one to two year projects covering infrastructure redesign and retrofits or local bylaw updates that use nature-based solutions such as stormwater parks, with an emphasis on equity, forward-looking data and natural systems.","insights":["**The sequencing is the instrument.** A planning grant is the gate to an action grant, and designation is the token that passes it, so the state buys a pipeline of appraised projects rather than fielding unprepared applications. That is how a public funder manufactures bankability, and it is transferable to any concessional lender facing the same problem.","97 per cent municipal coverage is the number that makes this more than a programme description: near-universal take-up means the planning stage is not self-selecting toward already-capable communities, which is the usual failure of a competitive planning grant.","The programme runs in two stages, a planning grant that produces a municipal vulnerability assessment followed by an action grant that funds what the assessment identifies, so the money is released against a plan the municipality has already made rather than against an application.","Action grants explicitly exclude studies and plans, so the second stage buys construction rather than more analysis. Combined with the planning-grant gate, the programme separates the two kinds of spending that public resilience funding usually conflates."],"keywords":["Municipal Vulnerability Preparedness","planning grant","action grant","MVP Community designation","Executive Order 569","stormwater park","sea level rise","state resilience program"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":513,"title":"Southeast Florida Regional Climate Action Plan 3.0","authors":"","date":"2022-11-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Southeast Florida Regional Climate Change Compact","Miami-Dade County","Broward County","Palm Beach County","University of Miami","Florida International University","The Nature Conservancy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://southeastfloridaclimatecompact.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/SEFL_RCAP3_Final.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Regional Climate Action Plan 3.0 is the Southeast Florida Regional Climate Change Compact's five-year update, setting goals, recommendations and supporting strategies across 11 focal areas: agriculture, energy, equity, informed and engaged communities, natural systems, public health, public policy advocacy, regional economic resilience, risk reduction and emergency management, sustainable communities and transportation, and water. It serves Broward, Miami-Dade, Monroe and Palm Beach counties, 109 municipal governments, the Seminole Tribe of Florida and the Miccosukee Tribe of Indians of Florida, a regional population of more than 6.2 million. The twin objectives are net-zero greenhouse gas emissions by 2050 against a 2005 baseline and strengthened adaptive capacity and climate resilience for the region's communities, institutions and economy.","insights":["The plan serves four counties, 109 municipal governments and two sovereign tribal nations across a regional population of more than 6.2 million, which is the governance unit that makes it a regional plan rather than a county one.","It was developed with more than 150 named subject matter experts drawn from county staff, universities, utilities, chambers of commerce, federal agencies and community organisations, and each of the 11 focal areas carries a named chapter lead from a member county.","Regional Economic Resilience is a focal area in its own right alongside Risk Reduction and Emergency Management, which separates the economic continuity question from the emergency management one rather than folding the first into the second.","The plan is explicitly iterative, on a five-year update cycle, and positions itself as a springboard for harmonised regional action while leaving implementation to local conditions, so its recommendations are not binding on member governments."],"keywords":["regional climate action plan","Southeast Florida","adaptive capacity","regional economic resilience","sea level rise"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":453,"title":"Marsh McLennan Report: Mobilizing Insurance Sector Capabilities to Advance Community-Level Climate Risk Reduction and Adaptation","authors":"","date":"2022-11-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Marsh","United Nations Climate Change High-Level Champions","Adrienne Arsht-Rockefeller Foundation Resilience Center"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.guycarp.com/insights/2022/11/marsh-mclennan-mobilizing-insurance-sector-capabilities.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A joint report of the UN Climate Change High-Level Champions, the UN Race to Resilience, the Adrienne Arsht-Rockefeller Foundation Resilience Center and Marsh McLennan, presented at COP27 as the Sharm el-Sheikh Resolution on Insurance Sector Leadership on Climate Risk Reduction and Adaptation. Its argument is that the insurance sector should expand its role beyond risk transfer into actively advancing community-level climate risk reduction and adaptation. The High-Level Champions' statement of purpose frames the sector as uniquely positioned because it is built on the mathematics of risk, and calls on it to assemble the tools, networks and capabilities to de-risk, finance and accelerate community-level risk reduction, invoking the industry's legacy of enhancing societal resilience against new and complex risks. The operative phrase is that insurers should help communities cope with climate change not just through innovative risk transfer mechanisms but by actively deploying their capabilities to flatten the climate risk curve. The report carries a capabilities framework and a set of pioneer projects as supporting materials, and sits within the Champions' 2030 Adaptation Outcomes for Finance.","insights":["The ask is explicitly beyond risk transfer: insurers are called on to deploy their analytical and network capabilities to reduce risk, not only to price and carry it, which reframes the sector as an adaptation actor rather than a counterparty.","Flattening the climate risk curve is the framing metaphor, and it makes the sector's commercial interest and the public interest point the same way, since a lower loss curve preserves insurability.","A capabilities framework plus pioneer projects is the structure of the report, so it is a template and a set of precedents rather than an argument, which is what makes it usable.","Presenting this as a COP27 resolution gives it standing as a sector commitment rather than a consultancy view, though the commitments are voluntary.","Marsh McLennan is the publisher, so the delivery-model proposal comes from a broker with a commercial position in the market it is describing."],"keywords":["insurance sector capabilities","community-level risk reduction","COP27","Race to Resilience","de-risking","adaptation finance","flattening the risk curve"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":583,"title":"Climate Gentrification in Miami: A Real Climate Change-Minded Investment Practice?","authors":"Han Li; Richard J. Grant","date":"2022-10-10","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of Miami","Federal Emergency Management Agency","University of Johannesburg"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cities.2022.104025","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A paper in Cities by Han Li and Richard J. Grant of the University of Miami, published online 10 October 2022, testing whether climate gentrification in Miami is genuinely a climate-change-minded investment practice. Their answer is that it is not. Homebuyers' awareness of sea level rise is not the major factor pushing buyers to higher ground: climate gentrification in Miami is better explained as a rational economic response to expensive flood insurance than to sea level rise itself. Buyers are steered by the flood insurance FEMA requires and by the historical record of flooding, rather than by elevation or by scientific projections of storm surge. Unlike other global cities, Miami's wealthy and well-educated homebuyers show fewer concerns about rising seas. The authors also find that climate gentrification is being used by some developers and both interpreted and misinterpreted by local policymakers to facilitate neoliberal redevelopment and place marketing, while the real estate industry fails buyers by not providing accurate sea level rise information. They propose two policy instruments: include sea level rise risk assessment in homebuyer contracts, and improve FEMA insurance maps to incorporate sea level rise scenarios.","insights":["The finding inverts the usual causal story: the transmission from climate risk to housing markets runs through **insurance pricing**, not through risk perception. Buyers are not pricing the hazard, they are pricing the premium, which means the instrument is doing the work the information was supposed to do.","That has a direct consequence for adaptation finance. If premiums are the signal, then anything that suppresses premiums, subsidy, rate caps, or a map that omits future risk, also suppresses the adaptation response, and the record names the specific artifact: FEMA maps that do not incorporate sea level rise scenarios.","Historical flooding beats projected flooding in buyer behaviour. A model that prices 2050 exposure is not what the market is reading.","The two remedies the paper proposes are both disclosure rather than financing: a risk assessment in the purchase contract, and better maps. Neither moves capital; both change what the price means."],"keywords":["climate gentrification","flood insurance","sea level rise","Miami","housing prices","FEMA maps","risk perception","homebuyer disclosure"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":618,"title":"Developing Adaptive Resilience in Infrastructure Systems: An Approach to Quantify Long-Term Benefits","authors":"Prerna Singh; Adjo Amekudzi-Kennedy; Baabak Ashuri; Mikhail Chester; Samuel Labi; Thomas A. Wall","date":"2022-09-29","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Georgia Institute of Technology","Arizona State University","Purdue University","Argonne National Laboratory"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23789689.2022.2126631","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An open access article in Sustainable and Resilient Infrastructure, volume 8 supplement 1 pages 26 to 47 for 2023, published online 29 September 2022, by Prerna Singh, Adjo Amekudzi-Kennedy and Baabak Ashuri with Mikhail Chester, Samuel Labi and Thomas A. Wall, on developing adaptive resilience in infrastructure systems and an approach to quantifying its long-term benefits. The treatment is hazard-specific rather than abstract, with hurricane the recurring case. The paper has been viewed 4,036 times and cited 10 times.","insights":["Adaptive resilience is a distinct concept from resilience as robustness, and the distinction has a financing consequence: a system designed to change as conditions change has a benefit stream that only appears over a long horizon, which is exactly the stream a single discount rate destroys.","The paper's target is the quantification of long-term benefit, and it is explicit that what counts as a benefit is itself contested rather than given, which is why the method spends its effort on defining the benefit stream before discounting it.","The author list spans civil engineering, transport and a national laboratory, which places the work in infrastructure asset management rather than in finance, so its units are engineering ones rather than financial."],"keywords":["adaptive resilience","infrastructure systems","long-term benefit quantification","asset management","hurricane","system adaptability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":621,"title":"Data-Driven Parametric Insurance Framework Using Bayesian Neural Networks","authors":"Subeen Pang; Chanyeol Choi","date":"2022-09-12","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","California","Texas"],"institutions":["Massachusetts Institute of Technology"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2209.05307","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An arXiv preprint, DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2209.05307, by Subeen Pang and Chanyeol Choi of MIT and Wecover Platforms, proposing a data-driven parametric insurance framework built on Bayesian neural networks. Their argument is that traditional insurance risk models rest on statistical analyses that are inaccurate and becoming more flawed as climate change makes weather more erratic and extreme, and that data-driven parametric insurance can supplement traditional cover. They use the deep sigma point process, a Bayesian neural network approach, for the data analysis part of parametric insurance, with residential internet connectivity dropout in the United States as the case study.","insights":["**The case study is the interesting choice**: residential internet connectivity dropout as the index. It is a continuously measured, nationally available proxy for infrastructure disruption that needs no weather station and no damage survey.","A Bayesian approach gives calibrated uncertainty rather than a point estimate, which matters for a parametric trigger specifically: the quantity an underwriter needs is the distribution of basis risk, not its expected value.","The authors' framing is that traditional statistical risk models are becoming more flawed as weather grows more erratic, and the Bayesian approach is offered as an answer to that non-stationarity rather than as a refinement of fit.","It is a preprint and should be cited as one: the contribution to take from it is the trigger idea and the method rather than a peer-reviewed result."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","Bayesian neural network","deep sigma point process","connectivity dropout","alternative trigger","non-stationarity","machine learning underwriting"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":76,"title":"Homebuyers with Access to Flood-Risk Data Bid on Lower-Risk Homes","authors":"Lily Katz, Daryl Fairweather, Sebastian Sandoval-Olascoaga","date":"2022-09-12","year":2022,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Redfin","University of Southern California","National Bureau of Economic Research","Massachusetts Institute of Technology"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.redfin.com/news/redfin-users-interact-with-flood-risk-data/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A study by Redfin revealed that access to flood-risk data significantly influences homebuyers' decisions, leading them to bid on homes with 54% lower flood risk. The randomized controlled trial demonstrated that users who were initially interested in homes with severe flood risks shifted toward lower-risk options once they were informed about the risks involved, with particular trends observed in flood-prone areas like Cape Coral, FL, and Houston, TX.","insights":["Homebuyers with access to flood-risk data are more likely to consider lower-risk properties.","Redfin users who initially viewed homes with high flood-risk scores shifted to bidding on homes with significantly lower risk after accessing relevant data.","The study involved a randomized controlled trial of 17.5 million users, half of whom had access to flood-risk scores.","There is a growing awareness among homebuyers of the impact of climate risk on property values.","Users in flood-prone areas were more likely to engage with flood-risk information when searching for homes.","Home values in flood-prone regions could decline as awareness of flooding risks increases.","Areas with lower flood risk may experience rising home values as buyers seek safer options.","Online search behaviors shifted, with homebuyers becoming more selective in their housing searches when provided with flood-risk data.","The experiment's findings suggest stronger buyer demand for homes with minimal flood risk, especially as the market cools."],"keywords":["real estate","homebuyer behavior","flood risk","housing market","data influence"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":601,"title":"California Senate Bill 852","authors":"California State Legislature","date":"2022-09-09","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["California State Legislature"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://legiscan.com/CA/text/SB852/id/2606300","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The text of California Senate Bill 852, Chapter 266, introduced by Senator Dodd and approved by the Governor and filed with the Secretary of State on 9 September 2022, adding Division 6 commencing with Section 62300 to Title 6 of the Government Code and titled Climate resilience districts: formation: funding mechanisms. It authorises a city, a county, a city and county, a special district, or any combination of those entities to form a climate resilience district for the purpose of raising and allocating funding for eligible projects and the operating expenses of eligible projects. Each district is deemed to be an enhanced infrastructure financing district and must comply with the existing law governing those districts except as specified, and a district may finance only projects meeting the bill's definition of an eligible project. The digest sets that against existing law, under which a legislative body may establish an enhanced infrastructure financing district to finance public capital facilities or other projects of communitywide significance including projects that enable communities to adapt to the impacts of climate change, financed among other ways by the issuance of bonds serviced by property tax increment revenues, with a public financing authority established as the district's governing board before the district is formed and an infrastructure financing plan adopted.","insights":["**The statute invents no new instrument.** By deeming a climate resilience district to be an enhanced infrastructure financing district, it borrows an existing financing machinery, bonds serviced by property tax increment revenue, and points it at adaptation. That reuse is why it was passable.","The mechanism has an uncomfortable logic: tax increment financing is repaid out of rising property values inside the district, so a resilience project funds itself by making the protected area more valuable, and the repayment structure therefore depends on the displacement pressure it creates.","Allowing a combination of entities to form a single district is a deliberate answer to jurisdictional mismatch: flood risk does not respect municipal boundaries, and under this statute the financing district does not have to either.","Funding operating expenses as well as capital projects is unusual and important. Most public resilience money buys construction and leaves maintenance unfunded, which is how a green stormwater asset degrades into a liability."],"keywords":["climate resilience district","enhanced infrastructure financing district","tax increment financing","property tax increment","SB 852","eligible project","operating expenses"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":125,"title":"Sinking Tax Base: Land & Property at Risk from Rising Seas","authors":"","date":"2022-09-08","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Climate Central"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatecentral.org/report/sinking-tax-base","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report 'Sinking Tax Base' by Climate Central assesses the impact of sea level rise on the tax base of coastal U.S. counties, highlighting the potential loss of taxable properties as rising waters alter boundary lines between public and private land. This shift threatens local tax revenues, which are crucial for funding schools and services, with projections indicating that over 648,000 tax parcels could be affected by mid-century, resulting in significant financial implications.","insights":["Over 648,000 tax parcels in coastal U.S. counties could be partly below tidal boundaries by mid-century.","Approximately 4.4 million acres of land are at risk from rising sea levels.","More than 48,000 properties may be entirely submerged below tidal boundaries.","Florida, Louisiana, and Texas are the most affected states in terms of the number of impacted properties.","Rising sea levels may cause significant shifts in property values and local tax revenues.","At least $108 billion in assessed property value is at risk by 2100 due to sea level rise.","Densely-developed areas along the coast are reaching a tipping point as sea levels rise.","The report includes over 250 individual county-level analyses to assess risks more accurately."],"keywords":["sea level rise","tax base","coastal governance","property boundaries","economic implications"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":508,"title":"Pragmatic Cost\u2013Benefit Analysis for Infrastructure Resilience","authors":"Russell M. Wise, Tim Capon, Brenda B. Lin, Mark Stafford-Smith","date":"2022-09-05","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD)"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-022-01468-5","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Wise and colleagues argue that infrastructure investment must account for climate change, but that most day-to-day projects are small and poorly served by economic assessment processes, and propose four simple adjustments to cost-benefit practice. They note strong convergence between the World Bank, the OECD, the UK National Infrastructure Commission and Infrastructure Australia on two points: that resilient infrastructure is robust, profitable and urgent, with the extra cost as little as 3 per cent of overall investment against at least a fourfold return, and that technical and institutional capacity to manage climate risk must be developed alongside access to information and tools, specifically cost-benefit analyses that properly value resilience and consider risk and uncertainty. The scale problem is the core of the argument: investment plans are large in aggregate, the US Bipartisan Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act committed $1.2 trillion and low- and middle-income countries spend around $1 trillion a year, but they are implemented through many small projects run by local planners who often lack the capability for complex appraisal. The US bill included $110 billion for aging roads and bridges, with more than 43,500 of about 620,000 bridges considered structurally deficient; Frankston City, one of 537 Australian local councils, spends nearly half its annual budget on 706 km of roads, 996 km of pathways, 950 km of pipes and drains, 160 bridges, 331 buildings and 525 parks. The paper names the well-known limitations of how cost-benefit analysis is normally deployed, how discount rates are applied, how residual values are treated, the timeframe of analysis and the inclusion of social and environmental benefits, and observes that comprehensive solutions exist but are complicated for practitioners in real institutional settings.","insights":["The argument is about the unit of decision rather than about the method: a $1.2 trillion programme is spent through thousands of small projects appraised by local planners, so improving national guidance does nothing unless the fix is simple enough for a council to apply.","3 per cent extra cost for a fourfold return is the resilience premium stated plainly, and it is why the authors treat the binding barrier as appraisal capability rather than economics.","The four named limitations, discount rate, residual value, timeframe and omitted social and environmental benefits, all bias the same way, against long-lived resilience investment, so the errors compound rather than cancel.","Framing this as needing four SIMPLE adjustments rather than a better method is the practical contribution: the sophisticated approaches already exist and are not being used, which makes adoption the problem.","The Frankston City inventory is a useful reminder of what a resilience capital plan is actually made of at the local level: pipes, pathways and small bridges, not headline megaprojects."],"keywords":["cost-benefit analysis","discount rate","residual value","infrastructure resilience","appraisal capability","resilience premium"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":78,"title":"Global Finance and Urban Adaptation: Governing Climate Risk in Miami, Florida","authors":"Savannah P. Cox","date":"2022-09-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["ProQuest"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4nv0c0d0","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource 'Global Finance and Urban Adaptation: Governing Climate Risk in Miami, Florida' explores the complex interplay between global finance and urban adaptation strategies to address climate risks in Miami. It emphasizes the necessity of effective governance, public-private partnerships, and community engagement in developing sustainable urban infrastructure. The document highlights the importance of innovative financial mechanisms, adaptive governance structures, and collaborative frameworks to enhance resilience against climate impacts such as rising sea levels and extreme weather events.","insights":["Financial mechanisms are critical for funding urban adaptation projects to mitigate climate risks.","Effective governance structures must be adaptive and inclusive to tackle climate-related challenges.","Public-private partnerships are essential for mobilizing resources and expertise in urban adaptation efforts.","Community engagement is crucial for developing sustainable urban policies that address local needs.","Innovative financial instruments are needed to fund urban adaptation initiatives sustainably.","Data-driven decision-making enhances the effectiveness of adaptation strategies in urban environments.","Collaborative governance involving local communities and stakeholders increases adaptation efforts' success.","Investment in climate-resilient infrastructure is vital, particularly in coastal cities like Miami."],"keywords":["urban infrastructure","governance","sustainability","resilience strategies","stakeholder engagement"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":554,"title":"Resilience in the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act: What Funding Opportunities Does IIJA Provide for Regional Networks?","authors":"Georgetown Climate Center","date":"2022-08-18","year":2022,"resource_type":"Tool / Resource Hub","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Georgetown Climate Center","United States Department of Transportation","United States Department of Energy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.georgetownclimate.org/adaptation/toolkits/resilient-infrastructure-investments/what-funding-opportunities-does-iija-provide-for-regional-networks.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Georgetown Climate Center toolkit page inventorying the resilience and adaptation programmes created by the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act, Public Law 117-58, and asking specifically which of them are open to regional collaboratives rather than to single jurisdictions. Each entry carries the public law section number and page, the administering agency, the total appropriation with its year-by-year split, the list of eligible entities, whether Justice40 applies, and what the page calls the programme's collaborative feature. The largest is PROTECT, Promoting Resilient Operations for Transformative, Efficient, and Cost-saving Transportation, at $8.7 billion split $7.3 billion formula and $1.4 billion competitive. Others include the Electric Grid Reliability and Resilience Research, Development and Demonstration Program at $1 billion over fiscal years 2022 to 2026, the Healthy Streets programme at $500 million for cool and porous pavements and tree cover, and Transportation Resilience and Adaptation Centers of Excellence at $250 million in planning grants and $750 million in capital construction grants.","insights":["The page's organising question is not how much money exists but who may apply together. It sorts programmes by whether the collaborative feature sits in the eligible-entity list or in the programme's own stated purpose, which is the distinction a regional collaborative with no legal authority of its own actually needs.","PROTECT's $7.3 billion formula share dwarfs its $1.4 billion competitive share, so most of the largest federal transportation resilience programme reaches states without a competition, and the collaborative route runs through multi-state or multijurisdictional joint applications.","Georgetown's framing of regional climate collaboratives, drawn from its own Lessons in Regional Resilience, is that they are loose membership networks with no legal authority that build capacity and make recommendations, which is why eligibility language decides whether they can touch this money at all."],"keywords":["Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act","PROTECT","formula grant","competitive grant","regional climate collaborative","Justice40","grid resilience","Centers of Excellence","federal resilience funding"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":290,"title":"Climate-Related Risks in Financial Assets","authors":"Emanuele Campiglio, Louis Daumas, Pierre Monnin, Adrian von Jagow","date":"2022-07-22","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["University of Bologna","RFF-CMCC European Institute on Economics and the Environment","CIRED","Ecole des Ponts ParisTech","Council on Economic Policies (CEP)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/joes.12525","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This 2023 Journal of Economic Surveys article by Campiglio, Daumas, Monnin, and von Jagow provides a comprehensive critical review of empirical and theoretical literature on how climate-related risks (physical, transition, and liability), affect the pricing of financial assets across equities, bonds, loans, and real estate. The paper develops a four-channel transmission framework (assets, investment, production network, credit), reviews backward-looking evidence showing predominantly negative asset price effects, and surveys forward-looking stress tests that find share price declines up to 60% and earnings at risk up to 140% under disorderly transition scenarios. Appendix tables catalog study-level findings on carbon intensity, EU-ETS compliance premia, and policy-driven cost-of-capital shifts. The authors conclude that financial markets currently underprice climate risks and call for expanded network-effect modeling and improved disclosures.","insights":["Financial markets systematically underprice climate-related risks: backward-looking studies show that new climate information predominantly leads to negative asset price effects across equities, bonds, loans, and real estate, yet most papers find these effects are smaller than efficient pricing would require.","Four transmission channels carry climate costs to asset prices: (1) physical/transition destruction of capital assets (stranding); (2) forced infrastructure investment (CapEx increases); (3) supply chain and demand disruptions (revenue/opex impacts); and (4) credit channel repricing (higher interest rates and insurance premia).","Forward-looking stress tests report starker losses than long-term scenario models: share price declines of 20\u201360% under 'too late, too sudden' transition scenarios (Ralite & Thom\u00e4 2019), earnings at risk up to 140% for utilities (Trucost 2019), and equity losses amplified 2\u20133\u00d7 when interbank network second-round effects are included (Battiston et al. 2017).","Physical risk drivers predominantly reduce real estate prices: SLR exposure discounts properties by 7% (Bernstein et al. 2019), hurricane inundation areas show 36\u201348% markdowns (Atreya & Ferreira 2015), and a one-standard-deviation increase in SLR exposure raises municipal bond issuance costs by 23.4 basis points (Painter 2020).","A 'carbon premium' is documented empirically: higher corporate carbon emissions are associated with higher stock returns across sectors and continents (Bolton & Kacperczyk 2021), while scope-1 emissions increase cost of debt by 38\u201362 basis points per standard deviation (Jung et al. 2018); investors demand compensation rather than penalizing emitters through lower valuations."],"keywords":["physical risk pricing","transition risk","stranded assets","carbon premium","climate stress testing","asset mispricing","EU-ETS compliance","green credit ratio"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":200,"title":"Climate\u2011resilient Finance and Investment: Framing Paper","authors":"Michael Mullan, Nicola Ranger","date":"2022-07-21","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["OECD"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1787/223ad3b9-en","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Climate-resilient finance and investment: Framing paper' emphasizes the urgent need to align financial flows with climate resilience goals amidst growing climate challenges. It highlights the necessity for increased funding for adaptation, the redirection of current investments away from vulnerabilities, and the development of frameworks like the EU Taxonomy to assess adaptation-aligned finance. The paper underscores the importance of public and private collaboration, robust risk management, and the establishment of metrics for evaluation, all essential for fostering sustainable financial practices that contribute to climate-resilient development.","insights":["Aligning financial flows with climate resilience is critical for adapting to climate change impacts.","Public and private finance currently involves significant investments that must be redirected to support climate-resilient activities.","Strong inter-sectoral collaboration is essential for effective adaptation and resilience planning.","Investors increasingly demand adaptation-aligned finance, focusing on both financial returns and positive climate outcomes.","Metrics for adaptation alignment are still underdeveloped and require further methodological advancement."],"keywords":["sustainable development","financial instruments","investment strategy","public-private collaboration","risk assessment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":122,"title":"Return on Investment for Mangrove and Reef Flood Protection","authors":"Michael W. Beck, Nadine Heck, Siddharth Narayan, Pelayo Men\u00e9ndez, Borja G. Reguero, Stephan Bitterwolf, Saul Torres-Ortega, Glenn-Marie Lange, Kerstin Pfliegner, Valerie Pietsch McNulty, I\u00f1igo J. Losada","date":"2022-05-17","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Florida","Caribbean"],"institutions":["Ecosystem Services","University of California Santa Cruz","East Carolina University","Universidad de Cantabria","World Bank","ERM","The Nature Conservancy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoser.2022.101440","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The article discusses the economic feasibility of restoring coral reefs and mangroves for flood protection in over 20 Caribbean countries, emphasizing the strong Return on Investment (ROI) these ecosystems can provide for coastal adaptation. Despite the challenges of insufficient environmental funding and high restoration costs, the study finds that investing in these natural defenses could yield substantial long-term benefits and identifies potential funding sources to support such initiatives.","insights":["Coral reefs and mangroves provide significant flood protection benefits and can yield strong ROI for restoration projects.","Restoration costs for mangroves in the Caribbean can vary significantly, with the median cost being $23,000 per hectare, while Florida has much higher costs at $45,000 per hectare.","Tax districts and federal disaster recovery funds are underutilized sources for financing habitat restoration projects.","The study indicates robust potential for immediate flood risk reduction benefits from mangrove and coral reef restoration, affecting plans for disaster risk management.","Sensitivity analyses reveal that ROI remains largely positive across various discount rates and restoration benefit timelines.","Investment opportunities exist in regions such as Cuba, Bahamas, and Jamaica, where cost-effective restoration of coastal habitats can significantly reduce flood risk.","The article highlights a funding gap in ocean conservation, with only $4 to $10 billion annually being spent compared to an estimated $100 billion needed.","Restoration projects could lead to exponential benefits over time, which may enhance future funding opportunities for natural infrastructure.","Combining reef and mangrove restoration can maximize flood risk reduction, enabling better coastal resilience."],"keywords":["ecosystem services","flood risk","natural infrastructure","restoration costs","Caribbean"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":577,"title":"The Hidden Value of Trees: Quantifying the Ecosystem Services of Tree Lineages and Their Major Threats Across the Contiguous US","authors":"Jeannine M. Cavender-Bares; Erik Nelson; Jose Eduardo Meireles; Jesse R. Lasky; Daniela A. Miteva; David J. Nowak; William D. Pearse; Matthew R. Helmus; Amy E. Zanne; William F. Fagan; Christopher Mihiar; Nicholas Z. Muller; Nathan J. B. Kraft; Stephen Polasky","date":"2022-04-05","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Minnesota","University of Miami","United States Forest Service","Carnegie Mellon University","Bowdoin College","Temple University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pstr.0000010","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article in PLOS Sustainability and Transformation, published 5 April 2022, quantifying the ecosystem services of US tree lineages and the threats those services face, by fourteen authors across the University of Minnesota, Bowdoin, Maine, Penn State, Ohio State, the USDA Forest Service, Imperial College, Temple, the University of Miami, Maryland, the US Forest Service, Carnegie Mellon and UCLA. Combining macroevolutionary and economic valuation approaches with spatially explicit species and lineage data, the authors find that five key ecosystem services generated by US trees are worth $114 billion a year, with a low of $85 billion and a high of $137 billion in 2010 dollars. The non-market value from carbon storage and air pollution removal far exceeds the commercial value from wood products and food crops. Two lineages, pines and oaks, account for 42 per cent of that value. On threats, the majority of species face climate change pressure, many face increasing fire risk, and known pests and pathogens threaten 40 per cent of total woody biomass, with the most valuable species among the most threatened: the species most valuable for carbon storage are the ones most at risk from increasing fire threat.","insights":["The correlation between value and threat is the finding that matters, and it is the opposite of a diversified portfolio: the species carrying the most carbon value are the ones most exposed to fire, so the asset and its risk are concentrated in the same lineages.","Non-market value exceeding commercial value by a wide margin is the structural reason this asset class is underfinanced. The revenue a landowner can capture is the small part, which is the gap a payment-for-ecosystem-services instrument exists to close.","Forty per cent of total woody biomass threatened by known pests and pathogens is a biological exposure rather than a climate hazard in the usual sense: slow, chronic, and driven by a living agent.","Two lineages at 42 per cent of value is a concentration figure an underwriter would recognise immediately, and it makes a national tree-service valuation behave like a portfolio with two dominant positions.","The $114 billion is a per annum flow covering only the five services with adequate data, so it is a floor on the benefit rather than a total."],"keywords":["ecosystem services","non-market valuation","carbon storage","air pollution removal","tree lineages","pests and pathogens","fire risk","natural capital"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":386,"title":"National Flood Insurance Program: The Current Rating Structure and Risk Rating 2.0","authors":"Diane P. Horn","date":"2022-04-04","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","U.S. Army Corps of Engineers (USACE)","National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration","Wharton Risk Center","World Bank","Congressional Research Service"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R45999","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Congressional Research Service report (R45999, updated April 2022) explains the National Flood Insurance Program's legacy rating structure and documents FEMA's Risk Rating 2.0 overhaul, the most significant premium methodology change since 1968. The NFIP covers more than 5 million policies across 22,500 communities, collecting ~$4.6 billion in annual premiums against $1.3 trillion in coverage. Risk Rating 2.0, effective October 2021 for new policies and April 2022 for renewals, shifts from zone-based group pricing to property-specific actuarial rates incorporating replacement cost value, distance to water, and multiple flood types, with statutory caps of 5\u201318% annual increases for primary residences.","insights":["Risk Rating 2.0 projects premium increases for 77% of NFIP policyholders and immediate decreases for 23%, with FEMA estimating 50% of policies will reach full risk-based rates within 5 years and 90% within 10 years, signaling a decade-long repricing of U.S. residential flood risk.","The current national average NFIP premium is $818.70, but properties subject to 25% annual increases (non-primary residences, severe repetitive loss, etc.) carry an average premium of $5,878.15, a 7x gap that illustrates the cross-subsidy embedded in the program.","FEMA set a first-year premium cap of $12,125 for single-family primary residences under Risk Rating 2.0, down from a prior maximum of $45,925, a structural affordability floor with no parallel funding mechanism, creating a fiscal exposure FEMA lacks authority to remedy.","75% of primary residences would face increases greater than 18% annually if statutory caps did not exist, underscoring that actuarial risk far exceeds what statute currently permits to be charged, and that the program remains structurally under-reserved.","As of September 2018, approximately 13% of NFIP policies received a pre-FIRM subsidy, ~9% were grandfathered, and ~4% received a newly-mapped subsidy, together roughly 26% of the portfolio is cross-subsidized, representing a material pricing distortion for capital allocators assessing flood-exposed assets.","Risk Rating 2.0 expands flood risk sources modeled from two (fluvial and coastal) to include pluvial, tsunami, Great Lakes, coastal erosion, and leveed-area flooding, broadening the actuarial basis and potentially reclassifying millions of properties currently outside Special Flood Hazard Areas."],"keywords":["flood insurance pricing","actuarial risk rating","NFIP subsidies","floodplain management","catastrophe modeling","premium affordability","replacement cost value","Risk Rating 2.0"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":288,"title":"Incorporating Climate Risk in to Strategic Asset Allocation","authors":"Gavin Conn, Sohini Chowdhury","date":"2022-04-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Moody's"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.moodys.com/web/en/us/insights/resources/ins_5_incorporating-climate-risk-into-strategic-asset-allocation.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This April 2022 Moody's Analytics whitepaper outlines a five-step framework for climate-aware Strategic Asset Allocation (SAA) for insurance companies and institutional investors: target setting, asset universe definition, scenario analysis, portfolio optimization, and metric analysis. It describes how climate scenario analysis (using NGFS-aligned orderly, disorderly, and hot-house pathways), can be embedded into SAA and ALM processes, and introduces Moody's Climate Pathway Scenario Service and temperature alignment data covering ~4,400 publicly listed companies as tools to translate climate pathways into financial risk variables.","insights":["Insurance companies face a dual challenge: aligning investment portfolios with net-zero commitments while managing climate physical and transition risks that affect GDP, inflation, interest rates, and equity risk premium simultaneously.","The UK Prudential Regulatory Authority found 'significant variation between firms in translating climate science into targets and scenarios, on modeling climate-related market risk and capital modeling,' signaling that methodological standardization is still nascent and creates differentiated risk for allocators.","UK pension schemes (large) were required as of 1 October 2021 to report on at least three climate metrics: an absolute emissions metric, an emissions intensity metric, and one additional metric such as portfolio alignment or Climate Value-at-Risk, establishing a regulatory precedent that is likely to spread to insurers.","Moody's temperature alignment data assesses forward-looking emissions trajectories for approximately 4,400 large publicly listed companies, providing a quantifiable metric for comparing portfolio warming potential against established climate pathway indices.","A combined top-down (macroeconomic) and bottom-up (sector/company) climate scenario approach is recommended over top-down alone, as purely top-down analysis can net out regional and sectoral impacts, particularly in diversified portfolios, a methodological nuance material to allocators pricing physical and transition risk.","Three NGFS-derived scenarios (Orderly, Disorderly, and Hot House), anchor the Bank of England's biennial climate stress testing exercise (launched June 2021), and the same scenarios are available through Moody's Climate Pathway Scenario Service for insurer use in SAA and ALM."],"keywords":["strategic asset allocation","net zero investment","NGFS scenarios","climate value at risk","temperature alignment","ESG integration","transition risk","insurance investment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":557,"title":"Resilience Leaders Highlight Climate Adaptation Pathways for Affordable Housing","authors":"Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety","date":"2022-03-31","year":2022,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety","Louisiana Office of Community Development","Connecticut Housing Finance Authority","Fannie Mae","Enterprise Community Partners","Habitat for Humanity","Federal Home Loan Bank of Dallas"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://ibhs.org/ibhs-news-releases/resilience-leaders-highlight-climate-adaptation-pathways-for-affordable-housing/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A news release from the Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety on its first Making Affordable Housing Climate Ready roundtable, held 31 March 2022, which brought public and private participants together on how to encourage, support, incentivise and finance resilient construction in affordable housing. IBHS president Roy Wright frames the problem as a financing one: the science to build homes that withstand severe weather exists, but individual homeowners often cannot act on it, so the financial avenues have to be found to bring resilience into every construction and re-roofing project. Pat Forbes of the Louisiana Office of Community Development describes the state's use of resilient standards including FORTIFIED in state-funded affordable housing, naming the Audubon Estates, The New Isle and Les Maisons de Bayou Lafourche resettlements, and argues that publicly funded rebuilding must incorporate standards like FORTIFIED. The panel included the Connecticut Housing Finance Authority multifamily division, Fannie Mae's multifamily green financing business, Enterprise Community Partners and Habitat for Humanity Mississippi Gulf Coast.","insights":["The document is thin but its map is specific: it names the institutions that sit between a building standard and the capital that would pay for it, a state community development office, a state housing finance authority, a GSE multifamily green financing desk and a CDFI-adjacent nonprofit.","Louisiana's position is that disaster-prone geography and vulnerable population overlap, so the state applies resilient standards in publicly funded affordable housing rather than leaving the standard to the individual homeowner. That is a procurement answer to an insurance problem.","FORTIFIED appears here as a condition on public rebuilding money rather than as an insurance discount, which is a different transmission mechanism for the same standard."],"keywords":["FORTIFIED","affordable housing","resilient construction","housing finance authority","multifamily green financing","Louisiana","building standards","re-roofing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":435,"title":"Ready-to-Fund Resilience Toolkit","authors":"","date":"2022-03-16","year":2022,"resource_type":"Tool / Resource Hub","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["American Society of Adaptation Professionals"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://adaptationprofessionals.org/ready-to-fund-resilience-toolkit/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A practitioner toolkit built by the American Society of Adaptation Professionals with Climate Resilience Consulting, funded by the Climate Resilience Fund, aimed squarely at small and mid-sized local government staff and the organisations that support them. Its subject is not what to build but how to make a resilience project fundable: the toolkit is organised around ten characteristics of ready-to-fund projects, and argues that projects which secure funding are those benefiting from collaborative partnerships, intentional processes, comprehensive accounting practices, and enabling regulatory and policy frameworks. Alongside a funding and finance primer it carries quick reference sections on policy incentives, risk management mechanisms, financing mechanisms, discounting alternatives, US federal resilience funding sources and the characteristics of potential partners. The framing is deliberately operational: it teaches local government leads to work within the finance and policy systems that exist, by pulling policy levers, seeking partnerships, using innovative accounting practices, inverting power structures and redesigning internal processes.","insights":["The toolkit's premise is that fundability is a property of the project's preparation rather than of the hazard it addresses, which relocates the bottleneck from capital availability to project design.","Discounting alternatives get their own reference section, which is unusual and consequential: the discount rate is what decides whether a long-dated avoided-loss benefit can justify present spending.","The audience is explicitly small and mid-sized local governments, the jurisdictions least able to fund a dedicated resilience finance function, so the toolkit is a substitute for capacity rather than a supplement to it.","Comprehensive accounting practices are named as one of the four traits of a fundable project, which is the same point PRE Collective and others make: the benefit has to appear on somebody's balance sheet before it can be financed."],"keywords":["ready-to-fund","project preparation","local government","resilience funding","discounting","financing mechanisms","technical assistance","ASAP"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":67,"title":"Paying for the Paris Agreement: A Primer on Government Options for Financing Nationally Determined Contributions","authors":"Molly Caldwell, Natalia Alayza, Gaia Larsen","date":"2022-03-11","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wri.org/research/paying-paris-agreement-primer-government-options-financing-nationally-determined","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report explores financing options for Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) needed to meet global climate goals outlined in the Paris Agreement. It emphasizes the urgent need for governments to increase financial flows, utilize different financing instruments, and monitor their success in implementing climate commitments, especially in developing countries where the financial need is significant.","insights":["Substantial financial shifts are necessary for implementing NDCs effectively.","Global mitigation investment must increase 36 times by 2030 to limit warming to 2 C.","Developing countries have an urgent financial need totaling $4.3 trillion for climate action.","Governments should create financing strategies that outline funding requirements and governance structures.","A mosaic of actions, rather than a single solution, is needed to meet climate targets.","The process of financing NDCs varies greatly between high- and low-income countries.","Establishing systems to track financial flows is crucial for monitoring progress towards climate goals.","There is a challenge in aligning public finance with climate commitments across different contexts.","Various financial instruments need to be mobilized to channel funds towards climate objectives."],"keywords":["Nationally Determined Contributions","financial instruments","climate commitments","funding strategy","financial flows"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":613,"title":"This Changes Everything: Climate Shocks and Sovereign Bonds","authors":"Serhan Cevik; Joao Tovar Jalles","date":"2022-02-03","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["International Monetary Fund","Universidade de Lisboa","Nova School of Business and Economics"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S014098832200041X","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This Changes Everything: Climate Shocks and Sovereign Bonds, by Serhan Cevik of the IMF and Joao Tovar Jalles of Lisbon, in Energy Economics volume 107, article 105856, available online 3 February 2022 and copyright the International Monetary Fund. The paper's starting observation is that there is a large literature on the economic consequences of climate change and scarce research on the link between climate change and sovereign risk. It investigates the impact of climate change vulnerability and resilience on sovereign bond yields and spreads across 98 advanced and developing countries over 1995 to 2017, and finds that both have a significant effect on the cost of government borrowing after controlling for the conventional determinants of sovereign risk: countries more resilient to climate change have lower bond yields and spreads than countries with greater vulnerability. Partitioning the sample shows the magnitude and statistical significance of the effect are much greater in developing countries, which have weaker capacity to adapt to and mitigate the consequences of climate change.","insights":["**Resilience lowers the cost of sovereign borrowing, measured across 98 countries over 22 years.** The benefit of resilience is not only avoided loss, it is a cheaper yield curve, which means a government can in principle finance adaptation out of the spread it saves.","The effect being larger in developing countries cuts against the usual pattern of climate finance. The places with least access to capital are the places where resilience does most for the cost of it, which is the strongest available argument that concessional support for adaptation pays for itself in market terms.","The authors are explicit that the gap they fill is the sovereign one: physical risk had been priced at asset and company level, and this is the same exercise at the level of the state."],"keywords":["sovereign bonds","bond yields and spreads","climate vulnerability","climate resilience","cost of government borrowing","sovereign risk","developing countries"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":491,"title":"Lightsmith Group Closes Inaugural $186M Growth Equity Fund \u2014 First Focused on Climate Resilience and Adaptation","authors":"The Lightsmith Group (founding partners Jay Koh, Sanjay Wagle)","date":"2022-01-31","year":2022,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Lightsmith Group","Green Climate Fund","Rockefeller Foundation","European Investment Bank","Climate Policy Initiative","Pegasus Capital Advisors"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://lightsmithgp.com/news-posts/lightsmith-group-closes-inaugural-186-million-growth-equity-climate-fund-the-first-to-focus-on-climate-resilience-and-adaptation/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Lightsmith Group announced the final close of Lightsmith Climate Resilience Partners at $186 million of commitments, the first private equity fund focused on climate resilience and adaptation, investing in growth-stage technology companies that address the physical effects of climate change. Investors include PNC Insurance Group, the Rockefeller Foundation, Kinneret Group, Caprock Impact Partners, the Green Climate Fund, the European Investment Bank, the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank, KfW on behalf of the German development ministry, the Nordic Development Fund and the Government of Luxembourg. The Green Climate Fund committed $46 million to become the largest investor, with Pegasus Capital Advisors as its accredited entity. Climate Policy Initiative concluded the fund is the first private equity fund focused on climate resilience and reported that less than $500 million a year of private investment had targeted adaptation. The fund targets six technology areas: water efficiency and smart water management, resilient food systems, agricultural analytics, geospatial intelligence, supply chain analytics, and catastrophe risk modeling and risk transfer, against an estimated addressable market above $170 billion. Initial investments are SOURCE Global, an off-grid water harvesting company, and WayCool Foods, an Indian agriculture and food supply chain services company. The strategy derives from CRAFT, the Climate Resilience and Adaptation Finance and Technology-transfer facility, developed with the Global Innovation Lab for Climate Finance and others.","insights":["The capital stack is the point: a $186 million commercial private equity fund anchored by $46 million from the Green Climate Fund plus four other development finance institutions and a sovereign, which is blended finance used to establish an asset class rather than to subsidise a project.","CPI's finding that under $500 million a year of private capital targeted adaptation makes this single $186 million fund a material share of the entire private adaptation market at the time of close.","Two of the six target areas, catastrophe risk modeling and risk transfer and geospatial intelligence, are the infrastructure other resilience instruments depend on, so the fund is partly investing in the measurement layer that would let adaptation be priced.","The GCF's stated rationale is technology transfer to developing countries, which is a different objective from the fund's commercial return thesis, and the fund has to satisfy both.","The fund was assembled through a public facility, CRAFT, developed with the Global Innovation Lab for Climate Finance, so the design cost was carried by concessional money before any commercial capital was raised."],"keywords":["Lightsmith","CRAFT","Green Climate Fund","adaptation private equity","blended finance","anchor investor","catastrophe risk modeling"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":442,"title":"Unlocking Clean Water State Revolving Funds to Finance Resilience Projects Across the Great Lakes Region","authors":"Katy Hansen; Shaun Roberts; Sanjiv K. Sinha","date":"2022-01-26","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Environmental Policy Innovation Center","Cornell University","Great Lakes Protection Fund"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.policyinnovation.org/insights/srf-green-infrastructure","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A report on redirecting Clean Water State Revolving Fund money toward resilience and green stormwater infrastructure across the Great Lakes states. It sets out the scale of the programme: established by Congress in 1987, CWSRFs have provided more than $145 billion to finance more than 42,800 projects nationwide, and in FY2021 Congress appropriated a further $11.7 billion under the $1.2 trillion infrastructure law, an amount the authors note is well short of need but significant in catalysing public and private investment. Their starting premise is that water infrastructure, grey or green, is expensive and mostly financed through the municipal bond market, with intergovernmental aid an important additional source of capital. The report reviews how CWSRF dollars have actually been spent across the Great Lakes states and proposes greener priorities intended to shift environmental, economic, public health and social outcomes. The hazard case is made concretely: per NOAA Atlas 14, the 100-year rainfall in the Detroit area rose by nearly 0.8 inches, from 4.4 to 5.2 inches, between 1992 and 2019.","insights":["The lever is allocation rather than appropriation: the CWSRF already exists at $145 billion cumulative and $11.7 billion in a single year, so the report's proposition is to change what the existing revolving fund prioritises rather than to ask for new money.","Green stormwater infrastructure is positioned as the eligible use that delivers environmental, economic, public health and social outcomes together, which is the argument for preferring it inside a programme designed for water quality.","The municipal bond market is named as the primary financing route with the revolving fund as intergovernmental aid layered on it, so the two instruments are complements and the SRF's role is to lower the cost of the bond-financed project.","The rainfall change is given as a design-standard change, the 100-year Detroit event rising from 4.4 to 5.2 inches between 1992 and 2019, which is what makes existing systems undersized rather than merely old."],"keywords":["Clean Water State Revolving Fund","green stormwater infrastructure","Great Lakes","water infrastructure finance","NOAA Atlas 14","revolving loan fund","municipal bonds"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":631,"title":"Innovative Financing for Natural Infrastructure and Climate Adaptation in Mississippi River Basin","authors":"Quantified Ventures; Environmental Defense Fund","date":"2022-01-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures","Environmental Defense Fund"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/edf-qv-report-natural-infrastructure-mississippi-river-basin","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A joint press release of 3 November 2022 from Environmental Defense Fund and Quantified Ventures announcing their report 'Generating revenue to finance natural infrastructure projects in the Mississippi River Basin'. The framing is that the basin supplies drinking water to 20 million people, supports 65 per cent of US harvested cropland and more than a million jobs, and is imperilled by two linked problems, poor water quality and lengthening periods of drought and flood, both worsened by climate change. Restoring wetlands and floodplains and deploying other natural infrastructure can protect drinking water and reduce flood risk, but such projects have historically been hard to finance through public loan programmes such as the Clean Water State Revolving Fund because they lack a dependable revenue source. The report gives states, cities, towns and watershed groups five replicable ways to generate revenue and so make natural infrastructure financeable.","insights":["**The stated blocker is the absence of a revenue stream, not the absence of capital.** The Clean Water State Revolving Fund is a loan programme and a loan needs something to repay it; grey infrastructure has a rate base and a restored floodplain does not.","**It is the press release, not the report.** The five revenue mechanisms are named in the report rather than enumerated here, so this document carries the framing and the citation rather than the mechanisms themselves.","The scale framing, 20 million people's drinking water and 65 per cent of harvested cropland, is the argument for treating the Mississippi basin as infrastructure of national consequence, which is how a federal funding case would have to be made."],"keywords":["natural infrastructure","Mississippi River Basin","Clean Water State Revolving Fund","revenue generation","floodplain restoration","water quality","flood risk"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":580,"title":"The SEC\u2019s Climate Disclosure Rule: Critiquing the Critics","authors":"George S. Georgiev","date":"2022-01-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Securities and Exchange Commission","Emory University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4068539","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An article by George S. Georgiev of Emory University School of Law, published at 50 Rutgers Law Record 101 in 2022, analysing the principal legal challenges to the SEC's March 2022 proposed climate disclosure rule and finding them unpersuasive. The framing is that climate change entails a wide variety of physical risks as well as sizeable but underappreciated economic risks, and that the proposed rule addresses information gaps about how those risks affect firms. The article records that the proposal met opposition unusual in its intensity and consistency, with critics disputing the SEC's statutory authority and motivations, questioning the materiality of information about the economic impacts of climate change, and advancing administrative and constitutional law theories drawn from unrelated contexts. Reviewing the features of the traditional disclosure regime and of the rule itself, the author concludes that the SEC has the statutory authority to act, that its motivations are neither improper nor novel, that materiality properly understood presents no obstacle, and that the major questions and compelled speech theories are misplaced here. The author notes he co-authored a June 2022 comment letter to the SEC with Jill Fisch, Donna Nagy and Cynthia Williams on behalf of 30 corporate and securities law scholars.","insights":["The rule's core function as the author states it is to put in place an information-generating framework for capital markets, which is the upstream condition for pricing physical risk at all.","Materiality is the pivot. If information about the economic impacts of climate change is not material, the whole physical-risk disclosure project has no statutory hook, and the article's argument that materiality properly understood presents no obstacle is the legal load-bearing claim.","The article rebuts three constitutional challenges to the rule, two of them the major questions doctrine and a compelled-speech theory, so the obstacle it treats as binding is constitutional rather than technical or economic.","The author's disclosed role as a comment-letter co-author is provenance worth recording: this is advocacy scholarship by a participant in the rulemaking, which does not weaken the analysis but does place it."],"keywords":["SEC climate disclosure","materiality","major questions doctrine","compelled speech","statutory authority","notice-and-comment rulemaking","physical risk disclosure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":515,"title":"USDA Departmental Offices Climate Adaptation Plan 2022","authors":"","date":"2022-01-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["United States Department of Agriculture"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.usda.gov/sites/default/files/documents/13_DepartmentalOffices_ClimateAdaptationPlan_2022.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 2022 climate adaptation plan of four United States Department of Agriculture departmental offices, produced under Executive Order 14008 and USDA Departmental Regulation 1070-001, which directs the Office of the Chief Economist to require Mission Areas, agencies and staff offices to complete their own adaptation plans. The four offices covered are the Office of the Chief Economist, the Office of Budget and Program Analysis, the Office of Homeland Security, and the Office of Property and Environmental Management. Each assesses how climate change affects its own mission delivery and sets adaptation actions integrating those risks into planning, programmes, operations and management, alongside staff professional development needs, engagement with the USDA Climate Hubs, and alignment with departmental environmental justice initiatives.","insights":["Adaptation planning here is cascaded by regulation rather than by initiative: Departmental Regulation 1070-001 directs the Office of the Chief Economist to issue guidance requiring each contributing organisation to produce its own plan, which is the mechanism that turns one department-wide plan into many office-level ones.","The unit of analysis is mission delivery rather than estate or emissions, so each office asks how climate change affects the function it performs for the department rather than how to reduce its own footprint.","The Climate Change Program Office is responsible for ensuring agencies monitor and evaluate progress against their own plans and for reporting to the White House Council on Environmental Quality, which supplies the accountability loop the individual plans lack.","This is the departmental offices' plan and not the USDA-wide Action Plan for Climate Adaptation and Resilience released in October 2021, which it implements; a reader wanting the department-wide commitments needs that separate document."],"keywords":["federal adaptation planning","Executive Order 14008","USDA Climate Hubs","mission delivery risk","agency climate plans"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":475,"title":"Increasing Investment in Affordable Housing, Climate Resilience, and Small Business in Underserved Communities","authors":"","date":"2022-01-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Robert Wood Johnson Foundation","Kresge Foundation","Local Initiatives Support Corporation (LISC)","Rockefeller Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.rwjf.org/en/insights/our-research/2022/01/increasing-investment-in-affordable-housing-climate-resilience-and-small-business-in-underserved-communities.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Robert Wood Johnson Foundation provided a guarantee of up to $10 million to the Community Investment Guarantee Pool, a vehicle created by the Kresge Foundation that pools more than $42 million of unfunded guarantee commitments from 14 investors, 12 philanthropies, the health system Common Spirit and LOCUS's parent company Virginia Community Capital, with the intention of growing past $50 million. By aggregating unfunded guarantee commitments, CIGP uses the strength of philanthropic balance sheets, a relatively untapped resource, to unlock capital for affordable housing, small business and climate resilience in low-income communities and communities of color. The stated aims are to accelerate investment, give guarantee users more efficient access, demonstrate that a pooled model lets foundations better manage financial risk and gives intermediaries a one-stop shop for credit enhancement, and put foundation balance sheets to work. In its first full year of operation in 2020 CIGP deployed $1.5 million to Genesis LA for publicly supported supportive housing and naturally occurring affordable housing in Los Angeles County, $2 million to the Low Income Investment Fund backing a $20 million fund for affordable housing developers affected by COVID-19, and $3.6 million to the Local Initiatives Support Corporation for its $36 million Diverse Developer Loan Fund lending to minority-owned or led developers with limited balance sheets. Cumulative investments total $15.1 million in affordable housing and $1.5 million in small business. Other guarantors include the Chan Zuckerberg Initiative, the Annie E. Casey Foundation and CommonSpirit Health, with grant support from the Rockefeller, Hewlett and Wells Fargo foundations. CIGP is an LLC operated by the nonprofit consultancy LOCUS and a subsidiary of Virginia Community Capital, which has issued close to 800 loans totaling close to $1 billion.","insights":["The instrument is an unfunded guarantee, which is the point: a foundation pledges its balance sheet rather than moving cash, so a philanthropic commitment does credit enhancement work without leaving the endowment.","The leverage is visible in the deployments: $3.6 million of guarantee supports a $36 million loan fund and $2 million supports a $20 million fund, so the pool is operating at roughly ten to one on the guaranteed tranches.","Pooling is what makes it usable: an intermediary gets a one-stop shop for credit enhancement instead of negotiating with twelve foundations separately, and the transaction cost saved is the reason the vehicle exists rather than the subsidy.","Climate resilience is one of three mandates alongside affordable housing and small business, and the reported cumulative totals, $15.1 million in housing and $1.5 million in small business, name no climate deployment, so the resilience leg is authorised rather than exercised.","The vehicle is structured around the social determinants of health rather than around climate, which is how a health funder reaches resilience investment, and is a route into adaptation capital that does not run through climate finance at all."],"keywords":["Community Investment Guarantee Pool","unfunded guarantee","credit enhancement","philanthropic balance sheet","community development finance","pooled guarantee","Kresge Foundation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":61,"title":"Equitable Green Infrastructure in Buffalo, NY","authors":"","date":"2022-01-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures","Buffalo Sewer Authority"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/buffalo-eib","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Equitable Green Infrastructure initiative in Buffalo, NY, involves a $54 million Environmental Impact Bond aimed at enhancing green stormwater infrastructure to manage over 200 acres of impervious surfaces. By partnering with the Buffalo Sewer Authority, the project seeks to improve water quality, create jobs, and address environmental justice, while significantly increasing the pace of infrastructure installation to mitigate flooding risks and enhance community vitality.","insights":["The $54 million Environmental Impact Bond is the largest of its kind to date.","Green stormwater infrastructure (GSI) includes features like rain gardens and permeable pavement.","The project aims to increase the installation of green infrastructure from 9.5 acres to over 28 acres per year.","Buffalo's combined sewer system is vulnerable to combined sewer overflow events, especially during heavy rainfall.","The initiative is designed to create approximately 700 sustainable jobs in the community.","The focus areas for GSI projects are chosen based on both environmental needs and social equity considerations.","The Buffalo Sewer Authority is the first in the U.S. to link investment incentives to performance outcomes in such a bond.","The project also includes an option for early bond refinancing based on successful outcomes.","This initiative promotes the greening of the city while reducing stormwater fees for residents.","The EIB was recognized as the U.S. municipal green bond of the year."],"keywords":["green infrastructure","stormwater management","environmental justice","urban flooding","job creation"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":42,"title":"Blue Forest Asset Management (BFAM)","authors":"","date":"2022-01-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Blue Forest"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.blueforest.org/finance/blue-forest-asset-management/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Blue Forest Asset Management (BFAM) focuses on accelerating forest restoration through sustainable, community-based economic initiatives. The organization connects investors to diverse mission-aligned opportunities, while engaging in investments across the entire forestry value chain to enhance capacity for restoration and wildfire risk reduction, fostering workforce development in rural areas.","insights":["BFAM prioritizes sustainable forest restoration and community-based economic development.","The organization connects investors with mission-aligned investment opportunities beyond traditional projects.","Investments are made across the entire forestry value chain, from harvesting to utilization.","BFAM aims to build system capacity for achieving forest restoration and wildfire risk reduction goals.","The initiative promotes regenerative natural resource stewardship.","Workforce development and sustainable employment for rural communities are key components of BFAM's strategy.","BFAM highlights the importance of both scaling restoration work and developing a supportive economic framework.","The California Wildfire Innovation Fund (CWIF) is one of BFAM's related investment strategies."],"keywords":["forest restoration","sustainable employment","investments","community development","wildfire risk"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":8,"title":"Finance for Developing Country Climate Action","authors":"","date":"2022-01-01","year":2022,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Asia","Latin America","Oceania"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wri.org/initiatives/finance-developing-country-climate-action","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Finance for Developing Country Climate Action' project aims to promote financial support for climate initiatives in developing countries by addressing key capacity bottlenecks and facilitating access to multilateral climate funds. It works closely with national institutions to enhance coordination, decision-making, and alignment of climate-compatible projects with finance flows, which is essential for meeting Paris Agreement goals.","insights":["Developing countries often lack sufficient finance for climate action.","Identifying capacity bottlenecks at the national level is crucial for mobilizing finance.","Accessing multilateral climate funds is a key focus of the project.","Effective institutional coordination and decision-making is vital for climate finance.","The project aims to match climate-compatible projects with available finance flows.","Support is provided to finance and environment ministries in developing countries.","International finance mobilization is essential for enabling low-carbon pathways.","Knowledge products and tools are developed to help overcome financial constraints.","Collaboration with specialized climate funds enhances project implementation.","Past initiatives include analyzing funding strategies and the impacts of global events on climate finances."],"keywords":["capacity building","low-carbon pathways","multilateral funds","sustainable development","financial mobilization"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":301,"title":"Quantifying Insurance Benefits of a Nature-Based Approach to Reducing Risk: Wildfire Risk Reduction Buffers","authors":"","date":"2021-12-01","year":2021,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["The Nature Conservancy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.guycarp.com/insights/2021/12/wildfire-risk-reduction-buffers.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Using Paradise, California (devastated by the 2018 Camp Fire) as a case study, this Guy Carpenter report quantifies the insurance and property-loss benefits of nature-based wildfire risk reduction buffers. The authors apply a U.S. wildfire catastrophe model to assess how wildland buffer strategies, including ignition reduction and ember spotting mitigation, can lower expected insurance premiums and reduce property loss, with the goal of restoring insurability in communities currently deemed uninsurable. The framework is presented as replicable across other fire-dependent forest ecosystems in California and the broader western U.S.","insights":["The quantified result is a return-period shift rather than a percentage saving: fire buffer strategies combined with updated code enforcement would have cut value-at-risk across most probabilities by 42 percent, converting a one-in-100-year loss level into a one-in-350-year level.","A fire buffer along a single corridor in Paradise is found to be as effective at reducing fire risk as enforcing updated fire-resilient building codes, which puts a land-use intervention on a par with a building-standard intervention in the same catastrophe model.","The explicit purpose is to enable the risk transfer chain: the framework is offered as a way to make fire insurance accessible and affordable in areas currently viewed as uninsurable, so the benefit is expressed as avoided loss and lower expected premium.","Community-Based Catastrophe Insurance is named as the instrument that captures the benefit, with coverage arranged by the community for homes in its jurisdiction, so that community-scale mitigation can be reflected in pricing for insureds. Linking insurance to mitigation is what creates the incentive to invest.","The land-use corollary is that buffers doubling as urban growth boundaries could replace WUI sprawl with clustered development, which would in turn allow controlled burning to expand in fire-dependent ecosystems, restoring habitat while further reducing catastrophic fire risk.","The scale of the underlying loss is stated: the Camp Fire caused 86 deaths, destroyed 95 percent of the town's structures including 11,000 homes, and produced USD 12.5 billion insured and USD 16.5 billion total property loss, with only 500 homes rebuilt by the end of 2020."],"keywords":["Wildfire Risk Reduction","Risk analytics and modeling","Urban Greening","Building Code Adoption and Enforcement","Wildfire"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":485,"title":"A System for Resilience Learning: Developing a Community-Driven, Multi-Sector Research Approach for Greater Preparedness and Resilience to Long-Term Climate Stressors and Extreme Events in the Miami Metropolitan Region","authors":"Tiffany G. Troxler, Amy C. Clement, Katharine J. Mach, Jayantha Obeysekera, Shimon Wdowinski, and 25 others","date":"2021-11-11","year":2021,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of Miami Climate Resilience Institute","Florida International University","Miami-Dade County","Rockefeller Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1142/s2345737621500196","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A thirty-author collaboration spanning Florida International University, the University of Miami, Miami-Dade County, the cities of Miami and Miami Beach, and Miami civic organisations describes the design of a Resilience Learning System to support research on and implementation of the Resilient305 Strategy for Greater Miami and the Beaches. The strategy, developed over three years with support from the Rockefeller Foundation's 100 Resilient Cities programme, sets out 59 actions across resilient places, resilient people and resilient pathways. The system rests on three foundations: strong partnerships among community members, government and non-government leaders and researchers from multiple academic institutions; subject matter expertise and local knowledge to identify information and translational gaps, formulate metrics and evaluate outcomes from the community perspective; and a comprehensive understanding of civic engagement activities, technological tools and resilience-building capacities including policy and financial innovations. The paper reports initial co-produced metrics, mapping the strategy's forty intended outcomes to Core Area metrics and then reviewing them a second time to map metrics for unintended co-benefit outcomes, so triple dividends and unintended consequences can both be evaluated. On the funding context, nearly $1 billion in general obligation bond funding was approved in fiscal year 2018-19 by voters in Miami and Miami Beach, two of Miami-Dade County's thirty-four municipalities; in fiscal year 2020-21 the county's operating budget included nearly $705 million targeted at resilience and $3.285 billion of the $3.335 billion capital plan was dedicated to community resiliency. The Global Commission on Adaptation projects a 4:1 return from resilience investments over the next decade through triple dividends.","insights":["The system's purpose is measurement rather than money: it exists to establish whether $3.285 billion of dedicated capital spending is producing the resilience outcomes it was authorised for.","Mapping metrics twice, once for intended outcomes and once for unintended co-benefits, is the methodological move that makes triple dividends measurable rather than asserted, and it is what would let a co-benefit be pledged to an instrument.","The Miami Forever Bond and its Miami Beach counterpart, nearly $1 billion of general obligation borrowing approved by voters in two of thirty-four municipalities, is the concrete demonstration that a municipal balance sheet can carry adaptation when the electorate authorises it.","The paper insists that resilience trade-offs are as important as dividends and are anticipatable with better knowledge, which is the counterweight to a literature that reports only the upside of adaptation.","The author list itself is the finding about capacity: thirty authors across two universities, a county, two cities and six civic organisations were required to co-produce one metric set, which is the true cost of the community-driven approach.","Three co-authors write from the University of Miami and one from the Miami-Dade Office of Resilience, so the design is a joint academic and municipal product rather than an external evaluation."],"keywords":["Resilient305","Resilience Learning System","triple dividends","co-production","resilience metrics","Miami Forever Bond","100 Resilient Cities"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":462,"title":"Connecticut Public Law 21\u2013115: An Act Concerning Climate Change Adaptation","authors":"","date":"2021-07-06","year":2021,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US","Connecticut"],"institutions":["Connecticut Green Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.adaptationclearinghouse.org/resources/connecticut-public-law-21-eo115-an-act-concerning-climate-change-adaptation.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Connecticut Public Law 21-115, signed by Governor Ned Lamont on 6 July 2021, expands local resilience planning options, legal authorities and financing for adaptation projects through three components. It authorises municipal stormwater authorities, which develop stormwater management programmes, run public outreach, and may recommend property taxes to their municipal legislative bodies as a revenue source for green infrastructure and resiliency investment. It expands the purview of municipal flood and erosion control boards to include flood prevention and climate resilience, renaming them flood prevention, climate resilience and erosion control boards. And it expands the Connecticut Green Bank, established in 2011 for clean energy, by creating an Environmental Infrastructure Fund covering water, waste and recycling, climate adaptation and resilience, agriculture, land conservation, parks and recreation, and environmental markets, and usable to leverage state and federal infrastructure funding. The entry notes this last provision is significant because it lets a state infrastructure bank invest in climate adaptation rather than only clean energy and hard infrastructure. Across all provisions the law directs prioritised financing to frontline communities and areas of historical underinvestment.","insights":["The green bank expansion is the transferable piece: creating an Environmental Infrastructure Fund lets a state green bank capitalised for clean energy invest in adaptation, which is the same move Montgomery County made and a route available to any jurisdiction with an existing green bank.","Municipal stormwater authorities are given the power to recommend property taxes as a dedicated revenue source, so the law creates both the institution and its funding mechanism.","Renaming flood and erosion control boards to include climate resilience is not cosmetic: it extends existing legal authorities to a new purpose without creating a new body.","Prioritising frontline communities and areas of historical underinvestment is written into the financing provisions rather than added as guidance, which makes distribution a term of the instrument."],"keywords":["Connecticut Green Bank","Environmental Infrastructure Fund","municipal stormwater authority","flood prevention board","state infrastructure bank","frontline communities","adaptation financing"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":592,"title":"Catastrophe Bonds (A Primer)","authors":"Alexander Braun; Carolyn Kousky","date":"2021-07-01","year":2021,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["University of Pennsylvania","Aon"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://impact.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Cat-Bond-Primer-July-2021.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A July 2021 primer in the Wharton Risk Management and Decision Processes Center's primer series, by Alexander Braun and Carolyn Kousky, on catastrophe bonds. It places the instrument inside the wider category of insurance-linked securities, the route by which risk is placed directly in the capital markets rather than transferred to an insurer or reinsurer, and dates the ILS market's emergence to the aftermath of Hurricane Andrew in 1992, which was the costliest US natural disaster until Katrina in 2005 and left an insufficient supply of reinsurance for coastal catastrophe risk. Catastrophe bonds are the best-known type of ILS and the second-largest by volume, the first were issued in the mid-1990s and the market has grown steadily since. The primer explains the general mechanics, gives examples of use and outlines future opportunities. A footnote records that since 2012 the largest ILS instrument by volume has been collateralized reinsurance rather than catastrophe bonds, citing Aon Securities' 2020 ILS annual report.","insights":["A footnote carries the most consequential fact in the primer: **collateralized reinsurance has been the largest instrument in the insurance-linked securities market by volume since 2012**, so the catastrophe bond is the famous instrument rather than the dominant one.","The primer treats catastrophe bonds as one member of the insurance-linked securities family rather than as a standalone instrument, which is the level at which a new risk-linked security would enter and inherit conventions.","Carolyn Kousky is the author, and the document is a primer rather than a deal description, so it states the conventions that deal documentation assumes.","It pairs with the Chicago Fed letter in the same batch: two primers on one instrument, three years apart, one from a central bank and one from a business school, with no textual overlap. The pair is a usefully independent check on the shared account of the market's history."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond","insurance-linked securities","collateralized reinsurance","risk transfer","capital markets","Hurricane Andrew","Wharton Risk Center"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":576,"title":"Understanding the Improbable: A Survey of Fat Tails in Environmental Economics","authors":"Marc N. Conte; David L. Kelly","date":"2021-06-25","year":2021,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["University of Miami","Fordham University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-resource-102020-094143","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A survey in the Annual Review of Resource Economics, volume 13 pages 289 to 310, first published as a Review in Advance on 25 June 2021, by Marc N. Conte of Fordham and David L. Kelly of the University of Miami. It reviews the growing literature on fat-tailed distributions in environmental economics, examines their theoretical and statistical properties, and asks when those properties are likely to arise in environmental problems. The authors find that a number of variables in the field are fat tailed, including climate sensitivity, natural disaster impacts, the spread of infectious diseases, and stated willingness to pay, and argue that different fat-tailed distributions arise from common pathways. They review the policy implications and the controversies over interpretation, concluding that the literature has established when fat tails matter for optimal environmental policy but that much remains unclear: how alternative policies affect fat-tailed distributions, what the optimal policy is in a computational economy containing many fat-tailed problems, and how to account for imprecision in empirical tests for fat tails. The opening example is Hurricane Harvey, where 39.23 inches of rain fell on Beaumont, Texas in 48 hours against a late-August daily average of about 0.2 inches, causing $125 billion in damages.","insights":["The survey's honest residual is its most useful statement: it is not known how alternative policies affect a fat-tailed distribution. That is precisely what an instrument is for, since an instrument exists to change the distribution rather than to describe it.","Stated willingness to pay being fat tailed is a warning about the benefit layer specifically. If the valuations feeding a benefit estimate are drawn from a fat-tailed distribution, sample means are unstable and a benefit-cost ratio computed from them is less informative than it looks.","Grouping climate sensitivity, disaster impacts, disease spread and willingness to pay as arising from common pathways is the survey's organising move, and it is what makes the treatment general rather than specific to hazard modelling.","It is the general treatment by the same authors whose 2016 work established the hurricane-specific result, so the survey states the theory the applied paper instantiates."],"keywords":["fat tails","climate sensitivity","natural disasters","dismal theorem","willingness to pay","extreme value theory","environmental economics","Hurricane Harvey"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":632,"title":"Buffalo Sewer Authority Issues Largest-Ever U.S. Environmental Impact Bond","authors":"Quantified Ventures","date":"2021-06-22","year":2021,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","New York"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures","Buffalo Sewer Authority","Morgan Stanley","Ralph C. Wilson","Jr. Foundation","Community Foundation for Greater Buffalo"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/buffalo-sewer-largest-eib","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Quantified Ventures press release of 22 June 2021 announcing that the Buffalo Sewer Authority closed a $54 million Environmental Impact Bond, the largest in the United States at the time, to finance green infrastructure and stormwater mitigation under its Rain Check 2.0 initiative. The proceeds fund green infrastructure that captures stormwater, reduces combined sewer overflows and delivers community benefits including jobs. The transaction was assembled after The Ralph C. Wilson, Jr. Foundation and the Community Foundation for Greater Buffalo funded Environmental Consulting & Technology to bring newer delivery models, including alternative financing, to the Greater Buffalo region. Morgan Stanley and Quantified Ventures provided the underwriting and the outcomes-based financing structure. The release names the stated objectives as water quality, job creation and environmental justice, and points to the bond's issue details on MSRB EMMA and to the Rain Check 2.0 opportunity report.","insights":["**An environmental impact bond ties repayment terms to measured environmental performance**, so the resilience benefit is connected to a financial term by contract rather than by argument.","The route in is worth recording: a philanthropic pair funded the technical assistance that made the deal structurable at all. **The binding constraint was capacity, not capital**, and two foundations paid for the capacity.","At $54 million and billed as the largest US EIB at the time, this is a scale data point for a class usually described in pilot terms, and it sits in a municipal sewer authority rather than a special-purpose vehicle.","Stormwater and combined sewer overflow is a regulatory-compliance driver as much as a resilience one, which is part of why this deal had a sewer rate base to pledge against the bond."],"keywords":["environmental impact bond","outcomes-based financing","green infrastructure","stormwater","combined sewer overflow","municipal bond","environmental justice"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":503,"title":"The Economics of Climate Change","authors":"","date":"2021-04-22","year":2021,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Swiss Re"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.swissre.com/institute/research/topics-and-risk-dialogues/climate-and-natural-catastrophe-risk/expertise-publication-economics-of-climate-change.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Swiss Re Institute's landing page for The Economics of Climate Change. The headline finding is that by mid-century the world stands to lose around 10 per cent of total economic value from climate change if temperature increases stay on the current trajectory and neither the Paris Agreement nor 2050 net-zero targets are met. The Climate Economics Index stress-tests how climate risks will affect 48 countries representing 90 per cent of the world economy and ranks their overall climate resilience across four dimensions: total index, adaptive capacity, GDP impact and extreme weather risk. All countries are affected but some are more vulnerable: economies in south and southeast Asia are most exposed to physical risks and have the most to gain from limiting temperature increases, while many advanced northern hemisphere economies are both less exposed and better resourced to cope. Transition risks may also fall hardest on Asia, showing up as large shifts in asset values and higher costs of doing business as the world moves to a low-carbon economy. Group Chief Economist J\u00e9r\u00f4me Haegeli frames climate risk as systemic and addressable only globally. The page positions reinsurers as providers of risk transfer capacity, risk knowledge and long-term investment, using their understanding of risk to help households, companies and societies mitigate and adapt.","insights":["Ranking 48 countries on adaptive capacity as a separate axis from GDP impact is the analytically useful move: it separates how hard a country is hit from what it can do about it, which is the distinction an adaptation finance allocation needs.","A 10 per cent loss of global economic value by mid-century is the headline figure, and it is a gross loss estimate rather than a net-of-adaptation one, which is why it is larger than most sectoral numbers.","Placing the greatest physical exposure and the greatest transition exposure on the same region, south and southeast Asia, is a compounding finding rather than two separate ones.","The reinsurer describes its own role in three parts, risk transfer capacity, risk knowledge and long-term investment, which is the sector arguing it is an information provider and an investor as well as a bearer of risk.","This is the landing page rather than the report: the substantive text runs to about 1,500 characters and the sigma publication behind it is a separate document."],"keywords":["Climate Economics Index","adaptive capacity","physical risk","transition risk","Swiss Re","economic loss","stress test"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":487,"title":"Striving for Improvement: The Perceived Value of Improving Hurricane Forecast Accuracy","authors":"Renato Molina, David Letson, Brian McNoldy, Pallab Mozumder, Matthew Varkony","date":"2021-03-22","year":2021,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Miami Climate Resilience Institute","Florida International University","National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1175/bams-d-20-0179.1","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Molina and colleagues, four of them at the University of Miami, estimate the public's willingness to pay for further improvement in hurricane forecast accuracy, combining atmospheric modeling with a double-bounded dichotomous choice contingent valuation experiment. The survey covers 4,650 respondents in the FEMA-defined regions affected by Hurricanes Florence and Michael in 2018, 3,150 and 1,500 respectively, with a mean respondent aged 43, earning $65,176 and living 87 km from the coast. The Hurricane Forecast Improvement Project, established in 2008, had cut track and wind speed forecast error by more than 40 per cent each over the following decade, and respondents are asked to value maintaining that rate for another ten years. Average willingness to pay is $26.07 per household per year for storm track, $28.89 for wind speed and $21.63 for precipitation, so wind speed is valued most across both samples. Extrapolated to the Florence and Michael regions, annual totals are $60 million, $67 million and $50 million respectively. Extended to the most exposed US areas, those with historical hurricane wind speeds above 50 mph, the totals are $376 million, $428 million and $327 million; extending to less exposed areas raises them to $1.4 billion, $1.6 billion and $1.3 billion, with per capita values between $9.85 and $13.30 a year. Projected over ten years and discounted at 2 per cent, the present value of continued improvement is between $90.25 and $121.86 per person. For comparison, total federal meteorological funding in 2018 was $5.3 billion, 0.081 per cent of federal obligations, so a median income household's share of meteorological services and research was about $3.49, only a fraction of which goes to hurricane forecasting. The authors attribute the primacy of wind speed to the Saffir-Simpson scale being single-dimensional, familiar from media coverage and quick to assess.","insights":["The tax comparison is the argument: a household's share of all federal meteorological spending is about $3.49 a year while its stated willingness to pay for continued hurricane forecast improvement alone is $21 to $29 per attribute, so the perceived value exceeds the entire programme cost by an order of magnitude.","This is adaptation spending on information rather than on assets, and the benefit of the information product is quantified in dollars, which is what lets a public budget be sized against it.","Wind speed is valued most because the Saffir-Simpson scale is one-dimensional and familiar, which the authors call an availability effect, so the public pays most for the forecast attribute it can act on rather than the one that causes most harm.","The attributes are physically coupled, so research improving one improves the others, which means the sum of the three willingness-to-pay figures is an upper bound and the true joint value sits between the largest single estimate and that sum.","The estimates are six to eight times the earlier Lazo and Waldman figure of $15 per household per year, which the authors attribute to method and timing, and that gap is itself the reason a public funding decision based on contingent valuation remains contestable."],"keywords":["willingness to pay","contingent valuation","hurricane forecast","Hurricane Forecast Improvement Project","Saffir-Simpson","value of information","public funding of research"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":530,"title":"Climate Change and Financial Stability","authors":"Celso Brunetti, Benjamin Dennis, Dylan Gates, Diana Hancock, David Ignell, Elizabeth K. Kiser, Gurubala Kotta, Anna Kovner, Richard J. Rosen, Nicholas K. Tabor","date":"2021-03-19","year":2021,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Reserve System"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/climate-change-and-financial-stability-20210319.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Federal Reserve staff note describing how risks arising from climate change may affect financial stability, and connecting that to the Federal Reserve's existing financial stability monitoring framework. It reaches three conclusions: the monitoring framework is flexible enough to broadly incorporate many key elements of climate-related risks; climate change increases financial stability risks but substantial improvements in data and models are needed to incorporate them fully; and domestic and international transparency efforts around climate-related financial exposures may help clarify the nature and scope of those risks.","insights":["The central analytical move is to insist that climate-related economic or financial losses are not by themselves a financial stability problem: the economy can lose output and investors can lose money without the financial system amplifying it. Stability is at issue only when losses reach levered intermediaries, disrupt market functioning, or trigger sudden repricing of large asset classes.","Climate risk is mapped into the existing framework in two distinct ways, as SHOCKS and as VULNERABILITIES, which is what lets the Fed use machinery it already has rather than building a parallel regime.","Acute hazards such as storms, floods and wildfires are treated as information events as much as damage events: they can quickly change or reveal new information about future economic conditions or asset values, which is the channel by which a physical event becomes a repricing.","The note is explicit that the work is early: analysis and research on climate-related financial risk is at an early stage and substantial improvements in data and models are needed, which is a central bank declining to claim more than it has.","Transparency and disclosure are offered as the third lever alongside monitoring and research, on the argument that clarity about exposures is what makes the scope of the risk knowable at all."],"keywords":["financial stability monitoring","physical and transition risk","asset repricing","levered intermediaries","climate disclosure"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":230,"title":"Enabling Private Investment in Climate Adaptation and Resilience: Current Status, Barriers to Investment and Blueprint for Action","authors":"Arame Tall, Sarah Lynagh, Candela Blanco Vecchi, Pepukaye Bardouille, Felipe Montoya Pino, Elham Shabahat, Vladimir Stenek, Fiona Stewart, Samantha Power, Cindy Paladines, Philippe Neves, Lori Kerr","date":"2021-03-02","year":2021,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank","Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery (GFDRR)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/35203","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The report 'Enabling Private Investment in Climate Adaptation and Resilience' emphasizes the critical need to mobilize private sector investment to bridge the significant adaptation finance gap, particularly in developing countries. While public funding has increased, it remains inadequate to meet urgent climate adaptation needs. The document identifies barriers to private investment and presents a structured Blueprint for Action with five key entry points to facilitate private engagement, supported by case studies demonstrating successful strategies in nations like Bangladesh.","insights":["Private sector investment in climate adaptation is currently minimal, accounting for only 1.6% of total adaptation finance.","Barriers to private investment include lack of localized climate risk data, unclear investment gaps, and insufficient financial incentives.","The Blueprint for Action outlines five critical entry points to mobilize private capital: long-term adaptation planning, national investment plans, market assessment, project preparation, and transaction coordination.","Adaptation strategies can yield significant economic returns, with benefits estimated at $4 for every $1 invested.","Governments play a crucial role in creating an enabling environment through clear policies, legal frameworks, and innovative financial instruments."],"keywords":["financial instruments","institutional capacity","risk management","public-private partnerships","adaptation gaps"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":248,"title":"Resilience Rating System: A Methodology for Building and Tracking Resilience to Climate Change","authors":"Stephane Hallegatte; Viviane Clement; Sundus Siddiqi; Michelle Winglee","date":"2021-02-01","year":2021,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/35039","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Resilience Rating System (RRS), developed by the World Bank Group, offers a structured methodology to assess and enhance climate resilience in projects across various sectors and countries. By evaluating both the resilience of projects and their capacity to foster resilience in outcomes, the RRS aims to improve disaster risk management and facilitate better decision-making regarding investments in climate-resilient initiatives. The methodology incorporates rigorous risk assessments, sensitivity analyses, and stakeholder engagement, guiding project developers to implement effective adaptation measures throughout the project lifecycle while promoting transparency and best practices.","insights":["The RRS evaluates projects based on their design and outcomes related to climate resilience, with ratings ranging from C to A+.","A primary goal of the RRS is to inform decision-makers and create incentives for better climate adaptation investments.","The methodology emphasizes integrating risk assessments and adaptation options into all phases of project development.","It incorporates stakeholder engagement and contingent planning to address uncertainties and enhance project resilience.","Higher project ratings indicate greater confidence in achieving expected outcomes despite climate risks.","The RRS adapts to various contexts and is applicable to both public and private sector projects.","Regular monitoring and updates are essential for adaptive management as new climate data becomes available.","Best practices identified by the RRS can be scaled across sectors and countries to improve resiliency efforts."],"keywords":["methodology","vulnerability","adaptation measures","risk assessment","transparency"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":82,"title":"Climate Finance Policy in Practice: A Review of the Evidence","authors":"Rishikesh Ram Bhandary, Kelly Sims Gallagher, Fang Zhang","date":"2021-01-22","year":2021,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Climate Policy","Tufts University","Tsinghua University","Harvard Kennedy School"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14693062.2020.1871313","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This resource evaluates the effectiveness of various climate finance policies through empirical performance analysis, highlighting strengths and weaknesses while identifying critical knowledge gaps. It assesses nine policy types based on criteria such as mobilization effectiveness, economic efficiency, environmental integrity, and equity, emphasizing the importance of policy design features and the role of innovative financial instruments in enhancing climate action.","insights":["Different climate finance policies have varying strengths and weaknesses that affect their effectiveness.","Feed-in tariffs and tax credits effectively mobilize private finance, while the impact of national climate funds remains unclear.","Significant data and research gaps exist regarding the empirical effectiveness of many climate finance policies.","Balancing mobilization effectiveness with economic efficiency, environmental integrity, and equity is crucial for policy selection.","Investment barriers for renewable energy are significant and require targeted policy interventions.","Green bonds are emerging as a viable financing tool for low-carbon projects.","State investment banks play a crucial role in mobilizing private finance for climate innovation."],"keywords":["public policy","economic efficiency","investment mechanisms","risk management","policy frameworks"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":579,"title":"Investing in Blue Natural Capital to Secure a Future for the Red Sea Ecosystems","authors":"Maha J. Cziesielski; Carlos M. Duarte; Manuel Aranda; Andrew C. Baker; and others","date":"2021-01-15","year":2021,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Global","Asia"],"institutions":["King Abdullah University of Science and Technology","University of Miami","Ocean Conservancy","Stockholm University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2020.603722","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A policy and practice review in Frontiers in Marine Science, published 15 January 2021, on investing in blue natural capital to secure a future for Red Sea ecosystems. Led from KAUST by Maha J. Cziesielski with Carlos M. Duarte and Manuel Aranda and a large multi-institution author list that includes Andrew C. Baker of the University of Miami, it makes the case for treating Red Sea marine ecosystems, coral reefs, mangroves and seagrass among them, as natural capital worth investing in rather than as a conservation cost, at a moment of rapid coastal development on the Saudi Arabian coast.","insights":["The Red Sea is an unusually clean test case for blue natural capital: its reefs are among the most heat-tolerant in the world, so the investment case rests on development pressure rather than on thermal risk.","The author list spans some twenty institutions across the Red Sea states, Europe, North America and Australia, which makes the paper a consensus policy and practice review rather than a single group's position."],"keywords":["blue natural capital","Red Sea","coral reefs","mangroves","seagrass","marine ecosystem services","coastal development"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":549,"title":"Inter-American Development Bank Group Climate Change Action Plan 2021-2025","authors":"Amy Lewis, Claudio Alatorre, Adrien Vogt-Schilb","date":"2021-01-01","year":2021,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Latin America","Global"],"institutions":["Inter-American Development Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://publications.iadb.org/en/inter-american-development-bank-group-climate-change-action-plan-2021-2025","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The IDB Group's climate change action plan for 2021 to 2025, covering the IDB, IDB Invest and IDB Lab. It reviews progress and lessons from the previous 2016 to 2020 plan, then sets out actions organised against the MDB building blocks with sector-specific considerations, a monitoring framework, and a treatment of risks, resources and next steps. Its instruments of implementation are institutional rather than financial: climate risk assessment, disaster and climate change risk assessment, country strategies, the corporate results framework and development effectiveness tracking.","insights":["The plan is organised against the shared MDB BUILDING BLOCKS rather than against IDB's own structure, which is what makes it directly comparable with other development banks' climate plans.","It covers three entities with different instruments, the sovereign IDB, the private-sector IDB Invest and the innovation arm IDB Lab, so the plan has to reconcile concessional, commercial and grant capital in one framework.","Climate Risk Assessment and Disaster and Climate Change Risk Assessment appear as named institutional processes with abbreviations, which means climate screening is embedded in project appraisal rather than added as a review.","Monitoring is given its own section and its own tooling, the Corporate Results Framework and the DELTA development effectiveness instrument, so the plan commits to measurement in a way most institutional strategy documents do not.","Lessons learned from the 2016 to 2020 plan are carried in an electronic appendix rather than the main text, which is worth knowing: the operational review that would explain what did not work is a separate document."],"keywords":["IDB Group","climate change action plan","MDB building blocks","climate risk assessment","Latin America and the Caribbean"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":452,"title":"Community-Based Catastrophe Insurance: A Model for Closing the Disaster Protection Gap","authors":"Alex Bernhardt; Carolyn Kousky; Andy Read; Christopher Sykes","date":"2021-01-01","year":2021,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Marsh"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://impact.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Community-Based-Catastrophe-Insurance-February-2021.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The foundational description of community-based catastrophe insurance, a disaster insurance programme arranged by a local government, a quasi-governmental body such as a special-purpose district, or a community group, covering individual properties within that community. The report starts from the persistent US protection gap: many households and small businesses lack sufficient coverage to fund repair and rebuilding, for reasons the authors separate into affordability constraints, limited risk awareness, poor understanding of insurance, and behavioural biases in decision making. CBCI is offered as a delivery model rather than a product, with the potential to enhance community financial resilience, provide affordable and reliably available disaster insurance, and create incentives for risk reduction at both community and individual level. The authors are explicit that it can occupy several positions in the existing ecosystem: complementing traditional catastrophe markets with supplemental protection, acting as full-limit single-peril property protection in high-risk areas, or giving a community a way to work with carriers or private capital providers to rebuild and sustain insurance uptake in the face of loss volatility. The report covers possible CBCI structures and a roadmap to implementation, drawing on interviews with leaders and stakeholders.","insights":["The innovation is in who arranges the cover rather than in what it pays: a local government, special-purpose district or community group buying for individual properties changes the unit of purchase and therefore the risk pool, without requiring a new product.","Creating incentives for community-level and individual risk reduction is named as a purpose of the structure, so mitigation is intended to be internalised by the same body that buys the cover, which is the alignment most insurance arrangements lack.","The authors identify four distinct causes of the protection gap, affordability, risk awareness, comprehension and behavioural bias, and only the first is a price problem, which is why a delivery-model change can reach households a cheaper premium would not.","CBCI is presented as flexible enough to be supplemental, to be full-limit single-peril cover where risk is high, or to be a vehicle for working with carriers to sustain uptake through loss volatility, so it is three different instruments depending on the market it enters.","A special-purpose district as the purchasing entity is the structural move: the same body can both levy and insure, so the premium has a compulsory revenue source behind it."],"keywords":["community-based catastrophe insurance","protection gap","special-purpose district","group purchase","disaster insurance","risk reduction incentives","affordability"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":437,"title":"The National Flood Insurance Program and the Need for Reform","authors":"Diego S. Torres-Lugo","date":"2021-01-01","year":2021,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3920756","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A research paper examining the National Flood Insurance Program and arguing for reform through a market-based risk scheme. It organises the programme's problems into four: debt, inaccurate risk pricing, repetitive loss properties, and the unintended consequences of the programme itself. The paper traces the NFIP's emergence and the root causes of each issue, on the premise that the programme was created to give communities access to primary flood insurance and to mitigate flood risk by regulating floodplain development. Its proposed solution is deliberately two-part rather than a straight privatisation: integrating private insurance with federal grants and subsidies, with private insurance described as half of the solution and subsidies and grants as the other half. The context given is that floods are the most common natural disaster in the United States, with every state and nearly every city having experienced flood damage, and with rising sea levels, shoreline erosion and warmer seas increasing both the frequency and magnitude of storms.","insights":["The four problems are treated as connected rather than separate: debt follows from inaccurate risk pricing, repetitive loss properties follow from both, and the unintended consequences follow from the programme's own floodplain development role.","The proposed structure is explicitly hybrid, private insurance plus federal grants and subsidies, with the author naming each as half the solution, which rejects both the status quo and pure privatisation.","Regulating floodplain development was part of the NFIP's original purpose, so the paper's unintended-consequences argument is that the insurance arm undermined the land-use arm.","Affordability is the reason subsidies remain in the proposal: a market-based risk price is the fix for the pricing problem and the cause of an access problem, which is why the author keeps a public half."],"keywords":["National Flood Insurance Program","flood insurance reform","risk pricing","repetitive loss","private flood insurance","subsidies","floodplain development"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":430,"title":"The Value of US Coral Reefs for Flood Risk Reduction","authors":"Borja G. Reguero and colleagues","date":"2021-01-01","year":2021,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US","Florida","Hawaii"],"institutions":["Center for Coastal Climate Resilience at UC Santa Cruz","United States Geological Survey","United States Army Corps of Engineers"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-021-00706-6","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Nature Sustainability paper valuing the flood protection that US coral reefs provide. The authors set the physical stakes: coral reefs line more than 3,100 kilometres of the most at-risk US coastlines across Florida, Hawaii and the US Trust Territories, and recent measurements in Hawaii, Florida and the US Virgin Islands show reefs have eroded more than a metre vertically over recent decades, a trend expected to worsen with climate change and other stressors. The paper's argument is that ecosystem loss can escalate flood risk within years to levels sea-level rise would not reach for decades or a century, and that while the World Bank, the US Army Corps of Engineers and the insurance industry increasingly acknowledge the role of ecosystems in reducing loss, hazard mitigation and environmental management remain largely unaligned. The funding asymmetry is the sharpest point: in 2018 the United States provided about $15 billion to the Army Corps for flood and storm damage reduction, of which more than $10 billion went to states and territories hit by hurricanes Harvey, Irma and Maria, while the most ambitious US reef restoration project, Iconic Reefs in the Florida Keys, proposes roughly $5 million a year.","insights":["The funding asymmetry is the finding that matters for instrument design: about $15 billion in one year for Army Corps flood and storm damage reduction against roughly $5 million a year for the most ambitious US reef restoration project, a ratio of three thousand to one for interventions addressing the same hazard.","Ecosystem degradation is argued to advance flood risk faster than sea-level rise does, reaching in years levels not otherwise expected for decades or a century, which reframes reef loss as an acceleration of exposure rather than a conservation loss.","Reefs have eroded more than a metre vertically in recent decades in Hawaii, Florida and the US Virgin Islands, which is a measured loss of protective structure rather than a projection.","The paper names the institutional gap precisely: multilateral agencies, the Army Corps and insurers all acknowledge the protective role of ecosystems, and hazard mitigation and environmental management budgets remain unaligned, which is the gap a payment mechanism would have to bridge."],"keywords":["coral reefs","flood risk reduction","natural infrastructure","avoided damages","US Army Corps of Engineers","reef restoration","Florida Keys","ecosystem services"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":63,"title":"Hampton, VA: An Environmental Impact Bond to Fight Flooding","authors":"","date":"2020-12-02","year":2020,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures","Chesapeake Bay Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/hampton-eib","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Hampton, VA has launched Virginia's first Environmental Impact Bond (EIB) to address flooding through nature-based projects. The $12 million bond will finance three initiatives aimed at enhancing stormwater management in underserved communities, while providing measurable outcomes for investors and improving water equity.","insights":["The EIB aims to develop innovative solutions for flooding challenges in Hampton, VA.","The City of Hampton's projects are designed to improve stormwater storage capacity and water quality.","Public engagement and regional partnerships played a critical role in the EIB's development.","Low- to moderate-income communities will benefit from improved flood resilience and environmental conditions.","The structure of the EIB allows for enhanced impact measurement compared to traditional green bonds.","Hampton's EIB was oversubscribed, indicating strong demand from ESG-focused investors.","Investments from the EIB are expected to contribute to the city\u2019s long-term resilience strategy.","Nature-based projects include converting drainage ditches into bioswales and elevating key transportation corridors.","The bond is part of a multi-stage resilience pipeline to address chronic environmental stresses."],"keywords":["stormwater management","investment","urban resilience","environmental equity","community engagement"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":201,"title":"The Implications of Climate Change for Financial Stability","authors":"","date":"2020-11-23","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Financial Stability Board"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.fsb.org/2020/11/the-implications-of-climate-change-for-financial-stability/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The document examines the implications of climate change for financial stability, identifying physical and transition risks that can impact the financial system. It highlights the potential for these risks to interact and amplify, causing uncertainty and volatility in markets. While current estimates suggest that overall risks are manageable, concentrated exposures and data gaps pose challenges for financial institutions in risk management. The report calls for stronger regulatory measures and improved data availability to enhance resilience against climate-related risks, particularly in vulnerable economies.","insights":["Climate-related risks include physical risks from climate impacts and transition risks from moving to a low-carbon economy.","A disorderly transition could destabilize financial systems, amplifying both physical and transition risks.","Emerging markets are more vulnerable to climate-related risks due to their weaker financial defenses.","Self-reinforcing feedback loops may occur, decreasing lending and harming economic prospects.","A lack of consistent disclosures about climate risks contributes to market uncertainties and mispriced risks."],"keywords":["financial stability","transition risks","market volatility","risk management","insurance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":234,"title":"Adaptation Principles: A Guide for Designing Strategies for Climate Change Adaptation and Resilience","authors":"Stephane Hallegatte, Jun Rentschler, Julie Rozenberg","date":"2020-11-17","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/34780","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Adaptation Principles' resource outlines a comprehensive framework to assist countries in developing effective strategies for enhancing climate change adaptability and resilience. It emphasizes the integration of proactive, inclusive development strategies, robust institutional frameworks, cross-sectoral coordination, and financial planning to manage climate risks while addressing the needs of vulnerable populations. The guide underscores the importance of continuous monitoring, evaluation, and adaptation of strategies to ensure effective implementation and long-term economic stability amidst increasing climate-related challenges.","insights":["Proactive adaptation strategies can effectively reduce climate risks and promote sustainable development.","Governments play a crucial role in enhancing resilience by minimizing barriers for private actors and ensuring the accessibility of information.","Integrating climate considerations into economic recovery plans, particularly following crises like COVID-19, is essential for building long-term resilience.","Stakeholder participation is vital at every stage of the climate adaptation process to enhance ownership and accountability.","Monitoring and evaluating adaptation strategies is imperative for adapting to ongoing changes in climate uncertainty.","A layered approach to disaster risk financing combining various financial tools is recommended for effective risk management.","Investment in adaptive social protection systems can improve resource management in post-disaster situations."],"keywords":["sustainable development","risk management","public policy","stakeholder engagement","infrastructure resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":36,"title":"Building Codes Save: A Nationwide Study","authors":"","date":"2020-11-01","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","Compass PTS JV"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.fema.gov/emergency-managers/risk-management","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Building Codes Save: A Nationwide Study' analyzes the financial benefits of adopting hazard-resistant building codes in the U.S. since 1980, revealing that such codes significantly reduce losses from natural disasters, which have amounted to over $1.6 trillion during this period. The study highlights the importance of modern building codes, particularly the International Codes (I-Codes), in minimizing risks from floods, hurricanes, and earthquakes. It emphasizes that communities with updated codes see substantial economic savings and that ongoing advocacy for these standards is crucial for enhancing resilience against future disasters.","insights":["Natural disasters in the U.S. have caused over $1.6 trillion in losses since 1980, with significant savings achievable through the adoption of hazard-resistant building codes.","The study identifies an average annualized loss avoided (AALA) of $1.6 billion due to building codes, showing that approximately 51% of analyzed post-2000 buildings benefit from these codes.","Adopting and enforcing updated International Codes (I-Codes) can result in savings of $11 for every $1 invested in hazard-resistant construction.","Flooding remains the most common hazard, contributing to 90% of all natural disaster events, while improved building codes significantly lower potential losses from these events.","The study projects future savings of $132 billion if the adoption of I-Codes continues and expands, especially in communities prone to seismic, flooding, and hurricane risks."],"keywords":["building codes","disaster mitigation","economic benefits","community resilience","risk assessment"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":109,"title":"What Are Resilience Bonds and How Can They Protect Us Against Climate Crises?","authors":"Felipe Larrain Bascunan, Dominic Molloy, Berend Sauer","date":"2020-07-24","year":2020,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Global Center on Adaptation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://gca.org/what-are-resilience-bonds-and-how-can-they-protect-us-against-climate-crises/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Resilience bonds are innovative financial instruments designed to enhance climate resilience by raising capital specifically for adaptation projects. Despite the growing need for adaptation finance, current investments are significantly lower than required. Resilience bonds can unlock funding for countries to improve their climate readiness, while offering potential economic returns due to their ability to raise capital on favorable terms.","insights":["There is an increasing need for adaptation funding, estimated to reach $300 billion per year by 2030 for developing countries.","Investment in climate resilience offers significant economic benefits with potential returns of up to $7.1 trillion from a $1.8 trillion investment by 2030.","Resilience bonds are a subset of green bonds, aimed specifically at funding climate-resilient projects.","The market for green bonds has rapidly expanded, highlighting the potential for resilience bonds to grow if defined properly.","The first dedicated resilience bond was launched by the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development, raising $700 million.","Resilience bonds can help countries and regions leverage finances for climate resilience, although they may not be suitable for all countries facing debt issues.","Green bonds have experienced high demand, particularly for projects focused on sustainability and resilience initiatives.","California is planning to launch a resilience bond aimed at preparing for drought and wildfire risks.","Continued research and development into resilience bonds is essential for scaling their use in vulnerable countries."],"keywords":["financial instruments","investment","economic returns","sustainable development","public debt"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":202,"title":"Stocktake of Financial Authorities\u2019 Experience in Including Physical and Transition Climate Risks as Part of Their Financial Stability Monitoring","authors":"","date":"2020-07-22","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Financial Stability Board"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.fsb.org/2020/07/stocktake-of-financial-authorities-experience-in-including-physical-and-transition-climate-risks-as-part-of-their-financial-stability-monitoring/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The stocktake report evaluates how financial authorities incorporate physical and transition climate risks into their stability monitoring, revealing that while a significant majority are engaged, approaches vary widely. Challenges in data consistency, risk quantification, and the integration of these risks into supervisory practices persist, highlighting the need for improved disclosures, collaborative approaches, and international coordination.","insights":["Approximately 75% of financial authorities consider or plan to consider climate-related risks in their monitoring.","Quantifying climate-related risks remains challenging due to inconsistent data and the complex translation of climate outcomes into financial exposures.","Pension funds have reduced exposure to climate-risk affected securities, while banks and insurers maintained constant exposure levels.","The European Systemic Risk Board reports that economic costs of climate risk are not fully reflected in asset prices due to insufficient disclosures.","Current modeling techniques provide limited insight into long-term climate risks and physical risks are not adequately reflected in asset prices.","A unified set of scenarios is needed to compare impacts across firms, and significant collaboration is necessary to enhance data quality and sharing.","International coordination among regulators is vital to avoid market fragmentation and improve overall climate risk assessments."],"keywords":["financial stability","risk quantification","data standardization","regulatory challenges","supervisory practices"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":311,"title":"The Belt Line Center: The Michigan PACE Market's First Green Roof Project","authors":"","date":"2020-06-01","year":2020,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Michigan"],"institutions":["Lean & Green Michigan"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://leanandgreenmi.com/casestudy/belt-line-center/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This case study documents the Belt Line Center PACE project in Detroit, Michigan, the first green roof financed via PACE in Michigan. Located in Wayne County, the $1,035,185 project combines a 16,800 sq. ft. vegetated green roof with a rain garden to manage stormwater runoff and reduce energy use at a historic Albert Kahn building. Over a 20-year term, the project generates $1,079,375 in total savings, exceeding project cost, and eliminates an estimated 462 metric tons of CO2 annually. The project is administered through Lean & Green Michigan's public-private PACE program.","insights":["Total savings of $1,079,375 exceed the $1,035,185 financed amount over 20 years, demonstrating a positive net return and validating the economic case for green roofs as a PACE-eligible investment.","The project finances 100% of upfront costs via a property tax special assessment, allowing a 20-year repayment term that aligns repayment with energy savings, resolving the mismatch between typical 3\u20135 year commercial loan tenors and longer payback periods for efficiency projects.","The 16,800 sq. ft. green roof paired with a rain garden provides a layered stormwater management system, with the rain garden capturing infiltration overflow not retained by the green roof.","The project is estimated to eliminate 462 metric tons of CO2 per year, equivalent to removing 100 cars from the road annually, providing a quantified GHG co-benefit alongside energy savings.","Wayne County established a countywide PACE district in December 2013 under Lean & Green Michigan, using the county's property tax enforcement authority to provide lenders security equivalent to a tax lien, enabling fixed-rate, long-term private financing without public credit risk.","This is the first green roof project in Michigan financed through PACE, establishing a replicable proof point for green infrastructure financing via special assessment in Midwest urban markets.","PACE's structure shifts energy project financing from a capital expenditure requiring short-term debt repayment to a long-term obligation tied to the property, making projects cash-flow positive from year one, a model relevant to commercial building owners facing deferred capital reinvestment decisions."],"keywords":["PACE financing","green roof","stormwater management","Detroit","Wayne County","special assessment","energy efficiency"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":575,"title":"Flood Your Neighbors: Spillover Effects of Levee Building","authors":"Haoluan Wang","date":"2020-05-31","year":2020,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Maryland","Agricultural and Applied Economics Association"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.22004/ag.econ.304382","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A selected paper by Haoluan Wang of the University of Maryland, prepared for the 2020 Agricultural and Applied Economics Association annual meeting and dated 31 May 2020, on the spillover effects of levee building along the Mississippi River. The starting point is that measures taken to protect against natural catastrophes can raise the risk of damage by incentivising economic activity in catastrophe-prone areas, and the less analysed half of the problem is that one agent's self-protection can worsen catastrophic risk for others or force them to protect themselves in turn. Using the Great Mississippi Flood of 2011 as a plausibly exogenous shock and a difference-in-differences strategy on newly digitised data covering every levee along the Mississippi and its tributaries across eight states, the paper finds that levees in flooded counties were heightened by 0.7 to 1 per cent after the flood, and that a 1 per cent increase in upstream levee elevation increased downstream levee elevation by 0.7 to 1.3 per cent. The conclusion is that inter-jurisdictional cooperation on levee building matters for managing flood damage while limiting negative externalities across jurisdictions.","insights":["A downstream elasticity of 0.7 to 1.3 against a 1 per cent upstream increase means protective investment is very nearly self-propagating: one jurisdiction's levee obliges its neighbour's, so the aggregate capital requirement is set by the arms race rather than by the hazard.","This is maladaptation with a measured coefficient: the paper prices the externality a levee imposes on a neighbour rather than treating protective investment as unambiguously beneficial.","It has a direct financing implication the paper does not draw: a lender or insurer appraising a levee project on its own protective benefit is systematically overstating the net benefit, because the induced downstream response is a cost the project causes and does not bear.","The 2011 flood as an exogenous shock is what makes the estimate credible, and it also dates the evidence: this is a decade-old response pattern in a basin that has kept building since."],"keywords":["levee","spillover effect","spatial externality","Mississippi River","maladaptation","difference-in-differences","flood protection","inter-jurisdictional cooperation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":41,"title":"Forest Resilience Bond (FRB)","authors":"","date":"2020-04-23","year":2020,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Blue Forest"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.blueforest.org/finance/forest-resilience-bond/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Forest Resilience Bond (FRB) is a conservation finance model aimed at generating new revenue streams for forest restoration projects. By blending public and private funds, the FRB provides upfront capital for faster project implementation, alleviating cash flow constraints while supporting local restoration economies through long-term contracts and partnerships.","insights":["The FRB creates new revenue streams by quantifying ecosystem benefits.","It is a public-private partnership that alleviates financial burdens on restoration projects.","The FRB allows implementation partners to pay contractors quickly, enhancing cash flow.","It provides critical upfront capital for restoration efforts, enabling projects to scale effectively.","The initiative develops long-term contracts to support local economies involved in restoration.","It brings multiple stakeholders together, including land managers, governments, and non-profits.","Investment returns for private investors are modest, emphasizing a balance between profit and ecological benefits.","The FRB addresses the problem of overstocked forests that become susceptible to severe wildfires.","The FRB Catalyst Facility accelerates the development and launch of multiple FRB projects."],"keywords":["conservation finance","ecosystem restoration","public-private partnership","forest management","investment strategy"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":37,"title":"Natural Hazard Mitigation Saves: 2019 Report","authors":"Multi-Hazard Mitigation Council","date":"2020-04-17","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Institute of Building Sciences","Multihazard Mitigation Council"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://nibs.org/projects/natural-hazard-mitigation-saves-2019-report/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Natural Hazard Mitigation Saves: 2019 Report' provides a thorough analysis of the economic advantages of pre-disaster mitigation strategies, emphasizing the necessity for improved building codes and practices to enhance community resilience against escalating natural disasters. The report showcases extensive research on the benefit-cost ratios of various mitigation measures, highlighting their effectiveness in reducing losses, enhancing safety, and promoting economic stability.","insights":["Investments in natural hazard mitigation strategies produce significant societal benefits, with benefit-cost ratios (BCRs) demonstrating that for every dollar spent, major future losses can be avoided.","Effective building codes and compliance with updated standards lead to substantial reductions in disaster-related losses and improved community safety.","Collaboration among federal, state, and local entities is crucial for successful implementation and funding of hazard mitigation measures.","Understanding local risk profiles and tailoring mitigation strategies is essential for achieving optimal cost-effectiveness and resilience.","Economic analyses show a strong correlation between pre-disaster investments in infrastructure and post-disaster recovery speed and cost reductions.","Mental health impacts and social vulnerabilities are critical considerations in disaster preparedness and recovery planning.","Flood mitigation and retrofitting measures yield high returns on investment, particularly in vulnerable areas, preventing extensive property damages and loss of life."],"keywords":["natural hazards","mitigation","building codes","infrastructure","economic resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":38,"title":"Mitigation Saves: Mitigation Saves Up to $13 per $1 Invested","authors":"","date":"2020-04-15","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["National Institute of Building Sciences"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://nibs.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ms_v4_overview.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource 'Mitigation Saves: Mitigation Saves up to $13 per $1 Invested' highlights the substantial financial benefits of investing in climate mitigation strategies, claiming that every dollar spent can result in savings of up to $13 through avoided climate-related costs and damages. It emphasizes the economic viability and necessity of proactive investments in mitigation measures to enhance community resilience, create jobs, and reduce the fiscal burden of future climate impacts.","insights":["Investing in mitigation can yield savings of up to $13 for every $1 spent.","Mitigation strategies significantly reduce future climate-related costs and health impacts.","Proactive climate measures generate substantial economic returns and job creation.","Early investments in mitigation can prevent larger expenditures associated with disaster response.","Policy frameworks that support mitigation enhance community resilience and financial benefits."],"keywords":["economic impact","cost-effectiveness","sustainability","disaster prevention","job creation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":451,"title":"The Role of Insurance in Coastal Adaptation: Workshop Findings","authors":"Carolyn Kousky; Helen Wiley","date":"2020-03-01","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Pennsylvania","National Science Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://impact.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/The-Role-of-Insurance-in-Coastal-Adaptation-Workshop-Findings.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Findings from a Wharton Risk Center workshop on what insurance can and cannot do for coastal adaptation, organised around four problems the participants treated as distinct. The recovery of low-income families, where federal disaster aid is limited and delayed and the flood insurance gap is widest, and where standard flood policies do not cover temporary living expenses and other recovery costs. The protection of coastal ecosystems, which provide protection against some disaster risks but are under-funded for conservation and for restoration after the very storms they buffer. Meeting community fiscal needs, which is the municipal side of the same exposure. And homeowners policies and mitigation, where the workshop examined how standard cover interacts with risk reduction. The overview also names gaps beyond flood, including high deductibles for wind damage, and barriers to using insurance to relocate out of high-risk areas, a strategy the authors expect to matter more as the sea rises.","insights":["Standard flood insurance not covering temporary living expenses is a specific, fixable product gap that falls hardest on low-income households, and it is separate from the affordability problem usually discussed.","Coastal ecosystems are identified as under-funded for restoration precisely after the storms for which they provide protection, which is the same asymmetry the coral reef paper quantifies and points at a mechanism gap rather than a knowledge gap.","Barriers to using insurance proceeds to relocate rather than rebuild are named as a design problem, and the authors expect managed retreat to become more important as sea level rises.","High wind deductibles are flagged alongside the flood gap, so a coastal household can hold cover for both perils and still be materially unprotected.","The workshop format means these are participant findings rather than original analysis, which is worth noting before grounding a claim in them."],"keywords":["coastal adaptation","flood insurance gap","low-income recovery","coastal ecosystems","wind deductible","managed retreat","municipal fiscal needs"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":96,"title":"Florida Climate Outlook: Assessing Physical and Economic Impacts Through 2040","authors":"Daniel Raimi, Amelia Keyes, Cora Kingdon","date":"2020-02-06","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Resources for the Future"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.rff.org/publications/reports/florida-climate-outlook/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Florida Climate Outlook: Assessing Physical and Economic Impacts through 2040' report evaluates the expected physical and economic consequences of climate change on Florida, highlighting rising temperatures, sea level rise, and increased storm intensity. The assessment underscores the urgent need for adaptation strategies as climate-related risks escalate, particularly for coastal cities like Miami, with potential impacts on health, agriculture, and infrastructure.","insights":["Florida is projected to experience summer temperatures exceeding 83\u00b0F and significant sea level rise by 2040, increasing flooding risks.","The state's agricultural sector faces substantial threats, particularly citrus crops affected by diseases linked to climate change.","Major urban areas like Miami are at risk of flood-related damages, with significant economic assets threatened.","Health risks are expected to escalate due to extreme heat, particularly among vulnerable populations such as the elderly.","Environmental factors will decrease outdoor labor productivity by up to 17%, complicating agricultural operations.","The need for anticipatory adaptation measures could lead to the abandonment of certain areas due to unsustainable living conditions.","Low-income households may bear an increased burden from climate impacts due to higher relative energy expenditures.","Carbon taxation discussions are becoming more pertinent as they relate to economic modeling and emissions reduction."],"keywords":["sea level rise","agriculture","human health","economic risks","storm impacts"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":539,"title":"The Business Case for Resilience in Southeast Florida: Regional Economic Benefits of Climate Adaptation","authors":"","date":"2020-01-01","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["Urban Land Institute","AECOM","Southeast Florida Regional Climate Change Compact","Broward County","Miami-Dade County","Palm Beach County"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://knowledge.uli.org/reports/research-reports/2020/the-business-case-for-resilience-in-southeast-florida","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"An economic analysis of the regional benefits of climate adaptation in Southeast Florida, produced by ULI with AECOM's Sustainable Economics practice, the Southeast Florida Regional Climate Change Compact and a stakeholder group drawn from chambers of commerce, community foundations, developers and design firms. It was reviewed for consistency and accuracy of the original economic analysis. The framing is the business case: what the region gains economically from adapting, addressed to the private sector actors whose investment decisions determine whether adaptation is funded.","insights":["The project is structured to reach the private sector rather than only local government: the stakeholder group is chambers of commerce, a beacon council, developers, architects and a roofing contractor, alongside the four Compact counties.","The analysis values the actions rather than proposing them: it takes a regional climate action plan as given and asks what the plan is worth.","AECOM's Sustainable Economics practice carried the analysis and ULI states it was independently reviewed for consistency and accuracy, which is a stronger provenance claim than most grey literature makes for itself.","Brizaga, a local engineering firm, supplied both the local project manager and the design team, so the report is regionally authored rather than a national template applied to Florida.","The Compact is described as more than a decade of collaboration across Broward, Miami-Dade, Monroe and Palm Beach counties with bipartisan support, which is the institutional precondition the economic case depends on."],"keywords":["business case for resilience","regional economic benefits","climate adaptation","Southeast Florida","avoided loss"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":538,"title":"Climate Risk and Real Estate: Emerging Practices for Market Assessment","authors":"","date":"2020-01-01","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["Global","US"],"institutions":["Urban Land Institute","Heitman","Arup","Milliman"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://knowledge.uli.org/reports/research-reports/2020/climate-risk-markets","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"ULI and Heitman's second collaboration on climate risk in real estate, following their 2019 report on investment decision-making, turning from asset-level to MARKET-level risk. It finds that leading investment managers and institutional investors now treat market-scale climate risk assessment as something that will shape future investment decisions, mirroring advances already made at the asset level. The report covers the climate-risk context, how investors assess risk at market scale, how markets respond through infrastructure and policy, and an actuarial perspective on measuring real estate risk contributed by Milliman, with Arup and academic collaborators on city fiscal health. It is written from interviews with ULI members, reconnecting with the investors interviewed for the 2019 report.","insights":["The unit of analysis moves from the building to the city: investors are beginning to judge a market by its resilience strategy and its infrastructure and policy response, which makes municipal resilience investment an input to private capital allocation rather than only a public good.","The report pairs two capabilities that rarely appear in one document: an actuarial view of measuring real estate risk from three Milliman consulting actuaries, and city fiscal health analysis from Arup with academics at Nebraska-Omaha and North Carolina State. Fiscal capacity and catastrophe modelling are treated as two halves of one market judgement.","It was published into the COVID-19 period and says so: chief resilience officers had shifted focus to pandemic resilience and cities had cut funding for infrastructure and sustainability, which the preface treats as evidence that behaviour can change quickly rather than as a reason to defer.","Heitman is described as managing approximately $43 billion and as among the investment managers furthest along in quantifying climate risk, so the practices reported are from a participant rather than an observer.","The stated purpose is symmetrical: better market-level understanding is meant to inform city efforts to address risk through policy and infrastructure, not only to inform investors deciding whether to enter a market."],"keywords":["market-level climate risk","real estate investment","catastrophe modelling","city fiscal health","resilience strategy"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":203,"title":"Managing Climate Risk in the U.S. Financial System","authors":"Leonardo Martinez Diaz, J. M. Keenan","date":"2020-01-01","year":2020,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5247742","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"'Managing Climate Risk in the U.S. Financial System' outlines the urgent need for enhanced regulatory frameworks and collaboration between public and private sectors to address the systemic risks posed by climate change to the U.S. financial system. It discusses the inadequacies of traditional risk modeling, emphasizing the importance of climate risk management, improved data transparency, and standardized disclosures as crucial components for maintaining financial stability and supporting sustainable investments.","insights":["Climate change poses significant risks to the stability of the U.S. financial system.","Effective climate risk management requires improved data, tools, and communication between regulators and financial institutions.","The lack of common definitions and standards for climate-related data hinders effective risk management.","Financial regulators must enhance institutional capacity and knowledge around climate risk management through research and engagement.","Investments in climate adaptation and resilience technologies are crucial for mitigating potential losses across economic sectors.","Regulators should support the creation of consistent climate risk data and classification systems to facilitate better risk assessment.","Collaborative efforts between government and private sectors are crucial for innovative finance solutions.","There is a growing demand for transparent climate-related disclosures from companies to assess long-term risks.","Understanding both physical and transition risks is essential for banks and investors to make informed decisions."],"keywords":["regulatory frameworks","risk management","data transparency","financial stability","sustainable investments"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":64,"title":"Atlanta: First Publicly Offered Environmental Impact Bond","authors":"","date":"2019-12-30","year":2019,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/atlanta-eib","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Atlanta Environmental Impact Bond (EIB), structured by Quantified Ventures and Atlanta's Department of Watershed Management, is a pioneering $14 million bond designed to fund six green infrastructure projects aimed at improving stormwater management in historically underserved neighborhoods. Closed on January 31, 2019, this bond represents a significant shift towards outcomes-based financing in public municipal markets, showcasing potential benefits such as flood risk reduction, enhanced water quality, job creation, and increased access to green spaces.","insights":["The EIB closed on January 31, 2019, marking it as the first publicly offered Environmental Impact Bond.","The $14 million bond finances six green infrastructure projects in economically distressed neighborhoods along Proctor Creek.","The bond uses a two-tiered performance structure that incentivizes higher performance in stormwater capture.","Atlanta's Department of Watershed Management anticipates the projects will absorb 55 million gallons of stormwater annually.","The projects are expected to protect nearly 500 homes from future flooding and create dozens of local jobs.","The EIB is linked to measurable environmental outcomes, such as flood risk reduction and water quality improvement.","Public issuance of the EIB opens avenues for mainstream municipal bond investors to participate in outcomes-based financing.","Innovations in the EIB include using aerial imagery for project evaluation instead of continuous sensor monitoring.","The financing approach highlights the importance of social equity and community engagement in environmental project planning.","The bond serves as a proof case for mobilizing future funding for green infrastructure initiatives in other cities."],"keywords":["green infrastructure","stormwater management","urban development","public investment","environmental justice"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":289,"title":"CLIMAFIN Handbook: Pricing Forward-Looking Climate Risks Under Uncertainty","authors":"Stefano Battiston, Antoine Mandel, Irene Monasterolo","date":"2019-11-08","year":2019,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["University of Zurich","Vienna University of Economics and Business","University of Paris 1 Pantheon-Sorbonne","Climate Finance Alpha"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3476586","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The CLIMAFIN Handbook (Part 1) presents a science-based framework for pricing forward-looking climate transition risks in financial contracts under deep uncertainty. Developed by researchers at University of Zurich, Vienna University of Economics and Business, and Paris School of Economics, it introduces climate-scenario-adjusted valuation models for equity holdings, corporate bonds, and sovereign bonds, alongside novel risk metrics, the Climate Spread and Climate Value-at-Risk (Climate VaR). The framework addresses structural limitations of traditional financial pricing models when applied to non-linear, path-dependent, fat-tailed climate risks, and is designed to support both investors managing portfolio exposure and financial supervisors designing prudential measures for systemic risk.","insights":["Traditional financial pricing models (assuming normally distributed shocks, complete markets, backward-looking metrics) are structurally inadequate for climate risk, which exhibits deep uncertainty, non-linearity, fat tails, path-dependency, and endogeneity, requiring entirely new frameworks.","The CLIMAFIN tool introduces two new risk metrics: the Climate Spread (change in bond spread conditional on a climate policy shock scenario) and the Climate Value-at-Risk (worst-case portfolio loss conditional on forward-looking climate policy shock scenarios), providing actionable signals for portfolio risk management.","Climate policy shocks are modeled as transitions from Business-as-Usual to IPCC-aligned scenarios (450 ppm and 500 ppm targets), with shocks calculated at the sector, country, and regional level using LIMITS IAM database trajectories, quantifying GVA impacts on fossil fuel, electricity fossil, and electricity renewable sectors.","For sovereign bonds, the framework links climate policy shocks to sovereign default probability via changes in net fiscal assets (tax revenues minus subsidies from climate-relevant sectors), with the elasticity of profitability to market share assumed at 1 (empirical range: 0.2\u20130.6), providing an upper-bound estimate of shock magnitude.","The investor risk management strategy is formulated as a minimax problem: minimize the worst-case Climate VaR across all feasible forward-looking climate policy shock scenarios, explicitly accounting for incomplete information and the absence of prior probabilities on scenario occurrence.","Recent empirical research cited in the handbook finds that investors are not yet pricing climate-related risks in portfolios, and that market benchmarks are carbon-intensive, creating a systematic mispricing gap with potential implications for price volatility, portfolio performance, and financial stability."],"keywords":["climate transition risk pricing","Climate Value-at-Risk","sovereign bond default probability","climate policy shock","forward-looking risk metrics","integrated assessment models","carbon stranded assets"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":464,"title":"Texas Flood Infrastructure Fund","authors":"","date":"2019-11-01","year":2019,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US","Texas"],"institutions":["Texas Water Development Board"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.adaptationclearinghouse.org/resources/texas-flood-infrastructure-fund-a.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Texas established the Flood Infrastructure Fund in 2019 to finance drainage, flood mitigation and flood control projects for political subdivisions, administered by the Texas Water Development Board and offering both grants and zero-interest loans. Eligible activities fall into four categories: flood protection planning for watersheds; planning, acquisition, design, construction and rehabilitation; federal award matching funds; and measures immediately effective in protecting life and property. Funding covers structural and non-structural work, explicitly including nature-based approaches, property acquisition, natural system restoration and permeable pavements. The grant-to-loan mix is set by a means test: a community with annual median household income below 50 per cent of the statewide figure that has had a federally declared flood disaster in the last five years can receive 100 per cent grant funding for flood protection planning, and the grant share falls as income rises. The grant share can be raised again for high unemployment, declining population, rural status, or where at least 30 per cent of project costs are green or nature based. Applicants must show benefits exceed costs, and the project area must have floodplain ordinances enforced to at least National Flood Insurance Program standards. The fund was authorised by Senate Bill 7 in 2019, approved by voters through Proposition 8 as a constitutional amendment, and initially capitalised with $793 million.","insights":["The grant-to-loan ratio is set by a formula rather than by discretion, sliding with median household income and adjusted upward for unemployment, population decline, rural status and green content, which makes affordability an explicit term of the instrument.","A 30 per cent green or nature-based share of project cost earns a larger grant share, which prices nature-based solutions into the subsidy rather than merely permitting them.","Voter approval of a constitutional amendment lets the Water Development Board administer the money without further appropriations, which insulates the fund from the annual budget cycle.","Two conditions tie the money to regulation: benefits must exceed costs, and the project area must enforce floodplain standards consistent with the NFIP, so the fund buys land-use compliance as well as infrastructure."],"keywords":["Texas Flood Infrastructure Fund","zero-interest loan","grant sliding scale","nature-based","floodplain ordinance","Texas Water Development Board","Proposition 8"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":292,"title":"Climate Risks in Financial Assets","authors":"Emanuele Campiglio, Pierre Monnin, Adrian von Jagow","date":"2019-11-01","year":2019,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Council on Economic Policies (CEP)"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.cepweb.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/11/CEP-DN-Climate-Risks-in-Financial-Assets.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This 2019 Council on Economic Policies discussion note by Campiglio, Monnin, and von Jagow reviews academic evidence on how climate-related physical and transition risks affect equity and debt instrument prices. It finds that hurricanes, droughts, and extreme temperatures already reduce firm cash flows and increase non-performing loans; conservative stress tests estimate portfolio losses of up to 10% within five years from transition shocks, rising to 40% under sudden expectation revisions in a no-transition physical-risk scenario. The paper concludes that financial markets do not adequately price in future climate costs and recommends mandatory climate stress tests incorporating expectation revisions and second-round network effects.","insights":["Conservative transition-risk stress tests (Vermeulen et al., 2018) find Dutch insurer and pension fund portfolios (holding equities, bonds, and loans), could lose up to 10% of value within 5 years under a combined policy and technology shock, without accounting for second-round effects or expectation revisions.","CISL (2015) estimates that a sudden investor expectation revision pricing in a no-transition (high physical risk) scenario could produce a 40% correction in a balanced portfolio within one year; a transition to a 2\u00b0C world reduces this to a 10% correction.","Battiston et al. (2017) find that second-round contagion through bank cross-exposures can more than double the initial loss from a transition shock: first-round equity losses of ~13% across fossil fuel, utilities, and energy-intensive sectors rise to ~28% once second-round effects are included.","Empirical evidence of market under-pricing: Hong et al. (2019) show drought trends forecast food-sector stock returns across 31 countries (1985\u20132014), and Kumar et al. (2019) show firm temperature sensitivity predicts future returns, both consistent with markets underreacting to climate risk.","Addoum et al. (2019) find analysts make no forecast revisions after 8,584 identified extreme temperature events in the US, indicating analysts do not integrate physical climate impacts into earnings expectations.","Chinese Clean Air Action (2013) caused default rates of high-polluting firms to rise ~50%, yet Chinese banks' loan spread increase of only 5.5% to high-polluting firms was insufficient to match the observed default risk, illustrating systematic under-pricing of transition risk in credit markets."],"keywords":["physical risk pricing","transition risk stress test","stranded assets","non-performing loans","climate Minsky moment","second-round effects","carbon risk premium"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":286,"title":"Introduction to Climate Value-at-Risk: Methodologies and Tools to Evaluate the Financial Impact of Climate-Related Risks and Opportunities","authors":"","date":"2019-11-01","year":2019,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["MSCI"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://feem-media.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/msci-presentation.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This November 2019 MSCI/Carbon Delta document introduces the Climate Value-at-Risk (CVaR) methodology, a framework for quantifying the net-present-value financial impact of climate-related risks and opportunities on companies and portfolios. It covers three components, policy (transition) risk, technology opportunities, and physical risk, across 1.5\u00b0C, 2\u00b0C, and 3\u00b0C warming scenarios, applied to listed equities, fixed income, and real estate. The tool draws on facility-level emissions data, 90 million patents across 400+ low-carbon technology groups, and probabilistic hazard models to produce aggregated CVaR metrics for regulatory compliance, risk management, and portfolio construction.","insights":["Carbon Delta's CVaR methodology decomposes climate financial impact into three components: policy/transition risk (emission compliance costs), technology opportunity (low-carbon patent valuation), and physical risk (facility-level hazard exposure), each projected 15 years forward to 2100.","Company-level scenario analysis illustrates stark divergence in compliance costs: a sample company with 34.1 MtCO2e emissions faces annual costs of -$140M under a 3\u00b0C/NDC scenario, -$551M under 2\u00b0C, and -$2.0B under a 1.5\u00b0C scenario, a 14x cost range across scenarios.","Physical risk is calculated as Expected Cost = Vulnerability \u00d7 Hazard \u00d7 Exposure, applied across seven hazard types (extreme heat/cold, heavy precipitation/snowfall, wind gusts, wildfires, coastal flooding, tropical cyclones, and fluvial flooding) using facility-level asset location data covering 500,000+ enterprise assets globally.","Technology opportunity is proxied through patent analysis, 90 million patents across 10,000+ companies and 400+ low-carbon technology groups, allowing CVaR to capture upside from R&D investment in sectors such as e-vehicles, wind, photovoltaic, and smart grids.","CVaR outputs are designed for multiple capital-markets use cases: regulatory compliance (TCFD reporting), 2\u00b0C alignment assessment, new financial product development, portfolio optimization, shareholder engagement, and alpha generation through green technology overweights.","The methodology uses Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) as the basis for the 3\u00b0C policy scenario, integrating country-level emission trajectories into facility- and company-level cost calculations, directly linking sovereign climate commitments to corporate financial valuation."],"keywords":["Climate Value-at-Risk","scenario analysis","transition risk","physical risk quantification","patent-based opportunity","carbon pricing","portfolio warming potential"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":617,"title":"Parametric Loss and Damage Insurance Schemes as a Means to Enhance Climate Change Resilience in Developing Countries","authors":"Morten Broberg","date":"2019-07-15","year":2019,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["University of Copenhagen"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14693062.2019.1641461","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A synthesis article in Climate Policy, volume 20 issue 6 pages 693 to 703, published online 15 July 2019, by Morten Broberg of the Faculty of Law at the University of Copenhagen, on parametric loss and damage insurance schemes as a means of enhancing climate change resilience in developing countries. It takes its starting point from Article 8 of the 2015 Paris Agreement, which calls on the parties to cooperate to address loss and damage, and examines what parametric schemes can and cannot do against that mandate. The article has been viewed 8,361 times and cited 15 times.","insights":["A law faculty rather than an economics or insurance one is the useful provenance. Article 8 of the Paris Agreement is a legal instrument, and the question of whether a parametric payout discharges a loss and damage obligation is a legal question before it is an actuarial one.","Loss and damage is the one part of climate finance where the instrument is asked to do compensation as well as risk transfer, and parametric cover is poorly suited to compensation because the payout is keyed to a trigger rather than to a loss. That tension is what the article exists to examine.","Eight thousand views and fifteen citations for a 2019 synthesis article make this a reference point in the literature rather than a primary result, and it should be cited as a synthesis."],"keywords":["loss and damage","parametric insurance","Paris Agreement Article 8","Warsaw International Mechanism","developing countries","compensation","climate resilience"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":597,"title":"California Wildfire Fund (Assembly Bill 1054)","authors":"California State Legislature","date":"2019-07-12","year":2019,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["California Public Utilities Commission"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=201920200AB1054","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The text of California Assembly Bill 1054, approved by the Governor on 12 July 2019, which restructured how catastrophic wildfire costs are paid for in California. It establishes the Wildfire Fund to pay eligible claims arising from a covered wildfire, continuously appropriating the money to a Wildfire Fund Administrator, and requires the Public Utilities Commission to open a rulemaking on using its existing authority to make electrical corporations collect a nonbypassable charge from ratepayers to support the fund, with funding coming from utility contributions and that charge. It authorises an electrical corporation to apply for a financing order allowing recovery of catastrophic wildfire costs through bonds secured by a rate component. It creates a safety certification that changes the burden of proof in a cost-recovery proceeding: ordinarily the utility must show by a preponderance of the evidence that its conduct was reasonable, but a utility holding a valid safety certification for the period in which the covered wildfire ignited has its conduct deemed reasonable unless a party raises serious doubt, at which point the burden returns. Reasonableness is defined against what a reasonable utility would have done in good faith at the time on the information then available. It also establishes a seven-member California Wildfire Safety Advisory Board, requires wildfire mitigation plans to cover at least three years from 2020, and authorises a penalty of up to three times the normal amount where a utility seeks recovery of costs already authorised.","insights":["**The safety certification is a resilience-linked incentive written into liability law rather than into a price.** Mitigation investment buys a presumption of reasonableness, which is worth real money in a cost-recovery proceeding, so the statute converts hardening expenditure into a reduction in regulatory risk. That is the resilience-bond logic in a regulatory instrument.","Three distinct financing mechanisms sit in one statute: a mutualised catastrophe fund, a ratepayer charge to capitalise it, and rate-secured securitisation of past costs. Combining all three in one jurisdiction is what makes the design unusual.","A nonbypassable charge is the design detail that makes the fund work and also makes it regressive: every ratepayer pays, whether or not they are in a fire zone, which is the cross-subsidy question any pooled resilience fund has to answer.","This is primary legislation rather than commentary on it, so its provisions can be read from the statutory text rather than from a description of them."],"keywords":["Wildfire Fund","nonbypassable charge","financing order","rate-secured bonds","safety certification","burden of proof","wildfire mitigation plan","AB 1054","cost recovery"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":296,"title":"An Inconvenient Cost: The Effects of Climate Change on Municipal Bonds","authors":"Marcus Painter","date":"2019-06-15","year":2019,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Moody's","S&P Global","Federal Emergency Management Agency","Bloomberg Philanthropies","University of Kentucky","Saint Louis University"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2019.06.006","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed Journal of Financial Economics study (2020) provides the first empirical evidence that municipal bond markets price physical climate change risk. Using a sample of 327,152 U.S. municipal bond issues from 2004\u20132017, the author finds that counties with higher sea-level-rise exposure, measured as mean annual loss as a percentage of GDP from Hallegatte et al. (2013), pay 23.4 basis points more in annualized issuance costs for long-term bonds (\u226525-year maturity) but no premium for short-term bonds. The effect is concentrated in bonds rated below AA\u2212/Aa3, and a difference-in-differences test around the 2006 Stern Review release confirms investor attention as a causal mechanism.","insights":["A 1% increase in county-level climate risk (sea-level-rise expected annual loss as % of GDP) raises long-term municipal bond annualized issuance costs by 23.4 basis points, translating to an average additional $1.7 million in annualized issuance costs per affected county over the sample period.","The climate risk premium is entirely absent for short-term bonds (\u003c25-year maturity) across all specifications, confirming the market prices the temporal horizon of physical risk, a key signal for allocators structuring long vs. short duration exposure in climate-exposed jurisdictions.","The premium is driven by bonds rated below AA\u2212/Aa3: a 1% increase in climate risk raises issuance costs by 52.7 basis points for sub-AA\u2212 long-term bonds, versus an insignificant effect for high-grade bonds, evidence that market pricing already differentiates credit quality in relation to climate risk, which has direct implications for Moody's proposed climate-driven downgrades.","Before the 2006 Stern Review, the issuance cost difference between climate and non-climate long-term bonds was an insignificant \u221215.9 basis points; after the Stern Review, the interaction term rises to 60.7 basis points (significant at 5%), demonstrating that investor attention, not just underlying risk, is a prerequisite for physical risk to be priced into fixed income.","New Orleans (Orleans Parish) carries the highest climate risk at 1.479% mean annual GDP loss; New York/Newark is lower in percentage terms (0.089%) but carries the largest absolute expected annual loss at over $2.1 billion, illustrating that percentage-based risk metrics can obscure enormous dollar exposures in dense urban markets."],"keywords":["sea level rise pricing","municipal bond issuance costs","physical risk premium","investor attention","credit rating climate risk","long-term debt maturity","coastal flood exposure"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":134,"title":"Uncertainty of Climate Policies and Implications for Economics and Finance: An Evolutionary Economics Approach","authors":"Irene Monasterolo, Andrea Roventini, Tim J. Foxon","date":"2019-06-05","year":2019,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global","Europe"],"institutions":["Ecological Economics","Vienna University of Economics and Business","Sant'Anna School of Advanced Studies","University of Sussex"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolecon.2019.05.012","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This 2019 special issue introduction in Ecological Economics argues that traditional climate economics and financial risk models (constrained by equilibrium conditions, linearity, and representative agents), are inadequate for capturing the complexity of climate risks and policy uncertainty. The authors survey nine papers applying evolutionary economics, agent-based models, Stock-Flow Consistent models, and network models to assess climate-aligned policies and financial instruments, identifying a USD 2.5 trillion annual investment gap through 2050 and highlighting systemic risks such as pension funds holding up to 45% of equity portfolios in carbon-intensive sectors.","insights":["A USD 2.5 trillion per year investment gap in climate mitigation and adaptation persists through 2050, requiring ambitious fiscal policies, development banks, and central bank engagement beyond market forces alone.","Pension funds hold up to 45% of their equity portfolios in carbon-intensive sectors; financial network reverberation can amplify these direct exposures into systemic risk, as shown by Battiston et al.'s (2017) climate stress-test.","Financial markets were not yet pricing climate risks into asset valuations as of 2018, despite evidence of higher risk associated with high-carbon indices emerging after the Paris Agreement.","Climate policy uncertainty creates a self-reinforcing barrier: investors unable to anticipate policies face abrupt asset price volatility and carbon stranded-asset losses once policies are introduced, which in turn discourages pre-emptive green investment.","Agent-based integrated assessment models (e.g., the DSK model by Lamperti et al.) generate larger climate damages than standard IAMs under comparable scenarios, and endogenously produce tipping points that shift growth from self-sustained to stagnation, findings with direct implications for physical risk pricing.","A mix of market-based carbon taxes and centralized public R&D spending on resource-saving technologies outperforms either policy alone for achieving low-carbon transition under hysteresis conditions.","Climate change causes long-term negative impacts on GDP growth, bank leverage, and asset prices in global simulations (2016\u20132120); central bank green QE programmes can partially mitigate climate-induced financial instability.","Voluntary TCFD-style climate disclosure is assessed as insufficient to scale up green private capital investment to the levels required by the Paris Agreement, pointing toward mandatory disclosure and taxonomy standards."],"keywords":["evolutionary economics","agent-based models","carbon stranded assets","climate stress-testing","green taxonomy","financial network models","low-carbon transition","climate policy uncertainty"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":599,"title":"Quintana Roo Coral Reef Parametric Insurance (Coastal Zone Management Trust)","authors":"Swiss Re","date":"2019-06-01","year":2019,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["Latin America"],"institutions":["Swiss Re","The Nature Conservancy"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.swissre.com/our-business/public-sector-solutions/case-studies/mexico-windstorm-cover.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Swiss Re's case study of the parametric hurricane cover on the Mesoamerican coral reef off Quintana Roo, Mexico, the first insurance policy written on a natural asset. It opens with the scale: the total economic value of the world's reefs is estimated at USD 9.9 trillion, a healthy reef is connected to a region's ability to sustain itself economically, and reefs provide protection against disasters and erosion. Quintana Roo's economy depends heavily on the reef and the tourism income tied to it, and both the public and private sectors wanted a more reliable source of funding for repair work after regular storm damage. Swiss Re developed the initial solution, insuring a stretch of reef and the adjacent beaches along the Yucatan Peninsula, with The Nature Conservancy and the State Government of Quintana Roo. As part of a public-private partnership the Coastal Zone Management Trust was established to take out the policy and to maintain the reef continuously. The cover is triggered by wind speed within a pre-defined area, and a payout is used by the Trust to fund the Reef Brigades, a team of local divers, marine biologists and others who collect and mend broken coral. The Swiss Re Foundation provided the grant that created the Reef Brigades. In 2020, Hurricane Delta triggered the insurance.","insights":["**The insured is a trust, not an owner.** Nobody owns a coral reef, so the structure had to manufacture an insurable interest: the Coastal Zone Management Trust holds the policy and the maintenance obligation together, which is what makes the payout usable. That device is the transferable part of this deal.","The payout funds **labour, not repair contracts**. The Reef Brigades are a standing team of divers and biologists, so the instrument pays for a capability that exists before the storm rather than for reconstruction afterwards, and the Swiss Re Foundation grant that created them is the concessional layer under the commercial one.","**Hurricane Delta triggered the policy in 2020**, so this is an instrument that has actually paid out rather than one announced or designed.","Wind speed in a pre-defined area is a cheap and fast trigger and a crude proxy for reef damage, which is the basis risk this structure accepts in exchange for speed of payout."],"keywords":["parametric insurance","coral reef","Coastal Zone Management Trust","Reef Brigades","Quintana Roo","natural asset insurance","wind speed trigger","Hurricane Delta","insurable interest"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":105,"title":"High Tide Tax: The Price to Protect Coastal Communities from Rising Seas","authors":"Sverre LeRoy, Richard Wiles","date":"2019-06-01","year":2019,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Center for Climate Integrity (CCI)","Resilient Analytics"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.climatecosts2040.org/files/ClimateCosts2040_Report.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This 2019 study by the Center for Climate Integrity and Resilient Analytics provides the first contiguous U.S. estimate of seawall construction costs needed to protect public coastal infrastructure from sea-level rise under moderate emissions scenarios (RCP4.5) by 2040. The headline finding: $416 billion in seawall costs across 22 states by 2040, rising to $518 billion by 2100, covering 50,145 miles of barriers. Florida faces the largest state-level bill at ~$76 billion. The study explicitly frames these costs as a fraction (10\u201315%) of total adaptation needs and positions fossil fuel industry liability as the central policy question.","insights":["The contiguous U.S. faces an estimated $416 billion in seawall construction costs by 2040 under a moderate emissions scenario (RCP4.5, 50th percentile), rising to $518 billion by 2100, representing only 10\u201315% of estimated total coastal adaptation costs over the same period.","Florida alone accounts for ~$76 billion in seawall costs by 2040 across 9,243 miles of coastline, with 23 counties each facing at least $1 billion in expenses, making it the single most financially exposed state in the analysis.","More than 130 counties face at least $1 billion in seawall costs by 2040; in 19 small communities the per-capita cost exceeds $1,000,000, and in 178 communities it exceeds $100,000, thresholds that signal managed retreat as the only financially viable option for many.","The study covers 137 congressional districts across 22 states; Republican districts collectively face $224 billion in costs vs. $192 billion for Democratic districts, but Republicans hold a disproportionate share of the highest-cost districts, with 24 of the top 40 most-expensive districts represented by Republicans.","Seawall costs are modeled only for 1-year storm surge protection against public infrastructure, not private property or broader resilience line items (utilities, healthcare, transportation, etc.); New York City's own comprehensive plan shows coastal protection is just 16\u201320% of total city-level resilience costs.","The study uses geographically specific, locally calibrated per-linear-foot cost estimates differentiated between coastal seawalls and inland bulkheads, with 150 m\u00b2 grid resolution tied to NOAA Medium Resolution Shoreline data, providing a replicable, scalable methodology for planning-level financial exposure assessment."],"keywords":["seawall construction costs","coastal infrastructure protection","sea-level rise projections","managed retreat","per capita adaptation cost","fossil fuel liability","RCP4.5 scenario"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":527,"title":"The Economic and Health Consequences of Climate Change: Testimony of Roy E. Wright to the House Committee on Ways and Means","authors":"Roy E. Wright","date":"2019-05-15","year":2019,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Federal Resilience Policy & Funding","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Insurance Institute for Business & Home Safety","Federal Emergency Management Agency","National Institute of Building Sciences"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://ibhs.org/public-policy/the-economic-and-health-consequences-of-climate-change/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Congressional testimony arguing that adaptation is a sound fiscal strategy as well as a public health and humanitarian one, and that much severe weather damage is avoidable if the whole building performance chain is addressed: where you build, how you design and construct, and how well you maintain and repair. IBHS is a 501(c)(3) funded by the property insurance industry to produce building safety science, and the testimony sets out three priorities: lead with the roof, solve with research on vulnerability and loss, and prevent avoidable damage. It presses for cost-effective provisions such as the sealed roof deck to be written into model building codes.","insights":["The headline return figure cited is the National Institute of Building Sciences finding that federal grants from FEMA, the Economic Development Administration and Housing and Urban Development returned a national benefit of $6 for every $1 invested.","Roof failure is identified as the dominant loss pathway: when roofs fail they trigger water infiltration, projectile damage and destruction of rooftop equipment, accounting for as much as 70 to 90 percent of insured residential losses in some disasters.","The sealed roof deck is offered as the worked example of asymmetric cost and benefit: taping horizontal and vertical joints before underlayment adds several hundred dollars to a roofing installation and can save tens of thousands if the roof cover is blown off.","IBHS's FORTIFIED standard is positioned as a voluntary specification for owners who want resilience beyond the mandatory levels in state and local building codes, and the testimony argues the sealed roof deck requirement should move from voluntary into the codes themselves.","The testimony connects adaptation to emissions without conflating them: the same actions reduce post-disaster debris that overwhelms landfills and the carbon dioxide released when buildings burn, which is offered as a co-benefit rather than a purpose."],"keywords":["FORTIFIED standard","sealed roof deck","building codes","benefit-cost ratio","insured residential losses"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":87,"title":"DC Water: First Ever Environmental Impact Bond","authors":"","date":"2019-05-01","year":2019,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Quantified Ventures"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.quantifiedventures.com/dc-water","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The DC Water Environmental Impact Bond represents the first instance of using this innovative financing mechanism to support green infrastructure projects aimed at mitigating combined sewer overflows in Washington, DC. By engaging private investors and linking returns to environmental performance, DC Water sought to improve water management, reduce pollution, and generate community benefits while minimizing taxpayer risk. The successful implementation of the bond led to significant reductions in stormwater runoff and the creation of green spaces, setting a precedent for similar initiatives in other cities.","insights":["DC Water implemented the first-ever Environmental Impact Bond to finance green infrastructure.","The bond was issued for $25 million, focusing on projects like permeable pavements and green roofs to address stormwater management.","The bond structure shifted performance risk from the government to private investors, reinforcing a pay-for-success model.","The project aimed to reduce combined sewer overflows significantly, improving water quality in local rivers and the Chesapeake Bay.","The bond's success resulted in the creation of new green spaces, enhancing community health and providing workforce development opportunities.","Upon achieving targeted metrics, the bond facilitated scaling up green infrastructure investments, potentially avoiding costly gray infrastructure solutions.","The Environmental Impact Bond not only tackled environmental goals but also engaged community stakeholders by creating visible benefits.","The National Green Infrastructure Certification Program emerged as part of workforce development efforts linked to the green infrastructure projects.","The success of the DC Water EIB has encouraged other cities to explore similar outcomes-based financing models for various infrastructure challenges.","The project demonstrated the potential of integrating financial innovation with environmental stewardship to deliver broad societal benefits."],"keywords":["infrastructure","stormwater","innovation","investors","community benefits"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":40,"title":"State of California Tackles Drought with IoT & Blockchain","authors":"Haley Walker","date":"2019-02-07","year":2019,"resource_type":"News Article / Blog Post","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["The Freshwater Trust","IBM Research","SweetSense Inc.","University of Colorado Boulder"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://thefreshwatertrust.org/state-of-california-tackles-drought-with-iot-blockchain","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The State of California is addressing groundwater sustainability challenges through a collaboration involving The Freshwater Trust, IBM Research, and SweetSense Inc. This initiative utilizes blockchain technology and IoT sensors to monitor and manage groundwater use in real-time, enabling transparent tracking and trading of water usage shares. The project aims to support farmers and other stakeholders in achieving sustainable groundwater practices while responding to the requirements of the Sustainable Groundwater Management Act.","insights":["The project is a partnership between The Freshwater Trust, IBM Research, and SweetSense Inc.","IoT sensors will be used to monitor groundwater usage and transmit data to satellites.","Blockchain technology will provide a transparent and immutable record of groundwater transactions.","Water consumers can trade groundwater shares as needed, helping to optimize resource allocation.","The initiative is aligned with California's Sustainable Groundwater Management Act, aiming for sustainable practices by 2040.","The pilot program will be implemented in the Sacramento-San Joaquin River Delta, a crucial water system for the state.","The project leverages previous research and technologies developed for water management in Kenya.","Data from the sensors will aid in decision-making and improve access to sustainable water supplies.","The Freshwater Trust has developed various programs in the basin since 2016 for groundwater and surface water management."],"keywords":["sensors","blockchain","groundwater","sustainability","agriculture"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":450,"title":"Financing Third Party Wildfire Damages: Options for Electric Utilities","authors":"Carolyn Kousky; Katherine Greig; Brett Lingle","date":"2019-02-01","year":2019,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["University of Pennsylvania"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://impact.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Financing-Third-Party-Wildfire-Damages-Options-for-Californias-Electric-Utilities.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Wharton Risk Management and Decision Processes Center report setting out how California's electric utilities could finance third party wildfire damages, written in February 2019 in the aftermath of utility-ignited fires. The report is organised as an options analysis rather than a recommendation. It reviews the financing mechanisms utilities currently use, then examines six disaster risk financing strategies in turn: funded self-insurance, commercial insurance, catastrophe bonds, an industry captive, a risk pool, and recovery bonds. It then addresses layering, that is how those instruments combine into a structure covering different tranches of loss, before turning to the separate question of funding, which it treats as a distinct choice among ratepayers, taxpayers and shareholders. That separation of financing mechanism from funding source is the report's organising distinction: the instruments determine when and how money is available, while the funding question determines who ultimately bears the cost.","insights":["The report's structure carries its argument: six financing mechanisms are analysed separately from three funding sources, so the instrument choice and the question of who pays are treated as independent decisions rather than one.","Layering is given its own section, which is the practical point for a risk this large: no single mechanism covers the full loss distribution, so self-insurance, commercial cover, catastrophe bonds, a captive, a pool and recovery bonds occupy different tranches.","An industry captive and a risk pool are both considered, which are the two ways multiple utilities could mutualise a risk none can carry alone.","Recovery bonds appear as a financing mechanism, which is the securitisation of a future ratepayer charge and therefore the instrument that most directly puts the cost on ratepayers.","Third party damages, rather than a utility's own asset losses, is the exposure being financed, which is a liability rather than a property risk and is why conventional catastrophe cover is only part of the answer."],"keywords":["wildfire liability","electric utilities","catastrophe bonds","industry captive","risk pool","recovery bonds","self-insurance","risk layering","California"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":593,"title":"Catastrophe Bonds: A Primer and Retrospective (Chicago Fed Letter No. 405)","authors":"Andy Polacek","date":"2018-11-01","year":2018,"resource_type":"Policy Brief","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.chicagofed.org/publications/chicago-fed-letter/2018/405","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Chicago Fed Letter number 405 of 2018, by senior research analyst Andy Polacek, a primer and twenty-year retrospective on the catastrophe bond market, DOI 10.21033/cfl-2018-405. It dates the market's origin to the aftermath of Hurricane Andrew, which struck Florida and the Gulf Coast in 1992 causing $27 billion in damages of which $15.5 billion was insured, was the costliest hurricane to make US landfall at the time, and led to the failure of eight insurance companies while pushing others toward insolvency. Insurers re-evaluated coastal exposure, homeowners' premiums in coastal communities rose markedly, and large insurers and reinsurers initially cut catastrophe exposure, so new capital had to be brought into reinsurance and the industry created the catastrophe bond. The first were issued in 1997, giving insurers access to broader financial markets and offering hedge funds, pension funds and mutual funds a return uncorrelated with other financial instruments in exchange for assuming catastrophe risk. The mechanics are explained through Mariah Re Ltd., issued in November 2010 for American Family Mutual Insurance Co.: a special purpose vehicle sat between investors and the insurer, transferring severe thunderstorm and tornado risk across the US off AFMI's balance sheet, providing up to $100 million of compensation once estimated industry losses exceeded an $825 million attachment point, paying $1 for each additional $1 of covered losses to the limit, with investors receiving a 6.25 per cent annual coupon over a three-year term and their $100 million of principal held in a US Treasury money market fund.","insights":["The market's origin story is a capacity story rather than a pricing story: after Hurricane Andrew there was not enough reinsurance capital at any price, so the instrument exists to widen the pool of capital rather than to lower the cost of the existing pool.","Uncorrelated return is the investor-side reason the instrument works, and it is also its fragility: the appeal depends on catastrophe risk staying uncorrelated with financial markets, which is exactly what a systemic climate repricing would break.","Mariah Re is a usefully honest example because the deal failed in practice. Naming the attachment point, the industry-loss trigger and the coupon lets a reader see how an industry-index trigger separates the sponsor's recovery from its own losses.","A Federal Reserve bank publishing a catastrophe bond primer in 2018 is itself evidence about where the instrument sat in official thinking: explained to a policy audience rather than confined to the reinsurance trade."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond","Hurricane Andrew","attachment point","special purpose vehicle","industry loss trigger","uncorrelated return","Mariah Re","reinsurance capacity"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":463,"title":"RELEASE: Forest Resilience Bond to Help Fund $4.6 Million Restoration Project to Mitigate Wildfire Risk in Tahoe National Forest","authors":"","date":"2018-11-01","year":2018,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["World Resources Institute","Blue Forest","Rockefeller Foundation","Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation","Calvert Impact Capital","United States Forest Service"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.wri.org/news/release-forest-resilience-bond-help-fund-46-million-restoration-project-mitigate-wildfire-risk","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Forest Resilience Bond, developed by Blue Forest Conservation with the World Resources Institute, reached its first deployment: a $4.6 million restoration project across 15,000 acres of the North Yuba River watershed in Tahoe National Forest. The structure is the point. Private capital funds the upfront cost of forest restoration today, and multiple beneficiaries share the cost of reimbursing investors over time, on the logic that everyone who gains from forest health contributes a portion. Financing came from The Rockefeller Foundation, the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Calvert Impact Capital and CSAA Insurance Group, a mix of philanthropic, impact and insurance capital. The restoration itself uses ecologically based tree thinning, meadow restoration, prescribed burning and invasive species management, all prescribed by the Forest Service, subject to public comment and carried out by existing restoration crews, and designed to reduce severe fire risk, improve watershed health and protect water resources. WRI's Todd Gartner frames the rationale as everyone contributing a little toward forest health so that ecological and economic benefit is realised by all.","insights":["This is a worked Benefit to Instrument structure: the diffuse benefits of forest restoration, reduced fire risk, watershed health and water security, are converted into a repayment stream by having multiple beneficiaries share the cost of reimbursing investors.","The capital stack mixes philanthropic, impact and insurance money, and the presence of an insurer among the investors is notable because the insurer is also a beneficiary of reduced wildfire loss.","The restoration is prescribed by the Forest Service and delivered by existing crews, so the instrument finances the timing of public land management rather than substituting for it, which is what makes it replicable.","The bond moved from idea to deployment at $4.6 million over 15,000 acres, which is small enough to be a demonstration and specific enough to price."],"keywords":["Forest Resilience Bond","Blue Forest Conservation","wildfire risk reduction","beneficiary cost-sharing","forest restoration","impact investment","Tahoe National Forest","prescribed burning"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":15,"title":"Climate Adaptation Finance and Investment in California","authors":"Jesse M. Keenan","date":"2018-10-26","year":2018,"resource_type":"Book","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Harvard University Graduate School of Design","CA Governor's Office of Land Use and Climate Innovation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://lci.ca.gov/docs/20181106-Keenan_Climate_Adaptation_Finance_and_Investment_in_California_2018.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource 'Climate Adaptation Finance and Investment in California' discusses innovative financial strategies and investment opportunities to enhance climate resilience in California. It reviews the current landscape of climate adaptation financing, highlights the importance of integrating public and private funding, and stresses community engagement to effectively address climate vulnerabilities. The document outlines various funding mechanisms and frameworks aimed at systematically supporting adaptation initiatives to promote sustainable development while addressing specific climate risks across the state.","insights":["California is actively seeking innovative financial solutions to address climate adaptation challenges.","Public-private partnerships play a crucial role in mobilizing funds for climate adaptation projects.","Investment in green infrastructure and technology is critical for enhancing urban resilience.","Community engagement is essential for ensuring that climate adaptation efforts meet local needs and priorities.","Monitoring and evaluation frameworks are necessary to assess the effectiveness of climate adaptation investments.","There is a need for clear policies and guidelines to streamline financing processes for adaptation initiatives.","Data-driven approaches are vital for informing investment decisions and prioritizing adaptation projects.","Equity considerations must be integrated into climate finance strategies to ensure vulnerable communities benefit."],"keywords":["innovation","collaboration","sustainability","infrastructure","community engagement"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":535,"title":"Procuring Resilience","authors":"","date":"2018-10-01","year":2018,"resource_type":"Tool / Resource Hub","themes":["Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Kresge Foundation","Quantified Ventures","Meridiam"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.refocuspartners.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Procuring_Resilience.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A toolkit treating procurement as an entry point for resilience innovation rather than an obstacle to it, produced by re:focus partners and The Atlas Marketplace from the Procuring Resilience Opportunities project launched in January 2018. Seven cities and eight private sector implementing partners applied three procurement tools already used in large cities to water infrastructure problems in smaller ones: the Request for Ideas, the Competition or Challenge, and the Performance Contract. Its deliberate scope is working inside existing procurement systems rather than reforming procurement law, and its three elements are problem framing, tool selection, and procurement definition.","insights":["The performance contract is the financing instrument in the set: a form of agreement where payments are linked to achieving specific pre-stated measurable benchmarks or verified outcomes, which is outcomes-based finance arriving through the procurement door rather than the capital markets door.","The design constraint is what makes this usable: it explicitly declines to reform procurement processes or change state or federal legislative rules, and instead finds what cities can do immediately within their current systems, which is why capacity rather than authority is the selection variable.","Problem framing is treated as the step that determines whether innovation is possible at all: being too prescriptive limits innovation and discourages solutions with wider benefits, so the toolkit works backward from target outcomes, using the Ansari X-Prize as the model.","Tool selection is scored rather than chosen, by questionnaire and scorecard, and assigns a degree of difficulty matched to the problem and to the city's available resources and local implementation capacity.","The seven pilot cities are Anchorage, Camden, El Paso, Gary, Imperial Beach, Norfolk and Providence, which is a deliberate small and mid-sized city cohort rather than the large cities the tools came from.","The implementing partner list is named in full, Quantified Ventures with SCAPE, OptiRTC, Infrashares, RePipe, Meridiam North America, RISE and 120WaterAudit, so the delivery chain for the procurement model is specified rather than implied."],"keywords":["procurement innovation","performance contract","request for ideas","water infrastructure","municipal capacity"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":523,"title":"Financing the Response to Climate Change: The Pricing and Ownership of U.S. Green Bonds","authors":"Malcolm Baker, Daniel Bergstresser, George Serafeim, Jeffrey Wurgler","date":"2018-10-01","year":2018,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Harvard Business School","Brandeis University","New York University","National Bureau of Economic Research","Climate Bonds Initiative","Sustainalytics","Bloomberg L.P.","European Investment Bank","World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w25194","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A study of the United States green bond market, defining green bonds as bonds whose proceeds fund an environmentally sensitive purpose, and testing pricing and ownership predictions from a framework in which some investors derive nonpecuniary utility from holding a security. The sample is 2,083 green US municipal bonds issued 2010 to 2016 and 19 green US corporate bonds issued 2014 to 2016, about $12 billion of par outstanding each at the end of 2016. The findings are that green municipal bonds are issued at a premium to otherwise similar ordinary bonds, that green bonds are more closely held than ordinary bonds, particularly small or nearly riskless ones, and that both effects are strongest for bonds externally certified as green. The subject is mitigation finance rather than resilience or adaptation.","insights":["Green municipal bonds are issued at after-tax yields roughly 6 basis points below otherwise equivalent ordinary bonds, controlling for ratings, maturity, the yield curve, tax status and issuer fixed effects. Investors accept a lower return, so the premium is paid by the issuer's benefit rather than earned by the buyer.","CERTIFICATION IS PRICED, AND MORE THAN THE GREEN LABEL IS. Bonds registered with the Climate Bonds Initiative yield 26 basis points below comparable ordinary bonds in the first specification, which the authors note is the yield reduction equivalent to climbing two ratings notches, and still 15 basis points per year with issuer fixed effects and the full control set.","Certification was rare and new in this sample: 11.7 per cent of green municipal bonds carried any third-party certification, most often from Sustainalytics, and only 6.6 per cent of the green sample was registered with the Climate Bonds Initiative. The first third-party certified bonds were issued at the end of 2014 and every CBI-registered bond in the sample was issued in 2016.","The market was small in relative terms: about 2 per cent of new municipal issuance was green in 2016 and about 0.3 per cent of corporate issuance, despite the two segments carrying similar total par outstanding of roughly $12 billion each.","Green is a use-of-proceeds label and not a credit structure, and the data shows the difference: only 14.7 per cent of green municipals are general obligation bonds against 46.8 per cent of ordinary municipals, so a green bond is far more likely to be secured on a specific project's revenue than on the issuer's tax base."],"keywords":["green bonds","municipal bond pricing","third-party certification","Climate Bonds Initiative","ownership concentration","nonpecuniary utility"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":99,"title":"Adaptation Finance Challenges: Characteristic Patterns Facing California Local Governments and Ways to Overcome Them","authors":"Susanne C. Moser, Julia A. Ekstrom, Julia Kim, Samantha Heitsch","date":"2018-08-01","year":2018,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","California"],"institutions":["Susanne Moser Research & Consulting","University of California Davis","Local Government Commission","ICF International","California Energy Commission"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.energy.ca.gov/sites/default/files/2019-12/Governance_CCCA4-CNRA-2018-007_ada.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The resource outlines the significant challenges California local governments face in accessing adaptation finance for climate resilience initiatives. It identifies barriers such as limited funding, bureaucratic hurdles, insufficient technical capacity, and governance complexities, while suggesting strategies like collaboration, community engagement, innovative financing mechanisms, and capacity building to enhance local resilience against climate impacts.","insights":["Local governments struggle with securing adequate funding for climate adaptation due to bureaucratic complexities and inconsistent resource availability.","Community engagement and stakeholder inclusivity are critical for the success of adaptation initiatives and for garnering public support.","Collaboration among different governmental levels and sectors can enhance resource mobilization and improve access to adaptation finance.","Capacity building within local governments is essential for effectively managing adaptation projects and navigating funding landscapes.","Innovative financing mechanisms, including public-private partnerships, can unlock additional resources for climate adaptation efforts."],"keywords":["funding challenges","community engagement","capacity building","collaboration","governance"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":449,"title":"The Emerging Private Residential Flood Insurance Market in the United States","authors":"Carolyn Kousky; Howard Kunreuther; Brett Lingle; Leonard Shabman","date":"2018-07-01","year":2018,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Pennsylvania"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://impact.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Emerging-Flood-Insurance-Market-Report.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Wharton Risk Center report on the emerging private residential flood insurance market in the United States, written when private flood cover was beginning to grow alongside the National Flood Insurance Program. The report maps residential flood insurance as it then stood: an overview of private flood insurance, background on the NFIP, the size and shape of the flood insurance gap, and the market's structure. Its structural analysis is the substantive contribution, distinguishing admitted carriers from the excess and surplus lines market, examining how policies are distributed, and treating reinsurance as a determinant of how much private capacity exists. The framing question throughout is what role private capital can play in a market where a federal programme has been the dominant writer for decades, and what the growth of private cover implies for the NFIP, for policyholders and for the gap between those who hold flood insurance and those exposed to flood risk.","insights":["The admitted versus excess and surplus distinction is the analytical core, because a surplus lines policy is not subject to the same rate and form regulation, which is what allows private carriers to price flood risk where admitted carriers will not.","Reinsurance is treated as the determinant of private capacity, so the availability of residential flood cover depends on a market most policyholders never see.","The report is a 2018 baseline of a market that has since changed substantially, which makes it most useful as the reference point for measuring how far private flood cover has actually grown.","Distribution is analysed separately from underwriting, which matters because a flood policy that exists but is not offered through the channels homeowners use does not close the gap."],"keywords":["private flood insurance","NFIP","admitted carriers","excess and surplus lines","reinsurance","flood insurance gap","policy distribution"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":45,"title":"Natural Hazard Mitigation Saves Interim Report","authors":"","date":"2018-06-11","year":2018,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Federal Emergency Management Agency","National Institute of Building Sciences"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/2020-07/fema_mitsaves-factsheet_2018.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The 'Natural Hazard Mitigation Saves Interim Report' emphasizes the critical benefits of investing in natural hazard mitigation, demonstrating how proactive measures significantly reduce disaster-related damages and costs. By showcasing compelling data and case studies, the report argues for early investments in mitigation as a strategic approach to enhancing community resilience and achieving substantial long-term savings, with every federal dollar spent on mitigation reportedly saving an average of $6 in avoided losses.","insights":["Natural hazard mitigation leads to significant long-term cost savings, with every dollar spent saving multiple dollars in avoided damages.","Investments in mitigation enhance community resilience and improve public safety, contributing to fewer disaster-related fatalities.","Historical data supports that proactive investments in hazard mitigation yield economic benefits that exceed their costs.","Mitigation strategies tailored to local conditions improve the effectiveness of resilience efforts and community engagement.","Data-driven decision-making is essential for maximizing the impact of hazard mitigation projects and ensuring sustained investment."],"keywords":["mitigation","infrastructure","community resilience","economic resilience","disaster prevention"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":511,"title":"Climate Resilience 2018 Handbook","authors":"David Batchelor, Joel Makower, Lucy Nottingham, Jaclyn Yeo, Michael Lierow, Sirko Siemssem, Cornelia Neumann, Andreea Achimescu, Bob Orr, Eric Nelsen, Geoff Murray, Bjoern Maul, John Davies, Jane Ambachtsheer, John Colas, Ilya Khaykin, Alban Pyanet, Peter Reynolds, Gaurav Kwatra, Alex Bernhardt, Charles Whitmore, Thomas London, Robert Wykoff, Amal-Lee Amin, Benjamin Chee, Chantale LaCasse, Tom Markovic, Ellen Charnley, Melita Simic, Bev Adams, Duncan Ellis","date":"2018-02-01","year":2018,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Disclosure / Metrics / Taxonomies","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global","US","Europe"],"institutions":["Marsh","Guy Carpenter","Mercer","Oliver Wyman","Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures","Financial Stability Board","World Bank","Inter-American Development Bank","Climate Bonds Initiative","Flood Re","Caribbean Catastrophe Risk Insurance Facility","United Nations Environment Programme","OECD","World Economic Forum","European Investment Bank","BlackRock","Bank of England","NERA Economic Consulting"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.guycarp.com/insights/2018/02/marsh-mclennan-companies-climate-resilience-handbook-2018.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The inaugural Marsh & McLennan Companies Climate Resilience Handbook collects twenty short articles from Marsh, Guy Carpenter, Mercer and Oliver Wyman in three sections: strategies for climate resilience, financing for climate resilience, and risk management for climate resilience. The financing section is the substantive core for this corpus, covering TCFD scenario analysis for banks, the mitigation skew of a green bond market of nearly $900 billion set against the adaptation financing deficit, public private flood pools such as Flood Re, sovereign and corporate disaster risk financing, credit enhancement to close a green financing gap estimated at $2.5 trillion to $4.8 trillion, and the World Bank Pilot Auction Facility's pay for performance price guarantees. The risk management section adds captive insurers used to fund climate exposures, gaps in Director and Officer cover as climate litigation begins, and satellite and drone imagery for post event loss assessment. The financing section reprints the 2017 standalone collection held as NNN 016, so handbook pages 34 to 67 are already covered by that record's twenty claims.","insights":["UNEP puts the annual climate adaptation investment requirement at $56 to $76 billion in 2015, rising to $140 to $300 billion by 2030, roughly $1.5 to $3 trillion over fifteen years, yet only 3 to 5 percent of green bond issuance has been tied to an adaptation project and all of it sits in the water sector.","Labelled green bonds stood at $221 billion outstanding in 2017, with a further $674 billion identified as climate aligned by the Climate Bonds Initiative for a market of nearly $900 billion, and the vast majority of the projects financed pursue mitigation rather than adaptation.","The green financing gap is estimated at $2.5 trillion to $4.8 trillion and is attributed largely to inadequate risk adjusted returns rather than to a shortage of capital, which is why credit enhancement and de risking instruments are offered as the remedy.","The UK's Flood Re pool is backed by a GBP 2.1 billion multi-year reinsurance programme and is expected to benefit an estimated 350,000 high flood risk homeowners, and is presented as the worked example of a public private risk transfer mechanism.","Sovereign catastrophe risk transfer is established rather than experimental: Mexico's cat bond covered US$160 million across three regions, and CCRIF operates as a regional catastrophe fund letting Caribbean governments limit fiscal exposure.","A one percent rise in insurance penetration is associated with a 13 percent reduction in total uninsured losses, a 22 percent reduction in taxpayer contribution following a disaster, and additional investment equivalent to one percent of national GDP.","Insurance covers an estimated 40 percent of catastrophic losses in developed countries, and of the record 2017 catastrophe year US$133 billion was insured, with the remainder falling on public and private balance sheets."],"keywords":["adaptation financing deficit","green bonds","TCFD disclosure","sovereign risk transfer","captive insurance","credit enhancement","Flood Re","insurance protection gap"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":573,"title":"Using Simulations to Forecast Homeowner Response to Sea Level Rise in South Florida: Will They Stay or Will They Go?","authors":"Galen Treuer; Kenneth Broad; Robert Meyer","date":"2017-12-22","year":2017,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["University of Miami","University of Pennsylvania"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2017.10.008","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A paper in Global Environmental Change by Galen Treuer and Kenneth Broad of the University of Miami with Robert Meyer of the University of Pennsylvania, using simulations to forecast how South Florida homeowners will respond to sea level rise, and asking whether they stay or go. It is a behavioural study of the decision that sits underneath every property-market projection in a subsiding coastal market: what an owner actually does as flooding becomes more frequent, rather than what a discounted-cash-flow model says the property is worth.","insights":["The homeowner decision is the link the paper models rather than assumes: it simulates how owners respond to risk information instead of inferring the response from property market outcomes.","Stay or go is not a binary in financing terms: an owner who stays but stops maintaining, or who cannot sell, produces a different credit outcome from one who leaves, and the simulation approach is what lets those paths be separated before the data exists.","South Florida is the test bed, chosen because the exposure is severe and the market is liquid enough for the simulated responses to be observable.","The article is paywalled, so the version of record for any quotation is the cached text rather than the publisher's page."],"keywords":["sea level rise","homeowner behaviour","South Florida","simulation","property markets","managed retreat","coastal risk"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":72,"title":"Miami Forever Bond","authors":"","date":"2017-11-01","year":2017,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Florida"],"institutions":["City of Miami Office of Capital Improvements"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.miami.gov/My-Government/Departments/Office-of-Capital-Improvements/Miami-Forever-Bond","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Miami Forever Bond is a $400 million general obligation bond approved in 2017 to fund citywide infrastructure and resilience projects over 30 years. It finances stormwater system upgrades, seawall repairs, wetland restoration, park improvements, affordable housing, urban tree canopy expansion, road and sidewalk elevations, water quality enhancements and equitable community initiatives to address sea-level rise and flooding.","insights":["The bond is executed in three phases, Immediate Impact, Gain Momentum and Long Term Solutions, with a first tranche of $58.7 million across more than 100 ready projects, so the programme is structured around deployment capacity rather than around a single capital raise.","Tranche one is split into Series A for infrastructure, including sea-level rise and flood prevention, and Series B for affordable housing, which puts resilience and housing inside one general obligation instrument.","The Gain Momentum phase is explicitly a capacity assessment, addressing whether the city can deliver 100 projects in three years, and names alternative procurement methods and a risk management plan as the response.","The long-term phase commits to refining the project selection model from lessons learned and to updating zoning, planning and building standards so new development maximises resilience benefits, which is a regulatory output funded by a bond.","A city commission appointed civilian oversight board is named as part of the financial monitoring and reporting, which is the accountability structure attached to the instrument."],"keywords":["sea-level rise","flood mitigation","green infrastructure","affordable housing","water quality"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":544,"title":"A Guide for Public-Sector Resilience Bond Sponsorship: Insuring for Resilience","authors":"Shalini Vajjhala, James Rhodes","date":"2017-09-01","year":2017,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["Rockefeller Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.refocuspartners.com/wp-content/uploads/pdf/RE.bound-Program-Report-September-2017.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A practical guide for public-sector sponsors of resilience bonds, the second phase of the RE.bound Program, which in its first phase assembled insurance industry collaborators to validate the concept of a new class of bonds built on catastrophe bond structures. It sets out the building blocks of resilience bonds, the bond structure, why governments should look to insurance for resilience finance, and a sample bond design, then gives guidance on whether a resilience bond is a good fit and three entry points to insuring for resilience. Five worked opportunities follow, organised by structure: re:issue for wind and earthquake, re:balance for transit systems and flood protections, and re:align for small islands and urban slums.","insights":["THE CORE MECHANISM IS THE REBATE: a resilience bond is a catastrophe bond whose pricing reflects the risk reduction delivered by a resilience project, so the premium saving becomes a funding source for the project itself.","The report names three distinct structures rather than one product, and the names describe the mechanism: re:issue where an existing cat bond is reissued at a lower price after a project, re:balance where risk transfer and risk reduction are traded off in one portfolio, and re:align where the sponsor and the beneficiary are different parties.","The framing argument is fiscal: governments are insurers of last resort and bear disaster costs whether or not they buy cover, so insurance pricing is already implicitly on the public balance sheet and a resilience bond makes it explicit.","The five opportunities are deliberately unlike each other, spanning wind, earthquake, transit systems, flood protections and small islands with urban slums, which is how the report tests whether the structure generalises beyond coastal flood.","The report is careful to state what it is not: it contemplates no specific financial transaction and endorses no specific instrument, a disclaimer that travels with any claim taken from the sample bond design.","This is phase 2 of a two-phase programme; the phase 1 output, Leveraging Catastrophe Bonds as a Mechanism for Resilient Infrastructure Finance of December 2015, is a separate document."],"keywords":["resilience bond","catastrophe bond","premium rebate","risk reduction pricing","public-sector sponsor"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":598,"title":"Resilience Dividend Valuation Model: Framework Development and Initial Case Studies","authors":"Craig A. Bond; Aaron Strong; Nicholas Burger; Sarah Weilant; Uzaib Saya; Anita Chandra","date":"2017-08-01","year":2017,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["US","Global"],"institutions":["RAND Corporation","Rockefeller Foundation"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RR2129.html","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A 2017 RAND Corporation report, ISBN 978-0-8330-9896-2, developing the Resilience Dividend Valuation Model in partnership with the Rockefeller Foundation, with six case studies selected jointly by the two organisations. Its starting complaint is precise: policymakers, practitioners and investors who want the greatest benefit from the resilience projects they support lack effective tools to estimate net benefits, and quantification has instead relied on indices and scorecards in which indicators assumed to correlate with resilience are measured and aggregated. Those indices, the authors argue, may be useful once validated but say nothing about the value of a resilience investment, which is what a decision-maker needs to make an evidence-based choice consistent with maximising social welfare. The report defines the resilience dividend as the difference in net benefits between the outcome of a resilience project and business as usual, meaning no project at all, and presents a conceptual modelling framework for evaluating that difference in a decision-analysis setting. It is written for practitioners, analysts and researchers to use in conceptualising systems and interventions in a structural economic framework and in planning the data collection and modelling needed to estimate parts of the dividend.","insights":["The distinction between an index and a valuation is the whole argument: a resilience score cannot be financed, a net benefit can, and the model exists to produce the second rather than the first.","Defining the dividend against business as usual rather than against a counterfactual project is a strong and contestable choice. It makes the dividend large and it makes it hard to attribute, because the comparison is with doing nothing rather than with the next best use of the money.","The authors are explicit that the framework lets a user estimate **parts** of the dividend, not the whole, which is an honest boundary and the reason the model has not displaced scorecards in practice.","The report is a RAND and Rockefeller Foundation product and is the first-party statement of the resilience-dividend framework, rather than a description of it."],"keywords":["resilience dividend","net benefit valuation","resilience indices","decision analysis","business as usual counterfactual","RDVM","social welfare"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":16,"title":"Financing for Climate Resilience","authors":"Jane Ambachtsheer, John Colas, Ilya Khaykin, Alban Pyanet, Peter Reynolds, Gaurav Kwatra, Alex Bernhardt, Charles Whitmore, Jaclyn Yeo, Thomas London, Robert Wykoff, Amal-Lee Amin","date":"2017-06-02","year":2017,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Mercer","Oliver Wyman","Marsh"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.marsh.com/content/dam/marsh/Documents/PDF/US-en/Climate-resilience-handbook-2018.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The excerpt highlights the critical need for green financing, which is essential for building resilient organizations and societies against climate change. With a significant financing gap estimated between $2.5 trillion to $4.8 trillion, greater collaboration between public and private sectors is required to close this gap and effectively support sustainable investment and climate resilience initiatives following the 2015 Paris Climate Agreement.","insights":["The global green financing gap is estimated at $2.5 trillion to $4.8 trillion.","Sustainable investment increased by 25% from 2014 to 2016, reaching $23 trillion.","Closing the green financing gap is crucial for meeting sustainable development goals.","Collaboration between public and private sectors is necessary to mobilize private capital.","Efficient fund transmission to green projects is key to addressing climate challenges.","Organizations face challenges in financing climate resilience and sustainable growth.","Expert insights from Marsh & McLennan Companies highlight barriers to financing flows.","The 2018 Climate Resilience Handbook aims to provide strategies for financing resilience."],"keywords":["sustainable investment","public-private collaboration","financing gap","fund transmission","development goals"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":112,"title":"Does Federal Disaster Assistance Crowd out Flood Insurance?","authors":"Carolyn Kousky, Erwann O. Michel-Kerjan, Paul A. Raschky","date":"2017-05-30","year":2017,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Pennsylvania","Monash University"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeem.2017.05.010","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This peer-reviewed empirical study (Journal of Environmental Economics and Management, 2018) provides the first causal estimates of whether U.S. federal disaster aid crowds out household flood insurance purchases. Using a ZIP-code-level panel of NFIP policies (2000\u20132011) combined with FEMA Individual Assistance grant and SBA disaster loan data, the authors employ fixed-effects and instrumental variable models, instrumenting for aid using politically motivated variation in disaster declarations tied to swing counties and presidential election years. They find that receiving IA grants reduces average flood insurance coverage per policy by $4,000\u2013$5,000 (roughly 3% of mean coverage), while SBA loans have no crowding-out effect. The insurance purchase requirement attached to IA grants prevents declines in take-up rates.","insights":["Receiving FEMA Individual Assistance grants reduces average flood insurance coverage per policy by $4,000\u2013$5,000 in IV models, equivalent to roughly 3% of mean policy-level coverage ($151,716), and exceeds the average IA grant size of $2,984, implying the behavioral response to aid outweighs its dollar value.","Crowding out occurs on the intensive margin (coverage amounts) but not the extensive margin (take-up rates); the federal requirement that IA recipients purchase flood insurance appears effective at maintaining take-up levels, a policy lever directly relevant to NFIP solvency.","A $1,000 increase in the average IA grant reduces average coverage by approximately $1,600 per policy (IV model), suggesting a non-linear, amplified behavioral response, coverage reduction exceeds the grant increment received.","SBA low-interest disaster loans (up to $200,000 for homeowners) show no systematic crowding-out effect and may have a small crowding-in effect, likely because loans must be repaid, preserving the incentive to insure.","Presidential disaster declarations increased from 15\u201320/year in the 1950s to ~60/year in the 2000s, peaking at 99 in 2011; two-thirds are flood-related, and federal relief for the 2005 hurricane season covered roughly 70% of total estimated damages, illustrating the fiscal exposure dynamic the paper quantifies.","The IV strategy exploits politically motivated aid variation: Garrett and Sobel (2003) found federal disaster relief averages $140 million higher in election years; this exogenous variation isolates causal effects on insurance demand and is applicable to future research on public-private risk transfer.","NFIP covered $1.278 trillion in assets and collected $3.2 billion in premiums in 2011; any systematic crowding-out of coverage by federal aid directly undermines program revenue and increases taxpayer exposure to uninsured flood losses."],"keywords":["flood insurance demand","Samaritan's dilemma","NFIP","individual assistance grants","disaster declarations","crowding-out effect","swing county instrument"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":86,"title":"Coastal Wetlands and Flood Damage Reduction: Using Risk Industry-Based Models to Assess Natural Defenses in the Northeastern USA","authors":"Siddharth Narayan, Michael W. Beck, Paul Wilson, Christopher Thomas, Alexandra Guerrero, Christine Shepard, Borja G. Reguero, Guillermo Franco, Jane Carter Ingram, Dania Trespalacios","date":"2016-10-01","year":2016,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Center for Coastal Climate Resilience at UC Santa Cruz","The Nature Conservancy","RMS","Guy Carpenter & Company Ltd.","Wildlife Conservation Society"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://coastalresilience.org/coastal-wetlands-and-flood-damage-reduction/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The study highlights the economic benefits of coastal wetlands in reducing flood damage during Hurricane Sandy, estimating that these natural defenses saved over $625 million in property damages across the northeastern USA. It emphasizes the crucial role that wetland conservation and restoration can play in managing flood risks, particularly as coastal areas become increasingly vulnerable to severe weather events due to climate change.","insights":["Coastal wetlands reduced Hurricane Sandy-related property damages by an estimated $625 million.","In New Jersey alone, wetlands prevented approximately $425 million in flooding damages.","Coastal wetlands can reduce average annual flood losses by over 20% for properties located behind them.","The benefits of wetlands in reducing damage vary significantly by state, with Maryland experiencing up to a 29% reduction.","Wetlands were found to have cumulative benefits across different townships, providing flood protection even in areas with few remaining wetlands.","During Hurricane Sandy, coastal wetlands contributed to more than a 10% reduction in property damages in census tracts where they existed.","Wetlands can sometimes increase flooding in areas directly in front of them by blocking water flow, similar to the effects of man-made barriers.","The study underscores the importance of integrating natural defenses like wetlands into risk management and insurance industry models.","Loss of wetlands due to urban development significantly heightens flood risks for surrounding communities.","Incorporating the protective value of wetlands into financial models can promote conservation and enhance coastal resilience strategies."],"keywords":["flood risk","natural defenses","storm surge","economic assessment","environmental conservation"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":291,"title":"\u2018Climate Value at Risk\u2019 of Global Financial Assets","authors":"Simon Dietz, Alex Bowen, Charlie Dixon, Philip Gradwell","date":"2016-04-04","year":2016,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Nature Climate Change"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/nclimate2972","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This 2016 Nature Climate Change letter by Dietz, Bowen, Dixon, and Gradwell introduces \"climate value at risk\" (climate VaR) as a framework for estimating the impact of 21st-century climate change on the present market value of global financial assets. Using a Monte Carlo simulation of an extended DICE integrated assessment model, the authors find an expected climate VaR of 1.8% (US$2.5 trillion) under business-as-usual emissions, rising to 16.9% (US$24.2 trillion) at the 99th percentile. Limiting warming to 2\u00b0C reduces expected climate VaR by 0.6 percentage points and makes financial assets worth an expected 0.2% more in present value terms including mitigation costs.","insights":["Expected climate VaR of global financial assets under business-as-usual is 1.8% (US$2.5 trillion based on a US$143.3 trillion asset base); the 99th percentile risk is 16.9% or US$24.2 trillion, a tail risk exceeding the estimated US$5 trillion total market cap of fossil-fuel companies.","Limiting warming to 2\u00b0C reduces expected climate VaR by 0.6 percentage points and the 99th percentile by 7.7 percentage points, demonstrating that mitigation is disproportionately valuable for tail-risk reduction rather than mean-loss reduction.","Including mitigation costs, global financial assets have an expected present value 0.2% higher under a 2\u00b0C scenario than BAU, but ~65% of the simulated distribution shows BAU outperforming in PV terms, reflecting near-term drag from abatement costs (including stranded fossil-fuel assets).","Most climate VaR accrues in the second half of the century; applying a high 7% discount rate cuts expected climate VaR to 1.0% (99th percentile 7.7%), illustrating how discount rate choice is a first-order lever for whether institutional investors price long-dated climate risk.","Climate sensitivity is the single most important parameter driving expected climate VaR, followed by TFP growth rate, with the curvature of the damage function least important, guidance for risk modelers on where to focus uncertainty reduction efforts.","The framework assumes current financial asset prices do not incorporate climate forecasts, making these estimates a measure of potential asset-price corrections rather than additional losses on top of already-repriced assets, relevant for ratings agencies and stress-testers.","Tail climate risk is structurally unhedgeable: global incidence of impacts, long required holding periods, and irreversibility (no reversion to prior trend growth path) mean standard portfolio diversification cannot eliminate the exposure."],"keywords":["climate value at risk","integrated assessment model","DICE model","asset price correction","tail risk","stranded assets","Monte Carlo simulation"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":582,"title":"An Imperfect Storm: Fat-Tailed Hurricane Damages, Insurance, and Climate Policy","authors":"Marc N. Conte; David L. Kelly","date":"2016-02-02","year":2016,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["University of Miami","Fordham University"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.herbert.miami.edu/_assets/files/repec/WP2016-01.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A working paper by Marc N. Conte of Fordham and David L. Kelly of the University of Miami, dated 2 February 2016, on whether aggregate US hurricane damages are fat tailed and what that means for insurance and climate policy. Two tests reject the hypothesis that the damage distribution is thin tailed at the 95 per cent confidence level, even after correcting for inflation, population and per capita income growth, and the point estimates of the shape parameter indicate a distribution with finite mean but infinite variance. The second half develops a microfoundations model of insurance and storm size that generates the fat tail, and identifies its source: the distribution of the number of properties within a random geographical area lying in a hurricane's path. The authors confirm that the distribution of US coastal city population is itself fat tailed, and show both empirically and theoretically that other random variation, including the distribution of hurricane strength and the distribution of damages across individual properties, does not generate fat tails. They then consider climate mitigation, policies encouraging adaptation, reducing subsidies for coastal development, and disaster relief policies that distort insurance markets.","insights":["The policy conclusion is the sharpest thing in the paper and it is uncomfortable: such policies **can reduce the thickness of the tail but do not affect the shape parameter or the existence of the fat tail**. Adaptation and subsidy reform change the scale of catastrophe, not its character, so an instrument that needs a finite variance to price will never get one.","Locating the fat tail in the spatial distribution of exposed properties rather than in hurricane intensity is a structural finding with an obvious corollary: the tail is a land-use artifact, so the lever is where building happens rather than how strong storms get.","Infinite variance is the technical reason catastrophe risk resists ordinary insurance pricing, and the paper establishes it for US hurricanes specifically rather than in the abstract.","The same two authors published a 2021 Annual Review survey of fat tails in environmental economics; this working paper is the hurricane-specific result rather than the general treatment.","Disaster relief is named as a distortion of insurance markets rather than as a backstop, so public post-event spending is modelled here as part of the pricing problem rather than as its remedy."],"keywords":["fat tails","hurricane damages","infinite variance","shape parameter","property insurance","coastal development subsidy","disaster relief","catastrophe risk"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":80,"title":"Lessons from U.S. Coastal Wind Pools About Climate Finance and Politics","authors":"Donald T. Hornstein","date":"2016-01-01","year":2016,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Boston College Environmental Affairs Law Review","University of North Carolina School of Law"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://scholarship.law.unc.edu/faculty_publications/129/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This resource discusses the experiences and insights gained from U.S. Coastal Wind Pools to inform better practices in climate finance and the associated political landscape. Through analysis, it highlights the interplay between financial strategies and political actions in addressing climate-related challenges.","insights":["U.S. Coastal Wind Pools provide a framework for understanding localized climate finance mechanisms.","Political dynamics play a crucial role in the success of climate finance initiatives.","Collaboration among stakeholders is essential for effective climate action.","Financial strategies must be adaptable to changing environmental conditions.","Lessons learned from wind pools can inform future resilience financing approaches.","Public policy can significantly influence investment in climate-resilient projects.","Evaluating stakeholder interests helps identify potential barriers to effective financing.","Transparency in funding allocations enhances trust and engagement among communities."],"keywords":["stakeholder engagement","public policy","environmental finance","localized strategies","investment dynamics"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":9,"title":"NDC Partnership","authors":"","date":"2016-01-01","year":2016,"resource_type":"Initiative / Finance Model","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization","Policy / Regulation / Supervision"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["NDC Partnership"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://ndcpartnership.org/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The NDC Partnership is a global coalition aimed at accelerating climate action by supporting countries in implementing their Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) under the Paris Agreement. With over 260 members, including countries and institutions, it mobilizes tailored support for developing countries to enhance their climate and development efforts. The partnership emphasizes coordinated resources, collective impact, and the integration of climate ambitions into national agendas.","insights":["The NDC Partnership was established in 2016 to address the implementation of the Paris Agreement.","It has more than 260 members, including developed and developing countries as well as various institutions.","The partnership has mobilized over USD 1.8 billion in technical assistance and capacity building support.","Countries define their needs, and the partnership coordinates tailored support from its members.","The NDC Partnership focuses on supporting three key pathways: implementation, finance, and ambition.","The partnership serves as a neutral broker to facilitate resource deployment and best practice sharing.","It recognizes the diverse needs of its members and tailors support accordingly.","The governance structure includes Co-Chairs and a Steering Committee for strategic guidance.","The aim is to ensure that support aligns with national priorities to improve efficiency and effectiveness.","The NDC Partnership plays a crucial role in linking climate action with sustainable development goals."],"keywords":["coalition","sustainability","partnership","support","governance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":600,"title":"Leveraging Catastrophe Bonds as a Mechanism for Resilient Infrastructure Project Finance","authors":"Shalini Vajjhala; James Rhodes","date":"2015-12-01","year":2015,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","Blended / Catalytic / Outcomes-Based Finance"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Rockefeller Foundation","Swiss Re","RMS"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.refocuspartners.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/RE.bound-Program-Report-December-2015.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"Leveraging Catastrophe Bonds as a Mechanism for Resilient Infrastructure Project Finance, the December 2015 report of the RE.bound Program, an initiative of re:focus partners led by Shalini Vajjhala and James Rhodes as principal investigators and lead authors and funded by the Rockefeller Foundation, with RMS and Swiss Re as collaborating organisations. It runs under the programme's standing header, Insuring for Resilience. re:focus partners describes itself as a design firm developing integrated resilience solutions and innovative public-private partnerships for vulnerable communities, and identifying systemic opportunities to create both public value and new private investment potential through projects with sound financial returns and economic, social and environmental integrity. The report is the concept paper for using catastrophe bond structures to finance resilient infrastructure rather than only to transfer post-event loss.","insights":["The move the report makes is to run a catastrophe bond backwards: instead of paying out after a loss, the structure prices the loss the project will prevent, so the premium saving becomes the project's revenue.","The collaborator list is the argument's credibility: a risk modeller and a reinsurer alongside a design firm and a foundation, which is the exact set of parties needed to price avoided loss and then sell it."],"keywords":["resilience bond","catastrophe bond","RE.bound","project finance","avoided loss","premium reduction","public-private partnership","re:focus partners"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":586,"title":"The Triple Dividend of Resilience: Realising Development Goals Through the Multiple Benefits of Disaster Risk Management","authors":"Thomas Tanner; Swenja Surminski; Emily Wilkinson; Robert Reid; Jun Rentschler; Sumati Rajput","date":"2015-01-01","year":2015,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Resilience Returns & the Economic Case","Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Overseas Development Institute","World Bank","Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.gfdrr.org/sites/default/files/publication/The_Triple_Dividend_of_Resilience.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Triple Dividend of Resilience, subtitled Realising development goals through the multiple benefits of disaster risk management, a 2015 report produced jointly by the Overseas Development Institute and the World Bank through GFDRR, by Thomas Tanner, Swenja Surminski, Emily Wilkinson, Robert Reid, Jun Rentschler and Sumati Rajput. It is the source document for the triple dividend framing: that disaster risk management delivers avoided losses when a hazard strikes, unlocked economic potential from reduced background risk even when no hazard strikes, and development co-benefits from the investment itself. The argument reframes disaster risk management from a cost incurred against a probability into an investment with returns that accrue whether or not the disaster happens, which is what makes the second and third dividends the important ones.","insights":["The second dividend is the one that matters for financing and the one most often dropped when the phrase is quoted: reduced background risk unlocks economic activity that would not otherwise happen, so the return does not depend on the hazard occurring. An instrument that only pays on a trigger captures the first dividend and leaves the second on the table.","Swenja Surminski is among the co-authors, which places the framework in the same body of insurance and resilience work as her later writing on risk transfer."],"keywords":["triple dividend","avoided losses","unlocked economic potential","development co-benefits","disaster risk management","GFDRR","background risk"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":39,"title":"Floodplain Conservation as a Flood Mitigation Strategy: Examining Costs and Benefits","authors":"Carolyn Kousky, Margaret Walls","date":"2014-05-22","year":2014,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Nature-Based Solutions & Natural Capital","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Missouri"],"institutions":["Ecological Economics","Resources for the Future"],"permissions":"Paywall / Proprietary","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolecon.2014.05.001","access":"paywall","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"This 2014 peer-reviewed study by Resources for the Future evaluates the costs and benefits of floodplain conservation as a flood damage reduction strategy, using the Meramec Greenway in St. Louis County, Missouri as a case study. Using FEMA's Hazus-MH model at the parcel level and a hedonic property value model, the authors estimate annual avoided flood damages of $7.7 million, an annual opportunity cost of $17.2 million, and annual proximity benefits capitalized in home prices of ~$23 million for ~9,000 acres of protected floodplain. The proximity benefits alone exceed opportunity costs, and are more than three times the avoided flood damages.","insights":["Average annual avoided flood damages from the Meramec Greenway (9,000 acres protected) are estimated at $7.7 million/year using parcel-level Hazus-MH modeling; without the Greenway, annual flood damages in St. Louis County's Meramec floodplain would be ~59% higher.","Annual opportunity cost of the conserved floodplain lands is $17.2 million (based on $344M total present value annualized at 5% in perpetuity), meaning avoided flood damages alone offset only ~45% of opportunity costs, flood risk reduction alone does not justify the investment.","Hedonic model finds every 1,000 ft closer to a protected area raises home sale price by ~1% (~$2,156 for a median-priced home); aggregated across 36,000+ proximate parcels, this yields ~$352.6M in total capitalized value, or ~$22.9M/year, more than three times the avoided flood damage estimate and exceeding opportunity costs.","Spatial distribution of flood risk is highly skewed: 50% of protected parcels have an average annual loss per acre below $6,407, while the top 10% exceed $52,583/acre, demonstrating that targeted parcel selection could substantially improve the cost-effectiveness of floodplain conservation for flood mitigation specifically.","100-year flood damages to counterfactual development (2,075 hypothetical single-family homes on 2,178 protected acres) are estimated at $103.6 million in a single event, providing a tangible worst-case exposure figure for capital allocators evaluating floodplain development risk.","The analysis illustrates that floodplain conservation co-benefits (recreation, aesthetics, proximity value) are substantially larger than pure flood-risk-reduction benefits in this setting, suggesting that flood mitigation framing alone understates the full return and that multi-benefit funding coalitions (FEMA grants + parks + water quality funds) could unlock greater combined value."],"keywords":["floodplain conservation","hedonic property values","Hazus-MH","benefit-cost analysis","riparian greenway","co-benefits valuation","parcel-level flood modeling"],"has_attachment":false},{"nnn":259,"title":"Building Resilience : Integrating Climate and Disaster Risk into Development","authors":"","date":"2013-11-01","year":2013,"resource_type":"Report","themes":["Adaptation Finance Flows & Capital Mobilization"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["World Bank"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1596/16639","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The World Bank's resource on integrating climate and disaster risk into development underscores the urgent need for building climate resilience to mitigate poverty and foster prosperity. It argues for proactive investments and effective strategies that interconnect climate change and development challenges, emphasizing the importance of financial frameworks, stakeholder engagement, and institutional coordination. The resource supports its arguments with case studies and stresses the necessity of collaboration among various stakeholders to enhance resilience and manage risks effectively.","insights":["Investments in climate resilience can yield long-term benefits, despite higher initial costs.","Vulnerable populations face the greatest risks from climate-related disasters, exacerbating poverty and inequality.","Proactive measures such as early warning systems have proven effective in saving lives and reducing disaster impacts.","Collaboration among stakeholders, including government, communities, and the private sector, is essential for effective climate resilience planning.","Predictable financing over extended periods is required to build effective resilience programs."],"keywords":["disaster risk management","financial instruments","community resilience","participatory planning","institutional frameworks"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":591,"title":"MetroCat Re Ltd. (Series 2013-1): The First Storm Surge Catastrophe Bond","authors":"Artemis","date":"2013-07-01","year":2013,"resource_type":"Case Study / Deal Profile","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Capital Markets / Credit / Risk Pricing","Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience"],"geographies":["US"],"institutions":["Metropolitan Transportation Authority","Goldman Sachs","S&P Global","National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration","United States Geological Survey"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/metrocat-re-ltd-series-2013-1/","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The Artemis deal directory entry for MetroCat Re Ltd. (Series 2013-1), the first catastrophe bond written purely on storm surge. The cedent is First Mutual Transportation Assurance Co., the New York State-licensed captive insurer and subsidiary of the New York Metropolitan Transportation Authority; MetroCat Re Ltd. is a Bermuda special purpose insurer issuing a single tranche that collateralises a reinsurance agreement with FMTAC. Size $200 million against a preliminary $125 million, issued July 2013, three years of per-occurrence cover, parametric trigger, rated BB minus by S&P, with GC Securities as lead manager, joint structuring agent and sole bookrunner and Goldman Sachs as co-senior manager and joint structuring agent, and RMS as risk modeller. The trigger is recorded storm surge height: an event index of 8.5 feet or more for Area A, the tidal gauges at The Battery, Sandy Hook and Rockaway Inlet, or 15.5 feet or more for Area B, East Creek and Kings Point. S&P reports that RMS data make The Battery and Kings Point the gauges most correlated with MTA exposure, and those were the two areas whose flooding entered subway and transit tunnels under Sandy, so they carry the highest weighting. On trigger the payment is 100 per cent of outstanding principal with no sliding scale. Cover is limited to named storms that were tropical cyclones, tropical storms or hurricanes at some point, so extra-tropical systems such as the Great Nor'easter of 1992 are excluded. RMS found no non-hurricane has ever breached the trigger, and historically only Hurricane Donna in 1960 at a modelled 9.52 feet in Area A and Superstorm Sandy in 2012 at 10.93 feet would have. Surge data comes from NOAA and USGS gauges, with mobile backup gauges to be deployed at all five locations and high-water marks as a final fallback.","insights":["The MTA's stated motivation is the benefit itself: expand and diversify its sources of reinsurance, and obtain parametric cover that should **pay out more quickly than indemnity coverage**. Liquidity timing, not loss compensation, is what the instrument delivers, and the sponsor says so in its own words.","A 100 per cent principal payment with no sliding scale is a deliberate simplification that buys speed at the cost of basis risk, and the entry states the consequence plainly: the payout may not necessarily correlate with the sponsor's actual losses.","The sponsor is a **captive insurer**: a public transit authority using its own captive to reach the capital markets directly, rather than buying cover from a commercial insurer that would then reinsure it.","Two design details are worth carrying: the exclusion of extra-tropical cyclones means a 1992-type nor'easter is uncovered even though it can produce surge, and the mobile backup gauges plus high-water-mark fallback are an answer to the data-failure risk every parametric trigger carries."],"keywords":["catastrophe bond","storm surge","parametric trigger","captive insurer","insurance-linked securities","MTA","basis risk","special purpose insurer","tidal gauge"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":79,"title":"Financing the Resilient City","authors":"Jeb Brugmann","date":"2012-04-01","year":2012,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Infrastructure & Real Estate Resilience","State / Local / Community Resilience Finance"],"geographies":["Global"],"institutions":["Environment and Urbanization","The Next Practice"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956247812437130","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"The paper 'Financing the Resilient City' advocates for a paradigm shift in urban risk reduction towards enhancing the performance and resilience of urban assets and systems. By positioning disaster risk reduction as an investment opportunity, the paper emphasizes the need for innovative financing mechanisms, comprehensive planning, and multi-sector collaboration to address the financial risks of climate-related disasters in rapidly urbanizing areas.","insights":["The strategy reframes the adaptation problem from risk reduction as an end in itself to financing the performance of urban assets, areas and systems, which is what lets conventional private investment flows recognise it.","Resilience is proposed as an urban design and investment metric measuring the ability of assets to perform for users and investors across a wide range of conditions, so the metric is performance rather than hazard.","The explicit premise is that international adaptation funds will not mobilise enough capital, so the paper is about capital above and beyond those funds.","Mainstreaming is the stated objective: a performance-oriented approach supplies a business logic that attracts conventional investment into climate and disaster risk reduction."],"keywords":["urban development","investment strategies","risk management","infrastructure","economic performance"],"has_attachment":true},{"nnn":448,"title":"A Methodological Approach for Pricing Flood Insurance and Evaluating Loss Reduction Measures: Application to Texas","authors":"Jeffrey Czajkowski; Howard Kunreuther; Erwann Michel-Kerjan","date":"2012-01-01","year":2012,"resource_type":"Academic Paper","themes":["Insurance / Risk Transfer","Climate Risk Data / Models / Analytics","Resilience Returns & the Economic Case"],"geographies":["US","Texas"],"institutions":["University of Pennsylvania"],"permissions":"Open Access","url":"https://impact.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/WhartonRiskCenter_TexasFloodInsurancePricingStudy.pdf","access":"source","mirror_url":null,"mirror_licence":null,"summary":"A Wharton Risk Management and Decision Processes Center white paper from January 2012 presenting a methodological approach for pricing flood insurance and evaluating loss reduction measures, applied to Texas. The paper's contribution is the method rather than the state: it sets out how a risk-based flood premium can be constructed and how the effect of loss reduction measures on that premium can be evaluated, then demonstrates the approach on Texas data. The authors are the Travelers and Willis Re research fellow Jeffrey Czajkowski, and Howard Kunreuther and Erwann Michel-Kerjan, the centre's co-directors, whose long-standing subject is how society manages low-probability high-consequence events. The work sits at the point where catastrophe modelling, insurance pricing and mitigation evaluation meet, which is the analytical foundation any mitigation-discount scheme requires: without a defensible link from a physical loss reduction measure to a premium, an insurer cannot price the mitigation and a homeowner cannot be rewarded for it.","insights":["The contribution is methodological: it sets out how to price a flood premium and how to evaluate what a loss reduction measure does to that premium, which is the analytical step a mitigation-discount scheme needs in order to be priced rather than asserted.","Applying the method to Texas makes it a worked example rather than a framework, so the approach can be checked against a real exposure set.","It dates from 2012 and predates both the NFIP's Risk Rating 2.0 and the current private flood market, so it should be read as the methodological ancestor of those rather than as a description of current practice.","The authors' affiliations include insurer-funded research fellowships, which is context for a paper about how insurers should price."],"keywords":["flood insurance pricing","loss reduction measures","catastrophe modeling","risk-based premium","Texas","mitigation evaluation","Wharton Risk Center"],"has_attachment":true}]},"editorial":{"_readme":"CURATOR-OWNED. No pipeline writes this file. scripts/export_repository_site.py owns repository.json beside it and overwrites that file on every run, so any prose kept there would be destroyed by the next export. Everything a person writes lives here instead, and this is the file to edit to change the words on the landing page. No count is ever stored: numbers are written as {tokens} that the page fills from the payload at load, so this file cannot go stale against the corpus.","updated":"2026-09-21","_tokens":"Usable anywhere in intro prose. {records} how many records. {yearMin} {yearMax} the publication range. {themes} how many finance themes. {sourceTypes} how many kinds of source. {organizations} how many organizations are named. {recentFrom} the first year of the recent window. {recentShare} the share published from that year onward, as a percentage. An unknown token is left on the page as written, so a typo is visible rather than silent.","_removed_2026_09_16":"Three blocks were REMOVED on curator instruction, along with the page sections that read them: start_here (foundational reading), questions (question-led saved views) and lab_outputs (the Lab and partner feed). They are not lost and are not to be restored on the assumption that they went missing. The shapes they used are recorded in site/README.md so the decision can be reversed deliberately rather than reconstructed.","intro":{"beta":"Beta release. The collection is growing and some records are still being reviewed, so expect gaps and the occasional rough edge. Please review the underlying research before making decisions.","lede":"Academic research, case studies, deal profiles, and media coverage on how risk reduction and re